/* DealCraft — Company Law, Chapter 1 supplement: application and cross-angle questions (Tjio Ch 1)
   Lessons 1A–1D. Ids cl1-033 onward. Paragraph refs [01.xxx] are to the chapter. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.companyLaw = (window.DC_BANK.companyLaw||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 1A Introduction ---------- */
{ id:"cl1-033", lesson:"1A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mei holds every share in Mei Trading Pte Ltd and is its sole director. The company owns a warehouse. Short of cash, Mei signs a sale agreement in her own name, selling the warehouse to a friend as 'my property'.",
  prompt:"On the text's account of the company, what is the core problem with Mei's sale?",
  options:[
    {t:"The warehouse belongs to the company, not to Mei. Her 100% shareholding gives her shares (rights against the company), not ownership of its assets, so she has nothing to sell in her own name.", correct:true,
     why:"This is the starting point of company law applied to a one-person company. Once the business is incorporated, the separate corporate entity owns the assets, and the shareholders own something quite different: shares, which are choses in action giving them rights against the company in terms of its due administration. That is true whether there are a thousand shareholders or one.\n\nMei's position as sole shareholder and sole director means she can cause the company to sell the warehouse, by acting as its director in the company's name. But that is the company selling its own property. Selling it as 'my property' confuses the two layers that incorporation creates. Salomon is the classic illustration of how seriously the law takes this separation even in a one-man company."},
    {t:"There is no problem: a 100% shareholder is the beneficial owner of all the company's assets", correct:false,
     why:"This is the co-ownership view the text rejects. Even a sole shareholder has no proprietary interest in the company's assets while it is a going concern. Her interest is a spes in the company's proper administration. The bounded co-ownership claim to the surplus fund only appears after creditors are paid, typically on a winding up."},
    {t:"There is no problem because she is the sole director, and directors own the assets they manage", correct:false,
     why:"Directors manage the company's property; they do not own it. Historically, directors of joint stock companies were treated as trustees of the company's property in their hands, and some modern judges still use that language, but even on that view the property is the company's. Being sole director gives Mei authority to act for the company, not title to its assets."},
    {t:"The only problem is that she did not first obtain a shareholders' resolution", correct:false,
     why:"A resolution is beside the point if Mei is purporting to sell in her own name. The issue is title, not internal procedure: the asset belongs to a different legal person. If the company itself sold the warehouse, the question of internal approvals might arise, but that is a separate matter."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.003], [01.005]; fn 21, 35", takeaway:"Even a sole shareholder owns shares, not the company's assets." },

{ id:"cl1-034", lesson:"1A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Hup Seng Pte Ltd is profitable and solvent, with $2m in the bank. A shareholder holding 10% writes to the board: 'As 10% owner, $200,000 of that cash is my property. Please transfer it to me.'",
  prompt:"How should the board respond, on the text's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Refuse: while the company is a going concern, a shareholder has no proprietary right in the corporate fund. His co-ownership claim only appears after creditors are paid, and perhaps only on a final winding up.", correct:true,
     why:"The text separates the segregated fund of corporate assets from the shareholders' 'ultimate bounded co-ownership rights' in that fund. The fund (the $2m) exists now and belongs to the company. The co-ownership rights only become evident after creditors are paid off, and perhaps only when the company is finally wound up. While the company is a going concern those rights 'in fact do not exist'.\n\nWhat the shareholder has today is a chose in action in seeing to the company's proper administration. He can insist that the constitution and the Act are observed, vote, and receive dividends if and when the company properly declares them. He cannot carve his percentage out of the bank balance. Note also the word 'bounded': even at the end, his share of the surplus is limited to what is left after creditors."},
    {t:"Pay him: a shareholder can withdraw his proportion of surplus cash at any time while the company is solvent", correct:false,
     why:"No such right exists. Distributions to shareholders happen through the company's own decisions (dividends, capital reductions, buy-backs) and are policed by the capital maintenance rules in Part IV. Solvency does not convert a shareholder's spes into ownership of the company's cash."},
    {t:"Pay him, but only if the other 90% of shareholders agree", correct:false,
     why:"Even unanimous shareholder consent does not change the fact that the cash is the company's property. The company could choose to make a lawful distribution, but that is the company acting, subject to creditor-protection rules, not shareholders taking their own property."},
    {t:"Refuse, because shareholders own nothing at all until winding up", correct:false,
     why:"This reaches the right outcome for the wrong reason. The shareholder does own something now: his shares, which are property. What he does not own is any part of the company's assets. Keep the two layers apart."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.005]", takeaway:"Going concern: the fund is the company's; the shareholder's claim to it is only residual and later." },

{ id:"cl1-035", lesson:"1A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A manager negotiated a supply contract strictly on behalf of her company, made clear she was acting only for it, and gave no personal assurance. The goods turn out to be defective. The buyer sues the manager personally in negligence because the company has few assets.",
  prompt:"Which point in the text bears most directly on this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where individuals acted for the company without assuming personal responsibility for a purely corporate transaction, blame may not be pinned on them, so the separate entity cannot be avoided: the liability is the company's", correct:true,
     why:"The text uses exactly this kind of case to show that even a contractarian has to accept the company's separate existence. Where it is not possible to pin the blame on any individual who acted on behalf of the company and did not assume personal responsibility for what was purely a corporate transaction, the entity is the only answer (Williams v Natural Life Health Foods, applied in Singapore in Sim Tee Meng v Haw Wan Sin David, fn 12).\n\nOn these facts the manager acted only for the company and gave no personal assurance, so the starting point is that the claim lies against the company. The fact that the company has few assets does not by itself shift liability to her; that is the cost to outsiders of dealing with a limited company. The position can be different for fraud, where a person cannot hide behind the company (Standard Chartered v Pakistan National Shipping, also in fn 12)."},
    {t:"Employees are always personally liable for corporate torts, because a company can act only through people", correct:false,
     why:"That a company acts through people does not mean those people always answer personally. If it did, the separate entity would add little. The text's point is that, absent an assumption of personal responsibility (or a wrong like fraud), the transaction is the company's and so is the liability."},
    {t:"The buyer can sue the shareholders instead, since the company has few assets", correct:false,
     why:"Limited liability means shareholders are liable only to the extent of what they have committed or promised to contribute. A company's lack of assets is not a ground for reaching the shareholders. This option confuses separate personality with an insurance policy for creditors."},
    {t:"Nobody is liable, because the entity is a fiction", correct:false,
     why:"The text's point is the reverse. Because the entity is real, there is someone to be liable: the company. Treating the entity as a fiction would leave the buyer with no defendant at all in a purely corporate transaction."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.006], fn 12", takeaway:"No personal assumption of responsibility → the liability is the company's." },

{ id:"cl1-036", lesson:"1A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A reform paper proposes that every Singapore company must insert a binding 'corporate purpose' statement into its constitution, setting out its social and environmental role.",
  prompt:"Which caution from the text's discussion of corporate purpose is most relevant?",
  options:[
    {t:"Mandated purpose statements risk going the way of wide objects clauses, which were drafted to circumvent the ultra vires rule. Elsewhere the trend is to permit rather than mandate them.", correct:true,
     why:"The text notes that the separate entity is back in focus with the reinvention of corporate purpose, based on the company's own sustainability, its role in society and its constitution. But footnote 17 records Davies's warning that mandating purposes will end up like the old wide objects clauses, which companies drafted as broadly as possible to escape the ultra vires rule. A compulsory statement tends to become boilerplate.\n\nThe footnote also gives the comparative picture. The French Civil Code (Art 1835) merely 'allows' a company to state its raison d'être in its articles, and the 30-odd US states with constituency statutes made them permissive, not mandatory. Voluntary codes are said to be more likely. This also fits the text's internal view: in theory it is up to the constituents to decide the company's purposes."},
    {t:"Companies are legally barred from having any purpose other than profit", correct:false,
     why:"The text does not say this. It says that in practice shareholder primacy is still the starting point, but that the constituents can in theory decide the purposes the company pursues, and that corporate purpose is being reinvented. A bar on non-profit purposes would be inconsistent with all of that."},
    {t:"Purpose statements are unnecessary because the State already sets each company's purpose", correct:false,
     why:"The State confers personality and sets mandatory rules, but the text treats purpose as largely a matter for the internal, contractual side of the company. The State does not assign each company a purpose."},
    {t:"Purpose statements would make all constituents equally important", correct:false,
     why:"The text says expressly that a broad, inclusive view of company law 'does not mean … that all corporate constituents are of equal importance'. A purpose statement would not change that, and it is not the caution the text raises."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.004], [01.007], fn 17", takeaway:"Mandated purpose clauses risk becoming boilerplate; the trend is permissive." },

{ id:"cl1-037", lesson:"1A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because the text prefers a broad, inclusive view of the constituents behind the company, it treats all constituents (shareholders, creditors, employees and so on) as equally important.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text says one can ignore many constituents and focus on management and shareholders, but that would miss the 'rich tapestry' visible with a broader and more inclusive view. Any strict contractual view must take account of communitarian concerns arising from the many constituencies behind the entity.\n\nIt then immediately adds that this 'does not mean, however, that all corporate constituents are of equal importance'. Elsewhere it notes that shareholder primacy is still the starting point in practice. The right reading is inclusive but ranked: many voices count, not all count equally."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This overstates the text. Inclusiveness is about which constituents the analysis takes into account, not about giving them equal weight. The text expressly denies that all constituents are of equal importance, and it accepts that shareholder primacy remains the practical starting point."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.004], [01.007]", takeaway:"Inclusive view ≠ equal weight; shareholder primacy is still the starting point." },

{ id:"cl1-038", lesson:"1A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The text records the view that company law today is 'but a series of boilerplate provisions'. What is that boilerplate said to do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Reduce the cost users of the law would otherwise incur in bargaining for such a legal structure", correct:true,
     why:"This is a major theme of contractarian company law. If the interactions among directors, managers, shareholders, employees and creditors are largely contractual, then the Act, the default rules (such as the model constitution) and the mandatory rules are a standard form that saves everyone from negotiating the structure from scratch. The boilerplate reduces transaction costs.\n\nThe text accepts this as far as it goes but pushes back on a strict contractual view. The quotations it cites suggest that communitarian concerns, from the many constituencies behind the entity, also have to be taken into account. And in some situations the entity itself matters, for remedies and responsibility."},
    {t:"Prevent the parties from contracting at all", correct:false,
     why:"Boilerplate in the contractarian sense supplies terms the parties would probably have agreed to anyway. It reduces bargaining, it does not prohibit it. Mandatory rules, which cannot be derogated from, are a separate category."},
    {t:"Protect only the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The boilerplate serves all the parties who would otherwise have to bargain over the structure, not just shareholders. The text's interactions include directors, managers, employees and creditors."},
    {t:"Replace the need for any mandatory rules", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the interactions are governed by contract, default rules and 'a certain number of mandatory rules found in the Act itself'. Mandatory rules are still needed internally to prevent abuse and externally to protect third parties."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.004], [01.021]", takeaway:"Contractarian view: company law as boilerplate that saves bargaining costs." },

{ id:"cl1-039", lesson:"1A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which statement best captures the book's own methodological position on the company?",
  options:[
    {t:"Legal personality is conferred by the State, and the State, aware of the constituents' needs, sets enough mandatory rules to reify the structure while leaving room for individual liberty", correct:true,
     why:"The book takes a middle path. Legal personality is something conferred by the State and a product of company law, and the State is conscious of the constituents' needs when it creates mandatory corporate rules. At the end of the chapter it adds that there are enough mandatory rules in the Act to reify the corporate structure, in a way that harmonises individual liberty and state preferences.\n\nIt expressly eschews an overly contractarian or overly contextual approach, and recommends an integrative approach that keeps the external (property, organisational) and internal (contractual) dimensions in view. It also sees regulation as furthering good and sustainable business rather than impinging on it."},
    {t:"The company is purely a nexus of contracts; the State's role is limited to registration", correct:false,
     why:"This is the strict contractarian position the book declines to adopt. It accepts that much of the internal law is contractual, but insists the entity matters for remedies and responsibility and that the State's mandatory rules give the company its structure."},
    {t:"The company is a real person that exists independently of the State", correct:false,
     why:"The book's position is the opposite: personality is conferred by the State. A 'real entity' theory that the company exists independently of law is not the book's approach."},
    {t:"Company law is purely public regulation of business", correct:false,
     why:"The text treats much of company law as private and contractual, and records the view that even the Companies Act is a form of private law. It sees industry codes as a hybrid of private ordering and public regulation, not as purely public law."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.006]–[01.007], [01.029]", takeaway:"State-conferred personality + enough mandatory rules, balanced with contractual freedom." },

{ id:"cl1-040", lesson:"1A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the text warn that court decisions about 'the company' must be read with some care?",
  options:[
    {t:"Pragmatism often trumps a generalised notion of what the company is, so corporate theory gives a picture that is correct from some perspectives but not others", correct:true,
     why:"The text says that in many cases pragmatism trumps a generalised theory of the company. Corporate theory is useful, but it usually gives a view of the company that is correct from certain perspectives and not others.\n\nIt uses the image of a legal prism made up of common law, equity and statute: the image of the company changes once the subject matter or the viewer shifts even slightly. So a case that treats the company as a mere vehicle for its members in one context should not be read as rejecting separate personality in general. This is the practical side of the 'when to see the entity, when to look through it' puzzle."},
    {t:"Most company law cases were wrongly decided", correct:false,
     why:"The text does not say cases are wrong. It says they are pragmatic and context-specific, which is why a single theory rarely explains all of them."},
    {t:"Courts are bound to follow one official theory of the company", correct:false,
     why:"There is no official theory. The text's point is that no single theory fits every perspective, and courts decide according to context."},
    {t:"Only English cases are relevant in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"English cases are persuasive, and Malaysian, Australian and New Zealand cases are also used as guides. Jurisdiction is not the reason the text gives for reading cases carefully."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.002]", takeaway:"Cases are pragmatic; each theory is right only from some angles (the 'legal prism')." },

/* ---------- 1B Brief history ---------- */
{ id:"cl1-041", lesson:"1B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"England, 1850. Arthur bought shares in a company registered under the Joint Stock Companies Act 1844 and sold them two years ago; the transfer was registered. The company is now being wound up with large unpaid debts.",
  prompt:"What is Arthur's likely exposure?",
  options:[
    {t:"He can still be personally liable for the company's unsatisfied debts, because the 1844 Act gave no limited liability and that liability continued for three years after a registered transfer", correct:true,
     why:"The 1844 Act allowed incorporation by registration, but it did not include limited liability. Shareholders therefore remained personally liable for the unsatisfied liabilities of the company in its winding up. Footnote 28 adds that this personal liability continued for three years after any registered transfer of the shareholding.\n\nArthur's transfer was registered two years ago, so he is still within the three-year window. Selling his shares did not end his exposure. Compare a company formed after the Limited Liability Act 1855 with 'Limited' in its name: there, members are liable only to the extent of what they committed or promised to contribute."},
    {t:"Nothing: he has sold his shares and is no longer a member", correct:false,
     why:"Under the 1844 regime, ceasing to be a member did not immediately end liability. The three-year tail after a registered transfer was designed precisely so that members could not escape by selling out shortly before a collapse."},
    {t:"Only the unpaid amount on his shares, because the 1844 Act introduced limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"Limiting liability to the amount unpaid on shares is the limited liability model, and it came with the 1855 Act, not the 1844 Act. Under the 1844 Act liability for the company's debts was not capped."},
    {t:"Nothing, because a registered company is a separate person and its debts are never its members' debts", correct:false,
     why:"Separate personality and limited liability are different things, a point the text makes about Salomon. A company can be a separate person and still have members who are liable for its debts, as unlimited companies show. In 1850 registered companies had the first without the second."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.013], fn 28", takeaway:"1844: registered, but no limited liability; exposure ran 3 years after transfer." },

{ id:"cl1-042", lesson:"1B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The text says the most important trait older business associations might have shared with the modern company is separate legal personality. How does it say this trait is 'more accurately' described?",
  options:[
    {t:"Affirmative asset partitioning, or entity shielding", correct:true,
     why:"When discussing older forms of business association that pooled resources for profit, the text says the most important trait of the modern company is separate legal personality, 'or, as we will see later, more accurately affirmative asset partitioning or entity shielding' (citing Hansmann, Kraakman and Squire).\n\nEntity shielding protects the firm's assets from the personal creditors of its owners: a shareholder's creditor cannot seize the company's factory. That is distinct from owner shielding, which is limited liability, protecting the owners' personal assets from the firm's creditors. Chapter 2 develops both, and footnote 19 notes that Chapter 13 calls a related idea 'capital lock-in'."},
    {t:"Limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"Limited liability is owner shielding: it protects the owners from the firm's creditors. The text identifies the more fundamental trait as the reverse protection, entity shielding. It also notes that limited liability can be created for unincorporated entities, which suggests it is not the defining trait."},
    {t:"Transferable shares", correct:false,
     why:"Transferability was the early focus of the deed of settlement company (the 'joint stock' feature). But the text names separate personality, more accurately entity shielding, as the most important trait, not transferability."},
    {t:"Perpetual succession", correct:false,
     why:"Perpetual succession is a feature of incorporated bodies, but it is not the term the text uses here. The text's refinement of 'separate legal personality' is affirmative asset partitioning or entity shielding."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.008], fn 19", takeaway:"Separate personality ≈ affirmative asset partitioning (entity shielding)." },

{ id:"cl1-043", lesson:"1B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"If the Bubble Act was hardly enforced, why did its repeal in 1825 matter?",
  options:[
    {t:"It removed the legal risk of investing in unincorporated deed of settlement companies, whose numbers then grew, though wider investor protection had to wait for the 1844 Act", correct:true,
     why:"In practice the Bubble Act was not enforced in any systematic way, so deed of settlement companies flourished. But the Act still made them legally risky, because in effect they were what it prohibited. There were also periodic frauds and over-speculation in their shares. Meanwhile incorporated entities kept their advantages, such as suing and being sued in their own name, but needed state approval with the moral hazard that came with it.\n\nRepeal in 1825 removed the legal risk, and the number of unincorporated companies grew significantly. But repeal did not provide investor protection against other risks. That waited for the Joint Stock Companies Act 1844. Footnote 26 adds that even after repeal some courts still thought the common law prohibited the unincorporated joint stock company."},
    {t:"It introduced limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"Limited liability came in 1855. Repealing a prohibition does not create a new protection for investors."},
    {t:"It made incorporation available by registration", correct:false,
     why:"Incorporation by registration was the work of the 1844 Act. In 1825 incorporation still needed an Act of Parliament or a Royal Charter; what changed was that the unincorporated alternative stopped being illegal."},
    {t:"It abolished the South Sea Company", correct:false,
     why:"The repeal concerned the legality of unincorporated joint stock companies, not the fate of any particular chartered company."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.011], fn 26", takeaway:"1825 repeal: legalised the deed of settlement route; protection came in 1844." },

{ id:"cl1-044", lesson:"1B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"It is 1840. The members of an unincorporated deed of settlement company want to cap their liability for its debts.",
  prompt:"On the text, which route had the better chance of working at the time?",
  options:[
    {t:"Contracting with each third party for limited liability, a practice the courts were starting to recognise; a clause in the deed alone was of doubtful effect", correct:true,
     why:"The text says there may have been attempts to create limited liability for members 'through third-party contracting (as opposed to simply having a clause to such effect in the deed)', but that it was not always clear this worked. A clause in the deed binds the members among themselves; it cannot by itself bind outsiders who never agreed to it.\n\nFootnote 29 notes that courts had started recognising that companies could contract with third parties for limited liability for their shareholders (Hallet v Dowdell, 1852). The general statutory route only arrived with the Limited Liability Act 1855. The broader lesson, which Chapter 3 develops, is that limited liability is not unique to the corporation: it can be created for unincorporated entities."},
    {t:"Inserting a limited liability clause in the deed of settlement, which bound all creditors", correct:false,
     why:"A deed is an agreement among the members and trustees. Creditors are not parties to it and are not bound by its terms. That is why the text contrasts contracting with third parties with 'simply having a clause to such effect in the deed'."},
    {t:"Registering under the Limited Liability Act", correct:false,
     why:"In 1840 there was no Limited Liability Act; it was passed in 1855. Even registration under the 1844 Act, once available, did not give limited liability."},
    {t:"Adding 'Limited' to the company's name", correct:false,
     why:"Using 'Limited' was a disclosure condition attached to the statutory limited liability of 1855. Before that, putting the word in a name had no legal effect on members' liability."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.009], [01.013], fn 29", takeaway:"Pre-1855 limited liability: by contract with third parties, not by a deed clause." },

{ id:"cl1-045", lesson:"1B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: For much of the 19th century, 'the company' was regarded as the associated members rather than as a legal entity distinct from them.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"The text says full separate personality was accorded to the company only once it was eventually seen to be quite different from its members, and that 'the company' was regarded as the associated members rather than the legal entity for much of the 19th century (fn 33, Sealy).\n\nThat change in thinking led to an enormous increase in use of the corporate form, especially small private companies. It culminated in Salomon (1897), where the House of Lords treated even a one-man company as a person distinct from its controller. It also explains why the history is told as a gradual shift rather than a single moment."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite. For much of the 19th century the company was thought of as its associated members. Full separate personality came later, and Salomon is the point at which it was settled for small companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.013]–[01.014], fn 33", takeaway:"19th century: 'the company' = its members; full separateness came later." },

{ id:"cl1-046", lesson:"1B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why, according to the text, did unincorporated partnerships keep flourishing until the end of the 19th century even after limited liability became available?",
  options:[
    {t:"Lingering suspicion of limited liability for small companies made third parties reluctant to trade with them", correct:true,
     why:"For a while there was still suspicion of limited liability where small companies were concerned. Third parties' reluctance to trade with them may explain why unincorporated partnerships continued to flourish, and why some 'co-partnerships' were actually registered as unlimited companies. Footnote 34 notes that in Song Jianbo v Sunmax Global Capital Fund 1, the Singapore court discussed Houldsworth v City of Glasgow Bank (a co-partnership case) and declined to apply its rule to modern limited companies.\n\nOnly at the end of the century did the modern small private company fully crystallise, with wider acceptance of limited liability and separate personality culminating in Salomon in 1897."},
    {t:"Partnerships offered limited liability while companies did not", correct:false,
     why:"General partnerships did not offer limited liability; their partners were personally liable. The UK Limited Partnerships Act came only in 1907. The reason partnerships persisted was distrust of limited companies, not a liability advantage."},
    {t:"Companies could not be formed with fewer than 25 members", correct:false,
     why:"That was true under the 1844 Act, but the 1856 and 1862 Acts allowed smaller private companies with seven members. The text attributes the persistence of partnerships to suspicion of limited liability, not to membership minimums."},
    {t:"The Bubble Act still prohibited small companies", correct:false,
     why:"The Bubble Act was repealed in 1825. It has no role in the late-19th-century story."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.014], fn 34", takeaway:"Distrust of small limited companies kept partnerships alive until Salomon's era." },

{ id:"cl1-047", lesson:"1B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Once incorporation became widely desirable, where did the deed of settlement (trust-based) structure survive, and what does the text say this shows?",
  options:[
    {t:"In collective investment schemes such as the unit trust; it shows trust law's flexibility, based partly on its contractarian nature", correct:true,
     why:"After limited liability (or perhaps separate personality) became widely accepted, the deed of settlement structure fell out of favour, except for collective investment schemes like the unit trust. The text says the fact that trust law could have created a business structure at all is a good example of its flexibility, which rests partly on its contractarian nature.\n\nBut for actively managed businesses, both the partnership and the trust were overtaken by the company, even though the UK enacted a Limited Partnerships Act in 1907. The commercial trust has seen a recent revival, partly because of its tax treatment, which Chapter 3 examines (REITs and business trusts)."},
    {t:"In listed operating companies; it shows the trust is better than the company for active businesses", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite: for actively managed businesses the company overtook both the partnership and the trust. The trust survived in investment vehicles, not in ordinary operating businesses."},
    {t:"Nowhere; it disappeared entirely", correct:false,
     why:"It survived in unit trusts and, more recently, in REITs and business trusts. The text stresses its revival."},
    {t:"In government agencies; it shows the State prefers trusts", correct:false,
     why:"The text says nothing of the kind. The survival it identifies is in collective investment schemes."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.014]", takeaway:"Trust structure survived in unit trusts; shows trust law's contractarian flexibility." },

{ id:"cl1-048", lesson:"1B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The text calls the company 'an unruly horse' that has created enormous wealth. How does it explain why other business vehicles have not done as well?",
  options:[
    {t:"Perhaps political rent-seeking, but there must also be intrinsic advantages of the corporate structure that other vehicles find difficult to mimic", correct:true,
     why:"The text offers two explanations, and does not rely on just one. It mentions political rent-seeking (citing Ribstein's 'Why Corporations?'), meaning that companies may have benefited from favourable law and lobbying. But it adds that 'there must also be some intrinsic advantages to the corporate structure that other vehicles find difficult to mimic'.\n\nChapter 3 then examines the alternative vehicles and why they have revived. Partly it is tax treatment. Partly, as Stout argues there, the company's capital lock-in reassures creditors in a way partnerships and trusts struggle to match."},
    {t:"Other vehicles were prohibited by law until recently", correct:false,
     why:"Partnerships and trusts were never prohibited in this sense (the Bubble Act targeted unincorporated joint stock companies and was repealed in 1825). The text explains the company's dominance by rent-seeking and intrinsic advantages, not by prohibition."},
    {t:"Only companies can have limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"The text says expressly that limited liability can be created with unincorporated entities, and LLPs, LPs and business trusts all offer it today. So limited liability cannot be the explanation."},
    {t:"Other vehicles are always taxed more heavily", correct:false,
     why:"Tax often favours the alternatives: the text says the commercial trust has revived partly because of its tax treatment. Tax is a reason some businesses leave the company form, not a reason they stay in it."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.014], fn 38", takeaway:"Company's success: maybe rent-seeking, but also hard-to-copy intrinsic advantages." },

/* ---------- 1C Focus & organisation ---------- */
{ id:"cl1-049", lesson:"1C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A venture capital fund invests in a Singapore start-up through convertible preference shares, planning to exit through an SGX listing in five years. You want to research (i) the preference share terms and (ii) the SGX listing process.",
  prompt:"How much of this does the textbook cover?",
  options:[
    {t:"(i) is within scope, since the book follows the company up to outside financing including VC preference shares; (ii) is outside it, since listing and SGX rules are left to securities regulation texts", correct:true,
     why:"The book concentrates on the birth and life of the company up to the stage when it needs outside investors. That expressly includes mezzanine financing from venture capitalists with preference shares and other hybrid instruments, so the terms of the VC's convertible preference shares are covered (in Part IV).\n\nIt does not include listing on a securities exchange, even though, as the text notes, the prospect of an exit through listing may be what persuaded the venture capitalist to invest. SGX listing rules, and share trading issues like insider trading and market manipulation under the SFA, are left to a securities regulation text (fn 42, Tjio, Wan and Yee)."},
    {t:"Both are covered in detail", correct:false,
     why:"The book draws the line at listing. It acknowledges that many companies raising later-stage outside finance list on the SGX at the same time, but it still leaves the listing rules out of detailed treatment."},
    {t:"Neither is covered, since the book stops at incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"The book goes well beyond incorporation. It follows the company up to outside financing, including bank secured debt and VC financing."},
    {t:"Only (ii) is covered, because preference shares are a securities law topic", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the line. Preference shares as a means of financing the company are core company law and within the book. Listing and secondary trading are the securities law topics left out."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.016], fn 40, 42", takeaway:"VC preference shares: in. IPO and SGX rules: out." },

{ id:"cl1-050", lesson:"1C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the text treat the Code of Corporate Governance, and what is that Code mainly concerned with?",
  options:[
    {t:"No dedicated portion of the book, though it comes up when listed companies are discussed; its main concern is how boards of listed companies should be structured, increasingly alongside stewardship principles for institutional shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"Because the listing rules are outside the project, there is no specific portion of the book devoted to the Code of Corporate Governance. It does come up at various points where public companies listed on the SGX are discussed (fn 52 points to a specialist governance text).\n\nThe text says the Code's concern is largely with how boards of directors in publicly listed companies should be structured to ensure good governance. Increasingly there are also stewardship principles guiding how shareholders, especially institutional shareholders, should monitor what goes on in the company. The text later lists such codes as examples of soft law, and notes criticism of the Code's 'comply or explain' approach (fn 87)."},
    {t:"A full chapter; its concern is shareholders' personal remedies", correct:false,
     why:"There is no dedicated chapter, and shareholder remedies (Chapters 10 and 11) come from the Act and the common law, not from the Code of Corporate Governance."},
    {t:"It is ignored entirely because it applies only to private companies", correct:false,
     why:"The Code is aimed at listed companies, not private ones, and the book does refer to it when discussing them. It is not dealt with as a topic of its own."},
    {t:"It is treated as subsidiary legislation under the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"The Code is soft law. The text groups it with the Take-over Code and the Code on Collective Investment Schemes as examples of codes that are more responsive to the market than legislation."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.018], [01.027], fn 52, 87", takeaway:"CG Code: no chapter; it's about listed boards (plus stewardship)." },

{ id:"cl1-051", lesson:"1C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Part IV deals with 'the other aspect of the bifurcated ownership structure created by the company'. What does that structure split, and into what?",
  options:[
    {t:"It splits private property: shareholders become residual claimants on the company's funds, while creditors have direct access to those funds in priority to them", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the company has changed the concept of private property by in effect splitting it. On one side are the residual claimants, the shareholders, who are entitled to what is left. On the other are the creditors, who have direct access to the company's funds in priority to the shareholders.\n\nPart IV takes the perspective of these investors financing the corporation and obtaining rights in or against it. That is why it covers shares and shareholdings, capital maintenance (which protects creditors' priority by restricting returns to shareholders), and debentures and charges. Notice how this ties back to Chapter 1's point that shareholders hold only a bounded, residual claim to the fund."},
    {t:"It splits management between the board and the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The separation of ownership and control is a governance theme for Part III. The 'bifurcated ownership' of Part IV is about who has claims on the company's funds: residual claimants and prior-ranking creditors."},
    {t:"It splits the company into a holding company and subsidiaries", correct:false,
     why:"Group structures are a separate topic. The bifurcation the text describes happens inside every single company, between shareholders and creditors."},
    {t:"It splits liability between the company and its directors", correct:false,
     why:"The text is talking about investors' claims on the company's funds, not about the allocation of liability for wrongs."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.018]", takeaway:"Part IV: shareholders = residual claimants; creditors = prior access." },

/* ---------- 1D Sources of corporate law ---------- */
{ id:"cl1-052", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's constitution states: 'Members may not requisition the directors to convene an extraordinary general meeting unless they hold at least 25% of the paid-up voting capital.' Members holding the lower percentage set by the Act serve a requisition.",
  prompt:"Is the constitutional clause effective to block them?",
  options:[
    {t:"No. Section 176 applies 'notwithstanding anything in the constitution', so the constitution cannot raise the statutory requisition threshold", correct:true,
     why:"Section 176 is the text's example of an older provision that is clearly mandatory. The position on shareholders requisitioning the directors to call an extraordinary general meeting applies '[n]otwithstanding anything in the constitution'. A clause that makes requisitioning harder is therefore ineffective, and the members holding the statutory percentage can requisition.\n\nThis is a good test case for the mandatory/default distinction. Mandatory rules are sometimes needed in internal governance to prevent abuse. If the majority could write the requisition right out of the constitution, minority shareholders would lose one of their basic tools for holding the board to account."},
    {t:"Yes. The constitution is a contract among the members, and the members agreed to it", correct:false,
     why:"Contract explains much of the internal side of the company, but mandatory rules cannot be contracted out of. Section 176 expressly overrides 'anything in the constitution', so consent to the constitution does not help."},
    {t:"Yes, if the clause was adopted by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The method of adoption does not matter. Section 176 overrides the constitution however the clause got there."},
    {t:"Yes, because CAWG listed s 176 as one of eight default provisions", correct:false,
     why:"Section 176 is not on CAWG's list. The eight default provisions are ss 147(2), 149B, 152(9), 181, 184(5), 184(6), 184A(5)(a)(ii) and 184DA(1). The text contrasts s 176 with them as an older mandatory provision."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.021], [01.024], fn 76", takeaway:"s 176 overrides the constitution; it can't be made harder." },

{ id:"cl1-053", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's constitution allows members holding a smaller percentage than the Act's default to call a general meeting themselves under s 177.",
  prompt:"Is this provision effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes. Section 177 itself allows the threshold to be 'such lesser number as is provided by the articles'", correct:true,
     why:"Section 177, which follows s 176, lets members holding a certain percentage of shares 'or such lesser number as is provided by the articles' call a meeting themselves. The Act therefore invites the constitution to make this easier. Lowering the threshold works with the statute, not against it.\n\nSet this beside s 176. Both concern members forcing a meeting, but s 176 overrides the constitution, while s 177 lets the constitution move the threshold in one direction (downwards). The text uses the pair to show that the Act contains both kinds of rule, and to argue that, where possible, it should be left flexible so that it can respond to the market. Note that the words permit a lesser number, so a clause raising the threshold would not have the same support."},
    {t:"No. Every Companies Act threshold is mandatory and cannot be changed by the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"This is the conservative assumption the text criticises. Section 177 shows on its face that some provisions can be varied by the articles."},
    {t:"No, because s 177 applies 'notwithstanding anything in the constitution'", correct:false,
     why:"That phrase belongs to s 176 (members requisitioning the directors). Mixing up the two sections is the trap. Section 177 expressly allows a lesser number to be set by the articles."},
    {t:"Only if ACRA approves the amendment", correct:false,
     why:"No ACRA approval is involved. The flexibility comes from the wording of s 177 itself."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.024]", takeaway:"s 177: articles may set a lower threshold. Contrast s 176." },

{ id:"cl1-054", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Alpha Pte Ltd (incorporated 2017) adopted the Model Constitution in the version in force at its incorporation. Beta Pte Ltd (incorporated 2017) adopted it 'as varied from time to time'. In 2024 the Model Constitution is amended to add a helpful new rule.",
  prompt:"Which company's constitution picks up the 2024 rule automatically?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only Beta's. Alpha chose the frozen version and must amend its constitution itself if it wants the new rule.", correct:true,
     why:"Under s 37 (in force since 2015), a company that opts for the Model Constitution for a Private Company Limited by Shares chooses between a version that may be varied from time to time and the version in force at incorporation. Beta chose the first, so later amendments flow into its constitution automatically. Alpha chose the second, so its constitution stays as it was in 2017.\n\nThis is the problem the old Table A had for every company: it applied in the version at incorporation, so when the Act and Fourth Schedule were amended in 2004 to allow single-director companies, older companies on earlier versions were left with rules assuming several directors. The text's point is that 'fuller delegation', adopting a default charter that incorporates State amendments, could have spared companies some of these difficulties."},
    {t:"Both, because Model Constitution amendments apply to every company that adopted it", correct:false,
     why:"That ignores the s 37 choice. A company that adopts the version in force at incorporation keeps that version unless it amends its own constitution."},
    {t:"Only Alpha's, because it adopted the model earlier in the year", correct:false,
     why:"Timing within the year is irrelevant. What matters is which form of the Model Constitution each company expressly opted for."},
    {t:"Neither, because the Model Constitution can never be amended after a company adopts it", correct:false,
     why:"The Model Constitution can be amended, and s 37 specifically provides for a version that varies from time to time. Beta's constitution moves with those amendments."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.023]; Ch 2 [02.030]", takeaway:"s 37: only 'as varied from time to time' picks up later changes." },

{ id:"cl1-055", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A company incorporated in 1995 still uses the Table A articles it adopted at incorporation. For the purposes of s 37 today, how is its constitution treated?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a customised constitution, because a company has to opt expressly for either form of the Model Constitution", correct:true,
     why:"The text says that the old Table A position, where the version at incorporation applied under the now repealed s 36, 'for the purposes of section 37 today would be seen as a customised constitution as you have to expressly opt for either form of the Model Constitution'.\n\nSo this company does not benefit from either s 37 option. Its Table A articles are frozen, and changes to the Model Constitution do not affect it. If it wants the newer rules, it has to amend its own constitution, or expressly adopt one of the Model Constitution forms. This is one reason the COVID-era meeting problem hit older companies hard."},
    {t:"As the Model Constitution 'as varied from time to time'", correct:false,
     why:"No company is put on the varying version by default. It has to opt in expressly, and a Table A company has not done so."},
    {t:"As the Model Constitution in force at incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"Table A is not the current Model Constitution, and a company has to opt expressly for either Model Constitution form. Table A articles are therefore treated as a customised constitution."},
    {t:"As void, because Table A was repealed", correct:false,
     why:"Repeal of s 36 did not invalidate existing constitutions adopted from Table A. They continue to bind, as customised constitutions."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.023]", takeaway:"Old Table A articles = a customised constitution under s 37." },

{ id:"cl1-056", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"March 2026. A company whose old constitution says nothing about electronic meetings wants to hold a fully virtual AGM. A junior lawyer says it must first pass a special resolution at a physical meeting to amend the constitution.",
  prompt:"Is the junior lawyer right?",
  options:[
    {t:"No. Since the 2023 amendments, s 173J allows companies to hold hybrid and fully digital meetings without first amending their constitution", correct:true,
     why:"The Companies, Business Trusts and Other Bodies (Miscellaneous Amendments) Act 2023 introduced s 173J, which provides that companies can hold hybrid and fully digital meetings without first having to amend their constitution. It also amended ss 387B–387D on electronic communications.\n\nSo the 2020 Catch-22, where a physical meeting was needed to amend the constitution to allow virtual meetings, does not arise any more. The junior lawyer's instinct reflects the conservatism the text describes: the assumption that legislation or constitutional change is needed before a company can do something new. The text adds that a contractarian solution could have worked all along, since CAWG framed its recommendation as 'clarifying' that a company may meet digitally unless its constitution provides otherwise."},
    {t:"Yes. Digital meetings are allowed only if the constitution expressly permits them", correct:false,
     why:"That was the practical assumption in 2020, which is why emergency legislation was needed. Section 173J now removes the need for prior constitutional amendment."},
    {t:"Yes, but only until the COVID-19 (Temporary Measures) Act expires", correct:false,
     why:"The COVID-19 (Temporary Measures) Act provided a temporary fix in 2020. The permanent position is now s 173J, which does not depend on the temporary Act."},
    {t:"No, because AGMs are no longer required for any company", correct:false,
     why:"The question is how to hold the meeting, not whether it is required. Section 173J deals with the format of meetings."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.023]", takeaway:"s 173J: digital/hybrid meetings without amending the constitution." },

{ id:"cl1-057", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On the text, when is a Companies Act provision LEAST likely to allow a company to contract out through its constitution?",
  options:[
    {t:"When the Act implicitly covers the area, even though it does not expressly prohibit a different arrangement", correct:true,
     why:"The text says it is not always easy to tell whether a provision allows contracting out. If the Act is silent on a matter, that does appear to allow derogation. But the Act may also implicitly cover an area even without saying so, and then contracting out is not allowed.\n\nIts example is the 2004 amendments that permitted electronic transmission of notices of meetings where the recipient agreed. In other jurisdictions, such as the UK (where a public company's AGM could be held electronically by amending its articles, as Jimmy Choo plc did in 2016), this could have been done through the constitution. In Singapore it was done by statute, reflecting the view that the Act's coverage of notices left no room for private variation. That is why the text says practitioners treat provisions as mandatory and exhaustive."},
    {t:"When the Act is completely silent on the matter", correct:false,
     why:"Silence is the situation where the text says derogation does appear to be allowed. The harder case is when the Act covers the area implicitly."},
    {t:"When the provision says 'or such lesser number as is provided by the articles'", correct:false,
     why:"That wording, from s 177, expressly invites the constitution to vary the rule. It is the clearest case for contracting out, not the least."},
    {t:"When the provision is one of CAWG's eight default provisions", correct:false,
     why:"CAWG's eight are, by definition, default provisions that the constitution can displace. They are the most likely to allow variation."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.022], fn 64–65", takeaway:"Silent → may derogate; implicitly covered → can't contract out." },

{ id:"cl1-058", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which of these is NOT one of the eight default provisions CAWG identified in the Companies Act?",
  options:[
    {t:"Section 176 (members' requisition of an EGM)", correct:true,
     why:"Section 176 applies 'notwithstanding anything in the constitution', so it is a mandatory provision and the opposite of a default rule. The text contrasts it with CAWG's eight.\n\nCAWG's eight default provisions (fn 76) are ss 147(2), 149B, 152(9), 181, 184(5), 184(6), 184A(5)(a)(ii) and 184DA(1). In Australia the equivalent concept is labelled 'replaceable rules', of which there are 42. CAWG thought it unnecessary to label Singapore's eight in the Act."},
    {t:"Section 147(2)", correct:false,
     why:"Section 147(2) is on CAWG's list of eight default provisions, so it can be displaced by the constitution. It is not the odd one out."},
    {t:"Section 181", correct:false,
     why:"Section 181 is on CAWG's list of default provisions. The provision that is not on the list is s 176, which overrides the constitution."},
    {t:"Section 184(5)", correct:false,
     why:"Section 184(5) is among the eight (as is s 184(6)). The mandatory provision in this set is s 176."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.024], fn 76", takeaway:"CAWG's 8 defaults: ss 147(2), 149B, 152(9), 181, 184(5), 184(6), 184A(5)(a)(ii), 184DA(1)." },

{ id:"cl1-059", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the text say mandatory rules are still needed even in a largely contractarian company law?",
  options:[
    {t:"Internally, to prevent abuse in the company's governance; externally, to protect third parties dealing with the company", correct:true,
     why:"The text notes that in the Anglo-American model mandatory rules may be seen as unusual, with the Act providing boilerplate to cut transaction costs. But it says mandatory rules are sometimes required in the internal governance of the company to prevent its abuse, and at other times in its external dimension to protect third parties dealing with the company.\n\nIn Singapore there is a further reason: a perception that shareholders and creditors need enough protection to be willing to invest in a company at all. This helps explain why many provisions are understood as mandatory. The text's complaint is not that mandatory rules exist, but that the Act is too readily treated as both exhaustive and mandatory."},
    {t:"Because contracts between members are unenforceable", correct:false,
     why:"Contracts among members, including the constitution itself, are enforceable. The case for mandatory rules rests on preventing abuse and protecting outsiders, not on any defect in contract law."},
    {t:"Because the State owns the company", correct:false,
     why:"The State confers personality; it does not own the company. Mandatory rules exist to protect constituents and third parties."},
    {t:"Only to ensure taxes are collected", correct:false,
     why:"Tax is not the reason the text gives. Its two reasons are preventing internal abuse and protecting third parties."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.021]–[01.022]", takeaway:"Mandatory rules: stop internal abuse + protect third parties." },

{ id:"cl1-060", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An SME association lobbies for a separate 'Small Business Corporations Act' because the Companies Act is too rigid for owner-managed companies.",
  prompt:"What is the text's response to this kind of call?",
  options:[
    {t:"Leaving the Companies Act flexible would let small companies craft constitutions that suit them, obviating the need for separate small business legislation; public companies would still converge because they need outside investors", correct:true,
     why:"The text argues that, where possible, the Companies Act should be left flexible so it can respond to the market. That should not lead to much deviation from the norm, because in larger public companies the need to attract outside investors makes constitutions very similar anyway.\n\nSmaller companies, by contrast, may need more flexibility to tailor their constitutions to their particular purposes. The text says that fluidity would 'obviate the need for small business corporation legislation', which it describes as a clarion call heard every few years from the SME sector, 'perhaps justifiably so'. Chapter 2 makes a related point: small companies are where shareholder agreements and tailored provisions are used most."},
    {t:"It supports a separate Act as the only solution", correct:false,
     why:"The text acknowledges the call may be justified, but its proposed answer is a more flexible Companies Act, which would make a separate statute unnecessary."},
    {t:"It says small companies should simply use partnerships instead", correct:false,
     why:"The text does not tell SMEs to abandon the company form. It wants the Act to give them room to craft suitable constitutions."},
    {t:"It says flexibility would cause public company constitutions to diverge widely", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite: the need to attract outside investors keeps public company constitutions very similar, so flexibility would not lead to much deviation there."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.024]; Ch 2 [02.030]", takeaway:"Flexible CA → no need for a separate SME statute; public cos converge anyway." },

{ id:"cl1-061", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bidder disagrees with an SIC ruling against it in a contested takeover. It asks the High Court to review the SIC's assessment of the commercial merits and substitute the court's own view.",
  prompt:"On the text, what can the court do?",
  options:[
    {t:"It cannot re-decide the merits, because s 139(7) SFA makes SIC decisions final on the merits; but it can entertain judicial review, since the SIC's power is reviewable by its nature", correct:true,
     why:"The finality clause in s 139(7) SFA provides that an SIC decision cannot be challenged on its merits. So the bidder cannot ask the court to substitute its own commercial judgment. But the courts can still grant judicial review to an applicant unhappy with an SIC decision, based on the nature rather than the source of the power (Datafin, fully endorsed by the Court of Appeal in Comptroller of Income Tax v ACC). Judicial review looks at legality and process, not at whether the SIC's view was the best one.\n\nThe text also explains why this division makes sense. SIC hearings are informal, the Code binds in spirit as well as letter, and an ex ante market regulator working with participants is argued to promote efficient takeovers better than a court judging matters ex post."},
    {t:"Full appeal on the merits, like any lower tribunal", correct:false,
     why:"Section 139(7) SFA rules out merits challenges. Treating the SIC like a court below on appeal ignores the finality clause."},
    {t:"Nothing at all, because the Code is a private contract", correct:false,
     why:"The Code is not purely contractual: it is underpinned by statute, and SIC decisions have legal effect. That is why judicial review remains available."},
    {t:"Nothing at all, because finality clauses exclude all court involvement", correct:false,
     why:"The finality clause stops merits challenges, but the text says judicial review is still available on the Datafin basis. 'Final' does not mean immune from review for legality."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.027]–[01.028]", takeaway:"SIC: no merits appeal (s 139(7)), but judicial review is available." },

{ id:"cl1-062", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What was the significance of Public Service Commission v Lai Swee Lin Linda in the text's discussion of judicial review of codes?",
  options:[
    {t:"The Court of Appeal appeared to accept Datafin's nature-of-power approach, but decided the case on the basis that where the source of power is contractual, judicial review is almost always unavailable", correct:true,
     why:"Footnote 92 explains that Datafin called for a more pragmatic assessment of the nature of the power in question, and that this appeared to be accepted in Lai Swee Lin. But the Court of Appeal went on to decide that case on the basis that where the source is contractual, judicial review is almost always unavailable, citing Lloyd LJ in Datafin that the source of the power 'will often, perhaps usually, be decisive'.\n\nThe broader Datafin principle was later fully endorsed in Comptroller of Income Tax v ACC. That matters for the Take-over Code: because it is underpinned by statute rather than being purely contractual, SIC decisions fall on the reviewable side of the line."},
    {t:"It held that SIC decisions can be appealed on the merits", correct:false,
     why:"Lai Swee Lin was not about the SIC, and merits appeals from the SIC are excluded by s 139(7) SFA. The case is cited for the source-versus-nature debate on judicial review."},
    {t:"It rejected Datafin entirely", correct:false,
     why:"The court appeared to accept Datafin. It decided the case on the narrower footing that a contractual source is usually decisive against review."},
    {t:"It held the Take-over Code was subsidiary legislation", correct:false,
     why:"The Code is not subsidiary legislation (SFA s 139(3)), and Lai Swee Lin did not decide anything about it."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.027], fn 92", takeaway:"Lai Swee Lin: Datafin accepted, but a contractual source usually bars review." },

{ id:"cl1-063", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Comptroller of Income Tax v ACC, why was the Comptroller's letter contrasted with SIC decisions?",
  options:[
    {t:"The letter was only an opinion on a party's withholding tax obligation, not a binding determination, whereas a quashing order is available for decisions with 'some form of actual or ostensible legal effect', like SIC rulings", correct:true,
     why:"The Court of Appeal, through Chan Sek Keong CJ, said a quashing order can be sought for decisions that have 'some form of actual or ostensible legal effect'. The Comptroller's letter was really an opinion about a party's obligation to withhold tax on payments to a foreign body, and clearly not a binding determination of law. SIC decisions, by contrast, carry real legal effect.\n\nChan CJ also noted that the UK Takeover Panel, the SIC's equivalent, had no statutory or contractual powers but 'immense de facto power', because a ruling against a party led to other sanctions such as exclusion from the London Stock Exchange. Footnote 95 adds two points: the Court of Appeal still upheld the quashing order below for procedural convenience, and seeking judicial review without first asking for a Ministerial exemption or remission was not an abuse of process."},
    {t:"The letter was a statute, while SIC decisions are contracts", correct:false,
     why:"Neither half is right. The letter was an administrative opinion, and the Code is neither statute nor a purely private contract: it is soft law underpinned by statute."},
    {t:"Tax decisions can never be reviewed", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not say this. The test was whether the decision had some actual or ostensible legal effect, and the court in fact upheld the quashing order for procedural convenience."},
    {t:"The SIC is a court whose decisions must be appealed, not reviewed", correct:false,
     why:"The SIC is a quasi-regulator, not a court. Its decisions are final on the merits under s 139(7) SFA, but subject to judicial review."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.027], fn 92–96", takeaway:"Review needs 'actual or ostensible legal effect': SIC rulings have it; an opinion letter doesn't." },

{ id:"cl1-064", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A Singapore company wants to rely on a 19th-century English common law rule developed for English commercial conditions. Opposing counsel argues local conditions differ in an important way.",
  prompt:"How should the Singapore court approach the rule?",
  options:[
    {t:"Apply it only so far as it is applicable to Singapore's circumstances and inhabitants, with any modifications those circumstances require, treating English authority as persuasive", correct:true,
     why:"Section 3(2) of the Application of English Law Act provides that the common law continues in force in Singapore 'so far as it is applicable to the circumstances of Singapore and its inhabitants and subject to such modifications as those circumstances may require'. Phang JA in Man Financial said this embodies principles that have existed since the dawn of the Singapore legal system.\n\nHe also warned that local courts must recognise that a uniform common law is no longer a given. Uniformity is more likely and more desirable in commercial matters, but even there it should not be taken too far. Man Financial itself concerned the blue pencil test for restraint of trade clauses, where Canada had departed from the English approach. The text adds that because the common law is context-specific, differences across jurisdictions should be expected."},
    {t:"Apply it exactly as English courts do, because English law binds Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"English decisions are persuasive, not binding, and Singapore judges declare the common law in accordance with local conditions."},
    {t:"Refuse to apply any English rule, because Singapore law is fully autochthonous", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore has sought a more autochthonous legal system, but it has not abandoned the common law. English rules continue to apply under the AELA, subject to local suitability."},
    {t:"Apply it only if Parliament has enacted it", correct:false,
     why:"Common law rules apply without enactment; that is the point of s 3 AELA. Statute and common law are separate sources."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.019], fn 53, 58", takeaway:"AELA s 3(2): English common law applies only as suited to Singapore." },

{ id:"cl1-065", lesson:"1D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company secretary needs to know (i) which forms to file with ACRA when proving a debt in a judicial management, and (ii) how to file a charge registration.",
  prompt:"Where does the text say she should look?",
  options:[
    {t:"(i) The Companies Regulations and the Companies (Filing of Documents) Regulations; (ii) Bizfile, ACRA's online system, which now handles forms such as charge registration administratively", correct:true,
     why:"The text uses exactly this example to show that the Act is only the framework. The Companies Act sets out the rules within which companies must operate, but to know what forms must be filed with ACRA, for instance when proving a debt in judicial management, you must look at the Companies Regulations and the Companies (Filing of Documents) Regulations.\n\nOther forms, such as those relating to charge registration, used to be in the Companies Regulations but are now managed administratively through Bizfile, ACRA's online filing and information retrieval system. Since Bizfile launched on 13 January 2003, physical forms have been converted to electronic ones. The lesson: in practice, the details that trip people up are usually in subsidiary legislation or administrative systems, not the Act."},
    {t:"Both in the Companies Act itself", correct:false,
     why:"The Act provides the framework, not the filing details. The text's point is that the details are in subsidiary legislation and, for some forms, Bizfile."},
    {t:"Both in the company's constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Filing requirements with ACRA are set by regulation and administrative practice, not by a company's own constitution."},
    {t:"Both in English case law", correct:false,
     why:"Filing forms are a matter of local subsidiary legislation and ACRA's systems. Case law does not prescribe forms."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.020]", takeaway:"Act = framework; forms = Regulations; charge filings now via Bizfile." },

{ id:"cl1-066", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the text say more substantive matters are increasingly placed in delegated rather than primary legislation?",
  options:[
    {t:"The complexity of financial regulation and the need to keep up with market developments make primary legislation unsuitable for provisions likely to need changing, even at enactment", correct:true,
     why:"The text says more substantive matters are now put into delegated legislation, especially given the complexity of financial regulation. Because rules must change to keep up with market developments, primary legislation may not suit provisions that are expected to need changing even when they are enacted.\n\nIt also mentions 'Henry VIII' powers, which give administrative agencies limited power, through delegated legislation, to amend primary legislation. This matters in practice: the rule you need may sit in regulations, or even have been altered by them."},
    {t:"Because Parliament no longer passes company legislation", correct:false,
     why:"Parliament continues to amend the Companies Act, as the 2014, 2017 and 2023 Acts show. The shift is about which matters suit delegated legislation, not about Parliament stopping."},
    {t:"Because subsidiary legislation cannot be challenged in court", correct:false,
     why:"The text gives no such reason. Its explanation is complexity and the need for change."},
    {t:"Because the common law has been abolished in this area", correct:false,
     why:"The common law remains a major source of company law. The point here concerns the balance between primary and subsidiary written law."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.020]", takeaway:"Delegated legislation: suits fast-changing, complex rules; Henry VIII powers exist." },

{ id:"cl1-067", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"CAWG said that, to be 'futureproof', the Companies Act should generally be neutral as to the digital media or technology used. What does the text draw from this?",
  options:[
    {t:"The Act should not require express derogation section by section; it should not be seen as both exhaustive and mandatory", correct:true,
     why:"Picking up its earlier discussion of electronic communications and meetings, the text suggests the Act needs more flexibility, so that a company does not need express permission in a particular section before it can do something different. It quotes CAWG's May 2019 report: '[t]o be futureproof, the CA should generally be neutral with respect to the type of digital media or technology used'.\n\nPut differently, the Act should not be seen as exhaustive and mandatory. The text adds that corporate law is said to be the 'least uncomfortable neutral choice' that businesses select (fn 85, Baum and Solomon, writing about why Delaware and England win the global corporate law race)."},
    {t:"The Act should list every permitted technology", correct:false,
     why:"Listing technologies is the opposite of technology neutrality. A list would date quickly and would reinforce the view that anything not listed is prohibited."},
    {t:"Electronic meetings should be banned until technology stabilises", correct:false,
     why:"CAWG and the text both favoured enabling electronic meetings, and s 173J now does so."},
    {t:"Only listed companies should use digital media", correct:false,
     why:"Technology neutrality applies across the Act. The text's examples concern companies generally, including private companies with Table A constitutions."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.026], fn 84–85", takeaway:"Technology-neutral Act; not exhaustive AND mandatory." },

{ id:"cl1-068", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the text contrast the premise of the 2002 Companies Act review with the direction of more recent reforms?",
  options:[
    {t:"In 2002 Singapore saw itself as more of a price taker, constrained by US capital inflows and its UK heritage; later reforms reflect a desire for a more autochthonous system, though the philosophy stays market-friendly", correct:true,
     why:"The text says most changes over the past 20 years or so have been characterised by a desire for a more autochthonous (home-grown) legal system, as Singapore's economy and legal landscape matured. Many major reforms since the late 1990s came from private sector-led committees, such as the Company Legislation and Regulatory Framework Committee (2002), the Disclosure and Accounting Standards Committee (2001) and the Corporate Finance Committee (1998).\n\nThe 2002 review, by contrast, proceeded on the premise that Singapore was more of a price taker, with its discretion in reform bounded by foreign capital inflows from the US and by path dependence from its UK heritage. Recent changes sit against the Singapore Academy of Law's promotion of Singapore law project (from 2007) and the Singapore International Commercial Court (planned from end-2013, established early 2015). Throughout, the philosophy is market-friendly and responsive."},
    {t:"The 2002 review aimed to make Singapore law independent of all foreign influence", correct:false,
     why:"That reverses the text. The 2002 review accepted that Singapore was constrained by foreign capital and its UK heritage. The autochthonous push came later."},
    {t:"Recent reforms have abandoned the market-friendly philosophy", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the legislative and regulatory philosophy is 'clearly one that is market-friendly and responsive'."},
    {t:"Reforms have been driven only by the courts", correct:false,
     why:"The text credits private sector-led committees, MoF consultations and CAWG. Courts develop the law too, but they are not the drivers of these statutory reforms."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.025], fn 80, 82", takeaway:"2002: price taker. Since: more autochthonous, still market-friendly." },

{ id:"cl1-069", lesson:"1D", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The text describes industry codes, such as those on corporate governance, takeovers and collective investment schemes, as purely private ordering with no public regulatory element.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text calls industry codes 'a hybrid mixture of private ordering and public regulation'. Corporate lawyers work within that framework in M&A (the Take-over Code), corporate governance and social responsibility (Codes of Corporate Governance and Stewardship Codes), and asset management (the Code on Collective Investment Schemes).\n\nCodes are more responsive to market change, and their market-based remedies may be more appropriate, but they must be handled with care because it can be argued they have no real legal effect. The Take-over Code shows the hybrid nature: not legislation, yet underpinned by statute and subject to judicial review. The text also records the view that even the Companies Act is a form of private law, a 'discourse on other discourses'."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite: codes are a hybrid of private ordering and public regulation. The Take-over Code, which is underpinned by the SFA and enforced by the SIC, is the clearest example of the public element."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.027], [01.029]", takeaway:"Industry codes = hybrid of private ordering and public regulation." },

{ id:"cl1-070", lesson:"1D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Who, besides judges, does the text say develops a great volume of company law, and what limit should judges respect?",
  options:[
    {t:"Solicitors and quasi-regulators such as the SIC and SGX; judges should allow their developments where they do not offend the mischief the Act or another rule seeks to cure", correct:true,
     why:"The text says a great volume of company law development is in the hands of solicitors, or quasi-regulators like the Securities Industry Council and SGX. Judges need to be sensitive to this, and allow it where it does not offend the mischief sought to be cured by the Act or some other rule. The growth of lex mercatoria (merchant custom) is cited as being in the same vein.\n\nThe floating charge is the classic example: practitioners drafted it, and equity accommodated it. The text's underlying argument is that regulators should not keep a tight rein on company legislation or reduce the impact of judges and solicitors on it."},
    {t:"Only Parliament; judges should never adapt to practice", correct:false,
     why:"The text says judges do develop the law and 'there is nothing wrong with that'. It also credits solicitors and quasi-regulators."},
    {t:"Foreign courts, whose decisions bind Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Foreign decisions are persuasive guides. The text's point here is about practitioners and quasi-regulators, not foreign courts."},
    {t:"Shareholders, through the general meeting", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders shape their own company's constitution, but the text is talking about the development of company law generally, which it attributes to judges, solicitors, the SIC and the SGX."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 1 [01.026], fn 83", takeaway:"Solicitors, SIC and SGX develop law too; judges allow it unless it defeats the Act's mischief." }

]);

;
