/* DealCraft — Company Law, Chapter 2: Different Perspectives of the Corporation (Tjio)
   Lessons 2A–2E. Paragraph refs [02.xxx] are to the chapter.
   Revised: answers re-checked against the chapter text; explanations expanded. New questions are in companyLaw2x.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.companyLaw = (window.DC_BANK.companyLaw||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 2A Contractarian view ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-001", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On the contractarian view, why does the company exist at all?",
  options:[
    {t:"Transaction costs are real, and the company is a set of boilerplate terms and relationships that reduces them", correct:true,
     why:"The contractarian view is largely a theory of the firm: why it exists when individuals could simply contract with each other on and through markets. Building on Coase, the starting point is that transaction costs in society are not de minimis, and the company is a set of boilerplate contractual terms and relationships used to reduce them. Using the firm as a set of standardised default terms is cheaper than contracting through the market each time.\n\nEasterbrook and Fischel add that if there were no transaction costs, parties could bargain their way to an efficient outcome. Their normative conclusion is that 'the law completes open-ended contracts' and should not impose terms that defeat actual bargains or reduce the venturers' joint wealth. Courts therefore have a limited role in light of party autonomy, and corporate 'personhood' is 'a matter of convenience rather than a reality'."},
    {t:"Because the State requires all businesses to incorporate", correct:false,
     why:"Incorporation is optional. Sole proprietorships, partnerships, LLPs, LPs and business trusts all remain available, as Chapter 3 shows. The contractarian explanation is about why people choose the corporate form, and its answer is lower transaction costs, not legal compulsion."},
    {t:"Because only companies can own property", correct:false,
     why:"Individuals, trustees and (in a limited way) partnerships can own property. Capacity to own is not the contractarian explanation. In fact, contractarians downplay the entity, treating personhood as a convenience."},
    {t:"To pursue public policy goals set by regulators", correct:false,
     why:"Contractarians say the opposite. Company law should follow what private parties would bargain for in their own narrow self-interest. Any social goals should be achieved by exogenous public legislation, and companies are 'empty vessels' with no goals of their own beyond what the parties decide."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.002]–[02.003], [02.005]", takeaway:"Contractarian: the firm is boilerplate that cuts transaction costs." },

{ id:"cl2-002", lesson:"2A", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Easterbrook and Fischel: 'a manager told to serve two ____ … has been freed of both and is answerable to neither'.",
  accept:["masters"], answer:"masters",
  why:"This line is the contractarian case for shareholder primacy. Most of the time a company is set up for the benefit of its residual claimants, the shareholders. When that is so, it should focus only on them and maximise shareholder value, without worrying about other constituents. Asking managers to serve several constituencies at once leaves them accountable to none, because any decision can be justified by pointing to one group or another.\n\nThe text notes the counter-point (fn 11, Kraakman et al) that maximising shareholder value, especially long-term shareholder value, can also advance overall social welfare. Later in the chapter it questions whether a strict single-objective model captures how boards actually mediate among constituents.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.003]", takeaway:"'Two masters' = the contractarian argument for a single objective." },

{ id:"cl2-003", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What 'slight contradiction' does the text find in some contractarians' position?",
  options:[
    {t:"They champion shareholder value yet resist giving shareholders more rights against managerial discretion: board control focused on shareholder primacy", correct:true,
     why:"Contractarian theory was developed partly because of unhappiness with management power in large US corporations with dispersed shareholdings. Yet some contractarians argue that the focus on shareholder value should not mean giving shareholders more rights at the expense of managerial discretion.\n\nOn this view we should keep following the lead of 'self-serving managers rather than disinterested regulators', trusting the invisible hand of the market to produce an efficient outcome. Managers, especially in widely held companies, are said to be better placed than shareholders to make decisions that benefit shareholders. The text summarises the result as 'board control focused on shareholder primacy': shareholders are the goal, but managers hold the controls."},
    {t:"They reject transaction cost economics", correct:false,
     why:"Transaction cost economics, from Coase onward, is the foundation of the contractarian position. The contradiction the text identifies is internal to how contractarians treat shareholders and managers, not a rejection of their own economic premise."},
    {t:"They favour heavy regulation of companies", correct:false,
     why:"The text says contractarian theory was developed partly to explain the status quo and to stave off increased regulation of corporate governance. Favouring heavy regulation would be the opposite of their position."},
    {t:"They deny that shareholders are residual claimants", correct:false,
     why:"Contractarians accept that shareholders are the residual claimants, which is why they argue for shareholder value. The tension is that they then deny shareholders the control that would normally go with that status."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.002], [02.004]", takeaway:"Contractarian tension: shareholder value, but board control." },

{ id:"cl2-004", lesson:"2A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because s 39 makes the constitution a contract, it is enforced like an ordinary private contract with the usual contractual remedies.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text's phrase is that '“contractarian” does not mean contractual'. Long-term contracts like the corporate structure are seen differently from one-off transactions. Williamson's analysis of hold-up costs explains why: a party making an investment specific to a relationship may be unable to recoup it from a single transaction, so it needs a longer-term structure before it will invest. The company provides that structure.\n\nSo although s 39 recognises the constitution as a contract between the company and its members and among the members inter se, it cannot be enforced in the same way as a normal private contract, with the remedies familiar from first-year contract law (developed in Chapter 5). The fact that the Act creates fiduciary duties that cannot be fully contracted out of for status-based fiduciaries like directors also shows the long-term, relational nature of the arrangement."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The text expressly warns that the constitution cannot be enforced in the same way as a normal private contract. Treating it as an ordinary contract overlooks its long-term nature, its effects on third parties and the special remedies company law uses (for example, personal rights to have the constitution observed rather than damages for every breach)."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.006]", takeaway:"'Contractarian' ≠ contractual: s 39 contract, but special remedies." },

{ id:"cl2-005", lesson:"2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An article required shareholder approval to increase the company's authorised capital. Later legislation abolished authorised capital. A minority now argues that, under the article, the size of every new share issue needs shareholder approval.",
  prompt:"How did the Court of Appeal resolve this in Lian Hwee Choo Phebe v Maxz Universal Development Group?",
  options:[
    {t:"Interpreting the article dynamically, it gave shareholders only a discretion to set a limit or price; if they did not exercise it, compliance with s 161 was enough", correct:true,
     why:"Authorised capital used to be a notional cap on the number of shares a company could issue. Once legislation removed the concept, the article requiring approval to raise authorised capital became otiose. The appellants argued that it should now be read as requiring approval of the quantum of every new issue, which would be more onerous than s 161 (the general requirement of ordinary shareholder approval for share issues, with the board deciding timing and quantum).\n\nThe Court of Appeal held that long-term contracts like articles allow a more dynamic interpretation than James Miller v Whitworth Street Estates, which bars interpreting a contract in light of later events. Read as at the present, the article only gave shareholders a discretion to set a limit or price on issues; if they did not exercise it, compliance with s 161 was enough. The text adds a caution: because the constitution can affect third parties who were not privy to its drafting, courts should be wary of interpreting it too readily with extrinsic evidence."},
    {t:"The article required approval of the size of every future share issue", correct:false,
     why:"This was the appellants' argument, which the Court of Appeal rejected. It would have imposed a requirement more onerous than s 161, and it did not follow from a present-day reading of an article written for a concept (authorised capital) that no longer existed."},
    {t:"The article became void when authorised capital was abolished", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not treat the article as void. It gave it a dynamic reading in light of the changed legislation, leaving shareholders a discretion they could still choose to exercise."},
    {t:"Directors could issue shares without any shareholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"Section 161 still requires ordinary shareholder approval for share issues. The only question in Maxz was whether the article added something more on top of s 161."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.007]", takeaway:"Maxz: articles get dynamic interpretation; s 161 approval was enough." },

{ id:"cl2-006", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Chan Siew Lee v TYC Investment, what did Menon CJ say must be considered when implying terms that affect the balance between directors' management powers and shareholders' reserve powers?",
  options:[
    {t:"The interests of innocent creditors and other third parties outside the company", correct:true,
     why:"Menon CJ said it was 'plainly right that the interests of innocent creditors will have to be considered'. Disputes between factions within the company can paralyse it and prejudice innocent third parties outside it, and that will often bear on how the court strikes the balance between the competing factions.\n\nThe text uses this alongside Maxz to show that the constitution is not a purely private bargain among insiders. It affects outsiders, which shapes both how it is interpreted (caution about extrinsic evidence) and what terms are implied into it. That is one more reason 'contractarian' does not mean 'contractual'."},
    {t:"Only the majority shareholder's wishes", correct:false,
     why:"The court looked beyond the internal factions altogether, to the outsiders affected by a deadlock. Deferring only to the majority would ignore the very third-party interests Menon CJ highlighted."},
    {t:"The SGX listing rules", correct:false,
     why:"TYC Investment was not a listing rules case. The point concerns implied constitutional terms and the interests of third parties outside the company."},
    {t:"The directors' remuneration", correct:false,
     why:"Remuneration has nothing to do with this issue. The concern was the balance between board management powers and shareholders' reserve powers, viewed in light of innocent creditors."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.007]", takeaway:"Implying constitutional terms: consider innocent creditors outside the company." },

{ id:"cl2-007", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which law governs a company's corporate governance and internal management?",
  options:[
    {t:"The law of the place of incorporation", correct:true,
     why:"The classic view (International Credit and Investment Co v Adham) is that a company, as an artificial creature that owes its existence to the laws of its place of incorporation, is properly managed by the courts of that place. The law of incorporation still applies to most corporate law issues, and may include transfers of directly held non-bearer shares.\n\nIn Oro Negro the Singapore court said the law of incorporation governs corporate governance and internal management 'as a matter of law and right', and that the place of incorporation is prima facie the more appropriate forum. The text's point is that the separate entity cannot be reduced to contract: an overly contractarian view, applying the proper law of a contract to non-contractual corporate questions, creates conflict of laws difficulties."},
    {t:"Whatever law the shareholders choose in a shareholders' agreement", correct:false,
     why:"Parties can choose the law governing their own contracts, including a shareholders' agreement. But the company's internal affairs follow the law of the place where it was incorporated, because that law created the entity. A choice of law clause between shareholders cannot change the company's lex incorporationis."},
    {t:"The law where the company's main assets are located", correct:false,
     why:"The law of the place where property is situated (lex situs) matters for some property questions, such as transfers of land. It does not govern the company's internal management."},
    {t:"The law of the forum where a dispute is first filed", correct:false,
     why:"Filing first does not decide the governing law. Oro Negro points the other way: the place of incorporation determines both the governing law and, prima facie, the appropriate forum."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.009], fn 38–39", takeaway:"Internal management → law (and prima facie forum) of incorporation." },

{ id:"cl2-008", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Where the Companies Act is silent on whether the constitution can contract out of a provision, what does the regulators' approach imply?",
  options:[
    {t:"The provision is treated as mandatory, not enabling, so the default rules sit mainly in the constitution, which cannot override the Act", correct:true,
     why:"Regulators have introduced flexibility by expressly stating when a provision can be varied by the constitution. The text says the way they have done so suggests that when a provision is silent on contracting out, it is mandatory rather than enabling. On that view, most sections of the Companies Act are not default provisions; the default rules are found largely in the constitution, and the constitution cannot derogate from the Act.\n\nThe text then asks the bigger question: does the Act prescribe the only way a company can be structured, so exhaustively that a constitution cannot even add 'optional extras'? It suggests this issue be revisited and the Act made more flexible as a starting point, noting that English legislation, Singapore's root, was more contractarian at inception than US law."},
    {t:"Silence means the constitution can freely override the provision", correct:false,
     why:"That would be a more contractarian reading, and Chapter 1 notes that silence 'does appear to allow for derogation' in principle. But it is not the inference the regulators' practice supports, which is that silence signals a mandatory rule."},
    {t:"Silence means the court decides case by case with no presumption", correct:false,
     why:"In practice, regulators and practitioners work on a presumption that such provisions are mandatory. That is the problem the text highlights."},
    {t:"Silence means the provision applies only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Whether a provision can be contracted out of says nothing about which type of company it applies to. Scope and derogability are separate questions."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.005]", takeaway:"Silence in the Act → treated as mandatory." },

{ id:"cl2-009", lesson:"2A", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each thinker to their contribution.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Coase", r:"The firm replaces a series of contracts with one, cutting transaction costs"},
    {l:"Williamson", r:"Hold-up costs explain the value of long-term relationships"},
    {l:"Jensen & Meckling", r:"The firm as a nexus of contracts; agency costs"},
    {l:"Kraakman et al", r:"More accurately a 'nexus for contracts'"}
  ],
  why:"Coase explained why firms exist at all: transaction costs make it cheaper to organise activity through a firm than through endless market contracts. The text adds that Coase did not believe a world of zero transaction costs existed; his theorem was meant to help bargaining and alternative arrangements in the real world.\n\nWilliamson showed that a party making an asset-specific investment may need a long-term relationship to avoid hold-up, which the corporate form provides. Jensen and Meckling framed the firm as a nexus of contracts among its constituents and named agency costs (1976). Kraakman and colleagues, who regard creating a separate entity as still the primary function of corporate law, suggested it is more accurate to call the corporation a 'nexus for contracts'.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.002], [02.005]–[02.006], [02.008], [02.011], [02.034]", takeaway:"Coase cost · Williamson hold-up · J&M nexus of · Kraakman nexus for." },

/* ---------- 2B Five features ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-010", lesson:"2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Besides legal personality, which four features are conventionally associated with the corporation?",
  options:[
    {t:"Limited liability, transferable shares, delegated management and investor ownership", correct:true,
     why:"It has become an accepted norm (following Kraakman et al) to analyse the company by five characteristics: legal personality, limited liability, transferable shares, delegated management and investor ownership.\n\nThe text's main point is that not all of these are necessarily created by company law, at least not as mandatory rules. Limited liability, often thought the key to the spread of the small private company, can be contracted for or contracted away, and the Act itself recognises unlimited companies. That is why the text concludes that separate personality, not limited liability, is the great invention of incorporation by registration."},
    {t:"Perpetual succession, a common seal, a registered office and auditors", correct:false,
     why:"These are formal incidents of a registered company. They are not the five functional features the text uses to analyse the corporate form. A common seal is no longer even compulsory for Singapore companies."},
    {t:"Tax transparency, unlimited liability, mutual agency and novation", correct:false,
     why:"These describe general partnerships rather than companies. Chapter 3 shows that partners are agents of each other, are personally liable, and need novation to admit new partners."},
    {t:"Directors' duties, oppression remedies, derivative actions and charges", correct:false,
     why:"These are legal mechanisms within company law, mostly responses to problems created by the features (for example, delegated management creates agency costs, which directors' duties address). They are not the defining features themselves."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.012], fn 54", takeaway:"Five features: personality, limited liability, transferable shares, delegated management, investor ownership." },

{ id:"cl2-011", lesson:"2B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Incorporation necessarily gives members limited liability.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The Companies Act recognises three types: companies limited by shares, companies limited by guarantee and unlimited companies (fn 55: ss 4(1), 17(2), 22(1)). Members' liability as contributories was set by s 250(1) of the Companies Act and is now in s 121 of the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act 2018.\n\nSection 121(1)(d) provides that for a company limited by shares, no contribution is required from a member beyond the amount (if any) unpaid on the shares for which he is liable as a present or past member. That cap exists because of the type of company, not because of incorporation as such. So incorporation is 'quite consistent with unlimited liability' if that is what members want."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Unlimited companies are expressly recognised by the Act, so incorporation cannot necessarily carry limited liability. In practice, founders of small companies also often give up limited liability through personal guarantees for large transactions."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.012], fn 55", takeaway:"Incorporation ≠ limited liability: unlimited companies exist." },

{ id:"cl2-012", lesson:"2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which feature does the text regard as the 'great invention' of incorporation by registration?",
  options:[
    {t:"Separate personality, whose crucial role is asset partitioning", correct:true,
     why:"The text builds the argument step by step. Limited liability can be contracted for. In small companies it is often contracted away through personal guarantees. It was seriously considered for partnerships before the Limited Liability Act 1855. And today LLPs, LPs and business trusts all offer it. So limited liability is not peculiar to corporations.\n\nWhat is hard to replicate is separate personality. The more crucial role of the rules behind companies and trusts is asset partitioning: isolating the fund so it can be used more easily in dealings with external parties like creditors. Linked to this is capital lock-in, which makes the company's fund more permanent than the funds of alternative vehicles. Together they explain why the company remains the vehicle of choice for asset-heavy businesses."},
    {t:"Limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"The text deliberately downplays limited liability as the defining feature, because it can be created by contract, abrogated by guarantees, and is now available through several non-corporate vehicles."},
    {t:"Delegated management", correct:false,
     why:"Partnerships can appoint managing partners and trusts are managed by trustees, so delegation is not unique to companies. It is also the source of the agency costs problem rather than the invention that makes incorporation valuable."},
    {t:"The right to a dividend", correct:false,
     why:"There is no general right to a dividend, even when a company makes profits; dividends depend on the company's decision and on capital maintenance rules. It cannot be the great invention of incorporation."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.012]–[02.014]", takeaway:"The great invention: separate personality and asset partitioning." },

{ id:"cl2-013", lesson:"2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer acquired a Singapore holding company whose Indonesian subsidiary owned hospitals. After completion it found charges over the hospital land and under-declared tax that had been dressed up as revenue, inflating EBITDA. The share price had been based on EBITDA among other factors.",
  prompt:"What did Columbia Asia Healthcare v Hong Hin Kit Edward decide on damages?",
  options:[
    {t:"Cost of cure for the charges and indemnity for the tax, but the buyer could not prove a purchase-price loss because the price rested on many factors, of which EBITDA was only one", correct:true,
     why:"For the charges over the Indonesian land, the court held that the right measure was the cost of cure, not the fall in the value of the shares. The vendors had warranted that the subsidiary's pre-completion tax was discharged and its accounts were accurate, so the under-declared tax was a breach of the tax warranties, and the vendors had to indemnify the buyer to put it in the position as if there had been no breach.\n\nWhat the buyer could not prove was loss measured as the difference in purchase price, because the price rested on many factors, only one of which was EBITDA. In the Court of Appeal, Phang JA said the value of shares 'as is' is what a willing buyer would have paid a willing seller knowing the true facts, and that valuation is 'a multi-faceted and fact-centric' exercise with no uniform approach. The text uses the case to show how the company's bifurcated ownership structure breaks the link between share price and underlying asset value."},
    {t:"Damages equal to the full fall in EBITDA multiplied by the deal multiple", correct:false,
     why:"The buyer could not establish loss on a purchase-price basis because EBITDA was only one of many pricing factors. The text notes courts may be uncomfortable with claims for share losses priced at a multiple of earnings."},
    {t:"No damages, because the loss was suffered by the subsidiary, not the buyer", correct:false,
     why:"The vendors were liable under the warranties and indemnities they gave the buyer. The difficulty lay only in measuring a price-based loss, not in whether the buyer had any claim."},
    {t:"Rescission of the whole share sale", correct:false,
     why:"The case concerned the measure of damages and the scope of the indemnity. Rescission was not the remedy in issue."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.015]–[02.016]", takeaway:"Columbia Asia: cost of cure; share valuation is multi-faceted." },

{ id:"cl2-014", lesson:"2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Vita Health Laboratories v Pang Seng Meng, a managing director misstated the accounts to attract buyers for his shares. What did the court award?",
  options:[
    {t:"Substantial damages to the company itself, though it suffered no actual loss, holding him to the false picture he had created", correct:true,
     why:"The case is a variant of the transferred loss problem. The MD deliberately misstated the accounts to paint a positive picture and attract outside investors to buy his shares. The loss appeared to sit with the share buyers, but the court held that the company itself was entitled to substantial damages against him, even though it had not actually suffered loss.\n\nBecause of his fraud, the MD was bound to the misleading picture he had created. Equity, in a sense, deemed done what ought to be done: reinstating the company's finances to what he had said they were. The recovery at company level then feeds into share prices as investors see fit. The text suggests this may be fraud unravelling all, so the veil 'never applies in the first place'. V K Rajah JC said 'creative accounting may require creative remedies'. Later, Haneda Construction Machinery v Huttons Asia said Vita Health should be seen as a breach of warranty case rather than a misrepresentation claim."},
    {t:"Rescission of the share sales only", correct:false,
     why:"The striking feature of the case is the company-level remedy, despite the company not having suffered actual loss. Rescission at shareholder level was not what the court ordered."},
    {t:"Nothing, because the company suffered no loss", correct:false,
     why:"That would be the orthodox answer, and it is exactly what the court avoided. It awarded the company substantial damages despite the absence of actual loss, to hold the fraudulent MD to his own representations."},
    {t:"Criminal penalties only", correct:false,
     why:"This was a civil remedy case about who could recover and how much. Any criminal consequences were not the subject of the decision."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.017], fn 72, 74", takeaway:"Vita Health: 'Creative accounting may require creative remedies.'" },

{ id:"cl2-015", lesson:"2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why can a new investor buy into a company more easily than into a partnership?",
  options:[
    {t:"Shares are transferable, while bringing in a new partner generally needs novation and the consent of all partners", correct:true,
     why:"Transferability of shares was the other key feature of the early joint stock companies. A new investor can subscribe for new shares from the company or buy existing shares from a member. Partnerships, including LLPs and LPs, still largely require some form of novation before a new partner can join, and the text's footnote 64 points to s 19 of the Partnership Act (partners' mutual rights can be varied only by the consent of all the partners).\n\nThe text adds a refinement. With private companies, which have share transfer restrictions, a transfer to an outsider looks like a form of novation too, because the board's approval is needed. With public companies, the novation may in effect be pre-approved (fn 65). Shares are 'reified' in a way that makes them easy to deal in, but they can still be hard to value."},
    {t:"Partnerships cannot accept new capital", correct:false,
     why:"Partnerships can raise new capital from existing partners or by admitting new ones. The friction lies in admitting new partners, which needs everyone's consent and novation, not in accepting capital as such."},
    {t:"Company shares are always listed", correct:false,
     why:"Most companies are private and unlisted, and their shares are subject to transfer restrictions. Transferability is a legal feature of shares, not a consequence of a listing."},
    {t:"Partnership interests are void if assigned", correct:false,
     why:"A partner can assign his share in the partnership, but the assignee only gets the right to receive the assigning partner's profits; he does not become a partner or gain management rights without consent (Chapter 3)."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.015], fn 64–65", takeaway:"Shares transfer; partnership interests need novation." },

{ id:"cl2-016", lesson:"2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How did Cook and Deakin contrast 'outsider' (dispersed) and 'insider' (concentrated) corporate systems?",
  options:[
    {t:"Outsider systems may do better in rapid, destabilising change by moving capital to new industries; insider systems may do better at incremental innovation with existing technologies", correct:true,
     why:"Cook and Deakin found little evidence that either the Anglo-American dispersed 'outsider' model or the Japanese/German 'insider' model was superior, and were cautious about broad conclusions. At most, they suggested that outsider systems could be at an advantage in periods of rapid, destabilising change, because they can mobilise capital to newly emerging industries, while insider systems would do better at exploiting existing technologies through incremental innovation.\n\nThe text's own position is that we should not adopt dogmatic notions that one structure is better. It cites evidence that investors in state-owned enterprise offerings do better where the government keeps a majority and new management runs the business as a business, and that family-owned companies may perform better on return on assets. Both are common in Singapore."},
    {t:"Outsider systems are always superior", correct:false,
     why:"Cook and Deakin, and the text, expressly reject that conclusion. Each system has relative strengths depending on the economic environment."},
    {t:"Insider systems are always superior", correct:false,
     why:"No such conclusion was drawn. Insider systems are said to do better only in one respect, incremental innovation with existing technologies."},
    {t:"There is no difference between them", correct:false,
     why:"The systems do differ, in who controls the company and how capital moves. The text's point is that the differences need not carry a value judgment, not that they do not exist."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.019]", takeaway:"No dogma: outsider = adapt to change; insider = incremental innovation." },

{ id:"cl2-017", lesson:"2B", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Where shareholders have economic (income) rights but fewer or no votes, entrenching founders, the company has ____ shares.",
  accept:["dual class","dual-class","dual class shares","dual-class shares"], answer:"dual class",
  why:"Dual class shares separate income rights from control rights. Some shareholders have economic interests in the company but fewer or no votes, which entrenches management through founder shares carrying enhanced votes.\n\nThe text mentions them as something that 'confuses' the neat picture of delegated management and investor ownership. Companies range from quasi-partnerships to large conglomerates, and from widely held to controlled by the State or families. Dual class structures add another layer, as does the fact that institutional investors can be passive or active, without any objective basis for saying which is preferable.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.018]", takeaway:"Dual class = income rights without matching votes." },

/* ---------- 2C External & internal perspectives ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-018", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Hansmann and Kraakman mean by 'reverse limited liability'?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company's assets are insulated from the creditors of its shareholders and directors, so it can pledge them without consulting shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"Externally, the company is like an individual: it owns its business, sues and is sued, and can borrow and commit its assets as collateral without referring to its shareholders (though it must act constitutionally and in its best interests). Hansmann and Kraakman said this last characteristic is the most crucial, and called it 'reverse limited liability'. It protects the entity: its assets are insulated from the bankruptcy or insolvency of its shareholders and directors.\n\nThis affirmative asset partitioning lets the ring-fenced fund be used freely as security. It lowers creditors' monitoring costs and so the cost of credit, especially where a floating charge covers the whole undertaking. Unlike limited liability, which can be created by contract, it is very hard to create contractually, because it would need the agreement of countless third parties."},
    {t:"Shareholders become liable for the company's debts", correct:false,
     why:"That is unlimited liability, the opposite of any kind of limited liability. Reverse limited liability is about protecting the company's assets, not exposing shareholders."},
    {t:"Creditors can claim against shareholders' personal assets", correct:false,
     why:"That describes removing owner shielding, which is what people usually mean by lifting the veil. Reverse limited liability works the other way: it keeps shareholders' creditors away from the company's assets."},
    {t:"Directors are shielded from all liability", correct:false,
     why:"The concept concerns the company's assets and the creditors of its insiders. It does not shield directors from liability for their own wrongs."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.020]–[02.021]", takeaway:"Reverse limited liability = entity shielding: company assets safe from shareholders' creditors." },

{ id:"cl2-019", lesson:"2C", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each concept to its meaning.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Owner shielding", r:"Shareholders' personal assets are protected from the company's creditors"},
    {l:"Entity shielding", r:"The company's assets are protected from shareholders' creditors"},
    {l:"Capital lock-in", r:"Contributed capital cannot be withdrawn at will"},
    {l:"Veil piercing (Prest)", r:"Disregarding personality to stop evasion of an existing obligation"}
  ],
  why:"Owner shielding is limited liability. Entity shielding is the affirmative asset partitioning that the text treats as more fundamental. Hansmann, Kraakman and Squire think entity shielding is more open to abuse today than owner shielding, given how many entities now receive separate-entity status. Capital lock-in makes the corporate fund more permanent than the funds of alternative vehicles.\n\nOn veil piercing, Lord Sumption in Prest said true piercing applies only where a person under an existing obligation deliberately evades or frustrates it by interposing a company he controls. Hurstwood confirmed that UK piercing is very narrow: it is about making the company liable for its controller's fault (removing entity shielding), not removing the controller's limited liability for the company's debts. The text notes Singapore's position is wider (Chapter 6).",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.014], [02.022], [02.025]–[02.026]", takeaway:"Owner shield = limited liability; entity shield = asset partitioning." },

{ id:"cl2-020", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Tjong Very Sumito v Chan Sing En, what two justifications for veil piercing did Steven Chong J identify?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company is one and the same as, or a mere front for, its controller; or the corporate structure is abused for an improper purpose", correct:true,
     why:"Chong J analysed the law up to that point and concluded there were really only two justifications for piercing the veil. First, where the company is one and the same as the controller, or is used as a mere front for the controller's affairs. Second, where the corporate structure is abused to further an improper purpose.\n\nThe UK Supreme Court in Prest and VTB later criticised labels like 'façade', 'sham' and 'alter ego' as unhelpful. In the UK, concealment can be handled by working harder with ordinary causes of action, and true piercing is confined to evasion. The text says Singapore's position remains wider than Lord Sumption's evasion principle, a point developed in Chapter 6."},
    {t:"Whenever it is just and equitable, or whenever creditors are unpaid", correct:false,
     why:"There is no general 'justice' ground and no unpaid-creditor ground. If unpaid creditors could pierce, limited liability would mean nothing. Courts protect separate personality carefully."},
    {t:"Group enterprise and economic reality", correct:false,
     why:"Being part of a corporate group does not by itself justify piercing. Each company in a group is a separate person; corporate groups are discussed in Chapter 6."},
    {t:"Breach of contract and negligence", correct:false,
     why:"These are ordinary causes of action. The UK cases suggest using such existing causes of action instead of piercing, especially in concealment cases. They are alternatives to piercing, not grounds for it."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.023]–[02.024], [02.026]", takeaway:"Tjong Very Sumito: (1) mere front/alter ego; (2) abuse for improper purpose." },

{ id:"cl2-021", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A controller runs a business through a thinly capitalised company that incurs large debts and then fails. A creditor argues that the veil should be pierced because it is unfair that the controller escapes liability.",
  prompt:"How would Lord Sumption's approach in Prest v Petrodel resolve this?",
  options:[
    {t:"No piercing: causing the company to incur a liability is not an abuse. Piercing applies only where a person deliberately evades an existing obligation by interposing a company.", correct:true,
     why:"Lord Sumption said: 'It is not an abuse to cause a legal liability to be incurred by the company in the first place. It is not an abuse to rely upon the fact (if it is a fact) that a liability is not the controller's because it is the company's.' He added that the objection would be just as strong if the liability had not been consensual, even though tort claimants have less chance to bargain about limited liability.\n\nTrue piercing applies only where a person is under an existing legal obligation, liability or restriction which he deliberately evades, or whose enforcement he deliberately frustrates, by interposing a company he controls. Here the debts were incurred by the company after incorporation. The text says the veil should never be pierced in such cases, because the controller's personal estate was never committed to the transaction, however unfair the result looks to the creditor."},
    {t:"Pierce the veil, because the company was thinly capitalised", correct:false,
     why:"Under Prest, thin capitalisation and perceived unfairness are not enough. Limited liability exists precisely so that a company's creditors look only to the company's assets."},
    {t:"Pierce only if the creditor's claim was in tort", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Sumption said expressly that the objection to piercing would be just as strong where the liability was non-consensual. Tort creditors do not get a special piercing route."},
    {t:"Wind up the controller personally", correct:false,
     why:"The controller is not personally liable for the company's debts merely because the company failed. Bankruptcy proceedings against him would need a debt he personally owes."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.024]–[02.025]", takeaway:"Prest: no piercing for company-incurred debts; only evasion of existing obligations." },

{ id:"cl2-022", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Pek Seng Co v Low Tin Kee, a Mareva injunction against a holding company was extended to its wholly owned subsidiary's assets. How did Chan J protect the subsidiary's own creditors?",
  options:[
    {t:"The injunction allowed the subsidiary to carry on business and pay ordinary debts and expenses, weakening entity shielding without fully piercing the veil", correct:true,
     why:"This was not the familiar 'runt of the litter' problem of making a parent pay a subsidiary's debts. It was the reverse: freezing a subsidiary's assets because of a claim against its parent. Chan J said that 'it would fly in the face of reality and common sense' to hold, for Mareva purposes, that the sole beneficial owner of a company's shares is not indirectly the owner of its net assets.\n\nBut counsel objected that fully lifting the veil would prejudice the subsidiary's creditors. So Chan J did not treat the assets as fully belonging to the parent. The injunction was granted on the agreed basis that the subsidiary could carry on business and pay ordinary debts and expenses in the normal course. The bifurcated ownership structure survived; entity shielding was weakened, not removed. The text says this shows the central importance of the company's proprietary characteristics."},
    {t:"It ordered the subsidiary's creditors to be paid first from the holding company's assets", correct:false,
     why:"No such order was made. The solution was a carve-out letting the subsidiary keep trading and paying its ordinary debts from its own assets."},
    {t:"It fully pierced the veil and treated the assets as the holding company's", correct:false,
     why:"Chan J expressly avoided full piercing because it would have aligned the shareholder's claim with the company's assets and prejudiced the subsidiary's own creditors."},
    {t:"It refused the injunction entirely", correct:false,
     why:"The injunction was granted, subject to the ordinary-course carve-out. Chan J accepted that the parent was, for Mareva purposes, indirectly the owner of the subsidiary's net assets."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.027], fn 117", takeaway:"Pek Seng: entity shielding weakened, not removed; ordinary-course carve-out." },

{ id:"cl2-023", lesson:"2C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In Jhaveri Darsan Jitendra v Salgaocar Anil Vassudeva, a shareholder successfully used veil piercing to claim that it owned the company's assets.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The High Court held that the shareholder could not use veil piercing to claim that it owned the company's assets, although it might have an indirect economic interest in them. That would reverse the usual scenario, in which third parties argue that the company and its shareholder are one so they can reach the shareholder (or the company).\n\nThis fits Chapter 1's starting point: shareholders own shares, not the company's assets. Compare Pek Seng, where a court treated a sole shareholder as indirectly owning a subsidiary's net assets for the limited purpose of a Mareva injunction sought by a third party, and even then protected the subsidiary's creditors."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The claim failed. A shareholder cannot pierce its own company's veil to treat the company's assets as its own. Doing so would undermine the very asset partitioning that protects the company's creditors."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.027], fn 72", takeaway:"Jhaveri: no reverse veil piercing for a shareholder." },

{ id:"cl2-024", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What point does the text take from Hansmann about the internal rules of large public companies?",
  options:[
    {t:"They almost always use the default rules, so the 'contract' is more theoretical than real, and standardisation lowers information costs for outsiders", correct:true,
     why:"Hansmann pointed out that in large public companies the default rules are almost always used, so even if the internal relationship is 'contract', that is more theoretical than real. Relationships among the many shareholders and other constituents are governed by standardised defaults. If these change at all, it is usually because the State has altered the boilerplate, such as the model constitutions.\n\nListing adds more standardisation: the exchange frowns on constitutions that stray from the norm, and listing rules impose further standard rules such as mandatory disclosure. Some agency costs are shifted to outsiders as information costs, and standardising internal rules lowers those costs, which can be large given how many outsiders deal with listed companies. Small private companies are the opposite: they make the most tailored use of shareholders' agreements, special constitutional terms and informal understandings."},
    {t:"They negotiate highly bespoke constitutions", correct:false,
     why:"That describes small private companies and quasi-partnerships, which the text calls the most contractarian. Large public companies converge on standard terms because they need to attract outside investors."},
    {t:"They are not bound by the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"Public companies are fully subject to the Act, and listed ones to listing rules as well. Standardisation adds rules rather than removing them."},
    {t:"Their internal rules are set by shareholders' agreements", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders' agreements are mainly a small-company tool. With thousands of shareholders, a public company relies on its constitution and the default rules."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.030]–[02.031]", takeaway:"Public companies run on standardised defaults; small companies tailor." },

{ id:"cl2-025", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Sim Yong Kim v Evenstar Investments, how did Chan CJ distinguish the formal constitution from the members' internal understandings?",
  options:[
    {t:"The memorandum and articles are conclusive as to the company's business in relation to third parties, while corporators in a quasi-partnership may have a more internal understanding among themselves", correct:true,
     why:"Chan CJ treated the formal memorandum and articles as conclusive evidence of the company's business in relation to third parties only, and contrasted this with the internal understanding that can arise among corporators in a quasi-partnership. The text calls this 'layers within layers', differing from company to company.\n\nThe governance structure is still relevant to the outside world. But there is an even more internal layer: informal restraints among shareholders inter se. These surface in s 216 oppression claims, an amorphous area with close links to the family context, where the usual remedy in a small private company is an order that the majority buy out the minority. The text adds that the company has both in rem and in personam characteristics, combining external asset partitioning with a contractually flexible internal structure."},
    {t:"The constitution overrides all informal understandings in every context", correct:false,
     why:"That would ignore the quasi-partnership layer that Chan CJ expressly recognised. Informal understandings among members can be enforced through s 216, even where they are not in the constitution."},
    {t:"Informal understandings bind third parties", correct:false,
     why:"Third parties rely on the formal documents, which are conclusive as against them. Informal understandings operate among the members only."},
    {t:"Quasi-partnerships are not companies", correct:false,
     why:"Quasi-partnerships are companies, incorporated in the ordinary way, whose members have partnership-like mutual expectations (Ebrahimi). The label describes the relationship, not a different legal form."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.028]–[02.029]", takeaway:"Layers within layers: formal documents face outward; quasi-partnership understandings face inward." },

/* ---------- 2D Internal conflicts ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-026", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When the board or the general meeting acts as an organ of the company, what is its relationship to the company in attribution terms?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is the company for that decision: identification, not agency or vicarious liability", correct:true,
     why:"Because the company is a fiction, it can only act through people, and sometimes liability needs intent or negligence as well as an act. So the law must pinpoint a constituency within the company, such as the board or the shareholders in general meeting, whose intent or act is attributed to it. Who 'is the company' is context-specific.\n\nWhen a rule identifies the company with an organ for a particular transaction, that is identification or attribution, which is different from agency in corporate contracting or vicarious liability. For relevant decisions, the board or general meeting is the company. In Gabriel Peter & Partners v Wee Chong Jin the court said '[t]here was no agency or master-servant relationship involved and no issue of vicarious liability'. Agency law comes in when the question is whether the company has given an individual authority to affect its legal position, which brings the three-party problems of Chapter 7."},
    {t:"It is the company's agent, and the company is vicariously liable", correct:false,
     why:"The text warns that the organic theory is easily confused with agency precisely because the company can only operate through people. But organs are identified with the company; they are not agents for whose acts the company is vicariously liable."},
    {t:"It is a third party contracting with the company", correct:false,
     why:"Organs are part of the company's own decision-making structure. Treating the board as an outsider contracting with the company would make every board decision a two-party contract, which is not how attribution works."},
    {t:"It is a trustee of the company's assets", correct:false,
     why:"Directors were once described as trustees, and some judges still use that language for specific purposes, but the general meeting is certainly not a trustee. Attribution is about identification, not trusteeship."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.032]–[02.033]", takeaway:"Organs are the company (identification); agents act for it (agency)." },

{ id:"cl2-027", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is an 'agency cost' in company law?",
  options:[
    {t:"The cost arising from managers' objectives diverging from shareholders' and from information asymmetry", correct:true,
     why:"Organs and agents alike can act in their own interest rather than their principal's. Directors are vested with most management powers by s 157A, which creates room for opportunism. This is the agency costs problem, first highlighted indirectly by Berle and Means (1932) and then specifically by Jensen and Meckling (1976).\n\nAgency cost is the cost to an organisation of problems such as divergent management-shareholder objectives and information asymmetry. The whole debate is about how to minimise managers' incentives to shirk, given how hard it is to monitor them. Separation of ownership and management has made companies efficient at creating wealth, but managers may divert that wealth to themselves. The text stresses that this economic concept is 'not quite the law of agency'."},
    {t:"The fee paid to an agent for arranging a contract", correct:false,
     why:"That is a commission or brokerage fee. Agency costs are an economic concept about the misalignment between those who control a company and those who fund it."},
    {t:"The cost of registering a company", correct:false,
     why:"Registration fees are transaction costs of incorporation. The text notes that incorporation may reduce hold-out costs among contracting parties but then creates agency costs, which are a different thing."},
    {t:"A penalty imposed by ACRA", correct:false,
     why:"Agency costs are not a regulatory sanction. They are the losses and monitoring expenses that arise when managers' interests diverge from investors'."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.031], [02.034]", takeaway:"Agency costs = misaligned managers + information asymmetry (Berle & Means; Jensen & Meckling)." },

{ id:"cl2-028", lesson:"2D", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each provision to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"s 157A CA (Singapore)", r:"Business managed by or under the direction of the directors"},
    {l:"s 169 CA (Singapore)", r:"Shareholder approval of directors' fees and emoluments"},
    {l:"s 439 CA 2006 (UK)", r:"Annual advisory vote on the remuneration report"},
    {l:"s 439A CA 2006 (UK)", r:"Binding vote on remuneration policy at least every three years"}
  ],
  why:"Section 157A gives directors most management powers, the source of the agency costs problem. Bebchuk and Fried argue that managers have used board control to raise their pay and keep it separate from performance, and they advocate greater shareholder control over executive pay.\n\nIn Singapore, shareholders approve directors' fees and emoluments under s 169 but have had little say on executive pay. The UK introduced an advisory vote on the directors' remuneration report in 2002, carried into s 439 of the Companies Act 2006 as an annual non-binding vote. The Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013 then added a binding vote on the remuneration policy once every three years, with the rest of the report still subject to the annual advisory vote. The text notes that in Singapore, with concentrated shareholdings, management is likely accountable to controlling shareholders on pay, but outside shareholders may be unhappy with that.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.034]–[02.035]", takeaway:"SG: s 157A powers, s 169 fees. UK: s 439 advisory, s 439A binding policy." },

{ id:"cl2-029", lesson:"2D", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The text suggests that more shareholder activism is the main answer to Singapore's governance concerns.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Many jurisdictions have Stewardship Codes to encourage institutional investors to be more active, and Singapore has its Stewardship Principles for Responsible Investors (2016), which apply to non-family businesses (with a separate set for family businesses). But the text says most of Singapore's concerns are less with shareholder activism, which seems to be the EU's focus, and more with board responsibility, which is closer to the US approach.\n\nThe reason is the less dispersed nature of shareholdings here. With concentrated ownership, greater shareholder control or involvement 'may in fact be part of the problem, and not the solution'. Focusing on the duties and responsibilities of the board, which may already be controlled by the major shareholder, therefore makes sense."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite. Where a major shareholder already controls the board, more shareholder power mostly means more power for that controller. The emphasis should be on board duties, to protect minorities."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.036], fn 151", takeaway:"Singapore: focus on board responsibility, not shareholder activism." },

{ id:"cl2-030", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did the Kay Review (July 2012) examine, and what example did it use?",
  options:[
    {t:"Short-termism in shareholder value, illustrated by ICI's decline as it chased its short-term share price", correct:true,
     why:"The Kay Review of UK Equity Markets and Long-Term Decision Making re-examined the basis of shareholder value and short-termism by both management and investors, publishing its Final Report in July 2012. It charted the decline of Imperial Chemical Industries from a leading chemical and pharmaceutical company to one that sold off its core business and focused on its short-term share price, to the ultimate detriment of its shareholders.\n\nKay distinguished longer-term investors from short-term traders. The text says focusing on the longer-term shareholder lets directors express concern for other constituents indirectly interested in the corporate fund. That sits between the extreme 'just raise the share price' view and the UK's 'enlightened shareholder value' in s 172(1)."},
    {t:"Directors' duties to creditors on insolvency", correct:false,
     why:"That debate arose from the CLRSG's proposals and the UK government's July 2002 White Paper, which rejected special creditor duties. The Kay Review was about equity markets and short-termism."},
    {t:"Dual class share listings", correct:false,
     why:"Dual class shares are mentioned elsewhere in the chapter as complicating investor ownership. They were not the Kay Review's subject."},
    {t:"The Cadbury takeover", correct:false,
     why:"Kraft's takeover of Cadbury is discussed separately, as prompting the UK to consider strengthening the entity focus in takeovers. It is not the Kay Review's example."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.037]", takeaway:"Kay Review: short-termism; ICI; long-term investors vs traders." },

{ id:"cl2-031", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does it mean to describe the board as a 'mediating hierarch'?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board balances the competing interests of the company's constituencies, acting in the interests of the company as a whole", correct:true,
     why:"The text treats conflicts among stakeholders (for example, shareholders against creditors) as separate from the agency costs problem. In these conflicts the board serves as a 'mediating hierarch', acting in the best interests of the company as a whole. It has been suggested this is part of the duty to exercise powers for proper purposes, reflecting a fiduciary requirement of impartiality or 'even-handedness' (Ng Eng Ghee v Mamata Kapildev Dave).\n\nDirector primacy then becomes more important, with directors as a neutral party separate from management, which Blair and Stout's team production theory says may not be a bad thing. It partly explains why directors' duties are owed to the company as an entity and not to individual shareholders. The text notes how quickly the debate has moved toward asking directors to serve corporate purposes including ESG objectives."},
    {t:"The board takes instructions from the majority shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Mediating means balancing interests even-handedly, not obeying one constituency. A board that simply follows the majority is the problem in many Singapore cases, not the mediating ideal."},
    {t:"The board resolves disputes like an arbitral tribunal", correct:false,
     why:"The term describes the board's governance role in weighing constituencies' interests when making decisions, not a formal dispute resolution function."},
    {t:"Directors owe duties directly to every stakeholder", correct:false,
     why:"Duties are owed to the company. Mediation happens through that duty and the proper purpose rule, not through separate duties owed to each stakeholder group."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.038], fn 158–162", takeaway:"Mediating hierarch: the board balances constituencies for the company as a whole." },

{ id:"cl2-032", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Activists buy a small stake in a listed oil major and bring a derivative claim alleging its directors breached their duties by not adopting a stronger climate strategy. Over 80% of shareholders had approved the existing strategy.",
  prompt:"What happened in ClientEarth v Shell plc, and what does it illustrate?",
  options:[
    {t:"The derivative claim failed and the petitioners had to pay Shell's costs, illustrating 'voice' rather than 'exit' conflicts inside listed companies", correct:true,
     why:"In listed companies, intra-shareholder conflict is usually minimised by listing rules and, more importantly, by the minority's ability to exit by selling on the secondary market. In close companies there is no market, so s 216 mimics an exit through a court-ordered buyout.\n\nOn contentious ESG issues, however, some shareholders deliberately buy in to use 'voice' rather than exit, bringing derivative actions to make directors act in what they see as the company's long-term interest, even at the cost of profits now. In ClientEarth v Shell, the derivative claim, together with the negligence claim, failed. In a departure from the usual position, the petitioners, who had bought a small shareholding, had to pay Shell's costs, given that some 80% of shareholders had approved the existing strategy."},
    {t:"The court ordered Shell to adopt the activists' climate plan", correct:false,
     why:"The claim failed, and courts do not generally dictate business strategy. No such order was made."},
    {t:"The court ordered a buyout of the activists under s 216", correct:false,
     why:"ClientEarth was an English derivative claim. Section 216 buyouts belong to Singapore's oppression regime and are typically used in close companies, not listed ones."},
    {t:"The claim succeeded because directors owe duties to the environment", correct:false,
     why:"Directors owe duties to the company. ESG considerations enter through those duties, for example under the proper purpose rule, and in this case the claim failed."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.039]", takeaway:"ClientEarth v Shell: an ESG 'voice' claim failed, with costs." },

{ id:"cl2-033", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the Singapore position on directors' duties when a company is near or in insolvency?",
  options:[
    {t:"No direct duty is owed to creditors, but creditors' interests come to the fore, and courts take a flexible view of when insolvency arises", correct:true,
     why:"In the vicinity of insolvency, whatever value remains in the company in a sense belongs to the creditors, and shareholders with nothing to lose may gamble with it. Insolvency law curbs this, and company law does so indirectly by saying the director's duty is to the company, not the shareholders. But the duty is not owed directly to creditors either.\n\nEarlier, Chip Thye spoke of a duty to present and future creditors. More recent decisions, W P Piling at first instance and the Court of Appeal in Progen, confirmed there is no direct duty to creditors even in insolvency, although 'the creditors' interests came to the fore' because the company was effectively being run with their money. Because the language is no longer a duty to creditors, courts are flexible about insolvency: Dynasty Line said strict going-concern and balance-sheet tests are 'of limited utility'. This matches the UK (s 172 has no direct creditor duty) and Australia (Spies v The Queen)."},
    {t:"Directors owe an independent fiduciary duty directly to each creditor", correct:false,
     why:"Progen rejected a direct duty, consistent with Spies in Australia. The duty stays with the company; creditors' interests are considered within it."},
    {t:"Directors may ignore creditors until a winding-up order is made", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors' interests come to the fore before any formal winding up, once the company is insolvent or near it. Waiting for a winding-up order would come too late to protect them."},
    {t:"Directors must immediately cease trading at the first sign of insolvency", correct:false,
     why:"That kind of chilling effect is exactly what the UK government feared in 2002 when it rejected special creditor duties as contrary to the 'rescue culture'."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.040]–[02.042], fn 178–181", takeaway:"No direct duty to creditors (Progen); their interests come to the fore." },

{ id:"cl2-034", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A listed company's holding company sells shares before the company discloses large trading losses. Some outside shareholders also sell to new investors during the period before disclosure.",
  prompt:"Which conflict does the text use China Aviation Oil to illustrate?",
  options:[
    {t:"Between present and future generations of shareholders, created by false or delayed disclosure", correct:true,
     why:"From 2005 Singapore saw several governance scandals at listed companies like China Aviation Oil Singapore, many involving failure to disclose losses. These prejudiced outside shareholders in favour of insiders: the holding company sold CAO shares before the trading losses were disclosed and paid a S$8m civil penalty.\n\nThey also pitted different generations of outside shareholders against each other. Those lucky enough to sell during the gap between when the information should have been disclosed and when it was, passed the loss on to newer buyers. Some cases equate the company's interest with present shareholders (Heron v Grade), others say directors should consider future shareholders (Dawson v Coats Paton), but neither creates an actual duty. The text suggests the best safeguard for inter-generational equity is a board fully empowered to mediate between present and future."},
    {t:"Between creditors and shareholders near insolvency", correct:false,
     why:"That conflict is real and discussed nearby, but this example is about the timing of disclosure and who among shareholders bears the loss."},
    {t:"Between directors and auditors", correct:false,
     why:"The text uses China Aviation Oil to show an insider-outsider and inter-generational shareholder conflict, not a dispute between directors and auditors."},
    {t:"Between two classes of preference shares", correct:false,
     why:"Class rights are a separate topic (Chapter 12). The CAO example concerns ordinary shareholders trading at different times."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.044]–[02.045], fn 192–195", takeaway:"China Aviation Oil: present vs future shareholders (S$8m civil penalty)." },

{ id:"cl2-035", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why did the UK government in 2002 reject the CLRSG's proposed special duties to creditors near insolvency?",
  options:[
    {t:"They ran counter to the 'rescue culture' and risked a chilling effect, with directors abandoning a going concern at the first hint of insolvency", correct:true,
     why:"The UK Company Law Reform Steering Group proposed a statement of directors' duties with two special creditor provisions: one where the company was more likely than not unable to meet its debts, and another where there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation. Singapore's Company Legislation and Regulatory Framework Committee also recommended such duties (fn 170).\n\nThe UK government's July 2002 White Paper rejected them as running counter to the 'rescue culture' it preferred. It noted that the CLRSG itself recognised such a duty would have 'a chilling effect, bringing with it the risk that directors may run down or abandon a going concern at the first hint of insolvency'. So s 172 of the Companies Act 2006 contains no direct duty to creditors, consistent with Spies v The Queen in Australia."},
    {t:"Creditors were already fully protected by contract", correct:false,
     why:"Some judges do say creditors should protect themselves contractually (Lord Reed in Sequana), but that was not the 2002 government's reason. Its reasons were the rescue culture and the chilling effect."},
    {t:"Directors owed no duties of any kind", correct:false,
     why:"Directors owe duties to the company, which s 172 codifies. The question was only whether to add special duties to creditors on top."},
    {t:"EU law prohibited such duties", correct:false,
     why:"The reasons given were domestic policy reasons about rescue and director behaviour, not an EU prohibition."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.041], fn 170", takeaway:"UK rejected creditor duties: rescue culture and chilling effect." },

/* ---------- 2E Resolving conflicts through the company ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-036", lesson:"2E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How did Jenkins LJ state the rule in Foss v Harbottle in Edwards v Halliwell?",
  options:[
    {t:"The proper plaintiff for a wrong done to the company is prima facie the company; and if the wrong can be ratified by a simple majority, no individual member may sue", correct:true,
     why:"Jenkins LJ said the rule 'comes to no more than this'. First, the proper plaintiff for a wrong done to a company is prima facie the company. Second, where the wrong is a transaction that could be made binding on the company and all its members by a simple majority, no individual member may sue, because if a majority favours what has been done, 'cadit quaestio' (the matter ends).\n\nThe text maps the two limbs onto two facets of the company. The first reflects its separate existence: the right of action belongs to the company. The second tests 'who is the company' for attributing a decision and handling inter-constituency disputes. Finding the proxy for the company is hard, especially when ratification arises, because the company is not in charge of its own destiny: different organs, controlled by others, may decide differently. Chapter 10 covers the rule in detail."},
    {t:"Any shareholder may sue for any wrong done to the company", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly what the proper plaintiff rule prevents. A shareholder who wants to enforce the company's rights must go through a derivative action, with leave."},
    {t:"Only creditors may sue for wrongs to the company", correct:false,
     why:"The company is the proper plaintiff. Creditors are outsiders to the company's cause of action, although a liquidator may sue for the company in insolvency."},
    {t:"Directors are personally liable for every corporate loss", correct:false,
     why:"Foss v Harbottle is about who can sue for a wrong to the company, not about the scope of directors' liability."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.046]", takeaway:"Foss v Harbottle: (1) the company sues; (2) the majority decides." },

{ id:"cl2-037", lesson:"2E", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Under the rule in Foss v Harbottle, the proper plaintiff for a wrong done to a company is prima facie the ____.",
  accept:["company","the company"], answer:"company",
  why:"The proper plaintiff rule follows from separate personality: the right of action 'reposes' in the company, so the company must bring the claim. The text links this to its broader view that, although much of company law is contractarian, the separate entity is conferred by the State and has clear proprietary consequences, of which the rule in Foss v Harbottle is one.\n\nThe difficulty is that wrongdoers often control the company, so it will not sue. Hence the derivative action: Singapore has legislated for the statutory derivative action in s 216A, and the text suggests that even multiple derivative actions (suing for a wrong to a subsidiary) may still be possible at common law or under s 216A.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.046]", takeaway:"Wrong to the company → the company sues." },

{ id:"cl2-038", lesson:"2E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Shareholders generally decide whether to ratify a breach. When can they NOT do so?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where ratification would amount to a fraud on creditors or offend capital maintenance rules", correct:true,
     why:"For ratification, the text says, 'it is the shareholders who decide'. That is why identifying who speaks for the company matters so much. But there are limits: shareholders cannot ratify what amounts to a fraud on creditors, or what offends capital maintenance rules (Sequana, especially Lady Arden; Raffles Town Club v Lim Eng Hock Peter).\n\nIt gets more complicated where the majority shareholder is itself a company. Then the 'genuine will' of that corporate member must be found too, which can turn into a double or multiple derivative action problem. Ratification by insiders who are the wrongdoers also raises separate problems examined in Chapter 10."},
    {t:"Whenever a director votes as a shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Interested voting raises its own issues, but a director-shareholder is not automatically barred from voting. The limits the text names are fraud on creditors and capital maintenance."},
    {t:"Only in public companies", correct:false,
     why:"The limits on ratification apply to companies generally. In fact, they matter most in small companies where the same people are directors and shareholders."},
    {t:"Ratification is never permitted", correct:false,
     why:"Ratification is generally available, which is why the second limb of Foss v Harbottle exists. Only these limits apply."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.046], fn 204–206", takeaway:"No ratification of fraud on creditors or capital maintenance breaches." },

{ id:"cl2-039", lesson:"2E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why might Singapore's statutory derivative action under s 216A not rule out a multiple derivative action?",
  options:[
    {t:"A 'complainant' includes any other person the court considers a proper person to apply", correct:true,
     why:"Singapore and the UK have legislated for the first-order single derivative action, where a member sues for a wrong to his own company. The text says this may not have ruled out the multiple derivative action, either at common law or under s 216A itself.\n\nThe reason is the definition of 'complainant' in s 216A, which includes 'any other person who, in the discretion of the Court, is a proper person to make an application under this section'. That could let, for example, a member of a holding company sue for a wrong done to a subsidiary where the holding company's own board will not act. The UK court in Fort Gilkicker referred to Singapore's position, and in the UK beneficiaries of a pension trust corporation have been allowed to bring such claims (fn 210)."},
    {t:"Section 216A applies only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 216A is not confined to listed companies. The point that matters here is the breadth of the 'complainant' definition."},
    {t:"Common law derivative actions were abolished", correct:false,
     why:"The text suggests the common law route may survive, particularly for multiple derivative actions. It does not say it was abolished."},
    {t:"Section 216A requires creditor consent", correct:false,
     why:"No creditor consent is needed. The court's discretion over who is a 'proper person' is what opens the door."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.046], fn 207–210", takeaway:"s 216A 'proper person' leaves room for multiple derivative actions." },

{ id:"cl2-040", lesson:"2E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The majority in a two-family company removes the minority family from management and stops consulting them, contrary to their understanding when the company was formed. The minority wants a remedy.",
  prompt:"How should this dispute be framed?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a personal claim: the s 216 oppression action. The dispute is outside the company as an entity and does not necessarily require a breach of directors' duties.", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the best way to mediate corporate disputes is through the corporate form as a starting point. But where, on closer analysis, the dispute is de hors the corporation as an entity, as when minorities claim to be unfairly prejudiced by the majority, it is a personal claim, such as the s 216 oppression action, which does not necessarily require considering directors' duties.\n\nHere the minority's complaint is about broken expectations among the members of a quasi-partnership, the innermost of Sim Yong Kim's 'layers'. The Court of Appeal in Ng Kek Wee v Sim City Technology stressed distinguishing 'company' from 'personal' wrongs, because that determines which actions can be brought, and reaffirmed this in Ho Yew Kong v Sakae Holdings, while accepting that the line is not always clear. The usual remedy would be a buyout of the minority."},
    {t:"As a company claim, which only the company can bring", correct:false,
     why:"The harm is to the minority's personal position and expectations as members, not a wrong done to the company. Framing it as a company claim would hand control of the claim to the very majority that caused the problem."},
    {t:"As a claim by creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors are not involved. This is a dispute among shareholders over their relationship."},
    {t:"As a breach of contract claim under s 39 only", correct:false,
     why:"The informal understanding is not in the constitution, so s 39 does not capture it. Quasi-partnership expectations are enforced through s 216, not ordinary contractual remedies."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.029], [02.047]", takeaway:"Unfair prejudice to a minority = personal wrong → s 216." },

{ id:"cl2-041", lesson:"2E", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Evidence of a breach of directors' duties can never be used to support an oppression claim under s 216.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the two causes of action are 'clearly not mutually exclusive': evidence of a breach of directors' duty that adversely affects minority shareholders can be used as evidence of oppression. But the underlying mental models are fundamentally different. A breach of duty is a wrong to the company; oppression is a wrong to members personally. Much depends on the nature of the claim and the relief sought.\n\nFootnote 212 adds the converse: a breach of directors' duty does not automatically constitute oppression. The text's closing line is that litigation lawyers may like to blur the distinction, but corporate advisers cannot afford to."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The text says expressly that such evidence can be used. The actions overlap in their evidence while differing in their nature and the relief available."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.047], fn 212", takeaway:"Duty breach can evidence oppression, but the actions differ in kind." },

{ id:"cl2-042", lesson:"2E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does Singapore's traditional formulation of a director's good faith duty differ from s 172 of the UK Companies Act 2006?",
  options:[
    {t:"Singapore sees the duty as owed to the entity; UK s 172 frames it as promoting the company's success 'for the benefit of the members as a whole'", correct:true,
     why:"Singapore law has traditionally seen a director's duty as one to the entity, not to any particular constituency. In slight contrast, s 172 of the UK Companies Act 2006 codifies the good faith duty as a duty 'to promote the success of the company for the benefit of the members as a whole', which the text reads as a proxy for long-term shareholders.\n\nAfter Kraft's takeover of Cadbury, where shareholders were thought to have taken too short-term a view in accepting the bid, the UK considered strengthening the entity focus in takeovers. The text's view is that even when the company looks different, at a takeover (shareholder interests more visible) or near insolvency (creditor interests more visible), the duty stays attached to the entity, though present shareholders as a whole remain the main concern."},
    {t:"Singapore requires directors to maximise the current share price", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore's focus is the entity. Maximising the current share price is the 'extreme version' of shareholder value the text says some, perhaps wrongly, associate with the US."},
    {t:"UK s 172 imposes a direct duty to creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Section 172 contains no direct duty to creditors; that proposal was rejected in 2002. Creditors' interests come in through s 172(3)."},
    {t:"There is no difference", correct:false,
     why:"The text identifies a 'slight contrast' between the entity focus in Singapore and the members-as-a-whole formulation in the UK."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.041]–[02.042], [02.046]", takeaway:"SG: duty to the entity. UK s 172: success for members as a whole." }

]);

;
