/* DealCraft — Company Law, Chapter 2 supplement: application and cross-angle questions (Tjio Ch 2)
   Lessons 2A–2E. Ids cl2-043 onward. Paragraph refs [02.xxx] are to the chapter. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.companyLaw = (window.DC_BANK.companyLaw||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 2A Contractarian view ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-043", lesson:"2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director of Lumen Pte Ltd diverts a contract to his own firm. The company loses $1m of profit, and the value of Wei's 20% shareholding falls by $200,000 as a result. Wei sues the director personally for his $200,000.",
  prompt:"What principle, noted by Chan CJ in Townsing v Jenton, stands in Wei's way?",
  options:[
    {t:"Reflective loss: a shareholder cannot recover a loss that merely reflects the company's loss; his claim must be for a loss distinct from the company's", correct:true,
     why:"In Townsing Henry George v Jenton Overseas Investment, Chan Sek Keong CJ described the company as an 'artificial construct', but a real one, and said this creates the problem of reflective loss. A shareholder with a personal claim is barred from claiming a loss that reflects the company's loss. Put differently, the shareholder's cause of action must be accompanied by a loss that is distinct from the company's.\n\nWei's $200,000 is simply his share of the company's $1m loss passing through to his shares. If the company recovers the $1m, the value of his shares is restored. Letting both sue would risk double recovery and would let a shareholder jump ahead of the company's creditors. The proper route is for the company to sue (Foss v Harbottle), or for Wei to seek leave to bring a derivative action under s 216A."},
    {t:"Limited liability: Wei cannot be liable for the company's loss", correct:false,
     why:"Limited liability is about Wei's exposure to the company's creditors, not about whether he can sue a director. It has nothing to say about recovering a fall in share value."},
    {t:"The ultra vires rule: the director lacked capacity", correct:false,
     why:"Ultra vires concerns the company's capacity, not who may sue a director for diverting an opportunity. The obstacle to Wei's claim is that his loss mirrors the company's."},
    {t:"There is no obstacle: any shareholder can recover his proportionate share of a corporate loss", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly what the reflective loss principle prevents. Wei must show a loss distinct from the company's, or proceed derivatively on the company's behalf."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.008], fn 30", takeaway:"Reflective loss: shareholder must show loss distinct from the company's." },

{ id:"cl2-044", lesson:"2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Kestrel Holdings is incorporated in the British Virgin Islands. Its business, assets and shareholders are all in Singapore, and its shareholders' agreement is governed by Singapore law. The shareholders dispute whether the board validly appointed a new director.",
  prompt:"Which law presumptively governs that question?",
  options:[
    {t:"BVI law, the law of the place of incorporation, because it governs corporate governance and internal management as a matter of law and right", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the law of the place of incorporation still applies to most corporate law issues, which stems from the continued importance of the company's separate entity status. A company owes its very existence to the law of the place where it was incorporated (ICIC v Adham). In Oro Negro the Singapore court said that law governs corporate governance and internal management 'as a matter of law and right', and that the place of incorporation is prima facie the more appropriate forum.\n\nWhether a director was validly appointed is a question of internal management, so BVI law governs it, even though everything else about Kestrel is Singaporean. The Singapore-law shareholders' agreement may govern the shareholders' contractual promises to each other (for example, a promise to vote for someone), but it cannot change the law that governs the company's own constitution."},
    {t:"Singapore law, because the shareholders' agreement chose it", correct:false,
     why:"A choice of law in a contract binds the contracting parties as to their contract. It cannot change the company's lex incorporationis. The text warns that an overly contractarian view, applying a contract's proper law to corporate questions, creates conflict of laws difficulties."},
    {t:"Singapore law, because the assets are in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"The location of assets matters for some property questions, not for internal management. The board's power to appoint directors comes from the company's constitution and the law that created it."},
    {t:"Whichever law the court thinks fairest on the facts", correct:false,
     why:"There is a settled rule here, not an open discretion: the law of incorporation governs internal management."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.009], fn 38–39", takeaway:"Internal management follows the law of incorporation, not the SHA's chosen law." },

{ id:"cl2-045", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A foreign client asks whether its new Singapore company can issue bearer shares, and where its registered (non-bearer) shares are situated for conflict of laws purposes. What does the text indicate?",
  options:[
    {t:"Bearer shares are not allowed in Singapore (s 66); non-bearer shares may be situated where the share register is kept (Macmillan v Bishopsgate)", correct:true,
     why:"Footnote 37 notes that bearer shares are not allowed in Singapore under s 66 of the Companies Act. Ownership of a Singapore company's shares must therefore be recorded, which fits the regulatory emphasis on transparency.\n\nOn where shares are situated, the same footnote cites Macmillan v Bishopsgate Investment Trust (No 3), where the Court of Appeal thought the lex situs of non-bearer shares could be the law of the place of the share register. The body text adds that the law of incorporation may govern transfers of directly held non-bearer shares. Either way, the answer follows the company and its register, reflecting the entity's importance, rather than a contract between buyer and seller."},
    {t:"Bearer shares are allowed if the constitution permits them; shares are situated wherever the holder lives", correct:false,
     why:"Section 66 prohibits bearer shares, and a constitution cannot override that. And the authority cited points to the place of the register, not the holder's residence."},
    {t:"Bearer shares are allowed for public companies only", correct:false,
     why:"The prohibition is general. There is no public company exception."},
    {t:"Shares have no situs because they are intangible", correct:false,
     why:"Intangibles still need a situs for conflict of laws purposes, and Macmillan locates non-bearer shares by reference to the register."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.009], fn 37", takeaway:"No bearer shares in Singapore (s 66); share situs ≈ the register." },

{ id:"cl2-046", lesson:"2A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A minister proposes that every Singapore company be required by its constitution to pursue a set of social goals. A contractarian law professor is asked to comment.",
  prompt:"What would the contractarian position, as the text describes it, say?",
  options:[
    {t:"Social goals belong in exogenous public legislation; companies are 'empty vessels' whose purposes are for the private parties to decide", correct:true,
     why:"According to the contractarians, company law should follow the path parties would take when privately ordering their affairs in their own narrow self-interest, and should not build public policy into the governance structure. Any social goals should be achieved through exogenous public legislation, such as environmental or labour laws that apply to everyone.\n\nCompanies have no goals of their own; in that sense they are 'empty vessels', and what they do is for the private parties to decide. Where, as usual, the company is set up for the residual claimants, it should maximise shareholder value. The text does not fully adopt this view, but it is the contractarian answer. Chapter 1 adds a practical caution from Davies: mandated purpose clauses tend to become boilerplate, like the old wide objects clauses."},
    {t:"Companies should pursue whatever goals regulators set", correct:false,
     why:"This is the opposite of the contractarian view, which keeps public policy out of the corporate governance structure and puts it in external legislation."},
    {t:"Directors should balance all stakeholders equally", correct:false,
     why:"Contractarians argue that a manager told to serve 'two masters' answers to neither. Equal balancing is what they reject."},
    {t:"Companies are real persons with moral duties of their own", correct:false,
     why:"Contractarians downplay the entity, treating personhood as 'a matter of convenience rather than a reality'. They would not ground social goals in the company's own moral personality."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.003]", takeaway:"Contractarian: social goals via external law; companies are empty vessels." },

{ id:"cl2-047", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the text trace the shifting focus of corporate theory that led to contractarianism?",
  options:[
    {t:"Early theory focused on the separate entity; then on managerial autonomy as US companies grew and shareholdings dispersed; then contractarian theory arose to explain the status quo and stave off more regulation", correct:true,
     why:"The text says early company law theory was very much taken with the separate entity status of the corporation. That was followed by a preoccupation with managerial autonomy in the 20th century, especially in the US, as companies expanded interstate funded by new share issues, diluting and dispersing shareholdings.\n\nGrowing unhappiness with management power then created a need for a contractarian theory to explain the status quo and to stave off increased regulation of corporate governance. Easterbrook and Fischel's line that corporate 'personhood' is 'a matter of convenience rather than a reality' shows how far the theory moved away from the entity focus. The text's own view brings the entity back in."},
    {t:"Theory began with contract and only recently discovered the entity", correct:false,
     why:"The sequence ran the other way. The entity was the early focus; the contractarian turn came later."},
    {t:"Contractarianism was invented to justify heavy state control", correct:false,
     why:"It was developed partly to fend off increased regulation, not to justify it."},
    {t:"The focus has always been on creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors appear later in the chapter as a constituency, but the historical sequence described is entity → managerial autonomy → contract."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.002]", takeaway:"Entity → managerial autonomy → contractarian theory (to explain and resist regulation)." },

{ id:"cl2-048", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the text characterise the attitude of Singapore's regulators and courts toward theories of the corporation?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not fundamentalist: with so much at stake in a small economy, there is little room for experimentation, and courts prefer to be 'solution-oriented rather than doctrine-oriented'", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the most important point is that Singapore regulators do not adopt fundamentalist views of what a corporation should be. Given the size and structure of the economy there is less room for legal experimentation, and Singapore's judges have been more temperate than some elsewhere. Chan CJ, extra-judicially, said the court prefers to be 'solution-oriented rather than doctrine-oriented'.\n\nLearned Hand warned against being too doctrinaire, and the strength of the common law is said to lie in pragmatism over dogmatism. So even if contractarianism explains much of the company, it does not explain it all. The cases, driven by the internal logic of precedent, are usually better predictors of outcomes than a priori theorising. Lan adds that the root theories of the legal models are pluralistic, which supports firm-specific rules and an 'integrative approach'."},
    {t:"Strictly contractarian, following US theory", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite: no single theory is adopted dogmatically, and the contractarian view does not explain the company completely."},
    {t:"Strictly communitarian, putting stakeholders first", correct:false,
     why:"That is also a fundamentalist position, which the text says Singapore avoids."},
    {t:"Indifferent to theory, relying only on statute", correct:false,
     why:"The text treats theory as useful for shedding light on the company, but says the cases matter more. It does not say theory is ignored, and statute is only one source."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.001], [02.011]", takeaway:"Singapore: pragmatic, 'solution-oriented rather than doctrine-oriented'." },

{ id:"cl2-049", lesson:"2A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the text say Singapore's regulators are 'not a disinterested third party' in the corporate bargain?",
  options:[
    {t:"They are concerned with economic growth and have made the Act more pro-business, and the State also holds shares in some listed and private companies", correct:true,
     why:"The open-ended corporate contract and the State's imprimatur on the entity mean regulators are not neutral. The Act has been increasingly amended to be more pro-business, reflecting regulators' concern with economic growth, sometimes more than the general population's concern with equitable distribution of income or wealth. Rule changes often favour management, sometimes shareholders, mimicking the negotiation between those two constituents.\n\nIn Singapore this is also explained by the State's own interest in these internal roles through its shareholdings in some SGX-listed entities, and perhaps some private companies. That, together with the prevalence of family companies, means management may focus on profits for the majority shareholder rather than all shareholders: private benefits of control. Conflicts also vary with the company's stage of life, with more fights 'as the pie gets smaller'."},
    {t:"They own every Singapore company", correct:false,
     why:"The State holds shares in some companies, not all. The point is that it has an interest in internal corporate roles, not that it owns the corporate sector."},
    {t:"They are bound to favour minority shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The text says rule changes often favour management and sometimes shareholders. There is no obligation to favour minorities, and private benefits of control are a noted risk."},
    {t:"They are prohibited from amending the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"Regulators drive frequent amendments to the Act. That activity is part of why they are not disinterested."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.010]", takeaway:"Regulators: pro-growth, State as shareholder → not neutral; watch private benefits of control." },

/* ---------- 2B Five features ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-050", lesson:"2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ravi owns all the shares of Ravi Logistics Pte Ltd. To win a large contract, the bank requires him to personally guarantee the company's $3m facility. The company later fails owing the bank $2m.",
  prompt:"Can Ravi rely on limited liability against the bank?",
  options:[
    {t:"No. By guaranteeing the facility, he contractually abrogated the limited liability the corporate framework would otherwise give him for that debt", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the reality in many small private companies entering a large transaction is that the incorporators must give some form of guarantee, 'thus contractually abrogating whatever limited liability may have been created by the corporate framework'. Ravi is liable to the bank under his own contract of guarantee, not because the company's debt is automatically his.\n\nThis is one of the reasons the text says limited liability is not the defining feature of incorporation: it can be contracted for, and just as easily contracted away. Footnote 58 adds Rose's observation that auditors may not sign off a financially suspect subsidiary's accounts, and third parties may not trade with it, without evidence of support from its holding company. Separate personality, by contrast, survives: the company remains a distinct debtor, and Ravi as guarantor has his own rights against it."},
    {t:"Yes. Limited liability always protects shareholders from company debts", correct:false,
     why:"Limited liability protects a shareholder from being made to pay the company's debts merely because he is a shareholder. It does not protect him from his own separate promise to pay, which is what a guarantee is."},
    {t:"Only if the guarantee was given before incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"Timing is irrelevant. A guarantee given at any point is Ravi's own obligation."},
    {t:"Yes, unless the bank can pierce the corporate veil", correct:false,
     why:"The bank does not need to pierce the veil. It sues Ravi on his guarantee. Veil piercing is a separate and much narrower doctrine."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.013], fn 58", takeaway:"Personal guarantees contract away limited liability." },

{ id:"cl2-051", lesson:"2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Nadia holds 10,000 shares of $1 each in a company limited by shares, on which $0.40 per share remains unpaid. The company goes into insolvent liquidation owing far more than its assets.",
  prompt:"What is the most the liquidator can require Nadia to contribute as a member?",
  options:[
    {t:"$4,000, the amount unpaid on her shares, under s 121(1)(d) IRDA", correct:true,
     why:"Section 121 of the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act 2018 (formerly s 250 of the Companies Act) sets the liability of present and past members as contributories. Under s 121(1)(d), for a company limited by shares, no contribution may be required from a member exceeding the amount, if any, unpaid on the shares for which she is liable.\n\nNadia has 10,000 shares with $0.40 unpaid on each, so her maximum contribution is 10,000 × $0.40 = $4,000, however large the deficit. If her shares had been fully paid, her contribution as a member would be nil. This is what limited liability by shares means in practice. In an unlimited company, by contrast, her liability would not be capped, which is why the text says incorporation is 'quite consistent with unlimited liability' if members want it."},
    {t:"$10,000, the nominal value of her shares", correct:false,
     why:"She has already paid $0.60 per share. The cap is the amount still unpaid, not the full value."},
    {t:"Her proportionate share of the whole deficit", correct:false,
     why:"That would be unlimited liability. For a company limited by shares, s 121(1)(d) caps contribution at the unpaid amount."},
    {t:"Nothing, because members are never liable in a liquidation", correct:false,
     why:"Members of a company limited by shares are liable up to any amount unpaid on their shares, and members of unlimited companies can be liable without limit."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.012]", takeaway:"s 121(1)(d) IRDA: contribution capped at amount unpaid on shares." },

{ id:"cl2-052", lesson:"2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A buyer pays $50m for all the shares of a private company, priced at 10 times earnings. After completion it finds that the vendors hid $3m of structural defects in the company's main factory. Earnings are unaffected and no one can say what the shares would have sold for had the defect been known.",
  prompt:"Applying the text's analysis, what is the buyer most likely to recover for breach of the asset warranty?",
  options:[
    {t:"The cost of cure at the company level (about $3m), since a share-price loss is hard to prove when price depends on many factors and on an earnings multiple", correct:true,
     why:"The text explains that the company's bifurcated ownership structure breaks the link between share price and underlying asset value. Shares are usually priced as a multiple of current or future earnings, and a breach may cause a large fall in asset value without much effect on earnings or on the share price. So buyers 'may attempt to claim the cost of cure at the corporate level even though there is a minimal loss in the value of the share itself'.\n\nColumbia Asia is the model. There the court held that the measure for undisclosed charges over the subsidiary's land was the cost of cure, not diminution in share value, and the buyer failed to prove a purchase-price loss because the price rested on many factors, only one of which was EBITDA. Phang JA described valuation as 'multi-faceted and fact-centric'. Conversely, courts may be uncomfortable with claims for share losses priced at a multiple of earnings."},
    {t:"$30m: the $3m defect multiplied by the 10x earnings multiple", correct:false,
     why:"Applying the deal multiple to an asset defect assumes the defect hits earnings and price in a mechanical way. The text says courts may be uncomfortable with such claims, and earnings here are unaffected."},
    {t:"Nothing, because the defect is the company's loss, not the buyer's", correct:false,
     why:"The warranty was given to the buyer, so the buyer has a claim. The question is how to measure it, and cost of cure at the corporate level is the measure the text points to."},
    {t:"Automatic rescission of the share purchase", correct:false,
     why:"Rescission is not the remedy discussed. The text and Columbia Asia concern how to measure damages."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.015]–[02.016]", takeaway:"Asset warranty breach in a share sale → cost of cure; share-price loss is hard to prove." },

{ id:"cl2-053", lesson:"2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shortly before selling his shares, a company's managing director deliberately inflates its reported profits. Investors buy his shares at a high price. The company itself has lost nothing; the profits were never there.",
  prompt:"On Vita Health, what remedy may be available, and how was the case later characterised?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company may recover substantial damages from the MD, reinstating its finances to what he represented; Haneda later treated Vita Health as a breach of warranty case rather than misrepresentation", correct:true,
     why:"This is the transferred loss problem: the loss seems to sit with the buyers, while any cause of action against the director for his conduct belongs to the company. In Vita Health the court let the company recover substantial damages from the MD even though it had not actually suffered loss. Because of his fraud, he was bound to the false picture he had created. Equity 'deemed done that which ought to be done', reinstating the company's finances to what he had said they were. The money then feeds into share prices as investors determine.\n\nThe text suggests this may be fraud unravelling all, so the veil 'never applies in the first place' (Rose). V K Rajah JC's phrase was 'creative accounting may require creative remedies'. But in Haneda Construction Machinery v Huttons Asia the court said Vita Health should be seen as a breach of warranty case rather than a misrepresentation claim, so its reach may be narrower than it first appears."},
    {t:"No remedy, because the company suffered no loss and the buyers have no claim", correct:false,
     why:"That is the orthodox gap Vita Health filled. The court did not allow the fraudulent MD to escape on the ground that the loss fell in the wrong place."},
    {t:"The buyers can recover from the company, because it published the accounts", correct:false,
     why:"Making the company pay would harm its creditors and remaining shareholders. Vita Health ran the remedy the other way: from the wrongdoer to the company."},
    {t:"Only a criminal prosecution", correct:false,
     why:"Vita Health was a civil case about who recovers and how much. Criminal liability is a separate question."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.017], fn 72, 74", takeaway:"Vita Health: company recovers from fraudulent MD; Haneda reads it as a warranty case." },

{ id:"cl2-054", lesson:"2B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A government is privatising a state-owned utility through an IPO. A fund manager asks which structure has historically been better for outside investors.",
  prompt:"Based on the study the text cites, which structure tends to serve investors better?",
  options:[
    {t:"The government keeps a majority of the shares, and new management runs the business as a business", correct:true,
     why:"The text cites the finding that 'investors are generally better off with offerings where (1) the government continues to own a majority of the shares … [and] new management runs the business as a business'. It adds Lim's point that such companies may not seek private benefits of control at minorities' expense, but create 'public' benefits that also help stakeholders, including minority shareholders.\n\nThe text uses this, along with evidence that family-owned firms may do better on return on assets (an NUS study of 692 SGX-listed firms), to make a broader point: we should not adopt dogmatic notions that one form of shareholding structure is better than another. State and family control are common in Singapore and are not inherently bad for outside investors."},
    {t:"The government sells all its shares so the company becomes widely held", correct:false,
     why:"The cited study points the other way: investors did better where the government kept a majority. The text resists the assumption that dispersed ownership is always superior."},
    {t:"The government keeps a majority and continues to run it as a government department", correct:false,
     why:"Retaining a majority helped only when combined with new management running it 'as a business'. Government-style management was not the favourable condition."},
    {t:"Structure makes no difference to investors", correct:false,
     why:"The study did identify a structure that tended to favour investors. The text's caution is against dogma, not a claim that structure never matters."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.019], fn 83–85", takeaway:"SOE IPOs: govt majority + business-minded management tends to serve investors." },

{ id:"cl2-055", lesson:"2B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The text says a share transfer in a private company involves 'a form of novation'. Why?",
  options:[
    {t:"Private companies have share transfer restrictions, so admitting a new member needs the company's approval, much as a new partner needs consent", correct:true,
     why:"New shareholders can subscribe for new shares or buy existing ones. With public companies this looks like a simple transfer. But the text argues that with private companies there is also a form of novation, because private companies have share transfer restrictions: the board (or members) must approve the incoming member, much as all partners must consent to a new partner.\n\nFootnote 65 adds that with public companies the novation may in effect be pre-approved, and that such a concept is possible in law (Mulkerrins v PricewaterhouseCoopers; Ma Hongjin v SCP Holdings). The text's wider point is that shares are reified in a way debentures may not be, yet they can still be difficult to understand."},
    {t:"Because every share transfer creates a new company", correct:false,
     why:"The company stays the same; only its membership changes. Novation here describes a new member stepping into the relationship with the company's approval."},
    {t:"Because shares in private companies cannot be transferred at all", correct:false,
     why:"They can be transferred, but subject to restrictions. That is precisely why the transfer resembles novation."},
    {t:"Because the Partnership Act applies to private companies", correct:false,
     why:"The Partnership Act does not govern companies. The comparison with partnership novation is an analogy."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.015], fn 65", takeaway:"Private co share transfer ≈ novation (transfer restrictions); public co ≈ pre-approved." },

{ id:"cl2-056", lesson:"2B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The text says there is an objective basis for concluding that active institutional investors are preferable to passive ones.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text says institutional investors 'can be passive or active, without any objective basis (as opposed to self-serving arguments) to say which is preferable'. It makes a related point later: some US academics, focusing on an idealised picture of activist hedge funds, think passive funds are a problem because they support management.\n\nThis fits the chapter's refusal to adopt dogmatic views on corporate structure. Dispersed versus concentrated ownership, retail versus institutional, State or family control, and dual class shares all complicate the simple picture of delegated management and investor ownership, and none is declared the best model."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The text expressly says there is no objective basis for preferring one over the other. Claims either way tend to be self-serving."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.018], [02.036]", takeaway:"Active vs passive investors: no objective basis to prefer either." },

/* ---------- 2C External & internal perspectives ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-057", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Daniel owes a personal creditor $500,000. Daniel owns 100% of Daniel Foods Pte Ltd, which owns a factory worth $2m. The creditor applies to seize the factory to satisfy Daniel's debt.",
  prompt:"What is the creditor's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"It cannot seize the factory: entity shielding protects the company's assets from its shareholder's creditors. It can pursue Daniel's own assets, including his shares.", correct:true,
     why:"This is reverse limited liability, or affirmative asset partitioning, which Hansmann and Kraakman called the most crucial feature of the company. The company's assets are insulated from the bankruptcy or insolvency of its shareholders. The factory belongs to Daniel Foods, a separate person, so Daniel's creditor has no claim on it.\n\nWhat Daniel owns is shares. His creditor can enforce against those, for example by obtaining an order over them and, if necessary, having them sold. Indirectly that gives the creditor the economic value of the company after its own creditors, but the company's creditors keep their priority over the factory. That priority is what lets the company use its assets as collateral cheaply. Compare Pek Seng, where a court only weakened this shield, and protected the company's own creditors while doing so."},
    {t:"It can seize the factory because Daniel is the sole shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Sole ownership of the shares does not make the company's assets Daniel's. Even in Pek Seng, where the court treated a sole shareholder as indirectly owning the net assets for Mareva purposes, it let the subsidiary keep paying its own creditors."},
    {t:"It can seize the factory only if the company consents", correct:false,
     why:"The company has no reason or obligation to give up its assets for its shareholder's personal debt, and its directors would breach their duties if they did so to the company's detriment."},
    {t:"It must first pierce the veil, which is available whenever a shareholder owes money", correct:false,
     why:"There is no veil-piercing ground simply because a shareholder owes money. Prest confines piercing to evasion of existing obligations through a company."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.020]–[02.021], [02.027]", takeaway:"Entity shielding: shareholder's creditors reach the shares, not the company's assets." },

{ id:"cl2-058", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The board of Orchid Pte Ltd wants to grant a bank a floating charge over the company's whole undertaking to secure a loan. One shareholder insists the company cannot pledge 'our assets' without asking every shareholder.",
  prompt:"Is the shareholder right?",
  options:[
    {t:"Generally no. The company can borrow and commit its assets as collateral without referring to its shareholders, provided it acts constitutionally and in its best interests", correct:true,
     why:"From the external perspective, the company 'can borrow money from creditors and commit its assets as collateral without referencing its shareholders (although this must be done constitutionally and in the company's best interest)'. The text, following Hansmann and Kraakman, says this ability is perhaps the most crucial characteristic of the corporate form.\n\nBecause the company's fund is ring-fenced from the shareholders and their creditors, lenders' monitoring costs fall and so does the cost of credit, particularly where a floating charge can be granted over the entire undertaking. The shareholder's premise is also wrong: the assets are the company's, not 'ours'. The two qualifications matter, though. If the constitution requires member approval for such a charge, or the directors grant it for an improper purpose, the analysis changes."},
    {t:"Yes. Every shareholder must consent because they own the assets", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders do not own the company's assets. That is the foundational point of Chapter 1, and it is what allows the company to pledge them."},
    {t:"Yes. Floating charges always need a special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"There is no general rule requiring a special resolution to grant a floating charge. The board can usually do it under its management powers, subject to the constitution."},
    {t:"No, and directors may grant it even for their own benefit", correct:false,
     why:"The power must be exercised constitutionally and in the company's best interests. Granting security for the directors' own benefit would breach their duties."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.020]–[02.021]", takeaway:"Company can pledge its assets without shareholders, if done constitutionally and in its interest." },

{ id:"cl2-059", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is entity shielding so hard to create by contract, even though limited liability can be?",
  options:[
    {t:"Giving the firm's creditors priority over the owners' creditors would need agreements with countless third parties; co-owners' agreements not to partition are time-limited and doubtfully bind creditors; and perpetuity rules bar unlimited restraints on alienation", correct:true,
     why:"The text explains that creating limited liability by contract is possible: a business can agree with each of its creditors that they will not pursue the owners. But establishing priority for the firm's creditors over the owners' personal creditors by contract is very difficult because of the number of third parties involved, most of whom never deal with the business.\n\nHansmann and Kraakman add that tenants in common can agree not to partition their property, but such an agreement must be limited in duration and probably would not bind the co-tenants' creditors. The law also prohibits unlimited agreements not to alienate, largely because of the perpetuity rules. So a fund with perpetual succession is something members cannot easily build for themselves, as the difficulties with gifts to unincorporated associations show. This is why the text says the separate entity 'is not something that is easily created contractually'."},
    {t:"Because entity shielding is prohibited by statute", correct:false,
     why:"Entity shielding is not prohibited; it is provided by company law. The point is that private parties cannot easily build it themselves."},
    {t:"Because creditors always refuse to lend to companies", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors lend to companies all the time, partly because entity shielding protects the fund they lend against."},
    {t:"Because limited liability and entity shielding are the same thing", correct:false,
     why:"They are different. Limited liability (owner shielding) protects owners from the firm's creditors; entity shielding protects the firm from the owners' creditors."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.021]", takeaway:"Entity shielding needs the State: contract can't bind all third parties or beat perpetuity rules." },

{ id:"cl2-060", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two professionals form a Singapore LLP. One later proposes running it with a single partner as a convenient asset-holding vehicle, and granting a bank a floating charge over all its assets, as a company would.",
  prompt:"Which points from the text tell against this plan?",
  options:[
    {t:"An LLP must have two or more partners carrying on a business, showing it is not meant as an asset protection device; and its charges are still governed by the Bills of Sale Act 1886, which complicates a full floating charge", correct:true,
     why:"The text uses the LLP to illustrate Hansmann, Kraakman and Squire's warning that entity shielding is more open to abuse as more vehicles receive separate-entity status. Singapore gave the LLP separate personality (s 4 LLP Act). But there may be difficulties in an LLP fully granting a floating charge over its assets, because its charge registration is still governed by the Bills of Sale Act 1886, and some argue the LLP is more of a 'hybrid' association.\n\nFurther, the continued partnership requirement that an LLP have two or more partners carrying on a business indicates it is not intended as an asset protection device. A one-member asset-holding LLP runs against both points. If the aim is a single-owner asset vehicle with a clean floating charge, a company is the more natural choice. Chapter 3 returns to the LLP in detail."},
    {t:"None: an LLP is identical to a company for these purposes", correct:false,
     why:"An LLP has separate personality, but the text highlights two differences here: the two-partner business requirement and the Bills of Sale Act regime for its charges."},
    {t:"Only that LLPs cannot own property", correct:false,
     why:"As a body corporate with separate personality, an LLP can own property. The concerns are about its purpose and its charges."},
    {t:"Only that LLP partners have unlimited liability", correct:false,
     why:"LLP partners generally have limited liability; that is the point of the vehicle. The text's concerns here lie elsewhere."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.022], fn 95–97", takeaway:"LLP: 2+ partners in business (not an asset shield); charges under Bills of Sale Act." },

{ id:"cl2-061", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Tom sells his business and promises the buyer not to compete for two years. A month later he sets up a new company, run by him, which starts competing for the same customers.",
  prompt:"On Lord Sumption's analysis in Prest, is this a case for piercing the veil?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, in principle: Tom is under an existing obligation and is deliberately evading it by interposing a company he controls, which is the evasion principle", correct:true,
     why:"Lord Sumption said true veil piercing applies 'when a person is under an existing legal obligation or liability or subject to an existing legal restriction which he deliberately evades or whose enforcement he deliberately frustrates by interposing a company under his control'. Tom's restraint existed before the company did, and the company is the device he uses to escape it.\n\nThe text says this second avenue is 'more concrete', directed at people trying to protect assets from their own creditors or escape a liability pressing on them (fn 105 cites Gilford Motor v Horne and Jones v Lipman, the classic examples). It concerns separate personality and entity shielding. Contrast a company that simply incurs debts after incorporation: there the controller's estate was never committed, and there is no piercing."},
    {t:"No: a company's acts are never attributed to its controller", correct:false,
     why:"The evasion principle exists precisely for this case. Separate personality is not allowed to be used to defeat an obligation the controller already owed."},
    {t:"No, because the buyer's contract was with Tom, not the company", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly why piercing is needed: the obligation was Tom's, and he is using the company to evade it."},
    {t:"Yes, because any competing business by a former owner justifies piercing", correct:false,
     why:"It is the existing obligation and its deliberate evasion that matter. Competing through a company without any prior restraint would not justify piercing."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.024]–[02.025], fn 105", takeaway:"Evasion principle: pre-existing obligation + company used to dodge it." },

{ id:"cl2-062", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In the UK after Prest and VTB Capital, how should 'concealment' cases (where the corporate structure hides who is really responsible) usually be handled?",
  options:[
    {t:"By working harder with existing causes of action, such as a direct duty of care (Vedanta, Okpabi) or agency, rather than by piercing the veil", correct:true,
     why:"The UK Supreme Court in Prest and VTB confirmed the unhelpfulness of labels like 'façade', 'sham' and 'alter ego'. In these cases, the concealment that comes with the corporate structure can be dealt with by working harder with existing causes of action: a direct duty of care owed by a parent company (Lungowe v Vedanta; Okpabi v Royal Dutch Shell, fn 104) or agency.\n\nThe other avenue, evasion, is where the veil is actually pierced. VTB also held that the fraud 'cannot be in a vacuum' but must relate to improper use of the corporate shell. Footnote 119 notes that in Prest Lord Neuberger appeared to link 'lifting' with concealment and 'piercing' with evasion, with only the latter interfering with separate personality."},
    {t:"By always piercing the veil, because concealment is a form of fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Concealment cases are generally handled without piercing. Piercing is reserved for evasion of existing obligations."},
    {t:"By treating the group as a single economic entity", correct:false,
     why:"Single economic entity reasoning is not the UK route. Each company remains separate, and responsibility is found through ordinary causes of action."},
    {t:"By refusing any remedy", correct:false,
     why:"Remedies are available, through negligence (direct duty of care) or agency, without disturbing separate personality."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.024], [02.027], fn 104, 107, 119", takeaway:"Concealment → ordinary causes of action; evasion → piercing." },

{ id:"cl2-063", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Landlords grant leases of empty buildings to shell SPVs to shift liability for business rates. One SPV is struck off, so its property vests in the Crown as bona vacantia. Another goes into voluntary winding up to claim a rates exemption. The councils ask the court to pierce the SPVs' veil and hold the landlords liable.",
  prompt:"How did the UK Supreme Court approach this in Hurstwood Properties v Rossendale BC?",
  options:[
    {t:"It refused to pierce the veil, confirming piercing is narrow, but held there was a triable issue under the rating legislation whether the landlords remained liable because ownership had not really shifted", correct:true,
     why:"The councils wanted to remove the landlords' limited liability for the SPVs' debts. The Supreme Court confirmed that veil piercing in the UK is very narrow: it is about making a company liable for its controllers' fault (removing entity shielding), not the reverse (removing the controllers' limited liability for the company's debts). Relying on Lord Sumption in Prest, it refused to pierce.\n\nBut as a matter of statutory interpretation of the rating legislation, it held there was a triable issue whether the landlords remained liable for business rates throughout the leases, because the ownership of the property had not in fact shifted to the SPV tenants. The text uses Hurstwood to show the difference between entity shielding and owner shielding, while noting that Singapore's piercing doctrine is wider (Chapter 6)."},
    {t:"It pierced the veil because the schemes were artificial", correct:false,
     why:"The court refused to pierce. Artificiality alone does not meet the narrow evasion test."},
    {t:"It held that the Crown was liable for all the rates", correct:false,
     why:"The bona vacantia point was how one scheme tried to shift liability, not the court's holding. The court found a triable issue as to the landlords' own liability under the statute."},
    {t:"It held the schemes were entirely lawful and dismissed the claims outright", correct:false,
     why:"The councils' claims were not simply dismissed. The statutory interpretation route left the landlords potentially liable."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.026], fn 112", takeaway:"Hurstwood: no piercing; solve it through the statute (who is really the owner?)." },

{ id:"cl2-064", lesson:"2C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A claimant has a strong fraud claim against HoldCo and fears its assets will be dissipated. HoldCo's only significant asset is its wholly owned subsidiary, an operating company with employees and trade creditors. The claimant wants a Mareva injunction freezing all of the subsidiary's bank accounts.",
  prompt:"Following Pek Seng v Low Tin Kee, what order is the court most likely to make?",
  options:[
    {t:"An injunction reaching the subsidiary's assets, but allowing it to carry on business and pay ordinary debts and expenses in the normal course", correct:true,
     why:"In Pek Seng, Chan J accepted that it would 'fly in the face of reality and common sense' to say that the sole beneficial owner of a company's shares is not indirectly the owner of its net assets, for the purposes of a Mareva injunction. So the injunction can reach the subsidiary's assets.\n\nBut fully treating the subsidiary's assets as the parent's would prejudice the subsidiary's own creditors, who rely on its separate fund. So the injunction was granted on the basis that the subsidiary could carry on business and pay ordinary debts and expenses in the normal course. Applied here, the subsidiary should still be able to pay salaries and trade creditors. The text describes this as weakening entity shielding without removing the bifurcated ownership structure or fully piercing the veil."},
    {t:"A total freeze, including payroll and trade creditors", correct:false,
     why:"That would in effect merge the subsidiary's assets with the parent's and prejudice the subsidiary's own creditors, which Chan J was careful to avoid."},
    {t:"No order, because the subsidiary is a separate person", correct:false,
     why:"Chan J did extend the injunction to the subsidiary's assets. Separate personality was respected through the ordinary-course carve-out, not by refusing relief."},
    {t:"An order transferring the subsidiary's assets to HoldCo", correct:false,
     why:"No transfer was ordered. A Mareva injunction preserves assets; it does not move them, and the subsidiary's assets were not treated as the parent's."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.027]", takeaway:"Mareva over a subsidiary: allowed, with an ordinary-course carve-out." },

{ id:"cl2-065", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the legal nature of SGX listing rules, according to the text?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not statutory as such but an agreement between the exchange and listed companies; the exchange's exercise of power under them is public or administrative and may have statutory backing", correct:true,
     why:"The text says that although listing rules 'are not statutory as such, and are really the subject of an agreement between the securities exchange and listed companies', the exchange's exercise of power under them is public or administrative in nature and may be backed by statutory enforceability. Footnote 131 adds that disclosure rules are statutory through s 203 of the SFA (continuous disclosure), while other listing rules come under ss 25 and 325 SFA.\n\nListing rules standardise listed companies' internal rules even further, for example by mandating disclosure, and the exchange frowns on constitutions that stray from the norm. That is part of why Hansmann says the contractual nature of public companies is more theoretical than real."},
    {t:"Subsidiary legislation made under the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"Listing rules are not regulations under the Companies Act. Their force comes from the exchange's agreement with issuers, backed in places by the SFA."},
    {t:"Purely private rules with no public element", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the exchange's exercise of power under them is public or administrative in nature, and some rules have statutory force."},
    {t:"Part of the company's constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Listing rules are separate from the constitution, though they shape what a listed company's constitution can contain."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.031], fn 131", takeaway:"Listing rules: contractual base, public exercise of power, some statutory backing." },

{ id:"cl2-066", lesson:"2C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the text say contractarian theory cannot fully explain the public company without some regulatory intervention?",
  options:[
    {t:"Agency costs and collective action problems among many outside shareholders; and incorporation, while reducing hold-out costs, creates agency costs, some of which are shifted to third parties as information costs", correct:true,
     why:"The text says it is partly because of agency costs in public companies with many outside shareholders, and the consequent collective action problems, that contractarian theory has not been able to explain the corporation fully without some regulatory intervention.\n\nThe contractarian rejoinder is that the market would have produced the same rules, because it too wants to minimise agency costs. But costs must be balanced. Incorporation may reduce hold-out costs among parties who would otherwise have to contract, but it creates agency costs. Some are not internalised and are shifted to third parties as information costs. Standardising the company's internal rules lowers those costs, which can be substantial for large listed companies dealing with many outsiders."},
    {t:"Because contracts between shareholders are illegal", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholder contracts are lawful and common, especially in small companies. The problem in public companies is collective action and agency costs."},
    {t:"Because public companies have no constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Every company has a constitution. Public companies' constitutions are simply highly standardised."},
    {t:"Because regulators always know better than markets", correct:false,
     why:"The text does not make that claim. It explains a specific market failure, not a general preference for regulation."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.031]", takeaway:"Public cos: collective action + agency costs → some regulation; standardisation cuts information costs." },

/* ---------- 2D Internal conflicts ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-067", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company is charged with an offence that requires proof of intent. The prosecution must show the company 'intended' the act.",
  prompt:"How does the text say the law approaches this?",
  options:[
    {t:"It must identify a constituency within the company, such as the board or a particular officer, whose intent and act are attributed to the company, and that choice depends on context", correct:true,
     why:"The complication with companies is that sometimes liability needs the necessary intent (or negligence, or intention to create legal relations) as well as the act. Unlike with a human, the law must pinpoint a constituency within the company, such as the shareholders in general meeting or the board, whose intent or act is attributed to it. That is how the company establishes legal relationships, sometimes involuntarily, with the rest of the world.\n\nThe text stresses that one 'cannot be dogmatic' about who is the company for a given purpose; the decision is context-specific. It may turn on 'the allocation and delegation of the company's own power to act'. Footnote 135 cites Singularis v Daiwa: whether an agent's knowledge is attributed depends on the context and purpose, even for a one-person company. This is identification, not vicarious liability."},
    {t:"Companies can never commit offences requiring intent", correct:false,
     why:"Companies can be liable for such offences; attribution rules are how intent is found. The text treats this as a problem to solve, not a bar."},
    {t:"The company is always vicariously liable for any employee's intent", correct:false,
     why:"Attribution (identification) is different from vicarious liability. Whose intent counts depends on the context and on the company's allocation of power."},
    {t:"Only the shareholders' intent can ever be attributed", correct:false,
     why:"The board, the general meeting or a particular officer may be the relevant constituency, depending on the rule and the transaction."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.032]–[02.033], fn 135–136", takeaway:"Attribution: find the right organ/officer; context-specific (Singularis)." },

{ id:"cl2-068", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At the AGM of a Singapore-listed company with a 60% controlling shareholder, minority investors want a binding annual vote on the CEO's pay package, as in the UK.",
  prompt:"Which statement best reflects the text's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Singapore shareholders vote on directors' fees under s 169 but have had little say on executive pay; with concentrated ownership, management is likely accountable to the controller on pay, though outside shareholders may be unhappy", correct:true,
     why:"The text contrasts Singapore with the UK. In Singapore, shareholders have had little say on executive pay, as opposed to directors' fees and emoluments, which s 169 makes subject to shareholder approval. The UK has an annual advisory vote on the remuneration report (s 439) and, since the Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013, a binding vote on remuneration policy once every three years.\n\nBut given the less dispersed shareholdings here, management is likely to be accountable to controlling shareholders on pay even without a vote. The minority's concern is that the controller's view of pay may not match theirs. The text draws a wider lesson: agency cost analysis can focus too much on the management-shareholder conflict, and sometimes we may need to increase director oversight to account for minority or external interests."},
    {t:"Singapore law already requires a binding annual vote on CEO pay", correct:false,
     why:"It does not. Section 169 concerns directors' fees and emoluments. The binding policy vote is a UK feature (every three years, not annually)."},
    {t:"The UK requires a binding annual vote on all pay", correct:false,
     why:"The UK's annual vote is advisory. The binding vote is on remuneration policy, at least every three years."},
    {t:"Pay is irrelevant to agency costs", correct:false,
     why:"Bebchuk and Fried's work on pay is one of the text's main examples of agency costs: managers using board control to raise pay and decouple it from performance."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.035]", takeaway:"SG: s 169 fees vote, little say on executive pay; controllers discipline pay." },

{ id:"cl2-069", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company is near insolvency. Its shareholder-directors propose using the last $5m of cash for a highly speculative venture: if it works, the shareholders recover something; if it fails, the company's creditors bear the loss.",
  prompt:"Which analysis from the text applies?",
  options:[
    {t:"Near insolvency the remaining value in a sense belongs to the creditors and shareholders have nothing to lose; directors' duty is to the company, with creditors' interests coming to the fore", correct:true,
     why:"The text describes exactly this risk. In companies 'in the vicinity of insolvency', shareholders may try to grab what remains or take enormous gambles in the hope of retrieving the situation, because they have nothing to lose. Any further losses to the diminished fund are borne by creditors, who would rank ahead of shareholders in an insolvency.\n\nInsolvency law reduces this opportunism, and company law helps indirectly because the director's duty is owed to the company, not to the shareholders. That duty is not owed directly to creditors (Progen), but creditors' interests 'came to the fore as the company was effectively running the company's business with the creditors' money'. Directors who approve the gamble mainly for the shareholders' upside, ignoring the creditors who bear the downside, are at real risk of breaching their duty to the company."},
    {t:"Directors must follow the shareholders' wishes, since they own the company", correct:false,
     why:"Directors owe their duty to the company, and near insolvency creditors' interests come to the fore. Shareholder wishes do not override that."},
    {t:"Directors owe a direct duty to each creditor and must get their consent", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore law rejects a direct duty to creditors (W P Piling, Progen). Creditors' interests are considered within the duty owed to the company."},
    {t:"Nothing applies until a winding-up order is made", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors' interests come to the fore in the vicinity of insolvency, before any formal order, and Dynasty Line says strict insolvency tests are of limited use here."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.040], [02.042]", takeaway:"Near insolvency: shareholders gamble with creditors' money; duty to company, creditors to the fore." },

{ id:"cl2-070", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director of an insolvent company uses its remaining cash to pay one trade creditor, an old friend, in full, leaving other creditors with nothing.",
  prompt:"Apart from any unfair preference claim under insolvency law, what does the text say about this?",
  options:[
    {t:"A director cannot prefer one creditor over another; that duty is separate from the unfair preference rules of formal insolvency law", correct:true,
     why:"The text warns that stating duties as owed 'to creditors' risks ignoring two complications. Other constituencies remain interested in the company even after insolvency, such as employees. And there may be conflicts among creditors themselves.\n\nOn the second, it says 'a director cannot prefer one creditor over another, which is something separate from the unfair preference rules provided by formal insolvency law' (fn 177, Moulin Global Eyecare v Olivia Lee, Hong Kong Court of Final Appeal). So the director may be in breach of duty to the company, quite apart from any liquidator's claim to unwind the payment as a preference."},
    {t:"Nothing: paying a genuine debt can never be a breach of duty", correct:false,
     why:"Paying a genuine debt can still breach duty when the company is insolvent and the payment prefers one creditor over others. The text treats this as a duty point distinct from insolvency avoidance."},
    {t:"Only the unfair preference rules can apply", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the duty not to prefer creditors is something separate from the unfair preference rules. Both may be in play."},
    {t:"The director owes a direct duty to the unpaid creditors to pay them personally", correct:false,
     why:"No direct duty to creditors exists in Singapore. The duty is owed to the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.042], fn 177", takeaway:"Director can't prefer one creditor: a duty separate from unfair preference rules." },

{ id:"cl2-071", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What risk does the text see in Chip Thye Enterprises' language of a duty owed to 'present and future creditors'?",
  options:[
    {t:"It may fail to recognise that other constituencies, such as employees, remain interested in the company after insolvency, and that creditors themselves may conflict", correct:true,
     why:"In Chip Thye v Phay Gi Mo the court said the law recognises a duty to present and future creditors where the company is insolvent or 'put in a situation where its creditors will be prejudiced'. The text's concern with stating so clearly that there are duties to creditors (as opposed to taking their interests into account, as s 172(3) of the UK Act does) is that it may overlook other constituencies still interested in the company despite insolvency, like employees.\n\nThere may also be conflicts among creditors. Later decisions (W P Piling, then the Court of Appeal in Progen) confirmed that directors owe no direct duty to creditors even in insolvency, while creditors' interests come to the fore. Because the language is no longer one of duty to creditors, courts have been more flexible about when insolvency arises (Dynasty Line)."},
    {t:"That it gave creditors too little protection", correct:false,
     why:"The concern is the opposite: framing the duty as owed to creditors can crowd out other constituencies and hide conflicts among creditors."},
    {t:"That it applied only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Chip Thye was not limited to listed companies, and the text's criticism is about who the duty is framed around."},
    {t:"That it was overruled by statute", correct:false,
     why:"The development came from later cases (W P Piling, Progen), not a statute."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.042], fn 174–180", takeaway:"'Duty to creditors' language risks ignoring employees and inter-creditor conflict." },

{ id:"cl2-072", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A US-style 'poison pill' is proposed by the board of a Singapore-listed company to block an unwanted takeover bid without shareholders' approval.",
  prompt:"What does the text's comparative discussion suggest about how this would be seen in Singapore?",
  options:[
    {t:"Defences US boards may adopt would in Singapore be seen as removing shareholders' right to choose whether to tender their shares; UK-style systems make shareholders more central than in the US", correct:true,
     why:"The text notes that US laws generally protect shareholders more than creditors (seen in Chapter 11 debtor-in-possession bankruptcy). Yet it has been said that shareholders in the UK and jurisdictions following it are 'far more powerful and far more central' than US shareholders (Bruner).\n\nThis is especially so in takeovers, where US boards are allowed to create defences 'which would in Singapore be seen as removing the right of shareholders to choose whether to tender their shares to the bidder'. Singapore's Take-over Code embodies that shareholder choice through its rule against frustrating action. So a board-imposed poison pill would cut against the Singapore approach."},
    {t:"It would be routine and lawful, as in Delaware", correct:false,
     why:"The text says the opposite: such defences would be seen in Singapore as removing shareholders' right to decide on the bid."},
    {t:"It would be lawful because directors owe duties to the company, not shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Directors' duty to the company does not let them take away shareholders' right to decide whether to sell their own shares. The text treats that right as central in Singapore."},
    {t:"It would require creditor approval", correct:false,
     why:"Creditor approval is not the issue. The comparison is about board power versus shareholder choice in takeovers."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.043], fn 186–187", takeaway:"Singapore: shareholders decide on bids; US-style board defences cut against this." },

{ id:"cl2-073", lesson:"2D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which comparative ranking does the text report on investor protection in Asia?",
  options:[
    {t:"On shareholder rights, Singapore, Hong Kong and Malaysia rank above Taiwan, Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea; on creditor protection, Indonesia ranks level with Hong Kong, Singapore and Malaysia", correct:true,
     why:"The text starts from the claim (La Porta et al) that common law countries usually give investors the strongest legal protection, then says the picture is more nuanced because the 'investor class' contains conflicting interests, and jurisdictions prioritise them differently, especially at different stages of a company's life.\n\nOn shareholder rights, Singapore, Hong Kong and Malaysia were ranked higher than Taiwan, Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea. On creditor protection, Indonesia was ranked at the same level as Hong Kong, Singapore and Malaysia, and higher than Thailand, South Korea and Taiwan. The text adds that Singapore's balance may have shifted with the 2017 introduction of US Chapter 11-style restructuring provisions, and that regulators watched the balance closely during COVID-19 (for example, suspending wrongful trading rules)."},
    {t:"Singapore ranks lowest on both shareholder and creditor protection", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore ranks among the higher jurisdictions on both measures reported."},
    {t:"Indonesia ranks highest on shareholder rights", correct:false,
     why:"Indonesia was ranked lower on shareholder rights; it was on creditor protection that it ranked level with Singapore, Hong Kong and Malaysia."},
    {t:"All Asian jurisdictions rank identically", correct:false,
     why:"The rankings differ, which is the text's point: jurisdictions prioritise constituents differently."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.043], fn 183–189", takeaway:"SG/HK/MY high on shareholder rights; Indonesia level with them on creditor protection." },

{ id:"cl2-074", lesson:"2D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During a takeover offer, some shareholders want to sell at the bid price now, while long-term investors want to hold. The directors must decide what to recommend and how to act.",
  prompt:"On the text's analysis, what must the directors balance, and to whom is the duty owed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Acting constitutionally and for proper purposes, they balance the interests of existing shareholders who wish to sell against longer-term holders; the duty remains owed to the company, with no actual duty to future shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"The text says the present law really only deals with the time horizons of existing shareholders. What directors must do, in fulfilling the duty to act constitutionally and for proper purposes, is 'balance the interests of existing shareholders who wish to sell and those longer-term investors who intend to hold on to their shares', the latter being in a sense the 'preferred' shareholder today.\n\nSome cases mention future shareholders (Dawson), but that falls short of any actual duty to them. The text approves of the missing language of duty to other constituents, because it reflects that the company is a separate entity and most disputes, even among its internal constituents, should be resolved through it. The best protection for inter-generational equity may be a board fully empowered to mediate between present and future."},
    {t:"They must recommend whatever maximises the immediate share price", correct:false,
     why:"That is the 'extreme version' of shareholder value. The text says directors balance sellers and long-term holders through the proper purpose duty."},
    {t:"They owe a separate duty to future shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The text says there is no actual duty to future shareholders; references to them in the cases stop short of that."},
    {t:"They owe a duty directly to the bidder", correct:false,
     why:"Directors owe no duty to the bidder. Conflicts with potential bidders are among those the present law does not frame in duty terms."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.044]–[02.045]", takeaway:"Takeover: balance sellers vs long-term holders via proper purposes; duty stays with the company." },

/* ---------- 2E Resolving conflicts through the company ---------- */
{ id:"cl2-075", lesson:"2E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The managing director of Juniper Pte Ltd, who controls the board and 70% of the votes, has taken $2m from the company. A 15% minority shareholder wants the money returned to the company.",
  prompt:"What is the correct starting analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"The proper plaintiff is the company (Foss v Harbottle); since the wrongdoer controls it, the minority should seek leave for a derivative action under s 216A rather than sue in her own name", correct:true,
     why:"Under the first limb of Foss v Harbottle (Edwards v Halliwell), the proper plaintiff for a wrong done to the company is prima facie the company. Taking the company's money is a wrong to the company, so the right of action 'reposes' in it. The minority cannot simply sue for herself, and the reflective loss principle would block a personal claim for her share of the loss.\n\nThe practical problem is that the wrongdoer controls the board and the votes, so the company will not sue. That is what the statutory derivative action in s 216A is for: with leave, a complainant brings the company's claim on its behalf, and the recovery goes to the company. Misappropriation of this kind is also not something the majority can simply ratify away, particularly if it harms creditors or offends capital maintenance."},
    {t:"The minority can sue the MD personally for 15% of $2m", correct:false,
     why:"That would be a claim for reflective loss, which a shareholder generally cannot bring. The loss is the company's, and the remedy belongs to the company."},
    {t:"Nothing can be done because the majority supports the MD", correct:false,
     why:"The derivative action exists for exactly this situation, where wrongdoers in control prevent the company from suing."},
    {t:"Only a creditor can sue for the company's loss", correct:false,
     why:"The company is the proper plaintiff, and a shareholder can pursue the claim derivatively under s 216A. Creditors are not given that role."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.008], [02.046]", takeaway:"Wrong to the company: company sues; if wrongdoers control it, s 216A derivative action." },

{ id:"cl2-076", lesson:"2E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The shareholders of a company that is already insolvent, who are also its directors, pass a resolution ratifying a large payment the company made to them, which was in substance a return of capital.",
  prompt:"Is the ratification effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"No. Shareholders generally decide on ratification, but they cannot ratify what amounts to a fraud on creditors or offends capital maintenance rules", correct:true,
     why:"For ratification 'it is the shareholders who decide, except if this amounts to a fraud on creditors or if it offends capital maintenance rules' (Sequana, especially Lady Arden; Raffles Town Club v Lim Eng Hock Peter).\n\nThis payment fails on both counts. The company is insolvent, so the remaining value in a sense belongs to the creditors, and the shareholders are trying to bless a transfer of that value to themselves. And a disguised return of capital to shareholders is exactly what capital maintenance rules (Part IV) are designed to stop. The fact that the same people are both directors and shareholders makes the point sharper: they cannot use their votes to excuse their own wrong at the creditors' expense."},
    {t:"Yes. Shareholders can ratify anything done by directors", correct:false,
     why:"Ratification has limits. Fraud on creditors and breaches of capital maintenance are beyond the shareholders' power to forgive."},
    {t:"Yes, if passed by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"A higher majority does not cure the problem. The limits protect creditors, who have no vote in the general meeting."},
    {t:"Only the court can ratify, never the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders are the usual ratifying body. The issue is the limits on their power, not who ratifies in ordinary cases."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.040], [02.046], fn 205", takeaway:"No ratification of fraud on creditors or capital maintenance breaches." },

{ id:"cl2-077", lesson:"2E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Alpha Holdings wholly owns Beta Pte Ltd. A director of both companies diverts Beta's assets. Alpha's board, which he controls, will not cause Alpha to make Beta sue. A minority shareholder of Alpha wants to act.",
  prompt:"What does the text suggest about the minority's options?",
  options:[
    {t:"A multiple derivative action may be possible, at common law or under s 216A, whose definition of 'complainant' includes any other person the court considers a proper person", correct:true,
     why:"This is the double or multiple derivative action problem. The wrong is done to Beta, so Beta is the proper plaintiff. Beta's shareholder is Alpha, whose 'genuine will' is controlled by the wrongdoer. The minority is a member of Alpha, not of Beta, so a simple first-order derivative action on Beta's behalf does not obviously fit.\n\nThe text says Singapore and the UK have legislated for the first-order single derivative action, but that may not rule out the multiple derivative action, either at common law or under s 216A itself, because 'complainant' includes 'any other person who, in the discretion of the Court, is a proper person to make an application'. So the minority could ask the court to treat it as a proper person to bring Beta's claim. The UK court in Fort Gilkicker referred to the Singapore position."},
    {t:"No remedy: only Beta's own members can ever bring a derivative claim", correct:false,
     why:"That reading ignores the 'proper person' limb of s 216A and the common law multiple derivative action the text says may survive."},
    {t:"The minority can sue in its own name for the fall in Alpha's share value", correct:false,
     why:"That would be a claim for reflective loss, two levels removed from the wrong. The text's route is a derivative claim on the wronged company's behalf."},
    {t:"Alpha's creditors must bring the claim", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors are not the route. The question is which shareholder or other proper person can enforce Beta's claim."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.046], fn 206–210", takeaway:"Wrong to a subsidiary: multiple derivative action via common law or s 216A 'proper person'." },

{ id:"cl2-078", lesson:"2E", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A breach of directors' duty automatically amounts to oppression under s 216.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text says evidence of a breach of directors' duty that adversely affects minority shareholders can be used as evidence of oppression, so the two are not mutually exclusive. But footnote 212 (Chew) notes that the converse is also not true: a breach of a director's duty does not by itself constitute oppression.\n\nThe reason is that they are different wrongs. A breach of duty is a wrong to the company, enforced by the company or derivatively. Oppression is a personal wrong to members, concerned with unfair prejudice to their interests or expectations. The Court of Appeal in Ng Kek Wee and Ho Yew Kong stressed distinguishing company and personal wrongs. Much depends on the nature of the claim and the relief sought."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The text, citing Chew, says a breach of duty does not automatically constitute oppression. The breach may be evidence of oppression, but s 216 asks a different question."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.047], fn 212", takeaway:"Duty breach ≠ oppression automatically; it may only be evidence of it." },

{ id:"cl2-079", lesson:"2E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why did the UK consider strengthening the 'entity focus' of directors' duties after Kraft's takeover of Cadbury?",
  options:[
    {t:"It was felt that shareholders may have taken too short-term a view in accepting the bid, so UK regulators looked at making bids harder and letting directors weigh interests beyond short-term shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"After Kraft Foods' successful takeover of Cadbury, it was felt that Cadbury's shareholders might have taken too short-term a view of the company in accepting the bid. The UK therefore considered whether to strengthen the entity focus even further in the takeover context. Footnote 200 says UK regulators looked at making it harder for bidders to succeed, and at allowing directors to take into account interests other than those of shareholders, or at least short-term shareholders.\n\nThe text reads s 172's 'members as a whole' as a proxy for long-term shareholders. Its own view is that even at a takeover, when shareholder interests are especially visible, the director's duty remains attached to the entity, though present shareholders as a whole are their main concern."},
    {t:"Because Cadbury's directors were found to have breached their duties", correct:false,
     why:"The text does not say Cadbury's directors breached any duty. The concern was about shareholders' short-termism in accepting the bid."},
    {t:"Because Kraft was a foreign company", correct:false,
     why:"The text frames the concern around shareholder short-termism, not the bidder's nationality."},
    {t:"To give creditors a veto over takeovers", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors were not the focus. The debate was about short-term versus long-term shareholders and the entity."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 2 [02.046], fn 200", takeaway:"Kraft/Cadbury: short-termism concern → stronger entity focus in UK takeovers considered." }

]);

