window.DC_BANK.companyLaw = (window.DC_BANK.companyLaw||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 3A Why VCCs & 'uncorporation' ---------- */
{ id:"cl3-001", lesson:"3A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the chapter not treat the variable capital company (VCC) as a true rival to the company?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is a 'company-minus' regime built specifically for investment funds, applying the Companies Act subject to the VCCA's modifications", correct:true,
     why:"The chapter is interested in business associations that are true rivals to the company. VCCs, formed since January 2020 under the Variable Capital Companies Act 2018, are instead a 'company-minus' regime enacted for certain investment funds, somewhat like the UK's open-ended investment company.\n\nStructurally the VCCA sits on top of the Companies Act. Section 5 applies the Companies Act subject to the VCCA's modifications, and s 6 deems Companies Act provisions incorporated except where the VCCA has a 'corresponding provision'. Section 29 allows segregation between sub-funds in a cellular structure, with each sub-fund capable of being wound up as if it were a separate legal person. Because the VCC is a cut-down company, the chapter focuses on the genuinely different vehicles: general partnerships, LLPs, LPs and business trusts."},
    {t:"It has no separate legal personality", correct:false,
     why:"A VCC is a body corporate with separate personality, which is why it is 'company-minus' rather than something else. Its sub-funds can even be treated as if they were separate legal persons for winding up (s 29)."},
    {t:"It is a form of general partnership", correct:false,
     why:"The VCC is a corporate vehicle governed largely by the Companies Act as modified. It has nothing in common with the aggregate, unlimited-liability general partnership."},
    {t:"It can only be used by sole proprietors", correct:false,
     why:"The VCC exists for collective investment funds, usually open-ended, with many investors. That is the opposite of a one-person business."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.001]", takeaway:"VCC = 'company-minus' for investment funds, not a true rival." },

{ id:"cl3-002", lesson:"3A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under s 35 VCCA, what must a VCC satisfy to repurchase or redeem its shares?",
  options:[
    {t:"Act in accordance with its constitution, with the shares fully paid up and cancelled on repurchase or redemption", correct:true,
     why:"Section 35 VCCA is a 'corresponding provision' that displaces the Companies Act rules. It enables repurchases and redemptions in accordance with the VCC's constitution, with the only requirement that the shares be fully paid and cancelled on repurchase or redemption. On its face the only other limit is that the transaction must not be a voluntary conveyance with intent to defraud creditors, which is voidable under s 438 IRDA.\n\nContrast an ordinary company. Sections 76B–76G of the Companies Act require both shareholder approval and a solvency statement before a company can buy back its shares, and some repurchased shares can be kept as treasury shares instead of being cancelled. The lighter VCC rule makes sense because an open-ended fund must be able to redeem investors routinely."},
    {t:"Shareholder approval and a solvency statement, as under ss 76B–76G CA", correct:false,
     why:"Those are the Companies Act buy-back requirements that s 35 VCCA displaces. Requiring them for every redemption would make an open-ended fund unworkable."},
    {t:"Court approval of every redemption", correct:false,
     why:"No court approval is involved. Redemptions follow the VCC's constitution, subject only to the fully paid and cancellation requirements and the general rule against defrauding creditors."},
    {t:"The shares must be held as treasury shares", correct:false,
     why:"It is the reverse: VCC shares must be cancelled on repurchase or redemption. Treasury shares are a Companies Act concept available to ordinary companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.001]–[03.002]", takeaway:"VCC buyback: constitution + fully paid + cancelled. No CA solvency statement." },

{ id:"cl3-003", lesson:"3A", type:"fill",
  prompt:"As a general rule, shares in a Singapore VCC that are issued, redeemed or repurchased must be transacted at ____ value.",
  accept:["net asset","nav","net asset value"], answer:"net asset",
  why:"The raison d'être of the Singapore VCC is to operate as an investment fund, which is usually open-ended, so its shares are continuously issued and redeemed. As a general rule, those transactions must be at the net asset value of the shares. That keeps incoming, outgoing and remaining investors fair to each other: no one buys in cheaply or exits at an inflated price at others' expense.\n\nThe exceptions are closed-end funds listed on the Singapore Exchange, whose shares trade at market prices, and transactions during an 'initial offer period'. The text says the VCC answers the call for more firm-specific corporate law rules.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.002]", takeaway:"VCC shares move at NAV, except listed closed-end funds and initial offer periods." },

{ id:"cl3-004", lesson:"3A", type:"order",
  prompt:"Put these developments in chronological order.",
  steps:[
    "Sing Property Trust, Singapore's first attempted REIT, fails (2002)",
    "Business Trusts Act comes into force (October 2004)",
    "Limited Liability Partnerships Act passed (2005)",
    "Limited Partnerships Act comes into effect (May 2009)",
    "Companies can be formed under the VCCA (January 2020)",
    "Business Trusts (Amendment) Act (2022)"
  ],
  why:"Singapore's first attempted REIT, Sing Property Trust, failed in 2002; its trust deed had tried to give the trust artificial entity status by copying the company's property structure. The Business Trusts Act, modelled on Australia's Managed Investments Act, came into force in October 2004, and the LLP Act followed the next year. The text calls both 'a radical shift in the regulatory landscape'.\n\nThe Limited Partnerships Act was enacted in late 2008 and took effect in May 2009. VCCs could be formed from January 2020, creating a corporate-lite vehicle that now challenges even the LP for investment funds. The Business Trusts (Amendment) Act 2022 added further corporate-style rules: a register of beneficial owners, more disclosure of conflicts and stronger unitholder rights.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.001], [03.003]–[03.004], [03.009], [03.042]", takeaway:"2002 → 2004 → 2005 → 2009 → 2020 → 2022." },

{ id:"cl3-005", lesson:"3A", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each Singapore statute to the model it drew on.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Business Trusts Act 2004", r:"Australian Managed Investments Act 1998"},
    {l:"Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2005", r:"UK, Jersey, Delaware and California legislation"},
    {l:"Limited Partnerships Act 2008", r:"UK, Jersey and Delaware legislation"},
    {l:"Variable Capital Companies Act 2018", r:"Comparable to the UK open-ended investment company"}
  ],
  why:"Singapore deliberately borrowed from several jurisdictions to offer the widest choice of business vehicles. The Business Trusts Act was modelled on Australia's Managed Investments Act 1998 (now Chapter 5C of the Corporations Act 2001). The LLP Act drew on the UK, Jersey, Delaware and California, and the LP Act on the UK, Jersey and Delaware. The VCC is somewhat like the UK's open-ended investment company.\n\nThe text links this to Delaware-style thinking: attracting businesses and management to a pro-business jurisdiction, and its 'credible commitment to producing responsive organizational law'. Despite its English heritage, Singapore never adopted the UK Limited Partnerships Act 1907, so the LP was a new form here in 2009.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.001], [03.003]–[03.004]", takeaway:"BTA ← Australia · LLPA ← UK/Jersey/DE/CA · LPA ← UK/Jersey/DE." },

{ id:"cl3-006", lesson:"3A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which three advantages does the text identify as driving the worldwide use of unincorporated business entities?",
  options:[
    {t:"Tax flow-through, more liberal rules on returning capital, and lighter, more flexible governance", correct:true,
     why:"First, tax. The 'entity' may not attract corporate tax, so income flows through to investors and is taxed at their own marginal rates. Since 2002 Singapore companies pay a flat single-level tax with no franking of tax credits to shareholders, which pushes investors toward tax-transparent vehicles.\n\nSecond, capital can be returned more freely. Companies are subject to capital lock-in, while the alternative statutes contain only specific, generally weaker, rules on withdrawing capital. Third, because of that lock-in, companies are more heavily regulated and corporate governance is more prescriptive. In trusts the deed governs, and in partnerships the agreement does, with the Partnership Act's defaults assuming equality. That gives new and emerging businesses more flexibility."},
    {t:"Separate personality, asset partitioning and perpetual succession", correct:false,
     why:"These are the company's strengths. Weaker asset partitioning is in fact the crucial drawback of the alternative vehicles, which is why creditor rights remain a concern with them."},
    {t:"Easier listing, cheaper audits and automatic limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"None of these is among the text's three advantages. General partnerships have no limited liability at all, and listed business trusts face listed-company-style requirements."},
    {t:"Stronger creditor protection, mandatory dividends and court supervision", correct:false,
     why:"Creditor protection is generally weaker in the alternatives, not stronger, because they lack capital lock-in and full asset partitioning."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.005]–[03.008]", takeaway:"Why uncorporate: tax, capital return, flexibility." },

{ id:"cl3-007", lesson:"3A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Business trusts with active underlying assets receive tax transparency from IRAS, like REITs.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"LPs and LLPs are usually granted tax transparency by IRAS and are taxed like general partnerships. Unit trusts and REITs that are authorised or recognised as collective investment schemes also obtain tax transparency (footnote 36 adds that REITs are exempt from entity-level tax if they pay out at least 90% of their income).\n\nBut IRAS has issued a circular stating that business trusts with active underlying assets will not receive tax transparency. They are treated like a corporation for tax purposes. Without tax transparency, which is usually the most important driver of structures in the market, the main advantage of the business trust is that it can pay out all its cash profits to unitholders without being constrained by corporate accounting rules."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Only CIS-regulated unit trusts and REITs get tax transparency among trust vehicles. Business trusts with active assets are taxed like companies, so their advantage lies elsewhere: paying out all cash profits."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.006], fn 36", takeaway:"Active business trusts: no tax transparency. Main perk is paying out all cash." },

{ id:"cl3-008", lesson:"3A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On Stout's analysis, why do larger asset-owning businesses keep using the company even though LLPs, LPs and business trusts also offer limited liability?",
  options:[
    {t:"Capital lock-in: creditors lend more readily because shareholders' contributions cannot easily be withdrawn", correct:true,
     why:"Stout argues that it is the capital lock-in of companies that distinguishes them from unincorporated entities, and that this facilitates corporate growth. Companies are subject to much stronger capital maintenance rules, which encourage creditors to lend, because what shareholders contribute cannot be withdrawn so easily.\n\nThat greater ability to borrow explains why larger asset-owning businesses continue to operate as companies even though the alternatives also provide limited liability. Partnerships, by contrast, thrive mainly where the business is largely built on human capital, such as professional firms. The alternative statutes do contain specific rules on withdrawing capital, examined later in the chapter, but they are weaker than the company rules."},
    {t:"Companies pay no tax", correct:false,
     why:"Companies pay a flat single-level tax. Tax is usually a reason to choose the alternative vehicles, not a reason to stay with the company."},
    {t:"Only companies can own land", correct:false,
     why:"Partners and trustees can own land, and LLPs own property in their own name. The explanation for the company's dominance in asset-heavy businesses is creditor confidence."},
    {t:"Companies need not hold meetings", correct:false,
     why:"Companies are more heavily regulated than the alternatives, not less, partly because of capital lock-in."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.007]", takeaway:"Stout: capital lock-in → creditor confidence → easier borrowing." },

{ id:"cl3-009", lesson:"3A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does Schwarcz say even flexible US commercial trusts work only in businesses with lower risk-reward ratios?",
  options:[
    {t:"Trustees owe a duty of impartiality, balancing income and capital beneficiaries, whereas directors owe their duty to the company and can take longer-term risks", correct:true,
     why:"Trustees owe a duty of impartiality, which requires them to balance the interests of income beneficiaries (who want payouts now) and capital beneficiaries (who want the fund preserved). That pushes against risk-taking. Directors owe their duty to the company, not to any particular constituency within it, so they can take greater risks focused on the longer term.\n\nThe text adds that there is no duty to pay dividends even when a company makes profits: all earnings can be retained. The idea behind many alternative vehicles is the opposite, to provide a steady income stream, and some of their tax advantages depend on paying out most cash earnings. Hence trusts suit stable income-producing assets more than high-risk growth businesses."},
    {t:"Trusts cannot borrow money", correct:false,
     why:"Trusts can borrow, through their trustees. Unsecured borrowing may be harder, but the risk-reward constraint Schwarcz identifies comes from the duty of impartiality."},
    {t:"Trust units cannot be transferred", correct:false,
     why:"Units in commercial trusts are generally transferable, and listed REIT and business trust units trade on exchange. Transferability is not Schwarcz's point."},
    {t:"US law caps the size of commercial trusts", correct:false,
     why:"There is no such cap. The constraint is a fiduciary one, not a size limit."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.008]", takeaway:"Trust impartiality limits risk; directors serve the entity long-term." },

{ id:"cl3-010", lesson:"3A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Singapore's first attempted REIT used a trust deed stating that a unitholder has no equitable or proprietary interest in the trust's underlying assets. The unitholder's right is limited to requiring due administration of the trust, including by suing the trustee or manager.",
  prompt:"What was this clause trying to achieve?",
  options:[
    {t:"To replicate the company's bifurcated property structure and give the trust artificial entity status", correct:true,
     why:"The crucial drawback of alternative vehicles is that they may lack full separate personality, or more accurately affirmative asset partitioning, so creditor rights remain a concern. That matters especially where bankruptcy remoteness is the goal. The text calls this clause in the Sing Property Trust deed 'perhaps the best testament to the corporate form'.\n\nA traditional trust splits legal and equitable interests at the level of each trust asset. This clause instead mirrors a shareholder's position: each unit is an undivided interest in the trust, but the unitholder has no proprietary interest in the underlying assets, only a right to due administration, enforceable by suing the trustee or manager. That is the company's 'dual bifurcated property structure' recreated by drafting. The court in Croesus later recognised a similar clause."},
    {t:"To give unitholders direct ownership of the properties", correct:false,
     why:"The clause did the reverse: it removed any equitable or proprietary interest in the underlying assets and any right to have assets transferred out."},
    {t:"To avoid registration under the Securities and Futures Act", correct:false,
     why:"REITs are regulated as collective investment schemes under the SFA, and this clause did not change that. It addressed the trust's property structure."},
    {t:"To make the trustee personally liable for all trust debts", correct:false,
     why:"The clause defines what unitholders hold. It says nothing about expanding the trustee's personal liability, and later practice in fact moved toward limiting it."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.009], fn 41", takeaway:"REIT deed copied the company: unitholders get due administration, not assets." },

{ id:"cl3-011", lesson:"3A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Before 2009, Singapore had adopted the UK Limited Partnerships Act 1907.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Despite its English heritage, Singapore never adopted the UK Limited Partnerships Act 1907. The LP form was therefore perhaps not used here, even though partners could try to create limited liability contractually through internal arrangements.\n\nThe problem with that route is that an internal arrangement may not bind third parties who rely on a partner's ostensible authority to commit his fellow partners, under the 'mutual agency' that applies to partnerships. Partnerships with limited liability may also have trouble getting it recognised across borders. The Limited Partnerships Act 2008, effective May 2009, filled the gap, drawing on UK, Jersey and Delaware law."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore never adopted the 1907 Act. The LP form arrived with the 2008 Act, which took effect in May 2009."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.004], fn 18", takeaway:"No UK 1907 Act in Singapore. LPs arrived in 2009." },

/* ---------- 3B General partnership ---------- */
{ id:"cl3-012", lesson:"3B", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Section 1 Partnership Act 1890: 'Partnership is the relation which subsists between persons carrying on a business in common with a view of ____.'",
  accept:["profit"], answer:"profit",
  why:"As a sole proprietorship grows, friends and employees may want to share in the profits rather than just draw a salary. Once two persons carry on a business in common with a view of profit, a general partnership comes into existence under s 1. No formality is required, and s 2 gives rules for deciding whether a partnership exists in specified situations (discussed in Rabiah Bee v Salem Ibrahim).\n\nFootnote 43 adds the size limits: a partnership needs at least two partners and, under ss 17(3) and 17(4) of the Companies Act, cannot exceed 20, except professional services firms governed by their own constituent legislation. Above 20, the business must use another vehicle such as a company or LLP.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.010], fn 43, 45", takeaway:"Partnership = business in common with a view of profit." },

{ id:"cl3-013", lesson:"3B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How are general partners liable for the firm's obligations?",
  options:[
    {t:"Jointly for contract debts (s 9); jointly and severally for wrongful acts of a partner in the ordinary course of business (ss 10 and 12)", correct:true,
     why:"A partnership is an aggregation of its partners, collectively called a firm (s 4); there is no separate legal entity. Order 56 r 1 of the Rules of Court 2021 lets partners sue and be sued in the firm name for convenience, but the individual partners are each liable without limit for the partnership's debts.\n\nIn contract, s 9 makes every partner jointly liable with the other partners. For torts, ss 10 and 12 make every partner jointly and severally liable, together and separately, for every wrongful act or omission of any partner acting in the ordinary course of the firm's business. Footnote 48 adds that under s 17 of the Civil Law Act, a judgment against one person jointly liable does not bar an action against another person jointly liable for the same debt."},
    {t:"Only up to their capital contribution", correct:false,
     why:"That is limited liability, which general partners do not have. Limited partners in an LP and partners in an LLP have forms of limited liability, but general partners are liable to an unlimited extent."},
    {t:"The firm alone is liable, as a separate legal person", correct:false,
     why:"A general partnership is not a separate legal entity. The firm name is a label attached for convenience and carries no general legal consequences (Alagappa Chettiar v Coliseum Café)."},
    {t:"Only the partner who acted is liable, in every case", correct:false,
     why:"That is close to the LLP rule (s 12 LLPA). In a general partnership every partner is liable for firm debts and for wrongs committed in the ordinary course of business."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.011], [03.013], fn 46, 48, 51", takeaway:"Contract: joint (s 9). Tort: joint and several (ss 10, 12)." },

{ id:"cl3-014", lesson:"3B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A solicitor in a law firm acted as an express trustee for a client and misapplied the funds. He also gave undertakings to third parties, but the client had made payments to him personally. The client sues his partners.",
  prompt:"How did the court approach the partners' vicarious liability in Lim Kok Koon v Tan Cheng Yew?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether an act can be within the firm's ordinary course of business is a question of law; whether it was done so is a question of fact. Acting as express trustee failed the first test, and the undertakings failed the second.", correct:true,
     why:"The court split the 'ordinary course of business' inquiry in two. Whether a particular act was capable of being performed in the ordinary course of business is a question of law. If it was, whether it was in fact so performed is a question of fact, which depends on how closely the act the partner was authorised to perform is connected to his wrongdoing.\n\nApplying this, it is not within a solicitor's business to act as an express trustee (as opposed to advising and acting for a separate trustee), so that limb failed as a matter of law. Furnishing undertakings to third parties is capable of being within a solicitor's business, but on the facts these were not given within the confines of the business, because the client had also made payments to the solicitor personally, which was not the normal course of things. So the partners were not vicariously liable."},
    {t:"All partners are liable for anything a partner does while a partner", correct:false,
     why:"Partners' vicarious liability under ss 10 and 12 is limited to wrongful acts in the ordinary course of the firm's business. Acts outside that do not bind the other partners."},
    {t:"Ordinary course of business is purely a question of fact", correct:false,
     why:"The court split it: whether the act is capable of being in the ordinary course is a question of law; whether it actually was is a question of fact."},
    {t:"Partners are never vicariously liable for each other's breaches of trust", correct:false,
     why:"They can be, if the act falls within the ordinary course of business. Here it did not, on both limbs."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.012], fn 50", takeaway:"Lim Kok Koon: capable of ordinary course (law) → actually in it (fact)." },

{ id:"cl3-015", lesson:"3B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does s 227 SFA 2001 treat partners for insider trading purposes?",
  options:[
    {t:"A partner is deemed to possess information another partner or employee possesses, and to know what they know or ought reasonably to know", correct:true,
     why:"The starting point is that a partnership, not being a separate entity, cannot itself be guilty of an offence. Section 227 SFA is an example where the statute is very clear that partners share knowledge. It deems a partner to possess information that another partner or employee possesses. It also provides that a partner knows or ought reasonably to know whatever another partner or employee knows or ought reasonably to know.\n\nThis is wider than the Malaysian equivalent, which deals only with possession and does not expressly attribute knowledge. The partner must still have the necessary mens rea, not merely possession, to breach the s 219 prohibition. But once 'afflicted' by attributed knowledge, the partner cannot deal in the securities without falling foul of s 219, which is why the Chinese Wall exception exists."},
    {t:"Only the partner who personally received the information is affected", correct:false,
     why:"Section 227 expressly attributes both possession and knowledge to the other partners. Limiting it to the recipient would defeat its purpose."},
    {t:"The partnership itself is criminally liable as a separate entity", correct:false,
     why:"A partnership is not a separate entity. Section 227 works by attributing information and knowledge to individual partners, who are then exposed under s 219."},
    {t:"Partnerships are exempt from insider trading rules", correct:false,
     why:"They are not exempt. Section 227 exists specifically to deal with partnerships."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.013]–[03.014], fn 55", takeaway:"s 227 SFA: possession and knowledge attributed across partners." },

{ id:"cl3-016", lesson:"3B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Partner A of a firm holds price-sensitive information about a listed company. The firm's trading desk, run by Partner B, buys shares in that company the same week.",
  prompt:"On which facts can B rely on the SFA's Chinese Wall exception for partnerships?",
  options:[
    {t:"Someone other than B made the decision, the firm had arrangements reasonably expected to stop the information or related advice reaching that person, and none did", correct:true,
     why:"Because s 227 is so wide, the SFA provides a Chinese Wall exception for partnerships. Despite the attribution of knowledge, a partner will not be found to have breached the insider trading prohibition if all three requirements are met: (a) the decision to enter into the transaction was taken by someone other than the partner; (b) the partnership had arrangements that could reasonably be expected to ensure the information was not communicated to the decision-maker and that no advice on the transaction was given by anyone in possession of it; and (c) the information was not in fact communicated and no such advice was given.\n\nFootnote 58 notes the exception may not cover procuring another person to trade; an Australian committee called the equivalent gap an oversight. The text adds that criminal provisions here should be interpreted where possible in favour of partners not directly involved."},
    {t:"The firm had a written Chinese Wall policy, though the information did reach the decision-maker", correct:false,
     why:"Requirement (c) fails. A policy on paper is not enough; the information must not actually have been communicated to the decision-maker."},
    {t:"B made the decision himself but had never spoken to A", correct:false,
     why:"Requirement (a) fails. The decision must be taken by a person other than the partner to whom knowledge is attributed. Since s 227 deems B to know what A knows, not having spoken to A does not help him."},
    {t:"The trade was small relative to the firm's business", correct:false,
     why:"Size plays no part in the exception. Only the three structural requirements matter."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.015], fn 58", takeaway:"Chinese Wall: different decision-maker + adequate arrangements + no leak." },

{ id:"cl3-017", lesson:"3B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the text urge caution before extending strict partnership liability to criminal liability?",
  options:[
    {t:"The principle against doubtful penalisation: partners not involved in the offence should not readily be convicted, and attributing one partner's mens rea to others is problematic", correct:true,
     why:"A partnership cannot itself be guilty of an offence, but statutes sometimes impose liability on 'the partnership', which becomes a matter of statutory interpretation. That may simply mean every partner is criminally liable for an act committed by a fellow partner, which has serious consequences. Most offences have mens rea requirements, and it is unclear how a wrongdoing partner's state of mind can or should be attributed to the others.\n\nSo while strict partnership liability may suit civil claims, caution is needed before extending it to criminal liability for uninvolved partners, under the 'principle against doubtful penalisation'. One possibility the text suggests, by analogy with tort, is to argue that committing a particular kind of offence is not within the ordinary course of a partnership's business."},
    {t:"Partnerships can never be named in any statute", correct:false,
     why:"Statutes sometimes do impose liability on 'the partnership', and footnote 52 notes English partnerships have long had 'weak entity shielding'. The question is how to interpret such provisions, not whether they can exist."},
    {t:"Criminal law applies only to companies", correct:false,
     why:"Individuals, including partners, are of course subject to criminal law. The concern is attributing one partner's offence to others."},
    {t:"Partners always have full indemnities from the firm", correct:false,
     why:"Indemnities are not the reason, and an indemnity cannot cover criminal liability in any event. The concern is fairness and attribution of mens rea."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.013], [03.016], fn 52, 59", takeaway:"Criminal liability: doubtful penalisation favours uninvolved partners." },

{ id:"cl3-018", lesson:"3B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under s 5 Partnership Act 1890, when is a partner's act in the usual course of business NOT binding on the firm?",
  options:[
    {t:"When the third party knows the partner has no authority, or does not know or believe him to be a partner", correct:true,
     why:"Under the 'mutual agency' concept, each partner is an agent of the other partners and of the partnership, and a fiduciary owing them good faith. Partners therefore have authority to bind the firm and the other partners to contracts made in the ordinary course of the partnership's business.\n\nSection 5 says an act carried out in the usual way business of that kind is carried on binds the firm unless 'the person with whom he is dealing either knows that he has no authority, or does not know or believe him to be a partner'. As with ostensible authority, notice of the lack of authority defeats the claim. The burden is on the third party to show the act was in the ordinary course of business (Lim Hsi-Wei Marc v Orix Capital), similar to proving ostensible authority and the indoor management rule. What counts is what is usual for firms in that business, not for this particular firm (Dinesh Kotak v Jagdish Kotak)."},
    {t:"Whenever the other partners did not specifically approve the act", correct:false,
     why:"Mutual agency means specific approval is not needed for acts in the usual course of business. Requiring it would make every partner's routine dealings uncertain for third parties."},
    {t:"Whenever the act turns out to be loss-making", correct:false,
     why:"Commercial outcome has nothing to do with authority. A bad bargain made with authority still binds the firm."},
    {t:"Only when the firm is insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"Insolvency is irrelevant to the s 5 test, which turns on the nature of the act and the third party's knowledge."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.017], fn 65, 67–68", takeaway:"s 5: binding unless the third party knows of no authority or doesn't know he's a partner." },

{ id:"cl3-019", lesson:"3B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Under s 19 Partnership Act 1890, partners' mutual rights and duties can be varied only by a written agreement.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Section 19 provides that the mutual rights and duties of partners, whether ascertained by agreement or defined by the Act, 'may be varied by the consent of all the partners, and such consent may be either express or inferred from a course of dealing'. The course of dealing must clearly and unequivocally show that the parties intended to vary the partnership terms (Dakshu Patel v Kesha Patel).\n\nThis fits the partnership's deeply contractual nature. A partnership can be created by express written or oral agreement or by conduct inferred from the circumstances. The agreement usually deals with management, profit and loss sharing and contributions, and where it is silent the Partnership Act's default provisions apply, starting from equality between partners."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"No writing is required. Consent of all partners can be inferred from a course of dealing, as long as the conduct clearly and unequivocally shows an intention to vary."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.017], fn 64", takeaway:"s 19: vary by all partners' consent, express or by course of dealing." },

{ id:"cl3-020", lesson:"3B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A partner assigns his partnership share to an investor. The investor then demands to see the partnership books and to have a say in management.",
  prompt:"What is the investor entitled to?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only the assignor's share of profits, accepting the account agreed by the partners. No management role, accounts or inspection of the books.", correct:true,
     why:"A partner cannot transfer his interest in a way that lets the transferee interfere in the management or administration of the business, require accounts of the partnership's transactions, or inspect the partnership books. Under s 31(1) he can assign his right to receive his share of profits, and the assignee must accept the account of profits agreed by the partners.\n\nA full transfer of the partnership interest requires a novation and the consent of all the other partners. This is the partnership's lack of transferability, the contrast with shares drawn in Chapter 2. There is also no perpetual succession: if a partner dies, leaves or retires, the partnership is dissolved by law unless the agreement provides otherwise (ss 32–33)."},
    {t:"Full partnership rights, because the assignment was valid", correct:false,
     why:"A valid assignment passes only the right to profits. The assignee does not become a partner without a novation and every partner's consent."},
    {t:"Nothing, because partnership interests cannot be assigned at all", correct:false,
     why:"The right to receive profits can be assigned. What cannot be passed without consent is the partner's status and management powers."},
    {t:"Inspection of the books, but no share of profits", correct:false,
     why:"It is the other way round: the assignee gets the profit share and must accept the partners' account, but has no right to inspect the books."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.018], fn 69–70", takeaway:"Assignee gets profits only (s 31(1)). Full transfer needs novation." },

{ id:"cl3-021", lesson:"3B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each rule to what it provides for general partnerships.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"s 24 Partnership Act", r:"Default management rules, subject to the partners' agreement"},
    {l:"ss 32–33 Partnership Act", r:"Dissolution on a partner's death or departure unless agreed otherwise"},
    {l:"s 36 Partnership Act", r:"Apparent members treated as still in the firm until notice of change"},
    {l:"Business Names Registration Act 2014", r:"Light-touch registration of the business"}
  ],
  why:"Like a sole proprietorship, a partnership must register under the Business Names Registration Act 2014, a form of light-touch regulation concerned mainly with business names (s 31 deals with the effect of non-registration). The partnership's real terms are in the partnership deed, aided by the Partnership Act's defaults, such as the management structure in s 24, which applies 'subject to any agreement express or implied between the partners'. Defaults matter because partnerships are often formed informally.\n\nThere is no perpetual succession: under ss 32–33, a partner's death or departure dissolves the firm unless the agreement provides otherwise. Under s 36, a person dealing with the firm after a change in its membership may treat all apparent members of the old firm as still members until he has notice of the change. The text adds that a partnership can probably be wound up as an 'unregistered company' under s 246 IRDA.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.017]–[03.018], fn 60, 70–72", takeaway:"s 24 defaults · ss 32–33 dissolution · s 36 apparent members · BNRA registration." },

/* ---------- 3C LLPs & limited partnerships ---------- */
{ id:"cl3-022", lesson:"3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the key structural difference between an LLP and an LP in Singapore?",
  options:[
    {t:"The LLP has separate legal personality; the LP remains an aggregation of partners with special features", correct:true,
     why:"Both must register with the Registrar. But under s 5 LLPA the LLP has its own legal personality distinct from its partners: perpetual succession, unlimited capacity to contract, and the ability to sue and be sued in its own name. It can do and suffer whatever bodies corporate may lawfully do and suffer. The LP has none of this; it remains an aggregation of partners with certain special characteristics.\n\nThat is probably why the LLP Act excludes the Partnership Act 1890, while the LP Act keeps it applying unless the LP Act expressly provides otherwise (s 4). The UK Law Commission considered giving all partnerships entity status but did not take the proposal forward, perhaps because entity status should go only to vehicles with sufficient mandatory rules."},
    {t:"The LP has separate personality; the LLP does not", correct:false,
     why:"It is the reverse. The LLP is the entity (s 5 LLPA); the LP is an aggregate to which the Partnership Act still applies."},
    {t:"Only the LP must register with the Registrar", correct:false,
     why:"Both the LLP and the LP require registration with the Registrar."},
    {t:"Only the LLP gives any partner limited liability", correct:false,
     why:"Limited partners in an LP also have limited liability, provided they are registered as such and stay out of management. The LP's general partners are the ones with unlimited liability."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.019], fn 73, 78–79", takeaway:"LLP = entity (s 5). LP = aggregate; Partnership Act still applies." },

{ id:"cl3-023", lesson:"3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"To whom do partners of an LLP owe their fiduciary duties?",
  options:[
    {t:"To the LLP as an entity, not to each other, so company-style rules like Foss v Harbottle appear to apply", correct:true,
     why:"Because the LLP is a separate entity, much like a corporation, its partners' fiduciary duties are not owed inter se to fellow partners, as they are in a general partnership or LP. They are owed to the entity, given its separate juristic status.\n\nIt follows that the principles from Chapter 2 on resolving disputes through a company, such as the rule in Foss v Harbottle, also appear to apply to LLPs (F C Alternative Investments v Barthelemy; Brown v InnovatorOne). A wrong to the LLP is the LLP's to pursue. Footnote 80 adds that an LLP partner in breach of fiduciary duty may have his right to a profit share forfeited (Hosking v Marathon Asset Management)."},
    {t:"To each fellow partner individually", correct:false,
     why:"That is the general partnership and LP position, where partners are mutual agents and fiduciaries to each other. In an LLP the duties run to the entity."},
    {t:"To the LLP's creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Duties run to the LLP itself. As with company directors, creditors are not the direct beneficiaries of the partners' fiduciary duties."},
    {t:"LLP partners owe no fiduciary duties", correct:false,
     why:"They do owe fiduciary duties; the question is only to whom. The answer is the LLP."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.020], fn 80–81", takeaway:"LLP partners' duties run to the LLP; Foss v Harbottle applies." },

{ id:"cl3-024", lesson:"3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bank wants a floating charge over all present and future assets of a Singapore LLP, as it would take from a company.",
  prompt:"What is the difficulty, and how does the text resolve it?",
  options:[
    {t:"s 131 CA charge registration doesn't extend to LLPs and the Bills of Sale Act covers only tangible property, but the LLPA Schedules suggest an LLP can grant a floating charge, at least over book debts", correct:true,
     why:"Even the LLP's separate juridical status has limits from an asset partitioning and creditor rights perspective. Unlike in the UK, no provision extends the company charge registration regime in s 131 CA to LLPs. Security from an LLP must instead be registered under the Bills of Sale Act 1886, which applies to individuals and ordinary partnerships but only for tangible property. It is hard to include future or not-yet-acquired property, the usual subject of a floating charge, in a statutory-form bill of sale.\n\nHowever, s 104 of the old Bankruptcy Act extended bill of sale registration to assignments of existing or future book debts. That provision is not in the IRDA but survives as para 83 of the Fifth Schedule to the LLPA. Together with the Fourth Schedule (receivers and managers) and Fifth Schedule (winding up), which clearly envisage floating charges, this suggests an LLP can grant a floating charge, at least over book debts. The LLP's insolvency regime is still only partial: there is no judicial management or scheme of arrangement."},
    {t:"There is no difficulty: LLPs register charges under s 131 CA like companies", correct:false,
     why:"No provision extends s 131 to LLPs in Singapore, unlike the position in the UK. That is the source of the difficulty."},
    {t:"LLPs cannot grant any security at all", correct:false,
     why:"LLPs can grant security, registered as bills of sale. The difficulty is specifically with floating charges over future and intangible assets."},
    {t:"The bank must take personal guarantees instead, because LLPs cannot own property", correct:false,
     why:"LLPs own property in their own name under s 5 LLPA. Guarantees might be prudent, but not for that reason."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.021], fn 82, 90–91", takeaway:"LLP floating charge: likely possible over book debts; partial insolvency regime." },

{ id:"cl3-025", lesson:"3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is it significant that s 3(5) LPA 2008 allows a corporation to be a general partner of an LP?",
  options:[
    {t:"A thinly capitalised corporate GP effectively gives the individuals who manage the fund limited liability, turning the LP into a 'limited liability limited partnership'", correct:true,
     why:"An LP must have at least one general partner with unlimited liability, and limited partners cannot take part in management. If the general partner is a company, it has unlimited liability, but it may itself have been incorporated with little capital. That effectively shields its individual controllers, who are the people actually managing the fund, and reduces the accountability expected of a general partner. The LP becomes in substance a 'limited liability limited partnership'.\n\nSection 3(5), unlike equivalent provisions in some jurisdictions, allows a corporation to be either a general or a limited partner. The text notes this may give the LP an advantage over the Singapore VCC, whose directors must be individuals, as with Singapore companies generally. A body corporate can also be an LLP partner (s 11 LLPA), but that matters less."},
    {t:"It gives the LP separate legal personality", correct:false,
     why:"A corporate general partner does not change the LP's nature as an aggregate of partners. The LP still has no separate personality."},
    {t:"It removes the need for any limited partners", correct:false,
     why:"An LP still needs at least one general partner and one limited partner."},
    {t:"It makes the LP tax-opaque", correct:false,
     why:"Tax treatment is not the point here. The significance is the shielding of the individuals who control the corporate GP."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.022]–[03.023], fn 92", takeaway:"Corporate GP → effectively an LLLP; edge over the SVCC." },

{ id:"cl3-026", lesson:"3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A partner of a Singapore LLP gives negligent advice to a client. The client sues that partner, a co-partner who was not involved, and the LLP.",
  prompt:"Who is liable?",
  options:[
    {t:"The negligent partner and the LLP; the co-partner is not liable for another partner's default", correct:true,
     why:"Under s 12 LLPA, an LLP partner is personally liable in contract, tort or otherwise for his own wrongful act or omission, but is not liable for another partner's default. In The Singapore Professional Golfers' Association v Chen Eng Waye, a partner was held not liable for passing off committed by his father, a fellow LLP partner.\n\nThe partners are agents of the LLP, so where liability is incurred in the course of the LLP's business, even from a partner's wrongful act, the LLP is liable to the same extent as the partner. This differs from a company director, who is usually not personally liable for acts done on the company's behalf, even negligent ones (Williams v Natural Life Health Foods), absent an assumption of responsibility or fraud. The shield for innocent co-partners is why LLPs are popular with professional firms."},
    {t:"All three, jointly and severally, as in a general partnership", correct:false,
     why:"That is the general partnership rule under ss 10 and 12 of the Partnership Act. The LLP's selling point is that innocent co-partners are not liable for each other's defaults."},
    {t:"Only the LLP, because partners act as its organs", correct:false,
     why:"Unlike directors, LLP partners remain personally liable for their own wrongs under s 12 LLPA, alongside the LLP."},
    {t:"Only the negligent partner, because the LLP is not liable for torts", correct:false,
     why:"The LLP is liable to the same extent as the partner where the liability arises in the course of its business."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.024], fn 95–96", takeaway:"LLP: wrongdoer + LLP liable; innocent co-partners shielded (s 12)." },

{ id:"cl3-027", lesson:"3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When does a limited partner in a Singapore LP lose the protection of limited liability?",
  options:[
    {t:"If not registered as a limited partner with the Registrar, or if he participates in management", correct:true,
     why:"In an LP the general partners are in the same position as partners in a general partnership, with unlimited liability. The limited partners are somewhat like LLP partners, but they lose limited liability if they are not registered as limited partners with the Registrar, or if they participate in managing the LP.\n\nThe limited partner is meant to be a sleeping partner, with management entirely in the general partner's hands. Section 6(1) LPA reinforces this: a limited partner has no power to bind the LP. The First Schedule lists acts that are not regarded as taking part in management (the UK has widened its list for private fund LPs). Contrast the LLP, where the default rule is that every partner takes part in management (First Schedule para 4)."},
    {t:"If the LP makes a loss in any year", correct:false,
     why:"Trading results have no effect on a limited partner's status or protection."},
    {t:"If he brings any claim against the general partner", correct:false,
     why:"A limited partner can bring a personal action against a general partner, in contract or for breach of fiduciary duty, without losing protection. Suing a third-party manager is the risky case (Henderson)."},
    {t:"Only if the LP is wound up", correct:false,
     why:"Protection is lost through non-registration or participation in management, at whatever time that happens, not by winding up."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.025], fn 99, 103", takeaway:"LP: register as limited partner and stay out of management." },

{ id:"cl3-028", lesson:"3C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A limited partner in a fund LP believes the third-party manager appointed by the partnership has breached the management deed, and sues the manager.",
  prompt:"What risk does this carry, following Henderson PFI Secondary Fund II?",
  options:[
    {t:"Suing the manager is a derivative claim on the partnership's behalf, which counts as participating in management, so he may be liable as a general partner for that period", correct:true,
     why:"A limited partner is able to bring a personal action against a general partner, either in contract or for breach of fiduciary duty. That is enforcing his own rights.\n\nSuing a third-party manager appointed to act for the partnership for breach of the management deed is different. The claim belongs to the partnership, so the limited partner is bringing a derivative claim on its behalf. In Henderson PFI Secondary Fund II, Cooke J held that this amounts to participating in the management of the LP during the period of the derivative claim, so the limited partner would be liable for the LP's debts and obligations at that time as if he were a general partner. The safer route is to press the general partner to act, or to sue the general partner personally."},
    {t:"No risk: limited partners can sue anyone without affecting their status", correct:false,
     why:"Henderson shows that a derivative claim against a third-party manager is treated as participation in management, with general-partner exposure for that period."},
    {t:"He must first convert the LP into an LLP", correct:false,
     why:"No conversion is involved. The issue is the effect of the claim on his limited partner status."},
    {t:"The claim is barred because only the Registrar can sue managers", correct:false,
     why:"The Registrar has no such role. The question is who properly brings the partnership's claim and with what consequences."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.025], fn 101–102", takeaway:"Henderson: LP suing a third-party manager = management → GP exposure." },

{ id:"cl3-029", lesson:"3C", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each vehicle to its capital-return rule.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Singapore LLP", r:"Refund distributions made within 3 years before winding up, if the partner knew or ought to have known of insolvency"},
    {l:"Singapore LP", r:"Refund distributions made within 1 year before the insolvency of every general partner, if he knew or ought to have known"},
    {l:"UK LP (1907 Act)", r:"No drawing out of contributions while the partnership continues"},
    {l:"Company", r:"Most capital returns need an ex ante solvency statement"}
  ],
  why:"Paragraph 84 of the Fifth Schedule to the LLPA makes an LLP partner liable to refund any distribution of profits or capital made within three years before the commencement of winding up, if he knew or ought to have known that the LLP was insolvent at the time or would be made insolvent by the distribution.\n\nFor the Singapore LP, s 7(1) LPA permits capital to be returned while the LP is a going concern, but s 7(2) makes a limited partner refund distributions made within one year before the bankruptcy or insolvency of every general partner, if he knew or ought to have known they were all insolvent at the time or would be made so. The UK 1907 Act forbids a limited partner from drawing out his contribution while the partnership continues, so UK limited partners contribute by loan. Companies need an ex ante solvency statement for most capital returns (Chapter 13). So capital lock-in is weakest in the Singapore LP, stronger in the UK LP, and strongest in the company.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.027]–[03.028]", takeaway:"LLP 3 yrs · SG LP 1 yr · UK LP locked in · company solvency statement." },

{ id:"cl3-030", lesson:"3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why do limited partners in English LPs commonly contribute funds by way of loan?",
  options:[
    {t:"To avoid the 1907 Act rule that a limited partner may not draw out any part of his contribution while the partnership continues", correct:true,
     why:"The UK Limited Partnerships Act 1907 provides that a limited partner shall not draw out or receive back any part of his contribution during the continuance of the partnership. The text calls this 'in fact a very strict capital maintenance requirement': a limited partner would have to wait until the fund is dissolved to get his capital back.\n\nEnglish practice avoids the rule by having limited partners put in most of their money as loans, which can be repaid during the fund's life, with only a nominal capital contribution. Singapore took a different approach: s 7(1) LPA permits capital return while the LP is a going concern, subject to the one-year clawback in s 7(2)."},
    {t:"Loans give limited partners voting control", correct:false,
     why:"Lending gives no management rights, and taking part in management would in any case endanger the limited partner's limited liability."},
    {t:"UK law forbids equity contributions to LPs", correct:false,
     why:"Capital contributions are allowed. The problem is that once contributed, they cannot be withdrawn while the partnership continues."},
    {t:"Loans are not subject to tax", correct:false,
     why:"The motive the text describes is the capital-withdrawal rule, not tax."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.028]", takeaway:"UK LPs: contribute by loan to escape the 1907 lock-in." },

{ id:"cl3-031", lesson:"3C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: An LLP must prepare, file and audit its financial statements every year.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Under the LLP Act, the LLP must make an annual declaration of solvency or insolvency (s 30). It must keep proper accounting records that would enable true and fair financial statements to be prepared (s 31). But there is no obligation on the LLP or its partners to prepare or file financial statements, or to have its accounts audited.\n\nThe same records duty applies to the LP, where it falls on the general partner. Both vehicles must state their name, registration number and the fact of limited liability on invoices and similar documents (s 22 LLPA; s 16 LPA). The light disclosure regime is part of why these vehicles appeal to private businesses and funds."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"There is no filing or audit obligation. The LLP must keep proper records and make an annual solvency declaration."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.029], fn 113–115", takeaway:"LLP: annual solvency declaration + proper records. No filed or audited accounts." },

{ id:"cl3-032", lesson:"3C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Can an LLP partner transfer his partnership interest to an outsider?",
  options:[
    {t:"No. A new partner needs a novation, but he may assign his right to payments or distributions, subject to the partnership agreement", correct:true,
     why:"Like a general partner, an LLP partner cannot transfer his partnership interest. A novation is required before a new partner can be introduced (First Schedule para 6, which also provides that no majority can expel a partner unless an express power has been agreed). Under s 17 LLPA, however, he may transfer or assign to anyone his right to receive any payment or distribution in respect of his interest, subject to any limitations in the partnership agreement.\n\nThe LP Act is silent on this. The text suggests LPs presumably follow the general partnership rule, because the common law and equity of partnerships and provisions like s 31(1) of the Partnership Act continue to apply to them. So a profit share can be assigned, but the assignee gets no management rights, accounts or access to the books."},
    {t:"Yes, freely, like shares in a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Free transferability is a corporate feature. Even though the LLP has separate personality, its partnership interests still need novation to change hands."},
    {t:"Yes, with a majority vote of the other partners", correct:false,
     why:"Admitting a new partner requires novation with consent, not a simple majority vote, unless the partnership agreement provides otherwise."},
    {t:"No, and he cannot assign even his right to distributions", correct:false,
     why:"Section 17 LLPA expressly allows assignment of the right to payments and distributions, subject to the agreement."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.026], fn 104–105", takeaway:"LLP interest: no transfer without novation; distributions assignable (s 17)." },

/* ---------- 3D Business trusts ---------- */
{ id:"cl3-033", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What governance structure does the Business Trusts Act 2004 require?",
  options:[
    {t:"A single trustee-manager that is a company, whose directors must prioritise unitholders' interests in a conflict", correct:true,
     why:"The Business Trusts Act addressed concerns about property trusts like REITs regulated as collective investment schemes, which centred on their governance structure: REITs have a separate trustee and manager. The BTA instead requires a single trustee-manager that is a company, and it prescribes the duties of both the trustee-manager and its directors (ss 10 and 11).\n\nIt specifically addresses the conflict built into this structure: the directors of the trustee-manager must act in the best interests of the trustee-manager's own shareholders and of the trust's unitholders. In a conflict, priority must be given to the unitholders. Footnote 120 notes that a similar provision now applies to REITs under the Code on Collective Investment Schemes, from January 2016."},
    {t:"A separate trustee and manager, as with REITs", correct:false,
     why:"That is the CIS model whose governance concerns the BTA was designed to address. Business trusts combine the roles in one corporate trustee-manager."},
    {t:"A board elected directly by unitholders", correct:false,
     why:"The board is the board of the trustee-manager company. The BTA protects unitholders through duties and certifications rather than by making them elect the board directly."},
    {t:"No manager at all: unitholders run the business", correct:false,
     why:"The trustee-manager runs the business. Unitholders are investors, closer to shareholders than to partners."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.030], fn 119–120", takeaway:"BTA: one corporate trustee-manager; unitholders come first." },

{ id:"cl3-034", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which board certification in a business trust's annual report must also be certified by the trustee-manager's CEO?",
  options:[
    {t:"That the board is not aware of any violation by the trustee-manager with a materially adverse effect on the trust or unitholders", correct:true,
     why:"The BTA subjects the trustee-manager's board to disclosure and certification requirements. The most important are that the board must certify in the annual report: (1) that fees and charges are in accordance with the trust deed; (2) that related party transactions are not detrimental to unitholders; and (3) that the board was not aware of any violations by the trustee-manager that would have a materially adverse effect on the business trust or the unitholders as a whole.\n\nThe third statement must also be certified by the trustee-manager's chief executive officer. Where they cannot certify, or their views diverge, a statement explaining why must be annexed to the profit and loss accounts (s 86). This is the kind of 'corporate'-type regulation the text says suits business trusts because they compare with large public companies."},
    {t:"That fees and charges accord with the trust deed", correct:false,
     why:"The board certifies this, but the CEO's additional certification attaches only to the statement about violations."},
    {t:"That related party transactions are not detrimental", correct:false,
     why:"This is a board certification. The CEO co-certifies the violations statement."},
    {t:"That the trust is tax transparent", correct:false,
     why:"There is no such certification, and business trusts with active assets are not tax transparent anyway."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.031]–[03.032], fn 122", takeaway:"CEO co-certifies: no known material violations by the trustee-manager." },

{ id:"cl3-035", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When may a registered business trust make a distribution?",
  options:[
    {t:"When the board is satisfied on reasonable grounds that, immediately afterwards, the trust's liabilities can be met from trust property as they fall due", correct:true,
     why:"As the MAS consultation paper observed, the advantage of the business trust over the corporate structure is that it is not subject to capital maintenance rules. All cash profits can be paid out without having to satisfy the accounting concept of profits that applies to companies, which would, for example, be net of depreciation. Distributions can be made as long as the board is satisfied on reasonable grounds that, immediately after the distribution, the trust's liabilities can be met from trust property as they fall due (s 33).\n\nCreditor protection is consequently somewhat weaker than in a company. There are also fewer detailed winding-up rules and no restructuring provisions; these matters are governed by the trust deed and the court's inherent supervisory jurisdiction under O 32 r 2 of the Rules of Court 2021. Secured creditors are fully protected by the trustee-manager's mandate to grant security over trust assets."},
    {t:"Only out of accounting profits, net of depreciation", correct:false,
     why:"That is the company dividend rule the business trust avoids. Escaping it is the business trust's main advantage, given that it lacks tax transparency."},
    {t:"Only after a solvency statement and unitholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"Those resemble company capital reduction requirements. The BTA test is a board-level cash-flow solvency judgment."},
    {t:"Only with court approval", correct:false,
     why:"No court approval is required for a distribution."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.034], fn 131–132", takeaway:"BT distributions: cash-flow solvency test (s 33), no capital maintenance." },

{ id:"cl3-036", lesson:"3D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An unsecured lender is owed money under a loan the trustee-manager took for a business trust. The trustee-manager had acted negligently in the transaction. The lender wants to reach the trust assets.",
  prompt:"What is the lender's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"It can reach trust assets only by subrogation to the trustee-manager's right of indemnity, which negligence defeats, and the BTA prevents that indemnity being expanded", correct:true,
     why:"Because there is no separate entity, unsecured creditors find it hard to reach trust assets directly. Their claim is against the trustee-manager personally. Under existing law they can reach the trust assets only indirectly, by being subrogated to the trustee's right of indemnity, which courts have recognised as a species of property. That indemnity covers fees, expenses and liabilities properly incurred for the trust. At common law it is lost if the trustee acts improperly, including negligently or without authority.\n\nTrust deeds often try to expand the indemnity, but s 27 BTA makes void any provision indemnifying a trustee-manager against liability for negligence, and s 28 requires its fees and indemnity to be stated in the deed. So the indemnity cannot be expanded, and business trusts may find unsecured borrowing harder than companies do. Secured creditors, by contrast, are fully protected."},
    {t:"It can sue the trust directly as a legal person", correct:false,
     why:"A Commonwealth business trust is not a juristic entity that creditors can sue directly. That is the central contrast with the company."},
    {t:"It can claim against unitholders personally", correct:false,
     why:"Unitholders are not liable for the trust's debts. The lender's counterparty is the trustee-manager."},
    {t:"The trust deed can always be drafted to indemnify the trustee-manager for negligence", correct:false,
     why:"Section 27 BTA makes such an indemnity void. A similar limit applies to CIS trustees under s 292 SFA."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.034]–[03.035], fn 137, 139–140", takeaway:"Unsecured BT creditors: subrogation only; s 27 bars negligence indemnities." },

{ id:"cl3-037", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Section 34 BTA, adapted from the Delaware Statutory Trust Act, says no creditor of a unitholder can reach the trust property. What does it achieve?",
  options:[
    {t:"A form of spendthrift trust limiting beneficiaries' interests, but not a juristic fund that trust creditors can claim against directly", correct:true,
     why:"Section 34 BTA, adapted from § 3805(b) of the Delaware Statutory Trust Act, provides that no creditor of a unitholder may obtain possession of, or exercise any remedy over, the trust property. That is a form of entity shielding: the fund is protected from the unitholders' own creditors, creating something like a spendthrift trust.\n\nBut it does not do the other half of what incorporation does. It does not create a juristic fund against which trust creditors can claim directly; their claim is still against the trustee-manager personally. The text notes that, increasingly, trustees limit their liability by contract with the creditor to the assets they hold as trustee, which is the move toward limited-recourse lending seen in REIT loan agreements."},
    {t:"Full separate legal personality for the trust", correct:false,
     why:"It shields the fund from unitholders' creditors, but does not make the trust a legal person that its own creditors can sue."},
    {t:"Limited liability for the trustee-manager", correct:false,
     why:"Section 34 concerns unitholders' creditors, not the trustee-manager's liability. Limiting the trustee's liability is done by contract."},
    {t:"Priority for unsecured trust creditors over secured creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Section 34 says nothing about creditor priority. Secured creditors remain fully protected."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.035], fn 136", takeaway:"s 34 BTA: unitholders' creditors kept out; still no juristic fund." },

{ id:"cl3-038", lesson:"3D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A private equity group offers to buy all the units in an SGX-listed business trust holding retail real estate and to privatise it through a trust scheme. The Business Trusts Act provides no restructuring mechanism.",
  prompt:"How did the court deal with this in Re Croesus Retail Asset Management?",
  options:[
    {t:"It analogised the trust to a company and made orders paralleling a s 210 scheme, requiring approval by a majority in number representing three-fourths in value of unitholders", correct:true,
     why:"In June 2017 Blackstone announced an offer for all units in Croesus Retail Trust, an SGX-listed business trust investing in Asia-Pacific retail real estate, to be implemented by a trust scheme through its SPV Cyrus Bidco, in compliance with the Take-over Code. The Business Trusts Act has some winding-up provisions but none for restructuring. The SIC indicated the scheme was exempt from various Code provisions, subject to unitholder and court approvals similar to those for a corporate scheme (and to appointment of an independent financial adviser for unitholders, fn 149).\n\nThose approvals included a majority in number representing three-fourths in value of unitholders, for both the scheme and the trust deed amendments. Aedit Abdullah JC, hearing the ex parte application, said the proposed orders 'largely paralleled' a s 210 application, ordered a scheme meeting within three months, and noted that Croesus differed from a traditional trust because unitholders had no equitable proprietary interest in the trust property, only a right to compel due performance."},
    {t:"It refused jurisdiction because the BTA has no scheme provisions", correct:false,
     why:"The court accepted the application and made orders modelled on the corporate scheme procedure, using the analogy with the company."},
    {t:"It required unanimous unitholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"The threshold mirrored corporate schemes: a majority in number representing three-fourths in value, not unanimity."},
    {t:"It treated unitholders as owners of the underlying malls", correct:false,
     why:"The court stressed the opposite: unitholders had no proprietary interest in the trust property, only a right to due performance. That is what made the corporate analogy apt."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.037], fn 146–151", takeaway:"Croesus: trust scheme run like a s 210 scheme (majority + 75% by value)." },

{ id:"cl3-039", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did the Privy Council hold in Investec Trust (Guernsey) v Glenalla Properties?",
  options:[
    {t:"Jersey's statute did not displace the common law: trust creditors still claimed through the trustee's indemnity, and such a 'radical departure' needs clear words", correct:true,
     why:"REITs had trouble borrowing on an unsecured basis, for example through bond issues, which is why trust deeds and loan agreements began limiting the trustee's liability to the trust assets. Some English academics support treating the trustee as an agent committing the trust fund, but UK courts have never recognised that.\n\nIn Investec v Glenalla, the Privy Council examined the Trusts (Jersey) Law 1984, which tried to limit a trustee's personal liability to trust creditors to the extent of the trust property. The Board accepted the statute was trying to modify English principles, under which creditors claim against trust assets only by subrogation to the trustee's indemnity, which depends on the state of accounts and whether the trustee breached trust. But it held the common law was not changed, because this was a 'radical departure which should not lightly be inferred or implied in the absence of clear words'."},
    {t:"Trusts are separate legal entities under Jersey law", correct:false,
     why:"The Board did not accept that the Jersey statute had changed the common law position. The trust remained a relationship, not a legal person."},
    {t:"Trustees are never personally liable to creditors", correct:false,
     why:"The trustee remained personally liable to creditors, with recourse to the trust fund only through its indemnity. That was the effect of the decision."},
    {t:"Unitholders are liable for trust debts", correct:false,
     why:"Beneficiaries' liability was not the issue. The case was about the trustee's liability and creditors' access to the fund."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.041], fn 160–161", takeaway:"Investec v Glenalla: no limited-recourse trust without clear statutory words." },

{ id:"cl3-040", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does s 86 of New Zealand's Trusts Act 2019 add for trust creditors?",
  options:[
    {t:"Subrogation to the trustee's indemnity even where the trustee is not fully entitled to it, if the creditor gave value in good faith and the trust property benefited, up to the value given", correct:true,
     why:"Following firm proposals by the New Zealand Law Commission (the final version differs from its draft), s 86 of the Trusts Act 2019, in force from 30 January 2021, lets a creditor be subrogated to the trustee's right of indemnity even if, for some reason, the trustee is not entitled to be fully indemnified. It applies only if the creditor gave value in good faith and the trust property received a benefit from the transaction, and only to the extent of the value given.\n\nThat closes the gap that arises when a trustee's misconduct defeats its indemnity. But these remain derivative claims, unlike the direct claims, secured and unsecured, that lenders have against companies. The text uses this to show 'the enormous benefits that the separate juristic status still confers on the company'."},
    {t:"A direct claim against the trust as a legal person", correct:false,
     why:"The claims remain derivative, through the trustee's indemnity. NZ did not make the trust a legal person."},
    {t:"Personal liability of beneficiaries", correct:false,
     why:"Beneficiaries are not made liable. The reform works through the trustee's indemnity."},
    {t:"Automatic priority over secured creditors", correct:false,
     why:"No priority change is made. The reform only removes one obstacle to unsecured creditors reaching the fund."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.036], fn 141–142", takeaway:"NZ s 86: subrogation despite trustee default, if good-faith value benefited the trust." },

{ id:"cl3-041", lesson:"3D", type:"fill",
  prompt:"In Perez De La Sala, Andrew Phang JA described beneficial ownership as 'a right against a ____'.",
  accept:["right"], answer:"right",
  why:"Phang JA said beneficial 'ownership' has been described as 'a right against a right': a right to constrain or control the way another person exercises his right to deal with a thing, rather than a right against the thing itself (citing McFarlane and Stevens). Even in a fixed interest trust, the beneficiary usually has no right to present enjoyment of the trust property; his interest shows itself in his right to protect the fund.\n\nSo a beneficiary may hold an 'equitable' interest that is not necessarily 'proprietary'. That brings trusts closer to how civilian jurisdictions see them, as obligations, since civil law finds it hard to accept two people having rights in the same property. Seeing units in REITs and business trusts as closer to shares makes Singapore trusts easier to understand abroad.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.038]–[03.039]", takeaway:"Beneficial ownership = a right against a right." },

{ id:"cl3-042", lesson:"3D", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The business trust is well suited to small family businesses that want a flexible, low-cost vehicle.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The text says business trusts should be compared with larger public companies, while the LP and LLP compare with the private company. Given its complex and costly governance structure, the business trust is likely to be used only to securitise large income-generating assets, probably infrastructure, usually with many outside investors (real property securitisation tends to stay in CIS-regulated REITs for tax reasons).\n\nThe SFA was amended in 2004 to require a prospectus for offers of business trust units, now merged with the general securities prospectus regime. In October 2023 there were 19 registered business trusts, 15 listed: eight in hospitality and healthcare, five in shipping and oil and gas, and three in technology infrastructure. Listed business trusts face listed-company-style administration, reporting and disclosure rules, and the Take-over Code applies to them."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The business trust is a public-company-scale vehicle with costly governance. A small family business would look to a private company, a general partnership, an LLP or an LP."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.032]", takeaway:"Business trusts suit large income assets and public investors." },

{ id:"cl3-043", lesson:"3D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In which direction does the text say Singapore business trusts and REITs are moving?",
  options:[
    {t:"Towards the US position, where the trust fund can be a separate entity, beneficiaries do not own the underlying property and the trustee can borrow without recourse to itself", correct:true,
     why:"Weakening the beneficiary principle lets the trust be seen as a separate entity in the same way as a company, whose shareholders own 'rights in a procedure'. Loan agreements for REITs and business trusts now include undertakings, matching the trust deed, that the trustee's liability is met only from the trust assets; In re EHT US1 (a Delaware bankruptcy case about a Singapore REIT) shows such clauses. This also allows the trust itself to be liquidated as an entity, whereas in most of the Commonwealth it is the trustee that is wound up (compare Valad in Australia).\n\nThe text says Singapore is 'quickly moving' to the US position. That might once have been seen as offending the 'irreducible core' of trust law, but it has been argued that what is central to the trust differs from mandatory rules that apply only to some trusts for policy reasons. The Business Trusts (Amendment) Act 2022 added further corporate-style rules: a register of beneficial owners, more disclosure of conflicts and stronger unitholder rights."},
    {t:"Back towards the traditional English split of legal and equitable interests in each asset", correct:false,
     why:"The movement is away from the traditional English model, which UK courts still hold to (Investec v Glenalla)."},
    {t:"Towards abolishing business trusts in favour of VCCs", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in the text suggests abolition. The 2022 amendments strengthen the business trust regime."},
    {t:"Towards treating unitholders as general partners", correct:false,
     why:"Unitholders are moving closer to shareholders, holding rights to due administration rather than interests in assets, not toward partners with unlimited liability."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 3 [03.040]–[03.042], fn 156–157, 162–165", takeaway:"SG trusts drift towards US entity-style funds; 2022 amendments add corporate rules." }

]);

;
