  "why": "The text treats the company as having two faces. Externally, where the company deals with third parties as a separate entity, company law works like organisational and property law: it tells outsiders which assets they can look to and who can bind the company.\n\nInternally, many rules are contractual, with terms that are flexible and negotiated among the constituencies interested in the company's proper administration. In theory, then, it is for those constituents to decide what purposes the company and its directors pursue. In practice the terms are fairly standardised and shareholder primacy remains the starting point.\n\nThe book's own position is that legal personality is conferred by the State as a product of company law, with the State conscious of the constituents' needs when it sets mandatory rules. It recommends an 'integrative approach' that keeps both faces in view, rather than reducing the company to either property or contract.",
