    "why": "Schwarcz accepts that even flexible US commercial trusts work only in businesses with lower risk-reward ratios, because of the duty of impartiality, which requires trustees to balance the interests of income and capital beneficiaries. A trustee taking big, long-term bets risks favouring one class over the other.\n\nDirectors, by contrast, owe their duty to the company rather than any particular constituency, so they can take greater risks focused on the longer term. There is no duty to pay dividends even when the company makes profits, so all earnings can be retained. Many alternative vehicles are built to deliver a steady income stream, and some of their tax advantages depend on paying out most of their cash. A long-horizon, reinvest-everything biotech is the opposite profile."
