/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 4 · Lesson 4F Promoters (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 4 [04.048]–[04.060], fns 206–234. (Lee's fns give Gluckstein v Barnes as "[1990] AC 240"; the report is [1900] AC 240.) */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl4-055", lesson:"4F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why are the common law rules on promoters of limited importance today?",
  options:[
    {t:"Prospectus regulation and near-instant incorporation", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.048] traces the rules to 19th-century England, where fraudulent promoters formed new companies, sold them property at inflated values, and then invited the public to subscribe. Equity responded by making promoters fiduciaries who had to disclose their profits, on pain of an account. Three developments have since reduced the rules' importance. Prospectus disclosure for public offers is now 'vastly more extensive' and backed by a range of sanctions under the Securities and Futures Act. In private companies the common law still applies, but its scope is 'slender at best', because the founders usually know all about the formation transactions. And since a company can be incorporated within a day or bought off the shelf, there is little need for anyone to contract before incorporation."},
    {t:"Promoters were abolished by statute", correct:false,
     why:"The Companies Act still uses the term in several places (eg ss 11(3), 154(9), 155(1)), though it does not define it (fn 207). Promoters still exist and still owe fiduciary duties; what has fallen is the practical importance of the common law rules."},
    {t:"Courts no longer treat promoters as fiduciaries", correct:false,
     why:"Promoters remain fiduciaries ([04.051]). Their duties are less often litigated, not abolished."},
    {t:"All companies must now be listed", correct:false,
     why:"The great majority of companies are private and unlisted. The common law rules still apply to them, though with limited practical scope."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.048], fns 206–207", takeaway:"Promoter law: displaced by prospectus rules and fast incorporation." },

{ id:"cl4-044", lesson:"4F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Who is a promoter?",
  options:[
    {t:"One who forms and launches a company for a project", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.049] adopts Cockburn CJ's description in Twycross v Grant: a promoter is someone 'who undertakes to form a company with reference to a given project and to set it going, and who takes the necessary steps to accomplish that purpose'. Bowen J in Whaley Bridge Calico Printing v Green called 'promoter' a term 'not of law, but of business' (fn 207). A rigid definition would be impractical and could let promoters escape liability. More than mere registration is required; purely ministerial acts and acts done only in a professional capacity, such as giving legal or financial advice, are insufficient. But a person need not do all the work to qualify, those who actively raise capital will find it hard to deny being promoters, and even an agent can be one if he actively participated (Lydney and Wigpool Iron Ore v Bird)."},
    {t:"Anyone named in the incorporation form", correct:false,
     why:"Being named in the form, for example as a first director or subscriber, is evidence of involvement but does not decide the question. Promoter status turns on what the person actually did to launch the company."},
    {t:"Only the company's first directors", correct:false,
     why:"Promoters are often directors, which makes this plausible, but they need not be, and a first director who did nothing to launch the company is not thereby a promoter."},
    {t:"Anyone falling within the Companies Act's definition of promoter", correct:false,
     why:"The Companies Act uses the term but does not define it. The Securities and Futures Act does define it, but narrowly, as those who took part in preparing a prospectus (ss 239(1), 282A(1), fn 207)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.049], fns 207–211", takeaway:"Promoter = forms and launches the company (fact, not law)." },

{ id:"cl4-045", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A lawyer drafts a new company's constitution and advises on its structure for a fee. Separately, Wei finds investors and negotiates their subscriptions, but plays no other part.",
  prompt:"Who is likely to be treated as a promoter?",
  options:[
    {t:"Wei, not the lawyer", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.049] says acts carried out only in a professional capacity, such as rendering legal or financial advice, 'are clearly insufficient', so the lawyer is not a promoter merely for drafting and advising. Wei is in the opposite position. He has not done everything needed to launch the company, but that is not required, and 'those who have actively participated in raising capital for the company will generally find it difficult to disclaim responsibility as promoters even if they were not involved in other aspects of the company's promotion'. Raising capital is at the heart of setting a company going."},
    {t:"The lawyer, not Wei", correct:false,
     why:"The lawyer's work is closely connected to formation, which makes this tempting. But professional services alone do not make someone a promoter. If the lawyer went further, for example by finding investors or taking a stake in the project, the answer could change."},
    {t:"Both of them", correct:false,
     why:"The lawyer acted purely in a professional capacity, which the chapter treats as insufficient."},
    {t:"Neither, because neither did all the work", correct:false,
     why:"A promoter need not carry out all the work necessary to get a company going (Emma Silver Mining v Lewis, fn 210)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.049], fn 210", takeaway:"Raising capital → promoter. Professional advice alone → not." },

{ id:"c4x-053", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Kumar is employed by an investment syndicate. Acting on its instructions, he identifies a mine to buy, negotiates its purchase, arranges the incorporation of a company to acquire it and personally persuades investors to subscribe. When the company later sues him over undisclosed profits, he argues that he was merely the syndicate's agent and so cannot be a promoter.",
  prompt:"Is the argument likely to succeed?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: active agents can be promoters", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.049] states that 'even a person acting as an agent may be liable as a promoter if he had actively participated in the company's promotion', citing Lydney and Wigpool Iron Ore Co v Bird. Promoter status is a question of fact about what the person did, not about the capacity in which he did it. Kumar did far more than ministerial or professional tasks: he identified and negotiated the project, arranged the incorporation and raised capital. That is a textbook case of undertaking to form a company for a given project and setting it going (Twycross v Grant). Being an agent may give him rights against the syndicate, but it does not shield him from the company."},
    {t:"Yes: only principals can be promoters", correct:false,
     why:"This would let syndicates insulate themselves by acting through employees, which is the kind of avoidance Lee warns a rigid definition would allow."},
    {t:"Yes, because he received no personal profit", correct:false,
     why:"Whether he is a promoter is a separate question from what remedy is available. Status depends on participation, not on whether he personally profited."},
    {t:"No, but only because he was also a director", correct:false,
     why:"The facts do not say he was a director, and it does not matter. Active participation in promotion is enough."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.049], fns 209, 211", takeaway:"An agent who actively promotes can be a promoter (Lydney & Wigpool)." },

{ id:"cl4-046", lesson:"4F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When does promotion begin and end?",
  options:[
    {t:"From intention plus an overt step until the business is launched", correct:true,
     why:"Because the concept of a promoter is not reducible to a clear definition, Lee [04.050] says the start of promotion 'cannot be answered with absolute certainty'. On the whole, a person becomes responsible as a promoter from the time he forms the intention to promote a company and takes an active or overt step towards it. Intention alone is not enough. Promotion does not necessarily end on incorporation: it ends when all the activities designed to start the company's business have been carried out, which is frequently on completion of the fundraising (Emma Silver Mining v Lewis). The timing matters for remedies, because an asset acquired before promotion began is the promoter's own, while one acquired during promotion may be held for the company ([04.057]–[04.058])."},
    {t:"It begins on incorporation and ends at the first AGM", correct:false,
     why:"Promotion usually starts before the company exists; that is why the promoter is described as a fiduciary of a company not yet formed ([04.051]). Its end is tied to launching the business, not to the AGM."},
    {t:"It begins when the name is reserved and ends on incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"Name reservation can be an overt step, but it is not the test, and promotion often continues after incorporation while capital is raised."},
    {t:"It lasts for exactly one year", correct:false,
     why:"No fixed period exists. The start and end are questions of fact."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.050], fns 212–213", takeaway:"Promotion: intention + overt step → business launched (often post-fundraising)." },

{ id:"cl4-047", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A syndicate buys an island and, through a nominee, sells it to a company the syndicate has formed. A board made up largely of the syndicate's nominees approves the purchase, and shares are then sold to the public. Months later, new directors discover the island was sold to the company at an overvalue.",
  prompt:"What did the House of Lords decide in Erlanger v New Sombrero Phosphate?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company could rescind for non-disclosure", correct:true,
     why:"Lord Cairns explained that promoters 'have in their hands the creation and moulding of the company' ([04.051]). To guard against abuse, the law makes the promoter a fiduciary of the company even though it did not exist when the relationship began, as an extension of agency or trust principles (Lydney and Wigpool). As a fiduciary, the promoter must act in good faith, avoid conflicts between interest and duty, and make no profit from his position without full disclosure; a contract made without adequate disclosure can be rescinded ([04.052]). In Erlanger, the sale was ratified by a board of E's 'puppets', so there was no real disclosure. About eight months after the public subscribed, the shareholders replaced the board, and the new board successfully sued to set the contract aside. Lindley LJ later explained in Lagunas Nitrate that it was the failure to disclose that the promoters were themselves the vendors that vitiated the contract (fn 218)."},
    {t:"The contract stood, because the board had approved it", correct:false,
     why:"Formal board approval is usually enough to authorise a company's contract, which is why this is tempting. But approval by the promoters' own nominees is not disclosure to the company in any meaningful sense."},
    {t:"Only the public shareholders could sue, each personally", correct:false,
     why:"The duty was owed to the company, so the company, acting through its new board, was the proper claimant."},
    {t:"The promoters were held liable for criminal fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Erlanger was a civil case about the equitable remedy of rescission for breach of fiduciary duty."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.051]–[04.052], fns 214–216, 218", takeaway:"Erlanger: no disclosure to an independent board → rescission." },

{ id:"cl4-048", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Promoters buy a property for £140,000, together with some debentures and a mortgage over it bought at a discount. They form a company and sell it the property for £180,000. The prospectus discloses the £40,000 profit on the property, but not the further £20,000+ they make by redeeming the debentures and mortgage at face value.",
  prompt:"What did Gluckstein v Barnes decide?",
  options:[
    {t:"He accounted for the ancillary profits too", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.053] uses Gluckstein to show that 'the proscription against secret profits is not limited to the gains derived directly from the sale of a property or an asset to the company, but extends also to benefits arising from transactions that are ancillary to such a sale'. The syndicate bought the property from a company in liquidation, together with outstanding debentures and a mortgage at a discount, formed a company with themselves as directors and sold it the property. Disclosing the £40,000 on the property did not disclose the separate gain of more than £20,000 on redeeming the debentures and mortgage at face value. The House of Lords unanimously held G liable to account for the undisclosed profit. The lesson: disclosure must be complete; a partial disclosure that conceals related gains is treated as no disclosure of those gains."},
    {t:"No liability, because the main profit was disclosed", correct:false,
     why:"This assumes disclosure of the headline figure covers everything. Gluckstein holds the opposite: each profit from the promotion must be disclosed, including those from ancillary transactions."},
    {t:"Only rescission was available", correct:false,
     why:"The remedy granted was an account of the undisclosed profits. Rescission is not the only response to non-disclosure, especially where the asset was acquired as part of the promotion."},
    {t:"Liability attached only to the £40,000", correct:false,
     why:"The £40,000 was disclosed, so it was not a secret profit. Liability attached to the undisclosed ancillary gains."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.053], fn 217", takeaway:"Gluckstein: full disclosure includes ancillary profits." },

{ id:"cl4-049", lesson:"4F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"To whom must a promoter disclose an interest in a transaction with the company?",
  options:[
    {t:"It depends on whether outsiders will invest", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.054] calls this 'a matter of some difficulty'. Erlanger seemed to suggest disclosure is effective only if made to a wholly independent board, but the English Court of Appeal in Lagunas Nitrate clarified that a contract is not vitiated merely because no independent board was installed. However, where the promoters' scheme includes inviting others to invest, disclosure must reach those intended investors; confining it to the promoters and their nominees 'would be absurd' because it 'would effectively legitimise all concealment' (Gluckstein). The resulting rule: disclosure to the board is enough if membership will not go beyond the promoters; otherwise it must go to an entirely independent board or to all existing and intended shareholders, and for a company to be listed it must be in the prospectus."},
    {t:"Always and only to a wholly independent board", correct:false,
     why:"This is the reading of Erlanger that Lagunas Nitrate corrected. An independent board is one way to satisfy the duty, not the only way, and not always necessary."},
    {t:"To ACRA, by filing a disclosure form", correct:false,
     why:"Promoter disclosure is a fiduciary obligation to the company and its intended investors, not a regulatory filing."},
    {t:"No disclosure is needed if the price is fair", correct:false,
     why:"Fairness of price is no substitute for disclosure. The fiduciary rule is about informed consent, not about whether the deal was a good one."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.054], fns 218–219", takeaway:"Disclose to the board (closed company) or to independent board/investors (outside money)." },

{ id:"c4x-054", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three friends form Trio Pte Ltd and are its only shareholders and directors. They have no plans to bring in anyone else. One of them sells Trio a van she owns, at a profit, after telling the other two at a board meeting exactly what she paid for it and what she stands to make.",
  prompt:"Has she discharged her duty of disclosure as a promoter?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: board disclosure suffices in a closed company", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.054] concludes that 'disclosure to the company's board is sufficient where there is no intention to enlarge the company's membership beyond the promoters'. The concern in Erlanger and Gluckstein was concealment from outside investors who were being invited to subscribe. Here there are no outsiders, actual or intended: the board consists of all the shareholders, and each knows the full facts. An independent board is not required (Lagunas Nitrate). The position would change if the friends later planned to raise money from new investors on the strength of the company's assets; disclosure would then need to reach those investors."},
    {t:"No: disclosure must always be made to a wholly independent board", correct:false,
     why:"This applies the strict reading of Erlanger that later authority rejected. In a closed company of informed founders, insisting on an independent board would serve no purpose."},
    {t:"No: a promoter may never profit from a sale to the company", correct:false,
     why:"A promoter may transact with and benefit from the company if full disclosure is made ([04.054]). The prohibition is on undisclosed profit."},
    {t:"Yes, but only because a van is a small asset", correct:false,
     why:"The size of the asset is irrelevant. The disclosure is sufficient because of who received it and the absence of any plan to bring in outsiders."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.054], fn 218", takeaway:"Closed company of informed founders: disclosure to the board suffices." },

{ id:"c4x-055", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The same three founders of Trio Pte Ltd now plan to sell 40% of Trio to angel investors. Before that, one founder sells Trio a warehouse at a large profit, disclosing her profit only to her two co-founders at a board meeting. The investment memorandum later sent to the angels does not mention it.",
  prompt:"Is her disclosure sufficient?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the intended investors had to be told", correct:true,
     why:"The change from the previous scenario is the plan to bring in outsiders ([04.054]). Lee explains that where it is part of the promoters' scheme to invite others to invest, 'the relevant disclosure [must] be extended to such intended investors'. Disclosing only to the promoters themselves 'would be absurd, as it would effectively legitimise all concealment of the promoters' interests'. Since the warehouse sale happened as part of a scheme that included the angel round, the founder needed to disclose to the intended investors, for example in the investment memorandum, or to an entirely independent board. Without that, Trio may rescind the purchase or, if she acquired the warehouse as a fiduciary during promotion, seek an account of her profit ([04.056]–[04.058])."},
    {t:"Yes: the board was told, and disclosure to the board is enough", correct:false,
     why:"Board disclosure is enough only where membership will not grow beyond the promoters. Once outside investors are part of the plan, it is not."},
    {t:"Yes, because the angels are sophisticated investors who should do their own diligence", correct:false,
     why:"The fiduciary duty does not depend on investor sophistication. The promoter cannot shift her disclosure obligation onto the investors' diligence."},
    {t:"Yes, because only listed companies need a prospectus", correct:false,
     why:"A prospectus is the required channel for a company to be listed, but the underlying duty to disclose to intended investors applies whenever outsiders are to be brought in."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.054], fn 219", takeaway:"Outside investors planned → disclose to them or to an independent board." },

{ id:"c4x-058", lesson:"4F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Apart from fiduciary duties, what other liability might a promoter face?",
  options:[
    {t:"Liability in tort or deceit, and contractual or regulatory liability", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.055] notes that the chapter focuses on fiduciary duties because they impose the most exacting standard, but other principles also apply. A promoter who misrepresents the value of an asset may be liable in tort; where the misrepresentation is dishonest, an action may lie in deceit. And if the promoter is bound by specific contractual or regulatory obligations, such as prospectus requirements under the Securities and Futures Act, he must answer for breach of those too. These routes can be useful where the fiduciary remedies are limited, for example where the asset was the promoter's own and rescission has been lost."},
    {t:"None: fiduciary duties exclude all other liability", correct:false,
     why:"Fiduciary liability is additional to, not exclusive of, other liabilities. The chapter expressly says other principles may also apply."},
    {t:"Only criminal liability under the Companies Act", correct:false,
     why:"Criminal liability may exist in some cases, but the chapter's point is about civil liability in tort, deceit, contract and regulation."},
    {t:"Liability only to the public shareholders, not the company", correct:false,
     why:"Tortious and contractual claims may be available to whoever was misled or contracted, including the company. The chapter does not confine them to shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.055]", takeaway:"Promoter may also face tort/deceit and contractual or regulatory liability." },

{ id:"cl4-050", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A third-party vendor pays a promoter an undisclosed commission for steering the company into buying the vendor's asset. The deal turns out well for the company, which decides to keep the asset.",
  prompt:"Can the company still recover the commission?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: liability is strict, whether or not it rescinds", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.056] treats this as the clear case. Where a third-party vendor sells to the company and rewards the promoter for arranging the deal, the company may strip the promoter of the reward if it was not disclosed (Bagnall v Carlton). 'This liability is strict, so the fact that the transaction benefits the company is no defence.' The company may also be able to rescind against the vendor, on the ground of material misrepresentation or the vendor's notice of the promoter's breach. But the account against the promoter is independent of rescission: the company can recover the secret profit even if it affirms the contract (Emma Silver Mining v Lewis) or gives up rescission in a compromise (Bagnall). Keeping a good asset and recovering the bribe are therefore compatible."},
    {t:"No: by keeping the asset it affirmed the deal", correct:false,
     why:"Affirmation bars rescission, which is why this is tempting. It does not bar the separate claim for an account of a secret commission."},
    {t:"No: the company suffered no loss", correct:false,
     why:"An account of secret profits is gain-based, not loss-based. Liability is strict and does not depend on any loss to the company."},
    {t:"Only if it also rescinds against the vendor", correct:false,
     why:"The account is available whether or not the contract is rescinded."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.056], fns 221–224", takeaway:"Secret commission from a vendor: strict account, even if the deal is kept." },

{ id:"cl4-051", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two years before deciding to form a company, Ravi bought land for himself. He later sells it to the company he promotes at a large profit, without disclosing that he is the vendor. After discovering this, the company affirms the contract and then sues for his profit.",
  prompt:"Will the claim for his profit succeed?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: for his own asset, only rescission was available", correct:true,
     why:"Where the promoter is himself the vendor, everything turns on whether he first acquired the asset for his own account or as fiduciary for the company ([04.057]). An asset acquired before promotion began is his own. On resale his only obligation is to disclose his identity as vendor; he need not disclose the profit he expects. Failure to disclose his identity entitles the company to rescind, but rescission is subject to the usual equitable bars: affirmation or ratification, impossibility of restitution (Lagunas Nitrate) and prejudice to third-party rights (Re Leeds and Hanley Theatres). Here the company affirmed, so rescission is lost, and it 'may not seek, in the alternative, to recover the promoter's profits' (Re Cape Breton Co). Since the land was his, he may set his own price; to make him account would 'impose on the promoter a contract he has not made'."},
    {t:"Yes: promoters must always account for profits on sales to the company", correct:false,
     why:"That is the rule for assets acquired as fiduciary for the company ([04.058]), not for assets the promoter owned before promotion began."},
    {t:"Yes, but only for half the profit", correct:false,
     why:"There is no apportionment rule. The company either has rescission or, for a fiduciary acquisition, an account; here it has neither."},
    {t:"No, because promoters owe no fiduciary duties", correct:false,
     why:"Promoters are fiduciaries. The issue is the scope of the remedy for a sale of the promoter's own pre-promotion asset."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.057], fns 225–227", takeaway:"Own asset: disclose identity; remedy is rescission only (Re Cape Breton)." },

{ id:"c4x-056", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before she had any thought of forming a company, Mei bought a factory for $2m. A year later she promotes NewCo and sells it the factory for $3m. She tells NewCo's independent board, and the investors, that she is the vendor, but says nothing about what she paid or the profit she will make.",
  prompt:"Can NewCo later rescind or claim her $1m profit?",
  options:[
    {t:"Neither: she disclosed she was the vendor", correct:true,
     why:"This is the other side of the rule in [04.057]. Because Mei bought the factory before promotion began, it was acquired for her own account. When she sold it to the company, 'his only obligation at the time of the resale is to disclose his identity as vendor, but he is not obliged to disclose the profits he expects to make'. Mei disclosed that she was the vendor, to an independent board and the investors, so she did what was required. The board and investors, knowing she was on the other side, could negotiate the price or investigate what she paid. There is no breach to found rescission, and no account, because she was entitled to set the price for her own asset."},
    {t:"NewCo can claim the $1m, because promoters must disclose their profits", correct:false,
     why:"The duty to disclose profit applies where the asset was acquired as fiduciary for the company or where a third party pays the promoter. For a pre-promotion asset, disclosure of identity as vendor is enough."},
    {t:"NewCo can rescind, because the price was above what she paid", correct:false,
     why:"An overvalue alone is not a ground for rescission against a fiduciary who disclosed her identity. Any claim would have to rest on misrepresentation or another ground."},
    {t:"NewCo can rescind and claim the profit together", correct:false,
     why:"Even where the promoter does breach, for a pre-promotion asset the company's remedy is rescission only (Re Cape Breton). And here there was no breach."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.057]", takeaway:"Pre-promotion asset: disclose that you're the vendor; profit need not be disclosed." },

{ id:"c4x-057", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before promotion began, Leon bought a ship for himself. He then sold it to the company he promoted without disclosing he was the vendor. By the time the company found out, it had sold the ship on to an unrelated buyer, who cannot be made to give it back.",
  prompt:"What can the company recover from Leon?",
  options:[
    {t:"Nothing: rescission is barred and no account replaces it", correct:true,
     why:"Leon breached his duty by not disclosing that he was the vendor, so the company had a right to rescind ([04.057]). But rescission is limited by the usual equitable constraints. It may be lost 'where restitution is no longer possible' (Lagunas Nitrate) or 'where unwinding the contract would adversely affect third party rights' (Re Leeds and Hanley Theatres). The ship has been sold to an unrelated buyer who cannot be made to return it, so both bars apply. For an asset the promoter acquired for his own account, 'the company may not seek, in the alternative, to recover the promoter's profits' (Re Cape Breton; Ladywell Mining v Brookes; Re Lady Forest). The company's only remaining routes would be other causes of action, such as misrepresentation or deceit if Leon made false statements ([04.055])."},
    {t:"An account of his profit, because rescission is no longer possible", correct:false,
     why:"This is the intuitive substitute remedy, but the cases reject it for pre-promotion assets: an account would impose on him a contract he did not make."},
    {t:"Rescission, with the company paying the buyer damages", correct:false,
     why:"Rescission requires the parties to be restored to their original positions. Where the asset has passed to a third party who cannot be made to return it, rescission is barred."},
    {t:"Both rescission and an account, because his breach was deliberate", correct:false,
     why:"Deliberateness does not expand the remedies for a pre-promotion asset. The rule in Re Cape Breton applies regardless."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.055], [04.057], fns 225–227", takeaway:"Own asset + rescission barred → no fallback account." },

{ id:"cl4-052", lesson:"4F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A promoter acquires an asset as fiduciary for the company and resells it to the company at a concealed profit. What can the company recover?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rescission, or an account of the profit net of expenses", correct:true,
     why:"Where the promoter acquired the asset as agent or fiduciary for the company, 'the asset prima facie belonged to the company', and he may not profit from dealing with it except with the company's fully informed consent ([04.058]). If he conceals his interest, the company may rescind or seek an account of the secret profit (Gluckstein v Barnes). Because the asset was acquired for the company, the company is entitled to it at the original purchase price, so the profit is the difference between what he paid and what he charged the company. He may deduct expenses legitimately incurred in connection with the promotion (Lydney and Wigpool). Whether an asset was acquired for the company or for himself is a question of fact; buying after promotion has begun does not automatically make it the company's ([04.057])."},
    {t:"Only rescission", correct:false,
     why:"Rescission-only is the rule for the promoter's own pre-promotion asset. For an asset acquired as fiduciary, an account is also available."},
    {t:"The full resale price, with no deductions", correct:false,
     why:"The company is entitled to the asset at cost, so it recovers the profit, not the whole price, and legitimate promotion expenses may be deducted."},
    {t:"Nothing, if the resale price was market value", correct:false,
     why:"Market value is irrelevant: the asset was the company's at its original cost, and the promoter's profit is a secret profit made from his fiduciary position."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.058], fns 228–229", takeaway:"Asset held for the company: account = resale − cost − legit expenses." },

{ id:"c4x-060", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During promotion of Aqua Ltd, and as part of the plan for its business, promoter Sara buys a water licence for $100,000 in her own name. She sells it to Aqua for $400,000 without disclosing her interest. She spent $20,000 of her own money on legal and registration costs of forming Aqua. Aqua wants to keep the licence.",
  prompt:"Assuming the court finds Sara acquired the licence as fiduciary for Aqua, what can Aqua recover?",
  options:[
    {t:"$280,000", correct:true,
     why:"If the licence was acquired as fiduciary for the company, it prima facie belonged to Aqua, which is entitled to it at Sara's cost of $100,000 ([04.058]). Because Sara concealed her interest, Aqua may either rescind or keep the licence and seek an account of the secret profit. Aqua wants to keep it, so it claims the account. The profit is the difference between her purchase price and the resale price: $400,000 − $100,000 = $300,000. She may deduct 'expenses legitimately incurred in connection with the company's promotion' (Lydney and Wigpool), here $20,000. So Aqua recovers $280,000. The first step, whether she acquired it for Aqua or for herself, is a question of fact; buying during promotion and as part of the plan supports the fiduciary finding."},
    {t:"$300,000, with no deduction", correct:false,
     why:"This ignores the allowance for legitimate promotion expenses. Note too that a promoter has no general right to be paid expenses ([04.059]); the deduction operates only to reduce the profit he must account for."},
    {t:"$400,000, the full resale price", correct:false,
     why:"Aqua is keeping the licence, so it cannot also recover the whole price. The account strips the profit, not the price."},
    {t:"Nothing, because Aqua chose to keep the licence", correct:false,
     why:"Keeping the asset is affirming the purchase, which bars rescission but not an account where the asset was acquired as fiduciary."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.057]–[04.059], fns 228–230", takeaway:"Fiduciary acquisition: keep asset + account for (resale − cost − legit expenses)." },

{ id:"cl4-053", lesson:"4F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A promoter is entitled to reasonable remuneration and reimbursement of incorporation expenses even without a contract.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [04.059] states that 'the promoter is not entitled to any remuneration for his services unless the company has agreed to such remuneration by way of a binding contract', and that he 'may not even recover the incorporation expenses unless such recovery is contractually provided for' (Re English and Colonial Produce Co). The traditional way promoters were rewarded was therefore the profit on selling an asset or business to the company, or on a transaction ancillary to the sale. That reward can be stripped from him if his interests were not fully disclosed. In practice, a promoter who wants fees or expenses must ensure the company enters into a binding contract to pay them once it is incorporated, for example by ratifying a pre-incorporation agreement under s 41."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"A claim for a reasonable fee on a quantum meruit basis might seem fair, but the cases deny a promoter remuneration or expenses without a binding contract with the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.059], fn 230", takeaway:"No contract, no pay, not even expenses." },

{ id:"cl4-054", lesson:"4F", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each authority to its rule on pre-incorporation contracts.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Kelner v Baxter", r:"Promoter signed as agent: personally liable"},
    {l:"Newborne v Sensolid", r:"Signature merely authenticated the company's: neither liable"},
    {l:"s 41(1) CA", r:"The company may ratify the contract once incorporated"},
    {l:"s 41(2) CA", r:"Until ratification, the person who contracted is personally bound"}
  ],
  why:"At common law a company is not bound by a contract made before it existed, and it cannot ratify one, because there was no principal at the time ([04.060]). Whether the promoter was liable instead turned on 'the uneasy distinction' between signing as agent, which made him personally liable (Kelner v Baxter), and signing merely to authenticate the company's signature, in which case neither he nor the company was liable (Newborne v Sensolid). The second result could leave the other party with no one to sue. Section 41 largely removes the problem: s 41(1) lets the company ratify the contract once incorporated, and s 41(2) provides that until ratification the person who purported to contract in the company's name or on its behalf is personally bound. Chapter 7 returns to this at [07.048]. Because companies can now be incorporated within a day or bought off the shelf, pre-incorporation contracts are less common than they were ([04.048]).",
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.048], [04.060], fns 231–234", takeaway:"Kelner liable · Newborne neither · s 41: ratify; until then personally bound." },

{ id:"c4x-059", lesson:"4F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before Bloom Pte Ltd is incorporated, its founder signs a lease 'for and on behalf of Bloom Pte Ltd (in formation)'. Bloom is incorporated two weeks later, but its board never ratifies the lease. The landlord sues the founder for unpaid rent; the founder says the lease was Bloom's, not his.",
  prompt:"Who is liable to the landlord?",
  options:[
    {t:"The founder, under s 41(2)", correct:true,
     why:"Section 41 governs ([04.060]). Bloom could have ratified the lease after incorporation under s 41(1), which would have made it bound. It never did. Under s 41(2), until ratification, the person who purported to contract in the name of or on behalf of the company is personally bound by the contract. The founder signed 'for and on behalf of' Bloom in formation, so he is bound. Under the old common law the result might have depended on whether he signed as agent (Kelner v Baxter, liable) or merely authenticated the company's signature (Newborne v Sensolid, not liable), but s 41 removes the need for that distinction. To protect himself, the founder should have Bloom's board ratify the lease."},
    {t:"Bloom, because it was incorporated two weeks later", correct:false,
     why:"Incorporation alone does not bind a company to a pre-incorporation contract. It must ratify under s 41(1)."},
    {t:"Neither, as in Newborne v Sensolid", correct:false,
     why:"Newborne reflects the old common law, under which an 'authentication' signature could leave nobody liable. Section 41(2) now makes the person who purported to contract for the company personally bound until ratification."},
    {t:"Both jointly, because the lease named Bloom", correct:false,
     why:"Bloom is not bound until it ratifies. Until then the founder is the only person bound."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 4 [04.060], fns 231–234", takeaway:"Pre-incorporation contract, no ratification → signatory personally bound (s 41(2))." }

]);

;
