{ id:"cl5-006", lesson:"5B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which of these is NOT something a constitution is required to state?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company's objects", correct:true,
     why:"Under s 23(1), a company has full capacity to carry on or undertake any business or activity, do any act or enter into any transaction, so it is not required to state its objects ([05.006]). The matters formerly required in the memorandum now go in the constitution under s 22(1) ([05.005]): the company's name; the liability statement appropriate to its type (limited by shares, limited by guarantee with the specified contribution, or unlimited); for an unlimited or guarantee company, the number of members at the time of applying for registration; the subscribers' full names, addresses and occupations; and a statement that the subscribers wish to form a company on the terms of the constitution and, if it has a share capital, agree to subscribe for a stated number of shares."},
    {t:"The company's name", correct:false,
     why:"The name is the first item of prescribed content in s 22(1). A company must have a reserved and approved name before it can be registered (Ch 4)."},
    {t:"For a company limited by shares, a statement that members' liability is limited", correct:false,
     why:"This is prescribed content. Third parties dealing with the company rely on it to know that members are not personally liable beyond their shares."},
    {t:"The full names, addresses and occupations of the subscribers", correct:false,
     why:"This is prescribed content, alongside the subscribers' statement that they wish to form the company and, where there is share capital, take a stated number of shares."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.005]–[05.006], fns 12–13", takeaway:"Objects are optional. Name, liability statement, subscribers are mandatory." },

{ id:"cl5-007", lesson:"5B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each type of company to the statement its constitution must contain.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Company limited by shares", r:"Members' liability is limited"},
    {l:"Company limited by guarantee", r:"Members will contribute up to a specified amount on winding up"},
    {l:"Unlimited company", r:"Members' liability is unlimited"},
    {l:"Unlimited or guarantee company", r:"Number of members when applying for registration"}
  ],
  why:"These items were previously required in the memorandum and are now prescribed for the constitution by s 22(1) ([05.005]). Each statement tells outsiders what they can expect from members if the company fails: for a company limited by shares, nothing beyond the unpaid amount on the shares; for a guarantee company, a contribution not exceeding the specified amount on winding up; for an unlimited company, unlimited contribution. Unlimited and guarantee companies must also state the number of their members at the time of the application for registration. Every constitution additionally states the company's name, the subscribers' full names, addresses and occupations, and their wish to form the company on its terms and, where there is a share capital, to subscribe for a stated number of shares.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.005], fn 12", takeaway:"Liability statement follows company type." },

{ id:"cl5-008", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Investors in a new company agreed to invest only because it would run a single logistics business. They want that limit built into the company's governing document.",
  prompt:"Can the company do this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it may state objects and restrict its capacity", correct:true,
     why:"Full capacity under s 23(1) is the default, not a mandatory rule. Section 23(1A) allows a company to state its objects, and s 23(1B) allows the constitution to restrict its capacities, rights, powers and privileges ([05.006]). Lee uses this very example: shareholders who invested on the basis that the company would only undertake a specified type of business may insist that the object be stated in the constitution. A company with objects in its constitution must also register the constitution even if it otherwise adopts a model constitution in full (s 37(4), [05.010]), and altering an objects clause later attracts extra procedural protections ([05.045])."},
    {t:"No: a company has full capacity, which cannot be restricted", correct:false,
     why:"Full capacity is the starting point, which is why this is tempting. But s 23(1B) expressly permits restrictions in the constitution."},
    {t:"Only by a separate shareholders' agreement", correct:false,
     why:"A shareholders' agreement is a possible route, but it binds only the parties to it. Putting the objects in the constitution binds the company and every member, including future ones."},
    {t:"Only if the company is a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Any company may state objects and restrict its capacity. Nothing in s 23 limits this to public companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.006], [05.010], fns 13–15, 28", takeaway:"Objects are optional but allowed, with capacity restrictions." },

{ id:"c5x-010", lesson:"5B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does the Companies Act require of a company's internal regulations?",
  options:[
    {t:"They must exist; their content is largely the company's choice", correct:true,
     why:"Section 35(1) requires every company to adopt in its constitution regulations governing its internal affairs ([05.007]). Within the limits of the Companies Act and relevant common law principles, the company is free to decide what those regulations are. In practice they make up most of the constitution and cover a wide range of matters. Lee lists the critical ones: the issue, allotment and transfer of shares; the organisation and conduct of board and shareholder meetings; the appointment and removal of directors and their powers and duties; the distribution and capitalisation of profits; and the distribution of surplus assets on a winding up."},
    {t:"Internal regulations are optional; only the prescribed content is required", correct:false,
     why:"The prescribed content under s 22(1) is about the company's identity and liability. Internal regulations are separately required by s 35(1)."},
    {t:"The regulations must follow the model constitution word for word", correct:false,
     why:"A company may adopt a model constitution in whole or in part, or draft its own ([05.010]). There is no obligation to follow the model, and public companies have no model at all."},
    {t:"The regulations are fixed by ACRA for each type of company", correct:false,
     why:"ACRA registers the constitution, but the content of internal regulations is for the company, subject to the Act and the common law."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.007], fn 16", takeaway:"s 35(1): internal regulations mandatory; content largely the company's choice." },

{ id:"cl5-009", lesson:"5B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under the old regime, what happened if a company registered its own articles without excluding or modifying Table A?",
  options:[
    {t:"Table A was presumed adopted", correct:true,
     why:"Table A in the Fourth Schedule to the old Act was a default set of articles ([05.008]). A company could adopt all or any part of it (old s 36(1)), and in practice most companies excluded it and adopted their own. But under old s 36(2), if a company failed to register its own articles, or registered articles without excluding or modifying Table A, it was presumed to have adopted Table A's regulations so far as applicable. The consequence survives today: for a pre-2016 company that has not converted, those Table A articles, whether expressly registered or deemed, are treated as its regulations under the current Act (s 35(3))."},
    {t:"Its own articles were void", correct:false,
     why:"Its own articles remained effective. Table A applied alongside them, so far as applicable, because the company had not excluded it."},
    {t:"The Registrar refused registration", correct:false,
     why:"Refusal is how the current regime handles a missing constitution ([05.011]). The old regime used a presumption of adoption instead."},
    {t:"The new model constitution applied", correct:false,
     why:"Model constitutions replaced Table A only after the 2014 amendments, and they have no gap-filling role. A pre-2016 company's position is governed by Table A."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.008], fns 18–21", takeaway:"Table A: deemed adopted unless excluded or modified." },

{ id:"c5x-009", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Cedar Pte Ltd was incorporated in 2005 and registered short articles of its own dealing only with share transfers. It did not exclude Table A, and it has never adopted a new-style constitution. In 2026 a question arises about how its board meetings must be convened, which its own articles do not address.",
  prompt:"Where should you look?",
  options:[
    {t:"To Table A, deemed adopted and still in force for Cedar today", correct:true,
     why:"Cedar registered articles without excluding or modifying Table A, so under the old s 36(2) it was presumed to have adopted Table A's regulations so far as applicable ([05.008]). Because it has not replaced its memorandum and articles with a new-style constitution, s 35(3) of the current Act treats those Table A articles as its regulations. Table A's provisions on board meetings therefore fill the gap in Cedar's short articles. This is a gap-filling result, but it comes from the old Table A presumption carried forward, not from the new model constitutions, which have no gap-filling role ([05.011])."},
    {t:"To the current model constitution for private companies", correct:false,
     why:"Model constitutions apply only if a company adopts them, and they have no gap-filling role. Cedar's gap is filled by Table A, not by the model."},
    {t:"Nowhere: the board may convene meetings however it likes", correct:false,
     why:"Cedar's constitution is not silent once Table A is taken into account. The deemed Table A regulations govern board meetings."},
    {t:"To the Registrar, who will prescribe a procedure", correct:false,
     why:"The Registrar does not supply internal regulations. The answer is found in Cedar's own (deemed) regulations."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.008], [05.011], fns 20–21", takeaway:"Pre-2016 co that didn't exclude Table A: Table A still fills gaps (s 35(3))." },

{ id:"cl5-013", lesson:"5B", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Since the 2014 amendments, Table A has been replaced by ____ constitutions prescribed by the Minister under s 36(1).",
  accept:["model","the model"], answer:"model",
  why:"The reform followed the UK, where Table A was replaced by model articles to make the default articles more user-friendly, current and relevant ([05.009]). Unlike Table A, Singapore's model constitutions are not scheduled to the Act; they are prescribed by subsidiary legislation, the Companies (Model Constitutions) Regulations 2015, and posted on ACRA's website for public access (fn 22). Because they sit in regulations, they can be updated more easily, which matters for companies that adopt the model 'as amended from time to time' ([05.010]).",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.009], fn 22", takeaway:"Table A → model constitutions (s 36(1)), in regulations not a schedule." },

{ id:"cl5-010", lesson:"5B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which companies do the model constitutions prescribed under s 36(1) CA cover?",
  options:[
    {t:"Private companies and guarantee companies", correct:true,
     why:"The UK reform produced separate model articles for private companies, public companies and companies limited by guarantee ([05.009]). Singapore's Steering Committee for the Review of the Companies Act took a different view on public companies: given the complex and diverse operations of such companies, many of which are listed, it would not be useful to prescribe a standard constitution for them. So the Singapore model constitutions apply only to private companies and companies limited by guarantee. A public company limited by shares must draft its own constitution."},
    {t:"All companies, including listed public companies", correct:false,
     why:"This would mirror the UK approach, which is why it is tempting, but Singapore deliberately did not prescribe a model for public companies."},
    {t:"Public companies only", correct:false,
     why:"Public companies limited by shares are exactly the ones left out. (Guarantee companies are public companies too, but they have their own model.)"},
    {t:"Foreign companies registering in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Model constitutions are for companies incorporated under the Act. Registered foreign companies keep their own constitutional documents under their home law (Ch 4)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.009], fns 24–25", takeaway:"Model constitutions: private companies + CLGs. None for public companies." },

{ id:"c5x-006", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The founders of Summit Ltd, a new public company limited by shares planning to list in three years, want to save legal costs by simply adopting 'the model constitution' at incorporation.",
  prompt:"What is the difficulty?",
  options:[
    {t:"No model exists for public companies limited by shares", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.009] explains that the Steering Committee considered a standard constitution for public companies would not be useful given their complex and diverse operations, many being listed. The s 36(1) models therefore apply only to private companies and companies limited by guarantee. Summit is a public company limited by shares, so there is no model for it to adopt. It must draft and register its own constitution, which must contain the prescribed content in s 22(1) and internal regulations under s 35(1). Since there is no gap-filling model ([05.011]), the drafting needs to be complete."},
    {t:"None: any company may adopt any model constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Model constitutions are tailored to company types, and none exists for a public company limited by shares."},
    {t:"It may adopt the private company model and convert it later", correct:false,
     why:"The private company model is designed for private companies, including the transfer restriction and member limit that define them. Adopting it would be inconsistent with Summit's status as a public company."},
    {t:"It must adopt the guarantee company model because it is a public company", correct:false,
     why:"Guarantee companies are public, but Summit is limited by shares. The guarantee model assumes no share capital and would not fit."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.009], fn 25", takeaway:"Public co limited by shares: no model; draft your own." },

{ id:"cl5-011", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Founders adopt the relevant model constitution in its entirety, with no additional provisions and no objects clause.",
  prompt:"What must they lodge, and what choice do they have?",
  options:[
    {t:"Cite its title; choose fixed or as-amended version", correct:true,
     why:"A company may adopt the relevant model constitution in whole or in part (s 37(1), [05.010]). If it adopts the model in its entirety, it need only refer to the model's title (s 37(2)), and it need not submit the constitution to the Registrar for registration unless it has added provisions or included objects (s 37(4)). It may also choose between the model as in force at the time of adoption and the model as amended from time to time (s 37(3)). The rolling option keeps the constitution up to date, but it can bring in undiscovered and perhaps unwanted amendments. Lee's advice is that, either way, the company should keep abreast of changes to company regulations."},
    {t:"They must lodge the full text with the Registrar", correct:false,
     why:"Full text is required only where the company adds provisions to the model or includes an objects clause (s 37(4))."},
    {t:"Nothing at all: the model applies automatically", correct:false,
     why:"The model is not applied by default ([05.011]). The company must expressly adopt it, which it does by referring to its title."},
    {t:"They must adopt the version in force at incorporation and cannot opt for updates", correct:false,
     why:"Section 37(3) gives them the choice between the fixed version and the version as amended from time to time."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.010], fns 26–29", takeaway:"Full adoption: cite the title; choose fixed or rolling version." },

{ id:"c5x-008", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The founders of Fern Pte Ltd adopt the private company model constitution but add one clause giving the founding shareholder a right of first refusal on all share transfers.",
  prompt:"Must Fern register its constitution with the Registrar?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: any added provision requires registration", correct:true,
     why:"The exemption from registration in s 37(4) is narrow ([05.010]). A company adopting the model in its entirety need only refer to its title and need not submit it, 'unless it has adopted provisions additional to those in the model constitution, or has included object clauses in its constitution'. Fern has added a right of first refusal, which is an additional provision. The Registrar and anyone searching the register need to see the actual terms, since the model's title alone would not reveal the added clause. Fern must therefore submit its constitution for registration."},
    {t:"No: it has adopted the model, so citing the title is enough", correct:false,
     why:"Citing the title is enough only for an unmodified adoption. Once a provision is added, the constitution is no longer simply the model."},
    {t:"No, provided the additional clause concerns share transfers", correct:false,
     why:"There is no subject-matter exception. Any additional provision takes the company outside the exemption."},
    {t:"Only if the clause is later challenged by a shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Registration is a requirement at adoption, not a response to a later dispute."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.010], fn 28", takeaway:"Model + any extra clause or objects → must register the constitution." },

{ id:"c5x-007", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Birch Pte Ltd adopted the private company model constitution 'as amended from time to time'. Three years later the Minister amends the model to change the default quorum for general meetings. Birch's directors were unaware of the change and called a meeting under the old quorum rule.",
  prompt:"What is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The amendment applies automatically; that is the rolling-option risk", correct:true,
     why:"Section 37(3) lets a company adopt either the model in force at adoption or the model in force as amended from time to time ([05.010]). Birch chose the rolling option. Its advantage is that the constitution is always current; its drawback, as Lee notes, is that it 'may also result in undiscovered (and perhaps undesired) amendments to its constitution'. Birch's constitution therefore now contains the amended quorum rule, whether or not its directors noticed. A meeting called on the old rule risks being inquorate. Lee's practical advice is that a company should 'vigilantly keep abreast with all changes to company regulations'."},
    {t:"The old rule applies until Birch passes a special resolution adopting the amendment", correct:false,
     why:"That would be the position if Birch had adopted the model as in force at the time of adoption. By choosing the rolling option, Birch accepted future amendments automatically."},
    {t:"The amendment is void as against Birch because shareholders did not approve it", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders approved the mechanism when Birch adopted the model 'as amended from time to time'. No further approval is needed for each amendment."},
    {t:"The Registrar must notify Birch personally before the change takes effect", correct:false,
     why:"No such notice requirement appears in the chapter. The burden of keeping up with amendments falls on the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.010], fn 29", takeaway:"Rolling model adoption: amendments flow in automatically, noticed or not." },

{ id:"cl5-012", lesson:"5B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: If a company's own constitution is silent on a matter, the relevant model constitution fills the gap.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.011] notes that, unlike the previous regime, the current Act does not give the model constitutions a gap-filling role. There is no presumption that a company which fails to register any constitution has adopted the model, because under s 19(1) a company cannot be incorporated without either submitting its own constitution or expressly adopting the relevant model; the Registrar must decline to register it otherwise. Lee observes that a more limited gap-filling role, for incomplete constitutions, could have been kept, as it was in the UK (Companies Act 2006 s 20(1)), but it 'appears not to have been retained in ours'. Pre-2016 companies are different: Table A may still fill gaps for them ([05.008])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This is the old Table A position and the UK position, which is why it is tempting. Singapore's current regime gives model constitutions no gap-filling role for companies that adopt their own constitutions."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.011], fns 31–33", takeaway:"No gap-filling by model constitutions (unlike the UK)." },

{ id:"c5x-005", lesson:"5B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Founders lodge incorporation papers for Pine Pte Ltd through Bizfile. They neither attach a constitution of their own nor state that they adopt a model constitution, assuming the model will apply by default.",
  prompt:"What should happen?",
  options:[
    {t:"The Registrar must refuse to register it", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.011] explains why the old presumption of adoption was dropped: 'it is not possible for a company to be incorporated without having either submitted its own constitution or expressly adopting the relevant model constitution in compliance with section 19(1)'. Since registration of the constitution is a prerequisite to incorporation (ss 19(3)–(4)), 'the Registrar must decline to register the company in such event'. The founders' assumption reflects the old Table A rule, under which a company that registered no articles was presumed to have adopted Table A ([05.008]). Today they must either lodge a constitution or expressly adopt the model by its title."},
    {t:"The company is registered with the model constitution deemed to apply", correct:false,
     why:"That was effectively the old Table A position. The current Act has no presumption of adoption."},
    {t:"The company is registered and has six months to lodge a constitution", correct:false,
     why:"No grace period exists. The constitution is part of what must be lodged before registration."},
    {t:"The company is registered with no constitution, and the Companies Act alone governs it", correct:false,
     why:"Every company must have a constitution containing prescribed content and internal regulations (ss 22(1), 35(1)). A company cannot exist without one."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.011], fns 31–32", takeaway:"No constitution and no express model adoption → no incorporation." }

