/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 5 · Lesson 5D Interpreting the Constitution (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 5 [05.025]–[05.039], fns 79–140. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl5-027", lesson:"5D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the starting point for interpreting a company's constitution?",
  options:[
    {t:"Objective meaning to a reasonable person, read in context", correct:true,
     why:"Because the constitution is a contract, it is generally interpreted by contractual rules ([05.025]). The cardinal starting point is to 'give effect to the intention of the parties as objectively ascertained'. The meaning of a provision is therefore what its language would convey to a reasonable person, not what any party actually or subjectively intended. In keeping with that, a contextual approach is used, so that as a general rule account may be had of relevant background reasonably available to all the parties. Lee immediately adds a caution: applying this to constitutions can raise unique issues, because the constitution differs from ordinary commercial contracts in important ways, especially its public character and its binding effect on future members ([05.033]–[05.039])."},
    {t:"The subjective intention of the original subscribers", correct:false,
     why:"Subjective intention is excluded even for ordinary contracts. For a constitution that binds people who joined long after the subscribers, relying on their private intentions would be especially unfair."},
    {t:"The literal words only, with no regard to context", correct:false,
     why:"The contextual approach is the general rule. What is distinctive for constitutions is restraint in the use of extrinsic background, not a return to pure literalism."},
    {t:"Whatever reading the majority shareholders prefer", correct:false,
     why:"The majority can alter the constitution by special resolution, but interpretation of the existing text is a question of law for the court applying objective principles."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.025], fns 80–81", takeaway:"Objective + contextual (with restraint for constitutions)." },

{ id:"cl5-028", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's Article 32 allows it to 'increase the share capital by such sum' as an ordinary resolution prescribes. It was designed for authorised capital, which was abolished in 2006. Minority shareholders argue it now requires shareholders to approve every share issue and fix the number of shares.",
  prompt:"How did the Court of Appeal rule in Lian Hwee Choo Phebe v Maxz Universal?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rejected: Article 32 was read dynamically, consistently with s 161", correct:true,
     why:"Ordinarily a contract is read by reference to the circumstances when it was made ([05.027]). The Court of Appeal held that this did not stop a more dynamic approach to Article 32, because a constitution is an instrument of governance intended to endure through changing circumstances, so the court must consider how it applies in the changed circumstances. To do that, the court must 'first carefully dissect the purposes and the values embedded within the relevant provisions', and reach an interpretation consonant with them. Article 32 was originally about authorised share capital. Reading it as requiring shareholders to approve every issue and prescribe the number of shares would impose a stricter requirement than s 161, which allows a general authority to the board without fixing numbers. That was 'intuitively unattractive' and inconsistent with Parliament's intention."},
    {t:"It accepted the argument, applying the article's literal words", correct:false,
     why:"The literal words 'increase the share capital' could be stretched to cover any issue, which is the minority's point. The court instead looked at the article's genealogy and purpose."},
    {t:"It held Article 32 void after 2006", correct:false,
     why:"The court interpreted the article in the changed legal context; it did not strike it down."},
    {t:"It rectified the article to remove the reference to share capital", correct:false,
     why:"A constitution generally cannot be rectified ([05.013]). The court proceeded by interpretation."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.026]–[05.027], fns 83–90", takeaway:"Lian Hwee Choo Phebe: dynamic, purpose-based reading within the statute." },

{ id:"c5x-029", lesson:"5D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What broader lesson does Lee draw from Lian Hwee Choo Phebe about interpreting constitutions?",
  options:[
    {t:"A constitution is read within its changing statutory setting", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.028] says the case 'usefully demonstrates the need to modify general principles of contractual interpretation to take into account the corporate constitution's design as a compact situated against a statutory context'. Two points follow. First, because the constitution derives its legality from the Companies Act, the Act's provisions necessarily constrain how it can be read; an interpretation that would override or tighten a statutory scheme such as s 161 is suspect. Second, the statutory context is dynamic, since the Act is amended to keep up with practice, so a constitution may have to be applied in a regulatory environment its drafters never contemplated. That can call for an approach different from that applied to an ordinary contract fixed at the moment of formation."},
    {t:"Constitutions should always be read literally, whatever changes occur in the law", correct:false,
     why:"Lian Hwee Choo Phebe rejected a literal reading because it would have clashed with the statute in its changed form."},
    {t:"When the law changes, outdated articles automatically lapse", correct:false,
     why:"The court interpreted the outdated article; it did not treat it as lapsed. Lapse would leave unpredictable gaps."},
    {t:"Courts may freely rewrite constitutions to suit modern practice", correct:false,
     why:"The court's task is interpretation guided by the provision's purposes and values, not rewriting. Changing the text is for the members by special resolution."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.028], fn 91", takeaway:"Constitution read as a compact within a changing statute." },

{ id:"cl5-029", lesson:"5D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When may extrinsic evidence be admitted under s 94(f) of the Evidence Act to interpret a document, per Zurich Insurance v B-Gold?",
  options:[
    {t:"Relevant, available to all, clear context; ambiguity not needed", correct:true,
     why:"The parol evidence rule, now in ss 93–100 of the Evidence Act 1893, prohibits extrinsic evidence to prove or vary a written document that on its face contains all the terms ([05.029]). But s 94(f) admits 'any fact … which shows in what manner the language of a document is related to existing facts', so extrinsic evidence can aid interpretation. Zurich Insurance held that s 94(f) admits contextual evidence that is relevant, reasonably available to all the contracting parties and relates to a clear and obvious context, and that ambiguity on the face of the document is not a precondition. Limits remain: subjective declarations of intention are inadmissible because they do not assist objective interpretation; prior negotiations and subsequent conduct may only tentatively be admitted if they meet the same tripartite requirements; and background may only interpret the document, never contradict, vary, add to or subtract from it."},
    {t:"Only if the document is ambiguous on its face", correct:false,
     why:"Older approaches required ambiguity first, which makes this plausible. Zurich Insurance held that ambiguity is not a precondition for s 94(f)."},
    {t:"Whenever a party testifies about what it intended", correct:false,
     why:"Subjective declarations of intention are inadmissible because the test is objective."},
    {t:"Whenever it helps to add a missing term", correct:false,
     why:"Background evidence can illuminate language but cannot add to the document. Adding a term is the province of implication, which has its own strict test."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.029], fns 92–100", takeaway:"s 94(f): relevant + reasonably available + clear and obvious context." },

{ id:"c5x-028", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a dispute about the meaning of a clause in Larch Pte Ltd's constitution, the lawyer who drafted it in 2017 offers to give evidence that she and the founders 'always intended' the clause to cover preference shares too.",
  prompt:"Is her evidence admissible to interpret the clause?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: subjective declarations of intention are inadmissible", correct:true,
     why:"Section 94(f) of the Evidence Act is wide, but 'it cannot be used to admit the parties' subjective declarations of intention' ([05.029]), because such evidence does not help ascertain intention objectively. The drafter's testimony about what she and the founders 'always intended' is exactly that kind of evidence. For a constitution there is a second reason to exclude it: the document binds members who joined later and is a public document that outsiders may rely on, so private understandings of the drafters are particularly unsuited to fixing its meaning ([05.033]–[05.034]). The court will read the clause objectively, in light of the constitution as a whole and any background that was reasonably available to those who rely on it."},
    {t:"Yes: the drafter is the best evidence of what the clause means", correct:false,
     why:"This treats interpretation as a search for actual intention. The law looks to what the words convey to a reasonable person."},
    {t:"Yes, if the clause is ambiguous", correct:false,
     why:"Ambiguity may make background more useful, but it does not make subjective declarations of intention admissible."},
    {t:"Yes, because the parol evidence rule does not apply to constitutions", correct:false,
     why:"If anything the reverse is true: constitutions attract a more restrictive approach to extrinsic evidence because of their public dimension."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.029], [05.033]–[05.034], fns 97–98", takeaway:"Drafters' 'we always intended…' evidence is inadmissible." },

{ id:"cl5-030", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's articles say nothing about how the board chairman is appointed. Its shareholders' agreement sets out a procedure, which the shareholders have consistently followed.",
  prompt:"Can the agreement's procedure fill the gap? (Golden Harvest v Golden Village Multiplex)",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, if their conduct shows a clear intention to follow it", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.030] notes that constitutions are unlikely to provide for every situation, and gaps may sometimes be bridged by incorporating terms from other sources. In Golden Harvest Films Distribution v Golden Village Multiplex, the Court of Appeal held that where the articles were silent on the procedure for appointing the board chairman, the procedure in the shareholders' agreement could be incorporated to fill the gap, because the shareholders had by their conduct shown a clear intention to abide by it. Obiter, the court accepted that past practice alone, without any extrinsic agreement, could also fill the gap, but only on concrete evidence of that practice. The case illustrates how the constitution and a shareholders' agreement can be read together where they are aligned ([05.079])."},
    {t:"No: only the model constitution can fill gaps", correct:false,
     why:"Model constitutions have no gap-filling role ([05.011]). The court in Golden Harvest used the shareholders' agreement."},
    {t:"No: a shareholders' agreement can never affect the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"The two documents are separate, but where they deal with the same matter and the parties' conduct shows they are aligned, the agreement can fill a gap in the constitution."},
    {t:"Only if the constitution is first amended by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"No amendment was needed in Golden Harvest. The gap was filled by incorporation."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.030], [05.079], fns 101–103", takeaway:"Golden Harvest: SHA procedure fills articles' gap if conduct shows intent." },

{ id:"c5x-030", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Juniper Pte Ltd's articles are silent on who chairs board meetings, and it has no shareholders' agreement. A director asserts that the founder has always chaired, so the founder must chair. The only evidence is the director's general recollection.",
  prompt:"Can past practice fill the gap?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only on concrete evidence of the practice, which is lacking here", correct:true,
     why:"In Golden Harvest the gap was filled by the shareholders' agreement procedure, but the Court of Appeal also accepted, obiter, that the gap could have been bridged by evidence of past practice alone, without any extrinsic agreement ([05.030]). It added a firm condition: 'concrete evidence of such practice must have been adduced to justify such a conclusion' (fn 103). Juniper's director offers only a general recollection, which falls short of concrete evidence such as board minutes showing the founder consistently chairing. Without that, the gap is unlikely to be filled by practice, and the board may need to resolve who chairs or the members may amend the articles."},
    {t:"Yes: any assertion of past practice fills a gap", correct:false,
     why:"This ignores the concrete-evidence requirement the court attached to the past-practice route."},
    {t:"No: gaps in a constitution can only be filled by a shareholders' agreement", correct:false,
     why:"A shareholders' agreement is one route, but the court accepted obiter that past practice alone could also suffice."},
    {t:"No: gaps must be filled by the model constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Model constitutions do not fill gaps in a company's own constitution ([05.011])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.030], fns 102–103", takeaway:"Past practice may fill a gap, but only on concrete evidence." },

{ id:"cl5-031", lesson:"5D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"According to Sembcorp Marine v PPL Holdings, when can a term be implied in fact?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only for a true gap that the parties never considered", correct:true,
     why:"Terms implied in fact give effect to the parties' presumed intention and operate as ad hoc gap fillers ([05.031]). But a gap is not enough by itself. Sembcorp Marine, which concerned both articles of association and a shareholders' agreement, held that a term may be implied only to fill a 'true' gap, meaning a matter the parties failed to consider when contracting. Two kinds of gap do not qualify. If the gap arose because the parties mistakenly assumed the express terms covered the matter, the proper remedy is rectification, which a constitution generally cannot have. If the parties considered the matter but could not agree a solution, implying a term would rewrite their bargain. In all cases, no term can be implied to contradict an express term."},
    {t:"Whenever there is any gap in the contract", correct:false,
     why:"This is the intuitive reading of 'gap filler'. Sembcorp Marine limits implication to true gaps."},
    {t:"Whenever implying the term would be fair or reasonable", correct:false,
     why:"Reasonableness is not the test. Both traditional formulations turn on necessity ([05.032])."},
    {t:"Only if both parties now agree to it", correct:false,
     why:"Present agreement would be a variation. Implication is about presumed intention at the time of contracting."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.031], fns 104–110", takeaway:"Implied term in fact: only for a true (unconsidered) gap." },

{ id:"c5x-022", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"When Aspen Pte Ltd was formed, the two 50:50 founders debated at length whether to include a deadlock-breaking mechanism, could not agree on one, and left it out. Years later they are deadlocked, and one founder asks the court to imply a term giving the chairman a casting vote.",
  prompt:"Will the court imply the term?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: that would rewrite a bargain they couldn't agree", correct:true,
     why:"Sembcorp Marine identifies three kinds of gap ([05.031]). Only a 'true' gap, one the parties failed to consider, can be filled by implication. A gap may instead arise because the parties, 'having given thought to a matter, decided not to express a solution because they could not agree on one'. Lee is clear that a term may not be implied to remedy this kind of gap, 'for to do so would be to rewrite the parties' bargain'. Aspen's founders consciously left deadlock unresolved. A casting vote for the chairman would give one side the very advantage they could not agree on. Their options lie elsewhere: agreeing an amendment, a buy-out, or in serious cases relief for oppression or a winding-up on the just and equitable ground."},
    {t:"Yes: a term is needed to give the constitution business efficacy", correct:false,
     why:"Business efficacy is a test for detecting a true gap. It cannot be used to supply a term the parties deliberately left out."},
    {t:"Yes, because deadlock is a gap the parties did not foresee", correct:false,
     why:"On the facts they did foresee it and debated it. That takes the gap outside the true-gap category."},
    {t:"Yes, by rectification of the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Constitutions generally cannot be rectified, and in any case rectification corrects a failure to record agreement. Here there was no agreement to record."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.031], fn 109", takeaway:"Considered-but-unagreed gap → no implied term." },

{ id:"c5x-023", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The founders of Hazel Pte Ltd all assumed that its pre-emption article covered transfers to members' family trusts. On a careful reading it does not. A member transfers shares to his family trust without offering them to the others, and they ask the court to imply a term extending the article.",
  prompt:"What is the analytical problem with their request?",
  options:[
    {t:"It's a rectification case, and constitutions can't be rectified", correct:true,
     why:"Sembcorp Marine held that a term should not be implied where 'the gap arose by reason of the parties' mistaken assumption that it has been addressed by the express terms' ([05.031]). In such a case the gap is better remedied by rectification, which corrects a document to reflect what the parties actually agreed. But Lee notes that a company's constitution cannot, as a general rule, be rectified ([05.013](e)). The founders therefore fall between two remedies: implication is inappropriate, and rectification is unavailable. Their practical route is to amend the article by special resolution for the future and, if the transfer was unfairly prejudicial, consider other remedies."},
    {t:"None: a term will be implied because all the founders shared the assumption", correct:false,
     why:"Shared assumption is the hallmark of a rectification case, which is precisely why implication is the wrong tool."},
    {t:"The court will rectify the article because the mistake is obvious", correct:false,
     why:"Rectification of a constitution is generally not available, because the statutory alteration procedure is the only means of correcting mistakes."},
    {t:"The court will imply the term using the officious bystander test", correct:false,
     why:"The officious bystander test frames terms for true gaps. It is not a way round the bar on implying terms to cure mistaken assumptions."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.013], [05.031], fn 110", takeaway:"Mistaken-assumption gap → rectification territory, unavailable for constitutions." },

{ id:"c5x-021", lesson:"5D", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A court may imply a term into a company's constitution even if it contradicts an express term, provided it is necessary for business efficacy.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.031] states that at common law 'no such term will be implied to contradict an express term'. Implication supplements an incomplete contract; it cannot override what the parties actually said. The business efficacy and officious bystander tests both turn on necessity ([05.032]), and it can never be necessary to imply something the parties expressly excluded. For a constitution, the point is reinforced by its public character: members and outsiders are entitled to rely on its express terms."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Business efficacy is a demanding test, but it operates only to fill gaps. A term contradicting an express term would be a rewrite, not an implication."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.031]", takeaway:"Implied terms never contradict express terms." },

{ id:"cl5-032", lesson:"5D", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each concept on implied terms to its role.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Business efficacy test", r:"Useful for detecting a lacuna"},
    {l:"Officious bystander test", r:"Defines the term reflecting presumed intention"},
    {l:"Common criterion of both tests", r:"Necessity"},
    {l:"Term implied in law", r:"Rare, because it affects all contracts of that type"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [05.032] sets out the two traditional formulations of the test for implying a term in fact. Under the business efficacy test, a term may be implied if necessary to give the transaction the efficacy the parties must have contemplated. Under the officious bystander test, a term is implied if it is so obviously intended that it goes without saying. Both share the criterion of necessity, so they complement each other: the business efficacy test helps detect a lacuna, and the officious bystander test is 'the device that enables the court to define that term', or 'the practical mode by which the business efficacy test is implemented'. Terms implied in law are different ([05.031]): once implied in one contract they apply to all contracts of that type, so courts imply them rarely, including in constitution disputes, where terms implied in fact are pleaded more often.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.031]–[05.032], fns 105–116", takeaway:"Efficacy finds the gap; bystander frames the term; both need necessity." },

{ id:"cl5-034", lesson:"5D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is a more restrictive approach to extrinsic evidence appropriate for corporate constitutions?",
  options:[
    {t:"Later members and outsiders rely on the text exactly as registered", correct:true,
     why:"Sembcorp Marine and Foo Jong Peng confirm that general contractual interpretation applies to constitutions ([05.033]). But Lee asks whether the constitution's differences call for modification, given that it binds future as well as current members and is a registered public document that third parties may rely on with no knowledge of how it was framed. V K Rajah JA in Zurich Insurance cautioned that for standard form contracts and documents for commercial circulation, the presumption that the writing is complete is 'almost impossible to rebut' and courts should be restrained with context, because third parties 'must be able to enforce the plain words'. The same reasoning applies to constitutions ([05.034]). The restriction depends on the general quality of the document and of the information, not on proof that a particular third party was actually prejudiced ([05.037])."},
    {t:"Because constitutions are always unambiguous", correct:false,
     why:"Constitutions are often unclear; that is why interpretation disputes arise. The reason for restraint is reliance by people who lack the background."},
    {t:"Because extrinsic evidence is never admissible for any contract", correct:false,
     why:"For contracts generally, s 94(f) admits contextual evidence meeting the Zurich requirements ([05.029])."},
    {t:"Only where a third party proves actual prejudice", correct:false,
     why:"The English approach requires only that third parties 'might (not will)' need to rely on the document without access to extraneous material."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.033]–[05.034], [05.037], fns 117–122, 133", takeaway:"Public document → strong completeness presumption → restraint." },

{ id:"cl5-033", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company formed to manage a block of flats argues that its articles must imply an obligation on every member to contribute to upkeep of the common areas, based only on the circumstances when it was set up. Nothing in the articles suggests such an obligation.",
  prompt:"How did the English Court of Appeal rule in Bratton Seymour v Oxborough?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: extrinsic circumstances alone can't support an implied term", correct:true,
     why:"The company argued that the obligation was needed to give its articles business efficacy ([05.035]). The court disagreed. Nothing in the articles suggested the obligation, and it could not be implied by reference only to extrinsic circumstances existing at incorporation. Doing so would amount to rectifying the constitution, which the court had no power to do, and would prejudice potential shareholders who knew nothing of the antecedent transactions. Lee stresses the limits of the decision ([05.036]): the court accepted that articles are a business document to be read for commercial efficacy and that a term can be implied where justified by the express language; and it excluded not all background, but only information not reasonably available to those who may rely on the constitution."},
    {t:"The term was implied to give the articles business efficacy", correct:false,
     why:"Business efficacy can support implication from the express language, but not from extrinsic circumstances alone that later members could not know about."},
    {t:"The court rectified the articles to add the obligation", correct:false,
     why:"The court had no power to rectify the articles, which was one reason it refused to imply the term."},
    {t:"Members were held liable in tort instead", correct:false,
     why:"No alternative liability was found. The claim simply failed."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.035]–[05.036], fns 124–130", takeaway:"Bratton: no implied term from extrinsic facts alone." },

{ id:"c5x-024", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Elm Ltd is listed. In a dispute over the scope of its objects clause, a shareholder seeks to rely on 1990s board papers, never published, showing why the founders drafted the clause as they did.",
  prompt:"How is the court likely to treat the board papers?",
  options:[
    {t:"Reluctantly: private drafting papers aren't public background", correct:true,
     why:"In Egyptian Salt and Soda Co v Port Said Salt Association, the Privy Council held that a company's objects clauses could be interpreted only by their express language, not by evidence of circumstances at the time of drafting, because the people who later rely on the memorandum, including shareholders, creditors and those dealing with the company, would not have known of those circumstances ([05.035]). Elm is listed, so the class of people who may rely on its constitution is wide and constantly changing. Unpublished board papers are the opposite of 'publicly known' background, the kind of material Belize Telecom allowed ([05.036]). Lee argues that this restrictive approach, grounded in the constitution's public dimension, should apply in Singapore too ([05.039])."},
    {t:"Freely, because s 94(f) admits all relevant background", correct:false,
     why:"Section 94(f) admits background that is reasonably available to all parties. For a listed company's constitution, private board papers are not reasonably available to the people who rely on it."},
    {t:"Freely, but only if the shareholder proves a third party will be prejudiced", correct:false,
     why:"The restriction does not depend on proof of actual prejudice ([05.037]). It follows from the general quality of the document and the information."},
    {t:"Only if the board papers were signed by all the founders", correct:false,
     why:"Signature does not make private material public. The issue is availability to those who rely on the constitution."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.035]–[05.039], fns 123, 131–134", takeaway:"Listed co: private drafting papers won't explain the constitution." },

{ id:"c5x-025", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Willow Brewers Ltd has about 40 members, all from a single trade cooperative, and its articles tightly restrict who may acquire shares. Explanatory notes sent to members with an EGM notice explained the purpose of an amended article. In a later dispute, a party asks the court to use those notes to interpret the article.",
  prompt:"Are the notes likely to be admissible?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: in a closely held company the notes count as public enough", correct:true,
     why:"In Lion Nathan Australia v Coopers Brewery, the Federal Court of Australia allowed the trial judge to consider an earlier version of the articles and the explanatory notes for an EGM convened to amend them ([05.038]). The notes went only to then-current members, but the company had a closely held membership and tight restrictions on share transfers, so the information was well known to existing members and easily ascertainable by anyone eligible to acquire shares. Lee explains that 'the nature of the company's membership may affect the characterisation of information as public and accessible'. Willow Brewers is closely analogous. The general restraint on extrinsic evidence still applies, but here the people who might rely on the article had ready access to the notes."},
    {t:"No: extrinsic material is never admissible to interpret a constitution", correct:false,
     why:"The approach is restrictive, not exclusionary. Publicly known background (Belize Telecom) and, in close companies, information accessible to all eligible members (Lion Nathan) may be used."},
    {t:"Yes, for any company, because the notes were sent to members", correct:false,
     why:"For a widely held or listed company, notes sent only to members at one point in time would not be accessible to later investors. Lion Nathan turned on the closely held membership and transfer restrictions."},
    {t:"Only if the notes were registered with ACRA", correct:false,
     why:"Registration would make them public, but Lion Nathan shows a more limited degree of publicity can suffice in a tightly controlled company."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.038], fns 135–138", takeaway:"Closely held + transfer limits → member-circulated notes may be usable." },

{ id:"c5x-026", lesson:"5D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"When Ivan bought his shares in Poplar Ltd, the seller told him privately that a certain article meant X. Other members bought on the understanding that it meant Y. In a dispute, Ivan argues that, as against him, the article must bear meaning X.",
  prompt:"Is his argument sound?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the constitution has one meaning for every member", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.037] states that 'the circumstances specific to each shareholder are irrelevant because the constitution is a single (albeit multilateral) contract binding the company and its shareholders. Its meaning cannot therefore be ascertained by reference to facts peculiar to each shareholder.' Each article has one meaning for everyone bound by it. Ivan's private understanding from the seller may give him a claim against the seller if he was misled, but it cannot change what the article means. Allowing it would mean the same constitution meant different things for different members, which is unworkable for a governance document."},
    {t:"Yes: each member's contract is interpreted in light of what that member knew", correct:false,
     why:"That would be the position if each member had a separate bilateral contract with the company. The s 39 contract is one multilateral contract with one meaning."},
    {t:"Yes, if the seller was a director", correct:false,
     why:"Even a director's private explanation cannot fix the meaning of the constitution for one member. The objective, uniform meaning governs."},
    {t:"Only if Ivan can prove he relied on the explanation", correct:false,
     why:"Reliance might matter in a misrepresentation claim against the seller, not in interpreting the constitution."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.037], fn 134", takeaway:"One constitution, one meaning: individual members' knowledge irrelevant." },

{ id:"cl5-035", lesson:"5D", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each authority to its treatment of background evidence for constitutions.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Egyptian Salt Soda v Port Said Salt", r:"Objects read by express language, not drafting circumstances"},
    {l:"AG of Belize v Belize Telecom", r:"Term implied from the articles' scheme and publicly known background"},
    {l:"Lion Nathan v Coopers Brewery", r:"EGM explanatory notes admitted for a closely held company"},
    {l:"Sembcorp Marine and Foo Jong Peng", r:"Contractual interpretation rules apply to constitutional documents"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [05.033]–[05.039] traces a consistent line. Sembcorp Marine (articles and a shareholders' agreement) and Foo Jong Peng (a clan association's constitution) affirm that general contractual interpretation applies to constitutional documents, including background facts and terms implied in fact for true gaps. English and Australian courts add restraint for these 'public' documents. Egyptian Salt confined the reading of objects clauses to their express language. Bratton refused to imply a term from extrinsic facts alone. Belize Telecom allowed a term to be implied from the articles' general scheme and, to a more limited extent, publicly known background. Lion Nathan admitted EGM explanatory notes for a closely held company with tight transfer restrictions, showing that the nature of the membership affects what counts as public. Lee argues the restrictive approach should apply in Singapore; the silence in Sembcorp Marine and Foo Jong Peng does not preclude it, because neither involved implying terms from extrinsic facts.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.033], [05.035]–[05.039]", takeaway:"Restraint, but publicly available background may be used." }

]);

;
