/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 5 · Lesson 5E Remedies for Breach of the Constitution (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 5 [05.040], fns 141–148. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl5-036", lesson:"5E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What remedies are most commonly sought for a breach of the constitution?",
  options:[
    {t:"Declarations and injunctions", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.040] notes that where a breach of the constitution is alleged, 'the remedies commonly sought are declaratory and injunctive reliefs'. A declaration settles what the constitution requires; an injunction restrains a threatened breach or compels compliance. Because both are discretionary, they are 'not available as of right but are subject to the usual equitable limitations', such as delay, acquiescence, the claimant's own conduct and whether the relief would serve any practical purpose. Damages are 'more complicated', because Houldsworth v City of Glasgow Bank suggests members cannot recover damages from the company in claims relating to membership interests."},
    {t:"Damages, available as of right", correct:false,
     why:"Damages are the default remedy for breach of an ordinary contract, which is why this is tempting. For constitutional claims by members against the company, damages are the problematic remedy because of Houldsworth."},
    {t:"Rectification of the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"A constitution generally cannot be rectified; the statutory alteration procedure is the only way to correct it ([05.013])."},
    {t:"Criminal prosecution of the directors", correct:false,
     why:"Breach of the constitution is a civil matter between the company and its members. Some breaches of the Companies Act carry criminal penalties, but that is a different question."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.040]", takeaway:"Usual remedies: declaration + injunction (discretionary)." },

{ id:"c5x-031", lesson:"5E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In January, Ash Pte Ltd's board allots shares in a way Olu, a member, considers a breach of the constitution. Olu knows at once but says nothing for 18 months, during which the new shareholders pay for and trade the shares. He now seeks an injunction setting aside the allotment.",
  prompt:"What is the main obstacle to relief?",
  options:[
    {t:"Discretionary relief may be refused for delay and reliance", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.040] stresses that the usual remedies for breach of the constitution are discretionary and 'not available as of right but are subject to the usual equitable limitations'. Equity looks at the claimant's conduct and at the effect of relief on others. Olu knew of the alleged breach immediately but waited 18 months, while new shareholders paid for and dealt in the shares in reliance on the allotment. Even if the allotment did breach the constitution, the court may decline to grant an injunction that would unwind those transactions, and may confine Olu to such other relief as is appropriate. Prompt action is the practical lesson for members who want to enforce the constitution."},
    {t:"None: a member who proves a breach is entitled to an injunction", correct:false,
     why:"That would make the remedy available as of right. Injunctions are discretionary."},
    {t:"He should have sued for damages instead, which are always available", correct:false,
     why:"Damages against the company are the more doubtful remedy because of Houldsworth, so they are not an easy alternative."},
    {t:"Only the company can complain about an allotment", correct:false,
     why:"Whether a particular breach is a personal right or an internal irregularity is a separate question ([05.023]). The facts point to the discretionary bars as the main obstacle."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.040]", takeaway:"Declaration/injunction: discretionary; delay and third-party reliance can bar them." },

{ id:"cl5-037", lesson:"5E", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the most convincing justification the chapter offers for the rule in Houldsworth v City of Glasgow Bank?",
  options:[
    {t:"Capital maintenance as the price of limited liability", correct:true,
     why:"Houldsworth suggests members cannot recover damages from the company in an action relating to membership interests ([05.040]). The prevalent rationale is that such an award would amount to an impermissible return or reduction of capital, so excluding it protects creditors. In BTY v BUA the High Court accepted, without discussion, that damages would 'constitute the shareholder a creditor of the company and elevate the shareholder into impermissible competition with the company's creditors'. Lee finds this 'not … entirely convincing': capital maintenance rules do not ring-fence a fund for creditors, and recovery from a solvent company would not prejudice them. The rule may still be justified if capital maintenance is understood more narrowly, as the quid pro quo for limited liability: a shareholder cannot seek recovery from the company and keep its shares, because that is the price of limited liability."},
    {t:"Damages would always prejudice creditors, even of a solvent company", correct:false,
     why:"This is the strong form of the creditor-protection rationale. Lee points out that recovery from a solvent company would not prejudice creditors, which is why she looks for a narrower justification."},
    {t:"The constitution is not really a contract", correct:false,
     why:"The constitution is a statutory contract under s 39(1). Houldsworth is a limit on one remedy, not a denial of contractual status."},
    {t:"Shareholders owe fiduciary duties to the company", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders generally owe no fiduciary duties to the company. This has nothing to do with Houldsworth."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.040], fns 141–145", takeaway:"Houldsworth: best justified as the price of limited liability." },

{ id:"c5x-032", lesson:"5E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Birch Capital Pte Ltd, a solvent company with ample assets, breaches its constitution by refusing to issue Mina the bonus shares she is entitled to. Mina sues the company for damages. The company argues that awarding her damages would put her in 'impermissible competition' with its creditors.",
  prompt:"Which response best reflects the current state of the law?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unsettled: Song Jianbo doubted Houldsworth, but only obiter", correct:true,
     why:"Both sides have authority ([05.040]). The company can point to Houldsworth and to BTY v BUA, where the High Court accepted that damages would make the shareholder a creditor in competition with the company's creditors. Mina can reply with Song Jianbo v Sunmax Global Capital Fund 1, where the High Court doubted Houldsworth because it fails to recognise the company's distinct status, is out of step with modern creditor protection based on assets and liabilities rather than paid-up capital, and can be distinguished as a case about an unlimited company whose members had to contribute to all its liabilities. She can add Lee's point that Birch Capital is solvent, so creditors would not be prejudiced. But Song Jianbo's remarks were obiter, and the narrower 'price of limited liability' rationale may still support Houldsworth, so the issue remains open until the Court of Appeal decides it."},
    {t:"The company is plainly right: Houldsworth is settled law in Singapore", correct:false,
     why:"Song Jianbo casts serious doubt on Houldsworth's continued applicability, so it cannot be called settled."},
    {t:"Mina is plainly right: Song Jianbo overruled Houldsworth", correct:false,
     why:"A High Court decision cannot overrule a House of Lords authority for Singapore in this way, and the discussion was obiter. The question is open."},
    {t:"Neither: damages are never sought for breach of a constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Damages are sought, which is why Houldsworth matters. They are simply the most contested remedy."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.040], fns 143–148", takeaway:"Houldsworth vs Song Jianbo: damages from a solvent company still unsettled." },

{ id:"cl5-038", lesson:"5E", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shareholder sues a solvent limited company for damages for breach of its constitution. The company relies on Houldsworth.",
  prompt:"What did the High Court say about Houldsworth in Song Jianbo v Sunmax Global Capital Fund 1?",
  options:[
    {t:"It doubted Houldsworth, but only obiter", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.040] notes that the rule in Houldsworth has been criticised because it denies shareholders otherwise legitimate private law remedies. In Song Jianbo, the High Court doubted its continued applicability on three grounds: it does not adequately recognise the distinct legal status of the company vis-à-vis its members; it is out of step with modern commercial reality, in which assets and liabilities, rather than paid-up capital, are the primary safeguard for creditors; and Houldsworth can be distinguished as involving an unlimited liability company whose shareholders had to contribute rateably to all its liabilities, so it did not lay down an absolute bar. These remarks suggest Houldsworth may not exclude shareholders' damages claims for breach of the constitution in Singapore, but because they were obiter, 'the issue still awaits the authoritative resolution of the Court of Appeal'."},
    {t:"It applied Houldsworth strictly and dismissed the claim", correct:false,
     why:"The court did the opposite: it questioned whether Houldsworth still applies."},
    {t:"It authoritatively overruled Houldsworth", correct:false,
     why:"The discussion was obiter, so it is persuasive rather than binding, and the Court of Appeal has yet to decide."},
    {t:"It held damages available only to directors, not shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The discussion was about shareholders' claims relating to membership interests."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.040], fns 146–148", takeaway:"Song Jianbo doubted Houldsworth (obiter). CA yet to decide." },

{ id:"c5x-033", lesson:"5E", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: One reason Song Jianbo gave for distinguishing Houldsworth is that Houldsworth concerned an unlimited liability company.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.040] records that the High Court in Song Jianbo would distinguish Houldsworth 'as a case involving an unlimited liability company where shareholders were obliged to contribute rateably to discharge all the company's liabilities'. In that setting, letting a shareholder recover damages from the company would be circular, since the shareholder would then have to contribute to pay the company's debts, including the damages. On that view Houldsworth 'did not lay down an absolute prohibition' against shareholders recovering damages for claims relating to membership interests, and its reasoning does not carry over neatly to limited companies."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"This is one of the specific grounds the court gave. The others were the company's distinct legal status and modern creditor protection."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.040], fn 147", takeaway:"Houldsworth was an unlimited company case." }

]);

;
