/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 5 · Lesson 5F Altering the Constitution, part 1 of 2:
   the power, its limits, objects, entrenchment (audited Oct 2026). Source: Lee Ch 5 [05.041]–[05.049], fns 149–183. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl5-039", lesson:"5F", type:"fill",
  prompt:"A company may alter its constitution by special resolution, which requires a majority of not less than ____% of the votes cast at a general meeting.",
  accept:["75","75%","seventy-five","seventy five","three quarters","three-quarters","3/4"], answer:"75",
  why:"Section 26(1) allows a company to alter its constitution by special resolution, and s 184(1) defines a special resolution as one passed by a three-quarter or higher majority of the votes cast ([05.041]). Lee explains the policy: because amendments can gravely affect members' rights and obligations, they need a clear rather than marginal majority, and for matters thought fundamental, such as the objects, the rules are even stricter. The flip side is that majority power to amend makes minorities vulnerable, which is why the law has developed the preventive and remedial limits covered in the rest of this lesson.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.041], fns 150–151", takeaway:"Alteration: special resolution, ≥75% of votes cast." },

{ id:"cl5-040", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A company alters its constitution by special resolution. Which statement is correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"Lodge within 14 days; prospective only; no added liability without consent", correct:true,
     why:"Three rules from [05.042] apply. First, under s 26(2), when the constitution is altered by special resolution, court order or other document, the company must lodge a copy of it with a copy of the amended constitution within 14 days; failure exposes the company and its officers to penalties (s 26(2A)). Second, the alteration is deemed part of the original constitution from the date of the special resolution (s 26(1AA)), consistent with the common law rule that alterations operate only prospectively, so they cannot undermine legal rights already accrued under the constitution. Third, s 39(3) protects members from new financial burdens: a member is not bound by an alteration requiring him to take more shares, increasing his liability to contribute to share capital or otherwise to pay money to the company, unless he consents in writing before or after the alteration."},
    {t:"The alteration is retrospective to the date of incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"Alterations operate from the date of the special resolution, and the common law forbids using them to undo accrued rights."},
    {t:"It must lodge within 60 days", correct:false,
     why:"Sixty days is the name reservation period from Chapter 4, which may be why it comes to mind. Lodgement of an altered constitution is due within 14 days."},
    {t:"It may increase members' liability to contribute capital by special resolution alone", correct:false,
     why:"Section 39(3) requires the affected member's written consent for any alteration that increases his liability to contribute or pay money to the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.042], fns 154–158", takeaway:"Lodge in 14 days · prospective only · no extra liability without consent." },

{ id:"c5x-034", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Members of Clubhouse Pte Ltd pass a special resolution (80% in favour) adding an article that every member must pay the company an annual $2,000 maintenance levy. Kai voted against and has never agreed in writing to pay.",
  prompt:"Is Kai bound to pay the levy?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: he hasn't consented in writing (s 39(3))", correct:true,
     why:"The special resolution validly alters the constitution, but s 39(3) carves out a protection for individual members ([05.042]). A member 'is not bound by a subsequent alteration that requires him to subscribe for more shares in the company, or which otherwise increases his liability to contribute to the company's share capital, or to pay money to the company, unless he has given his written consent either before or after the alteration'. An annual levy payable to the company is a requirement to pay money to the company. Kai has not consented in writing, so the article does not bind him, though it may bind members who do consent. The rule reflects the idea that limited liability means a member's financial exposure is fixed when he invests and cannot be enlarged by majority vote."},
    {t:"Yes: a special resolution binds every member, including dissenters", correct:false,
     why:"That is the general rule for alterations, and why this is tempting. Section 39(3) is the specific exception for alterations that increase a member's financial obligations."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if the levy is used for the company's benefit", correct:false,
     why:"The bona fide test controls the validity of alterations, but even a bona fide alteration cannot impose a new payment obligation on a non-consenting member."},
    {t:"No, because annual levies are illegal for companies", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing makes a levy unlawful in itself; members who consent in writing can be bound. The issue is Kai's lack of consent."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.042], fn 158", takeaway:"New payment/subscription obligation: binds only members who consent in writing (s 39(3))." },

{ id:"c5x-035", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In March, Quill Pte Ltd declares a final dividend of $1 per share under its constitution, payable in June. In April, before payment, the members pass a special resolution amending the constitution so that dividends declared but unpaid may be cancelled by the board, and the board then cancels the March dividend.",
  prompt:"Is the cancellation effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: an alteration can't destroy an accrued right", correct:true,
     why:"An alteration made by special resolution is deemed part of the constitution 'on and from the date of the special resolution' (s 26(1AA)), which reflects the common law rule that constitutional alterations operate only prospectively ([05.042], citing James v Buena Ventura Nitrate Grounds Syndicate and Allen v Gold Reefs). Lee draws the consequence: a company 'may not, by altering its constitution, undermine or otherwise adversely affect any legal right or interest that has accrued under the constitution prior to the alteration'. Once declared in March, the dividend became a debt owed to the members. The April amendment can govern future dividends, but it cannot be used to cancel a right that had already accrued."},
    {t:"Yes: the special resolution was validly passed, so the board may act on it", correct:false,
     why:"Valid passage does not give an alteration retrospective force. The question is what the amendment can reach, and accrued rights are outside it."},
    {t:"Yes, if the cancellation is bona fide for the benefit of the company", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith is necessary for a valid alteration but does not allow it to operate retrospectively on accrued rights."},
    {t:"No, because dividends can never be cancelled once the constitution allows them", correct:false,
     why:"Future dividend policy can be changed by amendment. The problem is retrospective interference with a dividend already declared."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.042], fns 156–157", takeaway:"Alterations can't destroy rights already accrued." },

{ id:"cl5-041", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company and all its shareholders sign an agreement that the company will not increase its share capital without the consent of every party. The majority later wants to increase capital.",
  prompt:"What did the House of Lords hold in Russell v Northern Bank Development?",
  options:[
    {t:"Invalid against the company, but valid between the shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"It is well established that a company may not, in its constitution or any other agreement, contract to deprive itself of its statutory power to amend its constitution ([05.043], citing Southern Foundries, Allen and Russell). In Russell, clause 3 of a shareholders' agreement provided that the company would not increase its share capital without all parties' consent. The House of Lords held that, so far as the company was concerned, this was an unlawful attempt to oust its statutory power. As regards the shareholders, however, it was simply an agreement about how they would exercise their votes, and shareholders, like other property owners, are free to deal with their shares, including their votes, as they see fit. A declaration was granted that clause 3 was valid between the shareholders. The practical result: if shareholders with enough votes to block a special resolution are contractually bound to vote against an amendment, the company is effectively prevented from making it."},
    {t:"The clause was wholly valid and bound the company", correct:false,
     why:"The company itself cannot be bound not to exercise its statutory power to amend. That part of the clause was unlawful."},
    {t:"The clause was wholly void", correct:false,
     why:"The House of Lords severed the company's obligation from the shareholders' and upheld the latter."},
    {t:"It would have been valid if placed in the constitution instead", correct:false,
     why:"Russell suggests that a fetter in the constitution itself would also be invalid. That is now qualified only by statutory entrenching provisions under s 26A ([05.044])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.043]–[05.044], fns 159–161", takeaway:"Russell: company can't fetter itself; shareholders can agree how to vote." },

{ id:"c5x-037", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three venture investors in Glint Pte Ltd, together holding 30% of the votes, sign a voting agreement among themselves promising never to vote for any amendment to Glint's anti-dilution article. Glint is not a party. The founders, holding 70%, propose to amend the article.",
  prompt:"What is the likely effect of the voting agreement?",
  options:[
    {t:"Valid among them, and their 30% blocks the special resolution", correct:true,
     why:"Russell distinguishes between the company fettering its own power, which is unlawful, and shareholders agreeing how to vote, which is valid ([05.043]). Glint is not a party, so there is no attempt by the company to oust its statutory power. The investors are simply exercising their freedom to deal with their votes. A special resolution needs at least 75% of the votes cast. The founders have 70%, and the investors' 30% are contractually committed against the amendment, so the resolution cannot pass if they vote as agreed. Lee notes the result: 'a company may effectively be prevented from altering its constitution so long as those shareholders with sufficient votes to block a special resolution are contractually bound to do so'. If an investor broke ranks, the others could seek to enforce the agreement against him."},
    {t:"It is void because it fetters the company's statutory power to amend", correct:false,
     why:"That would be true if Glint itself had promised not to amend. Russell upholds agreements among shareholders about how they will vote."},
    {t:"It is valid but irrelevant, because 70% is enough to pass a special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"A special resolution needs at least 75% of votes cast. With 30% bound to vote against, 70% falls short."},
    {t:"It is valid only if registered with ACRA", correct:false,
     why:"A shareholders' voting agreement is a private contract and does not need registration to be enforceable among its parties."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.043]", takeaway:"Blocking stake (>25%) bound by a voting pact → amendment effectively blocked." },

{ id:"c5x-036", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At incorporation, all five founders of Opal Pte Ltd agree to include in its constitution a clause stating that the article on board composition may not be altered except by a resolution passed by 90% of the votes cast.",
  prompt:"Is the clause effective, given Russell's rule against fettering the power to amend?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it is a valid s 26A entrenching provision", correct:true,
     why:"Russell suggests a company may not include in its constitution a regulation that fetters its power to amend, but Lee [05.044] explains that this is now qualified by the statutory endorsement of entrenching provisions. Under s 26A(4), an entrenching provision is one stating that specified provisions may not be altered as the Act provides, or may be altered only by a specified majority greater than 75% or where other conditions are met ([05.048]). It qualifies only if it was in the constitution at incorporation or is later inserted with all members' agreement (s 26A(1)). Opal's clause requires 90% and was included at incorporation with all founders agreeing, so it is a valid entrenching provision. It can be removed or altered only with all members' agreement (s 26A(2))."},
    {t:"No: Russell prohibits any fetter on the power to amend the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"That was the common law starting point. Section 26A now expressly permits entrenching provisions as a legitimate fetter."},
    {t:"Only if a court approves the clause", correct:false,
     why:"No court approval is required. The statutory conditions are the content of the clause and how it was included."},
    {t:"Yes, but it can be removed by an ordinary special resolution later", correct:false,
     why:"An entrenching provision, once validly included, can be removed or altered only with the agreement of all members."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.044], [05.048], fns 162, 180–181", takeaway:"s 26A entrenching provisions: lawful fetters on amendment." },

{ id:"cl5-042", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"S has a 10-year contract as managing director. After a takeover, the company amends its articles so the new parent can remove any director. S is removed as a director and so ceases to be managing director.",
  prompt:"What was decided in Southern Foundries v Shirlaw?",
  options:[
    {t:"Amendment valid, but its use breached S's contract", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.045] uses Shirlaw to show that 'even when a company is free to amend its constitution, it is not always free to act upon the amendment'. Southern's articles tied the managing directorship to being a director, and its removal power was expressly subject to any agreement with the director. After Federated took over, the articles were amended to let Federated remove any director freely. The amendment was not motivated by any wish to remove S, but it was eventually used to remove him when terms for his continued appointment could not be agreed. The House of Lords held, by a majority, that Southern was free to amend its articles but remained bound by its contract with S, which was breached by the premature termination. S recovered against Southern for breach of contract and against Federated for inducing the breach."},
    {t:"The amendment was invalid", correct:false,
     why:"The amendment itself was valid; a company cannot contract out of its power to amend. The problem was acting on it in breach of contract."},
    {t:"S had no claim, because his appointment depended on the articles", correct:false,
     why:"His appointment as managing director was linked to the articles, but he also had a 10-year contract, which the company broke."},
    {t:"Only the parent was liable", correct:false,
     why:"Both were liable: Southern for breach of contract, Federated for inducing it."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.045], fn 164", takeaway:"Shirlaw: you may amend, but not use the amendment to break a contract." },

{ id:"c5x-038", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under a five-year consultancy agreement, Moss Pte Ltd agreed to pay a founder $15,000 a month. The members later pass a special resolution adding an article capping any payment to a founder at $5,000 a month, and the company starts paying only $5,000, citing the new article.",
  prompt:"What is the founder's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The amendment is valid, but Moss is liable for breach", correct:true,
     why:"This follows Southern Foundries v Shirlaw ([05.045]). A company cannot contract out of its statutory power to amend its constitution, so the special resolution is valid as an amendment. But 'the altered constitution could not … be relied upon to justify the breach of a pre-existing legal obligation'. The founder's right to $15,000 a month comes from the consultancy agreement, not from the constitution. By paying only $5,000, Moss breaches that agreement and is liable in damages. The position is different where an outsider's terms come only from articles incorporated into his contract (Swabey, [05.021]); those terms can be changed prospectively by amendment. Here the rate is an independent contractual term."},
    {t:"The founder must accept $5,000, because the constitution prevails over contracts", correct:false,
     why:"The constitution does not override a company's separate contractual obligations. It can change internal rules, not discharge debts owed under contracts."},
    {t:"The amendment is void because it conflicts with a contract", correct:false,
     why:"The amendment is valid. A company cannot fetter its power to amend by contract. The remedy is damages for breach, not invalidity."},
    {t:"The founder can recover only if he is also a member", correct:false,
     why:"He sues on the consultancy agreement, not the constitution, so membership is irrelevant."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.043], [05.045], fns 159, 164", takeaway:"Amendment valid; breaching a prior contract still costs damages." },

{ id:"cl5-043", lesson:"5F", type:"order",
  prompt:"Put the steps for altering a company's objects clause in order.",
  steps:[
    "Give at least 21 days' written notice to members and debenture trustees or holders",
    "Pass the special resolution at a general meeting",
    "Wait 21 days, during which qualifying dissentients may apply to court to cancel",
    "Lodge with the Registrar within 14 days after that period or the court's decision",
    "The alteration takes effect on lodgement"
  ],
  why:"Objects clauses used to define a company's capacity, so altering them has historically attracted stricter rules ([05.046]). That function is now much attenuated, since objects are optional, but the safeguards remain for any company that has chosen to include objects. The company, private or public, must give at least 21 days' written notice of the proposal (s 33(2)) to all members and to all trustees for debenture holders, or, where a class has no trustee, to all debenture holders of that class (s 33(3)). The alteration is made by special resolution (s 33(1)). Members or debenture holders meeting the prescribed threshold may, within 21 days of the resolution, apply to court to cancel it (ss 33(5)–(6)); if they do, the alteration takes effect only if the court confirms it. The resolution may not be lodged until 21 days have passed (s 33(8)), and must then be lodged within 14 days after that period, or after the court decides any application (s 33(9)). It takes effect on lodgement (s 33(10)).",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.046], fns 165–174", takeaway:"21 days' notice → SR → 21-day window → lodge within 14 days → effective." },

{ id:"c5x-039", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Saffron Ltd's constitution contains an objects clause. On 1 March it passes a special resolution, after proper notice, replacing the objects. No one applies to court. On 5 March the company secretary tries to lodge the resolution with the Registrar so that the change takes effect immediately.",
  prompt:"What is wrong with this plan?",
  options:[
    {t:"It can't be lodged until 21 days pass, and takes effect on lodgement", correct:true,
     why:"For objects clauses, s 33 imposes a timetable that differs from ordinary alterations ([05.046]). The 21 days after the resolution are a window for qualifying members or debenture holders to apply to court to cancel the alteration. To protect that window, s 33(8) provides that the resolution 'may not be lodged with the Registrar until after the lapse of 21 days from the date of the resolution'. With no application made, Saffron must lodge within 14 days after the 21 days expire (s 33(9)), and the alteration takes effect on lodgement (s 33(10)). Lodging on 5 March is premature. Contrast an ordinary alteration, which is deemed effective from the date of the special resolution and must simply be lodged within 14 days (s 26(1AA), (2))."},
    {t:"Nothing: like any alteration, it is effective from the date of the resolution once lodged within 14 days", correct:false,
     why:"That is the rule for ordinary alterations under s 26. Objects alterations follow the special s 33 timetable."},
    {t:"The alteration needed a court order before lodgement", correct:false,
     why:"Court confirmation is needed only if a qualifying dissentient applies to cancel. Without an application, no court order is required."},
    {t:"The notice period should have been 14 days for a private company", correct:false,
     why:"For objects alterations, at least 21 days' written notice is required whether the company is private or public (s 33(2))."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.046], fns 166, 172–174", takeaway:"Objects change: don't lodge until 21 days pass; effective on lodgement." },

{ id:"c5x-040", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"If qualifying dissentients apply to court within 21 days to cancel an alteration of a company's objects, what can the court do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Confirm or cancel on terms, or adjourn for a buy-out", correct:true,
     why:"Once an application is made, 'the alteration will not take effect except with the confirmation of the court' (s 33(5), [05.046]). Under s 33(7), the court must have regard to the rights and interests of the company's members and creditors, and may make an order cancelling or confirming the alteration in whole or in part, on such terms as it thinks fit, or may adjourn the proceedings so that an arrangement can be made for purchasing the dissentient members' interests. The buy-out option is a practical way of letting the majority proceed while giving dissenters an exit, which reflects the historical view that the objects were part of the basis on which members invested."},
    {t:"Only confirm the alteration, because it was passed by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The point of the application is that the court can cancel the alteration, not merely rubber-stamp it."},
    {t:"Only cancel the alteration if the dissentients prove bad faith", correct:false,
     why:"The court's discretion under s 33(7) is broader than the bona fide test and looks at members' and creditors' interests."},
    {t:"Refer the matter to the Registrar for decision", correct:false,
     why:"The court decides. The Registrar's role is to receive the lodgement once the timetable is complete."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.046], fns 170–171", takeaway:"Objects challenge: court may confirm, cancel (in part), or adjourn for buy-out." },

{ id:"cl5-044", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A pre-2016 company's memorandum contains a provision, included before 1 April 2004, fixing the dividend rate on ordinary shares. How can it now be altered?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only by unanimous agreement of the members", correct:true,
     why:"Before 1 April 2004, s 26(1) provided that the memorandum could be altered 'to the extent and in the manner provided by this Act but not otherwise' ([05.047]). A memorandum provision could be altered only if the Act specifically authorised it, as s 28 did for a change of name. A provision on a matter with no statutory power of alteration, such as a dividend rate on ordinary shares, was therefore unalterable. This was meant to protect shareholders, but it proved excessive, because even unanimous members could not change such a provision. The 2004 amendment made all memorandum provisions alterable by special resolution, but provisions entrenched in the memorandum before 1 April 2004 may now be altered only by unanimous agreement of the members (s 26(1B)). Since pre-2016 companies may keep their memorandum, this abated form of entrenchment survives."},
    {t:"By special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Special resolution suffices for other memorandum provisions after 2004, but not for those entrenched before 1 April 2004."},
    {t:"It can never be altered", correct:false,
     why:"That was the position before 2004. Now unanimous agreement allows alteration."},
    {t:"By ordinary resolution with court approval", correct:false,
     why:"No such route exists. The statutory requirement is unanimity."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.047], fns 175–178", takeaway:"Pre-2004 memorandum entrenchment: alterable only unanimously." },

{ id:"cl5-045", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is an entrenching provision under s 26A CA?",
  options:[
    {t:"A clause entrenching others, adopted at birth or unanimously", correct:true,
     why:"Section 26A(4) defines an entrenching provision as one stating that other specified provisions of the constitution may not be altered in the manner the Act provides, or may not be so altered except by a resolution passed by a specified majority greater than 75%, or where other specified conditions are met ([05.048]). It qualifies only if included in the constitution at incorporation or later inserted with the agreement of all members (s 26A(1)), and once validly included it can be removed or altered only with all members' agreement (s 26A(2)). Lee [05.049] notes that such provisions cut across the common law ban on fettering the power to amend, but were permitted as a way of limiting majority rule: amendment of a protected provision can be made subject to minority approval or to conditions outside the majority's control."},
    {t:"Any provision passed by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"An ordinary special resolution can neither create an entrenching provision after incorporation nor remove one. Unanimity is needed for both."},
    {t:"A provision placed in the memorandum before 2004", correct:false,
     why:"That is the older, abated form of entrenchment under s 26(1B) ([05.047]), a different mechanism."},
    {t:"A clause in a shareholders' agreement", correct:false,
     why:"A shareholders' agreement can achieve a similar result through voting commitments (Russell), but an entrenching provision is a term of the constitution itself."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.048]–[05.049], fns 179–183", takeaway:"s 26A: added at birth or unanimously; removed only unanimously." },

{ id:"cl5-046", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A private company has 30 members. Twenty-eight vote to add a provision requiring 90% approval to amend the clause on minority board seats. Two members object.",
  prompt:"Is it a valid entrenching provision under s 26A?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: insertion later requires unanimity", correct:true,
     why:"The proposed clause has the right content: it requires a majority above 75% to alter a specified provision, which fits s 26A(4)(b)(i). But s 26A(1) adds a condition about how it gets into the constitution: it must be there at incorporation, or be inserted later 'with the agreement of all the company's members' ([05.048]). Twenty-eight of thirty is about 93%, comfortably a special resolution, but not unanimity. The clause therefore does not qualify as an entrenching provision. Lee [05.049] notes that this requirement makes entrenching provisions impractical for large or listed companies, and even in small private companies they will be rare, because minorities usually lack the bargaining power to insist on them."},
    {t:"Yes: it passed by more than 75%", correct:false,
     why:"A special resolution is enough for ordinary amendments, but inserting an entrenching provision after incorporation requires unanimity."},
    {t:"Yes, because the threshold it sets is above 75%", correct:false,
     why:"The 90% threshold is permissible content, but the insertion procedure still requires all members to agree."},
    {t:"Only if the court approves", correct:false,
     why:"There is no court route to insert an entrenching provision over objection. Unanimity is required."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.048]–[05.049], fn 180", takeaway:"Inserting an entrenching provision later: all members must agree." },

{ id:"c5x-041", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Topaz Pte Ltd's constitution has, since incorporation, contained a valid entrenching provision protecting the minority's right to appoint one director. Twenty years later, members holding 98% want to remove the entrenching provision; one member holding 2% refuses.",
  prompt:"Can the 98% remove it?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: removal needs every member's agreement", correct:true,
     why:"Section 26A(2) provides that an entrenching provision 'may not be removed or altered except with the agreement of all the company's members' ([05.048]). That is what gives it force: Lee [05.049] explains that it 'further strengthens the minority's position' because the majority cannot simply vote it away. The 2% holder's refusal therefore blocks removal, however large the majority. If the majority considers the 2% holder's position unreasonable, it would have to negotiate, for example by buying the shares, or in an extreme case look for some other remedy; it cannot use a special resolution."},
    {t:"Yes: 98% far exceeds the 75% needed for a special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Special resolution is irrelevant to removing an entrenching provision. Unanimity is required."},
    {t:"Yes, after 20 years an entrenching provision lapses", correct:false,
     why:"There is no time limit on entrenching provisions."},
    {t:"Yes, with the court's sanction", correct:false,
     why:"Section 26A provides no court route to override a non-consenting member."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.048]–[05.049], fn 181", takeaway:"Removing an entrenching provision: every member must agree." }

]);

;
