window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl5-047", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's articles give it a lien over partly paid shares for members' debts. Z, who holds both partly and fully paid shares, dies owing large arrears on calls. The company amends its articles to extend the lien to fully paid shares. Z is the only member practically affected.",
  prompt:"What was decided in Allen v Gold Reefs of West Africa?",
  options:[
    {t:"Upheld: made bona fide, and hurting only Z wasn't bad faith", correct:true,
     why:"A shareholder is generally free to vote as he pleases, but when altering the constitution the majority is constrained by the requirement that the alteration be made 'bona fide for the benefit of the company as a whole' ([05.050]). Lindley MR explained in Allen that the power to alter, like all powers that let majorities bind minorities, must be exercised subject to general principles of law and equity, in good faith for the company as a whole. Applying that test ([05.051]), the Court of Appeal upheld the alteration. Z had subscribed on the basis that the articles were alterable. The fact that Z was the only member practically affected was not evidence of bad faith; the majority was plainly acting in the company's interests in taking steps to recover a large debt."},
    {t:"It was struck down because it targeted a single member", correct:false,
     why:"Targeting is the instinctive objection. But Allen holds that an alteration affecting only one member is not for that reason made in bad faith."},
    {t:"It was valid only for debts incurred after the alteration", correct:false,
     why:"The extended lien was used to recover Z's existing arrears. The alteration did not take away an accrued right of Z's; it changed the company's security going forward over shares still held."},
    {t:"It was void because the lien was in the memorandum", correct:false,
     why:"The lien was in the articles, which were alterable by special resolution."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.050]–[05.051], fns 184–185", takeaway:"Allen: bona fide for the company; hurting one member ≠ bad faith." },

{ id:"cl5-048", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the court decide whether an alteration was made bona fide for the company's benefit (Shuttleworth v Cox Brothers)?",
  options:[
    {t:"The shareholders' honest belief; the challenger must prove bad faith", correct:true,
     why:"Bankes LJ put it this way in Shuttleworth: 'the test is whether the alteration of the articles was in the opinion of the shareholders for the benefit of the company' ([05.052]). The court may not substitute its own view, because it is the shareholders, not the court, who manage the company's affairs. The core element is good faith. An alteration is presumed valid, and the burden of proof lies on the challenger ([05.053], Peters' American Delicacy; Citco). Bad faith means fraudulent or malicious intent, for example an alteration aimed at injuring a minority without regard to the company's interests (Sidebottom). Since direct evidence of malice is rare, bad faith may be inferred only exceptionally: where the alteration is so oppressive as to call the majority's good faith into question, or where no reasonable person could have thought it beneficial. Where an alteration is prima facie beneficial, only direct evidence of bad faith will rebut the presumption."},
    {t:"It decides for itself whether the alteration benefits the company", correct:false,
     why:"This would make the court the arbiter of business judgment, which Shuttleworth expressly rejects."},
    {t:"The majority must prove the alteration was beneficial", correct:false,
     why:"The burden is the other way: the alteration is presumed valid, and the challenger must prove bad faith."},
    {t:"Any detriment to a minority makes the alteration invalid", correct:false,
     why:"Detriment alone does not establish bad faith ([05.054]). Allen and Shuttleworth both upheld alterations that hurt particular members."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.052]–[05.053], fns 186–195", takeaway:"Shuttleworth: shareholders' honest opinion; challenger proves bad faith." },

{ id:"c5x-042", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After a bitter personal falling-out with minority shareholder Lena, the majority of Garnet Pte Ltd amends the articles to strip Lena's class of shares, and only hers, of dividend rights. The company gains nothing; the majority's emails say the aim is 'to make Lena regret crossing us'.",
  prompt:"How is a court likely to treat the alteration?",
  options:[
    {t:"Invalid: direct evidence of malice is bad faith", correct:true,
     why:"The bona fide test is deferential, but it is not toothless ([05.053]). An alteration is made in bad faith 'if it is made with fraudulent or malicious intent', and Lee gives as the example 'one aimed at injuring a minority shareholder without regard to the company's interests' (Sidebottom v Kershaw, Leese). Normally the challenger struggles because direct evidence of malice is rare. Here there is direct evidence: the majority's own emails show the purpose is to punish Lena, and the company gains nothing. The presumption of validity is rebutted, and the special resolution does not save the alteration. Lena could also consider s 216, since a malicious alteration of this kind is likely to be commercially unfair ([05.076])."},
    {t:"As valid: it was passed by special resolution and courts defer to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Deference applies to the shareholders' honest assessment. It does not protect an alteration shown to be motivated by malice."},
    {t:"As valid, because detriment to one member never shows bad faith", correct:false,
     why:"Detriment alone does not show bad faith, but here there is direct evidence of a malicious motive, which does."},
    {t:"As valid unless the court thinks it unwise for the company", correct:false,
     why:"The court does not judge wisdom. It invalidates the alteration because of bad faith, not because it disagrees with a business decision."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.053], fns 192–193", takeaway:"Malice proven (aim to injure minority, no company benefit) → alteration fails." },

{ id:"cl5-049", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The articles are amended so that a director must vacate office when all the other directors ask. The amendment was prompted by the wish to remove the plaintiff director, who had failed to account for cash payments due to the company.",
  prompt:"Was the amendment valid? (Shuttleworth v Cox Brothers)",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: detriment without malice is not bad faith", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.054] uses Shuttleworth alongside Allen to show that 'the mere fact that a minority shareholder would be adversely affected by the alteration is not by itself a sufficient ground for inferring bad faith'. The amendment was prompted by the desire to end the plaintiff's directorship, and it operated to his detriment. But there was no evidence of malice. On the contrary, having discovered that he had failed to account for various cash payments due to the company, the majority was acting in good faith to protect the company from further harm. Since the shareholders honestly believed the change was for the company's benefit, the court would not interfere."},
    {t:"No: it was aimed at one director", correct:false,
     why:"An alteration aimed at one person can still be made in good faith, as here, where that person had harmed the company."},
    {t:"No: directors can only be removed by ordinary resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The articles may provide additional mechanisms for vacating office. (For public companies, s 152(1) entrenches removal by ordinary resolution as a floor, not an exclusive route.)"},
    {t:"Only if the court itself agreed the change benefited the company", correct:false,
     why:"The court defers to the shareholders' honest opinion and does not substitute its own."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.054], fn 186", takeaway:"Detriment to one + good faith = valid." },

{ id:"cl5-050", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A BVI company in cash-flow trouble amends its articles to create Class B shares with 50 votes each and issues them to T, the chairman, giving him indisputable control. Investors and bankers will only provide funds if T controls the company, and T, a major lender, threatens to recall his loan otherwise. T votes on the resolutions.",
  prompt:"What did the Privy Council decide in Citco Banking v Pusser's?",
  options:[
    {t:"Valid, and T could vote despite his interest", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.055] calls Citco a 'more striking' illustration. Converting T's position into indisputable control looks like a classic majority grab. But the Privy Council found the amendments were made in good faith. The company was facing cash-flow difficulties; prospective investors and bankers would supply fresh equity and credit only if T, seen as the key person to turn the business around, had unquestioned control; and T had lent the company a large sum that he would recall otherwise. Vesting control in T was critical to survival, so a reasonable shareholder could in good faith conclude the alteration was for the company's benefit. T was not barred from voting despite his personal interest, consistent with the general rule that a shareholder may vote on matters in which he is interested (North-West Transportation v Beatty). Lee concludes ([05.056]) that a finding of good faith will generally dispose of a challenge."},
    {t:"Invalid, because T gained a personal advantage", correct:false,
     why:"Personal advantage to the person voting does not invalidate an alteration made in good faith for the company's benefit."},
    {t:"Invalid, because weighted voting shares are unlawful", correct:false,
     why:"The case turned on good faith, not on the lawfulness of weighted votes as such."},
    {t:"Valid only because T abstained", correct:false,
     why:"T voted, and the Privy Council held he was entitled to."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.055]–[05.056], fns 196–197", takeaway:"Citco: control shift upheld; interested shareholder may vote." },

{ id:"cl5-051", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"The bona fide test has an objective component. Which explanation imposes a TRUE objective limit, rather than just being evidence of subjective bad faith?",
  options:[
    {t:"That the honest majority ignored matters it ought to have considered", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.056] describes the test as predominantly subjective, but qualified: an alteration will not stand if no reasonable person would consider it beneficial. She offers two explanations. On the first, the absence of any reasonable basis is simply evidence that the majority was not acting in good faith; the inquiry stays subjective, and the objective element is only a way of testing honesty. On the second, drawn from Shuttleworth, the majority may have acted with the best intentions but failed to take into account matters they ought to have considered. That 'imposes a true objective limit': an honest majority's alteration can fail because it omitted matters which, in the court's view, should have been considered."},
    {t:"That no reasonable person could have thought the alteration beneficial, which shows the majority was dishonest", correct:false,
     why:"This is the first explanation. It uses an objective yardstick, but only as evidence of subjective bad faith."},
    {t:"That the court may substitute its own business judgment", correct:false,
     why:"Both explanations stop short of this. The court does not decide what is best for the company."},
    {t:"That any detriment to a minority is objectively unfair", correct:false,
     why:"Detriment alone is not enough under either explanation ([05.054])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.056], fns 198–199", takeaway:"True objective limit: honest majority ignored relevant matters." },

{ id:"cl5-052", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The majority shareholders want to sell to an outsider without offering shares to other members first. They amend the pre-emption article so transfers to non-members are allowed if approved by ordinary resolution. G, holding 2%, loses his pre-emption rights while the majority keeps control over his transfers.",
  prompt:"What did the Court of Appeal decide in Greenhalgh v Arderne Cinemas?",
  options:[
    {t:"It stood: no dishonesty, and rights are alterable by 75%", correct:true,
     why:"The amended article applied to all shareholders equally on its face, but in effect it discriminated against minorities like G: they lost their pre-emption rights, while the majority, through its control of ordinary resolutions, kept control over the minority's transfers ([05.059]). The Court of Appeal still upheld it. There was no evidence of dishonesty, and a person who subscribes for shares must expect their rights to be altered by a three-quarter majority. Lord Evershed MR reformulated the test for this context ([05.060]): 'the company as a whole' meant the corporators as a general body, and one could ask whether the change was, in the honest opinion of those voting for it, for the benefit of an individual hypothetical member. Alternatively, an amendment would be impeached if it discriminated so as to give the majority an advantage denied to the minority. Yet the amendment, which arguably did exactly that, was upheld."},
    {t:"The amendment was struck down as discriminatory", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Evershed stated a discrimination test, which makes this tempting, but he did not apply it to strike the amendment down."},
    {t:"G was entitled to be bought out at fair value", correct:false,
     why:"No buy-out was ordered. Today a minority in G's position might seek one under s 216, but that is a different remedy."},
    {t:"The amendment needed unanimity", correct:false,
     why:"A special resolution was sufficient. Pre-emption rights in the articles were not entrenched."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.059]–[05.060], fns 201–204", takeaway:"Greenhalgh: 'hypothetical member' and 'discrimination' tests; amendment upheld." },

{ id:"cl5-053", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Where an amendment adjusts rights between shareholder factions with no real impact on the company, what approach do later decisions favour?",
  options:[
    {t:"Uphold it unless oppressive, unjust or ultra vires", correct:true,
     why:"Latham CJ observed in Peters' American Delicacy v Heath that where the question is 'simply a question as to the relative rights of different classes of shareholders the problem cannot be solved by regarding merely the benefit of the corporation' ([05.058]). Greenhalgh's attempts to recast the test did not help ([05.061]–[05.062]). The 'hypothetical member' is hard to construct when factions are at war, and the test would require members to vote altruistically although members owe each other no fiduciary duties. The 'discrimination test' contradicts authorities holding that disadvantage to a minority is not bad faith. Later decisions (Re Charterhouse Capital) therefore focus on vitiating factors ([05.063]): such an alteration stands 'provided that the amendment does not amount to oppression of the minority or is otherwise unjust or is outside the scope of the power'. The real complaint in such cases is unfair deprivation of the minority, so s 216 offers a broader review of fairness."},
    {t:"Apply the hypothetical member test strictly", correct:false,
     why:"Lee explains why the hypothetical member test is unworkable in faction disputes."},
    {t:"Invalidate any amendment that favours the majority", correct:false,
     why:"That is the discrimination test taken literally, and it would contradict Greenhalgh itself and the other authorities."},
    {t:"Refer the question to the Registrar", correct:false,
     why:"The Registrar has no role in reviewing the fairness of alterations."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.058], [05.061]–[05.063], fns 205–210", takeaway:"Faction disputes: look for oppression/injustice; consider s 216." },

{ id:"c5x-043", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Quartz Pte Ltd's two founding families hold 85% and 15%. The majority family amends the articles so that dividends on 'family B' shares (the minority's class) are paid only after family A shares receive a fixed preference. The company's own finances are unaffected; the change simply shifts returns between the families. The minority asks whether the bona fide test protects it.",
  prompt:"What is the best advice?",
  options:[
    {t:"The bona fide test is weak here; s 216 is the better route", correct:true,
     why:"This is the situation Latham CJ described in Peters' American Delicacy: when an alteration merely adjusts the relative rights of shareholders, 'the problem cannot be solved by regarding merely the benefit of the corporation' ([05.058]). The company itself is untouched, so asking whether the change benefits 'the company' does not get at the real complaint, which is that the minority has been unfairly deprived of value. Later cases uphold such an alteration unless it is oppressive, otherwise unjust or outside the power ([05.063]), so the minority would need to show more than disadvantage. Lee notes that an aggrieved shareholder 'may stand a better chance of securing redress' under s 216, which allows a broader review of the majority's conduct, including breaches of legitimate expectations ([05.076])."},
    {t:"The bona fide test will strike the change down because it discriminates against the minority", correct:false,
     why:"Discrimination alone has not been enough; Greenhalgh upheld a discriminatory change. The minority would need oppression, injustice or bad faith."},
    {t:"The change is automatically valid because it was passed by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"A special resolution is necessary, but the alteration remains subject to the bona fide test, the limits on oppression and injustice, and s 216."},
    {t:"The minority must show the change harms the company's creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Creditor harm is not the test. The issue is fairness between shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.058], [05.063], [05.076], fns 208–210", takeaway:"Pure faction dispute → bona fide test weak; look to s 216." },

{ id:"cl5-054", lesson:"5F", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each case to its outcome on compulsory transfer clauses.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Sidebottom v Kershaw, Leese", r:"Power to buy out competing members at fair value upheld"},
    {l:"Brown v British Abrasive Wheel", r:"9/10 majority power to force sales struck down"},
    {l:"Dafen Tinplate v Llanelly Steel", r:"Unrestricted transfer power invalid despite protective motive"},
    {l:"Re Bugle Press", r:"90% holders couldn't use their own vehicle to squeeze out the minority"}
  ],
  why:"Compulsory transfer clauses are enforceable if in the constitution from incorporation ([05.064]); the difficulty is inserting them later. In Sidebottom ([05.065]), directors were empowered to require a member carrying on a competing business to transfer his shares at fair value. The Court of Appeal upheld it as an honest attempt to protect the company, and held there is no doctrinal objection to inserting expropriation clauses by amendment. Brown ([05.067]) struck down a clause letting a nine-tenths majority force a sale at fair value, introduced so the majority would fund the company only with full control; Astbury J thought it not just and equitable and for the majority's benefit, though there was no finding of bad faith. Dafen ([05.068]) invalidated an unrestricted power to compel transfers despite a protective motive, because it was 'naked', though Peterson J accepted a narrower clause aimed at members interested in rival firms would be valid. Re Bugle Press ([05.069]) held that 90% holders could not use a company they set up to invoke the statutory squeeze-out against the minority; Harman LJ called it 'a barefaced attempt' to expropriate a minority.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.064]–[05.069], fns 211–220", takeaway:"Harm-based expulsion: OK. Naked expropriation: suspect." },

{ id:"c5x-047", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Flint Pte Ltd's members amend its articles to let the board compel any member to sell his shares at fair value to a person the board nominates, 'whenever the board considers it desirable'. The amendment was prompted by one member who had joined a competitor and was leaking trade information.",
  prompt:"Under the English authorities, what is the main vulnerability of this amendment?",
  options:[
    {t:"Its breadth: it goes beyond what the harm requires (Dafen)", correct:true,
     why:"The motive is the kind Sidebottom accepts: removing a member whose competing activities harm the company ([05.065]). But Lee's summary of the English position ([05.071]) adds a limit: such a clause 'may only be upheld as bona fide if its scope is no wider than necessary for the redress of the actual or threatened harm' (Dafen). Flint's clause applies to any member whenever the board finds it desirable, with no link to harm. In Dafen, Peterson J invalidated a similar unrestricted power despite a protective motive, while accepting that a narrower clause aimed at members interested in rival firms would have been valid ([05.068]). The safer drafting would confine the power to members who compete with or harm the company."},
    {t:"None: Sidebottom holds that any compulsory transfer clause can be added by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Sidebottom holds there is no doctrinal bar, but it upheld a clause triggered by competition, not an unrestricted power."},
    {t:"Its price: a fair value price makes any clause valid", correct:false,
     why:"Fair value was present in Brown and Dafen, and both clauses were struck down. Price alone does not cure an overbroad power."},
    {t:"The board cannot be given such a power; only members can", correct:false,
     why:"In Sidebottom the power was given to the directors. Who exercises it is not the main problem; its breadth is."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.065], [05.068], [05.071], fns 227–228", takeaway:"Expulsion clause must be no wider than the harm it addresses." },

{ id:"c5x-046", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shareholders holding 90% of Beryl Pte Ltd set up NewCo, which they own, to make an offer for all Beryl shares. They accept the offer themselves, so NewCo reaches 90%, and NewCo then invokes the statutory squeeze-out (s 215 in Singapore) against the dissenting 10%. The minority has done nothing to harm Beryl.",
  prompt:"How did the English Court of Appeal deal with this kind of scheme in Re Bugle Press?",
  options:[
    {t:"Refused: no independent acquirer; a barefaced expropriation", correct:true,
     why:"The facts track Re Bugle Press closely ([05.069]). The statutory power, then s 209 of the UK Companies Act 1948 and similar to s 215 here (fn 218), lets a person who has acquired at least 90% compulsorily acquire the rest. The Court of Appeal held it could be invoked only where the acquisition was made by a party independent of the majority. Otherwise the majority could always force out the minority by forming a nominally capitalised company to buy their own shares. Harman LJ called the scheme 'a barefaced attempt to evade that fundamental rule of company law which forbids the majority of shareholders, unless the articles so provide, to expropriate a minority'. Lord Evershed MR suggested the result might differ if the minority were acting destructively against the company's interests. Lee reads the case as suggesting that bare expropriation is prohibited except where necessary to protect the company from harm."},
    {t:"It allowed the squeeze-out because NewCo technically held 90%", correct:false,
     why:"The court looked at substance: the 90% came from the majority itself, not an independent bidder."},
    {t:"It allowed it if the price offered was fair", correct:false,
     why:"Fairness of price did not save the scheme. The objection was to using the statutory mechanism to expropriate without an independent acquirer."},
    {t:"It held compulsory acquisition of minority shares is never lawful", correct:false,
     why:"Statutory squeeze-outs are lawful where an independent acquirer reaches 90%. The problem was abuse of the mechanism."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.069], fns 217–220", takeaway:"Re Bugle Press: no self-made 90% squeeze-out." },

{ id:"cl5-055", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the English position on inserting a 'naked' compulsory transfer clause by amendment?",
  options:[
    {t:"Uncertain beyond harm-based clauses", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.071] summarises: compulsory transfer clauses are enforceable if in the constitution from the outset (Phillips v Manufacturers' Securities); a later alteration introducing one will likely be upheld if motivated by the need to remove members who have harmed or threaten to harm the company (Sidebottom; Staray Capital); and such a clause is valid only if no wider than necessary (Dafen). Beyond that, the position is unclear ([05.070]). Brown and Dafen predate Shuttleworth, which criticised Dafen for splitting the bona fide test into two limbs when it is a single composite test. Shuttleworth's subjective emphasis may suggest naked clauses are valid absent bad faith, even if aimed at positive gain, but no case has tested an outright compulsory transfer clause under that approach. The UK Company Law Review called the area 'surprisingly unclear' ([05.075]), and Constable v Executive Connections called it 'an untidy state of the law' (fn 229)."},
    {t:"Always invalid", correct:false,
     why:"Sidebottom shows that harm-based clauses inserted by amendment can be valid, so the position is not that rigid."},
    {t:"Always valid if passed by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The bona fide test still applies, and Brown and Dafen struck down clauses passed by the required majority."},
    {t:"Governed by Gambotto", correct:false,
     why:"Gambotto is an Australian High Court decision. The UK Company Law Review declined to adopt it."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.070]–[05.071], [05.075], fns 221–229", takeaway:"England: harm-based OK; naked expropriation unresolved." },

{ id:"c5x-044", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Since incorporation, Coral Pte Ltd's constitution has contained a drag-along clause: if holders of 75% accept a general offer for all shares, the remaining members must sell on the same terms. An offer is accepted by 80%, and a 5% holder who joined later objects that the clause expropriates him.",
  prompt:"How strong is his objection?",
  options:[
    {t:"Weak: original drag-alongs are enforceable", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.064] states that it is not disputed that expropriation provisions 'are legal and enforceable if they were included in the constitution from the time of the company's incorporation', and [05.071] repeats that naked share transfer provisions are enforceable if included from the outset (Phillips v Manufacturers' Securities). The 5% holder joined later and took his shares on the terms of the existing constitution. Fn 226 adds that once such a clause is in the constitution from the start, later amendments to its scope are not open to attack merely because of their involuntary nature (Re Charterhouse Capital), and fn 225 notes Re Charterhouse upheld a drag-along clause of this kind. The contested question in English law concerns inserting such clauses later, which is not this case."},
    {t:"Strong: drag-along clauses are naked expropriation and always invalid", correct:false,
     why:"Constable v Executive Connections suggested a drag-along is 'very close' to a naked clause (fn 225), but that concern relates to inserting one by amendment. A clause present from incorporation is enforceable."},
    {t:"Strong, unless Gambotto's proper purpose test is met", correct:false,
     why:"Gambotto is an Australian decision about inserting expropriation clauses by amendment. It has not been adopted in England or Singapore, and it concerned alteration, not original clauses."},
    {t:"Weak, but only if the offer price is above market value", correct:false,
     why:"An above-market price is not a condition of enforcing an original drag-along clause. The clause's terms govern."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.064], [05.071], fns 211, 225–226", takeaway:"Drag-along in the constitution from day one: enforceable." },

{ id:"cl5-056", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What test did the Australian High Court set in Gambotto v WCP for amendments conferring a power of expropriation?",
  options:[
    {t:"Proper purpose plus fairness; majority's onus", correct:true,
     why:"In Gambotto ([05.072]), the High Court struck down an amendment inserting a compulsory acquisition power even though the company stood to save over A$4m in tax and administrative costs once the majority had full control. It held the bona fide test inappropriate for expropriation (and, fn 231, for adjusting conflicting shareholder rights generally). An expropriation amendment is lawful only if (a) it is for a proper purpose and (b) it does not operate oppressively. On (a), the majority judges held that conferring a power to expropriate minorities does not fall within the purposes of the power to alter, even on fair terms, unless it is reasonably apprehended that the minority's continued holding is detrimental to the company and expropriation is a reasonable means of addressing that. So protecting the company from harm is proper; securing positive gain is not. The onus lies on those supporting the expropriation. On (b) ([05.073]), the alteration must be 'fair in the circumstances', procedurally (eg full disclosure) and substantively (a fair price, for which market value is useful but not conclusive)."},
    {t:"The standard bona fide test", correct:false,
     why:"Gambotto expressly held the bona fide test inappropriate for expropriation amendments."},
    {t:"Any amendment offering fair value is valid", correct:false,
     why:"Fair value relates to the second limb only. Without a proper purpose, a fair price does not save the amendment."},
    {t:"Only unanimous consent will do", correct:false,
     why:"Gambotto did not require unanimity. It required proper purpose and fairness, with the onus on the majority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.072]–[05.073], fns 230–238", takeaway:"Gambotto: proper purpose + fairness; onus on the majority." },

{ id:"c5x-045", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A minority shareholder of Jasper Ltd has become a director of its main competitor and is using his shareholder rights to obtain Jasper's pricing data. The majority amends the articles to allow compulsory acquisition of any shareholder who is a director or officer of a competitor, after full disclosure to all members and at an independently valued price.",
  prompt:"Would the amendment likely survive under Gambotto?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: it averts detriment, on fair terms", correct:true,
     why:"Gambotto's two limbs are both addressed ([05.072]–[05.073]). On proper purpose, the High Court accepted that expropriation may be proper 'where it is reasonably apprehended that the continued shareholding of the minority is detrimental to the company … and expropriation is a reasonable means of eliminating or mitigating that detriment'. A competitor's director mining pricing data through his shareholding fits that description, and the clause is targeted at that risk, not at minorities generally. On fairness, procedural fairness requires a fair process such as full disclosure, and substantive fairness largely requires a fair price, for which an independent valuation is good evidence. Those supporting the amendment bear the onus, but on these facts they are well placed to discharge it. The same amendment would also likely pass the English bona fide test under Sidebottom."},
    {t:"No: Gambotto forbids all expropriation amendments", correct:false,
     why:"Gambotto forbids expropriation for positive gain, not expropriation that protects the company from detriment."},
    {t:"No, because the majority benefits from removing a competitor", correct:false,
     why:"Benefit to the majority is inherent in any expropriation. Under Gambotto the question is whether the purpose is to avert detriment to the company, which it is here."},
    {t:"Yes, but only because the price is fair; purpose is irrelevant", correct:false,
     why:"Purpose is the first and decisive limb in Gambotto. Fair price is necessary but not sufficient."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.072]–[05.073], fns 235–238", takeaway:"Gambotto passes: avert detriment + fair process and price." },

{ id:"cl5-057", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which criticism of Gambotto is borne out by its own facts?",
  options:[
    {t:"It rewards tiny hold-outs", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.074] lists four criticisms of Gambotto. First, the benefit/detriment distinction is untenable: the financial result of a gain made and a loss avoided is the same, and both accrue to the majority after expropriation (a point McHugh J also made). Second, it is unclear how the 'proper purpose' of the power to alter is to be discerned when the statute states no limit. Third, labelling shares as property masks the value judgment the court must make and is out of step with seeing shares as a capitalised stream of income, which does not imply indefeasibility. Fourth, it is economically inefficient because it encourages minorities to hold out for unrealistic values. That last criticism is borne out by the facts: the 99.7% majority abstained, the resolution was carried by minority holders with about 0.2%, and the plaintiffs, with 0.1%, did not attend. By invalidating it, the court denied the supporting minority the same 'proprietary' right to exit at a price the plaintiffs conceded was fair."},
    {t:"It gave majorities too much power", correct:false,
     why:"The criticism runs the other way: Gambotto arguably gives small minorities too much power to block value-creating restructurings."},
    {t:"It ignored the price offered", correct:false,
     why:"Fair price was part of the Gambotto test, and the plaintiffs conceded the price was fair. The criticism is that a fair price was not enough."},
    {t:"It simply applied English law", correct:false,
     why:"Gambotto deliberately departed from the English bona fide approach."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.074], fns 239–245", takeaway:"Gambotto: hold-out problem, shaky benefit/detriment line." },

{ id:"cl5-058", lesson:"5F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What approach does the chapter suggest for Singapore on compulsory acquisition amendments?",
  options:[
    {t:"A sharper bona fide test, plus s 216", correct:true,
     why:"The issue has not yet arisen in Singapore ([05.075]). Lee suggests the traditional bona fide test, applied with greater exactitude about what good faith means here, can balance minority protection against beneficial restructuring. Her premise is that a share, as a creature of statute, is not designed as an indefeasible right. The Companies Act itself allows shares to be defeated without the holder's consent through capital reductions, schemes of arrangement, amalgamations and the s 215 compulsory acquisition after 90% acceptance. Its general approach is not to prohibit beneficial acquisitions but to add procedural safeguards for fair terms. The bona fide test fits that: it requires benefit to the company (avoiding detriment or positive gain), and an acquisition below fair value or a material procedural lapse, without reasonable justification, can be evidence that the majority acted to aggrandise itself rather than in good faith. Lee adds that s 216 may ultimately be the better protection against abuse."},
    {t:"Adopt Gambotto in full", correct:false,
     why:"Lee sets out the criticisms of Gambotto and notes the UK declined to adopt it. She prefers a sharpened bona fide test."},
    {t:"Prohibit all compulsory acquisition clauses", correct:false,
     why:"That would conflict with the Act's own recognition, through ss 215 and others, that compulsory acquisitions can be desirable."},
    {t:"Leave it entirely to the majority's discretion", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith, fairness of price and process, and s 216 all continue to constrain the majority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.075], fns 246–254", takeaway:"Singapore (suggested): sharper bona fide test + s 216." },

{ id:"c5x-048", lesson:"5F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In Singapore, Onyx Pte Ltd's 92% majority amends its articles to force out a passive 8% minority at 60% of an independent valuation, with the explanatory circular omitting a pending sale that would triple the share value. The minority has never harmed the company.",
  prompt:"Applying the approach Lee suggests for Singapore, how vulnerable is the amendment?",
  options:[
    {t:"Highly vulnerable: bad faith may be inferred, and s 216 applies", correct:true,
     why:"Lee's suggested approach ([05.075]) keeps the bona fide test but applies it 'with greater exactitude'. In this context, 'an acquisition at below fair value, or a material lapse in procedural propriety, may give rise to inferences of bad faith', so that 'the majority's failure to act fairly, or to offer a fair price, may (in the absence of reasonable justifications) be good evidence that they had not acted honestly with a view to promoting the company's interests but to aggrandise themselves'. Onyx's amendment has both defects: a price of 60% of independent value and a circular hiding a value-tripling sale. There is no harm by the minority to justify expulsion. And Lee stresses that the bona fide test does not exhaust the minority's remedies: s 216 may permit a broader view of oppression ([05.076])."},
    {t:"Safe: shares are not indefeasible, so a 92% majority may expropriate on any terms", correct:false,
     why:"Shares are not indefeasible, but the law permits expropriation only with fair terms and safeguards. Lee's approach uses unfair terms as evidence of bad faith."},
    {t:"Safe, because Singapore has adopted Gambotto", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore has not adopted Gambotto, and in any case Gambotto would also condemn this amendment for lacking a proper purpose and fairness."},
    {t:"Vulnerable only if the minority proves direct evidence of malice", correct:false,
     why:"Direct evidence is not always required. Bad faith may be inferred from an unfair price and a material procedural lapse."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.075]–[05.076]", takeaway:"Low price + hidden facts → inferred bad faith; s 216 too." },

{ id:"cl5-059", lesson:"5F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: An alteration passed by special resolution in good faith may still be invalidated on an application under s 216 CA.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [05.076] treats the oppression remedy as a constraint additional to the bona fide test. A shareholder adversely affected by an alteration may apply under s 216, and the alteration may be invalidated if the majority has conducted the company's affairs in a way that amounts to commercial unfairness. The alteration may be evidence of such unfairness if it violates a contractual or other legal obligation binding the company or the majority, or breaches the minority shareholder's legitimate expectations. Because s 216 looks at fairness rather than only honesty, an alteration that survives the bona fide test can still fall under it. Chapter 11 develops the oppression remedy in detail."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"This would be right if good faith were the only constraint. Section 216 operates alongside it and asks a different question: whether the conduct was commercially unfair to the applicant."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 5 [05.063], [05.076], fns 255–256", takeaway:"s 216 can undo an alteration even if bona fide." }

]);

;
