/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 6 · Lesson 6A Separate Legal Entity (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 6 [06.001]–[06.003], fns 1–3. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl6-001", lesson:"6A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does it mean that a company is a separate legal person?",
  options:[
    {t:"It holds its own rights and debts; members only vote", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.001] states the principle in s 19(5): an incorporated company is a separate legal person, distinct from those who own it. It is therefore fully capable of acquiring rights and incurring liabilities on its own. Its members do not own its rights and assets and are not personally liable for its liabilities. Their connection to the company's affairs runs through governance: they influence how its rights and liabilities are managed by voting at company meetings. Everything else in this chapter, from limited liability to the narrow exceptions that let courts look through the company, builds on this starting point."},
    {t:"Its members jointly own its assets", correct:false,
     why:"This is how a partnership's assets are often described, which is why it is tempting. A company's members have no legal or equitable interest in its property (Macaura, [06.009])."},
    {t:"It is an agent of its controlling shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Salomon held that a company, even a one-man company, is neither an agent nor a trustee for its controller; it carries on business for its own benefit ([06.003])."},
    {t:"Its directors are personally liable for its debts", correct:false,
     why:"The company incurs its own liabilities. Directors are liable only in specific situations, such as where they personally commit or procure a wrong (Ch 7) or under particular statutory provisions."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.001], fn 1", takeaway:"Company owns its rights and debts; members vote." },

{ id:"cl6-002", lesson:"6A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A sole trader transfers his boot business to a company in return for shares and secured debentures. His wife and five children hold one share each; he holds the rest. The company soon goes into liquidation. He seeks to enforce his debentures; the liquidator says he is personally liable for all the company's debts.",
  prompt:"What did the House of Lords decide in Salomon v A Salomon & Co?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company was a distinct person; his debentures were valid", correct:true,
     why:"Aron Salomon transferred his thriving business to 'Aron Salomon and Co, Ltd' in 1892, receiving shares and debentures, so he was both near-total owner and secured creditor ([06.002]). When the boot trade slumped and the company was liquidated, the liquidator succeeded at first instance and in the Court of Appeal. The House of Lords unanimously reversed ([06.003]). Once duly incorporated, the company was a distinct person able to own and operate its own business; it was neither agent nor trustee for Salomon, even though he substantially owned it. So it incurred its debts in its own right, Salomon was not liable for them, and the company could validly borrow from and give security to its own shareholder. His debentures ranked ahead of the unsecured creditors. Lee notes the case's primary significance is confirming that 'even a one-man company is an entity distinct from its shareholder' (fn 3)."},
    {t:"He was liable because the company was his agent", correct:false,
     why:"This was essentially the reasoning of the lower courts. The House of Lords rejected it: the company carried on the business for itself, not as Salomon's agent."},
    {t:"The debentures were void because he controlled the company", correct:false,
     why:"Control did not invalidate the debentures. A separate company can borrow from, and grant security to, its controlling shareholder."},
    {t:"The family's nominal shares made the company a sham", correct:false,
     why:"The family's one-share holdings did not make the company any less real. Salomon is authority that a company dominated by one person is still a separate entity."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.002]–[06.003], fns 2–3", takeaway:"Salomon: even a one-man company is a separate person." },

{ id:"c6x-002", lesson:"6A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ng owns 99.9% of Ng Trading Pte Ltd and makes every decision. When the company cannot pay a supplier, the supplier sues Ng personally, arguing that the company is merely Ng's agent or trustee because he controls everything it does.",
  prompt:"How does Salomon answer the supplier's argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"Control doesn't make a company its owner's agent or trustee", correct:true,
     why:"The supplier's argument is the one the House of Lords rejected in Salomon ([06.003]). Their Lordships held that a duly incorporated company is a distinct person 'neither an agent nor a trustee' for its controller, and that this is so 'even though the company was substantially or wholly owned' by one person. The debt was incurred by Ng Trading in its own right, and Ng is not liable for it merely because he owns and runs the company. The supplier would need something more, such as a personal guarantee from Ng, a genuine agency relationship on the facts ([06.068]), or one of the narrow grounds for disregarding the company discussed later in this chapter."},
    {t:"The argument succeeds, because a 99.9% owner effectively is the company", correct:false,
     why:"This is the intuition Salomon overturned. Economic identity between owner and company does not merge them in law."},
    {t:"The argument succeeds if Ng signed the contract personally on the company's behalf", correct:false,
     why:"Signing as the company's agent binds the company, not Ng (compare Lee v Lee's Air Farming, [06.006]). Ng would be liable only if he contracted in his own name or gave a guarantee."},
    {t:"The argument succeeds because the remaining 0.1% holder is a nominee", correct:false,
     why:"Salomon's family held one share each, and it made no difference. Even a single-member company is a separate person."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.003], fn 3", takeaway:"Control ≠ agency: Salomon rejects the 'mere agent' argument." },

{ id:"cl6-003", lesson:"6A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Salomon decided that a company cannot validly grant security to a shareholder who owns almost all its shares.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Salomon decided the opposite. Because the company is a distinct person, 'the company as a distinct person could borrow from and create security in favour of its shareholders' ([06.003]). Salomon's debentures were therefore valid, and he recovered the company's debt to him in priority to its unsecured creditors. The result was criticised as harsh on trade creditors, but it follows directly from separate personality: a shareholder who lends money to the company is a creditor like any other, and secured lending by owners remains common in practice."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This is what the liquidator argued and what the lower courts effectively accepted. The House of Lords upheld Salomon's debentures."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.003]", takeaway:"A company can owe its own shareholder, and secure the debt." },

{ id:"c6x-001", lesson:"6A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Founder Siti owns all the shares of Kiwi Pte Ltd. She lends it $500,000 secured by a registered charge over its assets. Kiwi later becomes insolvent with assets worth $600,000, unsecured trade debts of $900,000 and Siti's secured loan outstanding.",
  prompt:"On Salomon principles, how is Siti treated?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a secured creditor, paid first from the charged assets", correct:true,
     why:"This is Salomon in modern dress ([06.003]). Kiwi is a distinct person, so it can borrow from and give security to its sole shareholder. Siti's $500,000 loan, secured by a valid registered charge, is enforceable against the company's assets in priority to unsecured creditors, who share whatever is left. And because the trade debts are Kiwi's, Siti is not personally liable for them, despite owning all the shares. The trade creditors' best protection would have been to demand security or personal guarantees, or to rely on insolvency rules (for example on transactions at an undervalue or unfair preferences) if the charge was improper on its facts."},
    {t:"As an unsecured creditor, because a shareholder's loans are subordinated to trade creditors", correct:false,
     why:"No general rule subordinates a shareholder's secured loans. Salomon's debentures ranked ahead of unsecured creditors."},
    {t:"As personally liable for the trade debts, since she owns 100% of the company", correct:false,
     why:"Sole ownership does not make the owner liable for the company's debts. That is the core of Salomon."},
    {t:"Her charge is void because she cannot contract with a company she owns", correct:false,
     why:"A company can contract with its controller (Lee v Lee's Air Farming, [06.006]) and can secure debts owed to its shareholders (Salomon)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.003]", takeaway:"Owner-lender with security ranks ahead of unsecured creditors." }

]);

;
