{ id:"cl6-014", lesson:"6C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the better understanding of 'lifting' or 'piercing' the corporate veil?",
  options:[
    {t:"One consequence of separateness is suspended; the company survives", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.018] accepts that there are exceptional cases where a company's separate status may be disregarded: a court may order a remedy against a controller for a company liability, or against a company for a liability first incurred by its controller. These are 'metaphorically described as \"lifting\" or \"piercing\" the corporate veil'. But the expressions are not terms of art; they have been used in many contexts 'to different and confusing effects', so little is gained by reasoning from the metaphor. In particular, the imagery suggests the company ceases to exist once the veil is lifted, which is wrong. The better view is that 'a consequence of the separate entity doctrine is held in abeyance for a particular purpose, but at no point in time does the company cease to exist as a legal entity'."},
    {t:"The company is treated as dissolved", correct:false,
     why:"This is what the metaphor misleadingly suggests. The company remains a legal entity throughout; only one consequence of its separateness is set aside."},
    {t:"A precise legal doctrine with fixed requirements", correct:false,
     why:"Lee stresses that 'lifting' and 'piercing' are not terms of art. Their use across many contexts is part of why the area is confused."},
    {t:"An order removing the company's directors", correct:false,
     why:"Veil piercing concerns who bears a right or liability, not who manages the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.018], fn 33", takeaway:"Veil piercing = suspending one consequence, not killing the company." },

{ id:"cl6-015", lesson:"6C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Whether a statute requires a company's separate personality to be disregarded is a question of statutory interpretation.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.019] says exceptions to the separate entity rule may arise under statute or at common law, and 'whether or not a legislative provision has that effect is a matter of statutory interpretation'. The court reads the provision, express words and underlying policy, to see whether it treats related companies or controllers as one. Lesson 6D shows both forms: express provisions such as s 169 CA and the Income Tax Act group relief, and implied ones such as the policy-driven reading in Re FG (Films). The common law discretion is harder to rationalise, because the cases 'do not furnish clear guidance on when and how such discretion should be exercised'."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Statutory exceptions stand or fall on what the statute means. The difficult, discretionary questions arise at common law."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.019]", takeaway:"Statute: interpretation. Common law: unclear discretion." },

{ id:"cl6-016", lesson:"6C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A court orders a company's controller to pay a debt that the company incurred, on the ground of abuse of the corporate form.",
  prompt:"Which is the correct description?",
  options:[
    {t:"Forward veil piercing", correct:true,
     why:"An order against a controller for a company liability is the 'familiar situation' Lee describes as forward or standard veil piercing ([06.018], [06.031]). The reverse case, an order against the company for a liability first incurred by its controller, is reverse piercing. In neither case does the company cease to exist; a consequence of separateness is suspended only for the purpose in hand. And the remedy is limited to what is necessary to redress the controller's wrongdoing ([06.029])."},
    {t:"Reverse veil piercing", correct:false,
     why:"Reverse piercing runs the other way: the company is made to answer for its controller's obligations, or a shareholder claims unity with the company."},
    {t:"Attribution", correct:false,
     why:"Attribution imputes a person's acts or state of mind to the company, to establish what the company did. It does not make the controller liable for the company's debts."},
    {t:"Winding up", correct:false,
     why:"No dissolution is involved. The company continues to exist and may still have other creditors and contracts."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.018], [06.031]", takeaway:"Controller pays company debt = forward piercing." },

{ id:"c6x-015", lesson:"6C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Koh owes a judgment debt to a bank. Shortly before judgment, he transferred his assets to a company he wholly owns. The court orders the company to satisfy his debt to the bank.",
  prompt:"How is this order best described?",
  options:[
    {t:"Outsider reverse piercing", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.018] notes that, conversely to the usual case, 'an order may be made against a company in connection with a liability first incurred by its controller'. Here the bank, a third-party creditor of the shareholder, seeks to hold the company accountable for the shareholder's obligations, which [06.031] classifies as outsider reverse piercing. The High Court in Jhaveri Darsan v Salgaocar accepted that outsider reverse piercing is supported by authority. On facts like these, the likely ground would be the use of the company to evade an existing obligation ([06.032] ff). The company is not dissolved by the order; it simply cannot rely on its separateness to shield the transferred assets from Koh's creditor."},
    {t:"Forward veil piercing", correct:false,
     why:"Forward piercing makes the controller liable for the company's debts. Here the company is being made liable for the controller's debt."},
    {t:"Insider reverse piercing", correct:false,
     why:"Insider reverse piercing is pleaded by the shareholder himself, to claim the company's benefits. Here the claimant is an outsider, the bank."},
    {t:"Attribution of Koh's acts to the company", correct:false,
     why:"Attribution asks whether the company did something. The order here re-allocates liability, which is veil piercing."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.018], [06.031], fn 58", takeaway:"Company made to pay controller's debt = (outsider) reverse piercing." },

/* ---------- 6D ---------- */
{ id:"cl6-017", lesson:"6D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company wants to raise its director's pay without shareholder approval, so it arranges for its subsidiary to pay the increase instead.",
  prompt:"Does this avoid s 169 CA?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: group payments count as the company's own", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.020] uses s 169 as an example of a statute requiring separate personality to be disregarded so its purpose is achieved. Section 169 prohibits a company from providing or improving a director's emoluments unless first approved by the company. 'Emoluments' is defined (s 4) to include payments by the company and also by its holding company and its subsidiary. Those group payments 'are thus deemed as the company's own payments for purposes of section 169, so as to pre-empt attempts to circumvent the requirement for shareholders' approval by channelling director's remuneration through related companies'. The plan fails: the subsidiary's payment is treated as the company's, and still needs the company's approval."},
    {t:"Yes: the subsidiary is a separate legal person", correct:false,
     why:"That would be the default position under Salomon. Section 169's definition of emoluments overrides it for this purpose."},
    {t:"Yes, if the subsidiary's own board approves", correct:false,
     why:"Section 169 requires approval by the company whose director is being paid. A subsidiary's board approval is not a substitute."},
    {t:"Only if the subsidiary is wholly owned", correct:false,
     why:"The definition covers payments by subsidiaries generally, not just wholly owned ones."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.020], fn 35", takeaway:"s 169: group payments count as the company's own." },

{ id:"cl6-018", lesson:"6D", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each provision to how it departs from the separate entity rule.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"CA s 201(5)", r:"Consolidated group accounts, for a true and fair view"},
    {l:"Income Tax Act s 37B", r:"Group relief: use other group companies' losses"},
    {l:"Income Tax Act s 37O", r:"Holding company deducts acquisition subsidiary's capital expenditure"},
    {l:"CA s 169", r:"Director emoluments include group payments"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [06.020]–[06.021] gives these as statutory departures from the separate entity rule. Section 201(5) requires consolidated accounts for a group in place of the holding company's accounts, recognising that it is 'the performance of the group as a whole, rather than the holding company alone, that provides a true and fair view'. For tax, group companies are separate by default, but s 37B allows group relief, so one company can reduce its tax by using losses of other group companies. Two companies are in the same group for this purpose if one holds at least 75% of the other's issued ordinary shares, or a third company holds at least 75% of both (s 37B(3), fn 38). Section 37O lets a holding company that acquires another company through a wholly owned subsidiary claim deductions for the subsidiary's capital expenditure. Lee notes these tax departures reduce business costs and encourage enterprise. Section 169 counts group payments as the company's own for director emolument approvals.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.020]–[06.021], fns 35–38", takeaway:"Statute can treat the group as one for accounts, tax and pay." },

{ id:"c6x-018", lesson:"6D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three structures seek group relief under s 37B of the Income Tax Act: (1) Alpha holds 70% of Beta's issued ordinary shares; (2) Gamma holds 80% of Delta and 80% of Epsilon, and Delta wants to use Epsilon's losses; (3) Zeta holds 76% of Eta's issued ordinary shares.",
  prompt:"In which structures are the companies in the same group for this purpose?",
  options:[
    {t:"(2) and (3): each meets the 75% test; 70% falls short", correct:true,
     why:"Fn 38 to [06.021] sets out the s 37B(3) test: two companies are members of the same group 'if one company holds at least 75% of the issued ordinary shares of the other company, or at least 75% of the issued ordinary shares of both companies are held by a third company'. In (1), Alpha holds 70% of Beta, below 75%, so they are not a group for relief, even though Beta is plainly Alpha's subsidiary under s 5 CA (which needs only more than 50% voting control). In (2), Gamma holds 80% of both Delta and Epsilon, so the sister companies qualify through the third-company limb. In (3), Zeta's 76% satisfies the direct limb. The example shows that whether the law treats a group as one depends on the particular statute: the tax threshold differs from the Companies Act test."},
    {t:"All three, because each involves a holding company and a subsidiary", correct:false,
     why:"Subsidiary status under s 5 CA is not the tax test. Group relief requires 75% holdings."},
    {t:"(3) only, because sister companies cannot share losses", correct:false,
     why:"The third-company limb expressly covers sister companies held at least 75% by a common parent."},
    {t:"(1) and (3), because Delta and Epsilon have no direct shareholding link", correct:false,
     why:"A direct link is not required. Common 75%+ ownership by a third company suffices."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.021], fn 38", takeaway:"Group relief: 75% direct, or 75% of both by a common parent." },

{ id:"c6x-019", lesson:"6D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does s 37O of the Income Tax Act allow, and why does Lee treat it as a departure from the separate entity rule?",
  options:[
    {t:"The parent deducts its acquisition subsidiary's capital spending", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.021] gives s 37O as a further exception to the starting point that companies in a group are separate for tax. Where a holding company acquires another company through a wholly owned subsidiary, which is a common acquisition structure, it may claim the subsidiary's capital expenditure as tax deductions. Ordinarily, the subsidiary's expenditure would belong to the subsidiary alone. Lee explains that such departures 'have the effect of reducing business costs, thereby encouraging risk taking and enterprise'. The provision reflects the commercial reality that the acquisition vehicle is part of a single acquisition."},
    {t:"It allows any company to deduct its shareholders' personal expenses", correct:false,
     why:"Section 37O concerns acquisitions through wholly owned subsidiaries, not shareholders' personal expenses."},
    {t:"It treats all group companies as a single taxpayer for every purpose", correct:false,
     why:"Group companies remain separate for tax as a starting point. Sections 37B and 37O are targeted exceptions."},
    {t:"It allows a subsidiary to deduct its parent's expenditure", correct:false,
     why:"The direction is the other way: the holding company claims the subsidiary's capital expenditure."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.021]", takeaway:"s 37O: parent deducts acquisition sub's capital expenditure." },

{ id:"cl6-019", lesson:"6D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An English-incorporated company, substantially owned by an American, seeks to register a film as a 'British film'. The film was in reality produced mainly by an American company. The Act requires the 'maker' to be British.",
  prompt:"What did the court decide in Re FG (Films)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Refused: the English company wasn't the real maker", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.022] uses Re FG (Films) to show that a statute 'may not explicitly require a company's separate entity to be disregarded but may implicitly do so once its underlying policy is taken into account'. Under the Cinematograph Films Act 1948, a film qualified as British only if its 'maker' was a British subject or company. The applicant was British-incorporated, but the court found it was not the maker, because production was in fact substantially undertaken by an American company. That reading reflects the Act's policy to benefit British subjects 'that had produced the film in reality and not only in name'. The statute's purpose, not the applicant's formal nationality, determined the result."},
    {t:"Registration was granted because the applicant was British-incorporated", correct:false,
     why:"Formal incorporation in England would have sufficed if the court applied Salomon mechanically. The Act's policy required looking at who really made the film."},
    {t:"The American shareholder was held personally liable", correct:false,
     why:"The case was about eligibility for registration, not liability."},
    {t:"The English company was declared a sham and dissolved", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not dissolve the company or call it a sham. It interpreted 'maker' in light of the statute's purpose."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.022], fns 39–40", takeaway:"Re FG (Films): statutory policy can implicitly look through the company." },

{ id:"c6x-020", lesson:"6D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A hypothetical grant scheme funds products 'developed by a Singapore company'. A foreign group sets up a Singapore shell with one employee to apply, while all development is done by its overseas affiliate. The scheme's stated purpose is to build local research capability.",
  prompt:"How would the reasoning in Re FG (Films) bear on the application?",
  options:[
    {t:"Read 'developed by' purposively; the shell may not qualify", correct:true,
     why:"Re FG (Films) shows that statutory policy can implicitly require looking past a company's formal status ([06.022]). There, a British-incorporated company was not the 'maker' because production was really done by an American company, and the Act aimed to benefit those who produced films 'in reality and not only in name'. By analogy, if the grant scheme's purpose is to build local research capability, a court interpreting it may ask who actually did the development. A one-employee shell fronting overseas work may be a Singapore company in name but not the developer in substance. Whether this happens depends on interpreting the specific provision ([06.019]); it is not an application of a general veil-piercing discretion."},
    {t:"The application must succeed, because the shell is a separate Singapore company under Salomon", correct:false,
     why:"Salomon governs the company's status, but whether the statutory condition is met is a question of interpreting the scheme, which may look to substance."},
    {t:"The shell must be dissolved as a sham", correct:false,
     why:"Statutory interpretation does not dissolve the company. It decides whether the company meets the statute's conditions."},
    {t:"The overseas affiliate becomes personally liable for any grant paid", correct:false,
     why:"Re FG (Films) concerned eligibility. Liability for any grant wrongly paid would depend on the scheme's own terms and on other legal principles."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.019], [06.022]", takeaway:"Statutory purpose can look to who really did it, not who is named." },

{ id:"c6x-021", lesson:"6D", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Section 201(5) CA, which requires consolidated group accounts, reflects the view that the group's performance as a whole gives a truer picture of the holding company's worth than its own accounts alone.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.020] describes s 201(5) as requiring 'the preparation and presentation of consolidated accounts of a corporate group in place of the accounts of the holding company'. The provision 'recognises the commercial reality that it is the performance of the group as a whole, rather than the holding company alone, that provides a true and fair view of the company's worth'. A holding company's stand-alone accounts may show little more than its investments in subsidiaries, so consolidation treats the group as one economic unit for reporting, while each company remains a separate legal person for other purposes. Chapter 4 notes that for this purpose 'parent', 'subsidiary' and 'group' take accounting-standards meanings (s 209A)."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Consolidation is precisely a statutory recognition that, for reporting, the group's combined position matters more than the holding company's separate accounts."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.020], fn 36", takeaway:"s 201(5): consolidated accounts give the true picture." }

