/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 6 · Lessons 6F Evasion of Pre-existing Obligation and 6G The Sham/Façade Principle (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 6 [06.032]–[06.041], fns 64–91. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 6F ---------- */
{ id:"cl6-027", lesson:"6F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A former managing director, bound by a covenant not to solicit his ex-employer's customers, sets up a competing business and transfers it to a company whose only directors are his wife and an employee.",
  prompt:"What did the Court of Appeal order in Gilford Motor v Horne?",
  options:[
    {t:"Injunctions against both Horne and the company", correct:true,
     why:"Gilford Motor is the locus classicus for the idea that a company's separate existence may be disregarded to prevent deliberate evasion of a pre-existing obligation ([06.032]). Horne had covenanted not to compete with or solicit customers of his former employer. In breach, he set up a competing business and transferred it to a company run by his wife and an employee. The English Court of Appeal upheld injunctions against both. Against Horne, because he was in fact the person carrying on the competing business (treated as a finding of fact, fn 65). Against the company, because it was 'a mere cloak or sham' enabling Horne to carry on the prohibited business in breach of his covenant. Lord Sumption in Prest later treated the order against the company as an example of his evasion principle ([06.035])."},
    {t:"An injunction against him only", correct:false,
     why:"Restraining Horne alone would have let the company carry on the business. The court restrained the company too."},
    {t:"No injunction, because the company was a separate person", correct:false,
     why:"Separate personality did not protect a company being used to carry on its controller's breach."},
    {t:"Damages against his wife personally", correct:false,
     why:"The remedies were injunctions against Horne and the company. The wife was a director of the company, not a defendant held personally liable."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.032], fns 64–66", takeaway:"Gilford Motor: company used to dodge a covenant is restrained too." },

{ id:"cl6-028", lesson:"6F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lipman contracts to sell land to Jones, then transfers it to a nominally capitalised company he controls, solely to defeat Jones's right to specific performance.",
  prompt:"What did the court order in Jones v Lipman?",
  options:[
    {t:"Specific performance against both Lipman and the company", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.033] explains the reasoning on each side. Lipman could not resist specific performance by saying the land now belonged to someone else, because with absolute control and ownership of Alamed he could compel it to convey to Jones. Against Alamed, the court, relying on Gilford Motor, treated it as 'a creature of the defendant … a device and a sham' formed to evade his contractual obligation. Toulson J later offered an alternative explanation that needs no veil piercing ([06.036]): a purchaser who acquires property with actual notice that the transaction is a fraud on a prior purchaser takes subject to that purchaser's equity. Alamed, through Lipman's control, had notice of Jones's equity, so the same order would have issued against a natural person with notice."},
    {t:"Damages only, because the land had been transferred", correct:false,
     why:"That was precisely the outcome Lipman engineered the transfer to achieve. The court refused to let the transfer defeat specific performance."},
    {t:"Specific performance against the company only", correct:false,
     why:"The order ran against both Lipman and Alamed."},
    {t:"The transfer was set aside as an unfair preference", correct:false,
     why:"Unfair preference is an insolvency concept. The remedy here was specific performance of the original sale contract."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.033], [06.036], fns 67–68, 77", takeaway:"Jones v Lipman: can't dodge specific performance by conveying to your company." },

{ id:"cl6-029", lesson:"6F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under the evasion principle, whose and what motive matters?",
  options:[
    {t:"A motive to evade obligations that already exist", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.034] says it is necessary to consider the motive of the alleged perpetrator (Adams v Cape). The relevant motive is to evade obligations and constraints 'that existed or accrued prior to and independently of the company's involvement' (Adams; Prest). By contrast, using corporate structures to limit shareholders' future legal liability 'is … generally unobjectionable since the law accepts that to be the primary purpose of incorporation' (Adams). The distinction keeps the evasion principle from swallowing Salomon: everyone who incorporates intends to limit future liability, but only those who use a company to escape an obligation they already owe are caught."},
    {t:"Any motive to reduce personal liability", correct:false,
     why:"Reducing future personal liability is what incorporation is for. Only evasion of existing obligations engages the principle."},
    {t:"The motive of the company's creditors", correct:false,
     why:"The inquiry focuses on the controller who interposes the company, not on the creditors."},
    {t:"Motive is irrelevant; only the effect matters", correct:false,
     why:"Motive is central. The same structure can be legitimate or evasive depending on why it was used."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.034], fns 69–72", takeaway:"Evasion: pre-existing obligations only." },

{ id:"c6x-029", lesson:"6F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"(1) Before bidding for a risky construction project, Mr Lee incorporates a company to sign the contract so that any future losses fall on the company. (2) After a court grants a creditor judgment against him personally, Mr Chua transfers his assets to a newly formed company he controls.",
  prompt:"Which use of a company engages the evasion principle?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only (2): Chua evaded an existing liability", correct:true,
     why:"The two scenarios mark the line drawn in [06.034]. Lee incorporated before taking on the risk, so the liability, if it ever arises, will be the company's from the start; that is limiting future liability, which Adams v Cape treats as 'generally unobjectionable'. Chua, by contrast, already owed a judgment debt when he moved his assets into a company. That is using a company to evade an obligation 'that existed or accrued prior to and independently of the company's involvement'. Lord Sumption's formulation in Prest captures it: a person under an existing liability who deliberately frustrates its enforcement by interposing a company he controls ([06.035]). A creditor of Chua would argue for an order against the company to deprive it and Chua of the advantage they sought."},
    {t:"Both, because both men used a company to avoid paying", correct:false,
     why:"Avoiding future liability by incorporating is legitimate. Only avoiding an existing liability engages the principle."},
    {t:"Only (1), because the project is risky", correct:false,
     why:"Risk is exactly what limited liability is meant to allow investors to take. It is not evasion."},
    {t:"Neither, because the companies are separate persons", correct:false,
     why:"Separate personality is the default, but Gilford Motor and Jones v Lipman show it does not protect deliberate evasion of existing obligations."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.034]–[06.035], fns 69–74", takeaway:"Limit future liability: fine. Escape existing liability: evasion." },

{ id:"cl6-030", lesson:"6F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Lord Sumption say in Prest v Petrodel about veil piercing?",
  options:[
    {t:"Piercing is limited to deliberate evasion of an existing obligation", correct:true,
     why:"Lord Sumption characterised the orders against the companies in Gilford Motor and Jones as examples of the 'evasion principle', and said it is the only ground for piercing ([06.035]): 'there is a limited principle of English law which applies when a person is under an existing legal obligation or liability or subject to an existing legal restriction which he deliberately evades or whose enforcement he deliberately frustrates by interposing a company under his control. The court may then pierce the corporate veil for the purpose, and only for the purpose, of depriving the company or its controller of the advantage that they would otherwise have obtained by the company's separate personality.' Fn 74 notes this is a minority view: apart from Lord Neuberger, the other law lords had reservations about confining the jurisdiction so narrowly. The Singapore High Court endorsed the formulation in Manuchar Steel v Star Pacific Line."},
    {t:"The veil may be pierced whenever justice requires", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Sumption's approach is the opposite: an extremely narrow, single-ground jurisdiction."},
    {t:"The veil can never be pierced", correct:false,
     why:"He affirmed a limited principle; he did not abolish piercing."},
    {t:"Corporate groups should be treated as single entities", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in his formulation supports single-entity treatment of groups."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.035], fns 73–74", takeaway:"Prest (Sumption): evasion only, a minority view." },

{ id:"c6x-035", lesson:"6F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A court finds that Raj deliberately frustrated a freezing order against him by causing assets to be transferred into Sable Pte Ltd, which he controls. The claimant asks the court, in addition to reaching those assets, to hold Raj personally liable for all of Sable's existing supplier contracts.",
  prompt:"Under Lord Sumption's evasion principle, how far may the court go?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only far enough to strip the advantage, such as the assets", correct:true,
     why:"Lord Sumption's formulation contains its own limit: the court may pierce 'for the purpose, and only for the purpose, of depriving the company or its controller of the advantage that they would otherwise have obtained by the company's separate personality' ([06.035]). The advantage Raj sought was putting assets beyond the freezing order. An order reaching those assets removes it. Holding Raj liable for Sable's supplier contracts would go beyond that purpose, imposing on him liabilities that were undertaken only by Sable and have nothing to do with his evasion. This fits Lee's general point that remedies 'should only go so far as is necessary to redress the wrongdoing' ([06.029]) and the Rossendale observation that the evasion principle does not justify forward piercing to impose company liabilities on controllers ([06.036])."},
    {t:"Without limit: once evasion is proved, Raj and Sable are one person", correct:false,
     why:"This ignores the purpose limitation built into the evasion principle and the general rule that piercing is purpose-specific."},
    {t:"Not at all: Sable is a separate person and its assets are its own", correct:false,
     why:"Deliberate frustration of an existing order by interposing a controlled company is exactly what the evasion principle addresses."},
    {t:"Only by fining Sable for contempt", correct:false,
     why:"Contempt may be relevant, but the evasion principle itself allows the court to deprive Raj and Sable of the advantage gained, for example by reaching the assets."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.029], [06.035]–[06.036], fns 74, 79", takeaway:"Evasion remedy: remove the advantage gained, nothing more." },

{ id:"cl6-031", lesson:"6F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why have commentators and later courts doubted that Gilford Motor and Jones v Lipman are true veil-piercing cases?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both can be explained without piercing", correct:true,
     why:"Toulson J observed in Yukong Line that both cases can be understood as granting equitable relief against a company used by its controller to commit a continuing breach, a remedy not unique to companies ([06.036]). If Horne had procured his wife, or a new employer, to solicit Gilford Motor's customers, they would have been restrained too; the company was merely the channel. Jones can be explained by the rule that a purchaser with actual notice that a transaction is a fraud on a prior purchaser takes subject to that purchaser's equity: Alamed had Lipman's knowledge, and a natural person with notice would have been ordered to convey just the same. Lord Neuberger endorsed these observations in VTB Capital (fn 75). The UK Supreme Court in Rossendale then distanced itself from the evasion principle, doubting it is needed to explain these cases and saying that, if it exists, it covers only reverse piercing, not imposing on controllers liabilities undertaken only by the company. Those remarks were obiter, but suggest the jurisdiction will narrow further."},
    {t:"Both were overruled by Prest", correct:false,
     why:"Prest relied on them as examples of the evasion principle; it did not overrule them."},
    {t:"Both involved foreign companies", correct:false,
     why:"The objection is about the legal basis of the orders, not where the companies were incorporated."},
    {t:"Both turned on statutory provisions", correct:false,
     why:"Both are common law and equity cases. Statutory veil lifting is a separate category (6D)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.036], fns 75–79", takeaway:"Yukong Line, Rossendale: evasion cases explicable without piercing." },

{ id:"c6x-032", lesson:"6F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Suppose that, instead of using a company, Horne in Gilford Motor had persuaded his brother to set up the competing business in the brother's own name, with Horne secretly directing it and soliciting customers through him.",
  prompt:"On Toulson J's analysis in Yukong Line, what follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"The brother could be restrained, so no piercing was needed", correct:true,
     why:"This is Toulson J's point, as Lee presents it in [06.036], using C H Tham's illustration (fn 76). If Horne had procured his wife, or a new employer, to carry out the breach, they 'would likewise have been restrained'. The same goes for a brother acting as his front. 'The fact that a company was the channel through which the breach was perpetrated was thus of no moment.' The injunction rests on equity's willingness to restrain those knowingly used to carry on a continuing breach, not on any special doctrine about companies. That undermines treating Gilford Motor as authority for a distinct veil-piercing principle. Fn 75 notes another alternative: an implied term that the covenant extended to entities Horne controlled."},
    {t:"The brother could not be restrained, because he never signed the covenant", correct:false,
     why:"Equity can restrain those used to carry on another's continuing breach, even if they were not parties to the covenant."},
    {t:"Gilford Motor proves that only companies can be restrained in this way", correct:false,
     why:"Toulson J's analysis is the reverse: the remedy is not unique to companies."},
    {t:"Only damages would be available against Horne", correct:false,
     why:"Injunctions would still be available against Horne and those he used to carry on the breach."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.036], fns 75–76", takeaway:"Same relief against a human front: no special company doctrine needed." },

{ id:"c6x-031", lesson:"6F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Moss Pte Ltd, controlled by Mr Wong, signs a large lease and later defaults. The landlord, relying on Lord Sumption's evasion principle, argues that Wong should be held personally liable on the lease because he used Moss to take it on.",
  prompt:"What did the UK Supreme Court in Rossendale suggest about such an argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"The evasion principle (if any) is reverse-only, not forward", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.036] reports that in Rossendale Borough Council v Hurstwood Properties, the UK Supreme Court distanced itself from Lord Sumption's evasion principle and pointed out that the principle, if it existed, 'was confined to cases of reverse (or backward) veil-piercing. It would not justify attempts to forward pierce, namely, to impose upon a controller or shareholder a liability that in reality was undertaken only by the company' (at [72]–[74]). Here the lease was always Moss's obligation; Wong was not evading any pre-existing personal obligation by interposing Moss. The landlord's argument is therefore weak on two levels: no prior obligation of Wong's was evaded, and the remedy sought is forward piercing. The remarks were obiter, but they suggest further narrowing."},
    {t:"That the landlord succeeds because Wong controls Moss", correct:false,
     why:"Control is not evasion. And Rossendale suggests the evasion principle cannot be used to forward pierce."},
    {t:"That the landlord succeeds if Moss is lowly capitalised", correct:false,
     why:"Thin capitalisation is not a ground under the evasion principle. The landlord could have demanded a guarantee."},
    {t:"That the evasion principle requires the landlord to sue Wong's family", correct:false,
     why:"There is no such requirement. The point is that the evasion principle does not support imposing company liabilities on controllers."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.036], fns 78–79", takeaway:"Rossendale: evasion principle (if any) = reverse only, not forward." },

{ id:"cl6-032", lesson:"6F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Singapore courts have endorsed the evasion principle and actually applied it to pierce the veil.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.037] states that the suggestion has been endorsed on a number of occasions, for example in Dynasty Line v Sia Sukamto, Singapore Tourism Board v Children's Media, Sri Jaya v RHB Bank and Manuchar Steel v Star Pacific Line (fn 80), 'but in none of them was the principle actually applied'. In Children's Media, the High Court cited the evasion principle as a reason for imposing contractual liability on the company's controlling member, but the Court of Appeal relied only on alter ego to reach the same result (fn 81). In Total English Learning v Kids Counsel, the High Court was prepared to pierce on an evasion basis, but this was obiter because the plaintiffs lacked standing (fn 80). So while the evasion principle 'ostensibly forms part of our law', it is vulnerable to challenge for the reasons in [06.036]."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The principle has been endorsed but not actually applied in the cases Lee cites. The appellate result in Children's Media rested on alter ego."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.037], fns 80–81", takeaway:"Singapore: evasion endorsed, never actually applied." },

/* ---------- 6G ---------- */
{ id:"c6x-036", lesson:"6G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the status of Lord Keith's statement in Woolfson that the veil may be pierced 'only where special circumstances exist indicating that it is a mere façade concealing the true facts'?",
  options:[
    {t:"It was obiter, widely cited, and imprecise", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.038] notes that Lord Keith's observation in Woolfson v Strathclyde was obiter, yet it 'has been widely cited as affirming the \"sham/façade\" exception to the Salomon principle'. The difficulty is that 'what precisely is meant by \"sham\" or \"façade\" is less clear'. Courts have borrowed Diplock LJ's definition of sham from Snook ([06.039]), used the words for evasion cases ([06.040]), and sometimes suggested the company itself is a nullity. Lee concludes that the imagery is mostly unhelpful, and if there is any distinctive principle, it concerns concealing fraud ([06.041])."},
    {t:"It was the ratio of Woolfson and defines a precise test", correct:false,
     why:"Woolfson was decided on the ground that the business belonged to the subsidiary ([06.007]). The façade remark was obiter and imprecise."},
    {t:"It was rejected by later courts and is no longer cited", correct:false,
     why:"It remains widely cited; the problem is its vagueness, not its rejection."},
    {t:"It confined piercing to statutory cases", correct:false,
     why:"The remark referred to special circumstances showing a façade, a common law idea, not to statute."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.038], fn 82", takeaway:"Woolfson 'façade' dictum: obiter, widely cited, vague." },

{ id:"cl6-033", lesson:"6G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Diplock LJ mean by 'sham' in Snook v London & West Riding Investments?",
  options:[
    {t:"A shared intention to create a false appearance", correct:true,
     why:"Diplock LJ defined 'sham', in a rather different context, as 'acts done or documents executed by the parties to the \"sham\" which are intended by them to give third parties or to the court the appearance of creating between the parties legal rights and obligations different from the actual legal rights and obligations (if any) which the parties intend to create' ([06.039]). A line of Singapore High Court decisions beginning with Win Line v Masterpart adopted this definition for veil lifting (fn 85). Others, including Yukong Line, Ben Hashem and Mohamed Shiyam v Tuff Offshore, doubt its relevance (fn 86). A true sham requires all the parties to agree to create a false appearance; that makes the document or transaction a nullity, but 'is not by itself a sufficient reason to disapply the separate entity rule'."},
    {t:"Any company with low share capital", correct:false,
     why:"Thin capitalisation is a common complaint in veil cases, but it is not a sham in Diplock LJ's sense."},
    {t:"A company controlled by one person", correct:false,
     why:"One-man companies are genuine companies (Salomon)."},
    {t:"Any transaction one party later breaches", correct:false,
     why:"Later breach does not show that the parties intended a false appearance at the outset."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.038]–[06.039], fns 83–86", takeaway:"Snook sham: shared intention to create a false appearance." },

{ id:"c6x-037", lesson:"6G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Seah and his company Quill Pte Ltd both sign a 'loan agreement' under which Quill appears to lend Seah $1m. In fact, both intend the money as a gift and drafted the document only to mislead the tax authority.",
  prompt:"What is the legal significance of the sham, on Lee's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"The document is a nullity; Quill's personality is intact", correct:true,
     why:"This is a genuine sham ([06.039]): both parties intended the document to create an appearance of legal rights, a loan, different from what they actually intended, a gift. The consequence is that the document or transaction is 'a legal nullity'; the court will give effect to the true arrangement. But Lee stresses that this 'is not by itself a sufficient reason to disapply the separate entity rule'. Quill remains a separate person; it is the transaction, not the company, that is disregarded. Any tax consequences flow from the true nature of the transaction. Veil-piercing cases, by contrast, are usually not about shams of this kind at all."},
    {t:"Quill itself becomes a nullity because it took part in a sham", correct:false,
     why:"The idea that a duly incorporated company can itself be a sham has been 'largely discredited' ([06.040])."},
    {t:"Nothing: a signed document can never be disregarded", correct:false,
     why:"A true sham document can be disregarded as a nullity. That is what the Snook concept is for."},
    {t:"Seah is automatically liable for all of Quill's debts", correct:false,
     why:"A sham transaction does not by itself pierce the veil or make Seah liable for the company's debts."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.039]–[06.040]", takeaway:"Sham transaction → nullity of the transaction, not of the company." },

{ id:"cl6-034", lesson:"6G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company genuinely enters a supply contract, intending its legal effect. Later it emerges the company's controller never meant it to perform, and it defaults.",
  prompt:"Is the contract a 'sham' in the Snook sense?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: genuine intention plus later breach isn't a sham", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.039] states that 'if the parties at the time of the transaction actually intended the legal consequences of the legal form which they have adopted, the transaction is not a sham simply because it is subsequently discovered that one of them would not fulfil his part of the bargain'. Lord Cooke put it bluntly: 'to let in the concept of sham [in such cases] would be loose thinking' (fn 87). Veil-lifting cases typically involve this kind of situation, a real transaction followed by non-performance or misconduct, and 'are not usually concerned with sham transactions in the true sense'. The counterparty's remedies lie in breach of contract and, if there was deceit, in tort, not in calling the contract a sham."},
    {t:"Yes: non-performance proves it was a sham", correct:false,
     why:"Non-performance is a breach, not evidence that both parties intended a false appearance when they contracted."},
    {t:"Yes, if the company was thinly capitalised", correct:false,
     why:"Capitalisation has nothing to do with whether the parties intended a false appearance."},
    {t:"Only if the counterparty was also deceived", correct:false,
     why:"A Snook sham requires all parties to share the intention to mislead. A deceived counterparty is the opposite of a party to a sham."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.039], fn 87", takeaway:"Genuine transaction + later breach ≠ sham." },

{ id:"cl6-035", lesson:"6G", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A properly incorporated company can itself be treated as a sham and a legal nullity.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.040] explains that the words 'sham', 'façade' and 'device' have also been used where a company is used to evade an existing obligation, but that 'adds nothing to the evasion principle'. To the extent the usage suggests the company itself, rather than its act, document or transaction, may be a legal nullity, that view 'has largely been discredited, for it is difficult to see how a company that is properly incorporated with a legal personality of its own could ever be regarded a sham or nullity' (Neufeld; Ben Hashem; VTB Capital). Fn 89 notes Win Line arguably went down this route by considering whether the company was a structure of no legal substance. A notice of incorporation is conclusive of due incorporation (s 398, Ch 4), which reinforces the point."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This takes the 'sham company' metaphor literally. An act, document or transaction may be a sham; a duly incorporated company is a real legal person."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.040], fns 88–90", takeaway:"A company itself can't be a sham; its transactions might." },

{ id:"c6x-038", lesson:"6G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a veil-piercing claim, a claimant's pleadings describe the defendant's subsidiary as 'a sham, façade and device' because the parent used it to dodge a non-compete covenant, and ask the court to declare the subsidiary 'a nullity of no legal substance'.",
  prompt:"What is wrong with this pleading, on Lee's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"The labels add nothing, and a company can't be a nullity", correct:true,
     why:"Two points from [06.040] apply. First, where 'sham', 'façade' and 'device' are used to describe a company used to evade an existing legal obligation, as in Gilford Motor and Jones, the words 'add nothing to the evasion principle'. The claimant should plead and prove deliberate evasion of the pre-existing covenant by interposing the subsidiary ([06.032]–[06.035]). Second, asking for the company itself to be declared a nullity relies on a view that 'has largely been discredited'; a duly incorporated company has its own legal personality. The relief that fits is an order against the subsidiary depriving the parent and subsidiary of the advantage of the evasion, such as an injunction, as in Gilford Motor. Lee [06.041] adds that the imagery is 'mostly unhelpful and often confounding'."},
    {t:"Nothing: these labels are the established test for piercing", correct:false,
     why:"Lee argues the labels are expressions of moral indignation, not tests ([06.024], [06.041])."},
    {t:"The claimant should instead argue that the parent and subsidiary are a single economic unit", correct:false,
     why:"Single economic unit arguments are generally unsuccessful (6J). The proper basis on these facts is evasion."},
    {t:"The claim must fail because subsidiaries cannot be restrained", correct:false,
     why:"Gilford Motor shows a company used to carry on a controller's breach can be restrained."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.040]–[06.041], fns 88–90", takeaway:"'Sham/façade' adds nothing; plead evasion; no nullity of the company." },

{ id:"cl6-036", lesson:"6G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"If 'sham/façade' embodies any distinctive veil-piercing principle, when is it likely to apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where a company conceals people's part in a fraud", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.041] concludes that the imagery of 'sham', 'façade', 'device' and 'stratagem' is 'mostly unhelpful and often confounding'. Snook-type shams are about transactions, not companies ([06.039]); 'sham' in evasion cases adds nothing to the evasion principle ([06.040]); and a company itself cannot be a sham. If a distinctive principle remains, it is likely to apply only 'where a person or group of persons utilise a corporate vehicle to conceal their participation in a fraudulent or dishonest scheme'. That links sham/façade to the fraud ground in 6H, and to the VTB Capital threshold of dishonest misuse of corporate personality to conceal the true facts ([06.028], fn 53)."},
    {t:"Whenever a company is wholly owned by one person", correct:false,
     why:"Sole ownership is the Salomon situation. It is not a sham or façade."},
    {t:"Whenever corporate formalities are skipped", correct:false,
     why:"Informality may be relevant evidence in some cases, but it is not itself a sham."},
    {t:"Whenever a company is incorporated abroad", correct:false,
     why:"Where the company was incorporated has no bearing on whether it is a façade."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.041], fn 91", takeaway:"Sham/façade, if anything: concealing fraud." }

]);

;
