/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 6 · Lessons 6H Fraud and 6I The Company as Alter Ego (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 6 [06.042]–[06.057], fns 92–123. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 6H ---------- */
{ id:"cl6-037", lesson:"6H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two undischarged bankrupts with fraud convictions form a nominally capitalised company, City, which buys a quarrying licence for £3,500 and sells it for £18,000 to another company, Welsh, which they also formed. Welsh raises £20,000 from the public. Its prospectus names City as promoter but hides the bankrupts' role and profits.",
  prompt:"What happened in Re Darby?",
  options:[
    {t:"Darby accounted as promoter; City was his mere 'alias'", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.042] describes Re Darby as the case frequently cited for fraud as a ground of veil lifting, consistent with the idea that 'fraud unravels everything'. Welsh's prospectuses named City as promoter and vendor, but said nothing about Darby and Gyde's involvement or that they would receive the profits through City. After Welsh went into liquidation, its liquidator proved against Darby's estate (he was by then bankrupt again) for his secret profits as promoter. Darby could not argue that City was the promoter, because City was found to be 'no more than an \"alias\" or \"name\"' under which he perpetrated the fraud. Lee notes ([06.043]) that more recent cases, such as the Court of Appeal in VTB Capital, read Re Darby as a simple finding of fact that Darby was the promoter, directly or through City as agent. But the court's emphasis on Darby's fraud in using City to conceal his role suggests veil-piercing reasoning was in fact engaged."},
    {t:"Only City was liable, as the named promoter", correct:false,
     why:"This is the defence Darby tried. The court held City was merely the name under which Darby acted, so he was personally liable."},
    {t:"No one was liable because the prospectus disclosed City as promoter", correct:false,
     why:"The disclosure was misleading because it concealed who really stood behind City. Ch 4's promoter rules require full disclosure of interests."},
    {t:"The licence sale was rescinded", correct:false,
     why:"The remedy was a proof in Darby's bankruptcy for the secret profits, not rescission."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.042]–[06.043], fns 92–97", takeaway:"Re Darby: City an 'alias'; Darby accounts as promoter." },

{ id:"c6x-042", lesson:"6H", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Some recent cases explain Re Darby without any veil piercing, as a finding of fact that Darby himself was the promoter, either directly or through City as his agent.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.043] notes that more recent cases, including the English Court of Appeal in VTB Capital (fn 95), 'doubted the correctness of treating Re Darby as an instance of veil piercing'. They read it as a case where it was simply found as a fact that Darby was in truth the promoter, because he had either acted as promoter himself or done so through City as agent. On that reading, no consequence of separate personality is displaced. Lee does not fully accept it: the court in Re Darby placed 'significant emphasis' on Darby's fraud in using City to hide his involvement, and if his control and fraud were the chief factors that made him the promoter, then veil-piercing analysis was in fact engaged."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"The 'finding of fact' and agency readings are exactly how recent cases have reinterpreted Re Darby, though Lee questions whether they fit its reasoning."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.043], fns 95–97", takeaway:"Re Darby: veil piercing, or just 'Darby was the promoter'?" },

{ id:"cl6-038", lesson:"6H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the weightiest objection to fraud as a general ground for piercing the veil?",
  options:[
    {t:"It would override tort, contract and equity", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.044] notes that Singapore courts appear to accept fraud as a sufficient reason for disregarding separate personality; Quentin Loh J said in Lim Chee Twang that 'in cases where a company is used fraudulently as a front or to commit a fraud, the court will pierce the corporate veil' (fn 98). But 'no serious attempt has as yet been made to rationalise its scope'. The weightiest objection is breadth: 'liberally applied, it would have the effect of overreaching other liability rules (eg, in tort, contract and equity)'. Fraud already has well-developed remedies, such as deceit, rescission for misrepresentation, dishonest assistance and conspiracy. For this reason, fraud as a general ground has been rejected in more recent decisions elsewhere, notably VTB Capital v Nutritek and Antonio Gramsci Shipping v Lembergs (fn 99)."},
    {t:"Fraud is hard to prove", correct:false,
     why:"Fraud is demanding to prove, but that is not Lee's objection. Her concern is that a general fraud ground would override the rules other areas of law already provide."},
    {t:"Fraud is a criminal matter only", correct:false,
     why:"Fraud has extensive civil consequences in tort and equity, which is part of why a veil-piercing fraud ground may be unnecessary."},
    {t:"Courts lack jurisdiction over fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Courts plainly have jurisdiction over fraud. The question is whether veil piercing is the right tool."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.044], fns 98–99", takeaway:"Fraud as a ground: too broad; overrides other liability rules." },

{ id:"cl6-039", lesson:"6H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"STB advances about $12.8m in sponsorship to CML for a charity concert. H, who controls CML and its shareholder TTM, mixes their funds, ignores governance, withdraws the whole sum for himself and others, and keeps misrepresenting his commitment while having no intention to stage the event.",
  prompt:"What was decided in Children's Media v Singapore Tourism Board?",
  options:[
    {t:"CML, TTM and H were held jointly and severally liable", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.045] sets out the facts. CML contracted with STB to organise the concert, failed to deliver despite accommodations, and the contract was terminated after STB had advanced about $12.8m. H, with absolute control of CML and TTM, mixed their accounts and funds, ignored governance, used CML as a conduit to receive the sponsorship and withdrew it to pay himself and others, while making CML bear TTM's and others' liabilities. Having decided not to stage the event, the defendants kept pressing STB to dilute CML's obligations by dishonestly misrepresenting their commitment. Lai Siu Chiu J held that CML was 'a façade and/or a sham' and that CML and TTM were used by H 'to evade his legal obligations', so all three were jointly and severally liable. The Court of Appeal endorsed the result, calling the companies 'corporate puppets compliantly dancing to the tune' of H, and noted that H's lack of bona fides and his misrepresentations were by themselves ample reasons for personal liability, which hints that fraud or dishonesty suffices."},
    {t:"Only CML was liable; H was protected by separate personality", correct:false,
     why:"H and TTM were held jointly and severally liable with CML."},
    {t:"Only H was liable, and CML was dissolved", correct:false,
     why:"All three defendants were held liable, and no dissolution was ordered."},
    {t:"The contract was set aside as a sham", correct:false,
     why:"CML was held to have breached the contract, which shows it was a real contract, not a pretence."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.045], fns 100–103", takeaway:"Children's Media: H, TTM and CML jointly liable on CML's contract." },

{ id:"cl6-040", lesson:"6H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the best explanation of Children's Media, if it is truly a veil-piercing case?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a fraud case; 'sham' and 'evasion' don't fit the facts", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.046] tests the trial judge's labels against the facts. The contract was breached, so it was not a 'sham' in the sense of a mere pretence. CML was probably called a sham because H misapplied its assets without regard to its interests, which points to H's wrongdoing as the real reason for his liability; the label adds nothing. 'Evasion' does not fit either: H had not incurred any personal liability and then interposed CML to escape it; the liability was CML's from the start, and H simply procured CML's breach. So if piercing was justified, it must have been because H used CML and TTM for fraudulent or dishonest purposes. Fn 104 adds that it may not have been a veil case at all, because H's and TTM's direct liability could have been established through fraudulent misrepresentation, dishonest assistance in breach of a Quistclose trust, or conspiracy."},
    {t:"The company was a sham in the Snook sense", correct:false,
     why:"There was no shared intention to create a false appearance; the contract was real and was breached."},
    {t:"H evaded his own pre-existing contractual liability", correct:false,
     why:"H had no personal liability before CML's contract. He procured CML's breach; he did not evade an obligation of his own."},
    {t:"The group was a single economic entity", correct:false,
     why:"Single economic entity reasoning was not the basis of the decision, and it is generally not accepted as a ground (6J)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.046], fns 104–105", takeaway:"Children's Media best read as a fraud case." },

{ id:"c6x-040", lesson:"6H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Facts like Children's Media: a sponsor advances funds to a company for a specific event; the controller never intends to stage it, misrepresents his commitment and withdraws the money. Counsel for the sponsor wants a claim against the controller that does not depend on piercing the veil.",
  prompt:"Which routes does Lee suggest?",
  options:[
    {t:"Deceit, dishonest assistance and conspiracy against the controller", correct:true,
     why:"Fn 104 to [06.046] observes that 'the facts of this case well afford a variety of grounds for establishing the direct liability of H and TTM to the STB without displacing the corporate veil'. Fraudulent misrepresentation fits the controller's dishonest assurances that induced the sponsor to keep dealing. If the sponsorship was advanced for a specific purpose, it may have been held on a Quistclose trust, so a controller who dishonestly caused its misapplication may be liable for dishonest assistance. And those who combined to defraud the sponsor or to cause loss by breach of trust may be liable in conspiracy. These routes hold the controller responsible for his own wrongs, which is Lee's preferred approach ([06.047]–[06.049]), rather than making him a party to a contract he never signed."},
    {t:"None: the only route is piercing the veil to make the controller a party to the contract", correct:false,
     why:"Lee's point is the opposite: tort and equity provide direct routes that avoid displacing privity of contract."},
    {t:"A claim against the company's auditors", correct:false,
     why:"Auditors may have separate liabilities, but they are not the direct routes against the controller Lee identifies."},
    {t:"Rectifying the sponsorship contract to add the controller as a party", correct:false,
     why:"Rectification corrects a document to reflect agreed terms; it cannot add a party who never agreed."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.046]–[06.048], fns 104, 108", takeaway:"Sue the dishonest controller directly: deceit, dishonest assistance, conspiracy." },

{ id:"cl6-041", lesson:"6H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A lender defrauded through a company sues its controller, arguing the veil should be pierced so that the controller is treated as a party to the company's loan agreement.",
  prompt:"What did the UK Supreme Court hold in VTB Capital v Nutritek?",
  options:[
    {t:"No piercing to create contract claims against non-parties", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.047] points out that Children's Media had the effect of overriding the privity rule by making H and TTM parties to a contract they never entered. In VTB Capital, the UK Supreme Court held there is no authority for piercing the veil to this effect: the jurisdiction 'cannot be invoked to create a cause of action in contract where none existed before', and doing so would be 'an illegitimate extension of the circumstances in which the veil can be pierced'. That holds even where fraud was perpetrated through the company, because fraud has its own remedies, such as those for misrepresentation (fn 108). In Singapore ([06.048]), Children's Media does not settle the point, because it was not argued. The main objection is consent: a party that chose to contract with a thinly capitalised company generally accepted the risk of its default, as Sitt Tatt v Goh Tai Hock affirmed even without impropriety (fn 109)."},
    {t:"The controller was made a party because of the fraud", correct:false,
     why:"That is what the lender argued and what VTB rejected."},
    {t:"Piercing is available for contracts but not for torts", correct:false,
     why:"VTB's point is specifically that piercing cannot be used to create contractual liability for a non-party."},
    {t:"The lender could sue only in the company's insolvency", correct:false,
     why:"The lender had ordinary fraud remedies against the wrongdoers. It simply could not convert them into a contract claim by piercing."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.047]–[06.048], fns 106–109", takeaway:"VTB: no piercing to make a controller a contract party." },

{ id:"c6x-041", lesson:"6H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Pine Trading Pte Ltd has $1,000 paid-up capital and few assets. A supplier knowingly extends it $300,000 of credit without security. No one acts dishonestly; Pine's business simply fails. The supplier asks the court to make Pine's controller liable on the supply contract.",
  prompt:"How should the court respond, on Lee's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Refuse: the supplier accepted the risk of a thin company", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.048] identifies 'the consensual nature of contracts' as the main objection to using veil piercing to get round privity. 'A party who voluntarily contracted with a company with a low capitalisation or insubstantial assets must, in general, be taken to have accepted the risk of loss that arises when the latter defaults.' Fn 109 notes Singapore courts have affirmed this where no impropriety was involved (Sitt Tatt v Goh Tai Hock). Here there is no fraud at all. The supplier could have asked for security or a personal guarantee. Lee adds that even where fraud is alleged, adding or substituting parties to a contract may be neither proportionate nor necessary, since tort and equity remedies exist."},
    {t:"Grant the claim: thin capitalisation is itself abuse of the corporate form", correct:false,
     why:"Low capitalisation is common and lawful. It is a risk counterparties can price or protect against."},
    {t:"Grant the claim if the controller benefited from the supplies", correct:false,
     why:"A controller always benefits indirectly from the company's business. That is not a ground for piercing."},
    {t:"Grant the claim because the supplier is an involuntary creditor", correct:false,
     why:"The supplier chose to extend credit, which makes it a voluntary creditor, the clearest case for holding it to its bargain."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.048], fn 109", takeaway:"Voluntary creditor of thin company accepted the risk; no piercing." },

{ id:"c6x-043", lesson:"6H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does Lee conclude about fraud as a ground for departing from the separate entity rule in Singapore?",
  options:[
    {t:"It needs closer scrutiny; prefer a calibrated response", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.049] states that the extent to which fraud justifies departing from separate personality 'clearly requires closer judicial examination in Singapore'. The privilege of incorporation and limited liability ought not to be abused, 'it does not follow that an unqualified power to disregard the company's separate existence is needed to counteract such abuse'. She points ahead to her suggested framework in 6L, which favours a narrower, calibrated response. Her earlier analysis ([06.047]–[06.048]) explains why: a broad fraud ground would override privity and other entrenched rules, while tort and equity already offer redress for fraud."},
    {t:"Fraud should justify treating the company and controller as one for all purposes", correct:false,
     why:"Lee rejects an unqualified power of this kind. Remedies should go no further than necessary ([06.029])."},
    {t:"Fraud can never justify any departure from the separate entity rule", correct:false,
     why:"Lee does not go that far. She accepts abuse should be checked, but calls for a limited, calibrated approach."},
    {t:"The Court of Appeal has settled the scope of the fraud ground", correct:false,
     why:"Lee says no serious attempt has yet been made to rationalise its scope ([06.044])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.044], [06.049], fn 110", takeaway:"Fraud ground: needs scrutiny; prefer calibrated responses." },

/* ---------- 6I ---------- */
{ id:"cl6-042", lesson:"6I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the test for whether a company is its controller's 'alter ego' for veil-lifting purposes?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether it carries on the controller's business", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.050] notes that Singapore courts have accepted several times that the veil may be lifted where a company is used as its controller's 'alter ego' or 'mere extension'. The test, from NEC Asia v Picket Rail and endorsed by the Court of Appeal in Alwie Handoyo v Tjong Very Sumito (fn 111), is whether the company is in fact carrying on the business of its controller. Mere control is insufficient, because Salomon held that a company carries on its own business even when one shareholder controls it. The finding is factual, but courts have relied on the controller conducting the company's business as though it were his own, typically shown by habitual failure to observe corporate formalities and governance rules, or improper mixing of company and personal assets. Fn 111 adds that in Sun Electric v Menrva Solutions the Court of Appeal noted Prest appears to exclude alter ego as a ground, but declined to comment."},
    {t:"Whether one person owns all the shares", correct:false,
     why:"Sole ownership is the Salomon situation and is consistent with the company carrying on its own business."},
    {t:"Whether the controller is also a director", correct:false,
     why:"Controllers are usually directors. That fact alone says nothing about whose business is being carried on."},
    {t:"Whether the company is lowly capitalised", correct:false,
     why:"Low capitalisation is common and lawful, and not part of the alter ego test."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.050], fn 111", takeaway:"Alter ego: is it really the controller's business?" },

{ id:"c6x-047", lesson:"6I", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The Court of Appeal in Sun Electric v Menrva Solutions definitively held that alter ego remains a valid ground for piercing the veil in Singapore after Prest.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Fn 111 to [06.050] records that in Sun Electric Pte Ltd v Menrva Solutions Pte Ltd, the Court of Appeal noted that Prest v Petrodel 'appears to exclude alter ego as a ground for veil piercing', but 'declined to comment on the correctness of that approach since it was not required for resolving the dispute'. So the question is open. Singapore courts have applied alter ego in cases such as Asteroid Maritime and Tjong Very Sumito, and the Court of Appeal endorsed the 'carrying on the controller's business' test in Alwie Handoyo, but the ground's survival after Prest has not been definitively confirmed. Lee herself questions whether it is a true exception ([06.053])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The Court of Appeal expressly declined to decide the point, leaving alter ego's post-Prest status uncertain."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.050], [06.053], fn 111", takeaway:"Sun Electric: alter ego's survival after Prest left open." },

{ id:"cl6-043", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Owners of the Fidelity are unpaid by its charterer CCC, part of the Orri Group. They arrest the Saudi Al Jubail, nominally owned by OSC (a Maltese company) and then SAJ. It emerges CCC and SAJ never existed. Orri paid for the vessel, picked group entities to suit himself, and dealt with the vessel as his own.",
  prompt:"How was Asteroid Maritime v The Saudi Al Jubail decided?",
  options:[
    {t:"The arrest stood: Orri was charterer and beneficial owner", correct:true,
     why:"The plaintiffs could arrest the Saudi Al Jubail if the charterer of the Fidelity was also the beneficial owner of all the shares in the Saudi Al Jubail ([06.051], fn 113). The court found that CCC was a non-entity, so the true charterer was Orri, the only person in the group who could decide and conclude charters. OSC was a real Maltese company, but the court lifted its veil: Orri ran the group, it was his money, he picked entities to suit his purposes, and he 'dealt with and purported to dispose of the vessel as his own'; OSC and SAJ were 'mere corporate names which Mohammed Orri had abused as a cover'. Fn 114 adds that calling it a sham case (as NEC Asia did) fits the non-existent entities, since presenting a non-entity as a legal person is a true sham, but not OSC, which existed; that part is better seen as alter ego. Lee notes it could also be justified by a resulting trust, since Orri funded the purchase without intending OSC to own the vessel beneficially."},
    {t:"The arrest was set aside because OSC was a validly incorporated owner", correct:false,
     why:"OSC's valid incorporation did not stop the court finding that Orri was the beneficial owner."},
    {t:"Only the non-existent companies were liable", correct:false,
     why:"A non-entity cannot be liable for anything. The court found Orri was the real party."},
    {t:"The court treated the Orri Group as a single economic entity", correct:false,
     why:"The reasoning focused on Orri personally, as charterer and as the person who used the vessel as his own, not on the group as an economic unit."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.051], fns 112–114", takeaway:"Saudi Al Jubail: Orri used OSC as his own; arrest upheld." },

{ id:"c6x-048", lesson:"6I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Asteroid Maritime, how does Lee suggest the different parts of the judgment are best analysed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Non-entities were shams; OSC was alter ego; or a resulting trust", correct:true,
     why:"Fn 114 to [06.051] untangles the reasoning. Belinda Ang J in NEC Asia categorised Asteroid Maritime as a sham or façade case. Lee says this is apt for CCC and SAJ, which did not exist, because 'the representation of a non-entity as a legal entity is a \"sham\" in the true sense'. It does not fit OSC, which was always a duly incorporated company. Given the court's emphasis on Orri dealing with the vessel as his own, the OSC finding is better understood as an application of the alter ego exception. Lee adds that the conclusion that Orri beneficially owned the vessel 'could also have been justified on principles of resulting trust', since he funded the purchase without intending OSC to own it beneficially. That alternative, like agency and trust generally ([06.030]), would not require displacing separate personality at all."},
    {t:"The whole case is a single-economic-entity decision", correct:false,
     why:"Lee's analysis focuses on sham (for non-entities), alter ego and resulting trust, not group economic unity."},
    {t:"OSC was a sham because it was incorporated in Malta", correct:false,
     why:"Place of incorporation is irrelevant. OSC existed, so it was not a sham in the true sense."},
    {t:"Orri was liable only as a director of OSC", correct:false,
     why:"The finding was that Orri was the true charterer and beneficial owner, not that he was liable qua director."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.051], fn 114", takeaway:"Non-entity = true sham; OSC = alter ego; or resulting trust." },

{ id:"cl6-044", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Alwie has absolute control of OAFL. Sums paid to OAFL are in fact received by Alwie, he uses OAFL's money for personal purposes, and OAFL files no independent defence, speaking only through him.",
  prompt:"What happened in Tjong Very Sumito v Chan Sing En and on appeal?",
  options:[
    {t:"Alter ego accepted, but obiter because OAFL wasn't liable", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.052] lists the four factors the trial judge relied on: Alwie's absolute control of OAFL; sums paid to OAFL were beneficially received by Alwie; he used OAFL's money for personal purposes; and OAFL filed no independent defence, speaking entirely through him. On that basis the court held Alwie had used OAFL as an 'extension' of himself and was personally accountable for its liabilities in conversion and unjust enrichment. The Court of Appeal affirmed this reasoning, stressing that Alwie 'made no distinction between himself and OAFL'. But the holding was 'ultimately inconsequential (and hence obiter)' because the Court of Appeal reversed the finding that OAFL was liable in conversion or unjust enrichment, so there was nothing to impose on Alwie even with the veil lifted."},
    {t:"The Court of Appeal rejected the alter ego exception", correct:false,
     why:"The Court of Appeal affirmed the alter ego reasoning, though as obiter."},
    {t:"Alwie was held personally liable on appeal", correct:false,
     why:"With OAFL found not liable, there was no liability to impose on Alwie."},
    {t:"OAFL was held to be a sham and dissolved", correct:false,
     why:"The decision turned on alter ego and the underlying liability. No dissolution was ordered."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.052], fns 115–117", takeaway:"Tjong Very Sumito: alter ego accepted, but obiter." },

{ id:"cl6-045", lesson:"6I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the chapter question the alter ego exception?",
  options:[
    {t:"Informality alone doesn't justify piercing", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.053] asks why factors like corporate informality or commingling 'are by themselves sufficient reasons for displacing the Salomon principle'. Small and medium companies often operate informally, and 'absent any impropriety aimed at third parties, it is hard to see why the veil should be lifted'. A controller who mixes the company's property with his own may incur personal liability in tort, equity or other general principles, but that is not veil lifting. If informality or mixing are part of a fraudulent scheme, then 'it would be fraud, rather than informality, that provides the reason', and even then a general liability via veil lifting is problematic if it undermines other principles. An alternative view ([06.054]) treats alter ego as a species of implied agency: if the facts show the company and controller consented to an agency relationship, the controller is bound as principal, which leaves separate personality intact."},
    {t:"It is too narrow to be useful", correct:false,
     why:"Lee's concern is that it is unprincipled and potentially too broad, not too narrow."},
    {t:"Statute has abolished it", correct:false,
     why:"No statute abolishes it; its status is a matter of case law, left open in Sun Electric."},
    {t:"It applies only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Alter ego arguments typically arise in small private companies, which is part of Lee's concern."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.053]–[06.054], fn 118", takeaway:"Alter ego: weak on its own; maybe just implied agency." },

{ id:"c6x-044", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Teak Pte Ltd is a two-person family company that never holds board meetings, keeps sloppy minutes, and sometimes pays family expenses from its account, always reimbursed and recorded. A customer with an unpaid refund claim wants to hold the controlling shareholder personally liable as Teak's alter ego. There is no suggestion of dishonesty towards customers.",
  prompt:"How strong is the alter ego argument, on Lee's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"Weak: informality without impropriety isn't enough", correct:true,
     why:"The customer would point to the factors courts have used ([06.050]): failure to observe formalities and mixing of company and personal assets. But Lee [06.053] questions whether these are sufficient by themselves: 'it is not uncommon for small and medium companies to operate with a high degree of informality', and absent 'any impropriety aimed at third parties, it is hard to see why the veil should be lifted to hold a controller responsible for corporate liabilities'. Here expenses were reimbursed and recorded, and no one was misled. The controller might face personal liability on some other footing if he had taken company money, but not simply because Teak was run informally. The customer's claim is against Teak."},
    {t:"Strong: failure to hold board meetings proves the company is the controller's alter ego", correct:false,
     why:"Informality alone is weak evidence, and Lee argues it should not be enough to displace Salomon."},
    {t:"Strong, because any mixing of funds makes the controller liable for all company debts", correct:false,
     why:"Mixing assets may give rise to specific liabilities in tort or equity, but it does not automatically make the controller answer for the company's debts."},
    {t:"Strong, because family companies are partnerships in substance", correct:false,
     why:"A family company is a separate legal person like any other (Salomon). Treating it as a partnership would ignore its incorporation."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.050], [06.053]", takeaway:"Informal SME without impropriety: alter ego argument weak." },

{ id:"c6x-046", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ash Pte Ltd, wholly owned by Mr Goh, signs contracts that, the evidence shows, both Ash and Goh understood Ash was concluding on Goh's behalf: Goh negotiated personally, the goods went to Goh's personal project, and Ash was paid a small handling fee.",
  prompt:"How would the 'implied agency' view of alter ego analyse Goh's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"If both consented to agency, Goh is bound as principal", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.054] presents an alternative understanding: alter ego is 'not an independent ground for lifting the veil but merely a species of \"implied agency\"' (NEC Asia; Walter Woon, fn 118). The question is whether there are sufficient facts to infer that the company and its controller consented to create an agency relationship. Here there are strong indications: Goh negotiated personally, the goods went to his own project, and Ash was paid as a handler. If agency is established, Goh is bound as principal by the contracts Ash made for him. Crucially, this 'does not … in any way interfere with the ordinary incidents of the separate entity rule since a company, like other legal persons, is capable of acting as another's agent' ([06.030]). The counterparty's case rests on agency evidence, not on showing abuse."},
    {t:"Goh is liable only if the veil is pierced on a fraud ground", correct:false,
     why:"Under the implied agency view, no piercing is needed. Goh's liability comes from being the principal."},
    {t:"Goh cannot be liable because Ash is a separate person", correct:false,
     why:"Separate personality is what allows Ash to be an agent. A principal is liable for contracts made by his agent within authority."},
    {t:"Ash and Goh are treated as the same person", correct:false,
     why:"Agency treats them as two persons, principal and agent. That is why it does not conflict with Salomon."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.030], [06.054], fn 118", takeaway:"Alter ego as implied agency: controller bound as principal." },

{ id:"c6x-045", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a claim against Fig Pte Ltd, the plaintiff establishes that Fig's managing director, Ms Lau, was its 'directing mind and will' when it committed a regulatory breach. The plaintiff then argues that, since Ms Lau is Fig's 'alter ego', the veil should be lifted and she should pay Fig's debts.",
  prompt:"What is wrong with this argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"Being a directing mind (attribution) isn't veil lifting", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.055] warns that 'alter ego' is used in different senses. In the attribution context (Ch 7), the 'alter ego' is the person or group who makes decisions for the company, so their acts and mind are treated as the company's. In the veil-lifting context, it is the company that is said to be the controller's alter ego. 'It should not be assumed that the mere identification of a person as a company's \"alter ego\" or \"directing mind\" constitutes or warrants veil lifting.' Establishing Ms Lau as Fig's directing mind helps prove Fig committed the breach. It does not make her liable for Fig's obligations. For that, the plaintiff would need a personal basis, such as her own participation in a tort ([06.056]–[06.057]), or a genuine veil-piercing ground."},
    {t:"Nothing: a directing mind is always personally liable for the company's debts", correct:false,
     why:"That is exactly the confusion Lee identifies. Attribution makes the company responsible, not the individual."},
    {t:"Directing-mind analysis applies only to criminal cases", correct:false,
     why:"Attribution through directing minds is used in civil as well as criminal contexts (Ch 7). The flaw is the leap to veil lifting."},
    {t:"The plaintiff should argue Fig is a sham instead", correct:false,
     why:"Fig is a real company. Changing the label would not fix the conceptual error."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.055]", takeaway:"'Directing mind' (attribution) ≠ company as alter ego (veil lifting)." },

{ id:"cl6-046", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director and controller of a company personally authorises the import of unsafe slimming pills, which injure consumers. The Court of Appeal finds him personally liable, describing him as the company's 'controlling mind' and lifting the veil.",
  prompt:"What is the better view of TV Media v De Cruz Andrea Heidi?",
  options:[
    {t:"He was a joint tortfeasor; no veil lifting was needed", correct:true,
     why:"In TV Media, the Court of Appeal affirmed that the director-controller was personally liable for the company's negligent import of unsafe slimming pills because he had 'authorised, directed, or procured' the tort ([06.056]). Unfortunately, it also explained this as veil lifting, saying he was 'clearly the controlling mind and spirit' of the company and finding 'sufficient reason to lift [its] corporate veil'. Lee [06.057] argues the better view is that he was liable as a joint tortfeasor for his own participation: 'one is not freed from personal liability merely by committing the tort with a company' (Ch 7, [07.019], [07.021]). So TV Media 'is no authority for the wider proposition that a company's veil may be lifted to impose personal liability on its alter ego'. Fn 123 notes the High Court in Tjong Very Sumito nonetheless cited TV Media as a veil-piercing case, which shows how the confusion spreads."},
    {t:"It is good authority that a company's controlling mind is always personally liable", correct:false,
     why:"That conflates attribution with veil lifting ([06.055]). Personal liability needs personal participation in the wrong."},
    {t:"The director was liable only as the company's agent", correct:false,
     why:"Agents are not usually liable for their principal's torts merely by being agents. His liability rested on his own participation as a joint tortfeasor."},
    {t:"The company was held to be a sham", correct:false,
     why:"Sham played no part in the reasoning. The company really imported the pills."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.056]–[06.057], fns 119–123", takeaway:"TV Media = joint tortfeasor, not veil lifting." },

{ id:"c6x-049", lesson:"6I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Ho, sole director of Vine Media Pte Ltd, personally instructs staff to publish an article he knows is defamatory of a rival. The rival sues both Vine and Mr Ho.",
  prompt:"On the better view of TV Media, why might Mr Ho be personally liable?",
  options:[
    {t:"He procured the tort, so he is a joint tortfeasor", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.057] explains TV Media as 'a specific application of the general rule that a person may be jointly liable for committing a tort with another, and one is not freed from personal liability merely by committing the tort with a company'. Mr Ho did not merely hold office; he personally instructed the publication. That personal participation makes him liable alongside Vine. Nothing about Vine's separate personality needs to be set aside, and Mr Ho would not thereby become liable for Vine's other debts. Chapter 7 explores the limits of this 'authorising, directing and procuring' liability for directors in more detail."},
    {t:"Because he is Vine's sole director, so he is its alter ego", correct:false,
     why:"Office alone does not make a director liable for the company's torts. Liability turns on his own participation."},
    {t:"Because the veil of any one-director company is automatically lifted", correct:false,
     why:"Salomon shows one-person companies are separate persons. No automatic piercing applies."},
    {t:"He cannot be liable because only Vine published the article", correct:false,
     why:"Procuring a tort committed through a company is itself a basis for personal liability as a joint tortfeasor."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.056]–[06.057], fn 122", takeaway:"Director who procures the company's tort: joint tortfeasor." }

]);

;
