/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 6 · Lessons 6J Corporate Groups and 6K Circumventing the Veil (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 6 [06.058]–[06.078], fns 124–168. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 6J ---------- */
{ id:"cl6-052", lesson:"6J", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The Companies Act formally recognises 'horizontal' groups, companies owned by the same individuals rather than a holding company, as corporate groups.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.058] explains that the Act was largely formulated with the single entity in mind and conceives of a group as pyramidal or vertical: a holding or ultimate holding company controlling subsidiaries through levels of ownership. 'Group' is also used loosely for companies affiliated horizontally, whose shares are owned by the same natural persons rather than a holding company. The Act does not formally recognise these as a 'group'. But courts have accepted that the Act may still regulate their affairs where a provision's text admits a broad reading. In Lim Chee Twang v Chan Shuk Kuen, for instance, the court took into account the conduct of horizontally related companies' affairs in deciding whether a company's affairs had been conducted oppressively under s 216 (fn 126)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Only vertical groups fit the Act's concept of a group. Horizontal affiliates are reached only where a provision's wording allows."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.058], fns 124–126", takeaway:"CA groups are vertical; horizontal affiliates only by broad reading." },

{ id:"cl6-047", lesson:"6J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why are corporate groups most commonly used, and is this legitimate?",
  options:[
    {t:"To ring-fence liability; this is legitimate", correct:true,
     why:"Groups are used for many reasons, including tax planning, acquiring businesses by buying shares rather than assets, joint ventures, attracting investment into part of a business and statutory compliance (fn 127). But Lee [06.059] says they are most commonly used to minimise legal liability: by putting different activities in different subsidiaries, a group confines each activity's liabilities to the responsible subsidiary and shields other group companies' assets from its creditors. This is 'now widely accepted as legitimate'. Templeman LJ's remark in Re Southard, which Lee says also reflects Singapore law, captures the result: if one subsidiary 'turns out to be the runt of the litter and declines into insolvency', the parent and other subsidiaries 'may prosper to the joy of the shareholders without any liability for the debts of the insolvent subsidiary'."},
    {t:"To avoid all tax", correct:false,
     why:"Tax savings are one reason for groups, but Lee identifies liability minimisation as the most common, and 'avoiding all tax' overstates what group structures lawfully achieve."},
    {t:"Groups are illegitimate and courts treat them as one entity", correct:false,
     why:"Group structures are accepted as legitimate, and courts have rejected treating them as single entities (Adams v Cape; Goh Chan Peng)."},
    {t:"Because the Companies Act requires a subsidiary for each business line", correct:false,
     why:"There is no such requirement. Using subsidiaries is a commercial choice."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.059], fns 127–130", takeaway:"Groups ring-fence liability; legitimate (Re Southard)." },

{ id:"c6x-050", lesson:"6J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Conglomerate Ltd has five subsidiaries. One, Retail Sub Pte Ltd, becomes insolvent owing suppliers $20m. Conglomerate and the other four subsidiaries are highly profitable. The suppliers argue that the group as a whole should pay Retail Sub's debts.",
  prompt:"What is the likely outcome under Singapore law?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only Retail Sub is liable (Re Southard)", correct:true,
     why:"This is the 'runt of the litter' scenario Templeman LJ described in Re Southard, which Lee [06.059] says also reflects the Singapore position: the parent and other subsidiaries 'may prosper … without any liability for the debts of the insolvent subsidiary'. Each company is a separate legal person, and Singapore has rejected single economic entity reasoning as a basis for veil piercing (Goh Chan Peng v Beyonics, [06.066]). The suppliers would need some other basis against another group company: for example, a parent guarantee, a contract with the parent directly, a parent's own tortious duty (Chandler), or insolvency claims such as wrongful trading against those running Retail Sub."},
    {t:"The whole group is liable, because it is a single economic unit", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore courts have rejected this argument (Lew Syn Pau; Goh Chan Peng)."},
    {t:"The parent is liable, but not the sister companies", correct:false,
     why:"Being the parent does not make Conglomerate liable for Retail Sub's debts without some additional basis."},
    {t:"Only the most profitable sister company is liable", correct:false,
     why:"No rule makes the richest affiliate liable. Each company answers only for its own obligations."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.059], [06.066]", takeaway:"Insolvent subsidiary's debts stay with the subsidiary." },

{ id:"c6x-051", lesson:"6J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shipping subsidiary of a well-known group wins long credit terms from a fuel supplier by highlighting the group's strength in its pitch, but gives no group guarantee. The subsidiary then defaults.",
  prompt:"Which statement best reflects Lee's analysis?",
  options:[
    {t:"No recourse against other group companies", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.060] describes exactly this risk: a group member 'may be able to leverage on the group's collective financial strength to extract favourable contractual terms from third parties, but the fact remains that such parties will have no recourse against the parent or any of the other group companies when the contracting member defaults'. Some have argued company law should regulate groups as single entities to reflect economic reality, including by giving courts a discretion to treat group companies as one. But that 'line of reasoning has had no enduring impact in England and has never been accepted in Singapore'. The supplier's protection was in its own hands: it could have insisted on a parent guarantee ([06.077]). If the pitch contained false statements, it might have a misrepresentation claim, but not a single-entity claim."},
    {t:"The supplier can sue the parent because the subsidiary relied on group strength", correct:false,
     why:"Using the group's reputation in negotiations does not make the parent a party or a guarantor."},
    {t:"The supplier can treat the group as one entity under DHN", correct:false,
     why:"DHN has been confined to its facts and has not been followed in Singapore ([06.062], [06.066])."},
    {t:"The subsidiary's contract is void for misleading the supplier", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing suggests the contract is void. The supplier has a valid claim, but only against the subsidiary."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.060], [06.077], fn 131", takeaway:"Group strength in the pitch ≠ group liability; ask for a guarantee." },

{ id:"cl6-048", lesson:"6J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"DHN runs a grocery business on land owned by its wholly owned subsidiary Bronze; another subsidiary owns the lorries. All three share directors. The land is compulsorily acquired. Bronze is compensated for the land, but DHN is refused compensation for disturbance as a mere licensee.",
  prompt:"What did the English Court of Appeal decide in DHN Food Distributors v Tower Hamlets?",
  options:[
    {t:"DHN was compensated on a single economic entity basis", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.061] sets out the two grounds. First, DHN had an irrevocable licence to use the land, which was a sufficient interest to qualify for compensation. Second, and more controversially, the court pierced the veils to treat the group as a single economic entity, because the three companies had 'complete identity of commercial interest and personality': common directors, common ultimate shareholders and a common interest in keeping the business on the land. Lee [06.062] characterises DHN as 'an exceptional instance of insider reverse piercing', letting a company obtain a statutory benefit it could have had but for a technical failure to restructure the land ownership before acquisition. Later cases (Woolfson, Adams v Cape) distinguish it on its own facts and refuse to treat it as authority that group companies may be merged whenever control and economic interests coincide. In Singapore, insider reverse piercing has no non-statutory basis (Jhaveri Darsan, [06.031])."},
    {t:"DHN's claim failed because Bronze owned the land", correct:false,
     why:"That was the council's argument. The court allowed DHN's claim. (The House of Lords reached the opposite result on similar issues in Woolfson.)"},
    {t:"Bronze had to share its compensation with DHN", correct:false,
     why:"DHN received its own compensation for disturbance; the court did not reallocate Bronze's compensation."},
    {t:"The subsidiaries were declared shams", correct:false,
     why:"The reasoning was single economic entity, not sham. The subsidiaries were genuine companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.061]–[06.062], fns 132–135", takeaway:"DHN: single economic entity, since confined to its facts." },

{ id:"cl6-049", lesson:"6J", type:"order",
  prompt:"Put the events of Adams v Cape Industries in order.",
  steps:[
    "Asbestos victims sue Cape and its US subsidiary NAAC in Tyler, Texas (Tyler 1)",
    "Tyler 1 settles for US$5.2m",
    "Cape liquidates NAAC and a new marketing company, CPC, owned by M, takes over",
    "Cape stays out of Tyler 2, and default judgments are entered",
    "In England, the claimants' single economic entity argument for enforcement fails"
  ],
  why:"Lee [06.063] narrates the sequence. Cape, an English company, headed a group mining asbestos in South Africa; asbestos was sold to a factory in Owentown, Texas, with US marketing handled by its wholly owned US subsidiary NAAC. People injured around the factory sued Cape and NAAC in Tyler, Texas (Tyler 1), which settled for US$5.2m. Cape then liquidated NAAC and set up a new marketing entity, CPC, whose shares were held by M, NAAC's former president. When a second round of claims (Tyler 2) was brought, Cape and its subsidiaries chose not to participate, and default judgments were entered. To enforce in England, the claimants had to show Cape and its English subsidiary Capasco were 'present' in the US, and argued, citing DHN, that they formed a single economic entity with NAAC or CPC. The Court of Appeal rejected this ([06.064]–[06.065]): NAAC was a properly constituted separate subsidiary, and CPC was not even a subsidiary but was independently and beneficially owned by M.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.063]–[06.065], fn 136", takeaway:"Tyler 1 settled → NAAC replaced → Tyler 2 default → Adams v Cape rejects single entity." },

{ id:"cl6-050", lesson:"6J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Adams v Cape decide about corporate groups?",
  options:[
    {t:"Subsidiaries are separate, however integrated the group", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.064] summarises the Court of Appeal's reasoning. There is no jurisdiction to disregard group companies' separate personalities 'merely because a parent company exercised overall supervision and control over its subsidiaries' operational and financial affairs'. DHN is not authority for any broader proposition; it is better seen as a case where the specific wording of the compensation statute allowed the companies to be treated as one. That a highly integrated group may operate as a single economic entity in commercial reality 'does not alter the fact that each company is, at law, a distinct legal person'. Slade LJ put it this way: the law, 'for better or worse, recognises the creation of subsidiary companies … [which] will nevertheless under the general law fall to be treated as separate legal entities with all the rights and liabilities which would normally attach to separate legal entities'."},
    {t:"Highly integrated groups are a single legal entity", correct:false,
     why:"That is the argument Adams v Cape rejected."},
    {t:"Parent companies are liable for their subsidiaries' torts", correct:false,
     why:"No general parent liability was laid down. Parent liability now arises, if at all, on Chandler/Vedanta duty-of-care principles ([06.075])."},
    {t:"Only wholly owned subsidiaries are treated as separate", correct:false,
     why:"NAAC was wholly owned and was still treated as separate. Separateness does not depend on ownership percentage."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.064], fn 137", takeaway:"Adams v Cape: subsidiaries are separate, full stop." },

{ id:"cl6-051", lesson:"6J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A parent set up a subsidiary specifically so that potential future liabilities of a new business line would stay inside it. A creditor of the now-insolvent subsidiary argues the group was a single economic unit.",
  prompt:"How would a Singapore court likely respond?",
  options:[
    {t:"Reject it: Singapore doesn't pierce on single entity grounds", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.066] states that Singapore courts have confirmed there is no special discretion to displace separate personality in a group context. In PP v Lew Syn Pau, Sundaresh Menon JC held that the separate entity principle survives even if the group's companies are organised as a single economic unit. It makes no difference that the subsidiary was set up specifically to avoid potential future liabilities, which has long been unobjectionable (The Andres Bonifacio; The Skaw Prince, fn 139). In Goh Chan Peng v Beyonics, the Court of Appeal rejected single economic entity as a basis for piercing, because it would 'offend both principle and authority and fundamentally undermine the doctrine of separate legal personality'. Lee [06.067] adds that the only exceptions involve documents or statutes worded broadly enough to refer to a company and its related persons."},
    {t:"Accept it, following DHN", correct:false,
     why:"DHN has been confined to its facts in England and has never been accepted in Singapore for this proposition."},
    {t:"Accept it, because the parent's motive was to limit liability", correct:false,
     why:"Limiting future liability is the accepted purpose of incorporation and of using subsidiaries; it is not abuse."},
    {t:"Accept it only if the companies share directors", correct:false,
     why:"Common directors are typical of groups and do not change the analysis. Lew Syn Pau and Goh Chan Peng rejected the argument regardless."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.066]–[06.067], fns 138–141", takeaway:"Singapore rejects single economic entity (Lew Syn Pau, Goh Chan Peng)." },

{ id:"c6x-052", lesson:"6J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two unrelated companies, Ark and Bay, have no common shareholders or directors but have a joint commercial interest in a development project. A creditor of Ark argues they were a 'single economic entity' so Bay should pay Ark's debt.",
  prompt:"How strong is the argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"Very weak: it fails even within groups", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.066] notes that 'a fortiori, the \"single economic entity\" argument must fail if the companies concerned, despite having common economic interests, are not connected by common shareholders or directors' (Win Line v Masterpart, fn 142). Singapore has rejected the argument even for genuinely related group companies (Goh Chan Peng; Lew Syn Pau). The same a fortiori reasoning appeared in Adams v Cape, where CPC was not even a subsidiary of Cape ([06.065]). Ark's creditor would need a genuine basis such as a joint venture contract under which Bay assumed liability, a guarantee, or a partnership on the facts."},
    {t:"Strong, because they had common economic interests", correct:false,
     why:"Common economic interest is not enough even for related companies. Without any common ownership or control, the argument is weaker still."},
    {t:"Strong if the project was profitable", correct:false,
     why:"Profitability has nothing to do with whether one company is liable for another's debts."},
    {t:"Strong, under DHN", correct:false,
     why:"DHN involved a parent and wholly owned subsidiaries with complete identity of interest, and has been confined to its facts."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.065]–[06.066], fn 142", takeaway:"No common owners/directors → single entity argument fails a fortiori." },

{ id:"c6x-053", lesson:"6J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A financing agreement provides that an event of default occurs if 'the Borrower or any of its related corporations' becomes insolvent. A sister company of the borrower becomes insolvent; the borrower argues it is a separate legal person and its own finances are healthy.",
  prompt:"Can the lender call a default?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: the clause itself refers to related corporations", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.067] states that attempts to hold one group company responsible for another's obligations on the basis of common parentage alone have 'scant prospect of success', but 'the exceptions to this general position are narrowly confined to situations involving documents or statutory provisions, the text of which is worded broadly to refer to a company and its related persons'. Here the parties chose to make the borrower's position depend on its related corporations. The court gives effect to that bargain. This is not veil piercing: the sister company remains separate, and the borrower is not liable for its debts; the borrower has simply agreed that a related company's insolvency is a default. 'Related corporation' would be read in light of the drafting, and s 6 CA's definition of related companies is the natural reference point (Ch 4)."},
    {t:"No: separate personality means a sister company's insolvency is irrelevant", correct:false,
     why:"Separate personality does not stop parties contracting by reference to related companies. The clause applies by its terms."},
    {t:"Only if the lender pierces the veil", correct:false,
     why:"No piercing is needed. The default is triggered by the contractual words."},
    {t:"Only if the borrower guaranteed the sister company's debts", correct:false,
     why:"A guarantee would be a different obligation. The default clause operates on its own terms."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.067], [06.077]", takeaway:"Broadly worded documents can reach related companies." },

{ id:"c6x-054", lesson:"6J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Could a Singapore parent company be held liable as a shadow director of its subsidiary simply because the subsidiary's board usually follows its instructions?",
  options:[
    {t:"Doubtful: almost every parent would then qualify", correct:true,
     why:"Fn 143 to [06.067] notes that while control is not a reason for lifting the veil, it may attract liability on other grounds, and Australian courts have held a parent liable as a shadow director of its subsidiary (Standard Chartered Bank of Australia v Antico; Ho v Akai). But Lee suggests 'there are good reasons to surmise that such an argument may not succeed in Singapore'. If control, meaning the subsidiary's directors being accustomed to act on the parent's instructions, were the sole criterion, the test 'will likely be satisfied in the vast majority of cases involving holding and subsidiary companies'. That potential for extensive liability 'may undermine the very reason for setting up corporate groups in the first place'. Shadow directorship is covered in Chapter 9."},
    {t:"Yes, automatically, as in Australia", correct:false,
     why:"Lee doubts the Australian approach would be followed here, precisely because it would make parent liability almost automatic."},
    {t:"Yes, because shadow directors are always liable for their subsidiaries' debts", correct:false,
     why:"Shadow directors owe duties, but that is different from automatic liability for the company's debts. And Lee doubts parents would be shadow directors on control alone."},
    {t:"No, because a company cannot be a shadow director", correct:false,
     why:"Australian cases have held parents to be shadow directors. Lee's doubt is about using control alone, not about corporate shadow directors in principle."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.067], fn 143", takeaway:"Parent as shadow director on control alone: doubtful in Singapore." },

/* ---------- 6K ---------- */
{ id:"cl6-053", lesson:"6K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is a litigant usually better off relying on conventional principles rather than veil piercing?",
  options:[
    {t:"Those principles are established and leave separateness intact", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.068] stresses that veil piercing is 'a limited remedy invoked only in exceptional circumstances', and a litigant trying to attach a company's rights or liabilities to its controller 'is more likely to succeed' by relying on conventional principles. A shareholder may be liable for the company's debts because he guaranteed them, or because he authorised the company to contract as his agent; a controller may be a joint tortfeasor for authorising the company's tort; and a shareholder may own company property beneficially under a trust. In each case the usual incidents of separate personality are altered not by veil lifting but by principles of general application, which 'affirm (rather than disregard) the company's distinct legal status'. Fn 144 adds statutory routes: on insolvency, officers or controllers may be personally liable for fraudulent or wrongful trading or misfeasance (IRDA ss 238–239), which produce effects similar to veil lifting."},
    {t:"Veil piercing is never available", correct:false,
     why:"Veil piercing exists but is exceptional and uncertain. That is why other routes are usually better."},
    {t:"Conventional principles always produce larger awards", correct:false,
     why:"The advantage is a higher likelihood of success on established doctrine, not the size of the award."},
    {t:"Courts must try veil piercing first", correct:false,
     why:"The opposite: veil piercing is closer to a last resort."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.068], fn 144", takeaway:"Prefer guarantee, agency, tort, trust over piercing." },

{ id:"c6x-062", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Sole director Mr Pang kept Spruce Pte Ltd trading for two years while he knew it had no reasonable prospect of paying its debts, taking new deposits from customers. Spruce is now in liquidation. Customers want Pang to bear Spruce's losses.",
  prompt:"Apart from veil piercing, what route does Lee highlight?",
  options:[
    {t:"Fraudulent or wrongful trading liability under IRDA ss 238–239", correct:true,
     why:"Fn 144 to [06.068] notes that 'where a company is insolvent, an officer or controller may also be personally liable for the losses that the company suffered as a result of his fraudulent or wrongful trading or misfeasance: see ss 238 and 239 of the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act 2018'. These provisions 'produce effects similar to those of veil lifting', making the individual contribute to the company's losses, without disturbing separate personality. Pang's continuing to trade and take deposits while he knew there was no reasonable prospect of paying fits the kind of conduct these provisions target. Such claims are usually brought by the liquidator for the benefit of creditors generally, rather than by individual customers suing Pang directly."},
    {t:"None: limited liability protects Pang completely", correct:false,
     why:"Limited liability protects Pang as shareholder, but statutory insolvency provisions can make officers personally liable for misconduct."},
    {t:"Pang is automatically liable for all Spruce's debts because he was the sole director", correct:false,
     why:"Liability under the insolvency provisions depends on proving fraudulent or wrongful trading or misfeasance, not merely on being the sole director."},
    {t:"The customers must show Spruce was a sham", correct:false,
     why:"Sham is unnecessary and unlikely. The statutory routes do not require any veil-piercing ground."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.068], fn 144", takeaway:"Insolvency: fraudulent/wrongful trading liability (IRDA ss 238–239)." },

{ id:"c6x-057", lesson:"6K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under Lord Pearson's test in Garnac Grain v Faure & Fairclough, how is an agency relationship between a company and its controller established?",
  options:[
    {t:"By both parties' consent, express or implied; labels don't decide", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.069] notes that a company, like other legal persons, may act as its shareholder's or controller's agent. Lord Pearson's test is that the relationship 'can only be established by the consent of the principal and the agent', given expressly or by implication from words or conduct. They will be held to have consented 'if they have agreed to what amounts in law to such a relationship, even if they do not recognise it themselves and even if they have professed to disclaim it' (ex parte Delhasse). So labels do not decide the matter; the substance of what they agreed does. With an express agreement, proof is straightforward ([06.070]). The harder question is what facts justify inferring agency, which is where Smith, Stone & Knight and its critics come in."},
    {t:"Whenever the controller owns more than half the company's shares", correct:false,
     why:"Ownership and control do not establish consent to agency. Later cases stress that more than control is needed ([06.071])."},
    {t:"Only by a written agency agreement", correct:false,
     why:"Consent may be implied from words or conduct, so no written agreement is required."},
    {t:"Whenever the parties call the arrangement an agency", correct:false,
     why:"Labels are not decisive in either direction. Agency may exist despite a disclaimer, and a label alone does not create one."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.069]–[06.070], fn 145", takeaway:"Agency = consent of both, express or implied; labels not decisive." },

{ id:"cl6-054", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A parent acquires a waste paper business and forms a subsidiary to run it, but never transfers the business to the subsidiary. The parent owns almost all the shares, appoints its only manager, supplies the expertise, controls decisions and books the profits as its own. The premises are compulsorily acquired.",
  prompt:"What did Atkinson J hold in Smith, Stone & Knight v Birmingham Corp?",
  options:[
    {t:"The subsidiary ran the business as the parent's agent", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.070] presents the case as the regular example of 'implied agency'. Atkinson J relied on four factors: (a) the parent treated the subsidiary's profits as its own; (b) it appointed the manager and supplied the expertise; (c) it had complete say over management decisions; and (d) it was in effectual and constant control. Later cases treat it as a precedent of limited value ([06.071]). Toulson J in Yukong Line observed that those who conduct business through a company ordinarily intend to exclude agency, having the company act as principal, so something more than the parent's benefit and control is needed. Kerr LJ in J H Rayner said its facts 'were so unusual that they cannot form any basis of principle'. Lee [06.073] identifies the genuinely telling feature: the business had never been transferred to the subsidiary, so it lacked any beneficial interest in it."},
    {t:"The subsidiary owned the business, so the parent got nothing", correct:false,
     why:"That would follow Woolfson, but Atkinson J found an implied agency, partly because the business was never actually transferred."},
    {t:"The single economic entity doctrine applied", correct:false,
     why:"The finding was agency, which affirms the subsidiary's separate capacity rather than merging the companies."},
    {t:"The subsidiary was a sham", correct:false,
     why:"Sham was not the basis. The subsidiary was a genuine company acting as agent."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.070]–[06.071], fns 146–150", takeaway:"Smith, Stone & Knight: implied agency, now a weak precedent." },

{ id:"cl6-055", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shipping subsidiary has a paid-up capital of $2, and all its directors are also directors of its parent. A claimant argues the subsidiary must be the parent's agent.",
  prompt:"Following The Skaw Prince, is agency established?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: those features are typical of legitimate subsidiaries", correct:true,
     why:"In The Asean Promoter, A Wahab Ghows J applied Smith, Stone & Knight to hold, obiter, that a subsidiary carried on its shipping business as its parent's agent, because its $2 paid-up capital was too low for an independent existence and all its directors were also the parent's directors ([06.072]); he also confusingly called this lifting the veil (fn 152). Amarjeet Singh JC in The Skaw Prince called that finding 'erroneous', because it ran counter to the general rule that companies may incorporate subsidiaries to limit future liabilities. Such subsidiaries are typically lowly capitalised and share directors with the parent. Lee [06.073] concludes that consent to agency 'will not be lightly inferred'; facts merely reflecting control and interests are insufficient, and what matters more is evidence that the company lacks beneficial or commercial interest in the business or asset."},
    {t:"Yes, following The Asean Promoter", correct:false,
     why:"That aspect of The Asean Promoter has been discredited by The Skaw Prince."},
    {t:"Yes, because $2 capital shows it cannot exist independently", correct:false,
     why:"Thin capitalisation is common and legitimate for subsidiaries set up to limit liability."},
    {t:"Only if the parent guarantees its debts", correct:false,
     why:"A guarantee is a separate contractual basis of liability. It is not how agency is established."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.072]–[06.073], fns 151–153", takeaway:"Skaw Prince: $2 capital + common directors ≠ agency." },

{ id:"c6x-058", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mira Pte Ltd, controlled by a wealthy individual, signs a contract to build a private marina. Mira has no other business, will not own or operate the marina, and gets nothing from the project; the controller will own and use it.",
  prompt:"What inference may a court draw?",
  options:[
    {t:"That Mira contracted as its controller's agent", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.073] says the more pertinent evidence for implied agency is 'the company's lack of beneficial or commercial interests in the business or asset in question'. She cites a case (Hyundai Engineering v Prince Jefri Bolkiah, fn 154) where it was said that if a company contracts for the building of a marina but has 'neither beneficial nor commercial interests in the project, it may reasonably be inferred that the company is not contracting for itself but as agent for its controlling shareholder (who has demonstrable interests in the project)'. Mira's facts fit closely. If agency is inferred, the controller is bound as principal. This is not veil piercing; Mira is treated as a separate person acting as agent ([06.030])."},
    {t:"That Mira is a sham, so the contract is void", correct:false,
     why:"The contract is real. The question is on whose behalf Mira made it."},
    {t:"That Mira alone is liable, because it signed", correct:false,
     why:"An agent's signature can bind a principal. Mira's lack of interest in the project supports inferring that it signed as agent."},
    {t:"That the controller is liable only if he signed a guarantee", correct:false,
     why:"A guarantee is one route, but agency is another, and the facts here support it."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.073], fn 154", takeaway:"No beneficial/commercial interest in the project → inferred agent." },

{ id:"c6x-059", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Quah, a shareholder-director of Larch Pte Ltd, negotiates a sale on Larch's behalf and knowingly makes false statements to the buyer about the goods. Larch is liable to the buyer for deceit. Quah argues he acted only as Larch's agent.",
  prompt:"Is Quah personally liable?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: an agent stays liable for his own tort", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.074] states that 'if the shareholder commits a tort whilst acting as the company's agent, he is not exempt from personal liability even if the company is liable as principal for the same tort' (Standard Chartered Bank v Pakistan National Shipping Corp, fn 157). Quah personally made the fraudulent misrepresentations. That he did so on Larch's behalf makes Larch liable too, but does not shield him. Lee lists related routes: a shareholder who conspires with the company to injure another is jointly liable (Nagase v Ching Kai Huat; Lim Leong Huat, fn 155), and a shareholder-director who authorises, directs or procures the company's tort commits a tort himself ([06.056]–[06.057]). None of this requires piercing the veil."},
    {t:"No: an agent acting within authority is never personally liable", correct:false,
     why:"That is a rule about contractual liability. For torts an agent personally commits, the agent remains liable alongside the principal."},
    {t:"Only if the veil of Larch is pierced", correct:false,
     why:"No piercing is needed. Quah's liability rests on his own tort."},
    {t:"Only if Larch cannot pay", correct:false,
     why:"Quah's liability is not secondary. He and Larch are both liable for the deceit."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.074], fns 155–157", takeaway:"Agent who personally commits a tort stays liable (Standard Chartered v PNSC)." },

{ id:"cl6-056", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A brick loader employed by Cape Products in 1959–62 contracts asbestosis 50 years later. Cape Products, a subsidiary, has been dissolved. Its parent, Cape, set group health and safety policies, had expert knowledge of asbestos risks, and knew the factory had no side walls.",
  prompt:"What did the English Court of Appeal decide in Chandler v Cape?",
  options:[
    {t:"Cape breached its own duty of care; no piercing", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.075] explains that, although Cape Products carried on its own business as a separate entity, Cape retained control over certain aspects of it, including health and safety policies and practices, which showed it had assumed responsibility for a safe system of work for the subsidiary's employees. Cape also had superior knowledge of asbestos risks, having engaged medical and scientific experts, and knew of the systemic flaw in Cape Products' operations, a factory with no side walls. It ought to have advised Cape Products on precautions but did not. The claimant succeeded at trial and on appeal. The Court of Appeal 'emphatically disavowed' that this was veil piercing ([06.076]). Lee nonetheless warns that a liberal approach to parent liability 'would in practice create a significant inroad into the separate entity rule'."},
    {t:"Cape was liable by piercing the subsidiary's veil", correct:false,
     why:"The court expressly said it was not piercing the veil; Cape was liable for breach of its own duty."},
    {t:"No one was liable, because the employer had been dissolved", correct:false,
     why:"The subsidiary's dissolution was why the claimant sued Cape, and Cape was liable for its own negligence."},
    {t:"Cape was vicariously liable as the employer", correct:false,
     why:"Cape was not the employer. Its liability was direct, based on assumption of responsibility."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.075]–[06.076], fns 158, 165", takeaway:"Chandler: parent's own duty of care, not piercing." },

{ id:"cl6-057", lesson:"6K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"According to Lungowe v Vedanta, when may a parent company owe a duty of care for its subsidiary's activities?",
  options:[
    {t:"It depends on how far the parent intervened in operations", correct:true,
     why:"The UK Supreme Court endorsed Chandler in Vedanta ([06.075]). Whether a parent owes a duty depends on the extent it intervened in the management of the subsidiary's activities. Ownership alone does not suffice, but it creates the opportunity to intervene, so the critical consideration is 'the extent to which, and the way in which, the parent availed itself of the opportunity to take over, intervene in, control, supervise or advise the management of the relevant operations'. This is not reducible to control; the real issue is 'the extent to which the company did take over or share with the subsidiary the management of the relevant activity'. There is no general rule that laying down group-wide policies cannot create liability. A duty may arise if the policies contain systemic errors, or if the parent takes active steps to implement them through training, supervision and enforcement. Lee [06.076] sees this as underscoring the potential encroachment on separate personality."},
    {t:"Whenever it owns more than 50% of the subsidiary", correct:false,
     why:"Ownership alone does not create a duty; it only creates the opportunity for the kind of intervention that might."},
    {t:"Never, because of separate personality", correct:false,
     why:"Vedanta endorsed Chandler. A parent can owe its own duty without any piercing."},
    {t:"Only if it issued no group policies", correct:false,
     why:"The reverse can be true: flawed policies, or actively implemented ones, can found a duty."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.075]–[06.076], fns 159–166", takeaway:"Vedanta: duty turns on actual intervention in the activity." },

{ id:"c6x-061", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two parents each own 100% of a mining subsidiary. Parent A merely owns its subsidiary and receives dividends. Parent B issues a group-wide tailings-dam safety manual, trains the subsidiary's engineers on it and audits compliance. A dam fails at each mine, injuring villagers, and the subsidiaries are insolvent.",
  prompt:"Applying Vedanta, which parent is more exposed to a direct duty of care claim?",
  options:[
    {t:"Parent B, because it actively implemented policies", correct:true,
     why:"Vedanta makes ownership a starting point only ([06.075]): it creates the opportunity to intervene, but the question is how far the parent actually took over, intervened in, controlled, supervised or advised the relevant operations. Parent A did none of that. Parent B did: it set group-wide safety policies and, crucially, took 'active steps to implement them by providing … training, supervision and enforcement'. A duty may arise on that basis, especially if the manual contained systemic errors. The irony Lee points to ([06.076]) is that a parent that engages with safety may face more exposure than one that stays hands-off, which is part of why commentators see Chandler and Vedanta as a significant inroad on separate personality (fn 166). Neither parent would be liable through veil piercing."},
    {t:"Parent A, because owning 100% makes it responsible for its subsidiary", correct:false,
     why:"Ownership alone does not create a duty of care under Vedanta."},
    {t:"Both equally, because both are 100% owners", correct:false,
     why:"Equal ownership does not mean equal exposure. The duty turns on actual intervention in the relevant activity."},
    {t:"Neither, because group policies can never create a duty", correct:false,
     why:"Vedanta expressly rejected any general principle that group-wide policies cannot create liability."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.075]–[06.076], fns 160–166", takeaway:"Hands-on parent (implemented policies) > hands-off owner for duty exposure." },

{ id:"cl6-058", lesson:"6K", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each route around the veil to an example.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Contract", r:"Shareholder guarantee of a thinly capitalised company's loan"},
    {l:"Restrictive covenant", r:"Seller's non-compete extended to companies he controls"},
    {l:"Trust", r:"Prest: properties held on resulting trust for the husband"},
    {l:"Tort", r:"Director who authorises the company's tort is jointly liable"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [06.074]–[06.078] shows how conventional principles achieve what claimants often try to get through veil piercing. Parties are free to contract around separate personality ([06.077]): a lender unwilling to bear a thin company's default risk may insist on shareholder guarantees, and shareholders who give them lose limited liability for that debt. A well-drafted restrictive covenant on the sale of a business should bar competition not only by the seller but by companies he controls or is related to, which in effect ignores their separate identities. Trusts ([06.078]): in Prest, the Supreme Court refused to pierce the veils of the husband's companies but held unanimously that the properties were held on resulting trust for him, so they were available for the wife's financial claim. Tort ([06.074]): a shareholder-director who authorises, directs or procures the company's tort commits a tort himself and is jointly liable.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.074], [06.077]–[06.078], fns 156, 168", takeaway:"Guarantee · covenant · trust · joint tort." },

{ id:"c6x-063", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"You act for the buyer of a bakery business. The seller will give a three-year non-compete. The seller owns two dormant companies and could easily set up more.",
  prompt:"How should the covenant be drafted, given Lee's discussion?",
  options:[
    {t:"Cover the seller and any entity he controls or is related to", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.077] gives restrictive covenants as the typical example of contracting around separate personality. A seller 'may undertake not to compete with the purchaser or solicit the customers of the business after the sale. For this purpose, a well-drafted covenant should bar not only direct competition or solicitation by the seller, but similar activities carried out by companies controlled by or related to the seller.' If the covenant binds only the seller, the buyer may have to argue evasion or Gilford Motor-type reasoning if the seller competes through a company ([06.032], [06.036]). Clear drafting avoids that uncertain route by making the related-entity restriction part of the bargain."},
    {t:"To bind only the seller, since a court will always pierce the veil of his companies", correct:false,
     why:"Veil piercing is exceptional and uncertain. Relying on it instead of drafting is poor practice."},
    {t:"To require the seller to dissolve his dormant companies", correct:false,
     why:"That would not stop him forming new ones. Extending the covenant to controlled and related entities addresses the risk directly."},
    {t:"No covenant is needed, because Gilford Motor makes all non-competes extend to companies", correct:false,
     why:"Gilford Motor involved a deliberate evasion on its facts and is doubted as a veil-piercing authority. It is no substitute for clear drafting."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.032], [06.077]", takeaway:"Draft non-competes to cover controlled/related entities." },

{ id:"cl6-059", lesson:"6K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Complete shareholding control over a company is enough to show it holds its property on trust for the controller.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.078] says a company may hold property on trust for its controller, just as it may act as his agent. But the general rule is that a company's property is its own, and a shareholder has no direct or beneficial interest in it 'even if it has complete shareholding control' (Macaura; Beckkett). So 'the mere fact of control does not give rise to a trust relationship', and the usual elements of intention and certainty must be shown. Trust claims are often made to enlarge the pool of assets available to the controller's creditors, effectively circumventing the veil. In Prest, the Supreme Court refused to pierce the veils of the husband's companies, but the law lords unanimously held the properties were held on resulting trust for him, so the wife's claim succeeded."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This confuses control with beneficial ownership. A trust needs its own elements, such as intention or a resulting-trust basis like the controller having provided the purchase money."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.078], fns 167–168", takeaway:"Trust needs intention + certainty, not just control." },

{ id:"c6x-064", lesson:"6K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bank is asked to lend $2m to Elm Pte Ltd, a newly formed company with $100 paid-up capital. Its owner, Ms Ma, is wealthy. The bank wants recourse to Ms Ma if Elm defaults.",
  prompt:"What is the most reliable way to achieve this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Take a personal guarantee from Ms Ma", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.077] states that 'parties are free to contract around the separate entity rule. A lender who does not wish to accept the default risk of a lowly capitalised company may insist that the loan be secured by a guarantee of the company's shareholders.' Such arrangements are routine, and 'when shareholders accede to such requests, they lose the benefit of limited liability'. A guarantee gives the bank a direct contractual claim against Ms Ma, without relying on any veil-piercing argument. Without it, the bank as a voluntary creditor of a thin company would generally be taken to have accepted the default risk ([06.048]). Taking security over Elm's assets or Ms Ma's own assets would be a further option."},
    {t:"Rely on piercing Elm's veil if it defaults, since it is thinly capitalised", correct:false,
     why:"Thin capitalisation is not a ground for piercing, and a voluntary lender is generally taken to have accepted the risk."},
    {t:"Lend to Elm and treat Ms Ma as its alter ego", correct:false,
     why:"Alter ego is a weak and uncertain ground, and her ownership alone would not satisfy it."},
    {t:"Ask Elm to promise that Ms Ma will pay", correct:false,
     why:"Elm cannot bind Ms Ma personally by its own promise. The guarantee must come from Ms Ma herself."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.048], [06.077]", takeaway:"Want the owner on the hook? Take a personal guarantee." }

]);

;
