{ id:"cl6-060", lesson:"6L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Should the veil-piercing jurisdiction be abolished, according to the chapter?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: keep it, but give it real content", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.079] acknowledges that the confusion in this area 'may suggest that there is real merit in abolishing the jurisdiction', but notes the 'genuine concern that doing so would deprive the courts of an important means of remedying injustice occasioned by the illegitimate use of corporate structures'. That is why the Supreme Court in Prest affirmed the jurisdiction, though without a majority on its scope. Lee's position ([06.080]) is that if preserved, it 'should be accorded real content'. Some uncertainty is inevitable with a discretion aimed at abuse, because abuse takes myriad forms, so 'any attempt to reduce the jurisdiction to a single rule or principle will likely be impractical'. What is needed is 'a willingness to evaluate the rights, interests or policies at stake, and whether they should be permitted to override those that underpin the separate entity rule'. Clarity will emerge as courts do this."},
    {t:"Yes: the confusion is too great to justify keeping it", correct:false,
     why:"Lee recognises the argument for abolition but concludes the jurisdiction should be kept to counter illegitimate uses of corporate structures."},
    {t:"It should be reduced to Lord Sumption's evasion principle alone", correct:false,
     why:"Lee notes that even this limited jurisdiction 'may be difficult to defend' ([06.036]) and that a single-rule approach is likely impractical."},
    {t:"It should be expanded to treat all group companies as one", correct:false,
     why:"Single economic entity piercing is rejected in Singapore (Goh Chan Peng), and nothing in Lee's framework supports expanding it."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.079]–[06.080], fns 169–170", takeaway:"Keep the jurisdiction; give it content." },

{ id:"c6x-069", lesson:"6L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Lee argues that the veil-piercing jurisdiction should be reduced to a single clear rule so that it becomes predictable.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.080] takes the opposite view: 'some measure of uncertainty is inevitable when a discretionary approach is adopted', and a jurisdiction aimed at countering abuse 'must necessarily provide sufficient latitude for appropriately responding to the myriad forms in which \"abuse\" appears'. So 'any attempt to reduce the jurisdiction to a single rule or principle will likely be impractical'. Instead, she identifies a core set of constraining principles ([06.081]) that already appear in the authorities and that, if followed, keep the discretion within tight limits. Lord Sumption's single evasion principle in Prest is the leading single-rule attempt; Lee notes it was well received by some commentators (fn 169) but is itself vulnerable ([06.036])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Predictability is attractive, but Lee thinks a single rule cannot capture the variety of abuse. She favours constrained discretion."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.080]–[06.081], fn 169", takeaway:"No single rule; a constrained discretion." },

{ id:"cl6-061", lesson:"6L", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each core principle constraining veil piercing to its content.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Control", r:"However extensive, not by itself enough"},
    {l:"Wrongdoing", r:"Must be substantively linked to use of the company structure"},
    {l:"Last resort", r:"No piercing if another general principle gives an effective remedy"},
    {l:"Scope of remedy", r:"Only what's needed; company not negated for all purposes"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [06.081] distils six principles from the authorities. (a) A shareholder's control, however extensive, is not by itself enough (Salomon), and the veil cannot be pierced merely because justice requires it (Adams v Cape; Lim Chee Twang). (b) Piercing is possible only where the controller has used control over the company to conceal wrongdoing (Ord v Belhaven; VTB Capital). (c) It is not enough that the company was involved in wrongdoing; there must be a substantive link between the wrongdoing and the use of the company structure (Ben Hashem). (d) Piercing is a 'last-resort' remedy, not used where other general principles give an effective remedy; this comes from Prest, though it has been criticised (fn 175). (e) The remedy goes only as far as necessary to redress the controllers' wrong; the company is not negated for all purposes. (f) Piercing should be kept distinct from attribution where possible, which 'will go a long way in uncluttering the law'.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.081], fns 171–177", takeaway:"Not control · linked wrong · last resort · tailored remedy." },

{ id:"c6x-067", lesson:"6L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which of these is NOT one of the core principles Lee identifies as constraining the veil-piercing discretion?",
  options:[
    {t:"The veil may be pierced whenever a group of companies operates as a single economic unit", correct:true,
     why:"This is the one proposition Lee's framework excludes. Single economic entity reasoning has been rejected in England (Adams v Cape) and Singapore (Lew Syn Pau; Goh Chan Peng), and it is inconsistent with principle (a), that control alone is not enough. The other three options are among Lee's six principles in [06.081]: (b) the controller must have used control to conceal wrongdoing; (c) there must be a substantive link between the wrongdoing and the use of the company structure; and (d) piercing is a last resort, unavailable where other general principles give an effective remedy. The remaining principles are (a) control and 'interests of justice' are insufficient, (e) the remedy is tailored to redress the wrong, and (f) piercing is distinct from attribution."},
    {t:"The controller must have used control over the company to conceal wrongdoing", correct:false,
     why:"This is principle (b), drawn from Ord v Belhaven and VTB Capital."},
    {t:"There must be a substantive link between the wrongdoing and the use of the company structure", correct:false,
     why:"This is principle (c), from Ben Hashem: mere involvement of the company in wrongdoing is not enough."},
    {t:"The veil should not be pierced where an effective remedy is available under other general principles", correct:false,
     why:"This is principle (d), the last-resort principle from Prest."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.066], [06.081]", takeaway:"Single economic unit is not a ground; the six principles constrain." },

{ id:"c6x-065", lesson:"6L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Juniper Pte Ltd, controlled by Mr Khoo, negligently delivers defective goods. Khoo had nothing to do with the defect, and the company structure played no role in causing or hiding it. A buyer asks the court to pierce the veil because 'the company was involved in wrongdoing'.",
  prompt:"Which principle defeats the request most directly?",
  options:[
    {t:"The need for a substantive link to the company structure", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.081](c) states: 'it is not sufficient to show merely that the company was involved in some wrongdoing. There must also be a substantive link between the wrongdoing and the use of the company structure' (Ben Hashem, fn 174). Juniper's negligence is the company's own wrong, for which it is liable. The company structure was not used to commit or conceal anything, and Khoo was not involved. Principle (b) also fails, because Khoo did not use his control to conceal wrongdoing. The buyer's remedy is against Juniper, and against Khoo only if he personally committed or procured a tort ([06.074])."},
    {t:"The last-resort principle, because the buyer could sue Juniper's insurer", correct:false,
     why:"The last-resort principle matters where another remedy exists against the controller. Here the more direct problem is that there is no wrongdoing linked to the company structure at all."},
    {t:"The distinction between attribution and veil lifting", correct:false,
     why:"That distinction is relevant in other cases, but the decisive point here is the absence of a substantive link."},
    {t:"None: involvement in wrongdoing is enough to pierce", correct:false,
     why:"That is the proposition principle (c) rejects."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.081], fn 174", takeaway:"Company did wrong ≠ structure misused: no substantive link, no piercing." },

{ id:"c6x-066", lesson:"6L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Mr Bala, controller of Teak Pte Ltd, induced a customer to pay a large deposit to Teak by personally making fraudulent statements, then caused Teak to transfer the deposit to him. The customer asks the court to pierce Teak's veil and make Bala liable on Teak's contract.",
  prompt:"How does Lee's framework suggest the court should respond?",
  options:[
    {t:"Prefer deceit and recovery claims against Bala; piercing is last resort", correct:true,
     why:"Two parts of Lee's framework point the same way. Principle (d) treats veil piercing as a 'last-resort' remedy: 'the corporate veil should not be pierced when an effective remedy may be achieved through the application of other general legal principles' ([06.081], from Prest). Bala's fraudulent statements give the customer a direct claim in deceit, and his diversion of the deposit may support equitable claims. Second, [06.082] notes that in England the veil cannot be pierced to impose personal liability on a controller for a contract he did not enter (VTB Capital). Children's Media suggests Singapore courts may not be so constrained, but Lee's better view is that piercing should not undermine the parties' prior consensual allocation of risk, which would 'substantially erode commercial certainty'. The customer can be fully protected through Bala's own wrongs."},
    {t:"Pierce, because fraud justifies treating Bala as a party to Teak's contract", correct:false,
     why:"This is the Children's Media approach, which Lee criticises. VTB rejects it, and effective general remedies are available."},
    {t:"Refuse all relief, because Teak is a separate person", correct:false,
     why:"Separate personality does not protect Bala from liability for his own fraud. The customer has strong direct claims."},
    {t:"Pierce, but only if Teak is insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"Insolvency is not the test. The last-resort and privity concerns remain even if Teak cannot pay."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.047]–[06.048], [06.081]–[06.082], fns 175, 178", takeaway:"Fraudster controller: sue him in deceit/equity; piercing is last resort." },

{ id:"cl6-062", lesson:"6L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A supplier sold on credit to a lowly capitalised company that later defaulted. There's no fraud or concealment; the sole shareholder simply ran the company informally and exercised total control. The supplier asks the court to pierce the veil.",
  prompt:"Applying the chapter's framework, what is the likely outcome?",
  options:[
    {t:"No piercing: no linked wrongdoing, and the risk was accepted", correct:true,
     why:"Run through Lee's principles ([06.081]). (a) Total control is not by itself enough, and an appeal to justice is not a ground. (b) There is no wrongdoing concealed through the company. (c) Without wrongdoing, there is nothing to link to the company structure. Informality on its own, Lee argues, does not justify piercing either ([06.053]). And [06.048] adds the consensual point: a party who voluntarily dealt with a thinly capitalised company 'must, in general, be taken to have accepted the risk of loss' when it defaults, as Singapore courts have affirmed where no impropriety was involved (Sitt Tatt v Goh Tai Hock). The supplier's protection had to be contractual: security, a guarantee or tighter credit terms ([06.077])."},
    {t:"Pierce, because total control shows the company was an alter ego", correct:false,
     why:"Control and informality alone are insufficient under principle (a) and Lee's critique of alter ego."},
    {t:"Pierce, because justice requires it", correct:false,
     why:"'Interests of justice' is not a ground for piercing (Adams v Cape; Lim Chee Twang)."},
    {t:"Pierce, but only for half the debt", correct:false,
     why:"There is no basis to pierce at all, and no doctrine of partial piercing to split losses."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.048], [06.053], [06.081]", takeaway:"No linked wrongdoing → no piercing." },

{ id:"cl6-063", lesson:"6L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does England's position on piercing to impose contractual liability compare with Singapore's?",
  options:[
    {t:"England bars it (VTB); the better view is Singapore should too", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [06.082] notes that in England the discretion is further restricted by the principle that the veil cannot be pierced 'to impose upon a controller personal liability for a contract he did not enter into' (VTB Capital). In Singapore, Children's Media suggests courts are not so constrained, though the point was not argued there ([06.048]). Lee's better view is that the veil 'should not be pierced to undermine the parties' prior consensual allocation of risks as that would substantially erode commercial certainty'. She concludes ([06.083]) that some principles still need judicial clarification, such as the threshold for showing that no other effective remedy is available at general law, and that 'these hurdles are best crossed as they arise'."},
    {t:"Both jurisdictions freely allow it", correct:false,
     why:"England expressly bars it after VTB Capital."},
    {t:"Singapore bars it by statute", correct:false,
     why:"No statute addresses it. Children's Media points towards allowing it, which Lee criticises."},
    {t:"England allows it only where fraud is proved", correct:false,
     why:"VTB held that fraud does not change the answer, because fraud has its own remedies."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 6 [06.082]–[06.083], fns 178–179", takeaway:"VTB bars it; Singapore should too (better view)." }

