{ id:"cl7-047", lesson:"7I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A promoter signs a contract 'on behalf of' a company that hasn't yet been incorporated. Later the company is incorporated.",
  prompt:"What is the position under s 41 of the Companies Act 1967?",
  options:[
    {t:"It may ratify; until then the promoter is bound", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.051] explains that a company has no capacity to contract before it exists, so at common law it is not bound by pre-incorporation contracts and cannot ratify them afterwards. Section 41(1) reverses this: the company may ratify after incorporation, and a ratified contract 'takes effect from the date that it was purportedly entered into'. At common law, the person who purported to contract for a non-existent company might be personally liable, but only if he held himself out as agent or principal, not where he purported to act as the company itself (the Kelner v Baxter / Newborne v Sensolid distinction from Ch 4). Section 41(2) makes that difficult distinction immaterial: before ratification, the person who purports to act in the name or on behalf of the company 'shall', subject to any agreement to the contrary, be personally bound by and entitled to sue on the contract."},
    {t:"The contract is void and can't be ratified", correct:false,
     why:"That was the common law position, which s 41(1) reversed."},
    {t:"The company is automatically bound once incorporated", correct:false,
     why:"The company is bound only if it ratifies. Incorporation alone does not bind it."},
    {t:"The promoter is never personally liable", correct:false,
     why:"Under s 41(2), the promoter is personally bound until ratification, unless the parties agree otherwise."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.051], fns 136–141", takeaway:"s 41: ratify (backdated); till then signatory bound/entitled." },

{ id:"c7x-023", lesson:"7I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before Cove Pte Ltd is incorporated, its founder signs a supply agreement 'for Cove Pte Ltd'. The supplier's terms expressly state that the founder will have no personal liability under the agreement in any circumstances. Cove is incorporated but never ratifies the agreement, and the supplier sues the founder.",
  prompt:"Is the founder personally bound under s 41(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably not, given the contrary agreement", correct:true,
     why:"Section 41(2) is a default rule ([07.051]). It provides that, before ratification, the person who purports to act in the name or on behalf of the company 'shall, subject to any agreement to the contrary, be personally bound by or entitled to sue on the contract'. The supplier's own terms say the founder has no personal liability in any circumstances. That is an agreement to the contrary, so the statutory default does not apply against him. The supplier took the risk that Cove might never ratify. Without such a term, the result would be the opposite, as in the usual case where a founder signs a lease before incorporation and the company never ratifies (Ch 4, [04.060])."},
    {t:"Yes: s 41(2) always makes the signatory personally liable until ratification", correct:false,
     why:"The provision is expressly subject to any agreement to the contrary."},
    {t:"Yes, because the agreement was signed 'for' the company rather than as the company", correct:false,
     why:"Section 41(2) makes that common law distinction immaterial, and in any case the contrary agreement governs."},
    {t:"No, because Cove became bound automatically on incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"Cove is bound only if it ratifies. The founder escapes liability because of the contrary agreement, not because Cove is bound."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.051], fn 141", takeaway:"s 41(2) default yields to a contrary agreement." },

{ id:"c7x-024", lesson:"7I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What two things must be present for a duly incorporated company to create a binding contract?",
  options:[
    {t:"Capacity and the agent's authority", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.052] identifies the two issues: a company 'may create a binding contract if it has the legal capacity to do so and the agent executing the contract has the authority to act on its behalf'. The capacity issue 'is now of little practical significance following legislative reform aimed at protecting third parties', but Lee explains it for completeness and because it remains relevant to internal enforcement under s 25(2). In practice, the live issue is authority, which [07.061] calls 'the agency issue': 'it is extremely rare for a transaction to be defeated on the ground of the company's incapacity. More commonly, a contract is challenged on the ground that the agent executing it on the company's behalf has no authority to do so.' Lessons 7J–7L cover authority."},
    {t:"Board approval and shareholder approval", correct:false,
     why:"Neither is always required. A properly authorised agent can bind the company without either."},
    {t:"Registration of the contract with ACRA and a company seal", correct:false,
     why:"Ordinary contracts need not be registered or sealed."},
    {t:"A stated object covering the contract and a resolution of the board", correct:false,
     why:"Objects are optional, and a stated object is no longer needed for capacity (s 23(1))."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.052], [07.061]", takeaway:"Capacity + authority; authority is the live issue." },

{ id:"cl7-048", lesson:"7I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What was the ultra vires doctrine at common law, and what was its practical effect?",
  options:[
    {t:"Ultra vires acts were void, which bred sprawling objects", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.053] explains that at common law a company's capacity was limited by the objects stated in its constitution, historically the memorandum, on the idea that companies are formed for specific purposes. A contract for purposes outside the permitted objects was ultra vires, void from inception, unenforceable against the company and unratifiable by the general meeting. In practice, the doctrine caused 'substantial uncertainty and injustice' when contracting parties used it to escape unfavourable contracts. Companies responded with lengthy, all-encompassing objects clauses, often ending with a power for the company or directors to extend or define the objects as they saw fit. Ironically, the shareholders and creditors the doctrine was meant to protect 'were most involved in undermining the doctrine' to secure their contracts. Another tactic ([07.054]) was to list extensive powers and treat them as objects."},
    {t:"A rule that directors could not exceed their authority", correct:false,
     why:"That is a question of authority, which is distinct from the company's capacity. The ultra vires doctrine concerned the company's capacity."},
    {t:"A rule that only listed companies needed objects clauses", correct:false,
     why:"At common law, every company's capacity was defined by its objects."},
    {t:"A rule that ultra vires contracts were valid but voidable", correct:false,
     why:"Ultra vires contracts were void from inception, not voidable."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.053], fns 142–143", takeaway:"Common law ultra vires: void; led to sprawling objects." },

{ id:"cl7-049", lesson:"7I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company has power to give guarantees. Its directors cause it to give a guarantee for a purpose outside its stated objects. The beneficiary has no notice of this.",
  prompt:"Under Rolled Steel Products v British Steel, is the guarantee ultra vires as against the beneficiary?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: within power and capable of serving its objects", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.054] explains that powers and objects are conceptually distinct: a power, such as to borrow or give guarantees, is an act a company may do to further its objects, not an object in itself. Strict logic might suggest that a transaction within a power but entered into for a purpose outside the objects is ultra vires and void. But that result would be too hazardous for third parties. In Rolled Steel Products, the English Court of Appeal held that a contract within the company's express or implied powers is not, as against third parties, ultra vires so long as it is on its face capable of being performed in pursuit of one of its permitted objects. If the directors authorised it for an improper purpose, they breached their duty by exceeding their authority or abusing their powers, but that does not by itself make the transaction ultra vires the company. A good faith third party may enforce it unless put on notice of the lack of authority."},
    {t:"Yes, because it was given for a purpose outside the objects", correct:false,
     why:"That is the 'strict logic' Rolled Steel rejected as too hazardous for third parties."},
    {t:"Yes, because powers are the same as objects", correct:false,
     why:"Lee stresses that powers and objects are conceptually distinct."},
    {t:"No, because ultra vires was abolished before Rolled Steel", correct:false,
     why:"Rolled Steel was decided under the common law doctrine; it restricted the doctrine, not abolished it."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.054], fns 144–145", takeaway:"Rolled Steel: capacity vs authority; good faith third party protected." },

{ id:"cl7-050", lesson:"7I", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each Companies Act provision to its effect.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"s 23(1)", r:"Full capacity to undertake any business or transaction"},
    {l:"s 25(1)", r:"No act invalid by reason only of the company's incapacity"},
    {l:"s 25A", r:"Abolishes constructive notice of the constitution"},
    {l:"s 25B(1)", r:"For good faith dealers, directors' power is free of constitutional limits"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [07.055]–[07.060] describes four reforms. First ([07.056]), s 23(1) gives every company full capacity to carry on any business, do any act or enter any transaction, with full rights, powers and privileges, so a company need not state objects, though it may still do so or restrict its capacity; where the constitution is silent, ultra vires is irrelevant. Second ([07.057]), even if a company adopts objects, s 25(1) provides that no act, including an agreement and acts by an officer or agent under any purported authority, and no conveyance or transfer, is invalid by reason only of the company's lack of capacity or power. It appears also intended to stop an agent's lack of authority being argued solely from the company's incapacity. Third ([07.059]), s 25A abolishes the rule that third parties have constructive notice of the constitution because it is registered and publicly available. Fourth ([07.060]), s 25B(1) deems the directors' power to bind the company, or authorise others, to be free of any constitutional limitation in favour of a person dealing in good faith.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.055]–[07.060], fns 146–156", takeaway:"Capacity · no invalidity · no constructive notice · free of limits." },

{ id:"cl7-051", lesson:"7I", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Section 25 of the Companies Act abolishes the ultra vires doctrine entirely.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.058] stresses that s 25 'does not purport to abolish the ultra vires doctrine in entirety'. Section 25(2) expressly preserves it for 'internal enforcement': proceedings brought by members or debenture holders against the company, or by the company against its members or officers. Section 25(3) contemplates that, as a result of proceedings under s 25(2)(a), performance of a transaction may be set aside or restrained, and the court may then, where just and equitable, award compensation to the company or the other party for loss or damage. 'To this limited extent, third-party interests may still be affected' despite s 25(1). Taken together with ss 23(1), 25A and 25B, though, the reforms have so 'emaciat[ed]' the doctrine that the risk to an honest third party is now negligible ([07.061])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25(2) and (3) preserve the doctrine for internal enforcement, which can still affect third parties in limited ways."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.058], [07.061], fn 152", takeaway:"Ultra vires survives for internal enforcement (s 25(2))." },

{ id:"c7x-025", lesson:"7I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Reef Pte Ltd's constitution limits its objects to running restaurants. Its directors sign a contract to buy a shipping vessel. Before completion, a member of Reef learns of the contract and wants to stop it. The seller is unaware of the restriction.",
  prompt:"What can the member do, and what protection does the seller have?",
  options:[
    {t:"Restrain it under s 25(2); compensation is possible", correct:true,
     why:"Section 25(1) means the contract is not invalid 'by reason only' of Reef's lack of capacity, so the seller is generally protected ([07.057]). But s 25(2) preserves the ultra vires doctrine for internal enforcement, including proceedings by members against the company ([07.058]). A member who acts before completion can ask the court to restrain performance. If the court does so under s 25(2)(a), s 25(3) lets it, where just and equitable, award compensation to the company or the other party for loss caused. That is the limited way third-party interests can still be affected. The seller also benefits from s 25A, so it is not deemed to know of the restriction merely because the constitution is registered, and, if it dealt in good faith, from s 25B(1)."},
    {t:"The member can do nothing, because s 25 abolished ultra vires", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25(2) preserves the doctrine for internal enforcement by members."},
    {t:"The contract is void, and the seller has no remedy", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25(1) prevents invalidity by reason only of incapacity, and s 25(3) allows compensation if performance is restrained."},
    {t:"The seller is deemed to know the restriction because the constitution is public", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25A abolished constructive notice of the constitution."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.057]–[07.059]", takeaway:"Member can restrain ultra vires act (s 25(2)); court may compensate (s 25(3))." },

{ id:"c7x-026", lesson:"7I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Sage Ltd's constitution says the directors may not borrow more than $5m without shareholder approval. The board, without approval, borrows $8m from a bank that has never read the constitution and knows nothing of the limit. Sage later argues the loan is not binding.",
  prompt:"Which provisions most directly protect the bank?",
  options:[
    {t:"Sections 25A and 25B(1)", correct:true,
     why:"The problem here is not Sage's capacity, since borrowing is within its full capacity under s 23(1), but a constitutional limit on the directors' power. At common law, a third party was deemed to know the contents of the registered constitution, so the bank might have been fixed with notice of the $5m limit. Section 25A abolishes that constructive notice ([07.059]), so the bank is not put on notice merely because the limit is in a public document. Section 25B(1) then provides that 'in favour of a person dealing with a company in good faith, the power of the directors to bind the company, or authorise others to do so, is deemed to be free of any limitation under the company's constitution' ([07.060]). The bank dealt in good faith without knowledge, so Sage is bound. The directors may be liable to Sage for exceeding their powers, but that is an internal matter."},
    {t:"Section 41, because the loan was a pre-incorporation contract", correct:false,
     why:"Sage was already incorporated. Section 41 is irrelevant."},
    {t:"None: the bank should have read the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25A specifically removes the deemed notice that would have followed from failing to read it."},
    {t:"Only s 23(1), because borrowing is within Sage's capacity", correct:false,
     why:"Capacity is not the issue here; the directors' constitutional authority is. Sections 25A and 25B address that."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.059]–[07.060], fns 153–156", takeaway:"Constitutional limit on directors: s 25A + s 25B(1) protect good faith dealers." }

