/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 7 · Lesson 7K Ostensible Authority: Representation (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 7 [07.070]–[07.085], fns 174–213. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"c7x-032", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In which situation does ostensible authority typically matter?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where actual authority is missing but appears present", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.070] describes the typical setting. An agent may purport to contract for the principal without actual authority, yet that lack may not be known or apparent to third parties. That may be because the agent was previously authorised but the authority has 'since expired, or been curtailed or terminated by the principal', or because the agent occupies a position that would normally carry implied authority for such transactions, but that authority 'has in fact been restricted or withdrawn by the principal without notification to third parties'. In such cases the agent clearly has no actual authority, but the principal may still be bound if he acted within his apparent or ostensible authority. Ostensible authority protects third parties who reasonably rely on the principal's own representations."},
    {t:"Only where the agent has actual authority", correct:false,
     why:"If the agent has actual authority, the principal is bound on that basis and ostensible authority is unnecessary."},
    {t:"Only where the third party knows the agent lacks authority", correct:false,
     why:"A third party who knows of the lack of authority cannot rely on ostensible authority ([07.071](d))."},
    {t:"Only for pre-incorporation contracts", correct:false,
     why:"Pre-incorporation contracts are governed by s 41 ([07.051]). Ostensible authority applies to an existing principal's agents."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.070]", takeaway:"Ostensible authority fills the gap when actual authority is missing but looks present." },

{ id:"cl7-057", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which are the elements of ostensible authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"Representation, authorised source, reliance, no knowledge", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.071] explains that ostensible authority is not founded on the principal–agent agreement but is 'authority as it appears to those who deal with the company', often explained as estoppel by representation. An agent has it when: (a) the principal has represented, or permitted it to be represented, that the agent has authority to enter into the kind of transaction the third party seeks to enforce; (b) the representation is made by a person authorised to make it; (c) the representation induced the third party to contract; and (d) the third party does not know of the agent's lack of authority. When these are satisfied ([07.072]), the principal cannot deny the agent's authority, even though actual authority is missing. Lessons 7K and 7L take each element in turn."},
    {t:"A written contract between principal and agent and a board resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Those describe actual authority. Ostensible authority depends on representation and reliance."},
    {t:"The agent's own statement of his authority and the third party's belief in it", correct:false,
     why:"An agent's own representation is not enough; the representation must come from someone authorised to make it ([07.077])."},
    {t:"Registration of the agent's authority with ACRA", correct:false,
     why:"No registration is required."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.071]–[07.072], fn 174", takeaway:"Representation · by authorised person · reliance · no knowledge." },

{ id:"cl7-058", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"K, a director, was never formally appointed managing director, but the board knew he acted as one and engaged agents. K appoints architects to apply for planning permission. The company refuses to pay, saying K had no authority.",
  prompt:"What did the court hold in Freeman & Lockyer v Buckhurst Park Properties?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company was bound by K's ostensible authority", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.072] describes Freeman & Lockyer as the leading authority. The English Court of Appeal accepted there was insufficient evidence that the board had given K actual authority, express or implied, to employ agents. But the company was bound because the appointment fell within K's ostensible authority. Although K was never formally appointed managing director, the board was at all material times aware that he acted as one and had taken steps to employ agents. By permitting this, the board represented to third parties that K had the kind of authority a managing director would normally have. The plaintiffs were induced by that representation to contract, so the company was precluded from denying K's authority. The representation here was inferred from a course of dealing ([07.074])."},
    {t:"The company wasn't bound because K was never formally appointed", correct:false,
     why:"Formal appointment was not needed; the board's knowing acquiescence was a representation."},
    {t:"K was personally liable for the fees instead", correct:false,
     why:"The company was bound by K's ostensible authority."},
    {t:"The company was bound because K had express actual authority", correct:false,
     why:"The court found insufficient evidence of actual authority. The basis was ostensible authority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.072], fns 175–176", takeaway:"Freeman & Lockyer: acting MD with board's knowledge → MD's usual authority." },

{ id:"cl7-059", lesson:"7K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A forged document can never amount to a representation binding the company.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.073] notes that representations may be express, oral or written, or inferred from conduct, and that for substantial transactions it is usual and prudent to ask for evidence of board authorisation, such as certified copies or extracts of board resolutions or minutes. 'It used to be thought that a forged document could never constitute a representation that binds the company to the fraudulent agent's acts' (Ruben v Great Fingall Consolidated, fn 177). 'But it is now clear that this is not so. Forgeries, like other fraudulent acts of an agent, may in appropriate circumstances bind the principal so long as there is evidence that such acts fall within the agent's apparent authority' (fn 178). The key is still that the representation of authority can be traced to the principal; a forged document produced by an agent with no apparent authority to vouch for it will not bind the company, as Skandinaviska shows ([07.083])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"That was the older view (Ruben v Great Fingall). It is now clear forgeries may bind the principal if within the agent's apparent authority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.073], fns 177–178", takeaway:"Forgery can bind, if within apparent authority." },

{ id:"cl7-060", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When can a company's silence or inaction be a representation of authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only with knowledge and a duty to correct", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.074] says a representation may be inferred from a course of dealing, as in Freeman & Lockyer, or from specific acts. But 'silence or inaction would not usually constitute a representation unless the principal knew that a third party had misapprehended the facts but failed to correct the misapprehension'. In other words, the representation is inferred where the principal was under 'a legal or equitable duty to make the disclosure, communication or correction' (fn 180). This is a narrow route. It protects a third party where the company, knowing of the third party's mistaken belief in the agent's authority, stands by and lets it continue. Mere failure to supervise an agent, without knowledge of the third party's misapprehension, is not enough."},
    {t:"Always: silence is consent", correct:false,
     why:"Silence generally is not a representation. It becomes one only in the narrow circumstances described."},
    {t:"Never", correct:false,
     why:"Silence can be a representation where the principal knew of the misapprehension and had a duty to correct it."},
    {t:"Only if the board formally resolved to stay silent", correct:false,
     why:"No resolution is needed; the question is knowledge plus duty to correct."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.074], fns 179–180", takeaway:"Silence = representation only with knowledge + duty to correct." },

{ id:"cl7-061", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Implied actual authority and ostensible authority often coincide. Why does the distinction still matter?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is a third-party shield, and the agent can be sued", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.074] explains that appointing someone to a position is generally a representation that he has the authority usual for that position, so ostensible authority often coincides with implied actual authority; the same facts that suggest ostensible authority would usually confer implied actual authority unless restricted. But the two are 'conceptually distinct, and important consequences follow'. First, because ostensible authority rests on third-party perception and reliance, 'it may only be invoked by third parties against a company'. Second, a company bound by its agent's ostensible authority may claim against the agent for breach of authority, but has no such recourse where the agent acted with actual authority. The overlap also causes terminological confusion: 'usual authority' may mean both actual and ostensible authority where they co-exist, or ostensible authority only where actual authority has been restricted."},
    {t:"Ostensible authority binds the company more strongly", correct:false,
     why:"Both bind the company. The differences concern who can invoke them and the company's recourse against the agent."},
    {t:"Actual authority requires registration", correct:false,
     why:"Neither form of authority requires registration."},
    {t:"Only actual authority can be implied", correct:false,
     why:"Ostensible authority is often inferred from conduct and position too."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.074]", takeaway:"Ostensible: third-party shield; company can sue agent for breach." },

{ id:"c7x-033", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Larch Ltd appoints Ms Ho as its sales director, a position that usually carries authority to sign sales contracts up to $1m. Internally, the board restricts her to $200,000 but tells no one outside. She signs a $700,000 sales contract with a customer who knows nothing of the restriction. Larch is held bound.",
  prompt:"What follows for Larch and Ms Ho?",
  options:[
    {t:"Larch is bound and may claim against Ms Ho", correct:true,
     why:"This is the situation Lee [07.070] and [07.074] describe. Ms Ho's position carries a 'usual authority' of up to $1m, and appointing her is a representation of that authority. Larch restricted her actual authority to $200,000 without telling third parties. So the $700,000 contract is outside her actual authority but within her ostensible authority, and Larch is bound to the customer. Here 'usual authority' denotes ostensible authority only, because her actual authority was restricted. The distinction matters for Larch's recourse: 'a company that is bound to a third party by reason of its agent's ostensible authority may claim against the agent for breach of authority, but would obviously have no such recourse where the agent has acted with actual authority'. Larch may therefore pursue Ms Ho for exceeding the $200,000 limit."},
    {t:"Larch is not bound, because the board restricted her authority", correct:false,
     why:"An unpublicised internal restriction does not prevent ostensible authority binding the company to an unaware third party."},
    {t:"Larch is bound and has no claim against Ms Ho", correct:false,
     why:"Larch's recourse against Ms Ho exists precisely because it is bound only by ostensible, not actual, authority."},
    {t:"Ms Ho is personally bound to the customer instead of Larch", correct:false,
     why:"Larch, as principal, is bound by her ostensible authority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.070], [07.074]", takeaway:"Restricted but usual authority: company bound; can sue agent." },

{ id:"cl7-062", lesson:"7K", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each fact pattern to whether it was, or would be, a representation of authority.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Foreman given overall control of a site hires workers", r:"Yes: Fongsoon Enterprises"},
    {l:"Employee given keys and access card signs a lease", r:"No: Cavenagh Investment"},
    {l:"Engineer with company badge and purchase forms", r:"No: too commonly given to junior staff"},
    {l:"Genuine name card showing a senior appointment", r:"May suffice"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [07.075] says that for middle- and lower-ranking employees, a representation more commonly arises from the company's course of dealing than from appointment alone, and is determined by the totality of its conduct. The enquiry is factual, so a finding on one set of facts has limited precedential value. In Next of kin of Ramu Vanniyar Ravichandran v Fongsoon Enterprises, a foreman given overall control of day-to-day operations at a site had been held out as having ostensible authority to hire workers. But in Cavenagh Investment v Kaushik Rajiv, an employee entrusted with keys and an access card was not held out as authorised to lease the property, because such access is normally given to agents for viewings and ministerial acts. Where the act falls outside the usual authority for the position, unequivocal conduct is needed: an engineer is not clothed with authority to make purchases merely by wearing the company's badge, using its vehicle or having access to purchase order forms and rubber stamps, and letterhead alone is not a representation. However, 'a prima facie genuine name card indicating a sufficiently senior appointment may suffice' (fn 188).",
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.075], fns 181–188", takeaway:"Context decides; trappings ≠ authority; senior name card may." },

{ id:"c7x-034", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A junior procurement assistant at Fern Engineering Pte Ltd uses company letterhead, a company rubber stamp and blank purchase orders, all routinely available to clerical staff, to order $400,000 of equipment from a new supplier. Fern has never dealt with this supplier and never held the assistant out as a buyer.",
  prompt:"Is Fern likely bound by ostensible authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unlikely: stationery and stamps aren't a holding out", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.075] stresses that 'where the unauthorised act falls outside the usual authority of persons occupying the position of the agent, unequivocal conduct will be required to establish the agent's ostensible authority'. A junior procurement assistant does not ordinarily have authority to place $400,000 orders. Lee's examples are close: an engineer is not clothed with authority to make purchases 'merely because he is wearing the company's badge, or is permitted to use the company's vehicle, or has access to the company's purchase order forms and rubber stamps since similar access is commonly granted to employees of a clerical or junior position'. Letterhead 'is not by itself a representation as to the agent's authority', and possession of a document that could be used for fraud does not suffice. Fern had no prior dealing with the supplier and never held the assistant out, so there is no course of dealing either."},
    {t:"Yes: using company stationery and stamps is a representation by Fern", correct:false,
     why:"Such trappings are commonly given to junior staff and are not unequivocal conduct showing authority."},
    {t:"Yes, because the supplier acted in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith is necessary but not sufficient. There must be a representation by the company."},
    {t:"No, because only directors can have ostensible authority", correct:false,
     why:"Employees at any level can have ostensible authority if the company's conduct represents it, as with the foreman in Fongsoon."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.075], fns 185–187", takeaway:"Junior staff + stationery/stamps ≠ ostensible authority." },

{ id:"cl7-063", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An employee is issued an email address on the company's domain. He uses it to contract with third parties, claiming authority he doesn't have.",
  prompt:"Is the company bound by ostensible authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not by the address alone; tolerated misuse might suffice", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.076] considers how ostensible authority works for online commerce. In principle, 'the mere issue of an e-mail address with the company's domain name ought to be no different from the issue of a business card or access to company stationery'. It indicates the employee's association with the company and helps him do his job, but it 'does not provide sufficient information on the nature of the association so as to create the appearance of authority to contract'. A different inference may be drawn 'if the company were aware, or could reasonably have discovered, the use of the e-mail address to misrepresent the employee's authority but took no step to correct that misrepresentation'. Then 'the entire course of dealing (but not the mere grant of an e-mail address)' may clothe the agent with authority. This links to the narrow rule on silence as representation ([07.074])."},
    {t:"Yes, always: the domain name is a representation", correct:false,
     why:"An email address alone says nothing about the scope of the holder's authority."},
    {t:"No, never: electronic communications can't create authority", correct:false,
     why:"Electronic dealings can form part of a course of dealing that creates ostensible authority."},
    {t:"Only if the email included a digital signature", correct:false,
     why:"A signature does not answer the question whether the company represented his authority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.076], fn 189", takeaway:"Email address alone ≠ authority; tolerated misuse may." },

{ id:"c7x-035", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Who must make the representation of authority for ostensible authority to bind a company, according to Diplock LJ in Freeman & Lockyer?",
  options:[
    {t:"Those with actual authority over that part of the business", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.077] explains that the representation must be made by a person authorised to make it. The authority to make such representations is itself an act of management, typically held by the board or its delegates, such as a managing director or other executives with wide management powers. So, as a general rule, 'the notion of a \"self-authorising agent\" is not permitted'. Diplock LJ put it this way: where the agent has no actual authority for a particular kind of contract, 'the contractor cannot rely upon the agent's own representation as to his actual authority. He can rely only upon a representation by a person or persons who have actual authority to manage or conduct that part of the business of the corporation to which the contract relates.' The Ocean Frost applies this rule ([07.078])."},
    {t:"Any employee of the company", correct:false,
     why:"Only those with actual authority over the relevant part of the business can make the representation."},
    {t:"The agent himself, if he is senior enough", correct:false,
     why:"Self-authorisation is not generally permitted. First Energy is a controversial qualification, not the rule ([07.080])."},
    {t:"Any shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders as such do not manage the company's business and cannot make representations of this kind."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.077], fns 190–191", takeaway:"No self-authorising agents; representation from those who manage." },

{ id:"cl7-064", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shipbroker bribes M, a vice-president (transportation) and chartering manager, to sign a bogus three-year charterback, knowing the company would only agree to one year. M conceals it internally. The buyer relies on the charter.",
  prompt:"Was the company bound in The Ocean Frost?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the only representation was M's own", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.078] sets out the facts. Armagas was formed to buy a vessel from Mundogas and required Mundogas to charter it back for three years. Knowing Mundogas would only agree to one year, the broker J bribed M, the vice-president (transportation) and chartering manager, to enter into a bogus three-year charter, which J and M concealed from Mundogas's other staff. When Mundogas redelivered the vessel after one year, Armagas sued for breach of charter. The House of Lords held Mundogas not bound. Although M was authorised to enter into a straightforward sale, he had neither actual nor ostensible authority for the charterparty: his position was not known to carry general authority to enter into a charter of that nature. 'Consequently, the only representation that the plaintiff relied on was the representation made by M as to his own authority', which was not a representation by Mundogas's responsible management. The case also features in vicarious liability for deceit ([07.011])."},
    {t:"Yes, because M was a vice-president", correct:false,
     why:"His title did not carry authority to commit to a charter of this kind."},
    {t:"Yes, because the buyer acted in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith reliance on the agent's own representation is not enough."},
    {t:"Only for the first year", correct:false,
     why:"The one-year charter was genuine; the dispute was about the bogus three-year charter, which did not bind Mundogas."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.078], fns 192–193", takeaway:"Ocean Frost: agent's own representation doesn't bind." },

{ id:"c7x-036", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ash Ltd's company secretary, who has no authority to approve loans, tells a lender in writing: 'The board approved your facility at yesterday's meeting.' This is true; the board did approve it, and the secretary was asked to relay the decision. Ash later disputes the facility.",
  prompt:"Can the lender rely on the secretary's statement?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: he was authorised to relay the board's decision", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.079] distinguishes authority to transact from authority to communicate the principal's approval. 'As a matter of principle, if the agent is actually authorised to communicate the principal's approval, there is no reason why the third party may not rely on such representation.' The company secretary is the common example: although he typically has no authority to bind the company contractually, except administratively, he 'is nevertheless widely thought to be an authoritative source for relaying the approvals of the board or of particular directors or executives'. Here the secretary was actually asked to relay the board's decision, which was genuine. The lender can rely on it. The harder question, considered in First Energy and Skandinaviska, is whether such authority to communicate can be ostensible where the agent was not actually authorised to relay anything."},
    {t:"No: a secretary can never bind the company to a loan", correct:false,
     why:"The secretary is not binding Ash; the board approved the loan. The secretary is only communicating that decision, which he was authorised to do."},
    {t:"No, because the communication should have come from the board itself", correct:false,
     why:"Boards commonly communicate through officers. Nothing requires the board to speak directly."},
    {t:"Only if the lender had seen the board minutes", correct:false,
     why:"Reliance on an authorised communication of approval does not require the lender to inspect minutes."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.079], fn 194", takeaway:"Authority to communicate approval ≠ authority to transact." },

{ id:"cl7-065", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bank's senior manager at a branch tells a customer he has no authority to approve certain financing, but later writes that head office has approved it. It hasn't.",
  prompt:"What did the English Court of Appeal hold in First Energy v Hungarian International Bank?",
  options:[
    {t:"He could communicate approval, so the bank was bound", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.080] explains that despite Lord Keith's scepticism in The Ocean Frost about distinguishing authority to transact from authority to represent approval, the Court of Appeal in First Energy found that the bank's senior manager at its branch, who had told the customer he lacked authority to approve ad hoc financing, nevertheless had ostensible authority to communicate the bank's approval. That authority was inferred from his position as the highest-ranking person at the branch. The court saw 'no conceptual incongruity' in an agent without transactional authority having authority to inform outsiders of the principal's decision, and all three judges thought it reasonable for the customer to form that view. Holding otherwise would be commercially unrealistic, since customers would have to check every representation with London, defeating the purpose of a branch office."},
    {t:"The bank wasn't bound, applying The Ocean Frost", correct:false,
     why:"The court distinguished authority to communicate approval from authority to transact, and found the bank bound."},
    {t:"The manager was personally liable instead", correct:false,
     why:"The holding was that the bank was bound by his ostensible authority to communicate approval."},
    {t:"The customer was barred because it had been told he had no authority", correct:false,
     why:"That was the bank's argument. The court held the customer could still rely on his authority to communicate head office's approval."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.080], fns 196–199", takeaway:"First Energy: authority to communicate approval, from position." },

{ id:"cl7-066", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is First Energy controversial?",
  options:[
    {t:"It infers authority from position alone", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.081] explains that by holding that ostensible authority to make representations may be inferred solely from the agent's position in the branch, First Energy 'appears to undermine the very basis of the doctrine of apparent authority, which is the third party's reliance on a representation emanating from the principal'. Critics call the holding unnecessary or simply wrong (fns 201–202); others defend it as a realistic way to protect honest third parties' reasonable reliance (fn 203). It was bolstered by the Privy Council in Kelly v Fraser, which stressed that the law should keep pace with modern commerce, where companies commonly delegate authority to communicate approvals to people who cannot themselves approve. In Singapore ([07.082]), its status is unclear: it was applied in HSBC v Jurong Engineering, but the Court of Appeal in Skandinaviska noted it may be inconsistent with The Ocean Frost."},
    {t:"It abolished ostensible authority", correct:false,
     why:"It expanded ostensible authority; it did not abolish it."},
    {t:"It required unanimous board approval for all loans", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in First Energy imposes such a requirement."},
    {t:"It applied only to company secretaries", correct:false,
     why:"The agent in First Energy was a branch senior manager."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.081]–[07.082], fns 200–207", takeaway:"First Energy: realistic or unprincipled? Unsettled in SG." },

{ id:"cl7-067", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A finance manager obtains credit facilities from banks for his company, producing forged board resolutions as evidence of its acceptance. The banks deal only with him and never check his authority. They argue, relying on First Energy, that he had ostensible authority to represent the company's acceptance.",
  prompt:"How did the Court of Appeal respond in Skandinaviska?",
  options:[
    {t:"It failed: Chia was never held out as able to confirm", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.083] explains that Chia, APB's finance manager, asked banks for credit facilities and produced forged board resolutions as evidence of APB's acceptance; the banks dealt exclusively with him and never investigated. When they sued APB, they argued under First Energy that, even if Chia lacked authority to obtain credit, he had ostensible authority to represent that APB had accepted the facilities. The argument failed at trial and on appeal, because there was no evidence APB had held Chia out as authorised to confirm its acceptance or warrant the resolutions' genuineness. First Energy was distinguishable: there, the senior manager did have ostensible authority to report head office approval because of the wide powers of his branch position, while a finance manager's more limited position 'did not usually connote the specific authority to make representations on management approvals'. Chan CJ ([07.084]) suggested First Energy could be reconciled with The Ocean Frost as a case where, on its facts, the authority traced back to the principal."},
    {t:"It applied First Energy and held the company bound", correct:false,
     why:"The court distinguished First Energy and did not decide whether it was good law."},
    {t:"It held forged documents can never bind a company", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not say forgeries can never bind; the problem was the absence of any holding out by APB."},
    {t:"It held the banks liable for negligence", correct:false,
     why:"The issue was whether APB was bound; the banks' claim on that basis failed."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.083]–[07.084], fns 208–213", takeaway:"Skandinaviska: no holding out → no authority to confirm acceptance." },

{ id:"c7x-037", lesson:"7K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What threshold did Chan CJ in Skandinaviska suggest for finding ostensible authority to communicate the principal's approval of a transaction?",
  options:[
    {t:"A high one, given its effect", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.084] explains that Chan CJ was willing to accept that an agent without transactional authority could have ostensible authority to communicate the principal's consent, but 'also emphasised that the threshold for finding such authority is a high one'. A representation about the principal's approval, unlike 'general' representations, is one that 'goes to the heart of the agency relationship'. Although conceptually distinct from authority to transact, it brings about 'the same effect': an agent who can report the principal's decision effectively has the same power to affect its legal position as one who can transact. So where a third party knows the agent cannot bind the principal, 'it would require rather exceptional facts' to find authority to confirm the principal's assent, and it cannot be inferred merely from authority to make general representations, such as about the condition of goods. Lee [07.085] concludes First Energy may be absorbed into Singapore law if applied restrictively; appointment to a position of broad executive powers may itself suffice."},
    {t:"A low one: any senior employee can communicate approval", correct:false,
     why:"Chan CJ stressed the threshold is high precisely because such representations have the same effect as authority to transact."},
    {t:"The same as for any general representation about goods", correct:false,
     why:"Chan CJ distinguished specific representations about approval from general representations, such as about goods."},
    {t:"Only the board itself can ever communicate approval", correct:false,
     why:"Chan CJ accepted agents can have authority to communicate approval; the threshold is just high."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.084]–[07.085], fn 213", takeaway:"Authority to report approval: high threshold, same effect as transacting." },

{ id:"c7x-038", lesson:"7K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Birch Bank's regional head for South-East Asia, who runs the region with broad executive powers but needs head office approval for loans over $50m, tells a borrower in writing that head office has approved a $70m facility. It hasn't. The borrower knew the regional head could not approve such a loan himself.",
  prompt:"On Lee's reading of Skandinaviska, how strong is the borrower's argument based on First Energy?",
  options:[
    {t:"Arguable, given his broad executive powers", correct:true,
     why:"The borrower knows the regional head lacks transactional authority for $70m, so it must show ostensible authority to communicate head office's approval. Chan CJ said such authority needs a high threshold and 'rather exceptional facts' where the third party knows the agent cannot bind the principal ([07.084]). But Lee [07.085] notes that, applied restrictively, First Energy could be incorporated into Singapore law, and 'if one has regard to the facts of First Energy, it may be that the appointment of an agent to a position of broad executive powers may itself suffice'. A regional head with broad executive powers is closer to First Energy's branch senior manager than to Skandinaviska's finance manager. So the argument is serious, though not certain: a court would also ask whether Singapore accepts First Energy at all, given its possible inconsistency with The Ocean Frost ([07.082])."},
    {t:"Hopeless: Skandinaviska rejected First Energy", correct:false,
     why:"Skandinaviska distinguished First Energy without deciding whether it was good law, and suggested it could be reconciled with The Ocean Frost."},
    {t:"Certain to succeed: a senior person's statement always binds the bank", correct:false,
     why:"The threshold is high, and First Energy's status in Singapore remains open."},
    {t:"Irrelevant, because the borrower knew the regional head lacked authority to lend", correct:false,
     why:"Knowing he lacked authority to transact does not rule out authority to communicate approval, which is the point First Energy addresses."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.082]–[07.085]", takeaway:"Broad executive role may suffice for authority to report approval." }

]);

