/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 7 · Lesson 7L Reliance, Notice, Indoor Management, s 25B/25C, Ratification, Seal (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Lee Ch 7 [07.086]–[07.118], fns 214–321. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p2v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl7-068", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What must a third party show about reliance to invoke ostensible authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"That the principal's representation induced the contract", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.086] says the third party must have acted in reliance on the principal's representation; acting to his detriment on a belief founded on other facts is not enough, and a fortiori he cannot rely on a holding out he was unaware of. The representation need not be made to him specifically: he may rely on a general representation if he is objectively a person for whose benefit it was intended, but not if he knew or ought reasonably to have known it was not directed at him. [07.087] adds that reliance means the representation caused or induced him to contract. Detriment is often said to be required and must be proved, not presumed, but courts accept that a mere change of position suffices: being induced to contract with the principal is usually evidence of detrimental reliance, because the third party has lost the opportunity to pursue an alternative course."},
    {t:"That the representation was made to him personally, in writing", correct:false,
     why:"A general representation can be relied on by those it was objectively intended to benefit; no personal or written representation is required."},
    {t:"That he suffered substantial financial loss before contracting", correct:false,
     why:"A change of position, such as entering the contract, usually suffices as detriment."},
    {t:"Nothing: reliance is presumed", correct:false,
     why:"Reliance must be shown, and detriment is not presumed as a matter of law."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.086]–[07.087], fns 214–219", takeaway:"Reliance on the principal's representation; change of position = detriment." },

{ id:"cl7-069", lesson:"7L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: 'Actual knowledge' of an agent's lack of authority includes deliberately turning a blind eye to the obvious.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.088] explains that a third party cannot rely on apparent authority if he knows the agent lacks authority, because apparent authority is a form of estoppel protecting reasonable reliance, and reliance on a representation known to be untrue is not justified. [07.089] adds that actual knowledge, though about a subjective state of mind, is an objective fact that may be proved directly or, more often, inferred from circumstances. For example, a person who knows a transaction is for improper purposes or injurious to the company will usually be taken to know it is unauthorised. Actual knowledge also covers 'Nelsonian' or 'wilful' blindness, where the third party deliberately turns a blind eye or refrains from inquiring so as not to learn the truth. In substance, these are cases where the evidence suggests the third party 'could not honestly have believed that the agent was authorised'."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Wilful blindness is treated as actual knowledge. A third party cannot avoid knowledge by deliberately not looking."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.088]–[07.089], fns 220–224", takeaway:"Wilful blindness = actual knowledge." },

{ id:"cl7-070", lesson:"7L", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each authority to its approach to when a third party is put on notice of an agent's lack of authority.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"AL Underwood v Bank of Liverpool", r:"Objective, negligence-like: would a reasonable person have inquired?"},
    {l:"Akai Holdings (HK Court of Final Appeal)", r:"Only where reliance was dishonest or irrational"},
    {l:"Third suggested approach", r:"Would the circumstances, objectively, amount to notice?"},
    {l:"Banque Bruxelles Lambert v Puvaria", r:"No reliance with 'cognizance' of improper purpose"}
  ],
  why:"Apart from actual knowledge and the now-abolished constructive notice of the constitution, a third party may be imputed with notice if he fails to inquire in circumstances so unusual as to put a reasonable person on inquiry ([07.091]). The test is unsettled. In AL Underwood v Bank of Liverpool, a bank that collected company cheques and, on a director's instruction, paid them into his personal account should have inquired; that suggests an objective, negligence-like test. In Thanakharn Kasikorn Thai v Akai Holdings, the Hong Kong Court of Final Appeal preferred a higher threshold: notice only where reliance was dishonest or irrational, which may eviscerate 'imputed notice' because dishonesty or irrationality often justify inferring actual knowledge anyway. A third approach asks whether the circumstances, objectively construed, would constitute notice. In Singapore ([07.092]), Banque Bruxelles Lambert v Puvaria Packaging accepted that a third party with 'cognizance' of an improper purpose cannot rely on ostensible authority, without defining the term. Lee sees a trend towards protecting third parties' transactional security except in clearly undeserving cases.",
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.090]–[07.092], fns 225–237", takeaway:"Underwood (objective) · Akai (dishonest/irrational) · SG unsettled." },

{ id:"c7x-039", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director of Gale Pte Ltd presents a cheque payable to Gale at Oriel Bank and instructs the bank to credit the proceeds to his personal account. The bank does so without asking any questions. Gale later sues the bank.",
  prompt:"Under AL Underwood v Bank of Liverpool, can the bank rely on the director's apparent authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the unusual request put it on inquiry", correct:true,
     why:"These are essentially the facts of AL Underwood, described by Lee in [07.091]. A bank that collected cheques payable to a company and, on the director's instruction, paid the proceeds into his personal account 'ought to have inquired of the director's authority since such a request was highly unusual'. Having failed to inquire, it could not rely on the director's apparent authority. The reasoning suggests an objective test akin to negligence: whether a reasonable person in the bank's position would have inquired. Under the higher Akai threshold, the bank would be put on notice only if its reliance was dishonest or irrational. Singapore has not settled which test applies ([07.092]), though diverting company cheques to a director's personal account is the kind of red flag most tests would catch."},
    {t:"Yes: a director always has apparent authority to deal with company cheques", correct:false,
     why:"Apparent authority does not protect a third party who fails to inquire in highly unusual circumstances."},
    {t:"Yes, because s 25A abolished all forms of notice", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25A abolished only constructive notice based on registration of the constitution. Notice from unusual circumstances survives."},
    {t:"Yes, unless the bank actually knew of the fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Underwood imputed notice from the unusual circumstances, without requiring actual knowledge."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.091], fns 229–231", takeaway:"Company cheques to director's own account: put on inquiry (Underwood)." },

{ id:"cl7-071", lesson:"7L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Since 2004, a person dealing with a Singapore company is no longer deemed to know the contents of its constitution merely because it is registered.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.090] explains that at common law a person dealing with a company was deemed to have constructive notice of the constitutional limits on an agent's authority, because the constitution was lodged with the Registrar and open to inspection. The rule was unpopular because it put on third parties the unreasonable burden of inspecting constitutional documents before contracting. The indoor management rule mitigated it, but greater protection was thought warranted. So s 25A was enacted in 2004 to abolish this form of constructive notice: a person is not affected by, or deemed to have notice or knowledge of, the contents of a company's constitutional document merely because it is registered. Other forms of notice, such as from unusual circumstances ([07.091]), are unaffected."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25A abolished constructive notice based on registration."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.090], fns 225–228", takeaway:"s 25A (2004): no deemed notice from registration." },

{ id:"cl7-072", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's constitution allows directors to borrow on bond only if shareholders approve the sum. The directors give a bond to a bank, but shareholders never approved the amount. The company argues it isn't bound.",
  prompt:"What did the court hold in Royal British Bank v Turquand?",
  options:[
    {t:"The bank could assume compliance, so the company was bound", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.093] explains that before s 25A, the indoor management rule, or rule in Turquand's case, was the main way to avoid the ill effects of constructive notice. It provides that an outsider may, unless put on inquiry, assume all matters of internal management or procedure have been duly complied with. In Turquand, the directors executed a bond to secure the company's debt to a bank, though their power to borrow on bond depended on shareholders approving the sum. The Court of Exchequer Chamber held that even if the bank knew of the condition, it was entitled to assume without inquiring that the condition had been fulfilled, so it could rely on the directors' ostensible authority. A more extreme example is Mahony v East Holyford Mining, where a bank could assume that people acting as directors and secretary were properly appointed, though they were not. [07.094] gives the justification: outsiders have no legal right to see internal records, and requiring them to check every internal step would make business impossible (Uxbridge Building Society v Pickard)."},
    {t:"The company wasn't bound because shareholders hadn't approved", correct:false,
     why:"That was the company's argument, which failed."},
    {t:"The bank had to inquire into whether approval had been given", correct:false,
     why:"The rule is precisely that the bank need not inquire into internal compliance."},
    {t:"The directors were personally liable on the bond", correct:false,
     why:"The company was bound."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.093]–[07.094], fns 239–242", takeaway:"Turquand: outsiders may assume internal compliance." },

{ id:"cl7-073", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the indoor management rule relate to ostensible authority?",
  options:[
    {t:"It presumes regularity but cannot create authority", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.095] explains that because the indoor management rule protects a third party's perception of a company's internal operations, it resembles apparent authority in validating otherwise unauthorised acts. But the two are 'conceptually and functionally distinct'. The indoor management rule 'only creates a presumption as regards a company's compliance with its internal processes' and 'cannot be applied independently of agency rules to confer authority on a person'. Dawson J put it this way in Northside Developments v Registrar-General: 'the indoor management rule cannot be used to create authority where none otherwise exists'. It has scope 'only if it can be established independently that the person purporting to represent the company had actual or ostensible authority to enter into the transaction'."},
    {t:"It is the same doctrine under another name", correct:false,
     why:"Lee stresses the two are conceptually and functionally distinct."},
    {t:"It creates authority even where the agent has none", correct:false,
     why:"Northside Developments holds the opposite: it cannot create authority where none exists."},
    {t:"It applies only to directors dealing with their own company", correct:false,
     why:"Insiders such as directors generally cannot rely on it ([07.096])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.095], fns 243–244", takeaway:"Turquand presumes regularity; it doesn't create authority." },

{ id:"c7x-040", lesson:"7L", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each limitation on the indoor management rule to its content.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Knowledge of the irregularity", r:"Actual knowledge, or special circumstances putting him on inquiry"},
    {l:"Outright exclusion of authority", r:"Rule irrelevant where no internal act could cure the lack"},
    {l:"Insiders", r:"Directors generally cannot invoke it"},
    {l:"Closely connected director", r:"Barred only if his acts are so tied to his duties that he must know the limits"}
  ],
  why:"Lee [07.096] lists the limitations. First, the rule does not apply if the third party has actual or constructive knowledge of the irregularity; constructive notice arises only from special circumstances putting him on inquiry, for example where an internal decision's effect contradicts instructions previously given to him, or where the very nature of the transaction, especially one in breach of the agent's duties, puts him on inquiry. Second, the rule is irrelevant if the constitution simply excludes authority for certain transactions, for example directors may not mortgage company property, because no internal act or process could cure the lack. Third, insiders such as directors cannot rely on it. But a director seems to be barred only if his acts are so closely connected with his duties as a director that it would be impossible not to regard him as knowing the limits on the directors' powers (fn 249).",
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.096], fns 245–249", takeaway:"Knowledge · uncurable exclusion · insiders." },

{ id:"c7x-041", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Linden Ltd's constitution says flatly that 'the directors shall have no power to mortgage the company's land'. A lender who has read the constitution takes a mortgage over Linden's land executed by the board, arguing under Turquand that it could assume any necessary internal approval had been obtained.",
  prompt:"Does the indoor management rule help the lender?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: no internal step could cure an outright exclusion", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.096] states that the indoor management rule 'is irrelevant if the constitution simply excludes the authority to enter into certain transactions (eg, directors may not mortgage the company's properties) so that the lack of authority is not one that can be cured by any internal act or process'. Turquand works where authority depends on an internal step, such as shareholder approval of a sum, so an outsider can assume the step was taken. Here there is no internal step to presume; the directors simply have no such power. The lender also actually knew of the restriction, having read the constitution, so s 25A does not assist on notice. The lender's better argument, if any, would be under s 25B(1), which deems the directors' power free of constitutional limitations in favour of a person dealing in good faith, but its knowledge of the restriction would bear heavily on good faith ([07.106])."},
    {t:"Yes: Turquand lets outsiders assume all constitutional requirements were met", correct:false,
     why:"Turquand presumes internal procedural compliance. It cannot overcome an outright exclusion of power."},
    {t:"Yes, because s 25A means the lender is deemed not to know the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25A removes deemed knowledge from registration. It does not erase actual knowledge."},
    {t:"Yes, if the shareholders later approve", correct:false,
     why:"Later approval could raise ratification or amendment issues, but it does not make Turquand applicable."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.096], [07.106]", takeaway:"Outright exclusion of power: Turquand can't help." },

{ id:"c7x-042", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What role does the indoor management rule play today, given ss 25A and 25B?",
  options:[
    {t:"A residual one, where statute doesn't reach", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.097] says that though historically significant, the indoor management rule is now supplanted by statute in important ways. Section 25A abolished constructive notice from registration, which 'greatly reduces the occasions for invoking the rule'. It remains relevant only where the third party actually knows of the constitutional constraint without knowing whether the internal procedure has been complied with. Even that use is further attenuated by s 25B, which deems directors' powers free of constitutional limits for good faith dealers. So the rule matters 'only in situations where the statutory protections do not operate', for example where the agent is a manager, not a director, whose own authority is limited by the constitution ([07.107])."},
    {t:"It has been abolished by statute", correct:false,
     why:"It has not been abolished; it survives in a residual role."},
    {t:"It is now the main protection for third parties", correct:false,
     why:"Sections 25A and 25B now provide the main protection."},
    {t:"It applies only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing confines it to public companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.097], [07.107]", takeaway:"Turquand now residual: where statute doesn't reach." },

{ id:"c7x-043", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What are the requirements for protection under s 25B(1), and what does the provision not do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Dealing, good faith, constitutional limit; it's one-way", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.098] says s 25B(1), modelled on s 40 of the UK Companies Act 2006, relieves third parties of having to inquire into directors' constitutional powers. Protection requires (a) a person dealing with the company, (b) in good faith, and (c) constitutional limits on the directors' power to bind or authorise others. [07.099] adds important limits. It enlarges a director's ostensible authority to the extent it lets third parties disregard constitutional limitations, but does not otherwise displace agency principles, so the company's and members' rights against directors who acted without authority are unaffected. It gives directors no unlimited power: if a director lacks authority for some reason other than a constitutional limit, s 25B does not apply. And it is 'one-way': only a third party enforcing a transaction against the company can rely on it. A company wanting to enforce an unauthorised transaction must ratify it under ordinary agency rules."},
    {t:"It protects anyone, in any capacity, and makes directors' acts always valid", correct:false,
     why:"It protects good faith dealers only against constitutional limits, and does not validate all acts."},
    {t:"It lets the company enforce unauthorised contracts against third parties", correct:false,
     why:"The protection is one-way. A company must ratify to enforce."},
    {t:"It removes the company's right to sue directors who exceeded their powers", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25B(5) preserves directors' liability ([07.110])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.098]–[07.099], fns 252–258", takeaway:"s 25B: dealing + good faith + constitutional limit; one-way." },

{ id:"c7x-044", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Rowan Pte Ltd's director, Ms Lau, signs a contract that exceeds a limit in Rowan's constitution. Rowan, not the counterparty, now wants to enforce the contract because it turned out to be very profitable. The counterparty refuses, saying Ms Lau had no authority.",
  prompt:"Can Rowan rely on s 25B(1) to enforce the contract?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: s 25B is one-way; Rowan must ratify", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.099] explains that 'the protection of section 25B is \"one-way\" so it may only be relied upon by a third party seeking to enforce a transaction against the company. A company wishing to enforce an unauthorised transaction may (under usual agency rules) only do so by ratifying the transaction.' Section 25B(1) is framed 'in favour of a person dealing with a company in good faith', and its purpose is to protect outsiders, not to give the company a choice to hold the counterparty to a deal it never validly made. Rowan's route is ratification ([07.115]–[07.117]): a decision with full knowledge of the material facts, made by the board or, if outside the board's power, by the shareholders, within a reasonable time and without unfairly prejudicing third parties' rights."},
    {t:"Yes: s 25B deems the director's power free of limits for every purpose", correct:false,
     why:"The deeming operates only in favour of a good faith person dealing with the company."},
    {t:"Yes, because the contract is profitable for the company", correct:false,
     why:"Profitability is irrelevant to whether s 25B applies."},
    {t:"No, and Rowan can never enforce the contract", correct:false,
     why:"Rowan can enforce it if it validly ratifies."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.099], [07.115]–[07.117], fn 258", takeaway:"s 25B is one-way; company must ratify." },

{ id:"c7x-045", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How have the UK courts treated directors and shareholders as 'persons dealing with the company' under the equivalent of s 25B?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both were excluded, except shareholders dealing as outsiders", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.100] notes s 25B, unlike the UK s 40(2)(a), does not define 'dealing', but suggests a generous reading that serves the protective purpose. On directors ([07.101]), in Smith v Henniker-Major, a majority of the English Court of Appeal accepted the wording was wide enough to include a director but held a director responsible for the company's constitutional compliance could not rely on the provision, since otherwise he could validate a transaction by relying on his own error. Robert Walker LJ dissented, reasoning that the provision now in s 25C (then s 322A) assumes directors can fall within s 25B, which Lee finds more coherent. On shareholders ([07.103]), in EIC Services v Phipps, a shareholder receiving bonus shares could not rely on the provision, which protects third parties in bilateral transactions; a bonus issue is internal and needs no action from shareholders. But a shareholder dealing as an outsider, for example under a supply contract, should be protected. Singapore has likely inherited these uncertainties ([07.104])."},
    {t:"Both directors and shareholders are always protected", correct:false,
     why:"The UK cases deny protection to a director responsible for compliance, and to a shareholder acting qua shareholder."},
    {t:"Neither can ever be protected", correct:false,
     why:"A shareholder dealing as an outsider should be protected, and the dissent in Henniker-Major would protect directors too."},
    {t:"Singapore courts have authoritatively settled the point", correct:false,
     why:"Lee says the question must await the Singapore courts ([07.104])."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.100]–[07.104], fns 259–283", takeaway:"Directors (Henniker-Major) and qua-shareholders (EIC) excluded; outsiders in." },

{ id:"c7x-046", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lark Supplies Pte Ltd contracts with Oakwood Pte Ltd's managing director to supply equipment. Lark's sales manager knew that Oakwood's constitution required board approval for purchases above $1m, which this one was, and had seen nothing suggesting approval. Oakwood refuses to perform. Lark relies on s 25B.",
  prompt:"How is good faith likely to be assessed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Presumed good faith, but its knowledge may rebut it", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.105] notes that under s 25B(2)(a) a person dealing with the company is not bound to inquire into limits on the directors' power, and under s 25B(2)(b) is presumed to act in good faith unless the contrary is proved, so the onus is on Oakwood. But [07.106] contrasts Singapore with the UK. Section 40(2)(b)(iii) of the UK Act says a person is not in bad faith merely because he knows an act is beyond the directors' constitutional powers. 'Sensibly, section 25B does not contain the same provision.' Knowledge of an agent's lack of authority 'is often, though not always, cogent evidence of bad faith', so a person transacting knowing the agent lacks authority 'ought not to be allowed to take shelter under section 25B unless there are other circumstances that suggest he has good reasons for believing the transaction would be regularised'. Lark's sales manager knew of the limit and saw no approval, so Oakwood has a real case on bad faith."},
    {t:"Lark is automatically in good faith because s 25B(2)(a) means it need not inquire", correct:false,
     why:"Not needing to inquire is different from being able to ignore what you already know."},
    {t:"Lark is in bad faith only if it was dishonest about something other than authority", correct:false,
     why:"That is closer to the UK approach. Singapore's s 25B lacks the UK's carve-out, so knowledge of lack of authority can itself show bad faith."},
    {t:"Good faith is irrelevant because the MD has usual authority", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith is an express requirement of s 25B, and a known restriction displaces any reliance on usual authority."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.105]–[07.106], fns 284–289", takeaway:"Presumed good faith, but known lack of authority can rebut it (no UK carve-out)." },

{ id:"c7x-047", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three outsiders each deal with Quince Ltd. (1) A director exceeded a limit set by a shareholders' resolution. (2) A director exceeded a limit imposed by Quince's banking licence conditions. (3) A non-director general manager exceeded a limit on managers' powers contained in Quince's constitution.",
  prompt:"In which case can the outsider rely on s 25B(1)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only (1), the shareholders' resolution limit", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.107] stresses that s 25B 'is concerned only with the constitutional limits of directors' power'. Section 25B(3) defines those limits, substantive or procedural, to include limits derived from resolutions of the company or any class of shareholders, and agreements between members or any class of shareholders. So (1) falls within s 25B. The provision 'would not operate where the limitation is not derived from a constitutional source', for example 'where a director's power is restricted by regulatory or licensing conditions applicable to the company's trade', so (2) falls outside it; restrictions in a director's employment contract or operational by-laws may also fall outside. And because s 25B concerns directors' power, 'it is of no assistance where the unauthorised agent is not a director', so in (3) the outsider must fall back on the common law indoor management rule if put on notice of the constraint."},
    {t:"All three, because each involves a limit the outsider couldn't easily discover", correct:false,
     why:"Lee notes this may seem anomalous, but s 25B is confined to constitutional limits on directors' power."},
    {t:"(1) and (3), because both limits are in company documents", correct:false,
     why:"In (3) the agent is not a director, so s 25B does not apply."},
    {t:"(2) only, because licensing limits are public", correct:false,
     why:"Licensing limits are not constitutional limitations within s 25B(3)."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.107], fns 290–291", takeaway:"s 25B: constitutional limits (incl resolutions/member agreements) on directors only." },

{ id:"c7x-048", lesson:"7L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: It is settled that s 25B applies to the acts of a single director acting alone, as well as to the board acting collectively.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.108] explains that in the UK there are conflicting views on whether s 40 of the 2006 Act applies only to boards acting collectively or to single directors too. Its legislative history suggests the latter, because the predecessor s 35A referred to 'the power of the board of directors' and the 2006 Act changed this to 'the power of directors'. But leading English texts still say only collective board action is contemplated, consistent with the rule that the board acts collectively unless it delegates. On that view, a third party dealing with an agent may assume the board's power to authorise the agent is free of constitutional limits, but if the constitution limits the agent's own power, s 40 does not apply. For Singapore, the answer depends on how far the Legislature intends to shield third parties. If single directors are covered, the 'good faith' requirement would have to filter out cases where the third party took unreasonable risks dealing with a single director."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The point is contested in the UK, and Lee treats it as open in Singapore."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.108], fns 292–297", takeaway:"Single directors under s 25B: unsettled." },

{ id:"c7x-049", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Hawthorn Ltd's board is about to sign a contract that exceeds its constitutional powers. A member learns of it before signing. Separately, another contract signed last month, also beyond the board's powers, now binds Hawthorn to a good faith counterparty, and the member wants to stop Hawthorn performing it.",
  prompt:"What does s 25B(4)–(5) allow?",
  options:[
    {t:"Restrain the unsigned deal only; directors remain liable", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.109] explains that s 25B(4) preserves members' rights to bring proceedings to restrain any action exceeding the directors' powers, 'though no such proceeding may be brought in respect of any act to be done in fulfilment of a legal obligation that is binding on the company'. So members can restrain acts likely to culminate in legal obligations, but not the performance of legal obligations already binding. The unsigned contract can be restrained; the signed contract, which binds Hawthorn in favour of a good faith counterparty under s 25B(1), cannot. Section 25B(4) preserves only existing rights of action, so the member needs a basis in the constitution, the common law exceptions to Foss v Harbottle, or another statute. Under s 25B(5) ([07.110]), s 25B 'does not affect any liability incurred by the directors, or any other person, by reason of the directors exceeding their powers', so Hawthorn can still sue the directors, and possibly anyone implicated in their breach."},
    {t:"The member can restrain both contracts", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25B(4) does not allow restraint of acts done in fulfilment of a binding legal obligation."},
    {t:"The member can restrain neither, because s 25B validates all directors' acts", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25B(4) preserves members' rights to restrain acts that have not yet become binding obligations."},
    {t:"The directors are released from liability once the contract binds the company", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25B(5) preserves directors' liability for exceeding their powers."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.109]–[07.110], fns 298–302", takeaway:"Restrain before binding; can't stop binding obligations; directors still liable." },

{ id:"c7x-050", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Without the authority its constitution requires, Thorn Ltd's board sells a warehouse to a company owned by a director's spouse. The buyer has since sold the warehouse on to an unrelated purchaser who paid full value and knew nothing of the irregularity. Thorn wants to set the sale aside.",
  prompt:"What is Thorn's position under s 25C?",
  options:[
    {t:"Avoidance is barred, but the buyer and directors must account", correct:true,
     why:"Section 25C exists to stop directors and their associates benefiting from s 25B ([07.111]). Under s 25C(3), a company may avoid a transaction if the parties include its director, a director of its holding company, or a person connected with such a director, as defined in s 25D ([07.112]). A spouse's company is likely to be a connected person. But the right to avoid is lost if (a) restitution is no longer possible; (b) the company is indemnified for its loss; (c) rights acquired bona fide for value, without actual notice of the directors exceeding their powers, by a person who is not party to the transaction would be affected; or (d) the company affirms. Here (c) applies: the unrelated purchaser's rights would be affected. Even so, under [07.113], 'regardless of whether a company chooses to set aside the transaction', the connected person and any director who authorised it must account to the company for any gain and indemnify it for loss."},
    {t:"Thorn can set the sale aside regardless of the later purchaser", correct:false,
     why:"Avoidance is barred where a bona fide purchaser for value without actual notice would be affected."},
    {t:"Thorn has no remedy because s 25B protects the buyer", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25C makes transactions with directors and connected persons voidable, and preserves accounting and indemnity claims even where avoidance is barred."},
    {t:"Only the spouse's company is liable, not the directors", correct:false,
     why:"Any director who authorised the transaction is also liable to account and indemnify."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.111]–[07.113], fns 303–308", takeaway:"s 25C: voidable vs director/connected person; bars; account + indemnify regardless." },

{ id:"c7x-051", lesson:"7L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Where a transaction beyond the directors' powers involves both a director and outsiders, s 25C lets the court affirm, sever or set aside the transaction on just terms, without affecting the outsiders' rights.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.114] explains that where a transaction involves several parties, only some of whom are directors or persons connected with them, 'the rights of the \"outsiders\" are not affected but either the company or any other party to the transaction may apply to the court to affirm, sever or set aside the transaction on such terms as the court may consider just'. This lets the court deal fairly with mixed transactions: the director's or connected person's part can be undone or adjusted, while innocent outsiders keep the protection s 25B gives them. It complements the bars to avoidance in s 25C, including protection of bona fide purchasers ([07.112])."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25C gives the court exactly this power for mixed transactions."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.114], fn 309", takeaway:"Mixed parties: court can affirm, sever or set aside; outsiders protected." },

{ id:"c7x-052", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which statement about ratifying an unauthorised contract is correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is retrospective but doesn't excuse the agent", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.115] explains that once a company ratifies a contract made by an agent without authority, it is binding with retrospective effect, as if the agent had been authorised at the time. But the agent is not thereby exonerated, because the company may adopt the transaction as against the third party without waiving the agent's breach, which matters for directors' duties. Ratification requires the company to adopt the contract with full knowledge of the material facts. It may be express or implied and need not be communicated to the agent or third party; implied ratification requires conduct that unequivocally shows intent to adopt, and it may be inferred from acquiescence or silence, for example where the company knows it is being treated as principal and does nothing. Such silence may also found an estoppel if communicated to and relied on, but the concepts are distinct. Under [07.116], the board or its delegate decides, but shareholders' approval is needed where the act is outside the board's power."},
    {t:"Ratification must be communicated to the third party in writing", correct:false,
     why:"Ratification need not be communicated to the agent or the third party."},
    {t:"Ratification releases the agent from any liability for exceeding authority", correct:false,
     why:"The company can adopt the contract without waiving the agent's breach."},
    {t:"Only shareholders can ever ratify", correct:false,
     why:"The board ordinarily ratifies; shareholders are needed only where the act is outside the board's power."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.115]–[07.116], fns 310–315", takeaway:"Ratify: retrospective, informed, express/implied; agent not excused." },

{ id:"c7x-053", lesson:"7L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"(1) An unauthorised agent of Ivy Ltd gives notice exercising an option that had to be exercised by 30 June; Ivy's board purports to ratify on 15 July. (2) An unauthorised agent buys land for Ivy; before Ivy ratifies, the seller validly sells the land to someone else, and Ivy then purports to ratify to claim the land.",
  prompt:"Is ratification effective in either case?",
  options:[
    {t:"Neither: it is late in (1) and divests property in (2)", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.117] lists the limits. 'If an act has to be done within a specified time, ratification may not be made after the expiry of that time.' The option had to be exercised by 30 June, so ratification on 15 July cannot retrospectively validate the late exercise. More generally, ratification must be made within a reasonable time after the act. 'Further, ratification cannot be applied to divest a third party of its property rights.' In (2), the second buyer acquired the land before Ivy ratified, and Ivy cannot use the retrospective effect of ratification to take it away. 'All the above may be understood as instances of the more general principle that ratification is not permitted if its effect is to unfairly prejudice the rights of third parties.'"},
    {t:"Both, because ratification always operates retrospectively", correct:false,
     why:"Retrospectivity is limited where it would unfairly prejudice third parties."},
    {t:"(1) only, because options are internal matters", correct:false,
     why:"An option with a deadline cannot be validly exercised by ratification after the deadline."},
    {t:"(2) only, because the land was bought for Ivy", correct:false,
     why:"Ratification cannot defeat property rights acquired by a third party in the meantime."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.117], fns 316–318", takeaway:"No late ratification; no divesting third-party property." },

{ id:"c7x-054", lesson:"7L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Since 31 March 2017, how may a Singapore company without a common seal execute a deed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Director + secretary, two directors, or director + witness", correct:true,
     why:"Lee [07.118] explains that the common seal was traditionally how a corporation expressed its will, so a document under seal was seen as the corporation's own act, excluding agency principles. That mattered when, at common law, all corporate contracts had to be under seal. The requirement for contracting under seal 'has long been abrogated by statute', so corporate contracts are now largely executed by agents, and sealing is used only to comply with specific rules. Since 31 March 2017, companies need not have a common seal, though they may choose to. A company without one may execute deeds by the signature of (a) a director and the secretary; (b) at least two directors; or (c) a director in the presence of a witness who attests the signature (fn 321)."},
    {t:"Only by affixing a common seal, which every company must have", correct:false,
     why:"Since 31 March 2017, companies are not required to have a common seal."},
    {t:"Only by unanimous shareholder signature", correct:false,
     why:"Execution is by directors (and/or the secretary or a witness), not shareholders."},
    {t:"By the company secretary's signature alone", correct:false,
     why:"A secretary must sign with a director; the secretary alone is not one of the statutory options."}
  ],
  cite:"Lee Ch 7 [07.118], fns 319–321", takeaway:"No seal: director + secretary, two directors, or director + witness." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/8A.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 8 · Lesson 8A Organs & Division of Powers (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 8 [08.001]–[08.009], fns 1–25. Replaces the 8A questions in companyLaw8.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl8-001", lesson:"8A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which bodies does company law recognise as having original power, derived directly from the constitution, to act as the company?",
  options:[
    {t:"The shareholders in general meeting and the board of directors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.001] starts from the point that a company, although a separate legal person, cannot function without natural persons acting for it. It can appoint agents, and agency rules then attribute their acts to it (ch 7). But someone has to have the power to appoint those agents in the first place. Company law recognises only two bodies with that original power, meaning power drawn directly from the constitution: the shareholders in general meeting and the board of directors. The constitution sets the internal framework, including how each organ's meetings are run. If the proper procedure is followed, a decision of the general meeting or of a board meeting is treated as a decision of the company itself, not merely a decision of the people who voted for it."},
    {t:"The board of directors alone", correct:false,
     why:"The board is the organ that usually manages the business, which is why this answer is tempting. But the general meeting is also an organ with original power. It elects the directors, alters the constitution, and must decide matters the Act reserves to it, such as alterations of capital (s 71) and issues of shares (s 161). A company whose only organ was the board would have no body able to appoint or remove the board itself."},
    {t:"The managing director and the chairman", correct:false,
     why:"A managing director exercises powers delegated to him by the board ([08.003]). His authority is derivative, not original: it exists only because, and to the extent that, the board has conferred it. The chairman's role is procedural, presiding over meetings. Neither is an organ of the company in Koh's sense."},
    {t:"Any director acting individually", correct:false,
     why:"Koh [08.003] says it is a common misconception that an individual director can bind the company. Directors act as a collegiate body. An individual director's job is to take part in the board's decisions, and he has authority to bind the company only if he has been specifically authorised."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.001], [08.003]", takeaway:"Two organs with original power: the general meeting and the board." },

{ id:"cl8-002", lesson:"8A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: An individual director, simply by holding office, has authority to bind the company.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.003] says that, contrary to common belief, an individual director has no authority to bind the company unless he is specifically authorised. The board acts as a collegiate body. Powers can be delegated to one director, typically a managing director, or to a committee, and a director acting under such a delegation can bind the company within its scope. Without a delegation, a director's function is to participate in board decisions. This links to ch 7: a third party dealing with a lone director needs to show actual authority (from a delegation) or ostensible authority (from a representation by someone with actual authority). The title 'director' on its own does neither."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This is the popular assumption Koh corrects. Holding office as a director gives a seat at the board table and a vote there. It does not give a personal power to commit the company. Authority to act alone has to come from a delegation by the board, such as an appointment as managing director or a specific resolution."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.003]", takeaway:"A director binds the company alone only if specifically authorised." },

{ id:"c8x-001", lesson:"8A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Wei is one of five directors of Halcyon Pte Ltd. He has no executive role, and the board has never delegated any power to him. At a trade fair he signs an order form for $400,000 of equipment 'for and on behalf of Halcyon, as director'. The board, which had not discussed the purchase, refuses to take delivery.",
  prompt:"On the principles in Koh [08.003], what is the starting point?",
  options:[
    {t:"Wei's directorship alone gave him no authority; the company is bound only if some other basis of authority can be shown", correct:true,
     why:"The board acts collectively. An individual director has no authority to bind the company unless specifically authorised, and here no power was ever delegated to Wei, so he had no actual authority. That is the starting point. The supplier would then have to find another route, which is the subject of ch 7: for example, ostensible authority based on a representation by the board that Wei could make such purchases, or later ratification by the board. On these facts nothing suggests a representation, and the board has refused to adopt the contract, so the supplier is likely to have no claim against Halcyon. Wei may face personal liability to the supplier for breach of warranty of authority."},
    {t:"Halcyon is bound because every director is an agent of the company with authority to make ordinary purchases", correct:false,
     why:"This treats directors as if each were a general agent of the company. Koh is clear that this is a misconception. Directors exercise power as a board. A director has individual authority only to the extent the board delegates it, so being one of five directors does not authorise a $400,000 purchase."},
    {t:"Halcyon is bound because Wei signed expressly 'as director'", correct:false,
     why:"Describing yourself as a director does not create authority. The question is whether the company, through an organ with power to do so, authorised Wei or represented that he was authorised. Wei's own statement about his capacity is not a representation by the company."},
    {t:"The contract is void because only the general meeting can approve contracts of this size", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in the Act requires a general meeting to approve an equipment purchase. Under s 157A the board manages the business and could have approved this purchase itself. The problem is not that the wrong organ was needed, but that no organ authorised Wei."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.003]; see ch 7", takeaway:"Lone director, no delegation → no actual authority; look for ostensible authority or ratification." },

{ id:"c8x-002", lesson:"8A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Even under the historical, constitution-based allocation of powers, some matters had to be decided by the general meeting because legislation required it. Which of these is NOT one of the statutory examples Koh gives?",
  options:[
    {t:"Appointing the company's auditors' engagement partner", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.004], fn 4 lists four examples of matters the Companies Act 1967 requires the general meeting to resolve: alteration of capital (s 71), acquisition by the company of its own shares (ss 76B–76G), disposal of the company's undertaking or property (s 160) and the issue of shares (s 161). Choosing an engagement partner is not on that list, and is a matter for the audit firm in any event. The wider point is that these statutory reservations sit outside any allocation made by the constitution. Whatever the constitution says about the board's management powers, the board cannot do these things without the general meeting, because the Act puts them in the members' hands."},
    {t:"An issue of shares (s 161)", correct:false,
     why:"This is one of the statutory examples. Issuing shares dilutes existing members and changes the balance of control, so s 161 requires the general meeting's approval for directors to issue shares. The approval can be given in advance as a general mandate, but it must come from the members."},
    {t:"A disposal of the company's undertaking or property (s 160)", correct:false,
     why:"This is one of the statutory examples. A disposal of the whole or substantially the whole of the company's undertaking or property changes what the members have invested in, so s 160 requires the general meeting's approval. This matters in M&A: an asset sale of the business needs a members' resolution even if the board negotiated it."},
    {t:"An acquisition by the company of its own shares (ss 76B–76G)", correct:false,
     why:"This is one of the statutory examples. Share buy-backs return capital to some members and alter relative holdings, so the Act requires members' approval for them, through the procedures in ss 76B–76G."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.004], fn 4", takeaway:"Statute reserves some matters to the GM: s 71 capital, ss 76B–76G buy-backs, s 160 disposals, s 161 share issues." },

{ id:"cl8-003", lesson:"8A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's constitution vests general management in the board. Unhappy shareholders pass an ordinary resolution directing the board to sell a division. The board refuses.",
  prompt:"On the traditional view in John Shaw & Sons (Salford) v Shaw, what is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The shareholders cannot usurp management powers vested in the board, though they may alter the constitution to reallocate powers", correct:true,
     why:"Greer LJ in John Shaw & Sons (Salford) Ltd v Shaw [1935], quoted in Koh [08.004], said the company is distinct from both its shareholders and its directors. If the articles vest management powers in the directors, 'they and they alone can exercise these powers', and the shareholders cannot usurp them any more than the directors can usurp powers reserved to the members. So the ordinary resolution does not bind the board. The members are not powerless, though. The power to alter the constitution belongs solely to the general meeting (s 26, by special resolution), so they can change the division of powers for the future. They can also remove directors by ordinary resolution (s 152 for public companies) and replace them with directors who will sell."},
    {t:"The resolution binds the board because shareholders own the company", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders own shares, not the company, and the company is a separate person. On the division-of-powers theory, the allocation in the constitution binds both organs. An ordinary resolution on a matter vested in the board is outside the general meeting's competence."},
    {t:"The board must comply because any ordinary resolution overrides the articles", correct:false,
     why:"This gets the hierarchy backwards. The constitution can only be altered by special resolution (s 26), so an ordinary resolution cannot override it. A resolution that tries to direct the board on a management matter vested in it by the articles has no binding effect."},
    {t:"The resolution is valid only if every director consents", correct:false,
     why:"Directors' consent is irrelevant to the competence of the general meeting. If the matter is within the board's sphere, the board decides it through its own procedure. Directors agreeing individually to a shareholder resolution does not convert it into a board decision, and the general meeting does not need their consent for matters within its own sphere."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.004]", takeaway:"Neither organ may usurp the other's powers, but the GM can rewrite the constitution." },

{ id:"c8x-003", lesson:"8A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shareholders holding 80% of Arbor Ltd want the company to stop expanding overseas. The constitution vests management in the board. The board has decided to open three new offices abroad.",
  prompt:"Which course is open to the 80% on orthodox division-of-powers principles?",
  options:[
    {t:"Pass a special resolution altering the constitution, or replace the directors, so that future decisions reflect their wishes", correct:true,
     why:"On the traditional view in Koh [08.004], the general meeting cannot override the board on a management decision. But the power to alter the constitution belongs to the general meeting alone (s 26), so with 80% the members can pass a special resolution reallocating powers, for example by reserving specified decisions to themselves. They can also change the board: directors are elected by the general meeting ([08.003]) and can be removed by ordinary resolution. Either route works by changing the structure, not by giving instructions to the existing board. Note, though, that after s 157A there is a debate about how far a constitution can reserve wide management powers to members (see the next questions)."},
    {t:"Pass an ordinary resolution instructing the board to cancel the expansion", correct:false,
     why:"An ordinary resolution on a management matter vested in the board has no binding effect on the board. That is the point of John Shaw: the shareholders cannot usurp powers the articles give to the directors, however large their majority."},
    {t:"Apply to court for an order that the board follow the majority's wishes", correct:false,
     why:"No general jurisdiction allows the court to substitute the majority's commercial view for the board's. A court will intervene if directors breach their duties or if the affairs of the company are conducted oppressively (s 216, ch 11), but a disagreement about business strategy is not a breach of duty."},
    {t:"Nothing, because once powers are vested in the board they can never be taken back", correct:false,
     why:"This overstates the board's position. Koh [08.004] stresses that the general meeting retains the prerogative to adjust the division of powers, because it alone can alter the constitution. The board's sphere is protected from direct interference, not fixed for all time."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.003]–[08.004]", takeaway:"Members change the rules or the board, not the board's decisions." },

{ id:"cl8-004", lesson:"8A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does s 157A of the Companies Act provide?",
  options:[
    {t:"The business 'is to be managed by, or under the direction or supervision of, the directors', who may exercise all the company's powers except those the Act or constitution require the general meeting to exercise", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.005] quotes s 157A(1) and (2). The business is to be managed by or under the direction or supervision of the directors, and the directors may exercise all the company's powers except any power the Act or the constitution requires the company to exercise in general meeting. 'By, or under the direction or supervision of' recognises that in larger companies the board does not manage day to day; it supervises executives who do. The exception in s 157A(2) preserves two categories of members' powers: those reserved by statute, and those the constitution requires the general meeting to exercise. How wide the second category can be is the point of controversy in the rest of this lesson."},
    {t:"Shareholders may give directions to the board by ordinary resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157A says nothing of the kind. It vests management in the directors. Whether members can give directions depends on what the constitution validly reserves to them, and an ordinary resolution cannot override the constitution in any case."},
    {t:"Management powers are allocated entirely by the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"That describes the position before s 157A, when the allocation was left to the incorporators ([08.004]). Section 157A put a management rule into the statute itself. Whether that rule is mandatory or only a default is debated, but it is wrong to say the Act is silent on allocation."},
    {t:"The managing director manages the business", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157A vests management in the directors as a board. A managing director has whatever powers the board delegates to him. The Act does not make the managing director the default manager."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.005]", takeaway:"s 157A: business managed by or under the board; GM keeps only what the Act or constitution reserves." },

{ id:"c8x-004", lesson:"8A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh argues that the wording 'is to be managed' in s 157A suggests the section prescribes the allocation of powers. If that reading is right, which consequence follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board's authority comes from statute, the general meeting has only specific statutory or constitutionally reserved powers, and a wider general reservation to members would presumably be invalid", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.005] follows the prescriptive reading to its logical end. First, if the statute prescribes the allocation, the board's authority to manage is granted by the Act, not by the constitution. Second, as a corollary, the general meeting has only the powers specifically given to it by the Act and, as s 157A(2) allows, by the constitution. Third, any attempt to reserve to the general meeting something wider than specific powers, such as a general power to direct the board, would be contrary to the statute and presumably invalid. This is a stronger position than the old common law: under John Shaw the members could reallocate powers freely by altering the articles; on this reading they could not."},
    {t:"Members gain a general power to supervise the board by ordinary resolution", correct:false,
     why:"This is the opposite of the prescriptive reading. If s 157A prescribes management by the board, a general supervisory power in members would conflict with it. That kind of overriding power was what Credit Development found in the old Table A wording, and s 157A was introduced in response."},
    {t:"The constitution can reallocate any management power to members, as before", correct:false,
     why:"That is the default-rule or contractual reading adopted by Lee JC in TYC (HC), not the consequence of reading s 157A as prescriptive. Koh's point is that the two readings lead to opposite answers on this question."},
    {t:"Section 157A applies only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157A applies to companies generally. Koh notes in [08.009] that its formal model is more descriptive of large public companies, but that is an observation about fit, not a limit on the section's scope."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.005]", takeaway:"Prescriptive s 157A → board's power from statute; GM gets only specific powers." },

{ id:"cl8-005", lesson:"8A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's articles follow the old Table A art 73: management is vested in the board 'subject nevertheless … to such regulations … as may be prescribed by the company in general meeting'. A 7% shareholder wants resolutions on management tabled at the AGM.",
  prompt:"What did Lim Tiong Qwee JC hold in Credit Development v IMO?",
  options:[
    {t:"The qualifying words meant the general meeting kept an overriding power to intervene in the directors' exercise of their powers", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.006] explains that the old model article gave the board general management powers 'subject, nevertheless' to regulations prescribed by the company in general meeting. In Credit Development Pte Ltd v IMO Pte Ltd [1993], Lim Tiong Qwee JC read those qualifying words as preserving an overriding power for the shareholders to intervene in how directors exercised their powers. The decision was seen as departing from the English position, which had read similar words narrowly. Koh says it was this robust interpretation that prompted Parliament to add s 157A to the Companies Act in 2003. Companies that still have the old wording in their constitutions are the ones for whom the effect of s 157A matters most."},
    {t:"The qualifying words had no effect, so the board had exclusive control", correct:false,
     why:"That is closer to the English approach Credit Development was seen to depart from. Lim JC gave the qualifying words real effect, which is why the case was controversial and why Parliament responded with s 157A."},
    {t:"The shareholder had no right to requisition resolutions", correct:false,
     why:"The case was not decided on the shareholder's procedural right to put resolutions forward. It turned on what the general meeting could do once a resolution was before it, under the wording of the articles."},
    {t:"The articles were invalid for inconsistency with the Act", correct:false,
     why:"Lim JC upheld and applied the articles; he did not strike them down. The issue of possible inconsistency with a mandatory statutory allocation only arose after s 157A was introduced in 2003, ten years later."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.006]", takeaway:"Credit Development: old Table A wording gave the GM an overriding power; this prompted s 157A (2003)." },

{ id:"c8x-005", lesson:"8A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Section 157A was added to the Companies Act in 2003 largely in response to the robust reading of the old model article in Credit Development v IMO.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.006] says it was Lim JC's robust interpretation in Credit Development, seen as departing from the perceived English position, that precipitated the introduction of s 157A in 2003. That history is part of Koh's argument that s 157A was meant to be prescriptive: Parliament reacted to a decision giving members an overriding management power by enacting a statutory rule vesting management in the board. Lee JC's later reading of s 157A as a default rule (TYC (HC)) sits awkwardly with that history, which is one reason Koh questions it."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"The link between Credit Development and s 157A is made expressly by Koh at [08.006]. The section did not come out of nowhere; it was a legislative response to a specific judicial reading of the then model article."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.006]", takeaway:"Credit Development (1993) → s 157A (2003)." },

{ id:"cl8-006", lesson:"8A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How did Lee Kim Shin JC read s 157A in TYC Investment v Tay Yun Chwan Henry (High Court)?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a default rule that a company's articles may vary, so the division of powers remains a matter of contract", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.007] sets out the issue as Lee JC framed it: should an express term in a company's articles conferring management powers on shareholders be treated as invalid as a matter of law, or does s 157A set a default rule the articles can vary? Lee JC preferred the second view, so how powers are divided remains a matter for contract. His reasons included the practical point that corporate structures are too varied for one prescribed form of governance, and that there can be good commercial reasons to reserve some management powers to members. He also relied on s 157A(2), which allows the constitution to reserve powers, and on the fact that s 157A's language is repeated in the model constitution, suggesting a company may depart from it ([08.008])."},
    {t:"As mandatory, invalidating any reservation of management power to members", correct:false,
     why:"That is the reading Koh thinks the statutory language supports, but it is not what Lee JC held. He rejected the view that an express reservation of management powers to members is invalid as a matter of law."},
    {t:"As applying only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Lee JC did not limit s 157A by company type. Koh observes at [08.009] that the statutory model fits large public companies better, but that is commentary on practical relevance, not a holding about scope."},
    {t:"As abolishing the general meeting's power to appoint directors", correct:false,
     why:"No reading of s 157A affects the general meeting's power to elect and remove directors. Those powers come from the Act and constitution (eg s 149B, s 152) and are untouched by the management rule."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.007]–[08.008]", takeaway:"TYC (HC): s 157A is a default rule; division of powers stays contractual." },

{ id:"c8x-006", lesson:"8A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which of these is one of Koh's arguments against Lee JC's view that s 157A is only a default rule?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unlike its Australian model, s 198A of the Corporations Act 2001, s 157A is not expressed to be 'replaceable'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.007] points out that s 157A was inspired by s 198A of the Australian Corporations Act 2001. Under the Australian scheme, s 198A is a 'replaceable rule': it applies unless the company's constitution displaces or modifies it, so it is a legislatively dictated default. Section 157A was not given that label. Koh treats the omission as significant. She adds two more arguments. First, s 157A(2), which allows the constitution to reserve 'certain powers' to members, can be read equally as implying that a general supervisory reservation would be contrary to the section. Second, Parliament put the rule into the Act instead of just amending the model articles, which underscores its mandatory nature, and the general penalty in s 407 may even apply to non-compliance ([08.008])."},
    {t:"Corporate structures are too varied to prescribe a single form of governance", correct:false,
     why:"This is one of Lee JC's reasons for his view, not one of Koh's objections. Koh accepts that it is 'undoubtedly true' but says it does not settle the point, because s 157A(2) already allows certain specific powers to be reserved."},
    {t:"The language of s 157A is repeated in the model constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Lee JC used this point to argue that companies may depart from the statutory allocation. Koh turns it around in [08.008]: putting the rule in the Act itself, and not only in the model constitution, suggests Parliament wanted it to be mandatory. As stated, the option is Lee JC's argument."},
    {t:"Most Singapore companies are small and owner-managed", correct:false,
     why:"Koh makes this point in [08.009], but as a reason why there is 'much to be said' for Lee JC's view in practice, not as an argument against it. In small companies the members and managers overlap, so a strict division of powers matters less."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.007]–[08.008]", takeaway:"Koh: s 157A not 'replaceable' (cf s 198A), s 157A(2) cuts both ways, rule is in the Act, s 407 penalty." },

{ id:"cl8-007", lesson:"8A", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each point in the s 157A debate to what it suggests.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Mandatory 'is to be managed'", r:"The statute itself prescribes the allocation"},
    {l:"Section 157A(2)", r:"Only specific powers may be reserved to the GM"},
    {l:"Australia's s 198A", r:"Expressly replaceable, unlike s 157A"},
    {l:"Small companies", r:"Members and managers overlap, so contract fits"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [08.005] reads the mandatory phrase 'is to be managed' as prescribing the allocation of powers, so the board's authority comes from the statute. On s 157A(2), Lee JC used it to support the contractual view, but Koh says it equally supports the opposite: by allowing 'certain powers' to be reserved, it implies that reserving a general supervisory power goes too far ([08.007]). Australia's s 198A, the model for s 157A, is expressly a replaceable rule that a constitution may displace; s 157A carries no such label. Finally, Koh accepts at [08.009] that in practice Lee JC's view suits Singapore, where most companies are small and the same people are both members and managers, so they do not consciously separate their roles. The formal statutory model fits large, widely held public companies better.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.005], [08.007], [08.009]", takeaway:"Wording and s 198A point to mandatory; small-company practice points to contract." },

{ id:"c8x-007", lesson:"8A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two siblings each own 50% of a family trading company and are its only directors. They routinely make decisions over dinner without labelling them board or shareholder decisions. A lawyer advising them asks whether the formal s 157A division of powers matters much here.",
  prompt:"What is Koh's view of how the statutory model fits companies like this?",
  options:[
    {t:"It matters less here: in small owner-managed companies the roles overlap, and the formal model is more descriptive of large widely held public companies", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.009] says the exact purpose and effect of s 157A remain unclear, but the practical result of Lee JC's contractual view is consistent with how internal ordering has usually been seen in private companies. Where membership and management completely overlap, those involved are unlikely to distinguish their two roles consciously. The formal division of powers is more accurate as a description of large public companies, where it would be impractical to expect the general meeting to take daily management decisions. Since most Singapore companies are small, Koh says there is 'much to be said' for Lee JC's view. That does not mean formalities never matter for the siblings: if they fall out, questions about which organ made a decision, and whether it was properly made, can become decisive (see the Duomatic principle in 8H)."},
    {t:"It matters more here, because small companies are most at risk of abuse", correct:false,
     why:"Koh's point runs the other way. The formal separation of organs is most realistic in large companies with dispersed shareholders. In small companies the same people sit in both organs, so the separation is often artificial."},
    {t:"Section 157A does not apply to companies with two members", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157A applies to companies generally. Koh's observation is about practical relevance, not about the section's legal scope."},
    {t:"Their informal decisions are all void for failing to follow the statutory division", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in Koh's discussion suggests informal decisions in small companies are automatically void. The law recognises decisions made without formal meetings in various situations (8H). The question here is how well the statutory model describes reality, not whether informal decisions are nullities."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.009]", takeaway:"Statutory model fits big public companies; small companies blur the roles, so Lee JC's view is practical." }

]);

