/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 8 · Lesson 8B Reserve Powers of Shareholders (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 8 [08.010]–[08.015], fns 26–58. Replaces the 8B questions in companyLaw8.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl8-008", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What reserve power did Warrington J recognise in Barron v Potter?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where directors are unable or unwilling to exercise their powers, the company in general meeting may exercise them", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.010] quotes Warrington J in Barron v Potter [1914], who based the principle on 'plain common sense'. If directors with certain powers are unable or unwilling to exercise them, and so are in effect a non-existent body for that purpose, there must be some power in the company to do itself what would otherwise be done by them, so the general meeting may exercise the power. Read literally, this suggests a potentially total reversion of management powers to the general meeting when the board cannot act. That wide view has been called 'convenient, but difficult to reconcile in principle with the strict theory of a division of powers', and later cases (Massey v Wales, TYC) cut it back sharply."},
    {t:"The board may act when the general meeting is deadlocked", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the doctrine. Reserve powers concern the general meeting stepping in when the board cannot or will not act. A deadlocked general meeting does not hand its powers to the board; matters the Act reserves to members still need a members' decision."},
    {t:"The court may manage the company itself", correct:false,
     why:"Barron v Potter is about which organ of the company may act, not about the court taking over. Courts can order meetings (8G) or grant relief such as winding up or oppression remedies, but they do not assume general management of a company under this doctrine."},
    {t:"The chairman may act alone in an emergency", correct:false,
     why:"No such individual power arises from Barron v Potter. The principle reverts power to the company in general meeting, a collective organ, not to any individual officer."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.010]", takeaway:"Barron v Potter: board unable/unwilling → GM may act (wide view)." },

{ id:"c8x-008", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why has the wide Barron v Potter view of reserve powers been criticised?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is convenient but hard to reconcile with the strict theory that neither organ may intrude into the other's sphere", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.010] notes that the wide view of reserve powers has been described as 'convenient, but difficult to reconcile in principle with the strict theory of a division of powers'. The strict theory, from John Shaw, holds that powers vested in the board cannot be exercised by the general meeting, and vice versa. A doctrine that lets the general meeting take over all management powers whenever the board is stuck sits uneasily with that. The narrower approach in Massey v Wales is seen as more consistent with the theory, because it treats any reserve power as a matter of implication from the constitution, confined to what business efficacy or necessity requires."},
    {t:"It gives the board too much power over shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The criticism is the reverse: the wide view potentially gives the general meeting too much power, allowing it to take over management functions the constitution assigns to the board."},
    {t:"It was decided before companies could have boards of directors", correct:false,
     why:"Barron v Potter was decided in 1914, long after the board became the standard management organ. The criticism is doctrinal, not historical."},
    {t:"It has been overruled by s 157A", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157A does not mention reserve powers. Whether any reserve power survives depends on how s 157A is read; on Lee JC's contractual reading, narrow reserve powers can still be implied. Barron v Potter has been confined, not overruled by statute."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.010]", takeaway:"Wide reserve powers clash with the strict division-of-powers theory." },

{ id:"cl8-009", lesson:"8B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's only two directors are deadlocked. One of them controls the general meeting, which purports to ratify his commencement of legal proceedings. The constitution vests general management in the board and lets the general meeting appoint directors.",
  prompt:"What did the NSW Court of Appeal hold in Massey v Wales?",
  options:[
    {t:"No reserve power arose: any such power must be implied from the constitution on grounds of business efficacy or necessity, and the power to appoint directors could break the deadlock", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.010] summarises Massey v Wales (2003). Because general management had been vested in the board by the constitution, the general meeting had no power to ratify the commencement of proceedings. Any reserve power had to be implied from the constitution, on the basis of business efficacy or necessity. Where the general meeting has an express or even general power to remove and appoint additional directors, the implication is generally displaced, because the members already have a way to fix the problem. Here, appointing extra directors would break the deadlock, so implying a reserve power to ratify the litigation was unnecessary. Koh says at [08.011] the narrow approach was justified because the constitution in Massey used terms substantially similar to s 157A."},
    {t:"The general meeting could ratify because the board was deadlocked", correct:false,
     why:"That is what the wide Barron v Potter view would suggest, and it is exactly what Massey rejected. A deadlock does not by itself give the general meeting power where the constitution provides a mechanism, such as appointing directors, to resolve it."},
    {t:"The proceedings were valid because one director started them", correct:false,
     why:"A single director has no authority to commence proceedings in the company's name without authority from the board ([08.003]). The question in Massey was whether the general meeting could supply that authority; it could not."},
    {t:"The court itself appointed a third director", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not appoint anyone. Its point was that the members could do so under the constitution, which is why no reserve power needed to be implied."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.010]–[08.011]", takeaway:"Massey: reserve power implied only on necessity; displaced if GM can appoint directors." },

{ id:"c8x-009", lesson:"8B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Kestrel Pte Ltd has two directors, who have stopped speaking. A customer owes Kestrel $2m, and the limitation period is close. The constitution follows s 157A, lets the general meeting appoint additional directors by ordinary resolution, and contains no unanimity requirement for board decisions. The 70% shareholder calls a general meeting to resolve that the company sue the customer.",
  prompt:"Applying Massey v Wales and TYC, is the general meeting likely to have a reserve power to authorise the suit?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the members can break the deadlock by appointing a third director, so a reserve power is not reasonably necessary", correct:true,
     why:"Reserve powers are implied from the constitution only on the basis of (reasonable) necessity ([08.010], [08.012]). Massey holds that where the general meeting has power to appoint additional directors, the implication is generally displaced, because the deadlock can be broken through the ordinary machinery. Here the constitution has no unanimity requirement, so a third director appointed by the 70% shareholder could form a majority and authorise the suit through the board. That makes a reserve power unnecessary. Contrast TYC, where the constitution required both directors to agree, so adding directors would not have helped ([08.014]). The urgency of the limitation period does not change the analysis; it is a reason to appoint the third director quickly."},
    {t:"Yes: any board deadlock gives the general meeting a reserve power", correct:false,
     why:"That is the wide Barron v Potter approach, which Massey and TYC have rejected. After Chan Siew Lee, reserve powers are narrow and arise only when the ordinary machinery cannot break the deadlock and the further requirements are met."},
    {t:"Yes, because the claim relates to a debt owed to the company", correct:false,
     why:"Chan Siew Lee's further requirement is that the matter concerns performance of a bona fide obligation the company owes to a third party. A claim the company has against a customer is the reverse: it is a right of the company, not an obligation it owes. In any event, the threshold of necessity is not met here."},
    {t:"No, because only the court can authorise litigation by a company", correct:false,
     why:"Companies authorise their own litigation through the board in the normal course. The court's leave is needed only for a member to bring a derivative action (ch 10), not for the company to sue through its own organs."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.010]–[08.014]", takeaway:"If the GM can appoint a tie-breaking director, no reserve power is implied." },

{ id:"c8x-010", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh says the 'strict' theory of division of powers rests on a contractarian view of the constitution. What follows from that?",
  options:[
    {t:"The scope of reserve powers depends on the terms of each constitution, so a different, possibly wide view could be right in an appropriate case", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.011] reasons that if the allocation of powers comes from the constitution as a contract between company and members, then how the theory applies must depend on the terms of that constitution. Where the constitution vests general management in the board and reserves only specific powers to members, in terms like s 157A, a narrow view of reserve powers is a necessary corollary. The constitution in Massey was in exactly those terms, so the narrow approach was justified. But in Australia a constitution may expressly vest general management power in the general meeting, and Massey affirmed the contractual basis of the division of powers. So in an appropriate case a different, possibly wide and perhaps even unlimited, view of reserve powers may be called for."},
    {t:"Reserve powers are always narrow, whatever the constitution says", correct:false,
     why:"That is the conclusion a statutory model might support, not the contractarian one. On the contractarian view, a constitution reserving wide powers to members could produce wide reserve powers."},
    {t:"Reserve powers can be created only by the court", correct:false,
     why:"On the contractarian view reserve powers are implied terms of the constitution. The court identifies them by construing the constitution, but their source is the contract."},
    {t:"The constitution is irrelevant once a board deadlock arises", correct:false,
     why:"The constitution is central, not irrelevant. Both Massey and TYC ask what the constitution, properly construed, implies about who can act when the board is stuck."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.011]", takeaway:"Contractarian basis → reserve powers depend on each constitution's terms." },

{ id:"c8x-011", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh considers what would follow if s 157A created a statutory (not contractual) model of division of powers. In that model, what could members do if there were no competent board?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only appoint new directors to break a deadlock or replace an unwilling board; the general meeting would have no residual management powers", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.012] says the position in Singapore depends on the legal effect of s 157A. In a statutory model the power to manage comes directly from legislation. Since the statute gives no such power to the general meeting, it would have no residual powers, only the specific powers granted by statute or expressly reserved by the constitution under s 157A(2). If there were no competent board, the members' only option would be to appoint new directors to break the deadlock (s 149B, fn 40) or to replace an unwilling board by removing directors (s 152, fn 41). There would be no room for implied reserve powers at all. The High Court in TYC did not adopt this model, which is why narrow reserve powers remain possible."},
    {t:"Exercise all the board's powers until a new board is appointed", correct:false,
     why:"That describes the wide Barron v Potter view, which is inconsistent with a statutory model. If the Act gives management only to the board, members cannot take it over."},
    {t:"Apply to the Registrar to manage the company", correct:false,
     why:"The Registrar has no general management role. The answer in a statutory model is that members use their statutory powers over the board's composition."},
    {t:"Nothing at all, as members lose all powers when the board fails", correct:false,
     why:"Members keep their statutory powers, including the power to appoint and remove directors. That is precisely the route a statutory model leaves open."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.012], fns 40–41", takeaway:"Statutory model: no reserve powers; members can only appoint/remove directors." },

{ id:"cl8-010", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In TYC (HC), what 'predicate' of the compact under s 157A did Lee JC identify, and what follows if it fails?",
  options:[
    {t:"A board both competent and willing to manage; if absent, narrow reserve powers may be implied from the constitution on grounds of reasonable necessity", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.012] explains that, having held that s 157A does not displace the contractual model, Lee JC said a necessary 'predicate of the compact between the shareholders and the company under s 157A' is a board that is both competent and willing to manage the company's affairs. If that predicate fails, recognising some reserve powers in the general meeting ensures the company is not 'needlessly hamstrung' and 'powerless to act'. But the scope of those powers is narrow, a matter of implication from the constitution on the basis of reasonable necessity. Necessity limits reserve powers to what is needed to break the management deadlock. Reasonableness recognises that shareholders act under practical difficulty and uncertainty, so necessity is judged with some latitude."},
    {t:"A unanimous board; if absent, all powers revert to the general meeting", correct:false,
     why:"Lee JC did not require unanimity, and he rejected total reversion. Reserve powers go no further than what is reasonably necessary to break the deadlock."},
    {t:"A listed company; if absent, s 157A does not apply", correct:false,
     why:"TYC Investment was a private company and s 157A applied. The predicate concerns the board's competence and willingness, not listing status."},
    {t:"A shareholders' agreement; if absent, the court manages the company", correct:false,
     why:"The predicate is about the existence of a functioning board, not a shareholders' agreement. The consequence is narrow implied reserve powers in the general meeting, not court management."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.012]", takeaway:"Predicate = competent, willing board; if absent, narrow reserve powers by reasonable necessity." },

{ id:"c8x-012", lesson:"8B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each element of Lee JC's 'reasonable necessity' test to what it does.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Necessity", r:"Limits the power to what breaks the management deadlock"},
    {l:"Reasonableness", r:"Allows latitude for shareholders' practical uncertainty"},
    {l:"The predicate", r:"A board competent and willing to manage"},
    {l:"Source of the power", r:"Implication from the constitution"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [08.012] quotes Lee JC's explanation. The reference to necessity recognises that reserve powers arise because of a deadlock in management, so ordinarily they should go no further than what is needed to break it; this reinforces that reserve powers are narrow. The reference to reasonableness recognises the practical difficulties and uncertainties shareholders face when they try to exercise reserve powers, so necessity is judged 'with a degree of latitude'. The trigger for the whole analysis is the failure of the predicate of the s 157A compact, a board that is competent and willing to manage. And the source of the reserve power is implication from the terms of the constitution, consistent with Lee JC's view that the division of powers is contractual.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.012]", takeaway:"Necessity narrows; reasonableness gives latitude; both implied from the constitution." },

{ id:"cl8-011", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What two further cumulative requirements did the Court of Appeal add in Chan Siew Lee v TYC Investment before reserve powers can be implied?",
  options:[
    {t:"The matter must concern performance of a bona fide obligation the company owes a third party, and nothing must suggest honouring it is against the company's interests", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.013] says the Court of Appeal affirmed Lee JC's general propositions but added two cumulative requirements. First, the matter on which the general meeting intervenes must relate to 'the performance of a bona fide obligation owed by the company to a third party'. Second, there must be nothing to indicate that it would be otherwise than in the company's interests for the obligation to be honoured. The court explained that these limits stop the general meeting from simply stepping into the board's shoes and doing whatever the board could do. The reserve power is confined to the minimum necessary to keep the company going, by letting it meet its bona fide obligations, unless it is shown the company would be better off not meeting them."},
    {t:"A special resolution and the court's leave", correct:false,
     why:"Neither is required. The reserve power is implied from the constitution, and an ordinary resolution of the general meeting can exercise it. The court's leave is a feature of the statutory derivative action (s 216A), which the Court of Appeal pointed to as the proper route for suing a director."},
    {t:"Unanimous shareholder approval and a board deadlock lasting six months", correct:false,
     why:"No unanimity or time threshold exists. The requirements are qualitative: a bona fide obligation to a third party, and no indication that honouring it is against the company's interests."},
    {t:"The matter must be urgent and the directors must consent", correct:false,
     why:"If the directors consented, there would be no deadlock and no need for reserve powers. Urgency is not a stated requirement either."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.013]", takeaway:"CA adds: (1) bona fide obligation to a third party; (2) nothing suggesting honouring it is against the company's interests." },

{ id:"cl8-012", lesson:"8B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two directors, Tay and Chan, are deadlocked. The constitution requires both to agree before the company makes any payment. Tay calls a general meeting, which authorises him to pay certain obligations, appoint solicitors to determine the parties' positions, and sue Chan for breach of duty.",
  prompt:"What did the Court of Appeal decide in Chan Siew Lee v TYC Investment?",
  options:[
    {t:"The general meeting could authorise the payments and appoint solicitors to determine whether reserve powers applied, but not to sue Chan, since the statutory derivative action was available", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.014] sets out the Court of Appeal's conclusions. The requirement that both directors agree to any payment was a mechanism to stop one director acting unilaterally, and so was predicated on a co-operative board. With the directors deadlocked, and the company clearly both obliged to make the payments and better off making them, a reserve power to make them could be implied. The power to appoint solicitors followed from and was limited by this: only so far as needed to determine whether the reserve powers could be invoked. Suing Chan for breach of duty was outside the reserve power. It was unnecessary because s 216A already gave aggrieved shareholders a remedy, and the court's gatekeeping role under s 216A reduces the risk of proceedings brought for tactical reasons in a corporate feud."},
    {t:"The general meeting could do all three things", correct:false,
     why:"This is close to what the High Court held on the solicitors point, since it allowed solicitors to be appointed to sue Chan. The Court of Appeal disagreed: suing a director for breach of duty belongs in the s 216A derivative action, not in implied reserve powers."},
    {t:"The general meeting could do none of them, as the constitution required both directors", correct:false,
     why:"This takes the unanimity requirement too literally. The Court of Appeal held that the requirement presupposed a co-operative board and did not prevent a reserve power to meet the company's genuine obligations when the board was deadlocked."},
    {t:"Only the solicitors' appointment for any purpose was valid", correct:false,
     why:"The solicitors' appointment was valid only for a limited purpose, determining whether reserve powers could be invoked, and the payments were also authorised. Both halves of this option are wrong."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.014]", takeaway:"Chan Siew Lee: payments yes; solicitors only to test reserve powers; suing Chan → s 216A." },

{ id:"c8x-013", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How did the High Court and Court of Appeal differ in TYC on payments and on suing Chan?",
  options:[
    {t:"HC: no power to pay, but power to appoint solicitors to sue Chan. CA: power to pay, but no power to sue Chan.", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.014] traces the reversal. The High Court distinguished Massey: unlike there, appointing extra directors could not break the deadlock, because the constitution specifically required both directors to agree. So it was reasonably necessary for the general meeting to have a limited power to appoint solicitors, both to determine the parties' rights and to sue Chan for breach of duty. But it held there was no reserve power to make payments, since that would directly contradict the constitution's express requirement. The Court of Appeal reversed both points. Payments could be authorised, because the two-director requirement presupposed co-operation and the payments were bona fide obligations in the company's interests. Suing Chan could not, because s 216A was available."},
    {t:"HC: power to pay and sue. CA: no power to do either.", correct:false,
     why:"Neither court took either extreme. The High Court refused the payments, and the Court of Appeal allowed them."},
    {t:"The two courts agreed on both points", correct:false,
     why:"Koh specifically notes that the Court of Appeal's additional requirements led it to differ 'in part' from the High Court on the facts. The disagreement covered both payments and the solicitors' mandate."},
    {t:"HC: power to sue only. CA: power to sue and pay.", correct:false,
     why:"The Court of Appeal expressly refused a reserve power to appoint solicitors to sue Chan, holding the derivative action was the right route."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.014]", takeaway:"TYC: HC refused payments, allowed suit; CA allowed payments, refused suit." },

{ id:"c8x-014", lesson:"8B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Orchid Pte Ltd's two directors must both approve every payment under the constitution. They are deadlocked. The company owes undisputed rent of $60,000 to its landlord, and failure to pay would trigger forfeiture of its only premises. The majority shareholder calls a general meeting that resolves (1) to pay the rent and (2) to sign a new lease for a second, larger warehouse.",
  prompt:"Applying Chan Siew Lee, which resolutions are within the general meeting's reserve power?",
  options:[
    {t:"Resolution (1) only", correct:true,
     why:"Resolution (1) fits the Court of Appeal's test ([08.013]–[08.014]). The board cannot act, and adding directors would not help because the constitution requires both directors to approve payments, a requirement predicated on a co-operative board. The rent is a bona fide obligation the company owes a third party, and nothing suggests it would be against the company's interests to pay it; losing the premises would plainly harm it. Resolution (2) is different. Signing a new lease is not the performance of an existing obligation to a third party but a fresh commercial decision. Allowing the general meeting to make it would let members step into the board's shoes, which the Court of Appeal said reserve powers must not do. The power is limited to the minimum needed to keep the company going."},
    {t:"Both resolutions", correct:false,
     why:"This treats the reserve power as a general substitute for the board. Chan Siew Lee rejects that: the power is not to 'do whatever the board may do', but only to meet bona fide existing obligations. A new lease is a management decision, however sensible it may seem."},
    {t:"Resolution (2) only", correct:false,
     why:"This inverts the test. The existing rent obligation is the kind of matter the reserve power covers; a new expansion decision is the kind it does not."},
    {t:"Neither, because the constitution requires both directors to approve payments", correct:false,
     why:"The Court of Appeal held in Chan Siew Lee that a two-director approval requirement is designed to stop one director acting alone, and presupposes a co-operative board. It does not block the general meeting from authorising payment of genuine obligations when the board is deadlocked."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.013]–[08.014]", takeaway:"Reserve power: pay existing bona fide debts, yes; new commercial ventures, no." },

{ id:"c8x-015", lesson:"8B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why did the Court of Appeal in Chan Siew Lee say a claim against a director for breach of duty is usually best dealt with under s 216A, not through an implied reserve power?",
  options:[
    {t:"The court's involvement under s 216A reduces the risk that proceedings are brought for tactical reasons in corporate infighting", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.014] quotes the Court of Appeal: there is good reason why such a matter is usually best dealt with through s 216A, because the interposition of the court helps reduce the possibility that the proceedings are being brought for tactical reasons stemming from corporate infighting rather than to address a genuine grievance about a breach of duty. The court drew a distinction between disputes that involve breaches of directors' duties and those that do not. For the first kind, an adequate remedy already exists in the statutory derivative action, so implying a reserve power is unnecessary, and necessity is the touchstone for any implied power. Chapter 10 covers the leave requirements (notice, good faith, the company's interests) that do this filtering work."},
    {t:"Because a general meeting can never authorise litigation", correct:false,
     why:"The Court of Appeal allowed the general meeting to appoint solicitors for a limited purpose, to determine whether reserve powers could be invoked. The objection was specific to suing a director for breach of duty, not to litigation generally."},
    {t:"Because s 216A claims are cheaper", correct:false,
     why:"Cost was not the reasoning. The point was the court's filtering role and the existence of an adequate alternative remedy, which made a reserve power unnecessary."},
    {t:"Because directors cannot be sued by their own company", correct:false,
     why:"Directors' duties are owed to the company, so the company is the proper claimant against a director in breach (Foss v Harbottle, ch 10). The question was only which mechanism should authorise that claim when the board is deadlocked."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.014]", takeaway:"Breach-of-duty claims go through s 216A: the court filters tactical suits." },

{ id:"cl8-013", lesson:"8B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: After Chan Siew Lee, a general meeting faced with a deadlocked board can generally do whatever the board itself could have done.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"The Court of Appeal said the limits it imposed ensure that the power reserved to the general meeting 'is not to do whatever the board may do and hence, in effect, to step into the shoes of the board' ([08.013]). It is limited to the minimum needed to keep the company going by meeting its bona fide obligations, unless it is shown the company would be better off not meeting them. In the later case of Independent State of Papua New Guinea v PNG Sustainable Development Program Ltd [2020], the Court of Appeal observed that 'the members face an uphill task in acquiring even a limited degree of power to manage the affairs of the company' ([08.015]). So reserve powers in Singapore are narrow and hard to establish."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This describes the wide Barron v Potter view, which Singapore has not followed. The reserve power is implied only where reasonably necessary, only for bona fide obligations to third parties, and only where honouring them is not against the company's interests."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.013], [08.015]", takeaway:"Reserve powers keep the company going; they do not replace the board." },

{ id:"c8x-016", lesson:"8B", type:"fill",
  prompt:"In PNG Sustainable Development Program, the Court of Appeal observed that members face an '____ task' in acquiring even a limited degree of power to manage the company's affairs.",
  accept:["uphill"], answer:"uphill",
  why:"Koh [08.015] quotes the Court of Appeal in Independent State of Papua New Guinea v PNG Sustainable Development Program Ltd [2020] 2 SLR 200 at [33]: 'the members face an uphill task in acquiring even a limited degree of power to manage the affairs of the company'. The remark sums up the effect of TYC and Chan Siew Lee. Reserve powers must be implied from the constitution, must be reasonably necessary to break a deadlock that the ordinary machinery cannot resolve, and must concern bona fide obligations to third parties whose performance is in the company's interests.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.015], fn 58", takeaway:"Members face an 'uphill task' to gain management power." }

]);

;
