/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 8 · Lesson 8C General Meetings (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 8 [08.016], [08.018]–[08.024], fns 59–103. Replaces the 8C questions in companyLaw8.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"c8x-017", lesson:"8C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh describes the purposes that shareholder meetings serve. Which statement is accurate?",
  options:[
    {t:"Meetings give members a forum to debate and to question directors, encouraging accountability, but the law does not insist that every shareholder decision be made at a meeting", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.016] says company decision-making is generally collective, expressed through resolutions, with the majority principle at its core. Meetings provide the platform, before voting, for discussion and debate among shareholders, and give them the chance to question directors about the company's affairs and finances. In that sense meetings are a mechanism that encourages directors' accountability. But the law does not require that shareholder decisions be made only through resolutions at properly held meetings. In small companies where the members and directors are the same people, meeting formalities can be unnecessary and burdensome, so the Act lets private companies use written resolutions instead, and the general law recognises informal unanimous assent under the Duomatic principle (both covered in 8H)."},
    {t:"Meetings are required for every shareholder decision, without exception", correct:false,
     why:"This overstates the role of meetings. Both the Act (written resolutions for private companies) and the common law (the Duomatic principle) recognise valid shareholder decisions made without a meeting ([08.002], [08.016])."},
    {t:"Meetings exist mainly so that directors can give instructions to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Directors do not instruct shareholders. The accountability flows the other way: meetings let shareholders question directors about how they have run the company."},
    {t:"Meetings matter only in public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Meetings matter in all companies, which is why the Act regulates them generally. Private companies have more flexibility to avoid formal meetings, but a member of a private company that has dispensed with AGMs can still require one to be held ([08.021])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.016]", takeaway:"Meetings = debate + accountability; the law also allows decisions without meetings." },

{ id:"cl8-014", lesson:"8C", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each type of shareholder meeting to its description.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Annual general meeting", r:"Within six months of financial year end (four if listed)"},
    {l:"Extraordinary general meeting", r:"Any general meeting other than the AGM"},
    {l:"Class meeting", r:"Only a segment, or class, of members"},
    {l:"Statutory meeting", r:"Public company limited by shares, one to three months after it may commence business"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [08.018] distinguishes general meetings, which virtually all members have a right to attend, from class meetings, which involve only one class of members, such as preference shareholders voting on a variation of their rights. Public companies limited by shares must also hold a statutory meeting not less than one month and not more than three months after the date they are entitled to commence business (s 174(1)); the directors must first send members a statutory report. The annual general meeting must be held within six months after the end of each financial year, or four months for a listed public company (s 175(1), [08.019]). Every other general meeting is usually called an extraordinary general meeting ([08.022]).",
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.018]–[08.019], [08.022]", takeaway:"AGM (6/4 months) · EGM (any other) · class · statutory (public, 1–3 months)." },

{ id:"c8x-018", lesson:"8C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which statement about the statutory meeting of a public company limited by shares is correct?",
  options:[
    {t:"The directors must send members a statutory report beforehand, and any director who fails to take all reasonable steps to ensure the meeting is properly held commits an offence", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.018] explains that a public company limited by shares must hold a statutory meeting within a window of not less than one month and not more than three months after the date it is entitled to commence business (s 174(1)). Before the meeting the directors must send every member a statutory report (s 174(2)), which contains information such as the shares allotted and cash received, and details of the company's officers (s 174(3)). The meeting lets members discuss any matter relating to the company's formation or arising from the report (s 174(7)). Responsibility for ensuring it is properly held lies with the directors, and every director who fails to take all reasonable steps to ensure this is subject to criminal sanction (s 174(10))."},
    {t:"It must be held by every company within one month of incorporation", correct:false,
     why:"The statutory meeting applies only to public companies limited by shares, not to private companies. The time window also runs from the date the company is entitled to commence business, and is one to three months, not one month from incorporation."},
    {t:"It replaces the first annual general meeting", correct:false,
     why:"The statutory meeting is a separate, one-off meeting focused on the company's formation and the statutory report. The company must still hold AGMs within the s 175 time limits."},
    {t:"Only the company, not the directors, can be liable if it is not held", correct:false,
     why:"Koh specifically notes that the responsibility lies with the directors, and that each director who failed to take all reasonable steps is subject to criminal sanction (s 174(10))."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.018], fns 68–72", takeaway:"Statutory meeting: public cos ltd by shares; 1–3 months; statutory report; directors liable." },

{ id:"cl8-015", lesson:"8C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company listed on SGX has a financial year ending 31 December.",
  prompt:"By when must it hold its AGM, absent an extension?",
  options:[
    {t:"Within four months, by 30 April", correct:true,
     why:"Section 175(1), as described in Koh [08.019], requires a company to hold at least one general meeting within six months after the end of each financial year, but a listed public company has a shorter period of four months. Four months after 31 December is 30 April. The Registrar may extend the period on the company's application if there are special reasons to do so. If the company fails to hold the AGM, the company and every officer in default commit an offence, and any member may apply to court for an order that a meeting be called (s 175(4), [08.020])."},
    {t:"Within six months, by 30 June", correct:false,
     why:"Six months is the general period, which applies to unlisted companies. Listed public companies are subject to the shorter four-month period."},
    {t:"Within 15 months of the last AGM", correct:false,
     why:"The current rule is tied to the end of the financial year, not to the date of the previous AGM. A 15-month gap test is not the rule Koh describes."},
    {t:"Whenever the board chooses", correct:false,
     why:"A company may hold general meetings at any time with proper notice (s 175(3)), but the AGM is subject to a mandatory deadline backed by criminal sanction."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.019]–[08.020]", takeaway:"Listed: AGM within 4 months of FYE; others: 6 months." },

{ id:"c8x-019", lesson:"8C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Pebble Pte Ltd, an unlisted private company, has a financial year ending 31 March. Its accounts are delayed because its overseas subsidiary's auditors are late. It has not passed any resolution dispensing with AGMs.",
  prompt:"What is the deadline for its AGM, and what can it do about the delay?",
  options:[
    {t:"By 30 September; it may apply to the Registrar, who can extend the period if there are special reasons", correct:true,
     why:"Under s 175(1), described in Koh [08.019], an unlisted company must hold its AGM within six months after the end of its financial year: six months after 31 March is 30 September. The period may be extended if, on the company's application, the Registrar considers there are special reasons. A delay caused by a subsidiary's auditors is the kind of reason the company would put forward. If it simply misses the deadline without an extension, the company and every officer in default commit an offence (s 175(4)(a)), and any member may apply to court for an order that a meeting be called (s 175(4)(b), [08.020]). Because Pebble is private, it could also consider dispensing with AGMs for the future by a resolution of all members entitled to vote (s 175A, [08.021])."},
    {t:"By 31 July; no extension is possible", correct:false,
     why:"Four months (31 July) applies only to listed public companies. And extensions are available from the Registrar for special reasons."},
    {t:"By 30 September; the board may extend the deadline itself by resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The deadline is set by statute, so the board cannot extend it. Only the Registrar can, on the company's application, if there are special reasons."},
    {t:"There is no deadline because it is a private company", correct:false,
     why:"Private companies are subject to the AGM requirement unless they have passed a resolution under s 175A dispensing with it. Pebble has not."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.019]–[08.021]", takeaway:"Unlisted: 6 months from FYE; Registrar can extend for special reasons." },

{ id:"cl8-016", lesson:"8C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What threshold lets members require the company to circulate notice of their resolutions with the next AGM agenda?",
  options:[
    {t:"Members representing at least 5% of the total voting rights on the matter, or at least 100 members with an average of $500 paid up each", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.019] says that, provided the matter is one properly within the shareholders' power to decide, members representing not less than 5% of the total voting rights of those entitled to vote on it may requisition the company to circulate notice of their resolutions with the agenda for the next AGM (s 183). Footnote 81 adds the alternative: not less than 100 members holding shares on which an average of at least $500 per member has been paid up (s 183(2)(b)). The two alternatives let either a sizeable holder or a large group of small holders put items on the agenda. This mechanism was the context of Credit Development v IMO, where a 7% shareholder used it."},
    {t:"Members holding at least 10% of the issued shares", correct:false,
     why:"Ten per cent is the threshold for members to convene a meeting themselves under s 177 (two or more members holding 10% of the issued shares) or to requisition directors to call an EGM under s 176. The AGM resolution requisition under s 183 has the lower 5% threshold."},
    {t:"A majority of members", correct:false,
     why:"A majority is far more than is needed. The point of s 183 is to let a minority get its proposals in front of all members."},
    {t:"Any single member", correct:false,
     why:"A single member can attend and speak at a meeting (s 180(1)), but cannot force circulation of a resolution unless that member meets the 5% voting rights threshold."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.019], fn 81", takeaway:"s 183: 5% of voting rights, or 100 members averaging $500 paid up." },

{ id:"c8x-020", lesson:"8C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A 6% shareholder of Delta Ltd, whose constitution follows s 157A without reserving management powers to members, requisitions the company under s 183 to circulate a resolution 'that the company terminate the CEO's employment contract' with the next AGM agenda.",
  prompt:"What is the main legal obstacle to the requisition?",
  options:[
    {t:"The s 183 right extends only to matters properly within the shareholders' power to decide, and terminating the CEO is a management matter for the board", correct:true,
     why:"The 6% shareholder clears the 5% threshold. But Koh [08.019] stresses the qualification: the s 183 right is available 'as long as the matter is one which falls rightly within the shareholders' power to decide'. Under s 157A, and a constitution that does not reserve management powers, the employment and dismissal of the CEO is part of managing the business and belongs to the board. Credit Development v IMO arose in exactly this setting: directors refused to circulate a 7% shareholder's management resolutions and went to court. That case turned on old Table A wording giving the general meeting an overriding power, which Delta's constitution lacks. A more effective route for the shareholder would be a resolution to remove or replace directors, which is within the members' competence."},
    {t:"A 6% holding is below the threshold", correct:false,
     why:"The threshold is 5% of the total voting rights of those entitled to vote on the matter, so 6% is enough. The problem is the subject-matter of the resolution, not the size of the holding."},
    {t:"Only the board can propose resolutions for an AGM", correct:false,
     why:"Section 183 exists precisely so that members can put their own resolutions on the AGM agenda. The board does not control the agenda absolutely."},
    {t:"There is no obstacle; any resolution must be circulated if the threshold is met", correct:false,
     why:"This ignores the qualification that the matter must fall within the members' power to decide. A resolution on a matter the constitution assigns to the board is outside it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.019]; see [08.004]–[08.006]", takeaway:"s 183 covers only matters within members' competence." },

{ id:"c8x-021", lesson:"8C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A company fails to hold its AGM within the statutory period and has no extension. What follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company and every officer in default commit an offence, and any member may apply to court for an order that a meeting be called", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.020] identifies two consequences. First, the company and every officer of the company who is in default are guilty of an offence (s 175(4)(a)). Second, any member may apply to court for an order that a general meeting be called (s 175(4)(b)). The second consequence matters most for a minority member: one shareholder, with any size of holding, can use it to force the meeting at which accounts are laid and directors can be questioned. The general court power to order meetings under s 182 is a separate tool covered in 8G."},
    {t:"The company is automatically struck off", correct:false,
     why:"Striking off is not an automatic consequence of failing to hold an AGM. The statutory responses are criminal liability and a member's right to apply for a court-ordered meeting."},
    {t:"The directors' appointments automatically lapse", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in s 175 terminates directors' offices for a missed AGM. Koh describes only the offence and the court-order route."},
    {t:"Only members holding 10% can do anything about it", correct:false,
     why:"Under s 175(4)(b) 'any member' may apply to the court. No minimum holding applies to this remedy."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.020], fns 84–85", takeaway:"Missed AGM → offence + any member may ask the court to order a meeting." },

{ id:"cl8-017", lesson:"8C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A private company's shareholders want to stop holding annual general meetings.",
  prompt:"How can they do this, and what protection remains?",
  options:[
    {t:"Pass a resolution of all members entitled to vote; business can then be done by written resolution, but any member can still require an AGM to be held", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.021] says the formal AGM is not compulsory for private companies. Under s 175A a private company may dispense with it if it has in force a resolution passed by all members entitled to vote at the meeting. This reflects the informality of private companies, and the business that would have been done at the AGM can be done by written resolution. But a member's right to require an AGM is expressly preserved (s 175A(4)), because minority shareholders may sometimes want the chance to question those in control openly. Under fn 89, the member must give notice to the company not later than 14 days before the date by which the AGM would otherwise have had to be held under s 175."},
    {t:"Pass an ordinary resolution; no member can then insist on an AGM", correct:false,
     why:"Both parts are wrong. Dispensation needs a resolution of all members entitled to vote, not a simple majority, and every member keeps the right to require an AGM."},
    {t:"Apply to the Registrar for permanent exemption", correct:false,
     why:"The Registrar can extend the time for holding an AGM for special reasons, but dispensation is a matter for the members themselves under s 175A."},
    {t:"They cannot; every company must hold an AGM", correct:false,
     why:"Private companies can dispense with AGMs under s 175A. The mandatory rule applies to public companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.021], fns 86–89", takeaway:"Private co: unanimous resolution dispenses with AGMs; any member can still demand one." },

{ id:"c8x-022", lesson:"8C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Juniper Pte Ltd has three members. Five years ago all three passed a resolution under s 175A dispensing with AGMs. Mei, who holds 4%, has grown suspicious of the directors and wants to question them in front of the other members.",
  prompt:"What can Mei do?",
  options:[
    {t:"Give the company notice requiring an AGM, not later than 14 days before the date by which an AGM would otherwise have had to be held", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.021] explains that s 175A preserves each member's right to require an AGM, precisely because a minority may want to question those in control openly. Footnote 89 sets out the mechanics: the member gives notice to the company not later than 14 days before the date by which an AGM would have been required under s 175. No minimum shareholding applies, so Mei's 4% is enough. This is a useful practical point: the s 175A dispensation is a convenience for the members collectively, not a waiver of each member's right to an open meeting."},
    {t:"Nothing: the dispensing resolution binds her because she voted for it", correct:false,
     why:"The dispensation does not take away the individual member's right to require an AGM. That right is expressly preserved by s 175A(4) whatever the member voted earlier."},
    {t:"She must first obtain support from members holding 10%", correct:false,
     why:"The 10% threshold applies to convening or requisitioning EGMs (ss 176–177). The right to require an AGM after dispensation belongs to any member."},
    {t:"She must apply to court; the company cannot be required to hold an AGM after dispensation", correct:false,
     why:"No court application is needed. The member simply gives notice to the company within the time limit, and the company must then hold the AGM."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.021], fn 89", takeaway:"After s 175A dispensation, any member can still require an AGM by timely notice." },

{ id:"cl8-018", lesson:"8C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A valid general meeting always requires at least two members physically present at the same venue.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"This is false on two counts. First, although the conventional position is that a meeting is an assembly of at least two persons ([08.023]), there are exceptions: a class meeting where one shareholder holds all the shares of the class, a quorum set by share value instead of headcount, wholly owned subsidiaries (where a certificate by the holding company's representative replaces resolutions, s 179(6)), and single-member companies (where the member records and signs the resolution, s 184G). Second, physical co-location is no longer needed ([08.024]). In Byng v London Life Association, a meeting in separate rooms linked by adequate audio-visual equipment was held valid, and s 173J now expressly allows virtual meetings using 'virtual meeting technology', unless the constitution excludes or restricts them."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This was once broadly true, but both elements now have exceptions. One-person meetings and decisions are recognised in defined cases, and virtual presence suffices under s 173J."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.023]–[08.024]", takeaway:"Meetings: usually 2+ persons, with exceptions; virtual presence suffices." },

{ id:"c8x-023", lesson:"8C", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each situation to how decisions are made without the usual two-person meeting.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Wholly owned subsidiary", r:"Certificate by the holding company's representative (s 179(6))"},
    {l:"Single-member company", r:"Member records the resolution and signs the record (s 184G)"},
    {l:"Class with one holder", r:"Constitution may treat one person as a meeting"},
    {l:"Quorum set by share value", r:"Headcount rule may be unrealistic"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [08.023] starts from the conventional rule that a meeting is an assembly of at least two persons gathering to reach a collective decision, then lists exceptions where insisting on it would be unrealistic. Where the constitution provides for a class meeting and all shares in the class are held by one shareholder, or where the quorum is stated as a percentage of share value rather than a number of people, one person may suffice. For wholly owned subsidiaries, a certification by a representative of the holding company that a particular act has been done takes the place of resolutions at meetings (s 179(6)). For single-member companies, the member may pass a resolution by recording it and signing the record (s 184G).",
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.023], fns 91–93", takeaway:"Exceptions to the 2-person rule: one-holder class, value quorum, s 179(6), s 184G." },

{ id:"c8x-024", lesson:"8C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Maple Ltd's constitution says nothing about virtual meetings. The board plans a hybrid AGM: some members in a hotel ballroom, others joining by a secure video platform that lets them hear, speak and vote electronically. A dissident argues that remote members are not 'present' and that their votes are invalid.",
  prompt:"Who is right?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board: s 173J permits virtual meetings, recognises virtual attendance and electronic voting, and the constitution has not excluded them", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.024] explains that the Act used to be silent on how meetings are conducted, though courts had already accepted that members in separate rooms linked by adequate audio-visual equipment could hold a valid meeting (Byng v London Life). Section 173J now makes clear that companies may hold meetings using 'virtual meeting technology', defined in s 4(1) as any technology allowing a person to participate without being physically present at the place of meeting. Section 173J(4) spells out the consequences: virtual 'attendance' counts (s 173J(4)(a)), votes may be cast electronically (s 173J(4)(c)), and documents may be provided electronically. The company can exclude or restrict virtual meetings, or modify their effects, by provision in its constitution (s 173J(5)), but Maple's constitution is silent, so the default flexibility applies."},
    {t:"The dissident: a meeting requires members to be physically together", correct:false,
     why:"That was the old assumption. Koh says physical meetings are 'no longer necessary' and that virtual presence suffices, as s 173J confirms."},
    {t:"The dissident, unless the constitution expressly authorises virtual meetings", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the default. Under s 173J virtual meetings are permitted unless the constitution excludes or restricts them; express authorisation is not needed."},
    {t:"Neither: remote members may attend but cannot vote", correct:false,
     why:"Section 173J(4)(c) expressly accepts voting by electronic means. Remote participation is not limited to observing."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.024], fns 94–103", takeaway:"s 173J: virtual meetings allowed unless the constitution excludes them." }

]);

