/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 8 · Lesson 8K Appointment, Resignation & Removal (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 8 [08.069]–[08.075], fns 277–299. Replaces the 8K questions in companyLaw8.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"c8x-081", lesson:"8K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Who usually appoints directors, and how?",
  options:[
    {t:"The members, generally by ordinary resolution and typically at the AGM, so a majority holder can usually control the board's composition", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.069] says the power to appoint directors is usually vested in the members (s 149B; at common law the general meeting has an inherent power to appoint directors by ordinary resolution). Directors are generally appointed by ordinary resolution, typically at the AGM. It follows that a member who controls a majority of the voting shares can be fairly confident of controlling the board. That is why control of 50%+ of the votes matters so much in M&A: it carries the board. The general right can, however, be displaced by express provision in the constitution, for instance by giving the board exclusive power to appoint additional directors, or giving a particular member or third party a right to appoint."},
    {t:"The board, by co-opting new members at its discretion", correct:false,
     why:"The board may fill casual vacancies or appoint additional directors if the constitution allows ([08.071]), but the default power lies with the members."},
    {t:"The Registrar, on the company's nomination", correct:false,
     why:"The Registrar records director particulars (s 173) but does not appoint directors."},
    {t:"The members, but only by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Ordinary resolution suffices for appointment (s 149B). A special resolution is not required."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.069], fns 277–279", takeaway:"Members appoint directors by ordinary resolution; majority controls the board." },

{ id:"c8x-082", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Atoll Pte Ltd's lease of its factory gives the landlord a right to appoint one director. The constitution says nothing about this. The members now refuse to accept the landlord's nominee.",
  prompt:"What problem does the landlord face, on Koh's account?",
  options:[
    {t:"Enforcing the right may be difficult unless it is the subject of a validly enforceable contract or attached to a special class of shares", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.069] says the constitution may displace the members' general right to appoint, and the right to appoint a director is sometimes expressly conferred on someone else, such as a specific member or a third party like a lessor (Malayan Banking v Raffles Hotel). But enforcing such a right can be a problem unless it is the subject of a validly enforceable contract (ch 5, Wellness Group v Paris Investment) or is attached to a special class of shares (Cumbrian Newspapers). Here the right sits in the lease, not the constitution, so the landlord must show it is an enforceable contractual term against the company and that the remedy it seeks, such as specific performance or damages, is available. An outsider cannot enforce the constitution as such, which is why the source of the right matters."},
    {t:"None: any third party named in a contract can appoint directors at will", correct:false,
     why:"Koh specifically flags enforcement as a problem. A third party's right to appoint is only as strong as its legal basis."},
    {t:"The right is automatically void because only members can appoint directors", correct:false,
     why:"The members' right can be displaced, and third-party appointment rights are recognised. The issue is enforceability, not automatic invalidity."},
    {t:"The landlord must first become a member", correct:false,
     why:"Membership is not required for a contractual appointment right. It is one route (via a class of shares), but not the only one."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.069], fns 280–283", takeaway:"Third-party appointment rights need an enforceable contract or class-share basis." },

{ id:"c8x-083", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Under Islet Ltd's constitution, the board has exclusive power to appoint additional directors. The 60% shareholder calls a general meeting to appoint two more directors of its choosing.",
  prompt:"Can the general meeting make the appointments?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not as additional directors: the constitution has displaced the members' general power by vesting it exclusively in the board", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.069] says the members' general right to appoint directors may be displaced by express provision in the constitution, for example one that vests the power to appoint additional directors exclusively in the board. Footnote 280 points to art 8 of TYC Investment's articles as an example. If Islet's constitution does that, the general meeting cannot itself appoint additional directors. The 60% shareholder still has options: it can remove existing directors by ordinary resolution (for a public company, under s 152, which cannot be excluded) and appoint replacements to fill those vacancies if the constitution allows, or alter the constitution by special resolution (it would need 75%). This is also why, in TYC, appointing extra directors was not a straightforward route out of the deadlock."},
    {t:"Yes: members always have an overriding power to appoint directors", correct:false,
     why:"The members' power is a default that the constitution can displace for additional directors."},
    {t:"Yes, if they pass a special resolution at the same meeting", correct:false,
     why:"A special resolution could alter the constitution, but the shareholder only has 60%, short of 75%. Passing an appointment resolution by special resolution does not override the constitution."},
    {t:"No, and the shareholder has no way to change the board", correct:false,
     why:"Removal under s 152 (for a public company) is always available, and the members can alter the constitution with enough votes."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.069], [08.073], fn 280", takeaway:"Constitution can give the board exclusive power to add directors." },

{ id:"cl8-053", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At a public company's AGM, the chair proposes a single resolution appointing three new directors. One member objects.",
  prompt:"What is the effect if the resolution is passed anyway?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is void: two or more directors can be appointed by one resolution only if the meeting first unanimously agrees", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.070] says that for public companies the Act prohibits appointing two or more directors by a single resolution unless the meeting has first unanimously agreed to it (s 150(1)). Any resolution contravening this is void (s 150(2)). The rule protects members' ability to vote on each candidate individually; a bundled resolution would force them to accept all or none. One member's objection is enough to defeat the unanimity needed to bundle. The chair should put three separate resolutions. Footnote 286 adds that public company directors used to be subject to an age limit of 70, removed by the Companies (Amendment) Act 2014."},
    {t:"It is valid if passed by a simple majority", correct:false,
     why:"A simple majority cannot authorise bundling. The meeting must first agree unanimously."},
    {t:"It is valid but voidable at the objector's option", correct:false,
     why:"Section 150(2) makes a contravening resolution void, not voidable."},
    {t:"It is valid for private and public companies alike", correct:false,
     why:"The s 150 rule applies to public companies. Private companies follow their constitutions on appointment procedure."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.070], fns 286–287", takeaway:"Public co: one director per resolution unless the meeting unanimously agrees; else void." },

{ id:"c8x-084", lesson:"8K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Directors of public companies in Singapore must retire at age 70.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.070], fn 286 notes that there used to be an age limit of 70 for directors of public companies, but it was removed by the Companies (Amendment) Act 2014. A company's own constitution can still contain a retirement age, as the alteration in Dovechem did ([08.054]), but that is a matter of contract, not statute. The Act's only basic personal requirements are that a director be a natural person of full age and capacity (s 145(2), [08.076])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The statutory age limit was abolished in 2014. Only a company's own constitution can now impose one."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.070], fn 286", takeaway:"The statutory 70-year age limit was removed in 2014." },

{ id:"cl8-058", lesson:"8K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When the board fills a casual vacancy under a power in the constitution, what duties apply?",
  options:[
    {t:"The board must act bona fide in the company's interests, and the candidate must disclose all facts that might affect its deliberations", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.071] says that where the constitution gives the board power to fill casual vacancies or appoint additional directors, the directors must exercise it bona fide in the interests of the company; it is a fiduciary power like any other (ch 9). The director whose appointment or reappointment is being considered is also subject to the duty to act in good faith in the company's interests. It follows that he must disclose to the board all facts that might affect its deliberations about his appointment (Goh Kim Hai Edward v Pacific Can Investment Holdings). This catches the candidate who already sits on the board and is seeking reappointment, as well as a candidate who is a director in another capacity."},
    {t:"None: appointment is a matter of discretion", correct:false,
     why:"The power is discretionary, but it is a fiduciary discretion and must be exercised in good faith for the company's benefit."},
    {t:"The board must get shareholder approval first", correct:false,
     why:"If the constitution confers the power on the board, members' approval is not required for each appointment."},
    {t:"Only the candidate's consent is required", correct:false,
     why:"Consent to act is needed (s 146), but it is not the only requirement. The board's good faith and the candidate's disclosure duty also apply."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.071], fn 288", takeaway:"Board appointments: bona fide for the company; candidate must disclose relevant facts." },

{ id:"c8x-085", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Kumar, an existing director of Bay Ltd, comes up for reappointment by the board. He does not tell his colleagues that he is negotiating to join a competitor next year. The board reappoints him.",
  prompt:"What is the legal significance of his silence?",
  options:[
    {t:"He breached his duty to disclose to the board all facts that might affect its deliberations on his reappointment", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.071] says the director whose appointment or reappointment the board is considering is himself subject to the duty to act in good faith in the company's interests, and so must disclose to the board all facts that might affect its deliberations about his appointment (Goh Kim Hai Edward v Pacific Can). His plan to join a competitor is plainly relevant: the board might not reappoint someone about to compete. By staying silent he deprived the board of information it needed to exercise its own fiduciary power properly. The consequences of the breach, such as remedies for the company, are dealt with under directors' duties in ch 9."},
    {t:"None: candidates need not volunteer information", correct:false,
     why:"Goh Kim Hai holds the opposite. A director being considered for appointment must disclose facts that might affect the board's deliberations."},
    {t:"The reappointment is automatically void", correct:false,
     why:"Koh describes a duty of disclosure, not automatic voidness. The breach gives the company remedies; it does not undo the appointment by itself."},
    {t:"Only the members can complain, by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The duty is owed to the company, and the breach can be pursued by the company in the ordinary way."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.071]", takeaway:"Appointee must disclose facts relevant to the appointment decision." },

{ id:"c8x-086", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Tan, a director of Cove Pte Ltd (which has other directors ordinarily resident in Singapore), emails the company: 'I resign as director, effective only once the board formally accepts my resignation.' The board never meets to consider it.",
  prompt:"Is Tan's resignation effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: unless the constitution provides otherwise, written notice suffices, and a resignation cannot be made conditional on the company's acceptance", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.072] says that unless the constitution provides otherwise, a director may resign by giving the company notice in writing (s 145(4A)), and his resignation cannot be made conditional upon the company's acceptance (s 145(4B)). Tan has given written notice. The words 'effective only once the board formally accepts' attempt to impose exactly the kind of acceptance condition the Act disregards. The resignation is therefore effective without board acceptance. The only statutory bar on resigning is where the director is the last remaining director ordinarily resident in Singapore (s 145(5)), which is not Tan's position."},
    {t:"No: it takes effect only when the board accepts it, as Tan stipulated", correct:false,
     why:"Section 145(4B) prevents a resignation being made conditional on the company's acceptance."},
    {t:"No: resignation needs a members' resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Members do not need to approve a resignation. Written notice to the company suffices."},
    {t:"No: directors cannot resign mid-term", correct:false,
     why:"Directors can resign at any time by written notice, subject to the constitution and the last-resident-director rule."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.072], fns 289–290", takeaway:"Resignation: written notice; cannot be conditional on acceptance (s 145(4B))." },

{ id:"cl8-054", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's only director who is ordinarily resident in Singapore sends written notice of resignation.",
  prompt:"Is the resignation effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the last remaining ordinarily resident director cannot resign or vacate office, and any attempt is invalid", correct:true,
     why:"Every company must have at least one director ordinarily resident in Singapore (s 145, [08.059]). To preserve that, Koh [08.072] says a director who is the only remaining director ordinarily resident in Singapore cannot resign or vacate his office (s 145(5)), and any attempt to do so is invalid. In practice the director must wait until a replacement resident director is appointed. This overrides the normal rule that written notice of resignation is effective without acceptance."},
    {t:"Yes, once the board accepts it", correct:false,
     why:"Board acceptance is irrelevant. The resignation is invalid while he is the last resident director."},
    {t:"Yes, after 28 days' notice", correct:false,
     why:"No notice period cures the problem. Twenty-eight days is the special notice period for removal resolutions, not a resignation rule."},
    {t:"Yes, if a shareholder consents", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholder consent cannot override s 145(5). A replacement resident director must be in place first."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.059], [08.072], fn 291", takeaway:"Last resident director cannot resign (s 145(5))." },

{ id:"cl8-055", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A public company's constitution says a director may be removed only by unanimous board vote. The director's service contract has three years to run. The majority shareholder wants him out now.",
  prompt:"What is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The general meeting can remove him by ordinary resolution, regardless of the constitution or contract, though he may claim compensation under the contract", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.073] says that for a public company the right of removal is reserved to the general meeting, which may always remove a director by ordinary resolution before his term ends, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the constitution or in any service contract (s 152(1)). The board cannot remove a director, whatever the constitution or any agreement says (s 152(8)), so the 'unanimous board vote' clause is ineffective as a removal mechanism. The resolution needs special notice ([08.074]). The three years left on his contract do not prevent removal, but s 152(7) preserves his right to compensation for termination of an extraneous service contract. The majority shareholder therefore gets him out, at a possible cost to the company."},
    {t:"Only the board can remove him, unanimously", correct:false,
     why:"Section 152(8) says the board cannot remove a director of a public company, despite anything in the constitution."},
    {t:"He can't be removed until his contract ends", correct:false,
     why:"Section 152(1) operates notwithstanding any service contract. The contract affects compensation, not the power to remove."},
    {t:"A special resolution is needed", correct:false,
     why:"An ordinary resolution suffices, though special notice must be given."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.073]–[08.074], fns 292–298", takeaway:"Public co: GM removes by ordinary resolution despite constitution/contract; board can't; compensation preserved." },

{ id:"c8x-087", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ridge Ltd, a public company, has five directors. Four of them, frustrated with the fifth, pass a board resolution 'removing' him, relying on an article allowing the board to remove a director by majority vote.",
  prompt:"Is the removal effective?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the board cannot remove a director of a public company, notwithstanding anything in the constitution or any agreement", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.073] says that for public companies, removal is expressly reserved for the company in general meeting, and the board cannot remove a director notwithstanding anything in the constitution or in any agreement (s 152(8)). The article purporting to give the board that power is overridden. The four directors would need a member to give special notice of a removal resolution, at least 28 days before the general meeting (ss 152(2), 185), so that the members can decide after the director has had his chance to make representations and be heard."},
    {t:"Yes: the constitution expressly allows it", correct:false,
     why:"Section 152(8) overrides the constitution on this point."},
    {t:"Yes, if the decision is unanimous among the other directors", correct:false,
     why:"Unanimity among the other directors makes no difference. The board has no power to remove a public company director."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if the director's contract has expired", correct:false,
     why:"The contract's status is irrelevant to who may remove him. Only the general meeting can."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.073], fn 293", takeaway:"Public co: board cannot remove a director (s 152(8))." },

{ id:"cl8-056", lesson:"8K", type:"order",
  prompt:"Put the steps for removing a public company director in order.",
  steps:[
    "A member gives special notice to the company at least 28 days before the meeting",
    "The director receives a copy of the notice",
    "The director makes written representations",
    "The director is heard on the resolution at the meeting",
    "The meeting passes an ordinary resolution to remove him"
  ],
  why:"Koh [08.074] explains that a resolution to remove a director requires special notice (s 152(2)). Special notice is notice given to the company, not by it, of the intention to move the resolution, at least 28 days before the meeting (s 185). The affected director is entitled to receive a copy of the notice, to make written representations about it (s 152(3), subject to s 152(4)), and to be heard on the resolution at the meeting (s 152(2)). The general meeting then decides by ordinary resolution. The procedure protects the director's right to put his case to members before they vote. Because of these rights, a resolution needing special notice cannot be passed by written resolution (fn 198, 8H).",
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.074], fns 294–297", takeaway:"Special notice (28 days) → copy → representations → heard → ordinary resolution." },

{ id:"c8x-088", lesson:"8K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does 'special notice' mean in the context of a resolution to remove a director?",
  options:[
    {t:"Notice given to the company, by the member intending to move the resolution, at least 28 days before the meeting", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.074] stresses that special notice 'refers to notice which has to be given to the company of the intention to move the resolution', and that it must be given not less than 28 days before the meeting at which the resolution is to be moved (s 185). It is distinct from the notice of meeting the company gives members (14 or 21 days, [08.026]). The 28-day period gives the company time to send the director a copy and gives the director time to prepare written representations, which he can ask to be circulated, and to prepare to be heard at the meeting."},
    {t:"Notice given by the company to members at least 21 days before the meeting", correct:false,
     why:"That describes notice of a public company meeting for a special resolution. Special notice runs from the member to the company."},
    {t:"Notice of a special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Removal is by ordinary resolution. 'Special notice' is a separate procedural requirement, not a special resolution."},
    {t:"Notice given by the director that he will contest the resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The director responds with representations; the special notice comes from the member proposing removal."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.074], fn 295", takeaway:"Special notice = member → company, ≥28 days before the meeting." },

{ id:"c8x-089", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shoal Ltd, a public company, has a director appointed under its constitution to represent the preference shareholders. The ordinary shareholders pass a valid s 152 resolution removing him.",
  prompt:"When does the removal take effect?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not until his successor has been appointed", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.073], fn 292 notes a qualification to s 152(1): where a director being removed was appointed to represent the interests of a particular class of shareholders or debenture holders, the removal is not effective until his successor has been appointed. This stops the majority using its removal power to leave a protected class without representation on the board. The ordinary shareholders can still remove him, since s 152(1) applies regardless of the constitution, but the preference shareholders' seat cannot be left empty in the meantime."},
    {t:"Immediately on the resolution being passed", correct:false,
     why:"That is the normal position, but class-representative directors are an exception: removal awaits the successor's appointment."},
    {t:"Never: class-representative directors cannot be removed", correct:false,
     why:"They can be removed under s 152(1). The only qualification is timing."},
    {t:"After 28 days", correct:false,
     why:"Twenty-eight days is the special notice period before the meeting, not a delay after the resolution."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.073], fn 292", takeaway:"Class-representative director: removal effective only when successor appointed." },

{ id:"cl8-057", lesson:"8K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A private company's constitution may qualify the company's right to remove a director before his term expires.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.075] says that for a director of a private company, the company's right to remove him before his period of office expires may be qualified by the constitution (s 152(9)). This contrasts with public companies, where s 152(1) lets the general meeting remove a director by ordinary resolution notwithstanding anything in the constitution. Private companies often use this flexibility in joint ventures, for example by entrenching each party's right to keep a nominee director or requiring a higher majority for removal."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"The overriding removal power in s 152(1) is aimed at public companies. Private companies can qualify the right of removal in their constitutions."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.075], fn 299", takeaway:"Private co: constitution may qualify removal (s 152(9))." },

{ id:"c8x-090", lesson:"8K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two investors set up a joint venture. Version A: a private company whose constitution says each investor's nominee director can be removed only with that investor's consent. Version B: the same clause in a public company's constitution. In each, the 70% investor wants to remove the 30% investor's nominee by ordinary resolution.",
  prompt:"What is the likely result?",
  options:[
    {t:"Version A: the clause can protect the nominee. Version B: the general meeting can remove him by ordinary resolution despite the clause.", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [08.073] says the public company general meeting may always remove a director by ordinary resolution, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the constitution (s 152(1)). So in Version B the consent clause cannot stop removal, though the procedure (special notice, representations, hearing) must be followed, and if the nominee was appointed to represent a class, removal awaits a successor (fn 292). Koh [08.075] says that for private companies the right to remove may be qualified by the constitution (s 152(9)). So in Version A the clause can validly protect the 30% investor's nominee. The difference reflects the policy that public company shareholders must always be able to change the board."},
    {t:"In both versions the clause protects the nominee", correct:false,
     why:"Section 152(1) overrides constitutional protections in public companies."},
    {t:"In neither version does the clause protect the nominee", correct:false,
     why:"Private company constitutions may qualify the right of removal (s 152(9))."},
    {t:"Version A: removal by ordinary resolution; Version B: the clause protects the nominee", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the rules for private and public companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 8 [08.073]–[08.075]", takeaway:"Entrenched nominees work in private cos (s 152(9)), not public (s 152(1))." }

]);

