/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9A The Agency Problem & the Fiduciary (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.001]–[09.003], fns 1–19. Replaces the 9A questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-001", lesson:"9A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the law need to constrain how directors exercise their powers?",
  options:[
    {t:"Decision-making sits with the board while shareholders bear the risk, so directors' and shareholders' interests can diverge", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.001] explains that Singapore's framework grants significant managerial powers to the board (s 157A). Entrusting control of one's affairs and property to others inevitably raises the risk of fraud and wrongdoing: directors may enrich themselves at the company's expense, or fail to take sufficient care and so cause it loss. The concern is starkest in larger, widely held companies where the directors are often not the shareholders. That 'divide' separates decision-making (vested in the board) from risk-bearing (borne by shareholders), emphasising the potential disjunction between directors' interests and shareholders' interests. Footnote 2 notes that law-and-economics writers call this an 'agency problem'. Footnote 3 adds that in small closely held companies there is often no such separation."},
    {t:"Directors are presumed to be dishonest", correct:false,
     why:"There is no presumption of dishonesty. The concern is structural: whenever control is handed to others, there is a risk their interests diverge from yours."},
    {t:"Shareholders manage the company and need protection from employees", correct:false,
     why:"Under s 157A the board, not the shareholders, manages the company. The protection is needed precisely because shareholders hand control to directors."},
    {t:"Creditors appoint the board", correct:false,
     why:"Directors are generally appointed and removed by shareholders, not creditors (ss 149B, 152)."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.001], fns 2–3", takeaway:"Board decides, shareholders bear risk → agency problem → duties." },

{ id:"c9x-001", lesson:"9A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh identifies both a benefit of giving the board extensive powers and a limit on how far the law should go in constraining directors. Which pairing is accurate?",
  options:[
    {t:"Benefit: centralised, more efficient decision-making by those capable and willing to manage. Limit: the law should not stifle the risk-taking that profit-making needs.", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.001] says there is good reason for the structure: it centralises corporate decision-making in the hands of those capable and willing to manage, streamlining decision-making and improving the efficiency and effectiveness of management. But the same concentration of power creates the risk of self-dealing and carelessness. On the other side, regulators are mindful that the framework should not be so limiting that it stifles 'all degrees of risk-taking and entrepreneurial derring-do' often necessary in profit-making ventures. Some balance is needed, which is why the law targets accountability from several angles rather than requiring directors to avoid all risk. That balance also explains why courts are reluctant to second-guess honest commercial judgments (9E, 9H)."},
    {t:"Benefit: shareholders avoid all risk. Limit: directors must avoid all risk.", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders bear the risk of the business; that is the source of the agency problem. And the law deliberately does not require directors to avoid all risk."},
    {t:"Benefit: directors can act without accountability. Limit: none.", correct:false,
     why:"Koh describes a framework designed to extract accountability. Unaccountable power is the problem, not the benefit."},
    {t:"Benefit: creditors manage the company. Limit: directors must be shareholders.", correct:false,
     why:"Neither proposition appears in Koh's account. Directors need not be shareholders ([08.076])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.001]", takeaway:"Centralised board power is efficient; regulation must balance accountability against risk-taking." },

{ id:"cl9-002", lesson:"9A", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each accountability mechanism in the Companies Act to how it works.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Appointment and removal by shareholders", r:"Lets shareholders demand acceptable board performance"},
    {l:"Accurate information on performance", r:"Puts shareholders in a position to judge the board"},
    {l:"Statutory derivative action", r:"Lets a shareholder sue errant directors in the company's name"},
    {l:"Specific directors' duties", r:"Constrain how directors exercise their powers"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.001] says the Act targets board and directorial accountability from different angles. First, directors are generally appointed and removed by shareholders (ss 149B, 152), which, combined with rules requiring accurate information about corporate performance, puts shareholders in a position to demand acceptable standards; underperforming directors or boards risk removal. Second, the statutory derivative action (s 216A, ch 10) lets an individual shareholder bring proceedings in the company's name against errant directors; the risk that this leads to interference in management is tempered by strict access rules. Third, the Act imposes specific duties on directors, collectively and individually, which constrain how they exercise their powers. That third mechanism is the subject of ch 9.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.001]", takeaway:"Removal + information · derivative action · duties." },

{ id:"cl9-003", lesson:"9A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Section 157 of the Companies Act 1967 is a complete codification of directors' duties.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.001] says the position in Singapore is complicated by a partial codification of directors' duties in s 157. The section is not a complete codification because it expressly provides, in s 157(4), for the continued relevance and application of general law duties. A director is therefore subject concurrently to duties imposed by statute and those imposed at general law (common law and equity). That is why ch 9 has to consider both regimes and how they interact and overlap. In practice, claimants often plead both: a breach of the general law fiduciary duty and a breach of s 157, since the remedies and elements may differ (9D)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(4) expressly preserves the general law duties. The codification is partial."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.001], fn 7", takeaway:"s 157 is partial; s 157(4) keeps general law duties alive." },

{ id:"c9x-002", lesson:"9A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director accused of diverting a business opportunity argues: 'Section 157 sets out my duties. Since the specific corporate opportunity doctrine is not spelt out in s 157, it no longer binds me.'",
  prompt:"What is the flaw in his argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"Section 157 is only a partial codification; s 157(4) preserves the general law duties, which apply concurrently with the statute", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.001] explains that s 157 is the closest the Act comes to codifying directors' duties, but it expressly preserves the general law duties through s 157(4). A director is subject to both sets of duties at the same time. Many of these duties originated at common law and in equity because of the director's position of trust and responsibility towards the company. So even if a particular rule is not spelt out in s 157, it continues to apply as part of the general law. Diverting a corporate opportunity is a classic breach of the general law no-conflict and no-profit rules (9J, 9L), and may also fall within s 157(2) (improper use of position or information). The director cannot escape it by pointing at the statute's silence."},
    {t:"There is no flaw: statute displaces the general law", correct:false,
     why:"That would be true only if s 157 were a complete codification that excluded the general law. It is not; s 157(4) says the general law continues to apply."},
    {t:"The flaw is that general law duties apply only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"General law fiduciary duties apply to directors of all companies."},
    {t:"The flaw is that s 157 applies only to shadow directors", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(1) applies to directors generally, including those within the extended statutory definition."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.001]", takeaway:"Statutory silence doesn't remove general law duties (s 157(4))." },

{ id:"cl9-004", lesson:"9A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What makes a director's relationship with the company fiduciary?",
  options:[
    {t:"He has undertaken to act for the company and controls its property or can affect its interests, leaving it vulnerable to abuse", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.002] says directors are a well-established class of fiduciaries. A fiduciary relationship resembles a trust: the fiduciary, like a trustee, has undertaken to act for or protect the interests of another (Bristol and West v Mothew), and as a result either controls property belonging to that other or is placed in a position to affect that other's interests, legally and practically, for better or worse. The relationship gives the fiduciary 'a special opportunity to exercise the power or discretion to the detriment of that other person who is accordingly vulnerable to abuse' (Hospital Products v US Surgical). Directors have considerable autonomy over how they exercise their powers, so while the equivalence is not exact, they stand towards the company much as trustees stand towards beneficiaries."},
    {t:"He holds legal title to all the company's assets", correct:false,
     why:"The company, as a separate person, owns its assets. Directors control them; they do not hold title to them. Control or the ability to affect interests is what matters."},
    {t:"He is employed under a contract of service", correct:false,
     why:"Employment is generally not a fiduciary relationship by itself ([09.013]). A director's fiduciary status comes from his undertaking and power, not from any employment contract."},
    {t:"He owns shares in the company", correct:false,
     why:"Directors need not be shareholders. Shareholding has nothing to do with fiduciary status."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.002], fns 12–16", takeaway:"Fiduciary = undertaking to act for another + power over their interests + their vulnerability." },

{ id:"c9x-003", lesson:"9A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Because directors are fiduciaries, every rule that applies to trustees applies to directors in exactly the same way.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.002] says that not all fiduciaries are trustees, though they are subject to rules 'in essence and in origin, the same as the law of trusts' (Sealy). Footnote 10 cautions, citing Gummow J in Breen v Williams, that not all the incidents of a trust relationship necessarily apply to fiduciaries. Koh concludes that directors stand in a similar relationship to their companies as trustees to their beneficiaries, 'while the equivalence is not exact'. For example, directors are expected to take commercial risks with the company's assets in a way a traditional trustee would not, and the standard of care applicable to them has developed separately (9F)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The analogy with trustees is close but not exact. Koh expressly qualifies it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.002], fns 8–10", takeaway:"Directors are like trustees, but the equivalence is not exact." },

{ id:"cl9-005", lesson:"9A", type:"fill",
  prompt:"The distinguishing obligation of a fiduciary is loyalty: the principal is entitled to the 'single-minded ____' of his fiduciary.",
  accept:["loyalty"], answer:"loyalty",
  why:"Koh [09.003] says equity intervenes to supervise the fiduciary and prevent him from misusing his position, whether for his own or another's advantage. The distinguishing obligation of a fiduciary is loyalty, and the principal is entitled to the 'single-minded loyalty' of his fiduciary (Millett LJ in Bristol and West Building Society v Mothew). This high standard is enforced mainly through prescriptive and prophylactic rules: rules that do not wait for actual harm but forbid situations, such as conflicts, in which disloyalty might occur. Single-minded loyalty is also what separates a fiduciary from an employee who owes only contractual good faith and fidelity ([09.014]).",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.003], fns 17–18", takeaway:"Fiduciary core = single-minded loyalty, enforced prophylactically." },

{ id:"cl9-006", lesson:"9A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which of these is NOT among the fiduciary rules Millett LJ described?",
  options:[
    {t:"He must secure the best financial outcome for his principal", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.003] quotes Millett LJ in Bristol and West v Mothew: 'A fiduciary must act in good faith; he must not make a profit out of his trust; he must not place himself in a position where his duty and his interest may conflict; he may not act for his own benefit or the benefit of a third person without the informed consent of his principal.' These are rules about loyalty, not about results. A fiduciary who acts loyally but makes a poor decision does not breach these rules; his competence is governed by the separate duty of care and diligence (9F). So 'securing the best financial outcome' is not a fiduciary obligation at all."},
    {t:"He must act in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Acting in good faith is the first rule in Millett LJ's list."},
    {t:"He must not place himself where his duty and interest may conflict", correct:false,
     why:"This is the no-conflict rule, one of Millett LJ's core rules."},
    {t:"He must not make a profit out of his trust", correct:false,
     why:"This is the no-profit rule, also in Millett LJ's list."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.003], fn 19", takeaway:"Mothew: good faith; no profit; no conflict; no self/third-party benefit without informed consent." },

{ id:"c9x-004", lesson:"9A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director wants to sell his own warehouse to the company at market value. Before doing so he gives the company full details of his interest and the terms, and the company, through the proper organ, gives its informed consent.",
  prompt:"On Millett LJ's statement of fiduciary rules, what is the significance of the informed consent?",
  options:[
    {t:"A fiduciary may act for his own benefit if his principal gives informed consent, so the consent can authorise what would otherwise breach the no-conflict rule", correct:true,
     why:"Millett LJ's last rule, quoted in Koh [09.003], is that a fiduciary 'may not act for his own benefit or the benefit of a third person without the informed consent of his principal'. The rule is built around consent. Selling his own warehouse to the company puts the director's interest (a high price) in conflict with his duty (getting the company a good deal), so without consent it would breach the no-conflict rule even at market value. Full disclosure followed by informed consent from the company changes that. Which organ must consent, and what statutory disclosure is also required (such as under s 156), is covered in 9K and 9M."},
    {t:"It is irrelevant: fiduciaries can never transact with their principals", correct:false,
     why:"Millett LJ's formulation itself contemplates informed consent. The prohibition is on unauthorised self-dealing."},
    {t:"It is irrelevant because the price was market value", correct:false,
     why:"Fairness of price does not by itself cure a conflict. The prophylactic rule applies regardless of whether the terms are fair; consent is what authorises the transaction."},
    {t:"It is relevant only if the director is also a shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholding is irrelevant. The consent required is that of the company, his principal."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.003]", takeaway:"Informed consent of the principal can authorise self-interested dealing." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9B.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9B Duties Owed to the Company (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.004]–[09.010], fns 20–50. Replaces the 9B questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-007", lesson:"9B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"At general law, why can only the company bring a director to account for breach of duty?",
  options:[
    {t:"The duties are owed to the company, which follows from separate personality and avoids directors being harassed by many actions", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.004] says it is trite that at general law directors' duties are owed to the company, because they arise from the director's legal relationship with the company. So only the company has standing to bring an errant director to account. Two justifications support this. First, it rests on the separate legal entity doctrine from Salomon v A Salomon & Co, which underpins company law and reinforces the collective nature of the corporate enterprise. Second, channelling litigation through the company ensures directors are not harassed by a multiplicity of actions. The practical difficulty is that the company acts through organs that wrongdoers may control, which is the problem Foss v Harbottle and the derivative action address (ch 10)."},
    {t:"Shareholders have waived their right to sue", correct:false,
     why:"No waiver is involved. Shareholders lack standing because the duties are not owed to them."},
    {t:"Directors are immune from suits by individuals", correct:false,
     why:"Directors are not immune. They can be sued by individuals on personal causes of action, such as fraud, or where a special fiduciary relationship exists with a shareholder ([09.006])."},
    {t:"Only the Registrar can enforce directors' duties", correct:false,
     why:"Directors' duties are enforced by the company. Some breaches also carry criminal penalties, but the Registrar does not hold the civil cause of action."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.004], fns 20–26", takeaway:"Duties owed to the company → company sues (Salomon; no multiplicity)." },

{ id:"c9x-005", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Directors of Ivy Ltd negligently approve a disastrous acquisition. Ivy's share price falls 40%. A shareholder sues the directors in her own name for breach of their duty of care, claiming the fall in value of her shares.",
  prompt:"What is the main obstacle to her claim?",
  options:[
    {t:"The duties are owed to the company, not to her, so only the company has standing to sue for their breach", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.004] says directors' duties at general law are owed to the company, and only the company can bring a director to account for breach. Koh [09.005] adds that directors do not, merely because they are directors, owe any parallel duty to individual shareholders. The shareholder's loss is the fall in value of her shares, which reflects the company's loss; that is the company's claim. Her options are to persuade the company to sue, or to seek leave to bring a statutory derivative action in the company's name (s 216A, ch 10). Her personal claim also runs into the no-reflective-loss principle (ch 11)."},
    {t:"She needed a special resolution before suing", correct:false,
     why:"No resolution would give her personal standing. The problem is that the duty is not owed to her."},
    {t:"Directors cannot be liable for negligence", correct:false,
     why:"Directors owe a duty of care and diligence to the company (9F). The issue is to whom it is owed."},
    {t:"There is no obstacle: shareholders are the company's owners", correct:false,
     why:"The company is a separate legal person. Shareholders do not own its causes of action."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.004]–[09.005]", takeaway:"Shareholder can't sue personally for breach of duties owed to the company." },

{ id:"cl9-008", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shareholders sell their shares to the company's directors. The directors know of negotiations to sell the whole undertaking on favourable terms but say nothing. The shareholders would have asked for more had they known.",
  prompt:"What did the court decide in Percival v Wright?",
  options:[
    {t:"No fiduciary relationship existed between the directors and the shareholders, so there was no duty to disclose", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.005] says directors do not, merely because they are directors, owe any parallel duty, fiduciary or otherwise, to individual shareholders, and Percival v Wright [1902] is the usual authority. The plaintiffs sold their shares to the directors, who knew of negotiations to sell the company's whole undertaking on favourable terms (the sale was ultimately aborted, fn 29). The plaintiffs sought to set aside the sale on the basis that the directors were fiduciaries towards them and had to disclose. Swinfen Eady J refused to recognise such a relationship. To do so 'would place directors in a most invidious position, as they could not buy or sell shares without disclosing negotiations, a premature disclosure of which might well be against the best interests of the company'."},
    {t:"The sale was set aside for non-disclosure", correct:false,
     why:"The court refused to set it aside, because no fiduciary relationship existed with the selling shareholders."},
    {t:"Directors always owe shareholders a duty to disclose", correct:false,
     why:"Percival v Wright establishes the opposite default. Duties to shareholders arise only in special circumstances."},
    {t:"The directors were agents of the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"That was the finding in Allen v Hyatt on different facts. In Percival the directors had not acted as the shareholders' agents."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.005], fns 28–30", takeaway:"Percival v Wright: no default fiduciary duty to individual shareholders." },

{ id:"c9x-006", lesson:"9B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What reason did Swinfen Eady J give in Percival v Wright for refusing to recognise a fiduciary duty owed by directors to shareholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"It would put directors in an invidious position, unable to trade in shares without disclosing negotiations whose premature disclosure might harm the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.005] quotes Swinfen Eady J: recognising the relationship 'would place directors in a most invidious position, as they could not buy or sell shares without disclosing negotiations, a premature disclosure of which might well be against the best interests of the company'. The reasoning ties back to the primary duty owed to the company. Confidential negotiations, such as a possible sale of the business, may need to be kept secret for the company's benefit. A duty to shareholders requiring disclosure would pull directors in two directions. (Modern insider trading law separately restricts dealing on price-sensitive information, but that is a statutory regime, not the general law fiduciary duty.)"},
    {t:"Directors are entitled to profit from inside information", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not endorse profiting from inside information as a principle. It held only that no fiduciary duty to the selling shareholders existed on those facts."},
    {t:"Shareholders are always sophisticated investors", correct:false,
     why:"The reasoning did not depend on shareholder sophistication. Later cases do look at shareholders' dependence and vulnerability when deciding whether a special relationship exists."},
    {t:"The company had no interest in the negotiations", correct:false,
     why:"The opposite: the company's interest in keeping the negotiations confidential was central to the reasoning."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.005], fn 30", takeaway:"Percival's rationale: a duty to disclose could harm the company's interest in confidentiality." },

{ id:"c9x-007", lesson:"9B", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Percival v Wright is authority that directors can never owe duties directly to individual shareholders.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.006] says Percival v Wright represents the default position but is not authority that directors cannot ever owe duties directly to shareholders. Whether duties are owed depends on the nature of the particular relationship. If the relationship 'replicates the salient features of well-established categories of fiduciary relationships', fiduciary obligations may be imposed, as in Allen v Hyatt, where directors became the shareholders' agents. And the cases go further, finding fiduciary duties on special facts outside established categories, as in Coleman v Myers and Brunninghausen v Glavanics ([09.007]–[09.008])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This overstates Percival. It sets a default, which special facts can displace."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.006]", takeaway:"Percival is a default, not an absolute bar." },

{ id:"cl9-009", lesson:"9B", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each case to why a duty to shareholders was, or wasn't, found.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Allen v Hyatt", r:"Directors became the shareholders' agents for the transaction"},
    {l:"Coleman v Myers", r:"Family company, heavy reliance, information gap, orchestrated buyout"},
    {l:"Brunninghausen v Glavanics", r:"Sole effective director; the other shareholder almost totally powerless"},
    {l:"Tai Kim San v Lim Cher Kia", r:"Sellers were active directors, not dependent on the buyer: no duty"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.006]–[09.009] traces the exceptions. In Allen v Hyatt, the directors told shareholders that a proposed amalgamation depended on the directors obtaining options over their shares; they thereby became the shareholders' agents, a recognised fiduciary category. In Coleman v Myers, the relationship fitted no established category, but in an old family company where shareholders relied heavily on the directors, with a large information gap and a carefully orchestrated plan to acquire all the shares, a fiduciary relationship arose. Brunninghausen v Glavanics found a duty where the defendant, majority holder and sole effective director, bought out the only other shareholder, a family member who was 'almost totally powerless' to protect himself. In Tai Kim San v Lim Cher Kia, by contrast, the sellers were themselves active directors, not dependent on the buyer for information, so no duty arose.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.006]–[09.009]", takeaway:"Duty arises from agency, dependence and vulnerability; not where sellers are informed insiders." },

{ id:"c9x-008", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The directors of Fern Ltd tell all shareholders that a proposed merger with Rival Ltd can go ahead only if the directors first obtain options over the shareholders' shares. The shareholders grant the options. The directors then sell the options to Rival at a large profit they keep for themselves.",
  prompt:"Following Allen v Hyatt, how are the directors likely to be treated?",
  options:[
    {t:"As agents of the shareholders for the transaction, and so subject to the fiduciary duties of agents", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.006] says that if the relationship between director and shareholder replicates the salient features of a well-established fiduciary category, fiduciary obligations may be imposed. In Allen v Hyatt, the directors represented that an amalgamation depended on their obtaining options over the shareholders' shares. By doing so they were held to have become the shareholders' agents for that transaction. Agents are a recognised class of fiduciaries, so the directors were subject to the duties attached to agency, including the duty not to make an unauthorised profit. On these facts, the Fern directors would have to account to the shareholders for the profit they made. The source of the duty is the agency they took on, not their position as directors."},
    {t:"As owing no duty, following Percival v Wright", correct:false,
     why:"Percival is the default where directors merely deal with shareholders. Here the directors went further and acted for the shareholders, which is what distinguishes Allen v Hyatt."},
    {t:"As owing duties only to the company", correct:false,
     why:"They owe duties to the company in any event, but their conduct also created an agency relationship with the shareholders, with fiduciary duties owed directly to them."},
    {t:"As liable only if they are also shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Their shareholding is irrelevant. The agency arose from what they represented and undertook."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.006], fn 34", takeaway:"Directors who act as shareholders' agents owe them agents' fiduciary duties." },

{ id:"cl9-010", lesson:"9B", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which factors did the New Zealand Court of Appeal in Coleman v Myers treat as indicating a fiduciary relationship between director and shareholder?",
  options:[
    {t:"Dependence on directors for information and advice, a relationship of confidence, the transaction's significance, and positive action by the directors to promote it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.007] says the court accepted that no general test could be laid down for when a director owes fiduciary duties to a shareholder, but certain factors are indicative: whether the shareholders depended on the directors for information and advice; whether a relationship of confidence existed; the significance of the particular transaction for the parties; and the extent of any positive action by or for the directors to promote it. In Coleman, an old family company, the shareholders already relied heavily on the directors in all company matters. The directors invited that confidence in connection with their plan to acquire all the shares, exploited a high degree of information asymmetry, and carefully orchestrated the acquisition so as to force the minority out under compulsory acquisition provisions. Together these facts created a fiduciary relationship."},
    {t:"Only whether the director holds more than 50% of the shares", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholding size was not a stated factor. The focus was on reliance, confidence, significance and the directors' active promotion."},
    {t:"Whether the company is listed", correct:false,
     why:"Coleman involved a private family company. Listing was not a factor."},
    {t:"Whether the shareholder has signed a confidentiality agreement", correct:false,
     why:"No such factor was identified. The relationship of confidence is about trust and reliance, not a contract."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.007], fns 35–37", takeaway:"Coleman factors: dependence, confidence, significance, directors' active promotion." },

{ id:"c9x-009", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Brothers Arun (70%, the only active director) and Bala (30%, a teacher abroad who has never been involved in the company) own Opal Pte Ltd. Arun knows a buyer has offered to buy the whole company at three times book value. Without telling Bala, he offers to buy Bala's shares at book value, saying 'the business is struggling'. Bala accepts.",
  prompt:"Is Arun likely to owe Bala a fiduciary duty of disclosure?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: as in Brunninghausen, the close family relationship, Arun's position as sole effective director, and Bala's powerlessness support a fiduciary relationship for this transaction", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.008] describes Brunninghausen v Glavanics, on facts close to Percival v Wright, where the court found a fiduciary relationship between the defendant director and the only other shareholder in a share sale between them. The 'bare facts of the relationship' supported it: a close family association, the defendant's position as majority holder and sole effective director with obvious advantage in the transaction, and the plaintiff's lack of involvement, which left him 'almost totally powerless' to protect himself. Opal's facts mirror those closely. Arun also took positive action, misdescribing the business, which is a Coleman factor. The contrast with Tai Kim San is that Bala, unlike the sellers there, was not an active director and depended on Arun for information. Arun's statement may also found a claim in misrepresentation independently of any fiduciary duty."},
    {t:"No: Percival v Wright says directors never owe shareholders duties", correct:false,
     why:"Percival is the default. Brunninghausen and Coleman show special facts can displace it, and these facts are strongly special."},
    {t:"No: Bala could have checked the company's accounts", correct:false,
     why:"Bala's practical position is the point: he was uninvolved and dependent on Arun. Brunninghausen found a duty in just such circumstances."},
    {t:"Yes, because Arun owns more than 50%", correct:false,
     why:"Majority ownership alone does not create a fiduciary duty. It is the combination of factors, especially Bala's dependence and Arun's advantage, that matters."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.007]–[09.009]", takeaway:"Sole effective director buying out a dependent family member → likely fiduciary duty." },

{ id:"c9x-010", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Chen and Devi are both active directors and shareholders of Basil Pte Ltd, attend every board meeting and see all management accounts. After a dispute about strategy, Devi sells her shares to Chen. A year later Chen lists the company at a much higher valuation, having privately planned the listing at the time of the sale. Devi sues for non-disclosure.",
  prompt:"Following Tai Kim San v Lim Cher Kia, what is the likely outcome?",
  options:[
    {t:"No fiduciary duty: Devi was an active director, not dependent on Chen for information, so there is no basis for an exception to the general rule", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.009] describes Tai Kim San v Lim Cher Kia: after disagreeing about overseas expansion, the plaintiffs sold their shares to the defendant, unaware of his plan to take the company public. The court held there was no fiduciary relationship. It emphasised that the plaintiffs were themselves active directors, not dependent on the defendant for information on the company's performance or valuation, so they were not at any disadvantage relative to him. Devi's position is similar: she had full access to the company's information as a director. The general Percival v Wright rule therefore applies. The difference from Brunninghausen is the absence of dependence and powerlessness."},
    {t:"A fiduciary duty arises because Chen planned the listing", correct:false,
     why:"Tai Kim San involved exactly such an undisclosed listing plan, and no duty arose because the sellers were not dependent on the buyer."},
    {t:"A fiduciary duty arises because they were co-directors", correct:false,
     why:"Being co-directors does not make them fiduciaries to each other as shareholders. Their duties as directors are owed to the company."},
    {t:"The sale is void for non-disclosure", correct:false,
     why:"No duty to disclose arises on these facts, so non-disclosure cannot avoid the sale on fiduciary grounds."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.009], fn 47", takeaway:"Selling shareholder who is an informed active director → no special duty (Tai Kim San)." },

{ id:"cl9-011", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Directors hold inside information about plans for the company that would have changed some shareholders' decision to sell their shares. The directors had no dealings with those shareholders about the sale.",
  prompt:"What did the English Court of Appeal decide in Peskin v Anderson?",
  options:[
    {t:"No duty arose: there must be some dealing or involvement by the directors in the shareholders' affairs, and exclusive possession of inside information is not enough", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.010] stresses that for a fiduciary relationship to arise, there must be some dealing, communication or other relevant contact or involvement by the directors in relation to the shareholders' affairs. In Peskin v Anderson, the English Court of Appeal approved Coleman v Myers and Brunninghausen v Glavanics, but declined to impose a fiduciary obligation on directors to disclose information about contemplated plans that would have affected shareholders' own decisions to sell. The mere fact that the directors had exclusive possession of inside information was not enough to constitute the 'special facts' needed. In Coleman and Brunninghausen, by contrast, the directors were themselves on the other side of the share transaction or actively involved in it."},
    {t:"The directors had to disclose because they had inside information", correct:false,
     why:"Peskin held that exclusive possession of inside information is not sufficient on its own."},
    {t:"The court rejected Coleman v Myers", correct:false,
     why:"The court approved Coleman and Brunninghausen, but distinguished them because the directors had no dealings with the shareholders."},
    {t:"Directors always owe disclosure duties in share sales", correct:false,
     why:"There is no general duty. A special relationship based on dealings and dependence is needed."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.010], fns 48–50", takeaway:"Peskin: inside information alone isn't enough; directors must deal with or be involved in shareholders' affairs." },

{ id:"c9x-011", lesson:"9B", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Directors of Sage Ltd know a takeover approach is likely. Shareholder X, without contacting anyone at Sage, sells her shares on the market to an unconnected buyer. Shareholder Y sells his shares directly to the managing director, who approached him, after Y asked the MD whether there was any news that might affect the price; the MD said 'nothing at all'.",
  prompt:"Applying Peskin and Coleman, which shareholder has the stronger basis for a fiduciary claim against the directors?",
  options:[
    {t:"Y: the MD dealt directly with him in the transaction and he relied on the MD for information; X had no dealings with the directors at all", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.010] says a fiduciary relationship requires some dealing, communication or involvement by the directors in the shareholders' affairs; possession of inside information alone is not enough (Peskin v Anderson). X sold to a stranger without any contact with the directors, so she is in the Peskin position, and no duty arises. Y's case looks more like Coleman v Myers ([09.007]): the MD initiated the purchase, was on the other side of the transaction, and Y depended on him for information and asked directly. Those are indicative factors of a fiduciary relationship. The MD's false answer may also give Y a claim in misrepresentation, independently of any fiduciary duty."},
    {t:"X, because she sold first", correct:false,
     why:"Timing is irrelevant. X had no dealings with the directors, which is fatal to a fiduciary claim under Peskin."},
    {t:"Both equally, because the directors had inside information about both sales", correct:false,
     why:"Peskin holds that inside information alone does not create the relationship. The difference lies in the directors' dealings with Y."},
    {t:"Neither, because Percival v Wright bars all such claims", correct:false,
     why:"Percival is a default. Y's facts contain the special features that can displace it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.007], [09.010]", takeaway:"No dealings → no duty (Peskin); direct dealing + reliance → possible duty (Coleman)." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9C.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9C De Facto, Shadow Directors & Officers (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.011]–[09.014], fns 51–79. Replaces the 9C questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-012", lesson:"9C", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each category to its description.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"De jure director", r:"Properly appointed to the board"},
    {l:"De facto director", r:"Acts as a director without being appointed"},
    {l:"Shadow director", r:"Directs or instructs the board without a seat"},
    {l:"Officer", r:"Includes directors, secretaries and executive employees"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.011] recalls that the s 4 definition of 'director' goes beyond formally appointed (de jure) directors. Anyone who acts as a director without appointment falls within it as a de facto director. The definition also captures shadow directors: persons who exert influence on the de jure board, without board seats, by directing or instructing it. Koh [09.012] contrasts 'director' with 'officer', which s 4 defines to include directors, secretaries and 'a person employed in an executive capacity by the corporation'. The distinction matters because s 157(1) (honesty and reasonable diligence) applies only to 'directors', while s 157(2) (improper use of position or information) applies to the wider class of 'officers'.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.011]–[09.012]", takeaway:"De jure · de facto · shadow are directors; officers is wider." },

{ id:"c9x-012", lesson:"9C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Do de facto and shadow directors owe directors' duties?",
  options:[
    {t:"The statutory duties necessarily apply to them; whether general law duties apply is less clear, especially for shadow directors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.011] says that because de facto and shadow directors fall within the statutory definition of 'director', the statutorily imposed duties necessarily apply to them (subject to the provision-by-provision approach noted in Heap Huat, fn 52). Whether general law duties also apply is unclear. That would not matter much if s 157(1) mirrored the general law exactly, but Koh doubts it does, so the question has some significance. Before UK codification, authority distinguished the two: de facto directors are subject to general law fiduciary duties because they assume the status and functions of directors, but it was doubted whether shadow directors should be treated the same way (Ultraframe v Fielding)."},
    {t:"Neither owes any duty because neither was formally appointed", correct:false,
     why:"The extended statutory definition exists precisely to stop people escaping duties by avoiding formal appointment ([08.067])."},
    {t:"Both owe exactly the same general law duties as de jure directors in every case", correct:false,
     why:"De facto directors probably do, but whether shadow directors owe the full range of general law fiduciary duties is doubtful and may depend on their role."},
    {t:"Only shadow directors owe duties, because they control the board", correct:false,
     why:"De facto directors clearly owe duties. If anything, the uncertainty concerns shadow directors' general law duties."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.011], fns 52–55", takeaway:"Statutory duties apply to de facto and shadow directors; general law duties to shadows are uncertain." },

{ id:"cl9-013", lesson:"9C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Lewison J say in Ultraframe (UK) v Fielding about shadow directors and fiduciary duties?",
  options:[
    {t:"It would be odd to treat someone whose instructions may be inimical to the company as having undertaken a duty of loyalty to it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.011] quotes Lewison J: the instructions a shadow director gives, which the de jure directors act on, may be quite inimical to the company's interests. It would be odd if a person 'who has no direct relationship with the company and who consistently gives instructions inimical to its interests' were held to have undertaken a duty of loyalty to the company and agreed to subordinate his own interests to its interests. He added that the wider the statutory definition is read, the harder it becomes to impose the full range of fiduciary duties on everyone within it. The reasoning rests on the idea that fiduciary duties flow from an undertaking to act for another, which a shadow director may never have given."},
    {t:"Shadow directors owe exactly the same duties as de jure directors", correct:false,
     why:"Lewison J expressed the opposite view, doubting that shadow directors automatically owe full fiduciary duties."},
    {t:"Shadow directors owe no statutory duties", correct:false,
     why:"Lewison J's point concerned general law fiduciary duties. Statutory duties attach through the statutory definition."},
    {t:"De facto directors owe no fiduciary duties", correct:false,
     why:"De facto directors are subject to general law fiduciary duties because they assume directors' status and functions. Lewison J's doubt was about shadow directors."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.011]", takeaway:"Ultraframe: a shadow director may never have undertaken loyalty." },

{ id:"c9x-013", lesson:"9C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is Koh's assessment of Lewison J's view on shadow directors' fiduciary duties?",
  options:[
    {t:"It has been criticised, since a person exerting real influence should not escape by staying behind the scenes; but because shadow directorship is so wide, caution is justified, and the answer may depend on the person's role", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.011] notes that Lewison J's view has been criticised (Vivendi v Richards). If the shadow director is the very person exerting real influence over how the board uses its powers, it seems strange that he should escape fiduciary strictures simply by keeping out of sight. But Koh also points out how wide shadow directorship is: a person may be a shadow director even if he did not intend, expect or understand himself to be influencing the board, communications may take the form of advice, and the board need not feel any pressure to follow them (Deverell; Raffles Town Club). In that light there is good reason for caution. Koh suggests the true rule may be that there is no general proposition: whether general law duties apply depends on the alleged shadow director's particular role in the company's affairs."},
    {t:"Lewison J was clearly right: shadow directors never owe fiduciary duties", correct:false,
     why:"Koh does not endorse an absolute rule. She notes the criticism and proposes a role-based approach."},
    {t:"Lewison J was clearly wrong: all shadow directors owe full fiduciary duties", correct:false,
     why:"Koh accepts there is good reason for caution, given how wide the concept of shadow director is."},
    {t:"The issue is irrelevant because s 157 exactly mirrors the general law", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says there may be good reason to doubt that s 157 and the general law coincide exactly, which is why the issue matters."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.011], fns 56–58", takeaway:"Shadow directors' general law duties: no general rule; depends on role." },

{ id:"c9x-014", lesson:"9C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Kit, a consultant, occasionally emails Moss Ltd's board with industry advice. Without Kit intending or realising it, the board has come to follow her suggestions on product pricing as a matter of course. Kit uses information she learned from the board to trade on her own account.",
  prompt:"Why might a court hesitate to impose the full range of general law fiduciary duties on Kit, even if she is technically a shadow director?",
  options:[
    {t:"Shadow directorship can arise without intention or awareness and through mere advice, so she may never have undertaken loyalty to the company; the answer may depend on her actual role", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.011] explains that a person may be considered a shadow director 'even where he did not intend, expect, or understand himself to be exerting any influence on the board', that the communications can be advice, and that the board need not feel any pressure to follow them. Kit fits that description. Fiduciary duties rest on an undertaking to act in another's interests ([09.002]), and Kit may never have made such an undertaking. That is why Koh sees good reason for Lewison J's caution and suggests the imposition of general law duties depends on the person's particular role. Kit's use of information may still be caught on other bases, such as breach of confidence, insider trading law, or statutory duties attaching to directors under the extended definition."},
    {t:"Because shadow directors cannot be sued", correct:false,
     why:"Shadow directors are directors under the Act and can be held to statutory duties. The hesitation concerns general law fiduciary duties."},
    {t:"Because Kit is a professional adviser, which automatically excludes all duties", correct:false,
     why:"Section 4(2) prevents a professional adviser being a shadow director merely because the board acts on professional advice. It does not exclude all duties in every case, and Kit's pricing suggestions may not be professional advice in that sense."},
    {t:"Because the board did not feel compelled to follow her", correct:false,
     why:"Compulsion is not required for shadow directorship ([08.065]). The hesitation stems from the absence of any undertaking of loyalty."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.011]", takeaway:"Wide shadow-director concept → caution before imposing full fiduciary duties." },

{ id:"c9x-015", lesson:"9C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which part of s 157 applies to the wider class of 'officers', not just directors?",
  options:[
    {t:"Section 157(2), the prohibition on improper use of position or information", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.012] notes that the general duties in s 157(1), to act honestly and use reasonable diligence, apply only to 'directors'. By contrast, the proscription in s 157(2), against an officer or agent making improper use of his position or of information acquired by virtue of it to gain an advantage or cause detriment to the company, applies to 'officers', which s 4 defines to include directors, secretaries and persons employed in an executive capacity. So a non-director executive or company secretary can breach s 157(2) without being a director at all, while s 157(1) requires the executive to be found to occupy the position of director first."},
    {t:"Section 157(1), the duties of honesty and diligence", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(1) applies only to directors."},
    {t:"Section 157(4), which preserves the general law", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(4) is a saving provision, not a duty applying to officers."},
    {t:"None: s 157 applies only to directors", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(2) expressly extends to officers."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.012], fn 60", takeaway:"s 157(1): directors only; s 157(2): officers too." },

{ id:"cl9-014", lesson:"9C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A senior executive who is not a director is subject to the duties in s 157(1).",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.012] says s 157(1) applies only to 'directors'. In large companies, day-to-day control often lies with senior executives who are not board members, and given their real control it might seem appropriate to subject them to the same duties. Australia does this: its Corporations Act extends the duties of good faith (s 181) and care and diligence to 'officers' with capacity to affect the company's business or finances. But under the Singapore Act, an executive must be found to 'occupy the position of director', and so be a de facto director, before s 157(1) applies to him. Without that finding, he is subject only to s 157(2) as an officer, and to any fiduciary or contractual duties arising from his employment ([09.013]–[09.014])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"That is the Australian approach. In Singapore, s 157(1) reaches an executive only if he is a de facto director."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.012], fns 61–64", takeaway:"Singapore: executives get s 157(1) only as de facto directors (cf Australia)." },

{ id:"c9x-016", lesson:"9C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: At common law, an employment relationship is typically fiduciary, so every senior employee owes single-minded loyalty to the employer.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.013] says the employment relationship, governed by contract, is not typically fiduciary, and an executive is not a fiduciary merely by being employed (Nottingham University v Fishel). The reason is that the employer can stipulate by contract the nature and scope of the employee's powers and generally retains extensive ability to supervise him, so there is 'no compelling reason for Equity to protect him by imposing a general obligation'. Fiduciary duties can still arise from employment. The crucial question is whether the employee's undertaking obliges him to act solely in the company's interests, which is a factual inquiry."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the default. Employment is not typically fiduciary; fiduciary duties arise only where the employee's undertaking obliges him to act solely in the employer's interests."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.013], fns 65–67", takeaway:"Employment isn't fiduciary by default; employers can protect themselves by contract and supervision." },

{ id:"cl9-015", lesson:"9C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's executive vice-president, not on the board, has been given broad initiatives and responsibilities well beyond following instructions. He diverts a business lead to himself.",
  prompt:"How does the court decide whether he owed fiduciary duties?",
  options:[
    {t:"By asking whether his undertaking obliged him to act solely in the company's interests, a factual inquiry weighing seniority and authority", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.013] says fiduciary duties can arise from employment, and the crucial question is whether the employee's undertaking places him in a position obliging him to act solely in the company's interests, the essence of fiduciary loyalty. The inquiry is factual and no single factor is decisive. Seniority matters, but there is no general test of how senior an employee must be. In Canadian Aero Services v O'Malley, an executive vice-president 'charged … with initiatives and with responsibilities far removed from the obedient role of servants' was a fiduciary. Authority to make decisions binding the company is also relevant (Smile Inc Dental v Lui), though not conclusive. On these facts, the EVP's broad initiative-taking role points towards fiduciary status, making the diverted lead a likely breach."},
    {t:"Employees are never fiduciaries", correct:false,
     why:"Employees are not fiduciaries by default, but they can be, as Canadian Aero shows."},
    {t:"Anyone with signing authority is a fiduciary", correct:false,
     why:"Authority to bind is relevant but not conclusive; otherwise practically every manager with signing authority would be a fiduciary (Nagase)."},
    {t:"Only his title matters", correct:false,
     why:"Titles are not decisive. A 'senior employee with the title of director' was held not to be a fiduciary in Fishel."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.013], fns 68–73", takeaway:"Employee fiduciary? Did the undertaking oblige him to act solely for the company?" },

{ id:"c9x-017", lesson:"9C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lena is a finance manager at Thyme Pte Ltd, in middle management though fairly senior, with authority to sign payments up to $100,000. She sets up a side business and diverts some of Thyme's supplier discounts to it. Thyme sues her for breach of fiduciary duty.",
  prompt:"What is the likely analysis of whether she is a fiduciary?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably not a fiduciary on those facts alone: middle management, even fairly senior, and signing authority are not enough, though she still owes contractual duties of good faith and fidelity", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.013] cites Nagase Singapore v Ching Kai Huat, where 'a member of the middle management of the [company] albeit [holding] a fairly senior position' was not a fiduciary, and notes that authority to make binding decisions is relevant but not conclusive, 'for otherwise, practically every middle-level manager and every person with some signing authority in a company's finance department would have fiduciary duties'. Lena's profile fits Nagase. But that does not leave Thyme without a remedy. Koh [09.014] says employees owe contractual obligations of good faith and fidelity, usually implied, which constrain how they exercise their powers. Diverting the employer's supplier discounts is very likely a breach of those contractual duties, and may also be a breach of s 157(2) if she is an 'officer'."},
    {t:"Definitely a fiduciary, because she can sign payments", correct:false,
     why:"Signing authority alone is expressly said not to be conclusive (Nagase)."},
    {t:"A fiduciary because she is senior", correct:false,
     why:"Seniority is relevant, but there is no general seniority threshold, and fairly senior middle managers have been held not to be fiduciaries."},
    {t:"Not a fiduciary, so Thyme has no claim at all", correct:false,
     why:"Thyme can rely on her contractual duties of good faith and fidelity even if she is not a fiduciary."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.013]–[09.014]", takeaway:"Middle managers with signing authority usually aren't fiduciaries, but owe contractual fidelity." },

{ id:"cl9-016", lesson:"9C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: An executive who is not a fiduciary still owes the company contractual duties of good faith, fidelity and reasonable care and skill.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.014] says an employee will in any case be subject to contractual obligations of good faith and fidelity, which if not express are usually implied into the employment contract. It is also an implied term that the employee will exercise reasonable care and skill in performing his duties. So whatever the position under the Companies Act, the company has contractual recourse against an executive who breaches these duties or falls below the expected standard of care and skill. The difference from fiduciary status lies mainly in the remedies (for example, an account of profits is characteristically a fiduciary remedy) and in the intensity of the obligation."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Non-fiduciary employees still owe these implied contractual duties."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.014], fns 74–79", takeaway:"Non-fiduciary executives still owe implied contractual good faith, fidelity, care and skill." },

{ id:"c9x-018", lesson:"9C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does an employee's contractual duty of good faith and fidelity differ from fiduciary loyalty?",
  options:[
    {t:"It constrains how the employee exercises his powers, but does not demand single-minded or exclusive loyalty to the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.014] says the contractual obligations of good faith and fidelity tend to 'shade into' fiduciary concepts, but are not in themselves necessarily fiduciary duties. They may constrain how the employee exercises the powers and discretion given to him, but they do not require him to have 'single minded or exclusive loyalty' to the company, as a fiduciary must (Nagase; Ranson v Customer Systems). So an ordinary employee may pursue his own interests in ways a fiduciary could not, such as preparing to compete after leaving, provided he does not breach his contractual duties while employed. A fiduciary, by contrast, must subordinate his interests to the company's within the scope of his duty."},
    {t:"There is no difference: they are the same duty", correct:false,
     why:"Koh is careful to separate them. The contractual duty does not demand exclusive loyalty."},
    {t:"The contractual duty is stricter than fiduciary loyalty", correct:false,
     why:"Fiduciary loyalty is the stricter standard: single-minded loyalty enforced by prophylactic rules."},
    {t:"The contractual duty applies only to directors", correct:false,
     why:"The implied contractual duty arises from employment, so it applies to employees, whether or not they are directors."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.014], fns 75–77", takeaway:"Contractual fidelity constrains; fiduciary loyalty demands exclusivity." }

]);

;
