{ id:"cl9-017", lesson:"9D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the most obvious consequence of codifying directors' duties in s 157?",
  options:[
    {t:"Breach can bring criminal sanctions and disqualification, while the general law duties still apply", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.016] says the most obvious implication of codification is that the consequences of breaching s 157 are more severe than at general law. The errant director may face criminal sanctions (s 157(3)(b)) and may be disqualified from acting as a director or taking part in management (s 154). Given the stigma of criminal prosecution and the impact of disqualification on livelihood, it matters to define the offence with some certainty. Koh adds that a director who escapes liability under s 157 may still be in breach of his general law duties, because s 157 does not replace them but expressly preserves them (s 157(4)). Koh [09.015] also places s 157 in context: the Act contains many targeted provisions, such as on loans (ss 162–163) and director benefits (ss 168–169), but s 157 is the closest it comes to codifying the general duties."},
    {t:"It replaces the general law duties", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(4) preserves the general law duties. They run concurrently with the statute."},
    {t:"It limits liability to civil damages", correct:false,
     why:"The reverse: s 157 adds criminal liability and the risk of disqualification to civil liability."},
    {t:"It allows shareholders to waive the duties by ordinary resolution", correct:false,
     why:"Koh suggests the codified duties are probably mandatory and cannot be excluded ([09.018]); whether they can be ratified is unclear."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.015]–[09.016], fns 84–85", takeaway:"s 157 breach → criminal liability + disqualification; general law still applies." },

{ id:"c9x-019", lesson:"9D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director is prosecuted under s 157(1) for failing to act honestly. The court acquits him because the prosecution cannot prove the necessary mental element. The company then sues him civilly for breach of his general law fiduciary duty on the same facts.",
  prompt:"Does the acquittal defeat the company's claim?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: s 157 does not replace the general law, so he may still be liable for breach of his general law duties", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.016] says a director who escapes liability under s 157 may nevertheless be in breach of his duties at general law, because s 157 expressly preserves their continued application (s 157(4)). The statutory and general law duties are separate sources of liability, with different elements and remedies. An acquittal shows only that the criminal charge under s 157 was not proved to the criminal standard. The company's civil claim is assessed on the civil standard, under the general law, which also contains objective elements in some contexts (9H). Koh [09.024] relies on exactly this point: if the statutory duty of honesty requires a mental element, s 157(4) still allows objective review through the general law."},
    {t:"Yes: an acquittal under s 157 bars any further proceedings", correct:false,
     why:"No such bar exists. The general law duties are preserved by s 157(4) and are enforced separately by the company."},
    {t:"Yes, because s 157 has replaced the general law duty of good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157 is a partial codification and does not replace the general law."},
    {t:"Only if the shareholders consent to the claim", correct:false,
     why:"The company's claim is brought through its proper organs in the usual way; shareholder consent is not a precondition."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.016], [09.024]", takeaway:"Escaping s 157 doesn't escape the general law." },

{ id:"cl9-018", lesson:"9D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is notable about the remedies in s 157(3)?",
  options:[
    {t:"An officer accounts for profit without needing a fiduciary relationship, and 'damage … as a result of the breach' may bring in common law causation, foreseeability and remoteness", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.017] notes that s 157(3) makes an 'officer' who breaches any provision of the section liable to the company 'for any profit made by him or for any damage suffered by the company as a result of the breach'. Historically, liability to account for profits is imposed by equity and usually depends on a trust or fiduciary relationship (Sinclair Investments v Versailles). Under s 157(3), the liability to account appears to arise independently of any such relationship. That means the section imposes fiduciary-like obligations on employees who may not be fiduciaries at general law. The liability for 'damage suffered ... as a result of the breach' may have been worded deliberately to subject the compensation claim to common law principles, so causation, foreseeability and remoteness would limit recovery."},
    {t:"It allows only an account of profits", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(3) provides both for profits made and for damage suffered."},
    {t:"It applies only to directors", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(3) refers to 'officers', so it reaches beyond directors, although s 157(1) itself applies only to directors (fn 87)."},
    {t:"It requires proof of dishonesty", correct:false,
     why:"Liability under s 157(3) follows breach of any provision of s 157, including the objective duty of reasonable diligence."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.017], fns 86–88", takeaway:"s 157(3): profits without fiduciary status; damages on common law principles." },

{ id:"c9x-020", lesson:"9D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Dev, a regional sales executive of Clove Ltd, is not a director and is held not to be a fiduciary at general law. He uses Clove's customer pricing data, acquired through his job, to win contracts for his brother's firm, earning a $200,000 commission. This breaches s 157(2).",
  prompt:"What does s 157(3) add to Clove's position?",
  options:[
    {t:"Clove can recover the $200,000 profit from Dev as an officer, even though no fiduciary relationship exists at general law", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.017] says one implication of codification is that s 157 extends certain duties to 'officers', imposing fiduciary-like obligations on employees who may not be fiduciaries at general law. Section 157(3) makes an officer who breaches the section liable to the company for any profit made by him. That liability to account would ordinarily depend on a fiduciary relationship, but under s 157(3) it appears to arise independently of one. Dev is an officer (a person employed in an executive capacity), he breached s 157(2) by improperly using information acquired by virtue of his position, and he made a profit. So Clove can claim the $200,000 under the statute, without proving he was a fiduciary. Clove could also claim for damage suffered, subject to common law causation and remoteness."},
    {t:"Nothing: without fiduciary status, Clove cannot claim his profit", correct:false,
     why:"That would be the general law position. Section 157(3) provides a statutory profit remedy against officers that does not depend on fiduciary status."},
    {t:"Clove can claim only if it shows Dev was a de facto director", correct:false,
     why:"Being a de facto director matters for s 157(1). Section 157(2) and (3) apply to officers, which includes executives."},
    {t:"Clove can claim only damages, not profit", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(3) expressly covers 'any profit made by him'."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.017]", takeaway:"s 157(3) gives profit recovery against non-fiduciary officers." },

{ id:"c9x-021", lesson:"9D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh suggests the wording 'damage suffered by the company as a result of the breach' in s 157(3) may have been chosen deliberately. What would follow?",
  options:[
    {t:"The compensation claim would follow common law principles, so causation, foreseeability and remoteness would limit recovery", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.017] says the statutory liability for 'damage suffered by the company as a result of the breach' may be construed as a deliberate legislative choice to subject the compensation claim to common law, not equitable, principles. The practical significance is that the limiting common law concepts of causation, foreseeability and remoteness would be relevant in assessing recovery for loss. Equitable compensation for breach of fiduciary duty is often thought to be more generous to the claimant in these respects. So a company claiming loss under s 157(3) may face a more demanding test than a company claiming equitable compensation under the general law, which is one reason claimants plead both."},
    {t:"The company could recover any loss whatever, without proving causation", correct:false,
     why:"The phrase 'as a result of the breach' points towards causation being required, not dispensed with."},
    {t:"Only equitable principles would apply", correct:false,
     why:"Koh's suggestion is the opposite: the wording may signal common law, not equitable, principles."},
    {t:"Only nominal damages would be available", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing limits recovery to nominal damages. The point is that common law limits on recovery would apply."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.017]", takeaway:"s 157(3) damages: likely common law causation, foreseeability, remoteness." },

{ id:"c9x-022", lesson:"9D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Koh says the codified duties are apparently mandatory and cannot be excluded. What possible implications does she identify?",
  options:[
    {t:"They might be public duties, possibly giving persons other than the company standing to enforce them, and it is unclear whether ratification is available", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.018] says general law duties that have been codified appear to become mandatory and cannot be excluded; fn 89 also notes s 172, which nullifies provisions exempting officers from liability for breach of duty. If so, the codified duties may properly be characterised as public duties, but the implications are unclear. First, persons other than the company might have standing to enforce them; Koh points to ss 409 and 409A of the Act. Second, it is unclear how ratification interacts with them. At common law the company, as beneficiary of the duties, can usually forgive or ratify a breach. Whether that holds for a breach of the statutory duty is uncertain, though their mandatory nature suggests not, and Australian authority (Angas Law Services v Carabelas) suggests a breach of statutory duty cannot be ratified."},
    {t:"They can be excluded by the constitution if members agree", correct:false,
     why:"Koh suggests the opposite: codified duties appear mandatory, and s 172 nullifies exemption provisions."},
    {t:"Only the Registrar can enforce them", correct:false,
     why:"The company enforces them in the ordinary way. The open question is whether others may also have standing."},
    {t:"They apply only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157 applies to directors and officers of companies generally."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.018], fns 89–93", takeaway:"Codified duties: mandatory, maybe public; standing and ratification unclear." },

{ id:"cl9-019", lesson:"9D", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Australian authority suggests that a breach of the statutory duties can be ratified by the company.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.018] says Singapore courts have yet to consider the point, but there is Australian authority suggesting that it is not permissible to ratify a breach of statutory duty (Angas Law Services v Carabelas (2005)). This contrasts with the common law, where the company, as beneficiary of the duties, can usually forgive or ratify a director's breach. The mandatory nature of the statutory duties also suggests ratification is not available. So a director who breaches s 157 should not assume that a members' resolution after the event will cure the statutory breach, even if it might affect the company's general law claim."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The Australian authority Koh cites points the other way: a breach of statutory duty cannot be ratified."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.018], fn 93", takeaway:"Australia: statutory breaches can't be ratified; Singapore undecided." },

{ id:"c9x-023", lesson:"9D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director of Mint Ltd caused the company to enter into a transaction in breach of s 157(1). After the event, the members, with full knowledge, pass a resolution 'ratifying and forgiving' the breach. The director is later prosecuted.",
  prompt:"What is the likely significance of the members' resolution for the statutory breach?",
  options:[
    {t:"Doubtful: the statutory duty appears mandatory, and Australian authority suggests a breach of statutory duty cannot be ratified", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.018] explains that at common law the company, as beneficiary of the duties, can usually ratify a breach after the event. But it is not clear whether this is still so for a breach of the statutory duty, and the mandatory nature of the codified duty suggests not. Singapore courts have not yet decided the point, but Angas Law Services v Carabelas suggests ratification of a statutory breach is not permissible. The prosecution is brought by the state for a criminal offence, so a members' resolution forgiving the breach is unlikely to answer the charge. The resolution may still be relevant to the company's own general law claim against the director, subject to the limits on ratification considered in ch 10."},
    {t:"Decisive: ratification always cures a breach of duty", correct:false,
     why:"That is the common law position for general law duties. Whether it applies to statutory duties is doubtful."},
    {t:"Decisive, if passed by special resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The type of resolution does not resolve the doubt about whether a statutory breach can be ratified at all."},
    {t:"Irrelevant, because members can never ratify any breach", correct:false,
     why:"Members can usually ratify breaches of general law duties. The doubt concerns statutory duties."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.018]", takeaway:"After-the-event ratification of a s 157 breach is doubtful." },

{ id:"cl9-020", lesson:"9D", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does ex ante authorisation differ from ratification?",
  options:[
    {t:"Prior informed consent means no breach occurs at all; ratification condones a breach after the event", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.019] says that at common law, ratification also covers advance (ex ante) authorisation of an act that would otherwise be a breach of duty. When a director seeks the company's fully informed consent before embarking on a venture involving, say, a conflict of interest or substantial risk, he is obtaining authority to act. There is 'a clear difference, which is real and not semantic' (Sealy) between prior authorisation and affirming or condoning wrongful conduct after it has happened. With prior authorisation, there is no breach of duty at all. Whether this concept also defines when a breach of the statutory duty occurs depends on how s 157 is characterised, in particular whether it prescribes a norm of conduct, a question described as 'high-level theory' that Koh suggests legislation is best placed to resolve."},
    {t:"There is no difference", correct:false,
     why:"Koh quotes Sealy that the difference is 'real and not semantic'."},
    {t:"Ratification must come before the act", correct:false,
     why:"Ratification in the narrow sense follows the act. Authorisation comes before it."},
    {t:"Authorisation can only be given by the court", correct:false,
     why:"Authorisation is given by the company, through fully informed consent."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.019], fns 94–97", takeaway:"Prior informed consent → no breach; ratification → breach forgiven after the fact." },

{ id:"c9x-024", lesson:"9D", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before Mint Ltd's director invests company money in a high-risk venture in which his cousin has an interest, he discloses all the facts to the company and obtains its fully informed consent. The venture fails.",
  prompt:"At general law, what is the effect of the prior consent, and what remains uncertain?",
  options:[
    {t:"At general law there is no breach, because he acted with authority; whether the same applies to the statutory duty depends on how s 157 is characterised", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.019] says advance authorisation, obtained through the company's fully informed consent before embarking on a venture involving a conflict or substantial risk, gives the director the authority to act, so there is no breach of duty at all. That is different from ratification after the event. The director here disclosed fully and obtained consent before acting, so at general law he did not breach his duty. The failure of the venture does not change that. What remains uncertain is whether prior authorisation also determines whether a breach of the statutory duty has occurred. That depends on whether s 157 is meant to prescribe a mandatory norm of conduct, which Koh describes as 'high-level theory' best resolved by legislation."},
    {t:"There is a breach, because the venture failed", correct:false,
     why:"Loss does not by itself establish a breach. With fully informed prior consent, the director was authorised."},
    {t:"There is a breach, because consent can only be given after the event", correct:false,
     why:"Consent before the event is precisely what gives authority. After-the-event consent is ratification."},
    {t:"There is no breach under either regime, as settled law", correct:false,
     why:"The general law position is clear, but the statutory position is uncertain."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.019]", takeaway:"Prior informed consent prevents breach at general law; statutory effect uncertain." }

