/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9F s 157(1): Reasonable Diligence (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.028]–[09.031], fns 138–147. Replaces the 9F questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-026", lesson:"9F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the chapter infer that the Legislature meant to codify only diligence, not skill?",
  options:[
    {t:"Re City Equitable described the common law duty as requiring both skill and diligence, yet s 157(1) mentions only 'reasonable diligence'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.028] notes that s 157(1) requires a director to 'use reasonable diligence in the discharge of the duties of his office', and that this has generally been assumed to be the statutory version of the common law duty of care. But Romer J in Re City Equitable Fire Insurance Co, the classic exposition of the common law duty, described it as requiring a director to exercise 'some degree of both skill and diligence'. If the Legislature had meant to codify that composite duty, it could simply have used the word 'care'. Omitting any reference to 'skill' supports the compelling inference that it meant to codify only diligence, to a reasonable degree (Byrne v Baker, on an identical Victorian provision). Koh then notes that Singapore courts have nonetheless read skill into the statutory duty ([09.029])."},
    {t:"Because the section uses the word 'care'", correct:false,
     why:"The section does not use 'care'. That is the point: it uses only 'reasonable diligence'."},
    {t:"Because the courts have refused to read in skill", correct:false,
     why:"The courts have done the opposite: they have interpreted reasonable diligence as including skill."},
    {t:"Because skill is covered by s 157(2)", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(2) concerns improper use of position or information, not skill."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.028], fns 139–141", takeaway:"s 157(1) says 'diligence', not 'care'; textually skill is omitted." },

{ id:"c9x-034", lesson:"9F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How have Singapore courts actually interpreted the statutory duty of reasonable diligence, and what test follows?",
  options:[
    {t:"As incorporating skill as well; the test is whether the director exercised the skill and diligence of a reasonable director in his position", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.029] says that, whatever the textual inference, Singapore courts have interpreted the statutory duty of reasonable diligence as incorporating the duty of skill. The test is therefore whether the director exercised the same degree of skill and diligence as a reasonable director in his position (Lim Weng Kee). There is no single fixed standard. The precise standard depends on factors including the director's position and role in the company, the size and business of the company, and the nature of the act or decision in question. So a non-executive director of a small family company and the finance director of a listed bank are both judged against a reasonable director, but the reasonable director in each position looks quite different."},
    {t:"As diligence only, ignoring skill entirely", correct:false,
     why:"That is the textual inference Koh draws, but it is not how the courts have read the section."},
    {t:"As requiring the director to meet the highest standard in the industry", correct:false,
     why:"The test is a reasonable director in his position, not the industry's best."},
    {t:"As a purely subjective test of the director's own abilities", correct:false,
     why:"The test is objective. The director's personal shortcomings do not lower it ([09.029])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.029], fns 142–143", takeaway:"Courts read in skill: reasonable director in his position." },

{ id:"cl9-027", lesson:"9F", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Under s 157(1), the standard of diligence is lowered for a director with little knowledge or experience.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.029] says that in Lim Weng Kee the court noted the applicable standard 'will not be lowered to accommodate any inadequacies in the individual's knowledge or experience'. The yardstick is objective, and the director's personal attributes do not help absolve him from liability, civil or criminal. The standard works in one direction only: it will be raised if the director possesses, or holds himself out as possessing, special qualifications, knowledge or experience. So inexperience is no excuse, while expertise raises the bar."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Lim Weng Kee rules this out. Inexperience does not lower the objective standard; only special skill or experience changes it, upwards."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.029], fn 144", takeaway:"Standard never lowered for inadequacy; raised for special expertise." },

{ id:"c9x-035", lesson:"9F", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each factor to its effect on the s 157(1) diligence standard.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Director's lack of experience", r:"Does not lower the standard"},
    {l:"Director's special qualifications or holding out", r:"Raises the standard"},
    {l:"Director's position and role", r:"Shapes what a reasonable director would do"},
    {l:"Size and business of the company", r:"Shapes what a reasonable director would do"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.029] says there is no single fixed standard; the precise standard for a particular director depends on factors including his position and role in the company, the size and business of the company, and the nature of the act or decision in question. Those factors define who the 'reasonable director in his position' is. Personal inadequacy works differently: under Lim Weng Kee the standard 'will not be lowered to accommodate any inadequacies in the individual's knowledge or experience'. But the standard will be raised if the director has, or holds himself out as having, special qualifications, knowledge or experience. So an accountant on an audit committee, or a 20-year industry veteran, is judged against what a reasonable director with that expertise would do.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.029]", takeaway:"Role, size, business shape the standard; inadequacy doesn't lower it; expertise raises it." },

{ id:"c9x-036", lesson:"9F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Wei Ling, a chartered accountant, is a non-executive director of Pine Ltd and chairs its audit committee. She approves the annual accounts without questioning a large, unexplained receivable from a related party, which turns out to be fictitious. She says a lay director would not have noticed it either.",
  prompt:"How is her conduct likely to be assessed under s 157(1)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Against the higher standard of a reasonable director with her accounting qualifications and audit committee role; the lay-director comparison does not help her", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.029] says the test is objective, whether the director exercised the skill and diligence of a reasonable director in his position, and that the standard will be raised if the director possesses, or holds herself out as possessing, special qualifications, knowledge or experience. Wei Ling is a chartered accountant chairing the audit committee, a role that exists precisely to scrutinise the accounts. A reasonable director with her qualifications in that role would be expected to question a large unexplained related-party receivable. Comparing herself to a lay director misses the point: the standard is set by her position and her expertise. If she falls short, she may be in breach of s 157(1), and no mens rea is required for that limb ([09.031])."},
    {t:"Against the standard of an ordinary lay director, since she is non-executive", correct:false,
     why:"Being non-executive is part of her role, but her special qualifications and audit committee position raise the standard."},
    {t:"Leniently, since she acted honestly", correct:false,
     why:"Honesty is a separate limb. Diligence is objective, so honesty does not excuse a failure of care."},
    {t:"She cannot be liable unless she intended the fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Diligence requires no mens rea. Intent is relevant to honesty, not diligence."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.029], [09.031]", takeaway:"Special qualifications + role raise the diligence standard." },

{ id:"cl9-028", lesson:"9F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The managing director of three pawnshop companies lets valuable pledged items be released before the redemption cheques clear. The cheques bounce and the companies lose heavily. He has run the business for 20 years.",
  prompt:"What happened in Lim Weng Kee v Public Prosecutor?",
  options:[
    {t:"His conviction under s 157 was upheld: his 20 years' experience raised the standard, and no reasonable managing director with that experience would have acted so", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.030] describes Lim Weng Kee as a rare criminal prosecution under s 157. Lim, managing director of three pawnshop companies, allowed valuable pawned items to be released before the cheques presented to redeem them had cleared. The cheques bounced and the companies suffered substantial losses. In upholding the conviction, the court took account of Lim's 20 years running the business, which counted as 'special experience' that raised the expected standard. No reasonable managing director in his position, with his experience, would have done what he did, so his conduct fell short and he breached the statutory duty. The case shows the objective standard working upwards, and that the diligence limb can found a criminal conviction without proof of dishonesty."},
    {t:"He was acquitted because he acted honestly", correct:false,
     why:"Honesty is not a defence to breach of the diligence limb, which is objective. His conviction was upheld."},
    {t:"He was acquitted because prosecution needed proof of intent", correct:false,
     why:"The diligence limb requires no mens rea ([09.031]). The conviction stood."},
    {t:"His experience lowered the expected standard", correct:false,
     why:"His experience raised the standard. Experience never lowers it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.030], fn 146", takeaway:"Lim Weng Kee: 20 years' experience raised the standard; conviction upheld." },

{ id:"c9x-037", lesson:"9F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Hassan, aged 23, is appointed a director of his family's construction company with no business experience. He signs off on progress payments to a subcontractor without checking whether any work was done. The subcontractor disappears with $2m. Hassan says he did not know directors had to check such things.",
  prompt:"Is his inexperience a defence under s 157(1)?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the standard is not lowered for inadequacies in knowledge or experience, so he is judged against a reasonable director in his position", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.029] says the standard of reasonable diligence 'will not be lowered to accommodate any inadequacies in the individual's knowledge or experience' (Lim Weng Kee). The yardstick is objective, and personal attributes do not absolve a director from civil or criminal liability. A reasonable director authorising payments in a construction company would take basic steps to confirm that work had been done. Hassan's youth and inexperience do not change what that reasonable director would do. If he accepts a directorship, he takes on the standard that goes with it. His ignorance of what directors must do is not a defence."},
    {t:"Yes: the standard is subjective and adjusted for his abilities", correct:false,
     why:"The standard is objective. It is never adjusted downwards for personal shortcomings."},
    {t:"Yes, because family companies are exempt", correct:false,
     why:"No exemption exists for family companies. The size and business of the company shape the standard but do not remove it."},
    {t:"Yes, if he acted honestly", correct:false,
     why:"Honesty answers the honesty limb, not the diligence limb."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.029]", takeaway:"Inexperience is no defence to the diligence duty." },

{ id:"cl9-029", lesson:"9F", type:"fill",
  prompt:"By pegging the duty to a 'reasonable' standard, the legislation displaces the usual requirement for ____ in the duty of reasonable diligence.",
  accept:["mens rea","mensrea","guilty mind","a guilty mind"], answer:"mens rea",
  why:"Koh [09.031] says that by pegging the duty to a 'reasonable' standard, the legislation appears to have expressly displaced the traditional criminal law requirement for mens rea. So, quite unlike the duty of honesty (where Koh argues a mental element is required, [09.023]–[09.024]), there is no mens rea requirement for the duty to exercise reasonable diligence (Lim Weng Kee). A director can therefore be criminally liable under s 157 for a careless failure that falls below the reasonable director's standard, without any guilty intention.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.031], fn 147", takeaway:"Diligence limb: no mens rea; honesty limb: mens rea (Koh's view)." },

{ id:"c9x-038", lesson:"9F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Compare the two limbs of s 157(1). Which statement is accurate?",
  options:[
    {t:"Honesty arguably requires a mental element; diligence is objective with no mens rea, and the standard is never lowered for personal inadequacy", correct:true,
     why:"Koh treats the two limbs very differently. For honesty, she argues a mental element (Marchesi: consciousness that the act is not in the company's interests and deliberate disregard) should be required for criminal and probably civil liability, with objective review supplied by the general law through s 157(4) ([09.022]–[09.024]). For diligence, the 'reasonable' standard expressly displaces mens rea ([09.031]). The test is whether the director exercised the skill and diligence of a reasonable director in his position, raised for special expertise and never lowered for inadequacy ([09.029]). So an honest but careless director may breach diligence, and a careful but dishonest one may breach honesty."},
    {t:"Both limbs require mens rea", correct:false,
     why:"The diligence limb requires no mens rea ([09.031])."},
    {t:"Both limbs are purely subjective", correct:false,
     why:"Diligence is objective. Honesty is essentially subjective but has objective elements in the general law."},
    {t:"Diligence requires mens rea, honesty does not", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses Koh's analysis."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.022]–[09.024], [09.029]–[09.031]", takeaway:"Honesty: mental element; diligence: objective, no mens rea." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9G.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9G s 157(2): Improper Use (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.032]–[09.038], fns 148–169. Replaces the 9G questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-030", lesson:"9G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does s 157(2) prohibit?",
  options:[
    {t:"An officer making improper use of his position, or of information acquired through it, to gain an advantage for himself or another or to cause detriment to the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.032] says s 157(2) prohibits an 'officer' from making improper use of 'his position as an officer' or of 'any information acquired by virtue of his position as officer' either to gain an advantage for himself or another person, or to cause detriment to the company. 'Officer' includes directors, the company secretary and a person employed in an executive position, and fn 149 adds that the subsection also applies to 'agents', including present and former bankers, solicitors and auditors. Local authority on s 157(2) is scant, but it derives from s 124 of the Australian Uniform Companies Act 1961, so Australian decisions on that section and its successors are instructive. The elements are: (a) use of position or information, (b) impropriety, and (c) the purpose of gaining an advantage or causing detriment."},
    {t:"Any director competing with the company", correct:false,
     why:"Competition is not prohibited as such by s 157(2). Liability needs improper use of position or information for the prohibited purpose."},
    {t:"Directors holding shares in competitors", correct:false,
     why:"Holding shares in a competitor is not itself prohibited by s 157(2); it may raise conflict issues under the general law and s 156."},
    {t:"Negligent use of company information", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(2) requires a purposive element, an intention to gain an advantage or cause detriment (Chew v R). Negligence belongs to the diligence limb of s 157(1)."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.032], fns 148–154", takeaway:"s 157(2): improper use of position/information to gain advantage or cause detriment." },

{ id:"c9x-039", lesson:"9G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Who can be liable under s 157(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Officers (directors, secretaries, executive employees), including former officers, and agents such as bankers, solicitors and auditors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.032] says s 157(2) applies to 'officers', defined to include directors, the company secretary and 'a person employed in an executive position'. Section 157(5) extends 'officer' to 'a person who at any time has been an officer of the company', so former officers are covered. Footnote 149 notes that s 157(2) also applies to 'agents', which include present and former bankers, solicitors and auditors of the company. This makes s 157(2) much wider in reach than s 157(1), which applies only to directors. It is one way the statute imposes fiduciary-like obligations on persons who might not be fiduciaries at general law ([09.017])."},
    {t:"Only directors currently in office", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(2) covers officers and agents, and s 157(5) brings in former officers."},
    {t:"Only shareholders who control the company", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders as such are not within s 157(2)."},
    {t:"Only executive directors", correct:false,
     why:"It applies to all directors, secretaries, executive employees and agents."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.032], fns 149–151", takeaway:"s 157(2) reaches officers, former officers and agents." },

{ id:"cl9-031", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director resigns. Six months later, he uses information he learned on the board to the company's detriment.",
  prompt:"Can he be liable under s 157(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: s 157(5) extends 'officer' to former officers, and misuse of information acquired by virtue of his position can be caught even after he leaves", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.032] says s 157(5) provides that 'officer' includes, for the purposes of the section, 'a person who at any time has been an officer of the company'. Where the wrong is improper use of position, it must have been committed while he was still a director, but he cannot escape liability for that by resigning. Where the wrong is improper use of information, it appears that as long as the information was 'acquired by virtue of his position as officer', he may be liable even if the misuse occurred after he resigned. Here the information was learned on the board, so the post-resignation misuse can be caught, provided the other elements (impropriety and the purpose of causing detriment) are proved."},
    {t:"No: duties end on resignation", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(5) extends s 157 to former officers. The information limb can bite after resignation."},
    {t:"Only if he was still a director when he used it", correct:false,
     why:"That is the rule for misuse of position, not misuse of information."},
    {t:"Only if the information was a trade secret", correct:false,
     why:"'Information' is not limited to confidential information ([09.037])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.032], fns 151–152", takeaway:"Misuse of information acquired as officer is caught even after resignation." },

{ id:"c9x-040", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ex-director Paul left Reed Ltd a year ago. (1) Before leaving, he used his signing authority to award a contract to his wife's firm. (2) After leaving, he uses Reed's tender pricing, which he learned as a director, to undercut Reed in a bid for his new employer.",
  prompt:"How does s 157(2) apply to each act?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both can be caught: (1) is misuse of position while in office, which resignation does not erase; (2) is misuse of information acquired as officer, which can be caught after leaving", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.032] distinguishes the two limbs. For improper use of position, the wrong must have been committed while the person was still a director, but resigning does not let him escape liability for it. Act (1) was done while Paul held office and used his signing authority, so it can be pursued now. For improper use of information, as long as the information was acquired by virtue of his position as officer, the person may be liable even if the misuse happened after resignation. Act (2) uses tender pricing learned as a director, so it is within the information limb, made possible by s 157(5)'s extension to former officers. In each case the other elements must still be shown: impropriety, and the purpose of gaining an advantage or causing detriment."},
    {t:"Neither: he is no longer an officer", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(5) includes former officers, and act (1) occurred while he was in office."},
    {t:"Only (1), because position and information both require current office", correct:false,
     why:"Misuse of information acquired as officer can be caught after resignation."},
    {t:"Only (2), because misuse of position is forgotten on resignation", correct:false,
     why:"A director cannot escape liability for misuse of position by resigning."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.032]", takeaway:"Position misuse: must occur in office (still actionable later). Information misuse: can occur after leaving." },

{ id:"cl9-032", lesson:"9G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did the High Court of Australia hold in Chew v R about the mental element under the equivalent of s 157(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"'To gain' and 'to cause' are purposive: the officer must have intended his act to achieve what he believed was an advantage or a detriment", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.033] says the majority in Chew v R construed the word 'to' before 'gain … an advantage' and 'cause detriment' as purposive, not causative. So purpose is an element of liability, and it is 'the equivalent of a specific intention'. It is not enough, as the prosecution argued, to show a deliberate and objectively improper act that in fact gained an advantage or caused a detriment. Nor is it enough that the director knew what was done would result in a gain or detriment. What must be shown is that the director intended, by his act, to achieve a result he believed would be an advantage for himself or another, or a detriment to the company. Koh [09.034] adds that the intended gain or detriment need not actually materialise for the offence."},
    {t:"Proof that gain resulted is sufficient", correct:false,
     why:"Chew rejected a causative reading. A result alone does not establish the purpose element."},
    {t:"Knowledge of the likely result is sufficient", correct:false,
     why:"Chew held knowledge is not enough. Intention to achieve the believed advantage or detriment is required."},
    {t:"No mental element is required", correct:false,
     why:"Purpose, equivalent to specific intention, is an element."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.033], fns 155–157", takeaway:"Chew: purposive 'to' → specific intention; knowledge or result is not enough." },

{ id:"c9x-041", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director of Ash Ltd awards a services contract to a firm owned by her friend. She knows the friend will profit, but her purpose, which the court accepts, was solely to get the work done quickly for Ash. The contract was awarded without the tender process Ash's policies require.",
  prompt:"Applying Chew v R, is the purpose element of s 157(2) made out?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not on these facts: knowing her friend would benefit is not enough; she must have intended to achieve an advantage for him or a detriment to Ash", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.033] says Chew v R held that establishing purpose is not simply a matter of showing the director knew that what was done would result in a gain for another. It must be shown that the director intended, by her act, to achieve a result she believed would be an advantage for herself or another, or a detriment to the company. The court accepts her sole purpose was to benefit Ash. So even if bypassing the tender process was improper, judged objectively ([09.035]), the specific-intention element is missing. That does not leave Ash without recourse: she may have breached general law duties, the no-conflict rule (her friend's interest), or the diligence limb of s 157(1)."},
    {t:"Yes: she knew her friend would profit", correct:false,
     why:"Chew held that knowledge of the result is not the same as purpose."},
    {t:"Yes: the friend in fact profited", correct:false,
     why:"Chew rejected a causative reading. An actual gain does not prove purpose."},
    {t:"Yes: bypassing the tender process was improper", correct:false,
     why:"Impropriety is a separate element from purpose. Both must be shown."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.033], [09.035]", takeaway:"s 157(2) purpose = intention to benefit/harm; mere knowledge fails." },

{ id:"c9x-042", lesson:"9G", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: For the s 157(2) offence, the intended advantage or detriment must actually materialise.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.034] says it is not an element of the offence that the envisaged gain or detriment eventually materialises (Chew v R). The offence focuses on the purpose of the improper use. Footnote 158 notes that Deane J took the different view that no offence is committed unless the gain or detriment results. But materialisation does matter for civil remedies: it must be established before the court can order an account of profit or compensation for damage suffered by the company under s 157(3)(a). So an officer can be guilty of the offence even if the plan fails, but the company recovers only if there was a profit or loss."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The majority in Chew held the result need not materialise for the offence. It is required only for the s 157(3)(a) remedies."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.034], fns 158–159", takeaway:"Offence: result needn't happen. Account/compensation: it must." },

{ id:"c9x-043", lesson:"9G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How is 'impropriety' assessed under s 157(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Objectively and contextually, by the standards reasonably expected of a person in the officer's position; awareness of the impropriety is not required", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.035] says impropriety is a separate element that must be independently established. It cannot be measured by a uniform or inflexible standard for all officers; it depends on the particular duties and responsibilities of the officer concerned. In R v Byrnes, the High Court of Australia held the test is objective. There is impropriety if there is a breach of the standards of conduct reasonably expected of a person in the director's position, with his duties, powers and authority, in the particular context. It does not depend on the director being aware of the impropriety. Koh notes this objective element is where s 157(2) overlaps with the general law duty to act for proper purposes. So s 157(2) combines an objective impropriety element with a subjective purpose element (Chew)."},
    {t:"Subjectively, by whether the officer believed he was acting properly", correct:false,
     why:"Byrnes holds the test is objective. The officer's belief goes to the separate purpose element."},
    {t:"By a single fixed standard for all officers", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says impropriety is contextual, by reference to the particular officer's duties and responsibilities."},
    {t:"Only by whether the company suffered loss", correct:false,
     why:"Loss is not the test for impropriety, and need not materialise for the offence."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.035], fns 160–164", takeaway:"Impropriety: objective, contextual (Byrnes); purpose: subjective (Chew)." },

{ id:"cl9-033", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Directors of debt-laden Jeffcott also sit on the board of cash-rich Magnacrete. Without the board authority Magnacrete's constitution requires, they affix its seal to documents making A$2m of its funds available to support Jeffcott.",
  prompt:"What did the High Court of Australia decide in R v Byrnes?",
  options:[
    {t:"They made improper use of their positions; impropriety is tested objectively, and in a conflict of duties they needed to disclose and get Magnacrete's consent", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.035]–[09.036] describes R v Byrnes. The defendants were directors of Jeffcott, a cash-strapped, heavily indebted listed company, and of Magnacrete, a cash-rich company in which Jeffcott had a significant stake. To help Jeffcott raise funds, they caused Magnacrete's seal to be affixed, without the board authority Magnacrete's constitution required, to documents making A$2m of its funds available for Jeffcott's fundraising. The High Court held this was improper use of their positions as Magnacrete directors. They acted without required authority, for the purpose of gaining an advantage for Jeffcott. The court stressed they were in a position of conflicting fiduciary duties, and so were not entitled to use their Magnacrete powers for Jeffcott's benefit without clearly disclosing the benefit to Magnacrete and obtaining its consent."},
    {t:"No impropriety, because both companies were related", correct:false,
     why:"Being related companies did not entitle the directors to use one company's assets for the other. Each company is separate, and the directors owed duties to Magnacrete as such."},
    {t:"Impropriety requires the directors to know they acted improperly", correct:false,
     why:"Byrnes held the test of impropriety is objective. Awareness is not required."},
    {t:"No offence, because Magnacrete suffered no loss", correct:false,
     why:"Loss need not materialise for the offence ([09.034]). The improper use for Jeffcott's advantage was enough."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.035]–[09.036]", takeaway:"Byrnes: unauthorised use of one company's funds for another = improper use; disclose and get consent." },

{ id:"c9x-044", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Rani sits on the boards of both ParentCo and its 60%-owned subsidiary SubCo. ParentCo needs cash. Using her authority at SubCo, Rani arranges for SubCo to prepay ParentCo $5m for future services, on terms SubCo's other directors are not told about. Rani honestly believes this is good for the group.",
  prompt:"Applying R v Byrnes, what is the key problem?",
  options:[
    {t:"She is in a conflict of duties and used her SubCo position for ParentCo's advantage without clearly disclosing that benefit to SubCo and obtaining its consent", correct:true,
     why:"In R v Byrnes ([09.036]), directors sitting on two boards were held to have improperly used their positions at Magnacrete by deploying its funds for Jeffcott's benefit without authority. The High Court emphasised the conflict of fiduciary duties: such directors may not exercise their powers at one company for the benefit of another without clearly disclosing the nature of the benefit and obtaining the first company's consent. Rani's case is parallel. Her duty is to SubCo as a separate company with minority shareholders, and the prepayment is for ParentCo's advantage, concealed from SubCo's other directors. Her honest belief that it helps 'the group' does not change the objective impropriety. Her purpose, to gain an advantage for ParentCo, meets the Chew purpose test."},
    {t:"There is no problem, because groups are treated as one entity", correct:false,
     why:"Each company in a group is a separate legal person. Rani's duties at SubCo are owed to SubCo."},
    {t:"The only problem is that she did not make a profit herself", correct:false,
     why:"Section 157(2) covers gaining an advantage for another person, here ParentCo. Personal profit is not needed."},
    {t:"The only problem is that ParentCo is insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"ParentCo's solvency is not the key point. The concealed use of SubCo's position for ParentCo's benefit is."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.035]–[09.036]", takeaway:"Dual directors must disclose and obtain consent before using one company for another's benefit." },

{ id:"cl9-034", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shortly before his company goes into liquidation, a director changes its name. He then registers the old business name through another company he controls, which trades under it free of the first company's debts.",
  prompt:"What did the court hold in McNamara v Flavel?",
  options:[
    {t:"He acquired the information about the company's difficulties and imminent liquidation as a director and made improper use of it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.038] describes McNamara v Flavel. The director caused the company to change its name shortly before liquidation, then applied to register its former business name through another company in which he was director and shareholder, which began trading under that name. This 'bare-faced' scheme let the new company exploit the first company's goodwill free of its debts. The Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia held that he had acquired the information that the company was in financial difficulty and liquidation was imminent by virtue of his position as director, and that using it against the company's interests was improper use of information. The case shows that 'information' need not be confidential, and that knowledge of the company's own predicament can be misused."},
    {t:"No liability, because the financial difficulties were not confidential", correct:false,
     why:"'Information' is not limited to confidential information. What matters is how it was acquired ([09.037])."},
    {t:"No liability, because others knew of the difficulties", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says the fact that others may know the information is irrelevant ([09.038])."},
    {t:"Only the second company was liable", correct:false,
     why:"The director personally made improper use of information he acquired as director."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.037]–[09.038], fns 166–169", takeaway:"McNamara: knowledge of looming liquidation, acquired as director, misused." },

{ id:"c9x-045", lesson:"9G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"As a director of Quartz Ltd, Ben learns at a board meeting that Quartz will not renew a key distribution agreement. The fact is not confidential; industry gossip has already hinted at it. Ben immediately offers the distributor a deal through his own firm.",
  prompt:"Can this be 'information acquired by virtue of his position' under s 157(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: information means knowledge of facts, not only confidential information; it is how he acquired it that matters, and others' knowledge is irrelevant", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.037] says s 157(2) does not define the type of 'information' required, and Australian authority suggests that what matters is not the nature of the information but how it was acquired. 'Information' simply means knowledge of factual matters, and is not limited to confidential information (ASIC v Somerville; McNamara v Flavel). Koh [09.038] adds that the nature of the information still helps establish the causal link: if it is the sort of thing directors are privy to, the court may more readily infer it came to the director by virtue of his position. The fact that others may also know it is irrelevant. Ben learned it at a board meeting, the clearest case of acquisition by virtue of position. Impropriety and purpose must still be shown."},
    {t:"No: only confidential information counts", correct:false,
     why:"Australian authority Koh cites rejects a confidentiality requirement."},
    {t:"No: industry gossip means the information was public", correct:false,
     why:"That others may know the information is irrelevant. Ben acquired it at a board meeting."},
    {t:"Only if he paid for the information", correct:false,
     why:"Payment has nothing to do with it. The question is how he acquired it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.037]–[09.038], fns 165–167", takeaway:"Information = facts acquired through the position; confidentiality not required." },

{ id:"c9x-046", lesson:"9G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why are Australian decisions particularly useful in interpreting s 157(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Local authority is scant, and s 157(2) is drawn from s 124 of the Australian Uniform Companies Act 1961", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.032] says there is scant local authority on the requirements of s 157(2). Because the subsection is drawn from s 124 of the Australian Uniform Companies Act 1961, decisions on that section and its successor provisions are instructive. That is why the key cases on purpose (Chew v R), impropriety (R v Byrnes) and information (McNamara v Flavel; ASIC v Somerville) are Australian. Footnote 160 notes one difference: under the current Australian Corporations Act 2001, improper use of position (s 182(1)) and of information (s 183) are dealt with in separate provisions, whereas Singapore's s 157(2) combines them."},
    {t:"Because Australian law binds Singapore courts", correct:false,
     why:"Australian decisions are persuasive, not binding. They are instructive because of the common origin of the provisions."},
    {t:"Because s 157(2) was copied from English law", correct:false,
     why:"Koh traces s 157(2) to the Australian Uniform Companies Act 1961, not English law."},
    {t:"Because Singapore courts have rejected Australian approaches", correct:false,
     why:"Nothing in Koh's account suggests rejection. Australian authority is treated as instructive."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.032], fns 153–154, 160", takeaway:"s 157(2) comes from Australia's s 124 (1961); Australian cases guide it." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9H.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9H Good Faith: Discretion & Subjectivity (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.039]–[09.044], fns 170–187. Replaces the 9H questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-035", lesson:"9H", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Lord Greene MR in Re Smith and Fawcett: directors must exercise their discretion bona fide in what they consider, not what a ____ may consider, is in the interests of the company.",
  accept:["court","the court"], answer:"court",
  why:"Koh [09.039] says acting in the best interests of the beneficiary is a fundamental obligation of every fiduciary, and for directors it is often expressed in Lord Greene MR's words in Re Smith and Fawcett Ltd: directors 'must exercise their discretion bona fide in what they consider – not what a court may consider – is in the interests of the company'. The formula has two consequences explored in this lesson. First, it assumes the director actually exercises his discretion, so he may not delegate it without authority or fetter it. Second, the test is essentially subjective: the question is the director's own honest belief, not whether the court would have made the same decision ([09.043]).",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.039], fn 170", takeaway:"Smith and Fawcett: what the directors, not the court, consider in the company's interests." },

{ id:"c9x-047", lesson:"9H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the rule delegatus non potest delegare apply to directors?",
  options:[
    {t:"A director, as a delegate of fiduciary powers, cannot further delegate them unless authorised by the instrument of delegation, though such authorisation is common in practice", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.040] says the duty of good faith assumes that the director does exercise his discretion. So the common law rule delegatus non potest delegare (a delegate cannot delegate) applies: a director, being a person to whom fiduciary powers have been delegated, cannot further delegate their exercise without authorisation in the instrument of delegation (Cartmell's Case). In practice, given the commercial world directors work in, such authorisation is necessarily common; fn 172 points to art 73 of the old Table A. Constitutions typically allow the board to delegate to a managing director or committees. Without such authority, a director who simply hands his decisions to someone else has failed to exercise his discretion at all."},
    {t:"Directors may never delegate any of their powers", correct:false,
     why:"Delegation is permitted where the constitution authorises it, which Koh says is necessarily common."},
    {t:"Directors may always delegate any power to anyone", correct:false,
     why:"The default common law rule is the reverse: no delegation without authorisation."},
    {t:"The rule applies only to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The rule applies to delegates of fiduciary powers, which is what directors are."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.040], fns 171–172", takeaway:"Directors can't sub-delegate without authority; constitutions usually authorise it." },

{ id:"c9x-048", lesson:"9H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"To secure an investment, Lotus Pte Ltd's director Ken signs a side letter with the investor promising to 'vote on all board matters as the investor directs'. At the next board meeting he votes, as directed, to approve a contract he has not read.",
  prompt:"What duty has Ken breached?",
  options:[
    {t:"The duty to exercise his discretion independently, by binding himself in advance to vote as another person directs", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.041] says that since a director must exercise his discretion, he is not entitled to bind himself to decide in any particular way, for example by agreeing ahead of time to vote in accordance with the directions or wishes of another person. The duty requires him actively to exercise his discretion. A director who has bound himself to vote in a pre-arranged way cannot be said to have applied his mind to the matter, and so cannot have actively considered whether the decision is in the company's interests. Footnote 173 says this is often described as a separate duty not to fetter discretion, and fn 174 links it to the no-conflict rule. Ken's side letter and his vote on an unread contract are a textbook fettering."},
    {t:"None: he followed the investor's directions, which benefited the company", correct:false,
     why:"The question is not whether the result happened to benefit the company, but whether Ken exercised his own judgment. He did not."},
    {t:"Only the duty of diligence, for not reading the contract", correct:false,
     why:"Not reading the contract may also breach diligence, but the core problem is the fetter: he voted as directed, not on his own judgment."},
    {t:"None, because investors are entitled to control their nominees", correct:false,
     why:"The law subjects nominees to the same duties as other directors. The company is entitled to the undivided loyalty of all its directors."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.041], fns 173–174", takeaway:"Agreeing in advance to vote as told = fettering discretion." },

{ id:"c9x-049", lesson:"9H", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A nominee director appointed by a major creditor owes the company lesser duties, since everyone knows he is there to protect the creditor.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.041] says it is not uncommon for interested persons, including a major creditor or investor, to be given the right to appoint nominees to the board to protect and even promote the appointor's interests (Dairy Containers v NZI Bank). But the law is clear in subjecting such nominees to the same duties as other 'ordinary' directors. The company is entitled to the undivided loyalty of all its directors (Raffles Hotel v Rayner). Nominee directors are therefore particularly vulnerable to allegations that they have fettered their discretion by simply following their appointor's wishes. The tension between nominee directors' loyalties is considered further in 9K."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Nominee directors owe the same duties as any other director. Their appointment does not dilute their loyalty to the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.041], fns 175–177", takeaway:"Nominees owe the same duties; the company is owed undivided loyalty." },

{ id:"cl9-036", lesson:"9H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A board, having carefully considered it, signs a long-term supply contract that obliges the company to take certain board decisions in future years.",
  prompt:"Have the directors improperly fettered their discretion? (Thorby v Goldberg)",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the proper time to exercise discretion is often when negotiating the contract, so if they then judged it in the company's interests there is no breach", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.042] asks whether the rule against fettering stops directors from contracting in a way that binds their future conduct. In Thorby v Goldberg, the High Court of Australia held that contractual restrictions on the board do not necessarily involve impermissible fettering. The proper time for exercising the directors' discretion is often when the contract is negotiated, not when it is performed. So if the directors properly considered it in the company's interests to enter into the contract, the fact that it binds the board to act in a certain way later is not a breach. Any other rule would make companies unable to give binding long-term commitments, which commercial life requires (see also Ang Kong Meng v Lim Teck Boon, fn 179)."},
    {t:"Yes: directors may never bind their future conduct", correct:false,
     why:"Thorby v Goldberg rejects this. A considered decision to contract is itself the exercise of discretion."},
    {t:"Yes, unless shareholders unanimously approve", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholder approval is not required. What matters is that the board considered the contract in the company's interests at the time."},
    {t:"No, because the delegatus rule doesn't apply to directors", correct:false,
     why:"The delegatus rule does apply to directors ([09.040]). The answer turns on Thorby's timing point, not on any exemption."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.040]–[09.042], fns 178–179", takeaway:"Thorby: a considered contract binding future conduct isn't fettering." },

{ id:"c9x-050", lesson:"9H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Case 1: Juniper's board, after careful review, agrees in a joint venture contract that the company will vote its shares in the JV company in a specified way for five years. Case 2: Juniper's director Zoe privately promises a supplier that she will always vote in favour of renewing its contract, whatever terms are offered.",
  prompt:"Which involves an improper fetter on discretion?",
  options:[
    {t:"Case 2 only: Zoe has pre-committed her vote without considering future terms; in Case 1 the board exercised its discretion when it negotiated the contract", correct:true,
     why:"The two cases test the line between Thorby v Goldberg and the anti-fettering principle. In Case 1, the board considered the JV contract and concluded it was in the company's interests; under Thorby ([09.042]), the proper time to exercise discretion is often at negotiation, so the later restriction is not a breach. In Case 2, Zoe has bound herself to vote a particular way regardless of terms not yet known. Koh [09.041] says a director who binds herself to vote in a pre-arranged manner cannot be said to have applied her mind to the matter, so cannot have considered whether the decision is in the company's interests. Her promise is made privately to a third party, not as a considered corporate decision. Fn 174 notes this also engages the no-conflict rule."},
    {t:"Both, because both restrict future votes", correct:false,
     why:"Thorby shows a considered corporate contract can legitimately restrict future conduct. The difference is whether discretion was exercised at the time of commitment."},
    {t:"Case 1 only, because it binds the company for five years", correct:false,
     why:"Duration does not make it a fetter if the board properly considered the contract in the company's interests."},
    {t:"Neither, because directors may always agree how they will vote", correct:false,
     why:"A director's private pre-commitment to vote a certain way is exactly what the anti-fettering principle forbids."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.041]–[09.042]", takeaway:"Considered corporate contract: OK. Private pre-commitment of a director's vote: fetter." },

{ id:"cl9-037", lesson:"9H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In a subjective good faith test, what role does the objective yardstick play? (Regentcrest v Cohen)",
  options:[
    {t:"It goes to credibility: if reasonable directors would have acted very differently, it is harder to persuade the court of an honest belief", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.043] quotes Jonathan Parker J in Regentcrest v Cohen: the question is not whether, viewed objectively, the act was in fact in the company's interests, still less whether the court would have acted differently, but 'whether the director honestly believed that his act or omission was in the interests of the company. The issue is as to the director's state of mind.' The objective yardstick is not irrelevant, though. It bears on the director's credibility. If what he did compares unfavourably with what reasonable directors would have done, he will find it harder to persuade the court that he honestly held the belief he claims. That use of objective evidence does not change the subjective nature of the duty."},
    {t:"It replaces the subjective test", correct:false,
     why:"Regentcrest confirms the test is subjective. The objective yardstick is evidential, not a replacement."},
    {t:"None at all", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says the objective yardstick is not entirely irrelevant: it affects credibility."},
    {t:"It decides whether the transaction was in fact beneficial", correct:false,
     why:"Regentcrest expressly says the question is not whether the act was in fact in the company's interests."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.043], fns 180–181", takeaway:"Regentcrest: the issue is the director's state of mind; objectivity tests credibility." },

{ id:"c9x-051", lesson:"9H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Directors of Larch Ltd discussed at length, with advisers' reports, whether to sell a loss-making division. They decided to keep it, honestly believing a turnaround was likely. The division's losses deepened. A shareholder argues that any reasonable board would have sold.",
  prompt:"How will the court approach the good faith duty here?",
  options:[
    {t:"Subjectively: since the directors genuinely considered the company's interests, the court will not substitute its own view, though objective unreasonableness can bear on whether their belief was honest", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.043] says the test is essentially subjective: whether the director honestly believed the act was in the company's interests (Regentcrest v Cohen). Hellard v Carvalho adds that the subjective test applies where there is evidence the director actually gave consideration to the company's interests; in that case the court may not substitute its view for the director's considered decision. Larch's directors considered the matter at length, with advice. So the shareholder's argument that a reasonable board would have sold does not establish breach of the good faith duty. It is relevant only as evidence on credibility: if the decision were wildly out of line with what reasonable directors would do, it could cast doubt on whether the belief was genuine. A poor result is not a breach (and see the business judgment rule, 9I)."},
    {t:"Objectively: the court asks what a reasonable board would have done", correct:false,
     why:"The objective Charterbridge test applies where the directors failed to consider the company's interests at all (Hellard). These directors did consider them."},
    {t:"Strictly: any loss shows a breach", correct:false,
     why:"Loss does not establish breach. The duty concerns the directors' honest belief, not outcomes."},
    {t:"The shareholder must prove the directors profited personally", correct:false,
     why:"Personal profit is not an element of the good faith duty."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.043]", takeaway:"Considered decision → subjective test; court won't substitute its judgment." },

{ id:"cl9-038", lesson:"9H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A director simply continues an existing, plainly illegal practice without making inquiries or thinking about its implications for the company.",
  prompt:"Which test applies to his general law duty of good faith? (Hellard v Carvalho)",
  options:[
    {t:"The objective Charterbridge test, because he gave no actual consideration to the company's interests", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.043] explains that subjectivity is not absolute. In Hellard v Carvalho, the English High Court accepted that the subjective test is relevant only where there is evidence the director actually considered the company's interests. If he cannot show that, he has failed to show any exercise of discretion to which the subjective standard can apply, and the proper test is the objective Charterbridge test: whether an intelligent and honest man in the director's position could, in the circumstances, reasonably have believed the transaction was for the company's benefit. Scintronix illustrates this: the director simply continued an existing, undoubtedly illegal bribery practice without further inquiries or considering its negative implications. Having exercised no judgment at all, he was assessed objectively and found in breach."},
    {t:"The subjective test, so he is safe if he believed it was fine", correct:false,
     why:"The subjective test presupposes an actual exercise of judgment. He exercised none, so the objective test applies."},
    {t:"Strict liability", correct:false,
     why:"The test is objective (Charterbridge), not strict liability. An objective test can still exonerate ([09.044])."},
    {t:"The business judgment rule protects him", correct:false,
     why:"The business judgment rule protects honest, considered commercial decisions. It does not protect a director who never considered the company's interests."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.043], fns 182–185", takeaway:"No actual consideration → objective Charterbridge test (Hellard; Scintronix)." },

{ id:"cl9-039", lesson:"9H", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Once the objective test is applied, the director will be found liable.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.044] stresses that an objective assessment does not necessarily lead to liability. In Intraco Ltd v Multi-Pak Singapore Pte Ltd, directors of Multi-Pak were said to have breached their duty by causing it to buy an asset from a related company at a significant over-value. They had treated the companies as one economic entity and acted for the group as a whole. The Court of Appeal held that an honest and intelligent man in the directors' position could have inferred that the transactions were in the company's interests, so there was no breach. The objective standard exonerated them. The objective test asks whether the decision could reasonably be believed to benefit the company, not whether it was the best decision."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Intraco shows the objective Charterbridge test can exonerate directors."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.044], fns 186–187", takeaway:"Objective test ≠ automatic liability (Intraco)." },

{ id:"c9x-052", lesson:"9H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The directors of SubCo, who are also directors of its parent, cause SubCo to buy a property from a sister company at a price above market value. They say they were thinking of the group as a whole, not SubCo specifically. SubCo's liquidator sues.",
  prompt:"Following Intraco v Multi-Pak, what is the question for the court?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether an honest and intelligent director in their position could have reasonably believed the transaction was in SubCo's interests; if so, there is no breach", correct:true,
     why:"The directors admit they considered the group, not SubCo specifically, so they cannot show actual consideration of SubCo's interests, and the objective Charterbridge test applies (Hellard, [09.043]). In Intraco ([09.044]), on similar facts, directors caused Multi-Pak to buy an asset from a related company at a significant over-value, having treated the group as one economic entity. The Court of Appeal applied the objective test and asked whether an honest and intelligent man in the directors' position could have inferred the transactions were in Multi-Pak's interests. It concluded he could, so there was no breach. Whether SubCo's directors are exonerated depends on whether a reasonable director could see a benefit to SubCo itself, such as group support it depended on, despite the over-value. Koh [09.046] notes decisions have condoned considering group interests."},
    {t:"Whether the directors honestly believed in the group benefit", correct:false,
     why:"They did not consider SubCo's own interests, so the subjective test is not available. The question is objective."},
    {t:"Whether the price was above market, which automatically means breach", correct:false,
     why:"Intraco held directors not in breach despite a significant over-value."},
    {t:"Whether the parent company approved", correct:false,
     why:"Parent approval does not answer the duty owed to SubCo. The Charterbridge test does."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.043]–[09.044], [09.046]", takeaway:"Group transactions: objective Charterbridge test; can exonerate (Intraco)." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9I.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9I The Company's Interests & Creditors (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.045]–[09.049], fns 188–213. Replaces the 9I questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"c9x-053", lesson:"9I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What do 'the company's interests' mean for the purposes of the good faith duty?",
  options:[
    {t:"It varies by context: often the company as a separate commercial entity, but also readily identified with the shareholders as a whole, though not a particular segment of them", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.045] says what amounts to 'the company's interests' is likely to vary in different contexts (Brady v Brady). Often it simply means the interests of the company as a separate commercial entity (Raffles Town Club). But because the company is an artificial entity, its interests are also very readily identified with the interests of its shareholders as a whole, though not only a particular segment of them (Gaiman v National Association for Mental Health). Usually these align, but they can conflict. Chan Seng Onn J's example in Raffles Town Club: ploughing profits back into the company instead of paying dividends or bonuses prefers the company as a commercial entity over shareholders and employees as individuals."},
    {t:"Always and only the interests of the majority shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"The company's interests may be identified with shareholders as a whole, but not with one segment, let alone just the majority."},
    {t:"The interests of the directors themselves", correct:false,
     why:"Directors must act in the company's interests, not their own; that is the point of the fiduciary duty."},
    {t:"The interests of creditors in all circumstances", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors' interests become mandatory considerations only when the company is insolvent or nearly so ([09.047])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.045], fns 188–193", takeaway:"Company's interests: the entity, or shareholders as a whole, never one segment." },

{ id:"cl9-040", lesson:"9I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A board decides to reinvest profits instead of paying dividends. How does the chapter suggest directors should approach conflicts between the company and its shareholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"Undertake a balancing exercise and reach a considered decision on what is in the company's interests", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.045] says that when a director prefers the interests of the company as a separate entity over those of shareholders, he is not in breach, and there is English authority that the company's separate interests ought to prevail (Mutual Life Assurance v Rank Organisation). As a general proposition this must be right. But applying it too rigidly would suggest a director is in breach whenever he prefers shareholders' interests, which is too strict. The better position, given the subjective nature of the duty, is that the director undertakes a balancing exercise and reaches a considered decision on what is in the company's interests. Chan J in Raffles Town Club recognised this, saying the court should not view with suspicion every transaction that does not produce profitable returns to the company as a commercial entity."},
    {t:"Always prefer the shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders' interests do not automatically prevail. English authority suggests the company's separate interests should prevail as a general proposition."},
    {t:"Always prefer the company as a separate entity", correct:false,
     why:"Koh cautions against applying that rule too rigidly. The director should balance and decide."},
    {t:"Refer every such decision to the general meeting", correct:false,
     why:"Dividend policy and reinvestment are management decisions for the board, not matters to refer to members."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.045], fns 194–195", takeaway:"Balance entity and shareholder interests; reach a considered decision." },

{ id:"c9x-054", lesson:"9I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Fir Ltd has two classes of ordinary shareholders: founders (40%) and outside investors (60%). The board, dominated by founders, approves a share buy-back offered only to the founders at a premium. The board says this is 'in the shareholders' interests'.",
  prompt:"What is the main problem with the board's reasoning?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company's interests can be identified with shareholders as a whole, not with a particular segment of them", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.045] says the company's interests are readily identified with those of its shareholders as a whole, 'albeit not only a particular segment of them' (Gaiman). A buy-back offered only to the founders, at a premium, benefits one segment of the shareholders at the expense of the others, whose company pays out more than the shares are worth. Calling it 'in the shareholders' interests' does not help when only some shareholders benefit. The founder-dominated board also faces conflict-of-interest problems (9J–9K), and the buy-back would need to comply with the statutory share buy-back procedures in any event."},
    {t:"There is no problem: directors may always act for shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Directors may act for shareholders as a whole, not for a favoured segment."},
    {t:"The problem is only that buy-backs are illegal", correct:false,
     why:"Buy-backs are permitted under statutory procedures. The problem is the selective benefit to one segment."},
    {t:"The problem is that the board did not consult creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors' interests become mandatory only near insolvency. The issue here is favouring one group of shareholders."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.045], fn 191", takeaway:"Favouring one segment of shareholders ≠ the company's interests." },

{ id:"c9x-055", lesson:"9I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Can directors of a solvent company take into account the interests of stakeholders other than shareholders?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, with the company's interests firmly in view: employees (s 159), the corporate group, and even creditors, especially secured creditors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.046] says the subjective nature of the duty means a director may take account of a wider range of stakeholders, as long as he does so with the company's interests firmly in view. The Act expressly recognises that directors may consider employees' interests (s 159), and some decisions have condoned considering the interests of the corporate group (Intraco). Creditors are usually considered outsiders whose interests matter only on insolvency, but Koh sees little reason why a director cannot, if he considers it appropriate, take account of them while the company is solvent, especially secured creditors whose continued support is important. Koh [09.047] adds that this is facultative: directors may, but generally need not, consider these interests while the company is solvent."},
    {t:"No, they may consider only shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Section 159 recognises employees' interests, and cases condone considering the group. Koh also sees room for creditors' interests."},
    {t:"Yes, and they must consider all stakeholders in every decision", correct:false,
     why:"The position is facultative while the company is solvent ([09.047]). Directors may, but need not, consider them."},
    {t:"Only if the shareholders pass a resolution allowing it", correct:false,
     why:"No resolution is needed. The latitude flows from the subjective nature of the duty and s 159."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.046], fns 196–197", takeaway:"Directors may consider employees, group, creditors, with the company's interests in view." },

{ id:"cl9-041", lesson:"9I", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: While the company is solvent, directors must take account of employees, the corporate group and creditors in every decision.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.047] says it does not appear to be the law that the director must have regard to all these other potentially relevant interests. The position is facultative: directors may consider them, but are not obliged to. Commercial decisions made in good faith are not readily impugned, a manifestation of the 'business judgment' rule (ECRC Land v Ho Wing On). The position changes when the company is insolvent or nearly so, when directors become obliged to take account of creditors' interests as a whole. So 'must consider creditors' is true only near insolvency, and 'must consider employees and the group' is not a general rule."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"This turns a permission into a requirement. For solvent companies the position is facultative."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.046]–[09.047]", takeaway:"Solvent: other stakeholders may be considered, not must." },

{ id:"c9x-056", lesson:"9I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What policy did V K Rajah JC give in Vita Health Laboratories v Pang Seng Meng for the court's reluctance to second-guess directors' commercial decisions?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is for the marketplace, not the court, to punish honest but wrong commercial decisions; fear of liability would breed defensive judgment and stifle risk-taking", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.047] quotes V K Rajah JC: 'It is the role of the marketplace and not the function of the court to punish and censure directors who have in good faith, made incorrect commercial decisions. Directors should not be coerced into exercising defensive commercial judgment, motivated largely by anxiety over legal accountability and consequences.' Bona fide entrepreneurs should not fear that business failure means legal liability. A company is a vehicle for limited liability and the assumption and distribution of commercial risk, and undue legal interference would 'dampen, if not stifle, the appetite for commercial risk and entrepreneurship'. This is the policy behind the business judgment rule: decisions made in the bona fide belief they were in the company's interests should not readily be impugned."},
    {t:"Courts lack jurisdiction over commercial decisions", correct:false,
     why:"Courts have jurisdiction; the business judgment rule is a policy of restraint, not a lack of power."},
    {t:"Directors are always better informed than shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"That is not the reasoning given. The focus is on the market's role and the risk of stifling enterprise."},
    {t:"Shareholders have agreed to bear all losses caused by directors", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders bear business risk, but directors remain liable for breaches of duty. The rule protects only honest commercial judgments."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.047], fns 198–200", takeaway:"Vita Health: market, not court, punishes honest bad calls; avoid defensive management." },

{ id:"c9x-057", lesson:"9I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Yew Pte Ltd's board, after considering market studies, decides in good faith to expand into a new country. The venture fails and the company loses $10m, though it remains solvent. A shareholder sues the directors for breach of the good faith duty.",
  prompt:"What is the likely outcome?",
  options:[
    {t:"No breach: the decision was a commercial judgment made in good faith, and the court will be slow to intervene", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.047] says commercial decisions are properly for the directors, and as long as they were made in the bona fide belief that they were in the company's interests, the court ought to be slow to intervene. This is the business judgment rule. Vita Health explains why: it is for the market, not the courts, to punish honest commercial mistakes, and fear of liability would stifle entrepreneurship. Yew's board considered market studies and acted in good faith; the subjective test applies because they actually considered the company's interests ([09.043]). A $10m loss is not a breach. The company remains solvent, so creditors' interests did not have to be considered. The shareholder's claim would also face standing problems, as the duty is owed to the company ([09.004])."},
    {t:"Breach, because the venture lost $10m", correct:false,
     why:"Loss does not prove breach. The duty is concerned with good faith, not results."},
    {t:"Breach, because the directors should have considered creditors", correct:false,
     why:"The company remained solvent, so considering creditors was optional."},
    {t:"Breach, unless the shareholders had approved the expansion", correct:false,
     why:"Expansion is a management decision for the board. Shareholder approval is not required."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.043], [09.047]", takeaway:"Honest, considered commercial decisions aren't breaches just because they fail." },

{ id:"c9x-058", lesson:"9I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why must directors consider creditors' interests when the company is insolvent or nearly so?",
  options:[
    {t:"Because in insolvency shareholders cease in practice to be the main economic stakeholders; the insolvency rules apply the assets first to creditors, who are then primarily interested in them", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.047] says that where the company is insolvent or very nearly so, directors must take account of the interests of creditors as a whole, and ensure the company's assets are not dissipated or exploited to their prejudice (Progen Engineering). Footnote 202 notes this means creditors 'as a class rather than as a fixed group of individuals' (Lord Reed in BTI v Sequana). The rationale is that in insolvency, the shareholders cease, practically, to be the company's main economic stakeholder (Foo Kian Beng). Insolvency distribution rules require the assets to be used first to pay creditors, so creditors are in reality primarily interested in them. A director who overlooks creditors is treated like one who failed to consider the company's interests: the objective test applies."},
    {t:"Because creditors become shareholders on insolvency", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors do not become shareholders. Their economic interest in the assets is what changes."},
    {t:"Because creditors can then sue directors directly", correct:false,
     why:"The duty remains owed to the company and is enforced by the liquidator ([09.049])."},
    {t:"Because shareholders lose their votes on insolvency", correct:false,
     why:"That is not the rationale Koh gives. It rests on who bears the economic risk."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.047], fns 201–205", takeaway:"Near insolvency, creditors (as a class) become the real economic stakeholders." },

{ id:"cl9-043", lesson:"9I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company is close to insolvency. Its director approves a transaction without considering creditors' interests at all.",
  prompt:"How is his conduct assessed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Objectively: he is in breach unless there is objective justification for the failure", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.047] says a director who overlooks creditors' interests when the company is in financial difficulty is treated in the same way as one who fails to consider the company's interests: the objective test applies. He is in breach unless there is objective justification for his failure (Hellard v Carvalho). On the other hand, if he did take account of creditors' interests, the subjective test applies, and the court is unlikely to second-guess his decision. This mirrors the Hellard approach in [09.043]: actual consideration earns subjective review, while no consideration triggers objective review."},
    {t:"Subjectively: he is safe if he believed the deal was good", correct:false,
     why:"The subjective test is available only if he actually considered creditors' interests. He did not."},
    {t:"Creditors may sue him directly", correct:false,
     why:"The creditor-regarding duty is owed to the company and enforced by the liquidator ([09.049])."},
    {t:"Creditors' interests are irrelevant until a winding up order", correct:false,
     why:"Most cases accept the obligation arises before technical insolvency ([09.048])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.047]–[09.048], fn 206", takeaway:"Ignoring creditors near insolvency → objective test; considering them → subjective." },

{ id:"cl9-042", lesson:"9I", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each financial stage in Foo Kian Beng v OP3 International to what the duty requires.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Solvency", r:"Shareholders' interests can serve as a proxy for the company's"},
    {l:"Imminent insolvency", r:"Closer scrutiny of decisions that benefit only shareholders or directors"},
    {l:"Inevitable insolvency", r:"No transactions benefiting only shareholders or directors at creditors' expense"},
    {l:"Enforcement of the creditor-regarding duty", r:"By the liquidator, not individual creditors"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.048] says the precise point at which directors must consider creditors' interests is unclear, though most cases accept it arises before technical insolvency. The Court of Appeal in Foo Kian Beng v OP3 International laid down a two-stage approach: objectively determine which of three financial stages the company was in, then examine the director's subjective intentions. In category one (solvency), directors may treat shareholders' interests as a proxy for the company's, since shareholders and creditors 'broadly row in the same direction'. In category two (imminent insolvency), the court scrutinises the director's bona fides against the decision's benefits and risks; it is slow to second-guess honest commercial decisions, but decisions exclusively benefiting shareholders or directors attract closer scrutiny. In category three (inevitable insolvency), economic interests shift to creditors, and transactions exclusively benefiting shareholders or directors at creditors' expense are prohibited. Koh [09.049] adds that the obligation is owed to the company, so only the liquidator can enforce it.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.048]–[09.049], fns 207–213", takeaway:"Foo Kian Beng: solvent (proxy) · imminent (scrutiny) · inevitable (no shareholder-only deals)." },

{ id:"c9x-059", lesson:"9I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Elm Pte Ltd is struggling: it is meeting its debts but has lost its biggest customer and its cash will last four months. Its two director-shareholders cause it to pay them a $400,000 'special bonus'.",
  prompt:"Under Foo Kian Beng, how will the court approach the bonus?",
  options:[
    {t:"As category two (imminent insolvency): the court scrutinises the directors' bona fides closely, because the decision exclusively benefits the directors and shareholders", correct:true,
     why:"The first stage of Foo Kian Beng ([09.048]) is to determine objectively which financial category Elm was in. It is not yet insolvent, but it is in a difficult financial state with limited runway: category two, imminent insolvency. In category two, the court scrutinises the director's subjective bona fides against the potential benefits and risks of the decision to the company. It should be slow to second-guess honest, good-faith commercial decisions, but decisions that exclusively benefit shareholders or directors attract closer scrutiny. A $400,000 bonus to the directors themselves, with four months of cash left, is exactly such a decision. The directors will need to show a genuine company benefit; otherwise they are likely in breach. If the company were in category three, transactions exclusively benefiting them at creditors' expense would be prohibited outright."},
    {t:"As category one: the company is still paying its debts, so shareholders' interests are a proxy", correct:false,
     why:"Being able to pay current debts does not make the company comfortably solvent in Foo Kian Beng's sense. Its precarious state puts it in category two."},
    {t:"As category three: any struggling company is inevitably insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"Category three requires insolvency proceedings to be inevitable. Elm's position is difficult but not yet at that stage on these facts."},
    {t:"The bonus cannot be reviewed because it is a business judgment", correct:false,
     why:"The business judgment rule protects honest commercial decisions, but self-benefiting decisions near insolvency attract closer scrutiny."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.048]", takeaway:"Imminent insolvency: self-benefiting decisions get closer scrutiny." },

{ id:"c9x-060", lesson:"9I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Birch Pte Ltd's lender has called in its loan and the board knows winding up is inevitable within weeks. The directors cause Birch to pay a final dividend to its shareholders, who are the directors' families.",
  prompt:"What does Foo Kian Beng say about this?",
  options:[
    {t:"Category three: where insolvency proceedings are inevitable, directors are prohibited from authorising transactions exclusively benefiting shareholders or directors at creditors' expense", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.048] says that in category three, inevitable insolvency, the economic interests in the company shift from the shareholders to the creditors. Directors are then prohibited by their duty from authorising transactions that exclusively benefit shareholders or directors at the creditors' expense. A final dividend to shareholders when winding up is weeks away takes assets that, under the insolvency distribution rules, should go to creditors first. It is the clearest case of a prohibited transaction. The duty is still owed to the company, so the liquidator, once appointed, can sue the directors for breach and seek to recover the dividend ([09.049]). Capital maintenance and dividend rules (ch 13) would also be engaged."},
    {t:"Category one: dividends are always a matter for the board", correct:false,
     why:"With winding up inevitable, the company is not in category one, and shareholders' interests are no longer a proxy."},
    {t:"Category two: the court will be slow to second-guess the dividend", correct:false,
     why:"Category two is for imminent, not inevitable, insolvency. Here proceedings are inevitable, so the stricter category three rule applies."},
    {t:"No rule applies until the winding up order is made", correct:false,
     why:"The obligation arises before formal insolvency proceedings. Category three captures exactly this situation."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.048]–[09.049]", takeaway:"Inevitable insolvency: no shareholder-only transactions at creditors' expense." },

{ id:"c9x-061", lesson:"9I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After Birch Pte Ltd goes into liquidation, its largest unsecured creditor wants to sue the directors personally for breaching their duty to consider creditors' interests before the collapse.",
  prompt:"Can the creditor bring the claim?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the obligation is part of the duty owed to the company, so only the liquidator can enforce it, preserving the collective insolvency process and pari passu distribution", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.049] says the obligation to consider creditors' interests is not owed to creditors directly. It is part of the duty directors owe to the company (Progen Engineering), so it can be enforced only by the liquidator, not by individual creditors. Channelling recovery through the company ensures the collective procedure of insolvency is not bypassed and the pari passu principle, equal sharing among creditors of the same rank, is maintained. If one creditor could sue directly and keep the proceeds, it would jump ahead of the others. The creditor's proper course is to press the liquidator to pursue the directors, or to fund the liquidator's claim."},
    {t:"Yes: the duty is owed to creditors once the company is insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"The duty is to consider creditors' interests, but it remains owed to the company."},
    {t:"Yes, if the creditor is the largest creditor", correct:false,
     why:"Size of debt does not give standing. Only the liquidator can enforce the company's claim."},
    {t:"Yes, but only with leave of the court under s 216A", correct:false,
     why:"Section 216A derivative actions are for members (and certain others) of a company. The ordinary route in liquidation is the liquidator suing in the company's name."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.049], fns 212–213", takeaway:"Creditor-regarding duty is owed to the company; liquidator enforces (pari passu)." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9J.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9J The No-Conflict Rule (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.050]–[09.057], fns 214–236. Replaces the 9J questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-044", lesson:"9J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How do the objectives of the no-conflict and no-profit rules differ?",
  options:[
    {t:"The no-conflict rule stops the fiduciary being swayed by personal interest; the no-profit rule stops him actually misusing his position for his own advantage", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.050] says it is an 'inflexible rule' that a director cannot profit from his fiduciary position and must avoid situations that may tempt him to prefer his own or another's interests over the company's; good faith is irrelevant. Koh [09.051] says the no-profit rule is possibly conceptually distinct from the more general no-conflict rule, with a subtle difference in objective. The no-conflict rule seeks to prevent the fiduciary from being swayed by considerations of personal interest. The no-profit rule aims to preclude him from actually misusing his position for personal advantage (Deane J in Chan v Zacharia). The two often overlap. Footnote 219 notes that the UK Companies Act 2006, s 175, subsumes the no-profit rule within the duty to avoid conflicts, but Koh treats them separately for clarity."},
    {t:"They are identical", correct:false,
     why:"Koh describes them as possibly conceptually distinct with subtly different objectives, even if they often overlap."},
    {t:"The no-conflict rule applies only to self-dealing", correct:false,
     why:"Self-dealing is the most obvious example ([09.058]), but the no-conflict rule also covers opportunities, competing directorships and nominee positions."},
    {t:"The no-profit rule requires a conflict", correct:false,
     why:"The no-profit rule targets misuse of position for profit; it can apply where one rule seems more apt than the other."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.050]–[09.051], fns 214–219", takeaway:"No-conflict: don't be swayed. No-profit: don't misuse position for gain." },

{ id:"c9x-062", lesson:"9J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"While negotiating a supply contract for Hazel Ltd, its director Omar learns the supplier is selling a side business cheaply. He buys it himself and resells it at a large profit, without telling Hazel.",
  prompt:"Which fiduciary rules are engaged?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both the no-conflict and no-profit rules, since he exploited for himself an opportunity met while acting for the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.051] gives almost exactly this as the example of overlap: a director encounters a lucrative opportunity while negotiating a contract on the company's behalf and, putting his personal interest ahead of his duty, exploits it profitably for himself (Mona Computer Systems v Singaravelu). In such cases 'both rules are clearly relevant'. Omar's personal interest in buying the side business conflicted with his duty to tell Hazel about an opportunity he met in the course of its business (the no-conflict rule), and he made a profit by using his position (the no-profit rule). The company can claim an account of his profit. His good faith, if he had any, would be irrelevant ([09.050])."},
    {t:"Neither, because Hazel was not looking to buy businesses", correct:false,
     why:"Whether the company was actively looking is not decisive under the strict rules, especially when the opportunity arose while acting for it."},
    {t:"Only the duty of diligence", correct:false,
     why:"This is a loyalty problem, not a care problem."},
    {t:"Only the no-profit rule, because there was no conflict", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says both rules are clearly relevant in this situation."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.051], fns 217–218", takeaway:"Opportunity met while acting for the company and taken personally → both rules." },

{ id:"c9x-063", lesson:"9J", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A director who acted entirely in good faith has a defence to a claim under the no-conflict and no-profit rules.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.050] says that to ensure the director remains faithful, it is an 'inflexible rule' that he cannot profit from his fiduciary position and must avoid situations that may tempt him to prefer other interests over the company's. These rules are applied strictly, and the fact that the director might have acted in good faith is irrelevant. This contrasts with the duty to act bona fide in the company's interests, which is essentially subjective (9H). Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver is the paradigm: the directors acted in good faith but still had to account for their profits ([09.026])."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith is a central issue for the duty of good faith, but irrelevant to the strict no-conflict and no-profit rules."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.050]", takeaway:"No-conflict / no-profit: good faith is irrelevant." },

{ id:"cl9-045", lesson:"9J", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The no-conflict rule applies only where the director made a profit or deliberately put himself in conflict.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.052] says a director cannot place himself where his duty conflicts or may conflict with his personal interests or some other external loyalty; fn 221 notes the rule engages even if the conflicting interests are those of a third party (Sim Poh Ping v Winsta Holding). The rule's operation does not depend on any profit having been made (Parker v McKenna). Nor is it a prerequisite that the director intentionally placed himself in conflict. As Sedley LJ said in In Plus Group v Pyke, 'if, even accidentally, [the fiduciary] finds himself in such a position he must regularise or abandon it'."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Neither profit nor intention is required. Accidental conflicts must be regularised or abandoned."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.052], fns 220–225", takeaway:"No profit or intent needed; even accidental conflicts must be regularised or abandoned." },

{ id:"c9x-064", lesson:"9J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"If a director in a conflict position has made no profit, what consequences may still follow?",
  options:[
    {t:"Liability to compensate the company for its loss, setting aside of the tainted transaction, and injunctions restraining the transaction or his involvement", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.052] says that where the director has profited from the conflict, the company can claim an account of profits, requiring him to disgorge his gains. Where no profit is made, an account is obviously unavailable, but other consequences may follow. The director may be liable to compensate the company for any loss suffered; any transaction entered into in the shadow of the conflict may be set aside; and where appropriate, the court may restrain entry into such a transaction or restrain the director from involving himself in it (In Plus Group). Footnote 223 adds the statutory liability to account for profit under s 157(3)(a). None of these depends on the director having intentionally placed himself in conflict."},
    {t:"Nothing, because no profit means no breach", correct:false,
     why:"The no-conflict rule does not depend on profit. Other remedies remain available."},
    {t:"Only criminal penalties", correct:false,
     why:"The general law remedies are civil: compensation, rescission and injunction."},
    {t:"Only removal from office", correct:false,
     why:"Removal is for the members to decide. The remedies Koh lists are compensation, setting aside and injunction."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.052], fns 222–224", takeaway:"No profit: compensation, rescission, injunction remain." },

{ id:"c9x-065", lesson:"9J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Nadia, a director of Willow Ltd, inherits a 40% stake in Willow's main competitor from her late uncle. She did nothing to bring this about.",
  prompt:"What does the no-conflict rule require of her?",
  options:[
    {t:"Although the conflict arose accidentally, she must regularise it (for example by full disclosure and obtaining consent) or abandon it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.052] says it is not a prerequisite that the director intentionally placed himself in a position of conflict. Quoting Sedley LJ in In Plus Group v Pyke: 'if, even accidentally, [the fiduciary] finds himself in such a position he must regularise or abandon it'. Nadia's inheritance gives her a substantial personal interest in a competitor, which may conflict with her duty to advance Willow's interests. She can regularise the position by disclosing it fully and obtaining Willow's informed consent to her continuing with the stake (and making any statutory declaration of interest, 9M), or she can abandon the conflict, for example by selling the stake or stepping down from Willow's board. Doing nothing leaves her exposed to the consequences of an unresolved conflict."},
    {t:"Nothing, because she did not choose the conflict", correct:false,
     why:"The rule applies even to accidental conflicts."},
    {t:"She must immediately pay Willow the value of the stake", correct:false,
     why:"An account is available only for profits made from the conflict. Her obligation is to regularise or abandon the position."},
    {t:"Only the competitor's board must decide", correct:false,
     why:"Her duty is to Willow; the competitor's board has no role in resolving it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.052], fn 225", takeaway:"Accidental conflict → regularise or abandon (In Plus Group)." },

{ id:"c9x-066", lesson:"9J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What must the director's personal interest conflict with for the no-conflict rule to be breached?",
  options:[
    {t:"His duty to the company, so the scope of his duties must be examined first, though the broad duty to act in the company's best interests narrows the gap with 'interests'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.053] says the rule operates where there is a conflict between the director's duty to the company and an extraneous interest. The scope and ambit of the director's duties must be examined before any issue of breach can be considered. Liability does not attach merely because of a conflict between the company's interests generally and the extraneous concern (Boardman v Phipps). The concept of 'interests' is potentially much wider. But given the overriding obligation to act in the best interests of the company, and the breadth some cases give that duty, there may be little difference in practice. Bhullar v Bhullar illustrates how widely the duty can be drawn ([09.054])."},
    {t:"Any interest of the company, however remote", correct:false,
     why:"Formally, the rule requires conflict with the director's duty, not with the company's interests generally."},
    {t:"The shareholders' personal interests", correct:false,
     why:"Duties are owed to the company. The conflict must be with the director's duty to the company."},
    {t:"Only his duty under his employment contract", correct:false,
     why:"The relevant duty is his fiduciary duty as director, not merely contractual duties."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.053], fn 226", takeaway:"Conflict must be with duty; define the duty's scope first." },

{ id:"cl9-046", lesson:"9J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A property company is owned by two feuding family factions. One side has said the company should buy no more properties. A director from the other side learns by chance that the property next door is for sale and buys it through his own company without saying anything.",
  prompt:"What did the English Court of Appeal decide in Bhullar v Bhullar?",
  options:[
    {t:"He breached the no-conflict rule: the company was still trading and would have been interested, so he had a duty to tell it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.054] describes Bhullar v Bhullar. The company, whose business included acquiring investment properties, was held by two feuding family factions. The other faction had said it did not want the company to acquire more properties, and negotiations to divide the assets were underway. The defendants discovered by chance that the property next to one the company held was for sale and, without disclosing their plans, bought it through a company they controlled. There was no abuse of directorial position, so the operative rule was no-conflict. The court accepted that directors must communicate to the company any information of concern to it and relevant for it to know (see IDC v Cooley). The company was still trading, so acquiring the property would have been commercially attractive; the information was of interest to it, and the defendants should have passed it on. Failing to do so let their personal interests conflict with their duty."},
    {t:"No breach, because the other faction had refused further purchases", correct:false,
     why:"The faction's stance did not relieve the defendants of their duty to tell the company about information of interest to it."},
    {t:"No breach, because he learned of it by chance", correct:false,
     why:"How he learned of it did not matter. The information was of concern to the company, so he had to pass it on."},
    {t:"Breach of s 157(2) only", correct:false,
     why:"Bhullar is an English general law case. The court decided it under the no-conflict rule."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.053]–[09.054], fns 227–229", takeaway:"Bhullar: duty to tell the company of information of interest to it." },

{ id:"c9x-067", lesson:"9J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Ivy Pte Ltd operates restaurants and is actively opening new outlets. While on holiday, its director Sam learns from a friend that a prime restaurant site will soon be available. He leases it for his own new restaurant without telling Ivy. Sam argues he learned of it privately, not as a director.",
  prompt:"Applying Bhullar, is Sam likely to be in breach?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: the information is of concern to Ivy and relevant for it to know, so he had a duty to pass it on, however he learned it", correct:true,
     why:"In Bhullar ([09.054]) the defendants learned 'quite by chance' that a neighbouring property was for sale. The Court of Appeal still held that directors are under a duty to communicate to the company any information of concern to it and relevant for it to know. Since the company was trading and acquiring the property would have been commercially attractive, they should have told it. Ivy is actively expanding its restaurant business, so a prime restaurant site is plainly of concern to it. Sam's argument that he learned of it on holiday mirrors the 'by chance' point rejected in Bhullar. Koh [09.055] justifies this wide view: the company has, in reality, no access to such information except through its directors. Sam could have avoided breach by disclosing and obtaining Ivy's consent, or by having a contractual right to pursue such opportunities."},
    {t:"No: he learned of it outside his role as director", correct:false,
     why:"Bhullar shows that the circumstances of discovery do not prevent the duty arising where the information is of concern to the company."},
    {t:"No: the company is not entitled to freedom from competition", correct:false,
     why:"The company is not entitled to freedom from competition generally, but it is entitled to freedom from competition by those charged with promoting its interests ([09.055])."},
    {t:"Only if Ivy can prove it would have leased the site", correct:false,
     why:"The strict rule does not require proof that the company would have taken the opportunity. It was information of concern that he had to share."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.054]–[09.055]", takeaway:"How the director learned it doesn't matter if it concerns the company's business." },

{ id:"cl9-047", lesson:"9J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the chapter support a wide view of the director's duty for the no-conflict rule?",
  options:[
    {t:"The company has no access to information except through its directors, and it is entitled to freedom from competition by those charged with promoting its interests", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.055] notes that recognising so wide a duty has been said to 'revolutionise' directors' duties (Finn). But the wider conception is amply justified because the company in reality has no other access to relevant information except through its directors. A company is not entitled to general freedom from competition, but it 'is entitled to freedom from competition by those charged with the promotion of its interests'. Directors remain free, by contract, to reserve the privilege of competing with the company or exploiting certain opportunities while still directors. And a consistently strict standard ensures the rules are fairly settled, so everyone knows where they stand."},
    {t:"Directors are usually dishonest", correct:false,
     why:"The justification is structural (the company depends on directors for information), not a presumption of dishonesty."},
    {t:"The company is entitled to freedom from all competition", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says expressly the company is not entitled to general freedom from competition."},
    {t:"Because the rule can't be modified by contract", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says directors can reserve by contract the privilege of competing or exploiting opportunities."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.055], fns 230–231", takeaway:"Wide duty justified: company depends on directors for information; contract can carve out." },

{ id:"c9x-068", lesson:"9J", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A director may, by contract with the company, reserve the right to compete with it or to exploit certain business opportunities while still a director.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.055] says directors remain free, by contract, to reserve the privilege of competing with the company or to exploit certain business opportunities while still a director. This is the counterweight to the wide view of the no-conflict rule: the strict default protects the company, but the parties can agree to adjust it in advance. Such a contract works like advance informed consent ([09.019]): with prior authorisation, there is no breach. Any such arrangement would still need to be agreed by the company properly, with full disclosure, and is subject to statutory requirements."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Koh expressly recognises that directors can reserve these privileges by contract."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.055]", takeaway:"Contract can reserve a director's right to compete or take opportunities." },

{ id:"cl9-048", lesson:"9J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a two-director company, one director is cut off by the other: denied financial information, his drawings stopped, locked out of his office. He then sets up a new company and wins one of the old company's key customers.",
  prompt:"What did the English Court of Appeal decide in In Plus Group v Pyke?",
  options:[
    {t:"No breach: his duty had been 'reduced to vanishing point' by his exclusion, so there was no conflict", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.056] says a director's duty may be curtailed or modified in some circumstances, so that seemingly disloyal acts do not attract the rule. In In Plus Group v Pyke, a small company had two shareholders who were its only directors. The defendant, while still a director, set up a new company and poached an important customer, a paradigm conflict situation. Yet the Court of Appeal unanimously exonerated him. At the time, he had been effectively excluded from management by the other director, who had denied him financial information, stopped his monthly drawings against his loan account and locked him out of his office. He was a director in name only. Sedley LJ said his duty had been 'reduced to vanishing point' by his fellow director's acts, so there could be no conflict."},
    {t:"Breach, because poaching a customer is always a conflict", correct:false,
     why:"That is the usual position, but In Plus Group shows the duty itself can be reduced by exclusion, so no conflict arises."},
    {t:"Breach, because he hadn't resigned", correct:false,
     why:"He had not resigned, but his exclusion had reduced his duty to vanishing point."},
    {t:"The other director was liable for the loss", correct:false,
     why:"The case concerned the defendant's liability. The court did not hold the other director liable."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.056], fns 233–235", takeaway:"In Plus Group: excluded director's duty 'reduced to vanishing point'." },

{ id:"c9x-069", lesson:"9J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What approach did Rix LJ describe in Foster Bryant Surveying v Bryant for retiring or resigning directors?",
  options:[
    {t:"Fiduciary principles are unchanged, but their application requires care and sensitivity to the facts and to freedom to compete; courts adopt pragmatic, commonsense, merits-based solutions", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.057] says the In Plus Group approach matters especially for resigning directors, particularly where there are exclusionary acts. She quotes Rix LJ: while the fiduciary principles remain unamended, 'their application in different circumstances has required care and sensitivity both to the facts and to other principles, such as that of personal freedom to compete, where that does not intrude on the misuse of the company's property'. There has been some flexibility in the reach of the duties and in findings of liability. In the context of retiring directors, where the critical line between being and not being a director is hard to police, 'the courts have adopted pragmatic solutions based on a commonsense and merits-based approach'."},
    {t:"Resigning directors are always free to compete immediately", correct:false,
     why:"Rix LJ did not endorse unlimited freedom. Misuse of the company's property, including opportunities and information, remains off limits."},
    {t:"Resigning directors remain bound for life", correct:false,
     why:"Rix LJ emphasised pragmatic, merits-based solutions, not perpetual restraint."},
    {t:"The fiduciary principles have been amended for resigning directors", correct:false,
     why:"Rix LJ said the principles remain unamended; it is their application that is flexible."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.057], fn 236", takeaway:"Resigning directors: same principles, pragmatic merits-based application." },

{ id:"c9x-070", lesson:"9J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Thea is a fully involved director of Grove Pte Ltd, attending board meetings and seeing all financial information. Without telling the board, she sets up a competing company and secretly persuades Grove's biggest client to move to it. She resigns a week after the client moves. She argues In Plus Group protects her.",
  prompt:"Is In Plus Group likely to help her?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: In Plus Group turned on the director being excluded and his duty reduced to vanishing point; Thea was fully involved, so her duty applied in full", correct:true,
     why:"In In Plus Group ([09.056]), the defendant escaped liability because his co-director had excluded him from management, denied him information, stopped his drawings and locked him out of his office, making him a director in name only. That is what reduced his duty to vanishing point. Thea's position is the opposite: she had full access to information and management while she arranged the client's move. Her duty to Grove applied in full. Setting up a competitor and diverting the company's biggest client while still a director is a paradigm conflict, and her later resignation does not cure it. Rix LJ's pragmatic approach for resigning directors ([09.057]) gives flexibility on the merits, but the merits here are all against her."},
    {t:"Yes: any director may compete once she decides to resign", correct:false,
     why:"Deciding to resign does not end the duty. Thea acted while still a fully involved director."},
    {t:"Yes: the client chose to move", correct:false,
     why:"The client's choice does not excuse her having secretly solicited it while a director."},
    {t:"Yes, because she resigned within a week", correct:false,
     why:"Resigning after the diversion does not undo the breach committed while she was a director."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.056]–[09.057]", takeaway:"In Plus Group helps excluded directors, not fully involved ones." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9K.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9K Self-Dealing & Conflicting Duties (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.058]–[09.068], fns 237–279. Replaces the 9K questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-049", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A director sells an asset to his company on fair terms. How does the general law treat this self-dealing transaction?",
  options:[
    {t:"Remedial consequences follow automatically unless shareholders gave fully informed approval, or the constitution validly relaxes the rule", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.058] says the most obvious actual conflict is self-dealing, where the director, acting for the company as a board member, essentially stands on both sides of the transaction. The principle is strictly applied: remedial consequences follow automatically, and it is irrelevant that the terms are fair and in the company's interests, or that the company would have transacted on the same terms anyway. But since a rigid rule would prohibit all contracts between companies and their directors, often to the company's disadvantage, the company, for whose protection the rule exists, may waive or relax it. At general law, an effective waiver requires the fully informed approval of the transaction by the shareholders; with that approval, there is no breach. Koh [09.059] adds that the constitution may also mitigate the rule, as in Hely-Hutchinson v Brayhead."},
    {t:"It is valid because the terms are fair", correct:false,
     why:"Fairness is irrelevant to the strict rule. The transaction needs informed approval or a valid constitutional relaxation."},
    {t:"It is void and can never be approved", correct:false,
     why:"The company can waive the rule by fully informed shareholder approval."},
    {t:"It is valid if the director abstains from the board vote", correct:false,
     why:"Abstaining at board level is not the general law waiver, which requires informed shareholder approval, unless the constitution provides otherwise."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.058]–[09.059], fns 237–242", takeaway:"Self-dealing: strict; fair terms irrelevant; waived by informed shareholder approval or constitution." },

{ id:"c9x-071", lesson:"9K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Director Lim sells his warehouse to Cypress Ltd. An independent valuer confirms the price is 10% below market, so Cypress gets a good deal. Lim told the board but no shareholder approval was sought, and the constitution has no relevant clause. A shareholder later challenges the sale.",
  prompt:"What is the general law position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The no-conflict rule is breached and remedial consequences follow, even though the terms favoured the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.058] says that in self-dealing, Lim stands on both sides of the transaction, and the principle is strictly applied. Remedial consequences follow automatically, and it is irrelevant that the terms are fair and in the company's interests, or that the company would have contracted on the same terms without the conflict. The only general law route to avoid breach is the fully informed approval of the shareholders; telling the board is not enough at general law unless the constitution says so ([09.059]). Since Cypress's constitution has no relaxing clause and shareholders never approved, the rule is breached even though the price was good for the company. In practice, the company might choose not to set aside a beneficial sale, but the breach is established. The statutory disclosure duty under s 156 is a separate matter (9M)."},
    {t:"No breach, because the price favoured the company", correct:false,
     why:"The strict rule ignores whether the terms are fair or favourable."},
    {t:"No breach, because he told the board", correct:false,
     why:"At general law the waiver requires informed shareholder approval, unless the constitution provides for board-level disclosure."},
    {t:"No breach, because the valuer approved the price", correct:false,
     why:"An independent valuation goes to fairness, which the strict rule treats as irrelevant."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.058]–[09.059]", takeaway:"Even favourable self-dealing breaches the rule without proper approval." },

{ id:"c9x-072", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why may the company relax the self-dealing rule, and what does a general law waiver require?",
  options:[
    {t:"Applied rigidly it would bar all company–director contracts (even employing directors as executives) to the company's disadvantage; waiver requires fully informed shareholder approval", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.058] says that if the self-dealing rule were applied rigidly without concession, it would prohibit all contracts between the company and its directors. That would often work against the company: fn 239 gives the example that the company could not employ its directors in any executive position (Lord Herschell in Bray v Ford). So it is accepted that the company, being the entity the rule protects, may waive compliance or relax it as it thinks proper (Boulting v ACTT). At general law, an effective waiver needs the fully informed approval of the transaction by the shareholders (Boardman v Phipps). To the extent the company approves, the conflict is avoided and there is no breach (Movitex v Bulfield)."},
    {t:"Because self-dealing is always beneficial; no approval is needed", correct:false,
     why:"Self-dealing is not presumed beneficial. The rule is relaxed only through approval or the constitution."},
    {t:"Because the directors can waive it for themselves by board resolution", correct:false,
     why:"The waiver belongs to the company, and at general law it is given by informed shareholder approval, not by interested directors."},
    {t:"Because the court must approve every director contract", correct:false,
     why:"Court approval is not the mechanism. The company's informed approval is."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.058], fns 239–242", takeaway:"Rigid rule would bar all director contracts; company waives via informed shareholder approval." },

{ id:"c9x-073", lesson:"9K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Sequoia Ltd's constitution says: 'A director may be interested in any contract with the company and shall not be liable to account for any profit, provided the nature of his interest is declared at a board meeting.' Director Pam declares her interest at a board meeting and then (1) sells consulting services to Sequoia, and (2) causes Sequoia, a public company, to lend her $500,000 personally.",
  prompt:"What is the effect of the constitutional clause?",
  options:[
    {t:"It can protect Pam on (1), following Hely-Hutchinson v Brayhead, but not on (2), which is governed by the statutory restrictions on loans to directors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.059] says the company's constitution may contain provisions mitigating the strictness of the no-conflict rule, as in Hely-Hutchinson v Brayhead, where a director could contract with the company and not account for profits provided he declared the nature of his interest at a board meeting. The effectiveness of such clauses has generally been upheld. So Pam's declared consulting contract can fall within the clause. But Koh adds that this is subject to legislative provisions governing particular categories of self-dealing, including loans and other restricted transactions with directors (ch 8, s 162). A public company's loan to its director is prohibited by s 162 unless an exception applies, and a constitutional clause cannot override the statute."},
    {t:"It protects Pam on both transactions", correct:false,
     why:"The clause cannot override statutory prohibitions such as s 162 on loans to directors."},
    {t:"It protects her on neither, because the self-dealing rule cannot be relaxed", correct:false,
     why:"Constitutional relaxations like Hely-Hutchinson's have generally been upheld."},
    {t:"It protects her only if shareholders also approve each contract", correct:false,
     why:"Under such a clause, declaration at a board meeting is the condition. Shareholder approval is the alternative general law route."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.059], fns 243–245", takeaway:"Constitution can relax the rule (Hely-Hutchinson), subject to statutory limits like s 162." },

{ id:"cl9-050", lesson:"9K", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each authority to its position on what counts as a conflict.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Boardman v Phipps (majority)", r:"Any possibility of conflict suffices, however remote"},
    {l:"Lord Upjohn (dissenting)", r:"A 'real sensible possibility of conflict'"},
    {l:"Ng Eng Ghee v Mamata Kapildev Dave", r:"Gave three reasons supporting the stricter approach"},
    {l:"Ex parte James", r:"'No court is equal to the examination' of motives"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.060] says Lord Cranworth's formulation covers both actual and possible conflicts. In Boardman v Phipps, the House of Lords majority considered it irrelevant that the possibility of conflict was remote at best; so long as the possibility existed, the rule applied. Lord Upjohn, dissenting, would test whether a reasonable man, looking at the facts, would think there was a 'real sensible possibility of conflict' ([09.061]); his more pragmatic test has been applied in a good number of cases, including in Singapore (Dayco Products v Ong Cheng Aik). In Ng Eng Ghee v Mamata Kapildev Dave, the Court of Appeal said the position is not settled in Singapore but gave three reasons for the stricter approach ([09.062]–[09.063]). One relies on Lord Eldon LC in Ex parte James: 'no court is equal to the examination and ascertainment' of a fiduciary's state of mind.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.060]–[09.063], fns 246–256", takeaway:"Boardman: any possibility; Upjohn: real sensible possibility; Ng Eng Ghee leans strict." },

{ id:"c9x-074", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What three reasons did the Court of Appeal in Ng Eng Ghee give for preferring the stricter Boardman approach to conflicts?",
  options:[
    {t:"The rule is prophylactic and a wide view deters; inquiring into motives is notoriously difficult; and fiduciaries can obscure actual conflicts", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.062]–[09.063] sets out the reasons, given in the context of agents' fiduciary duties. First, the no-conflict duty is prophylactic, meant to deter the fiduciary from deviating from loyalty; a wide view of potential conflict discourages him from 'trying his luck'. Second, it is notoriously difficult to inquire into a person's state of mind or motives, and so to establish that an actual conflict occurred; as Lord Eldon LC said in Ex parte James, 'no court is equal to the examination and ascertainment' of these facts. Third, and relatedly, fiduciaries are quite capable of obscuring actual conflicts, making them hard to detect. A rule operating on theoretical conflicts therefore best serves the deterrent objective. The court noted the position in Singapore is not yet settled."},
    {t:"Fiduciaries are usually dishonest; courts are lenient; and conflicts are rare", correct:false,
     why:"None of these were the reasons. The reasoning rests on deterrence and the difficulty of proof."},
    {t:"Lord Upjohn's test is binding; Boardman is English; and agents differ from directors", correct:false,
     why:"The court favoured the majority in Boardman, not Lord Upjohn, and gave reasons of principle."},
    {t:"Strict rules raise revenue; reduce litigation; and protect creditors", correct:false,
     why:"These were not the reasons. The focus is the rule's prophylactic, deterrent function."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.062]–[09.063], fns 251–256", takeaway:"Ng Eng Ghee: deterrence, difficulty of proving motive, ease of hiding conflicts." },

{ id:"c9x-075", lesson:"9K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Aspen Ltd makes kitchen equipment. Its director Ray privately invests in a start-up developing pet-tracking software. Aspen has never shown interest in software or pets, and Ray sees no realistic chance that the two businesses will ever overlap.",
  prompt:"How might the Boardman majority and Lord Upjohn's tests differ on whether there is a conflict?",
  options:[
    {t:"Upjohn's 'real sensible possibility' test would probably find no conflict; the stricter Boardman approach, where even a remote possibility suffices, carries more risk for Ray", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.060] says the Boardman majority treated it as irrelevant that the possibility of conflict was remote; so long as the possibility existed, the rule applied. Taken together with the wide view of a director's duty, the theoretical test means the company would in theory benefit from virtually all entrepreneurial opportunities, so a director exploiting almost any opportunity risks a conflict. Lord Upjohn's dissent ([09.061]) asks whether a reasonable man would see a 'real sensible possibility of conflict'. On these facts, a kitchen equipment maker with no interest in software or pets, a reasonable person would likely see no real sensible possibility, so Upjohn's test points to no conflict. Ng Eng Ghee leaned towards the stricter view, though the position is unsettled in Singapore ([09.062]). Prudent advice for Ray is to disclose and obtain consent."},
    {t:"Both tests find a conflict, because all investments conflict", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Upjohn's test is designed to exclude fanciful conflicts like this one."},
    {t:"Neither test finds a conflict, so the position is certain", correct:false,
     why:"The stricter Boardman approach could still treat a remote possibility as enough, and Singapore has not settled which test applies."},
    {t:"Only Lord Upjohn's test would find a conflict", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the tests: Upjohn's is the narrower one."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.060]–[09.062]", takeaway:"Remote possibility: Boardman (maybe conflict) vs Upjohn (real sensible possibility needed)." },

{ id:"c9x-076", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Apart from personal interests, what other kind of conflict does the no-conflict rule cover, and where does it commonly arise?",
  options:[
    {t:"Conflicts of duties, where the director's duty to the company conflicts with a duty to another person, commonly with multiple directorships and nominee directors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.064] says the no-conflict rule applies not only to conflicts involving personal interests but also to a conflict of duties, where the duty a director owes the company conflicts with a duty he owes to someone else at the same time. Koh [09.065] says this commonly arises where a director holds directorships in more than one company, especially competitors. Koh [09.066] identifies the nominee director, appointed on an understanding that he represents someone other than the company, such as a JV partner, investor, creditor or employees, as another common case. In R v Byrnes, the High Court of Australia stressed that directors of both Jeffcott and Magnacrete were in a position of conflicting duties ([09.036])."},
    {t:"Conflicts between shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholder disputes are handled through oppression and derivative actions. The no-conflict rule concerns the director's own duties and interests."},
    {t:"Conflicts between creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Creditors' competing claims are dealt with by insolvency law, not the director's no-conflict rule."},
    {t:"Conflicts between statutes", correct:false,
     why:"The rule concerns fiduciary loyalty, not conflicts of law."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.064]–[09.066]", takeaway:"Conflict of duties: multiple directorships, nominee directors." },

{ id:"cl9-051", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"A director wants to join the board of a competitor. What does the chapter advise?",
  options:[
    {t:"Get the company's fully informed consent first; the Mashonaland case is a very limited principle and the law doesn't distinguish executive from non-executive directors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.065] notes the odd English authority, London and Mashonaland Exploration v New Mashonaland Exploration, suggesting nothing is inherently objectionable in a director sitting on competing boards provided he breaches no express restriction and discloses no confidential information. But in In Plus Group, Sedley LJ described the 'Mashonaland principle' as 'a very limited one'. Lord Mayo, the director in that case, had never acted as a director or attended a board meeting, which may explain Chitty J's decision. It is tempting to distinguish him as a non-executive director, but the law does not distinguish executive from non-executive directors for fiduciary purposes. So all directors are well advised to obtain the company's fully informed consent before taking a board seat at another company in the same line of business (as is the general law position, fn 265). Executive directors' employment contracts are also likely to prohibit competition."},
    {t:"Go ahead, as long as he discloses no confidential information", correct:false,
     why:"That is the Mashonaland view, which In Plus Group described as very limited. Koh advises obtaining consent."},
    {t:"He may do so freely if he is non-executive", correct:false,
     why:"The law does not distinguish executive and non-executive directors for fiduciary purposes."},
    {t:"It is automatically a criminal offence", correct:false,
     why:"No such offence exists. The issue is a fiduciary conflict, resolved by informed consent."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.064]–[09.065], fns 257–265", takeaway:"Competing directorship: get informed consent; Mashonaland is very limited." },

{ id:"c9x-077", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why might Chitty J have refused an injunction in London and Mashonaland Exploration Co v New Mashonaland Exploration Co?",
  options:[
    {t:"Lord Mayo had never actually acted as a director or attended a board meeting, and had not agreed not to join a similar company, so subjecting him to the no-conflict rule may have seemed inappropriate", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.065] quotes Brooke LJ's account in In Plus Group: Lord Mayo was director and chairman of the plaintiff company but never acted as a director or attended a board meeting, and never agreed, expressly or in the articles, not to become a director of a similar company. The plaintiff, which had used his name in its prospectus, objected when a rival formed four months later named him at the head of its board. Koh suggests that because he had never acted as a director, Chitty J may have considered it inappropriate to apply the no-conflict rule. That explains why modern courts treat the case narrowly. It is not a general licence to sit on competing boards."},
    {t:"Because competing directorships are always lawful", correct:false,
     why:"Koh and In Plus Group treat Mashonaland as very limited, not as a general rule of lawfulness."},
    {t:"Because Lord Mayo had obtained the plaintiff's consent", correct:false,
     why:"No consent is described. The explanation lies in his never having acted as a director."},
    {t:"Because the companies were in different industries", correct:false,
     why:"Both companies were formed for the same purpose; that was the plaintiff's complaint."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.065], fns 259–263", takeaway:"Mashonaland: Lord Mayo never acted; a very limited principle." },

{ id:"c9x-078", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is the nominee director's position described as having 'the greatest potential for conflict', and when does it become intractable?",
  options:[
    {t:"He owes his appointment to the nominator; usually his loyalties are known and aligned so he can serve two masters, but it becomes intractable when the company's and nominator's interests diverge", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.066] says nominee directors are commonplace in group enterprises and joint ventures, and investors, creditors or employees are often given board representation. The commercial reality is that the nominee owes his appointment to his nominator, so by his very appointment he is placed in a position with the greatest potential for conflict. Often there is little difficulty with the appointment itself, because the company knows of his extraneous loyalties, and in many cases the appointor's and the company's interests are aligned, letting him 'serve two masters to the great advantage of both' (Boulting). The position becomes quite intractable when the interests of the company and his appointor diverge (Scottish Co-operative Wholesale v Meyer), as in Golden Village."},
    {t:"Because nominees are never permitted by law", correct:false,
     why:"Nominee appointments are lawful and common. The difficulty is managing divergent loyalties."},
    {t:"Because nominees owe duties only to their appointor", correct:false,
     why:"Nominees owe the same duties to the company as any other director (OCBC v Justlogin)."},
    {t:"Because nominees cannot vote at board meetings", correct:false,
     why:"Nominees vote like other directors, which is precisely why their loyalties matter."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.066], fns 266–269", takeaway:"Nominee: aligned interests fine; divergence is the problem." },

{ id:"cl9-052", lesson:"9K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A cinema joint venture company's director was nominated by one JV partner. When the company sues a company in that partner's group, the director files affidavits for the group company.",
  prompt:"What did the court decide in Golden Village Multiplex v Phoon Chiong Kit, and how does the chapter view it?",
  options:[
    {t:"He breached his duty by openly siding against the company; the chapter calls this technically correct but notes he was between a rock and a hard place", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.067] describes Golden Village Multiplex v Phoon Chiong Kit. The plaintiff was a JV vehicle between an Australian and a Hong Kong cinema group, with each partner entitled under the shareholders' agreement to nominate three directors. The defendant was a Hong Kong nominee and also sat on the board of another Hong Kong group company, which became embroiled in a dispute with the plaintiff. When the plaintiff sued the Hong Kong company, the defendant filed affidavits on its behalf. The court found him in breach of his duties to the plaintiff for openly siding with the Hong Kong company. Koh accepts the decision is technically correct, but says he was between a rock and a hard place: the JV partners had fallen out, the plaintiff's and Hong Kong partner's interests no longer aligned, and it would have been unrealistic to expect him to remain concerned with his loyalty to the plaintiff."},
    {t:"No breach, because nominees may favour their appointor", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore applies the orthodox rule that nominees owe the same duties to the company as other directors."},
    {t:"No breach, because the shareholders' agreement allowed it", correct:false,
     why:"The shareholders' agreement allowed nomination, not siding against the company in litigation."},
    {t:"The chapter says the decision was plainly wrong", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says the decision was technically correct, while sympathising with the nominee's dilemma."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.066]–[09.067], fns 270–271", takeaway:"Golden Village: nominee siding against the company = breach (technically correct, harsh)." },

{ id:"c9x-079", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What alternative approach to nominee directors have some Australian and New Zealand cases taken?",
  options:[
    {t:"The appointment of a nominee to act for another party may itself be in the company's interests, so by agreeing to it the company may accept an adjusted form of fiduciary responsibility", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.068] says Golden Village shows how hard it is for nominees held to an undifferentiated standard. Some Australian (Levin v Clark) and New Zealand (Berlei Hestia v Fernyhough) cases accept that the very appointment of a nominee to represent and act for someone other than the company may itself be conducive to the company's interests. In that case, it is reasonable to conclude that the company, by agreeing to the appointment, accepted an adjusted form of fiduciary responsibility, one permitting the nominee to act for that other party. The degree of adjustment depends on the circumstances of the appointment. Singapore courts have generally endorsed the orthodox position, though Koh sees possible openings for flexibility."},
    {t:"Nominees owe no duties to the company at all", correct:false,
     why:"Even the adjusted approach keeps fiduciary duties; it only modifies their scope."},
    {t:"Nominees must resign when interests diverge", correct:false,
     why:"That is a practical option, not the doctrinal approach described in the Australian and NZ cases."},
    {t:"Nominees owe duties only to creditors", correct:false,
     why:"Nominee duties are owed to the company. The adjustment concerns how far the nominee may act for his appointor."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.068], fns 272–274", takeaway:"AU/NZ: agreeing to a nominee may mean accepting adjusted duties." },

{ id:"cl9-053", lesson:"9K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the Singapore position on nominee directors' duties, and where might flexibility enter?",
  options:[
    {t:"Orthodox: every director owes the same responsibility to the company (OCBC v Justlogin), but asking what obligations a fiduciary owes leaves room to vary the ambit of the duty", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.068] says Singapore courts have generally endorsed the orthodox position. In OCBC v Justlogin, the Court of Appeal affirmed it is 'settled law that every director owes the same responsibility to the company as a whole. It is no different where a director is the nominee of a group of shareholders or creditors'. But in Townsing v Jenton, Chan Sek Keong CJ prefaced his discussion of directors' duties with Frankfurter J's remark in SEC v Chenery: saying someone is a fiduciary only begins the analysis; one must ask what obligations he owes, how he failed to discharge them, and the consequences. That suggests courts may be open to a more flexible view. The no-conflict rule is strict when it applies, but whether it applies, and what the boundaries of the duty are, can depend on the surrounding circumstances."},
    {t:"Nominees owe lesser duties by statute", correct:false,
     why:"No statute reduces nominees' duties. OCBC v Justlogin affirms equal responsibility."},
    {t:"Nominees owe duties only to their appointor", correct:false,
     why:"Nominees owe duties to the company as a whole."},
    {t:"Singapore has adopted the adjusted-duty approach", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore has generally followed the orthodox approach; Koh only suggests possible openings for flexibility."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.068], fns 275–279", takeaway:"Singapore: orthodox (Justlogin); Townsing hints at flexible scope." },

{ id:"c9x-080", lesson:"9K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bank lends heavily to Rowan Pte Ltd and, under the loan agreement, nominates Yusuf to Rowan's board. Rowan breaches a loan covenant. The bank asks Yusuf to vote for an immediate fire sale of Rowan's best asset to repay it, though the rest of the board believes a slower sale would yield far more.",
  prompt:"On the Singapore position, how should Yusuf approach the vote?",
  options:[
    {t:"He owes the same duty to Rowan as any director and must decide what is in Rowan's interests, not simply follow the bank", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.068] says Singapore courts have generally followed the orthodox position: in OCBC v Justlogin the Court of Appeal said every director owes the same responsibility to the company as a whole, and it is no different where the director is a nominee of creditors. Koh [09.041] adds that nominees are particularly vulnerable to fettering their discretion by following their appointor. Here the bank's interest (quick repayment) diverges from Rowan's (maximising value), which Koh [09.066] says is when the nominee's position becomes intractable. Yusuf must exercise his own judgment on what is in Rowan's interests. If he cannot do so, his options include disclosing the conflict and abstaining, or resigning. The bank's protection lies in its contractual remedies under the loan, not in its nominee's vote. Townsing hints at possible flexibility in the scope of nominee duties, but on current authority he cannot simply vote as instructed."},
    {t:"Vote as the bank instructs, since that is why he was appointed", correct:false,
     why:"That would fetter his discretion and breach the orthodox duty owed to the company as a whole."},
    {t:"Vote as the bank instructs, because Rowan agreed to his nomination", correct:false,
     why:"That is the adjusted-duty view from some Australian and NZ cases, which Singapore has not generally adopted."},
    {t:"He has no duty because the company is in breach of covenant", correct:false,
     why:"A covenant breach does not suspend his duties. If anything, financial difficulty brings creditors' interests as a whole into view, not one creditor's."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.041], [09.066], [09.068]", takeaway:"Creditor nominee must still act for the company as a whole." }

]);

;
/* ---- data/t1p3/9L.js ---- */
/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9L Secret Profits & Corporate Opportunities (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.069]–[09.078], fns 280–315. Replaces the 9L questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-054", lesson:"9L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A director must account for a profit made from his position even if he acted honestly, the company lost nothing, and the company couldn't have made the profit itself.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.069] says a director may not retain any profit made out of his position unless he has the company's fully informed consent. The profits may come from any use of property, information or opportunities he has access to because of his directorship. The rule is strict: liability to account arises simply because the profit was made in the proscribed circumstances (Regal (Hastings)). It is irrelevant that the director acted honestly throughout, that the company suffered no damage, or even that the company itself could not have obtained the benefit or made the profit (In Plus Group). The strictness is the point: it removes any incentive to use the position for personal gain and spares the court from investigating motives and counterfactuals."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Each of these arguments is expressly irrelevant to the no-profit rule. Only the company's fully informed consent protects the director."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.069], fns 280–281", takeaway:"No-profit: honesty, no loss, company's inability — all irrelevant." },

{ id:"cl9-055", lesson:"9L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company sets up a subsidiary to lease a cinema. The landlord demands more paid-up capital than the directors honestly think the company can afford, so they subscribe for the balance personally. The shares are later sold at a profit.",
  prompt:"What did the House of Lords decide in Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver?",
  options:[
    {t:"The directors had to account: they profited by reason and in the course of their fiduciary relationship, and only a shareholder resolution could have saved them", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.070] describes Regal (Hastings). The company formed a subsidiary to take a cinema lease. The landlord required a minimum paid-up capital that, in the directors' honest opinion, the company could not afford, so the directors subscribed for the balance themselves. All the subsidiary's shares were later sold and the directors made a profit. The company, under new controllers, sued. The House of Lords held them liable to account because 'while standing in a fiduciary relationship to [the company], they have by reason and in course of that fiduciary relationship made a profit'. The only defence would have been 'a resolution (either antecedent or subsequent) of the [company's] shareholders in general meeting'. Their good faith, and the company's inability to afford the shares, were irrelevant."},
    {t:"No liability, because they acted in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith is irrelevant to the no-profit rule ([09.069])."},
    {t:"No liability, because the company couldn't afford the shares", correct:false,
     why:"The company's inability to take the opportunity is irrelevant ([09.069])."},
    {t:"A board resolution would have sufficed", correct:false,
     why:"The House of Lords said only a shareholders' resolution in general meeting would have been a defence."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.070], fns 282–284", takeaway:"Regal: profit by reason and in course of fiduciary relationship → account; only shareholders can approve." },

{ id:"c9x-081", lesson:"9L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Directors of Bay Ltd, like those in Regal (Hastings), personally take up shares in a deal the company could not afford, and later profit. Variation A: before subscribing, they disclose everything to a general meeting, which approves. Variation B: they say nothing beforehand, but after the profit is made a general meeting, with full knowledge, passes a resolution approving it.",
  prompt:"On Lord Russell's statement in Regal, which variations give the directors a defence?",
  options:[
    {t:"Both: the House of Lords said the defence was a shareholders' resolution in general meeting, 'either antecedent or subsequent'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.070] quotes the House of Lords: the only defence available to the directors would have been 'a resolution (either antecedent or subsequent) of the [company's] shareholders in general meeting'. Variation A is antecedent approval: informed consent obtained before the profit is made, which Koh [09.019] says means there is no breach at all. Variation B is subsequent ratification of the profit by the shareholders with full knowledge. Both are recognised in Regal as defences at general law. Note the limits: the approval must be fully informed, given by the shareholders (not the board), and, after the event, ratification may be restricted where wrongdoers control the vote or the breach is unratifiable (ch 10). For any parallel statutory breach of s 157, ratification is doubtful ([09.018])."},
    {t:"Only A: subsequent approval is never effective", correct:false,
     why:"Regal expressly refers to antecedent or subsequent shareholder resolutions."},
    {t:"Only B: prior approval is not recognised", correct:false,
     why:"Prior informed consent is the clearest defence: it prevents breach altogether."},
    {t:"Neither: the no-profit rule cannot be waived", correct:false,
     why:"The rule is strict but can be waived by the company's fully informed consent."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.019], [09.070]", takeaway:"Regal defence: shareholders' resolution, before or after." },

{ id:"c9x-082", lesson:"9L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The no-profit rule applies only if a conflict between the director's duty and interest is first established.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.071] says the no-profit rule does not depend on establishing a conflict of duty and interest, although in many cases a conflict will be discernible. What is crucial is that the director benefited from the unauthorised use of his position. In Regal (Hastings) there was use of the fiduciary position: the directors revised the original plan for the company to acquire the assets and 'framed resolutions by which they made a profit for themselves'. This is why Koh [09.051] treats the two rules as possibly conceptually distinct: the no-profit rule targets actual misuse of position for gain, while the no-conflict rule targets being swayed by personal interest."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"A conflict is often present but is not a prerequisite. Benefit from unauthorised use of position is the key."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.071], fn 285", takeaway:"No-profit needs unauthorised use of position + benefit, not proof of conflict." },

{ id:"cl9-056", lesson:"9L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A company's business consists entirely of contracts tendered for a railway company. Its directors arrange for a new contract to be awarded to a company they formed instead.",
  prompt:"What does Cook v Deeks illustrate?",
  options:[
    {t:"An egregious use of position to divert a corporate opportunity to themselves", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.071] calls Cook v Deeks a more egregious use of position than Regal. The company's business consisted entirely of contracts tendered for a particular railway company. On the occasion in question, the defendant directors caused the railway company to award a new contract to a company they had formed for the purpose. The evidence showed they had orchestrated the diversion by using their position to exclude the company from acquiring the contract. Because the contract was squarely within the company's existing business, it is also the paradigm of an opportunity the company had a present, extant interest in (fn 295), which is one possible definition of a 'corporate opportunity'."},
    {t:"A legitimate exploitation of an opportunity the company had rejected", correct:false,
     why:"The company did not reject the opportunity; the directors excluded it."},
    {t:"A case where no fiduciary duty was owed", correct:false,
     why:"The directors owed fiduciary duties and breached them."},
    {t:"A case of mere negligence", correct:false,
     why:"This was deliberate diversion, not carelessness."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.071], fns 286–287", takeaway:"Cook v Deeks: directors divert the company's core contract to themselves." },

{ id:"c9x-083", lesson:"9L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Lark Ltd's board, acting in good faith, decides not to pursue an offer to buy a mining claim because it lacks funds. Later, one of the directors buys the claim personally and profits. He argues the opportunity ceased to be a corporate opportunity once the board rejected it.",
  prompt:"How did Lord Wright in Regal (Hastings) treat a similar argument?",
  options:[
    {t:"He rejected it as 'dead in the teeth' of the fiduciary principle; the fiduciary may be the only person in the world who cannot take the opportunity", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.072] asks whether an opportunity the company has bona fide rejected, usually through the board, should cease to be a 'corporate' opportunity. That argument succeeded in the English Court of Appeal in Regal (Hastings), but in the House of Lords Lord Wright made short shrift of it, saying it was 'dead in the teeth' of the fiduciary principle. It has long been recognised that the principle often means the fiduciary is quite literally the 'only person in the world who could not avail himself of the opportunity'. Footnote 292 notes Peso Silver Mines v Cropper, where a Canadian court accepted the rejection argument, but Koh [09.073] favours the dissent there. A director in Lark's position should obtain the company's informed consent, ideally shareholders' approval, before taking the opportunity."},
    {t:"He accepted it, holding that a bona fide board rejection frees the director", correct:false,
     why:"That was the Court of Appeal's view, which the House of Lords reversed."},
    {t:"He held the board's decision made the director a trustee of the claim", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Wright's point was the strictness of the fiduciary principle; the remedy is an account of profits."},
    {t:"He said the issue depended on the company's line of business", correct:false,
     why:"The 'line of business' test is a separate American approach (Guth v Loft) discussed by Koh at [09.073]."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.072], fns 292–294", takeaway:"Board rejection doesn't free the director (Lord Wright: 'dead in the teeth')." },

{ id:"cl9-057", lesson:"9L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the chapter's concern about defining duties by reference to 'corporate opportunities'?",
  options:[
    {t:"No workable definition exists, so the concept brings uncertainty into an area that demands 'the punctilio of an honor the most sensitive'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.072] says the no-profit rule is often expressed in terms of corporate opportunities: an opportunity the company is actively pursuing has been described as 'properly belonging to the company' or a 'maturing business opportunity', and diverting it is akin to misusing company property. But should a director be free to exploit any opportunity outside that definition? The difficulty is a workable definition. Koh [09.073] lists candidates: opportunities in which the company has a present extant interest (Cook v Deeks), those it is actively pursuing (Canaero), or, more widely, a 'line of business' test (Guth v Loft), which raises the further question of how to define the line of business. There are no clear answers. Using the concept to define fiduciary duties introduces ambiguity into an area where the standard is 'not honesty alone, but the punctilio of an honor the most sensitive' (Cardozo J in Meinhard v Salmon), and detracts from the core rationale of undivided loyalty."},
    {t:"It is too strict on directors", correct:false,
     why:"Koh's concern is the opposite: a corporate opportunity concept might loosen the strict rule."},
    {t:"Courts have uniformly rejected it", correct:false,
     why:"Several cases use the concept, especially for resigning directors ([09.077]–[09.078])."},
    {t:"It applies only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"The concept is not limited by company type."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.072]–[09.073], fns 288–298", takeaway:"'Corporate opportunity' lacks a workable definition; risks diluting loyalty." },

{ id:"c9x-084", lesson:"9L", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each candidate definition of a 'corporate opportunity' to its source.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Opportunity in which the company has a present extant interest", r:"Cook v Deeks"},
    {l:"Maturing opportunity the company is actively pursuing", r:"Canadian Aero Services v O'Malley"},
    {l:"Opportunity within the company's line of business", r:"Guth v Loft"},
    {l:"Strict enforcement so complexity can't shield fraud", r:"Norris JA dissenting in Peso Silver Mines"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.073] canvasses possible definitions of a corporate opportunity. One looks to opportunities in which the company has a present extant interest, as in Cook v Deeks, where the contract was the company's core business (fn 295). Another looks to opportunities the company is actively pursuing, the 'maturing business opportunity' of Canaero (fn 296). A wider approach is the American 'line of business' test from Guth v Loft (fn 297), which raises the problem of how to define the company's line of business. Koh concludes there are no clear answers, and quotes Norris JA, dissenting in Peso Silver Mines v Cropper: 'the complexities of modern business are a very good reason why the rule should be enforced strictly in order that such complexities may not be used as a smoke screen or shield behind which fraud might be perpetrated'.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.073], fns 295–300", takeaway:"Extant interest · actively pursued · line of business; Norris JA: enforce strictly." },

{ id:"c9x-085", lesson:"9L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What practical conclusion does Koh draw for a director who wants to pursue a commercially attractive opportunity personally?",
  options:[
    {t:"He should get contractual dispensation or the company's consent; otherwise he risks breach, since settled rules matter more than perfect ones here", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.073] says that if self-denial of competition and commercially attractive opportunities is the rule, it should be clear to a director that if he wants to pursue one individually, he must either obtain contractual dispensation or seek corporate consent, or he risks breach. She adds Brandeis J's remark in Burnet v Coronado Oil & Gas: 'it is more important that the applicable rules of law be settled than that it be settled right'. The point is that a clear, strict rule lets directors plan: they know they must ask first. A vague corporate-opportunity test, by contrast, invites directors to gamble that a court will later find the opportunity was not 'corporate'."},
    {t:"He can proceed if he honestly believes the opportunity is not corporate", correct:false,
     why:"Honest belief does not protect him under the strict no-profit rule."},
    {t:"He can proceed if the company lacks funds", correct:false,
     why:"The company's inability to take the opportunity is irrelevant (Regal)."},
    {t:"He can proceed after informing one other director", correct:false,
     why:"Informal notice to one director is not corporate consent."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.073], fn 301", takeaway:"Want the opportunity? Get contractual dispensation or corporate consent first." },

{ id:"c9x-086", lesson:"9L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: As a general rule, a director's fiduciary duties that constrained him during his directorship end when he resigns, though he may still be liable for some profits made afterwards.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.074] says that as a general rule, once a director resigns he should no longer be subject to the fiduciary duties that constrained him while in office, because those duties exist only because of the special relationship underpinning them (A-G v Blake). Nevertheless, courts have accepted that in certain circumstances a director may remain liable to account for profits made after he ceases to be a director. This led to the view that fiduciary obligations may continue after resignation, with IDC v Cooley often cited. Koh [09.076] argues the better analysis of Cooley is that liability rests on a breach committed while he was still a director. Statutory liability for misuse of information acquired as an officer can also extend after resignation (s 157(5), 9G)."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"The general rule is that fiduciary duties end with the relationship. The exceptions arise from breaches during office or exploitation of opportunities linked to it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.074], fns 302–303", takeaway:"Duties generally end on resignation; liability can still reach later profits." },

{ id:"cl9-058", lesson:"9L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A managing director fails to win a contract for his company. The client later approaches him personally. He falsely claims ill health, resigns and takes the contract for himself. The company had only a 10% chance of winning it.",
  prompt:"How does the chapter analyse Industrial Development Consultants v Cooley?",
  options:[
    {t:"He was liable because, while still a director, he failed to pass on information he had a duty to convey, putting duty and interest in conflict", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.075] describes IDC v Cooley. The defendant, managing director responsible for new business, failed to win a contract for the company. The client then approached him personally, and he learned both of new opportunities and that if he resigned quickly he could likely get a valuable contract for himself. He falsely (and dishonestly, fn 305) claimed ill health, resigned and secured the contract. He was held accountable even though the company had only a 10% chance of obtaining it. Koh [09.076] says Roskill J did not base the decision on a post-resignation fiduciary duty. Liability arose because, while still a director, he knew of the opportunity and did not convey information that was 'of concern to … and was relevant for [the company] to know … which it was his duty to pass on'. Guarding it for himself put duty and interest in conflict. The later profit flowed from that breach, and resigning could not be used to evade the strict ethic."},
    {t:"Fiduciary duties continue indefinitely after resignation", correct:false,
     why:"Koh specifically argues the case is better analysed as a breach committed during office, not a continuing post-resignation duty."},
    {t:"He wasn't liable because the company had only a 10% chance", correct:false,
     why:"He was liable despite the 10% chance. The company's likely inability is irrelevant to the strict rules."},
    {t:"He was liable only for lying about his health", correct:false,
     why:"The lie was part of the scheme, but liability rested on failing to pass on the information while a director."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.074]–[09.076], fns 304–307", takeaway:"Cooley: breach during office (failure to pass on information); resignation can't evade it." },

{ id:"cl9-060", lesson:"9L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A director who decides to compete after leaving, and takes preliminary steps while still in office, is necessarily in breach of duty.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.077] says it is sometimes difficult to pin a director to a breach of the no-conflict rule if he did not exploit the situation while a director. Merely forming an intention to set up in competition after leaving (Coleman Taymar v Oakes), or even taking some preliminary steps towards that end (Balston v Headline Filters), is not necessarily a breach of any duty. The line is crossed when the director exploits the company's information, property or opportunities while still in office, or diverts business, as in Cooley. Because of this difficulty, some cases have turned to the concept of 'corporate opportunities' to impose liability on former directors."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Intention and preliminary steps are not necessarily breaches. Actual exploitation while in office is what attracts liability."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.077], fns 308–309", takeaway:"Planning to compete ≠ breach; exploiting while in office = breach." },

{ id:"cl9-059", lesson:"9L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What are Laskin J's three conditions in Canadian Aero Services v O'Malley for holding a former director liable?",
  options:[
    {t:"A maturing business opportunity, which the company was actively pursuing, and a resignation prompted or influenced by a wish to take it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.077] says some cases use the concept of corporate opportunities to impose liability on former directors, an approach traced to Laskin J in the Supreme Court of Canada in Canadian Aero Services v O'Malley. He set three conditions: first, there must be a 'maturing business opportunity'; second, the company must have been 'actively pursuing' it; and third, the director's resignation 'may fairly be said to have been prompted or influenced by a wish to acquire for himself' that opportunity. Koh [09.078] notes that a number of cases, including in Singapore (Innovative Corp v Ow Chun Ming), have accepted this as a viable basis for liability, though defining 'maturing' and 'actively pursued' is difficult."},
    {t:"Dishonesty, loss and causation", correct:false,
     why:"None of these is among Laskin J's conditions."},
    {t:"Any opportunity in the company's line of business", correct:false,
     why:"That is the wider Guth v Loft test, not Canaero."},
    {t:"Breach of a non-compete clause", correct:false,
     why:"Canaero is a fiduciary test; it does not depend on a contractual non-compete."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.077]–[09.078], fns 311–313", takeaway:"Canaero: maturing opportunity + actively pursued + resignation prompted by it." },

{ id:"c9x-087", lesson:"9L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"For six months, Fern Pte Ltd, through its director Kai, has been negotiating a large contract with a hospital group; terms are nearly agreed. Kai resigns, saying he wants a career change, and a month later his new firm signs the same contract with the hospital group.",
  prompt:"Applying Canaero, is Kai likely to be liable to account?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: the contract was a maturing opportunity Fern was actively pursuing, and his resignation may fairly be said to have been prompted by a wish to take it", correct:true,
     why:"Laskin J's three conditions in Canaero ([09.077]) are a maturing business opportunity, active pursuit by the company, and a resignation that may fairly be said to have been prompted or influenced by a wish to acquire the opportunity. Fern had been negotiating for six months and terms were nearly agreed, which fits 'maturing' and 'actively pursued' comfortably. Kai's stated reason for leaving is undercut by his new firm signing the same contract a month later; the court may fairly infer the resignation was prompted by the opportunity. Singapore cases have accepted this basis of liability ([09.078]). His position also resembles Cooley: if he took steps while still a director, he may be liable for breach during office as well."},
    {t:"No: fiduciary duties end on resignation", correct:false,
     why:"Canaero is precisely a basis for liability after resignation where the conditions are met."},
    {t:"No: he said he wanted a career change", correct:false,
     why:"The court assesses whether resignation may fairly be said to have been prompted by the opportunity, looking at all the circumstances, not just his stated reason."},
    {t:"Only if Fern can show it would certainly have won the contract", correct:false,
     why:"Certainty of success is not required. In Cooley, the company had only a 10% chance."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.077]–[09.078]", takeaway:"Near-final negotiations + timed resignation → Canaero liability." },

{ id:"c9x-088", lesson:"9L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What difficulty does Koh see in Laskin J's Canaero formulation?",
  options:[
    {t:"'Maturing' and 'actively pursued' are sliding-scale qualities that resist circumscription, so the question may come down to how egregious the court thinks the conduct is", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.078] accepts that a number of cases have adopted Canaero as a viable basis for imposing liability on a director who competes after resigning. But the difficulty with any concept of 'corporate opportunities' is defining its boundaries. In Laskin J's formulation, the opportunity must be both 'maturing' and 'actively pursued' by the company, and neither lends itself easily to circumscription, since both describe qualities assessed on a sliding scale. Ultimately the question may boil down to how egregious the court considers the particular director's conduct, which Koh says is 'not particularly helpful' as a guide. This ties back to her preference for a strict, settled rule ([09.073])."},
    {t:"It is too lenient on directors", correct:false,
     why:"Koh's concern is uncertainty of boundaries, not leniency as such."},
    {t:"Singapore courts have rejected it", correct:false,
     why:"Koh notes cases, including in Singapore, have accepted it."},
    {t:"It requires proof of criminal intent", correct:false,
     why:"Canaero has no criminal intent element."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.078], fns 313–315", takeaway:"Canaero's terms are sliding-scale; outcome may turn on egregiousness." }

]);

;
