/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 9 · Lesson 9N Proper Purposes (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 9 [09.088]–[09.102], fns 341–389. Replaces the 9N questions in companyLaw9.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl9-066", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Is the duty to act for proper purposes separate from the duty of good faith?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: it is a separate duty, tested objectively, so honest directors can still breach it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.088] says directors' powers are often granted in broad terms, but they must be exercised for the purposes for which they were conferred, not for any collateral purpose (Lord Greene MR in Re Smith and Fawcett). Koh [09.089] notes an initial debate: some argued proper purposes is simply part of the single duty to act honestly in the company's interests, since where the statute and constitution are silent no other 'purpose' constrains the power. But Koh [09.090] says the accepted position, even before codification in England and Australia, is that powers are constrained by a concept of proper purposes separate from the good faith duty and objectively tested. So directors' honest belief that they acted in the company's interests will not protect them if the court finds they acted for purposes outside the permitted limits. The doctrine's independence is now confirmed by statute in the UK (Companies Act 2006, s 171(b)) and Australia (s 181)."},
    {t:"No: it is simply part of the good faith duty", correct:false,
     why:"That is the 'unitary' view, which Koh says has not prevailed; the accepted position treats proper purposes as separate."},
    {t:"It is a purely subjective duty", correct:false,
     why:"Proper purposes is objectively tested, which is why good faith is no defence."},
    {t:"It applies only where the constitution has an objects clause", correct:false,
     why:"Objects clauses are one guide to permitted purposes, but the doctrine applies to all directors' powers, and objects clauses are now optional ([09.093])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.088]–[09.090], fns 341–352", takeaway:"Proper purposes: separate, objective; good faith no defence." },

{ id:"c9x-096", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did the court in Goh Kim Hai Edward v Pacific Can say about purpose, and how does it relate to the 'unitary' view?",
  options:[
    {t:"An action benefiting both company and an individual is fine if chiefly motivated by the company's interests, but improper if mainly to benefit the individual; this reads purpose largely through the lens of the company's interests", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.089] quotes the judge in Goh Kim Hai: 'The purpose of a director's action must be to benefit the company and not any particular individual. If the action does benefit both the company and an individual, the action is not a breach of duty as long as the considerations which led to its enactment were chiefly considerations of the interests of the company. If, however, the main consideration is benefiting the individual, then the action is taken for an improper purpose.' Koh says the judge appeared sympathetic to the unitary view, under which a breach of the proper purposes duty is no more than a breach of the general obligation to pursue the company's interests in good faith (see Teck Corp v Millar). Koh [09.090] explains that the accepted position nonetheless treats proper purposes as separate and objective, and that this is likely Singapore's position too."},
    {t:"Purpose is irrelevant if the company benefits at all", correct:false,
     why:"Goh Kim Hai made the main consideration decisive: if it is to benefit an individual, the purpose is improper."},
    {t:"Any benefit to an individual is automatically improper", correct:false,
     why:"Goh Kim Hai accepted an action could benefit both company and individual, so long as the company's interests were the chief consideration."},
    {t:"The court rejected any link between purpose and the company's interests", correct:false,
     why:"The judgment links them closely, which is why Koh sees sympathy for the unitary view."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.089], fns 344–347", takeaway:"Goh Kim Hai: chief consideration must be the company; reflects the unitary view." },

{ id:"c9x-097", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is likely to be the position on proper purposes in Singapore?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not considered in detail, but Quentin Loh J in Scintronix said the doctrine asks whether a power conferred for specific purposes was used for its intended ends, not whether the director was bona fide; Koh thinks this is representative", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.090] says the doctrine has not been considered in detail by Singapore courts. But Quentin Loh J in Scintronix Corp v Ho Kang Peng (High Court) said that 'the proper purpose doctrine is not conventionally understood … as a proxy for assessing bona fides, but is instead concerned with whether a particular power conferred for specific purposes has been exercised for the correct and intended ends'. Koh thinks it likely this represents the Singapore position. Note the tension with the Court of Appeal's statement in the same litigation that subsumed proper purposes within the statutory duty of honesty ([09.025]); Koh's view is that the general law treats it as separate, and that s 157(2) is the better statutory home for it ([09.027])."},
    {t:"The Court of Appeal has firmly adopted the unitary view", correct:false,
     why:"No such firm holding exists. Loh J's view, treating it as separate, is likely representative."},
    {t:"Singapore has abolished the doctrine", correct:false,
     why:"The doctrine is very much part of Singapore law."},
    {t:"Singapore follows a statutory codification like the UK's s 171(b)", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore has no equivalent express statutory provision; it is the UK and Australia that have confirmed the doctrine by statute."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.090], fns 353–356", takeaway:"Singapore: likely separate, objective doctrine (Loh J in Scintronix)." },

{ id:"cl9-067", lesson:"9N", type:"order",
  prompt:"Put the steps of the Howard Smith inquiry in order.",
  steps:[
    "Identify the power whose exercise is challenged",
    "Construe its nature and the limits on its exercise",
    "Find the actual substantial purpose for which it was used",
    "Decide whether that purpose was proper"
  ],
  why:"Koh [09.091] says the Privy Council in Howard Smith v Ampol Petroleum is the leading authority on proper purposes, and laid down a two-stage inquiry. The first stage requires a proper consideration of the power in question, to ascertain as fairly as possible its nature and the limits or conditions on its exercise. Koh [09.092] says this is generally a matter of construing the instrument conferring the power, usually the constitution, against a broad backdrop that includes the legislative framework and any specific obligations the company has undertaken. The second stage examines the actual substantial purpose for which the power was exercised, and asks whether that purpose was proper ([09.100]). The four steps listed are the two stages broken down.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.091]–[09.092], [09.100], fns 357–359", takeaway:"Howard Smith: (1) construe the power's purposes; (2) find the substantial purpose; proper?" },

{ id:"c9x-098", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"At the first stage of Howard Smith, how are a power's permitted purposes identified?",
  options:[
    {t:"By construing the instrument conferring the power, usually the constitution, against the legislative framework and the company's obligations; beyond express limits, this can involve 'naked value judgments'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.092] says identifying permitted purposes is generally a matter of construing the conferring instrument, usually the constitution, against an expansive backdrop including the legislation governing companies and any specific obligations the company has undertaken (Equitable Life v Hyman). Where the constitution has objects clauses or a provision restricting the company's capacity, rights, powers or privileges (s 23(1B)), directors must exercise their powers within those terms; acting for purposes outside the stated objects is an abuse of power (Rolled Steel). But Koh [09.093] says such constitutional guides will become rarer, since objects clauses are now optional (s 23). Beyond express boundaries, determining proper purposes gets harder; while the boundaries are a question of law, drawing them appears largely to involve 'naked value judgments' (Sealy)."},
    {t:"By asking the directors what they intended", correct:false,
     why:"Directors' intentions are relevant at the second stage, when the actual purpose is examined. The first stage is about the power itself."},
    {t:"By looking only at the company's objects clause", correct:false,
     why:"Objects clauses help where they exist, but many companies have none, and the backdrop is broader."},
    {t:"By asking the shareholders to vote on the purpose", correct:false,
     why:"The permitted purposes are a question of law for the court."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.092]–[09.093], fns 359–364", takeaway:"Stage 1: construe the instrument in context; can involve value judgments." },

{ id:"c9x-099", lesson:"9N", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Hemlock Ltd's board decides, honestly and after debate, to close a regional office and invest the savings in online marketing. Some shareholders think this is a commercial mistake and ask the court to review whether it was the 'proper' use of the board's management power.",
  prompt:"How is the court likely to respond?",
  options:[
    {t:"It will be slow to intervene: there is no appeal on merits from honest management decisions within the board's powers", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.094] says that where purely managerial powers are concerned, and their exercise falls within the directors' proper responsibility, the court will be slow to question the correctness of the decision. Lord Wilberforce in Howard Smith said: '[t]here can be no appeal on merits from management decisions to courts of law; nor will courts of law assume to act as a kind of supervisory board over decisions within the powers of management honestly arrived at'. Closing an office and redirecting spending is a classic management decision, made honestly after debate, with no effect on the balance of shareholder power. The shareholders' disagreement with the commercial merits does not engage the proper purposes doctrine. Their remedy, if they want a different strategy, is to change the board."},
    {t:"It will review the decision on its merits and substitute its judgment", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Wilberforce expressly rejected the court acting as a supervisory board on honest management decisions."},
    {t:"It will invalidate the decision because some shareholders object", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholder disagreement is not a ground for invalidating a management decision."},
    {t:"It will refer the decision to a general meeting", correct:false,
     why:"Management decisions belong to the board under s 157A; the court will not transfer them to members."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.094], [09.100], fns 365–366", takeaway:"Honest management decisions: no appeal on merits (Howard Smith)." },

{ id:"cl9-072", lesson:"9N", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Courts will review the merits of an honest management decision that falls within the board's powers.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.094] and [09.100] quote Lord Wilberforce in Howard Smith: 'There can be no appeal on merits from management decisions to courts of law; nor will courts of law assume to act as a kind of supervisory board over decisions within the powers of management honestly arrived at.' The proper purposes doctrine bites where powers are used for a purpose outside their permitted limits, especially where the exercise alters the balance of shareholder power. Honest commercial judgments about how to run the business are left to the board. This matches the business judgment rule discussed in 9I."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The courts expressly decline to act as a supervisory board over honest management decisions."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.094], [09.100]", takeaway:"No merits review of honest management decisions." },

{ id:"cl9-068", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Lord Sumption emphasise in Eclairs Group v JKX Oil?",
  options:[
    {t:"The rule concerns abuse of power, using a power within its scope for an improper purpose, and matters most in battles for control", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.094] quotes Lord Sumption: the rule that directors' fiduciary powers may be exercised only for the purposes for which they were conferred is 'fundamental to the constitutional distinction between the respective domains of the board and the shareholders'. It matters particularly when the company is in play between competing groups, and a battle for control is 'probably the one [situation] in which the proper purpose rule has the most valuable part to play'. Koh [09.095] adds Lord Sumption's 'important point': the rule 'is not concerned with excess of power by doing an act which is beyond the scope of the instrument creating it … It is concerned with abuse of power, by doing acts which are within its scope but done for an improper purpose.' So an act can be within the literal scope of a power and still be invalid."},
    {t:"The rule concerns acts beyond the scope of the power", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Sumption said the opposite: excess of power is a different concept; the rule concerns abuse of a power that is within scope."},
    {t:"Good faith is a complete defence", correct:false,
     why:"In Eclairs the directors' integrity was not in question, yet the exercise was held improper ([09.097])."},
    {t:"The rule doesn't apply to voting restrictions", correct:false,
     why:"Eclairs itself concerned a power to impose voting restrictions, which the rule did constrain."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.094]–[09.095], [09.097], fns 367–368", takeaway:"Eclairs: abuse (not excess) of power; most vital in control battles." },

{ id:"cl9-069", lesson:"9N", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A family company's share structure gives voting control to the founder for life. He allots new shares that defeat his wife's voting power. The constitution's allotment power plainly concerns conferring voting rights.",
  prompt:"What did the High Court of Australia decide in Whitehouse v Carlton Hotel?",
  options:[
    {t:"By 3–2, the allotment was for an improper purpose because it manipulated voting power in favour of one faction", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.096] describes Whitehouse v Carlton Hotel, an application to rectify the register to reflect an allotment made by the controlling shareholder-director. The family company's share structure, set by the constitution, vested voting control solely in him during his lifetime. The allotment defeated his wife's voting power. By a bare majority of 3–2, the High Court held that, in seeking to manipulate voting power in favour of one faction at another's expense, the allotment power had been used for an improper purpose. This was so even though the majority accepted the allotment would 'necessarily be, and was intended to be, concerned with the conferral of voting rights'. So even if, on construction, the constitution contemplated adjusting voting power through share issues, using it to favour one faction was not a permissible purpose."},
    {t:"The allotment was valid because the constitution contemplated voting rights", correct:false,
     why:"The majority held it improper despite accepting the power concerned voting rights."},
    {t:"The allotment was valid because he was the controller", correct:false,
     why:"Control does not license using the power to manipulate voting between factions."},
    {t:"The court refused to rectify the register on procedural grounds", correct:false,
     why:"The decision turned on the improper purpose of the allotment, not procedure."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.096], fns 369–370", takeaway:"Whitehouse: allotment to manipulate factional voting = improper, even if within scope." },

{ id:"c9x-100", lesson:"9N", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Juniper plc's constitution lets the board restrict the voting rights of shareholders who fail to answer information requests about their interests in shares. Dissident shareholders announce they will vote against the board's resolutions at the AGM. The board sends them requests, decides in good faith that their answers are inaccurate, restricts their votes and wins the AGM vote.",
  prompt:"Following Eclairs v JKX Oil, what is the likely outcome?",
  options:[
    {t:"The restriction is an abuse of power: though the directors acted in good faith and within the power's scope, their purpose was to influence the AGM outcome", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.097] describes Eclairs Group v JKX Oil. The constitution gave directors power to restrict voting rights of shareholders who failed to comply with information requests. Requests were sent to shareholders who had said they would oppose resolutions at the AGM; the board, in good faith, considered the responses materially inaccurate and imposed restrictions, so the resolutions passed. The UK Supreme Court held that although the directors' integrity could not be impugned, their purpose in exercising the power was improper and an abuse of power. The power existed to obtain information about interests in shares, not to influence the result of a shareholder vote. Juniper's facts track Eclairs closely, and as Lord Sumption said, a battle for control is where the proper purpose rule has its most valuable role ([09.094])."},
    {t:"Valid, because the dissidents' answers were inaccurate", correct:false,
     why:"The inaccuracy gave the power a trigger, but in Eclairs the purpose of using it to affect the vote was still improper."},
    {t:"Valid, because the directors acted in good faith", correct:false,
     why:"Good faith did not save the directors in Eclairs. Proper purposes is objective."},
    {t:"Valid, because the power was within the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Sumption stressed the rule is about abuse of a power within scope, not excess of power."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.094]–[09.097], fns 371–372", takeaway:"Eclairs: good-faith voting restrictions to win a vote = improper purpose." },

{ id:"c9x-101", lesson:"9N", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A hostile bidder holds 55% of Quince Ltd. The board, believing a friendly bidder would be better for the company, issues a large block of new shares to the friendly bidder, turning the hostile bidder into a minority. The directors honestly believe this is in Quince's interests.",
  prompt:"On the principles in Howard Smith, is the allotment proper?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: using the share issue power to destroy an existing majority and alter the balance of shareholder power is an improper purpose, whatever the directors' good faith", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.094] says that while courts defer to honest managerial decisions, it is 'a different matter if the exercise of the powers in question has the effect of altering the balance of shareholder power'. Courts have generally declined to countenance interference with shareholders' rights, especially in control contests. In Howard Smith itself (fn 373), the directors issued shares to one bidder to destroy the existing majority's position and enable the other bid; the Privy Council held this improper. Koh [09.100] notes that in the trial decision, Street J accepted the directors genuinely thought control by the allottee would be better for the company, so they acted bona fide, but 'honest behaviour by the directors will not prevent a finding of improper conduct' if the purpose was improper. Quince's directors are in the same position."},
    {t:"Yes: the directors honestly believed it was in the company's interests", correct:false,
     why:"Honest belief is not a defence to the objective proper purposes rule."},
    {t:"Yes: issuing shares is always within the board's powers", correct:false,
     why:"The power may be within scope, but using it to reshape control is an abuse (Eclairs)."},
    {t:"Yes, if the friendly bidder pays full value", correct:false,
     why:"Price does not cure an improper purpose of altering shareholder control."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.094], [09.100], fn 373", takeaway:"Share issue to defeat a majority/control bid = improper (Howard Smith)." },

{ id:"cl9-070", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How do courts treat gratuitous dispositions of a commercial company's assets?",
  options:[
    {t:"As likely improper without express authorisation in the constitution or by the general meeting", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.098] says that apart from cases affecting shareholder power, courts generally construe powers so as to uphold the commercial precepts thought to underpin the company's existence. So in a commercial company, gratuitous dispositions of corporate assets, including analogous transactions such as guarantees or security (fn 374), are likely to be regarded as illegitimate exercises of directors' powers unless expressly authorised by the constitution or the general meeting. Footnote 375 notes that if the recipient is a shareholder, unlawful return of capital issues arise too. In Bishopsgate Investment Management v Maxwell (No 2), the director was in breach for his part in causing parcels of shares to be transferred to another company for no consideration, without board authority and without inquiry."},
    {t:"As always valid if within the directors' powers", correct:false,
     why:"Gratuitous dispositions are likely improper absent express authority, even if the power appears wide."},
    {t:"As void only if the company is insolvent", correct:false,
     why:"Impropriety does not depend on insolvency, though insolvency adds creditor-regarding duties."},
    {t:"As a matter for business judgment", correct:false,
     why:"Giving away assets for nothing does not fit the commercial purposes for which directors' powers are conferred."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.098]–[09.099], fns 374–377", takeaway:"Gratuitous dispositions: improper without express authority (Bishopsgate v Maxwell)." },

{ id:"c9x-102", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When will exercising a power to benefit another person or entity be improper?",
  options:[
    {t:"Furthering the directors' self-interest is clearly improper, but context matters, and furthering related companies' interests may not be (Intraco)", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.099] says an exercise of directors' power for the benefit of another person or entity will often be improper, but context is important. Furthering the directors' own self-interest is clearly an improper purpose, and fn 378 notes this will usually also breach the good faith duty and the no-conflict rule. But the court may hold it is not improper to further the interests of related companies. In Intraco v Multi-Pak, the court assessed the transactions in 'their proper commercial context' and held the directors not in breach, despite evidence that they had also acted to benefit related companies and considered the 'group as a whole'. The question is whether, in the commercial context, the purpose is one the power was conferred for."},
    {t:"Any benefit to anyone other than the company is always improper", correct:false,
     why:"Intraco shows benefits to related companies can be permissible in context."},
    {t:"Benefits to directors are proper if disclosed to the board", correct:false,
     why:"Self-interest is clearly improper as a purpose. Board disclosure may matter for conflicts (s 156) but does not cure an improper purpose."},
    {t:"Only benefits to shareholders are improper", correct:false,
     why:"Benefiting shareholders as a whole may be proper; self-interest and factional advantage are the problem."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.099], fns 378–381", takeaway:"Self-interest: improper. Group benefit: may be proper in context (Intraco)." },

{ id:"cl9-071", lesson:"9N", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"During a takeover contest, directors hastily issue shares to one bidder, defeating the other. They testify that their purpose was to raise urgently needed capital for a construction project.",
  prompt:"Why did Street J in Ampol Petroleum v R W Miller reject their evidence?",
  options:[
    {t:"The context pointed the other way: concern over the takeover, haste, scant financial information, no tax analysis, listing breaches, and no explanation why a rights issue wasn't used", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.100] says the second stage of Howard Smith asks for the directors' 'substantial' purpose. Directors' subjective reasons must be borne out by independent evidence, as Street J's trial decision in Ampol v R W Miller shows. The directors said they voted to issue shares to raise financing for a pressing construction project. Street J rejected this in light of overwhelming contrary context: (a) events before the board meeting showed concern over the takeover; (b) the meeting was called and shares allotted in haste; (c) the board had little information about the company's financial needs; (d) it did not consider the tax consequences of a share issue versus a loan; (e) the allotment breached stock exchange requirements, and the directors knew delisting might result; and (f) there was no obvious reason why a rights issue to existing shareholders was not used if the aim was finance."},
    {t:"Raising capital is never a proper purpose", correct:false,
     why:"Raising needed capital is a classic proper purpose for issuing shares. The problem was that the evidence did not support it being the real purpose."},
    {t:"Directors may never issue shares during a takeover", correct:false,
     why:"No absolute ban exists. The issue is the substantial purpose of the issue."},
    {t:"The directors admitted the improper purpose", correct:false,
     why:"They asserted a proper purpose; Street J rejected their evidence on the circumstances."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.100], fn 384", takeaway:"Ampol: stated purpose rejected when context (haste, no analysis, no rights issue) contradicts it." },

{ id:"c9x-103", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What practical advice does Koh give directors who believe the main purpose of an exercise of power is legitimate despite a possible improper purpose?",
  options:[
    {t:"Ensure there is sufficient evidence for the legitimate purpose: keep proper minutes and documentation of any relevant independent advice", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.100] says that whether an exercise of power is for proper purposes depends heavily on whether the court accepts the directors' asserted state of mind, and courts look at all the circumstances surrounding the decision. Ampol shows how stated purposes can be rejected when the surrounding evidence points elsewhere. So directors who believe the main purpose is legitimate, despite the possible presence of an improper one, are well advised to ensure there is sufficient evidence to support that. It is necessary to keep proper minutes of meetings and proper documentation of relevant independent advice. In the Ampol example, a documented analysis of financing needs, tax consequences and why a rights issue was unsuitable might have changed the result."},
    {t:"Avoid keeping minutes so that purposes cannot be examined", correct:false,
     why:"The reverse: missing documentation makes stated purposes harder to prove."},
    {t:"Rely on their honest belief alone", correct:false,
     why:"Honest belief is not a defence to the objective proper purposes rule, and must be supported by evidence."},
    {t:"Always obtain shareholder approval for every decision", correct:false,
     why:"Koh's advice is about evidencing purpose, not referring management decisions to members."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.100]", takeaway:"Document the legitimate purpose: minutes and independent advice." },

{ id:"c9x-104", lesson:"9N", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Where directors act with mixed purposes, which tests have been proposed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Howard Smith: the 'substantial or primary' purpose. Whitehouse (and Lord Sumption in Eclairs): a causative 'but-for' test. Lord Mance doubted the but-for test would avoid forensic difficulties.", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [09.101] says difficult fact-finding arises with multiple, mixed purposes. In Howard Smith, Lord Wilberforce said the court should ascertain the 'substantial or primary' purpose, viewed objectively. The High Court of Australia in Whitehouse preferred a causative test: whether the impermissible purpose was dominant or just one significantly contributing cause, the exercise is invalid if 'but for its presence, the power would not have been exercised' (see also Mills v Mills). Koh [09.102] says Lord Sumption in Eclairs sided with Whitehouse: a but-for inquiry fits the rationale of the rule, and it would be 'irrational' to let a decision stand merely because proper considerations also influenced it. But a majority expressed reservations; Lord Mance said the but-for test is 'just as likely to give rise to artificial and defensive attempts to justify what was done'. Koh concludes the practical results may differ little."},
    {t:"Only the directors' stated purpose counts", correct:false,
     why:"Stated purposes are tested against evidence (Ampol). Neither test simply accepts what directors say."},
    {t:"Any improper purpose, however minor, invalidates the act under all tests", correct:false,
     why:"Howard Smith asks for the substantial purpose, and Whitehouse's test requires the improper purpose to be causative."},
    {t:"The UK Supreme Court unanimously adopted the but-for test", correct:false,
     why:"The court was not unanimous on this point; a majority expressed reservations."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.101]–[09.102], fns 385–389", takeaway:"Mixed purposes: substantial/primary (Howard Smith) vs but-for (Whitehouse, Sumption)." },

{ id:"c9x-105", lesson:"9N", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Sloe Ltd needs $5m for a factory upgrade. Its board also dislikes a dissident 20% shareholder. The board issues $5m of new shares to an ally, diluting the dissident to 12%. Evidence shows the company would have raised the money by a rights issue to all shareholders but for the wish to dilute the dissident.",
  prompt:"Applying the but-for test from Whitehouse and Lord Sumption, what is the result?",
  options:[
    {t:"The allotment is invalid: the improper purpose of diluting the dissident was causative, because without it the power would not have been exercised this way", correct:true,
     why:"Under the causative test from Whitehouse, endorsed by Lord Sumption in Eclairs ([09.101]–[09.102]), an exercise of power is invalid if the impermissible purpose was causative: but for it, 'the power would not have been exercised'. That holds whether the improper purpose was dominant or one of several significantly contributing causes. Sloe genuinely needed $5m, a proper purpose. But the evidence shows the board would have used a rights issue to all shareholders but for its wish to dilute the dissident. So the specific exercise, an allotment to an ally, would not have happened without the improper purpose, which makes it causative. Lord Sumption said it would be irrational to let the decision stand merely because it was also influenced by proper considerations. Koh notes the Howard Smith 'substantial purpose' test would probably reach the same result here, since the choice of allottee was driven by the improper aim."},
    {t:"Valid: the company genuinely needed the money", correct:false,
     why:"A genuine proper purpose does not save the exercise if an improper purpose was causative."},
    {t:"Valid: the dissident still holds 12%", correct:false,
     why:"The degree of dilution does not cure the improper purpose."},
    {t:"Valid if the ally paid market price", correct:false,
     why:"Price is not the issue. The improper purpose was to alter the balance of shareholder power."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 9 [09.101]–[09.102]", takeaway:"But-for test: if the improper aim changed what was done, the exercise falls." }

]);

;
