{ id:"cl10-012", lesson:"10C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Trade union members seek an injunction to stop union funds being used for an ultra vires purpose, and summary judgment for restoration of funds already misapplied.",
  prompt:"What did the court do in Taylor v National Union of Mineworkers (Derbyshire Area)?",
  options:[
    {t:"Granted the injunction but refused summary judgment, because restoring the funds was a corporate claim subject to Foss v Harbottle", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.011] says the rule in Foss v Harbottle does not apply where the wrong infringes the claimant's personal rights as a member. Some acts infringe both corporate and personal rights, and ultra vires transactions are an example. Knox J in Smith v Croft (No 2) said that where compensation is sought for the company's loss from ultra vires transactions, the wrong is to the company; where a minority shareholder seeks to prevent an ultra vires transaction, the wrong is the shareholder's own. Taylor v NUM applied this split (fn 57 notes unions are treated as analogous to companies). The court granted the members an injunction restraining use of union funds for an ultra vires purpose, a personal right, but refused summary judgment on restoring misapplied funds, because that was a corporate claim still subject to Foss v Harbottle."},
    {t:"Granted both", correct:false,
     why:"Restoration of funds was a corporate claim, which the members could not pursue personally."},
    {t:"Refused both", correct:false,
     why:"The injunction protected the members' personal right and was granted."},
    {t:"Refused the injunction but granted judgment", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the outcome: injunction (personal) granted; restoration (corporate) refused."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.011], fns 54–57", takeaway:"Restraining ultra vires = personal; recovering losses = corporate (Taylor v NUM)." },

{ id:"c10x-012", lesson:"10C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The board of Lotus Ltd is about to cause the company to enter into a transaction that is beyond its capacity under its constitution. Wei, a 2% shareholder, also wants to recover money the company lost on a similar transaction last year.",
  prompt:"Following Knox J in Smith v Croft (No 2), how should Wei's two claims be characterised?",
  options:[
    {t:"Restraining the threatened transaction is Wei's personal right; recovering last year's loss is the company's claim, subject to Foss v Harbottle", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.011] quotes Knox J: 'Where what is sought is compensation for the company for loss caused by ultra vires transactions the wrong … is a wrong to the company, which has the substantive right to redress. Where the minority shareholder is seeking to prevent an ultra vires transaction …, the wrong which needs redress is the minority shareholder's wrong.' Koh [10.016] explains why: the right to restrain a threatened ultra vires act flows from the statutory contract in the constitution and is personal to each member, so Foss v Harbottle does not apply to it. But where money or property has already been lost, the wrong is to the company, so the company is the proper plaintiff. Section 25(2) reflects this dual nature, preserving the right to rely on lack of capacity in proceedings to restrain an ultra vires act and in proceedings against present or former directors. Wei can seek an injunction personally, but needs a derivative route for the recovery claim."},
    {t:"Both are personal claims", correct:false,
     why:"Recovering the company's loss is a corporate claim."},
    {t:"Both are corporate claims", correct:false,
     why:"Restraining a threatened ultra vires act is a personal right of the member."},
    {t:"Neither can be brought, because s 25 validates ultra vires acts", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25(2) expressly preserves reliance on lack of capacity in both kinds of proceedings."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.011], [10.016]", takeaway:"Ultra vires: injunction = personal; recovery = corporate." },

{ id:"cl10-013", lesson:"10C", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A shareholder can recover, in a personal action, the fall in value of his shares caused by a wrong done to the company.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.012] says a shareholder's right to recover for personal wrongs is constrained. Where the loss is represented by a reduction in the value of his shares, courts treat it as merely reflective of the company's loss, and on policy grounds it may not be recoverable. The company is the right claimant for its own loss; if it recovers, the share value is restored. Allowing both company and shareholder to recover would mean double recovery and would prejudice the company's creditors. Koh develops the no-reflective-loss principle fully in ch 11 (11B)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"A fall in share value caused by a wrong to the company is reflective loss, generally not recoverable by the shareholder."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.012], fn 58", takeaway:"Fall in share value from a corporate wrong = reflective loss." },

{ id:"cl10-014", lesson:"10C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What must a shareholder show, as a preliminary point, to bring a derivative action at general law?",
  options:[
    {t:"A prima facie case that the company is entitled to the relief claimed, and that the action falls within an exception to Foss v Harbottle", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.013] says that at general law a shareholder may sue on behalf of his company if he establishes, as a preliminary point, a prima facie case that (a) the company is entitled to the relief claimed, and (b) the action falls within the proper boundaries of an exception to Foss v Harbottle (Prudential Assurance (CA)). The first requirement is that the shareholder 'show that the company has a reasonable, or legitimate, case against the defendant' ([10.014]). The second establishes his locus standi to represent the company and take command of the proceedings, limited to the ultra vires and fraud on the minority exceptions ([10.015]). Footnote 67 notes that the common law imposes no formal leave requirement, unlike s 216A, but the court still decides these preliminary issues."},
    {t:"That he holds at least 10% of the shares", correct:false,
     why:"No minimum holding is required at common law."},
    {t:"That the board has approved the action", correct:false,
     why:"If the board approved, the company would sue itself; the derivative action exists because it will not."},
    {t:"Only that the defendant is a director", correct:false,
     why:"The defendant's status is not enough. The company's entitlement and an applicable exception must be shown."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.013]–[10.014], fns 59–61", takeaway:"Common law derivative: (a) company has a prima facie case; (b) an exception applies." },

{ id:"c10x-013", lesson:"10C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why must a derivative claimant first show the company has a reasonable or legitimate case against the defendant?",
  options:[
    {t:"Because the derivative action is a 'mere matter of procedure' circumscribed by the company's own rights, and the requirement protects the company from spurious proceedings it did not choose", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.014] says the first requirement recognises that allowing a shareholder to sue derivatively is a 'mere matter of procedure', permitted only so that wrongs to the company do not go unremedied. The shareholder's right is necessarily circumscribed by the company's right had it sued itself, so any valid ground preventing the company from suing, such as ratification or a valid release (fn 64), defeats the derivative claim too. The requirement also reduces the risk that the company suffers loss from spurious proceedings not of its own making. Andrew Ang J in Sinwa SS (HK) v Morten Innhaug explained that a third party, at little or no risk to himself, is bringing proceedings the company chose not to bring; improperly used, the device can prejudice the company, as where a disgruntled minority sues the majority for extraneous reasons. 'Such abuse must clearly be nipped in the bud.'"},
    {t:"Because the shareholder will personally receive the damages", correct:false,
     why:"Any remedy goes to the company. The requirement protects the company, not the claimant's personal interests."},
    {t:"Because the court must always approve the board's decisions", correct:false,
     why:"The requirement concerns the merits of the company's claim, not approval of board decisions."},
    {t:"Because shareholders must pay the company's costs", correct:false,
     why:"Costs are dealt with separately. The requirement is about the claim's legitimacy."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.014], fns 62–66", takeaway:"Show the company's own case: derivative standing is procedural; spurious suits must be 'nipped in the bud'." },

{ id:"c10x-014", lesson:"10C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Maple Pte Ltd's members, with full disclosure and no wrongdoer votes, validly released a former director from liability for a negligent investment. A minority shareholder now starts a common law derivative action against him for the same loss.",
  prompt:"What is the likely outcome?",
  options:[
    {t:"It fails: valid grounds that would prevent the company suing, such as a valid release, equally defeat the derivative claim", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.014] says the shareholder's right to sue derivatively is necessarily circumscribed by the company's right had it brought the action itself, so if there are valid grounds preventing the company from suing, those grounds equally defeat the derivative claim. Footnote 64 gives ratification and a valid release of the company's rights as examples. Maple's members validly released the director, with full disclosure and no tainted votes, so the company itself could not now sue him. The minority shareholder cannot be in a better position than the company, so the first requirement, that the company has a reasonable or legitimate case, is not met. (Negligence without self-benefit is generally ratifiable, 10D.)"},
    {t:"It succeeds because the minority did not consent to the release", correct:false,
     why:"A valid release by the company binds it, and the derivative claimant can have no larger right than the company."},
    {t:"It succeeds because negligence can never be released", correct:false,
     why:"Negligence per se is generally ratifiable ([10.022])."},
    {t:"It succeeds if the minority holds more than 10%", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholding size does not override a valid release."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.014], fn 64", takeaway:"If the company couldn't sue (eg valid release), neither can the derivative claimant." },

{ id:"c10x-015", lesson:"10C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Is the Prudential Assurance (CA) statement about transactions requiring a special resolution a true third exception to Foss v Harbottle?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably not: failure to follow constitutional procedure is a breach of the statutory contract and more accurately a breach of members' personal rights, though classifying rights here is not straightforward", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.015] says the locus standi exceptions are limited to two: ultra vires and fraud on the minority. The Court of Appeal in Prudential Assurance said there is 'no room for the operation of the rule if the transaction complained of could be validly done or sanctioned only by a special resolution', since a simple majority cannot confirm what needs a greater majority. This is sometimes read as a third exception. But a true exception involves vindicating corporate rights while letting an individual control the action. Failure to follow a procedure laid down in the constitution is a breach of the statutory contract, which every member is entitled to have observed, so it is more accurately a breach of personal rights, as in Edwards v Halliwell. Koh cautions that categorisation is not straightforward, since the company is also a party to the statutory contract, and some obvious breaches have been treated as corporate wrongs (ch 11)."},
    {t:"Yes: it is a well-established third exception", correct:false,
     why:"Koh doubts it is a true exception, treating it as a personal rights case."},
    {t:"It is the same as the ultra vires exception", correct:false,
     why:"It concerns procedural requirements such as special majorities, not corporate capacity."},
    {t:"It is the same as fraud on the minority", correct:false,
     why:"It does not require fraud or wrongdoer control."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.015], fns 68–70", takeaway:"Special-majority breaches: personal rights, not a true third exception." },

{ id:"cl10-015", lesson:"10C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A trade union raises members' dues by ordinary resolution, although its rules require a two-thirds majority on a ballot. Members challenge the decision.",
  prompt:"How does the chapter classify Edwards v Halliwell?",
  options:[
    {t:"As an infringement of members' personal rights, not a true exception to Foss v Harbottle", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.015] uses Edwards v Halliwell to support the view that breach of constitutional procedure is a personal rights case. The union increased dues by ordinary resolution although its rules required a two-thirds majority on a ballot. Jenkins LJ declared the decision invalid for breach of the rules. The rule in Foss v Harbottle had no application because the gist of the complaint was not a wrong to the union but an invasion of the 'personal and individual rights of membership', so the members could seek redress in their own right. Koh treats this as showing that the supposed 'special resolution' exception is better understood as a personal rights case, while noting the classification of rights in the corporate setting is not always easy."},
    {t:"As a fraud on the minority", correct:false,
     why:"No fraud or wrongdoer control was involved; the members enforced their own rights."},
    {t:"As an ultra vires transaction", correct:false,
     why:"Raising dues was within the union's powers; the problem was the wrong procedure."},
    {t:"As a corporate wrong only the union could sue on", correct:false,
     why:"The court held the members could sue in their own right."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.015], fns 71–74", takeaway:"Edwards v Halliwell: wrong majority procedure → personal right of members." },

{ id:"cl10-016", lesson:"10C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why doesn't a shareholder relying on the ultra vires exception need to prove wrongdoer control or a failed attempt to get the company to sue?",
  options:[
    {t:"Ultra vires acts can't be ratified, even unanimously, so there is no need to show that control is blocking a ratifiable claim", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.016] says the ultra vires exception exists because acts beyond the company's capacity cannot be ratified or confirmed by the members, even unanimously. Koh [10.017] notes that, unlike fraud on the minority, standing under this exception does not depend on proving an unsuccessful attempt to get the company to sue, or that the defendants controlled the company. Koh [10.018] suggests why. When Lord Davey explained the exceptions in Burland v Earle, a decision not to sue was functionally treated as equivalent to ratification. Where a wrong has been validly ratified, there can be no litigation. Frauds on the minority involve wrongs potentially ratifiable but for wrongdoer control, so proving control is what renders them unratifiable. Ultra vires transactions are never ratifiable, so control is unnecessary. Footnote 82 notes the exception has been called 'moribund' in Singapore."},
    {t:"Because ultra vires claims are always personal", correct:false,
     why:"Claims to recover the company's losses from ultra vires acts are corporate ([10.016])."},
    {t:"Because s 25 abolished ultra vires", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25 validates transactions but preserves reliance on lack of capacity in restraint proceedings and proceedings against directors."},
    {t:"Because courts presume wrongdoer control", correct:false,
     why:"There is no presumption; control is simply irrelevant to this exception."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.016]–[10.018], fns 75–90", takeaway:"Ultra vires is never ratifiable → no wrongdoer-control requirement." },

{ id:"c10x-016", lesson:"10C", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does s 25 of the Companies Act reflect the dual nature of rights in ultra vires cases?",
  options:[
    {t:"Although it validates transactions that are without more ultra vires, s 25(2) preserves the right to rely on lack of capacity in proceedings to restrain an ultra vires act and in proceedings against present or former directors", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.016] says the dual nature of the rights involved, the member's personal right to restrain and the company's right to recover, is recognised in s 25. Despite the statutory validation of transactions that are, without more, ultra vires, s 25(2) explicitly preserves the right to assert or rely on the company's lack of capacity in two kinds of proceedings: proceedings to restrain the doing of an ultra vires act, and proceedings against present or former directors. The first corresponds to the member's personal right; the second to the company's claim against the directors who caused the ultra vires act, the corporate claim subject to Foss v Harbottle. Third parties dealing with the company are protected by the validation."},
    {t:"It abolishes all consequences of ultra vires acts", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25(2) expressly preserves reliance on lack of capacity in two kinds of proceedings."},
    {t:"It makes all ultra vires transactions void", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25 validates transactions that are without more ultra vires, protecting third parties."},
    {t:"It applies only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 25 is not limited to public companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.016], fns 75–80", takeaway:"s 25(2): lack of capacity still usable to restrain acts and to sue directors." },

{ id:"cl10-017", lesson:"10C", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Megarry VC in Estmanco: the test may simply be whether an ordinary resolution of the shareholders could validly carry out or ____ the act in question.",
  accept:["ratify"], answer:"ratify",
  why:"Koh [10.019] says the concept of ratifiability seems to link both exceptions. Identifying the 'fraud' in fraud on the minority depends heavily on whether the wrong is ratifiable, or more precisely, not ratifiable. Megarry VC was 'especially astute' in Estmanco (Kilner House) v Greater London Council when he said the test for whether a shareholder may act for the company may come down simply to 'whether an ordinary resolution of the shareholders could validly carry out or ratify the act in question'. If it could, majority rule governs and the individual shareholder cannot sue. If it could not, because the act is ultra vires or because wrongdoer control makes ratification improper, the shareholder may be allowed to sue.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.019]", takeaway:"Estmanco: could an ordinary resolution validly do or ratify it?" },

{ id:"c10x-017", lesson:"10C", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two shareholders of Birch Ltd, a Singapore company, sue derivatively at common law. Case A: they allege the directors caused Birch to guarantee a stranger's debt, which was beyond its capacity under its constitution. Case B: they allege the directors negligently sold an asset cheaply to an unconnected buyer. In neither case have they asked the company to sue.",
  prompt:"Which case is more likely to clear the locus standi hurdle without proof of wrongdoer control?",
  options:[
    {t:"Case A: under the ultra vires exception, standing does not depend on a failed attempt to get the company to sue or on wrongdoer control", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.017] says standing under the ultra vires exception is not dependent on proof of an unsuccessful attempt to get the company to sue, nor on proof that the defendants controlled the company. Case A alleges an act beyond Birch's capacity, which is not ratifiable even unanimously ([10.016]), so the ultra vires exception may apply (though Koh fn 82 notes it has been called 'moribund' in Singapore, and for a Singapore company s 216A would be the usual route). Case B alleges mere negligence without self-benefit. That is ordinarily ratifiable ([10.022]), so it is not a 'fraud', and even if it were, the shareholders would need to show wrongdoer control preventing the company from suing. With no attempt to get the company to sue and an unconnected buyer, Case B fails at common law."},
    {t:"Case B: negligence is always a fraud on the minority", correct:false,
     why:"Negligence per se is generally ratifiable and not a fraud unless it benefits the wrongdoers (Daniels v Daniels)."},
    {t:"Both equally, because neither requires asking the company first", correct:false,
     why:"Fraud on the minority requires showing wrongdoer control, including generally some attempt to get the company to sue ([10.024])."},
    {t:"Neither, because common law derivative actions are abolished", correct:false,
     why:"The common law action survives in Singapore ([10.002])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.016]–[10.017], [10.022]", takeaway:"Ultra vires exception: no control or demand needed; fraud exception: both needed." }

