{ id:"cl10-031", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does the court's role under s 216A differ from its role at general law?",
  options:[
    {t:"The court itself substantially makes the decision whether litigation is in the company's interests, considering factors such as ratifiability", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.038] says that under ss 216A and 216B a 'complainant' may apply to court for leave to bring an action or arbitration in the name and on behalf of the company, or to intervene in one to which the company is a party (s 216A(1)). The final decision whether litigation or arbitration is in the company's best interests, and may proceed under the complainant's control, is vested in the court. At general law, the court was concerned with whether the company's decision not to sue was properly made by an appropriate body. Under s 216A, the court substantially makes that decision itself, after considering all relevant factors, including ratification and ratifiability, which at general law would have stopped the shareholder (see s 216B(1), fn 184). Footnote 180 notes s 216A was introduced in 1993, modelled on the Canada Business Corporations Act."},
    {t:"The court defers entirely to the board", correct:false,
     why:"Under s 216A the court itself decides whether the action should proceed."},
    {t:"The court only checks the notice was given", correct:false,
     why:"Notice is one requirement; the court also assesses good faith and the company's interests."},
    {t:"There is no difference", correct:false,
     why:"Koh highlights a fundamental shift: from reviewing the company's decision to making it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.038], fns 180–184", takeaway:"s 216A: the court decides whether the action is in the company's interests." },

{ id:"c10x-031", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In what ways is s 216A broader than the common law derivative action?",
  options:[
    {t:"It widens who may apply (members, the Minister, and 'proper persons') and the range of matters, covering arbitrations and intervention, not just ultra vires or fraud by insiders", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.039] says s 216A has a considerably broader scope than the general law action. The common law exceptions mainly gave a shareholder standing only for ultra vires acts or wrongs by insiders amounting to fraud on the minority. Section 216A broadens both the class of persons who may use it and the range of corporate matters in which the complainant may intervene: it allows an application to bring an action or arbitration on the company's behalf, or to intervene in one to which the company is a party ([10.038]). It is not confined to 'fraud' or wrongdoer control; those concepts survive only as factors in the court's assessment."},
    {t:"It is narrower: it applies only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 216A is broader, and applies to Singapore-incorporated companies generally."},
    {t:"It requires proof of fraud on the minority", correct:false,
     why:"Section 216A does not require fraud on the minority."},
    {t:"It allows the complainant to recover damages personally", correct:false,
     why:"The action is in the company's name and for its benefit."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.038]–[10.039]", takeaway:"s 216A: wider applicants, actions and arbitrations, intervention; no fraud requirement." },

{ id:"cl10-032", lesson:"10F", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each type of complainant under s 216A to its basis for standing.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Registered member", r:"Standing as of right"},
    {l:"Minister for Finance", r:"Companies under investigation"},
    {l:"'Proper person'", r:"At the court's discretion"},
    {l:"Member who joined after the wrong", r:"Still qualifies: standing is judged at application"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [10.040] lists the complainants: a member of the company, the Minister for Finance for companies under investigation (Pt 4, fn 188), and 'any other person who, in the discretion of the Court, is a proper person'. It is fitting that a registered shareholder has standing as of right, since he has the greatest interest in remedies for corporate wrongs. There is no overt requirement that membership be contemporaneous with the acts complained of. In Richardson Greenshields v Kalmacoff, the Ontario Court of Appeal held it was enough that the complainant was a registered shareholder when it applied, even though it became one after the wrongs. Standing is assessed at the time of the application, so Koh suggests the complainant need not remain a member until the hearing or decision (but see Syed Ibrahim v Wavoo Abdusalam, fn 192).",
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.040]–[10.041], fns 188–192", takeaway:"Members (as of right) · Minister · proper persons; standing judged at application." },

{ id:"c10x-032", lesson:"10F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Birch Pte Ltd's directors misappropriated funds in 2023. Kumar bought 1,000 Birch shares in 2025, after learning of the misappropriation, and applies under s 216A. The directors argue he cannot complain of wrongs committed before he became a member.",
  prompt:"Is Kumar likely to have standing?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes: as a registered member he has standing as of right; there is no requirement that membership be contemporaneous with the wrongs, and standing is assessed at the time of application", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.040] says there is no overt requirement in s 216A for membership to be contemporaneous with the acts complained of. In Richardson Greenshields, the complainant became a shareholder only after the alleged wrongs, and indeed bought a token holding to gain standing ([10.048]), yet the Ontario Court of Appeal held it sufficient that it was a registered shareholder when it applied. Kumar is a registered member at the time of application, so he has standing as of right. Whether he gets leave is another matter: the court will still test his good faith and whether the action appears to be in the company's interests (10H–10I). Buying shares to sue could be scrutinised there, though in Richardson Greenshields it did not defeat the applicant."},
    {t:"No: he must have been a member when the wrong occurred", correct:false,
     why:"No contemporaneous-membership requirement exists."},
    {t:"No: buying shares to sue is an abuse that bars standing", correct:false,
     why:"Standing as a member is as of right; motives are tested at the good faith stage."},
    {t:"Only if he buys at least 10%", correct:false,
     why:"No minimum holding is required for a member's standing."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.040], [10.048]", takeaway:"Later-joining member still has s 216A standing." },

{ id:"cl10-033", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How should 'proper person' be interpreted, according to Teo Gek Luang v Ng Ai Tiong?",
  options:[
    {t:"Purposively, by reference to the mischief the procedure targets, rather than liberally just because it is remedial", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.042] says the statutory derivative provisions are often called 'remedial', which has led some courts to claim a liberal construction is justified (eg Richardson Greenshields). That approach was eschewed, rightly in Koh's view, by the High Court in Teo Gek Luang v Ng Ai Tiong in favour of a purposive approach (fn 197 quotes Scalia's description of 'remedial statutes are to be liberally construed' as 'lego-babble'). So how generously 'proper person' is understood depends partly on identifying the rationale for the procedure and the mischief it was designed to remedy. Koh [10.043] says the original impetus was to give 'more effective remedies to minority shareholders', which suggests a proper person should be in a position analogous to a minority shareholder."},
    {t:"Liberally, so anyone can apply", correct:false,
     why:"Teo Gek Luang rejected a liberal construction as such."},
    {t:"Strictly, limited to registered shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"The 'proper person' category exists precisely to go beyond members."},
    {t:"By the size of the applicant's shareholding", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholding size is not the test."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.042]–[10.043], fns 195–200", takeaway:"'Proper person': purposive, not merely liberal (Teo Gek Luang)." },

{ id:"c10x-033", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On the minority-shareholder analogy suggested by the select committee, who might be a 'proper person'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Those with a financial interest in how the company is managed and in litigation outcomes but limited ability to influence management, such as a person holding through a nominee or a dissenting minority director", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.043] says the original impetus for s 216A was to give minority shareholders more effective remedies. If 'proper person' is calibrated to that purpose, the select committee itself suggested such a person must be in a position analogous to a minority shareholder: someone with a financial interest in how the company is managed and in the outcome of litigation affecting it, but, like a minority shareholder, with limited ability to influence management. Even on this fairly narrow test, the class extends beyond the minority shareholder. Possible contenders include a person whose interest is held through a nominee (Tan Chun Chuen Malcolm v Beach Hotel), and a director who opposed the wrongdoing but is in the minority on the board and wants to act for the company."},
    {t:"Any member of the public", correct:false,
     why:"Even generous approaches require a connection to and interest in the company."},
    {t:"Only the majority shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"The analogy is to minority shareholders with limited influence."},
    {t:"Only the company's auditors", correct:false,
     why:"Auditors are not mentioned as natural candidates; the focus is financial interest plus limited influence."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.043], fns 202–207", takeaway:"Minority analogy: financial interest + limited influence (nominee holders, minority directors)." },

{ id:"cl10-034", lesson:"10F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An applicant is the beneficial shareholder of an entity that itself holds shares in the company he wants to sue on behalf of.",
  prompt:"What did the High Court decide in Ganesh Paulraj v A T Offshore?",
  options:[
    {t:"He was a proper person: his indirect interest gave him a clear interest and sufficient connection to the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.044] says a shareholder of the subject company's holding company is likely to have enough indirect interest to be a proper person. Although owning a share gives no interest in the company's assets as such (Short v Treasury Commissioners), the shares are property with real value, and the holding company's share value depends on its assets, including its shares in the subject company. A loss to the subject company therefore lowers the value of the holding company's shares, a real loss to its shareholder. In Ganesh Paulraj v A T Offshore, the High Court held that a beneficial shareholder of an entity that held shares in the subject company was a proper person, since his indirect interest gave him 'a clear interest and sufficient connection to the [subject] company'."},
    {t:"Only registered members can apply", correct:false,
     why:"The 'proper person' category extends beyond registered members."},
    {t:"Indirect interests never count", correct:false,
     why:"Ganesh Paulraj accepted an indirect interest as sufficient."},
    {t:"He had to buy shares directly first", correct:false,
     why:"He qualified as a proper person without being a registered member."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.044], fns 208–215", takeaway:"Ganesh Paulraj: indirect (upstream) holder can be a proper person." },

{ id:"c10x-034", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does Koh say about shareholders of a subsidiary or sister company of the subject company seeking to be 'proper persons'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Their interest is less obvious, so one may need to look beyond the minority-shareholder analogy to s 216A's wider purpose of improving corporate governance and accountability", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.045] says the position is less clear for a shareholder of the subject company's subsidiary or sister company, whose interest in how the company is managed and in the litigation is not as obvious. In such cases it may be necessary to look beyond the strict minority-shareholder analogy. The statutory procedure has been said to serve the wider purpose of regulating corporate management: Lai Kew Chai J in Teo Gek Luang said derivative actions 'are intended to improve the standards of private corporate governance since directors who breach their duties to the company could be made accountable'. Koh [10.046] adds Robins JA's similar statement in Richardson Greenshields. If that is the wider purpose, a more liberal approach, not requiring a strict financial interest, may be justified, though it must be balanced against the risk of 'backseat drivers'."},
    {t:"They automatically qualify as members", correct:false,
     why:"They are not members of the subject company; their standing depends on the court's discretion."},
    {t:"They can never qualify", correct:false,
     why:"Koh leaves room for them, particularly on a governance-focused view."},
    {t:"Their standing depends only on the size of their holding", correct:false,
     why:"The question is the nature of their connection and the procedure's purpose."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.045]–[10.046], fns 216–222", takeaway:"Sister/sub shareholders: look to s 216A's governance purpose." },

{ id:"c10x-035", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does Koh balance the risk of 'backseat drivers' against a generous view of 'proper person'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Standing is only the first step; the leave preconditions (notice, good faith, company's interests) remain and may be enough to prevent abuse, so a more generous view of standing may be acceptable", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.046] acknowledges that too liberal an approach to 'proper person' could impair majority rule and bring about what Foss v Harbottle tried to prevent. Ang J in Sinwa warned the court must 'be wary of backseat drivers who attempt to control the corporate vehicle by use of a derivative action'. But Koh points out that the issue here relates specifically to standing. Once a person is accepted as a proper person, the preconditions for leave must still be met, and a proper application of those conditions may be all that is needed to prevent abuse of s 216A. On that basis, there may be room for a more generous conception of 'proper person'. Koh [10.049] adds that non-member cases will remain rare, and the court can weigh all relevant factors to reach a balanced view."},
    {t:"By refusing standing to anyone who is not a registered member", correct:false,
     why:"Koh favours possible generosity at the standing stage, relying on the leave conditions as the filter."},
    {t:"By requiring a large shareholding", correct:false,
     why:"No such requirement; the filter is the leave preconditions."},
    {t:"By letting the board veto applications", correct:false,
     why:"The court, not the board, decides on leave."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.046], [10.049], fns 223–227", takeaway:"Standing generous; leave conditions filter out 'backseat drivers'." },

{ id:"cl10-035", lesson:"10F", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What test for a 'proper person' did McDonald J suggest in First Edmonton Place?",
  options:[
    {t:"Someone who could reasonably be entrusted with advancing the company's interests by seeking a remedy for the wrong", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.047] suggests a useful perspective from McDonald J of the Alberta Court of Queen's Bench in First Edmonton Place v 315888 Alberta: the proper person should be one who 'could reasonably be entrusted with the responsibility of advancing the interests of the corporation by seeking a remedy to right the wrong allegedly done to the corporation'. That pool is deeper than one measured solely by financial interest in the outcome. Given the ubiquity of corporate groups, and common directors' tendency to act for the group rather than the individual company, a member of another company within the group could plausibly fall within it. Richardson Greenshields illustrates the sort of inquiry this involves ([10.048])."},
    {t:"Anyone with a financial interest above a threshold", correct:false,
     why:"McDonald J's test focuses on trustworthiness to advance the company's interests, not a financial threshold."},
    {t:"Only creditors", correct:false,
     why:"The test is not limited to creditors."},
    {t:"Only former directors", correct:false,
     why:"The test is not limited by office."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.047], fn 228", takeaway:"First Edmonton: could reasonably be entrusted to advance the company's interests." },

{ id:"cl10-036", lesson:"10F", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A loan company's preferred shareholders veto renewal of an advisory contract with a company controlled by a director. The board then hires most of the adviser's staff instead. The lead selling agent from the preferred share IPO buys a few shares to gain standing and applies.",
  prompt:"Which factors did Robins JA treat as significant in Richardson Greenshields?",
  options:[
    {t:"The agent negotiated the veto as a brake on management, had a long connection with the shares, and represented many preferred holders", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.048] describes Richardson Greenshields. The loan company had closely held ordinary shares and publicly quoted preferred shares. A defendant director controlled both the ordinary shares and an advisory company with which the loan company had a contract. The preferred shareholders had limited votes but could approve renewal of the advisory contract, and when the company made losses they voted against renewal. The board then had the company employ most of the adviser's staff instead. The applicant, the lead selling agent on the preferred share IPO, had bought a token number of preferred shares to gain standing, so it was technically a complainant as of right. Robins JA found several factors significant: the agent had negotiated the preferred holders' veto specifically to manage the conflict, as 'a vital brake on management'; it had a long commercial connection with the class and knew the dispute; and it represented a substantial number of preferred holders. It was 'in a better position than most shareholders to pursue the complaint'."},
    {t:"The size of its shareholding", correct:false,
     why:"Its holding was a token one; the significant factors were its role and connection."},
    {t:"That it was a creditor", correct:false,
     why:"Its relevance came from its role in the share issue and representation of holders, not as a creditor."},
    {t:"That it had board representation", correct:false,
     why:"Board representation was not among the factors."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.048], fns 229–235", takeaway:"Richardson Greenshields: role, connection and representation made the agent apt." }

