{ id:"c10x-045", lesson:"10I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What standard does s 216A(3)(c) set at the leave stage, and why?",
  options:[
    {t:"The complainant need only show that the action appears to be prima facie in the company's interests, on affidavit evidence, because he usually lacks access to the company's documents", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.066] says the court is not called on at the leave stage to try the action or resolve factual disputes; it may rely on affidavit evidence. The complainant need not prove a prima facie case, only that one appears to exist. The low standard presumably recognises that a minority shareholder is usually not in a position to obtain enough evidence at the leave stage. As Prakash J said in Fong Wai Lyn Carolyn, the applicant often 'is not privy and does not have access to significant documents held by the company or its controlling directors', so complaints are often based partly on 'information received, belief and inference'. Contrast s 216A(3)(b), where the court must 'be satisfied' of good faith on the balance of probabilities ([10.060])."},
    {t:"He must prove the claim on the balance of probabilities", correct:false,
     why:"That would require trying the action. Only an apparent prima facie case is needed."},
    {t:"He must prove the claim beyond reasonable doubt", correct:false,
     why:"This is a civil leave application with a low threshold."},
    {t:"He must show the defendants have admitted liability", correct:false,
     why:"No admission is needed; affidavit evidence of an apparent case suffices."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.066]", takeaway:"s 216A(3)(c): 'appears' prima facie; affidavits; low bar for evidence-poor minorities." },

{ id:"cl10-047", lesson:"10I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did the Court of Appeal in Pang Yong Hock mean by 'a $100 claim may be meritorious but it may not be expedient to commence an action for it'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Legal merit is distinct from whether suing is in the company's interests", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.066] asks whether showing an arguable case with reasonable prospects is enough. A claim wholly without merit is obviously not in the company's interests, but does it follow that a legitimate claim is? That goes beyond legal merit. In Pang Yong Hock, the Court of Appeal said that having found good faith and a genuine claim, 'the court must nevertheless weigh all the circumstances and decide whether the claim ought to be pursued'. Whether the company stands 'to gain substantially in money or money's worth' relates to the company's interests rather than merit: 'A $100 claim may be meritorious but it may not be expedient to commence an action for it.' The court's use of 'must' shows it will consider the company's interests, but Koh notes it was unclear whether the complainant bears the onus, since the claim there failed on merit anyway."},
    {t:"Small claims can't be brought derivatively", correct:false,
     why:"No monetary floor exists; the point is cost-benefit, not size as such."},
    {t:"Only claims over $100 are meritorious", correct:false,
     why:"Merit and size are different questions."},
    {t:"The court can't consider costs", correct:false,
     why:"The point is precisely that cost-benefit is relevant."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.066]", takeaway:"Pang Yong Hock: merit ≠ expedience; court must weigh whether to pursue." },

{ id:"cl10-048", lesson:"10I", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each authority to its approach to s 216A(3)(c) or its equivalent.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Law Chin Eng v Hiap Seng", r:"Applicant must show real loss and prospects of redress"},
    {l:"Ang Thiam Swee v Low Hian Chor", r:"Low bar: only obviously unmeritorious claims culled"},
    {l:"Australian provision", r:"'Is' in the 'best interests', on the balance of probabilities"},
    {l:"Fong Wai Lyn; Urs Meisterhans", r:"Once legitimate, defendant must show it isn't in the interests"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [10.067] says most local cases following Pang Yong Hock put the burden on the complainant to show positive impact. Kan Ting Chiu J in Law Chin Eng v Hiap Seng said the benefit 'should be real', and the applicant must show the company sustained or may sustain real loss and that there are prospects of relief. But in Ang Thiam Swee v Low Hian Chor, the Court of Appeal said the standard is low and 'only the most obviously unmeritorious claims will be culled'; the court 'may' also examine practical and commercial interests ([10.067]–[10.068]). Australia ([10.069]) requires the court to be satisfied the action 'is' in the 'best interests' of the company, on the balance of probabilities after considering all circumstances (Palmer J), a much higher threshold. Koh [10.071] favours the High Court's approach in Fong and Urs Meisterhans: once the action is legitimate (a reasonable semblance of merit, not frivolous, vexatious or bound to fail), the defendant must show why it is not in the company's interests.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.067]–[10.071]", takeaway:"Law Chin Eng (real benefit) · Ang Thiam Swee (low bar) · Australia (best interests, BoP) · Fong (burden shift)." },

{ id:"c10x-046", lesson:"10I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the significance of the Court of Appeal using 'may' in Ang Thiam Swee when referring to commercial interests?",
  options:[
    {t:"Considering commercial merits is not obligatory, so the complainant need not adduce evidence of them, consistent with the low statutory standard; but the court may still take them into account", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.067] quotes Ang Thiam Swee: apart from a detached assessment of the legal merits, 'the court may also go further to examine whether it would be in the practical and commercial interests of the company for the action to be brought'. Koh [10.068] reasons that if considering commercial merits is not obligatory, the complainant need not adduce evidence to establish them. That fits the low standard required by the statutory wording; requiring proof of commercial interest as well as legal merit would place a significantly heavier burden on him. This affects only what the complainant must show, not the court's prerogative to consider commercial matters. As the High Court said in Wong Lee Vui Willie v Li Qingyun, commercial merit is important, and the matter is 'looked at in the round'."},
    {t:"The court is forbidden from considering commercial interests", correct:false,
     why:"'May' preserves the court's discretion to consider them."},
    {t:"The complainant must prove commercial benefit on the balance of probabilities", correct:false,
     why:"That is closer to the Australian approach, which Koh distinguishes."},
    {t:"Commercial interests override legal merit", correct:false,
     why:"Legal merit is the primary inquiry under Ang Thiam Swee; commercial interests are an optional further step."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.067]–[10.068]", takeaway:"Ang Thiam Swee: complainant shows legal merit; court may also weigh commerce." },

{ id:"c10x-047", lesson:"10I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is the Australian 'best interests' requirement a higher threshold than s 216A(3)(c)?",
  options:[
    {t:"It requires the court to be satisfied the action 'is' in the company's 'best interests', not that it 'appears' to be in its 'interests', so the applicant must prove it on the balance of probabilities considering all circumstances", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.069] says Australian courts generally require the applicant to show, beyond an arguable case, that the action would be of real practical benefit to the company, considering its character, the impact on its business, and whether the relief could be obtained without litigation against its will. Two textual differences explain this. First, the Australian section uses 'best interests', said to connote the company's 'separate and independent welfare', so the court must consider the action's likely impact. Second, the court must be satisfied the action 'is' in the best interests, not that it 'appears to be'. Palmer J said this is 'a far higher threshold', requiring the applicant to establish on the balance of probabilities a fact determined by all relevant circumstances. Singapore's s 216A(3)(c), based on the Ontario provision, uses the softer 'appears ... prima facie' and 'interests'."},
    {t:"Because Australia requires criminal proof", correct:false,
     why:"The standard is the civil balance of probabilities, not criminal proof."},
    {t:"Because Australia does not allow derivative actions", correct:false,
     why:"Australia has a statutory derivative action (s 237)."},
    {t:"Because Singapore's provision requires 'best interests' too", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore's provision refers to 'interests' and 'appears', not 'best interests' and 'is'."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.069]", takeaway:"Australia: 'is' + 'best interests' → BoP on all circumstances; Singapore softer." },

{ id:"cl10-049", lesson:"10I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Nemetz CJ say in Bellman v Western Approaches about 'appears to be in the interests' of the company?",
  options:[
    {t:"An arguable case suffices, but the court should then look at the directors' refusal to sue and ask whether it was impartial", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.070] says s 216A(3)(c) is textually closest to the Canadian provisions, but Canadian cases generally require the applicant to show, beyond legal merit, that the action appears to be in the company's interests; 'prima facie' refers only to the standard of proof. In Bellman v Western Approaches, Nemetz CJ said the section requires only that it 'appears' to be in the company's interests, meaning 'an arguable case be shown to subsist'. But he did not stop there. The common law logic was not meant to be disregarded: 'One must first look to the decision of the directors who, having been given reasonable notice ... decide not to assert a corporate right of action ... Can it be said that this refusal was given impartially?' On the facts, the refusal was not made by impartial directors. Some cases say an impartial board's refusal creates a presumption against the applicant, a harder task still."},
    {t:"The court must be satisfied the action is in fact beneficial", correct:false,
     why:"Nemetz CJ emphasised 'appears' and an arguable case, not satisfaction that it is beneficial."},
    {t:"The board's view is irrelevant", correct:false,
     why:"He said the directors' refusal must be examined for impartiality."},
    {t:"The common law logic must be disregarded", correct:false,
     why:"He said Parliament did not intend the common law logic to be disregarded."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.070]", takeaway:"Bellman: arguable case + scrutinise whether the board's refusal was impartial." },

{ id:"cl10-050", lesson:"10I", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In Katana v Avsenik, the court said leave should be granted if a claim has some possibility of success, even if it is unlikely to succeed.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.071] says some cases suggest that all that is needed is an arguable case. In Katana v Avsenik, the Ontario Superior Court said the issue is whether the action is bound to be unsuccessful; if there is 'some possibility of success of any of these claims', the court should allow it to proceed 'even if the Court is of the view that it is unlikely the action will be successful'. Koh says the choice between approaches may depend on the rationale for the statutory procedure: if it serves a broader corporate governance purpose of bringing wrongdoers to account, a lighter requirement and less weight on commercial impact may be justified."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"Katana did adopt the low 'some possibility of success' threshold."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.071]", takeaway:"Katana: 'not bound to fail' suffices." },

{ id:"cl10-051", lesson:"10I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the chapter see the burden-shifting approach in Fong Wai Lyn and Urs Meisterhans as a sensible compromise?",
  options:[
    {t:"The complainant often lacks evidence beyond an arguable case, while the defendants and company are better placed to explain why not suing is commercially justified", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [10.071] says the High Court in Fong Wai Lyn Carolyn and Urs Meisterhans v GIP placed the burden of showing that the action would not be in the company's interests on the defendant. Once the complainant shows the action is 'a legitimate one, ie, it has a reasonable semblance of merit and is not one which is frivolous, vexatious or bound to be unsuccessful', he has discharged his s 216A(3)(c) burden, and the defendant must show why leave should not be granted. This avoids an insurmountable hurdle for a complainant who, at this stage, rarely has enough evidence to go beyond an arguable case ([10.066]). Meanwhile the defendants or the company, who are better placed to explain whether refusing to sue is commercially justified, bear that burden, letting the court assess the matter objectively while respecting the logic behind Foss v Harbottle."},
    {t:"It removes the court's discretion", correct:false,
     why:"The court still assesses the matter objectively."},
    {t:"It requires full proof at the leave stage", correct:false,
     why:"It keeps the complainant's burden low: a legitimate, non-frivolous claim."},
    {t:"It applies the Australian 'best interests' test", correct:false,
     why:"It is a Singapore High Court approach, distinct from Australia's higher threshold."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.066], [10.071]", takeaway:"Fong/Urs Meisterhans: complainant shows legitimacy; defendant shows why not." },

{ id:"c10x-048", lesson:"10I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A minority shareholder of Lark Pte Ltd seeks leave to sue a director for a $40,000 secret profit. His affidavit, based partly on information and belief, shows a credible claim. The director's affidavit says legal costs would exceed $200,000, the director has no assets in Singapore, and the company has already recovered most of the profit by set-off against his salary.",
  prompt:"How would the Fong / Urs Meisterhans approach handle the 'company's interests' requirement?",
  options:[
    {t:"The complainant discharges his burden by showing a legitimate, non-frivolous claim; the burden then shifts to the director to show suit is not in Lark's interests, and his evidence on costs, recoverability and set-off may well succeed", correct:true,
     why:"Under the approach Koh favours ([10.071]), the complainant's burden under s 216A(3)(c) is discharged once he shows the proposed action is legitimate: a reasonable semblance of merit, not frivolous, vexatious or bound to fail. His affidavit, even if partly based on information and belief, can do that, since complainants often lack access to company documents ([10.066]). The burden then falls on the defendant to show why leave should not be granted, because he and the company are better placed to explain the commercial position. Here the director's evidence goes to exactly the considerations Pang Yong Hock said the court must weigh: costs out of proportion to the claim ('a $100 claim may be meritorious but it may not be expedient'), poor prospects of enforcement, and that the loss has largely been recovered. On that evidence the court may refuse leave."},
    {t:"Leave must be granted because the claim is credible", correct:false,
     why:"Legal merit is not the end of the inquiry; the court weighs whether suit is in the company's interests."},
    {t:"Leave must be refused because the complainant relied on information and belief", correct:false,
     why:"Reliance on information and belief is expected at the leave stage."},
    {t:"The complainant must prove commercial benefit on the balance of probabilities", correct:false,
     why:"That is the Australian approach, not Fong / Urs Meisterhans."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 10 [10.066]–[10.071]", takeaway:"Complainant shows legitimacy; defendant may show suit isn't commercially worthwhile." }

