/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 11 · Lesson 11A Personal Rights & the Constitution (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 11 [11.001]–[11.008]. Replaces the 11A questions in companyLaw11.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl11-001", lesson:"11A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When should the rule in Foss v Harbottle have no application to a shareholder's claim?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where the wrong breaches a duty or obligation owed to the shareholder personally, so the right to litigate vests in him", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.001] says that where a wrong is done to the company, the company is the proper plaintiff. But a shareholder may also be personally wronged by the company, its management or controllers. If the wrong results from breach of a duty or obligation owed to him personally, the right to litigate vests in him, Foss v Harbottle has no application, and litigation is entirely his matter. Koh adds that the position is not quite so absolute: the line between personal and corporate rights can be hard to draw, and even a breach of a personal right does not always give an unqualified damages claim. Much depends on whether the company also has a claim against the same defendant, the nature of the shareholder's loss, and whether it results from concurrent damage to the company (the reflective loss problem, 11B)."},
    {t:"Whenever the shareholder holds a majority of the shares", correct:false,
     why:"Holding size is irrelevant; the question is whose right was infringed."},
    {t:"Whenever the company has decided not to sue", correct:false,
     why:"A company's decision not to sue does not convert a corporate claim into a personal one."},
    {t:"Only where the claim is brought under s 216A", correct:false,
     why:"Section 216A is for corporate claims; personal claims are brought in the shareholder's own name."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.001]", takeaway:"Personal wrong → shareholder sues; but the line and the loss can be tricky." },

{ id:"cl11-002", lesson:"11A", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each source of a shareholder's personal rights to how it operates.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"A separate shareholders' agreement", r:"Usually enforceable by the shareholder in its own right"},
    {l:"The constitution", r:"A s 39 contract among members and with the company"},
    {l:"The Companies Act", r:"Includes the s 216 right to fair treatment"},
    {l:"A particular factual matrix", r:"Directors may owe duties to the shareholder directly"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [11.002] says a shareholder's rights may come from several sources. A separate contract with the company or other shareholders is usually enforceable by the shareholder in its own right. Where the facts justify it, directors may even owe duties directly to an individual shareholder (as in Coleman v Myers, 9B). The constitution confers personal rights, as a contract among members and between members and the company (s 39). And the Companies Act confers rights, notably the right to fair treatment in the conduct of the company's affairs, enforced through s 216, which gives the court a wide discretion to make remedial orders. Koh notes that because of that breadth, many shareholder complaints can now be dealt with under s 216, reducing (though not removing) the need to categorise rights; most of ch 11 is about the oppression action.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.002]", takeaway:"Personal rights: agreements, facts, constitution (s 39), Act (s 216)." },

{ id:"c11x-001", lesson:"11A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Section 39 makes the constitution a contract. What limits does Koh identify on the logic that every breach of it infringes each member's personal right?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only provisions conferring rights 'qua member' may be enforceable, and courts sometimes classify a breach of the constitution as a corporate wrong governed by Foss v Harbottle", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.003] says that by s 39 the constitution has contractual effect among members and between members and the company. A logical implication is that every member has a personal right to insist on its observance, so any breach infringes his personal right and Foss v Harbottle should be irrelevant. But there are limits. First, it appears not every provision is enforceable; there is judicial suggestion that only provisions conferring 'qua member' rights are (ch 5). Second, courts sometimes categorise a breach of the constitution as a corporate wrong and apply Foss v Harbottle. Mozley v Alston is an early example: shareholders complained that directors had not resigned as the constitution required, but Lord Cottenham held that 'if it be an injury at all, it is an injury not to the Plaintiffs personally, but to the corporation'."},
    {t:"The constitution is not a contract at all", correct:false,
     why:"Section 39 gives it contractual effect."},
    {t:"Only directors can enforce the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Members can enforce at least 'qua member' rights."},
    {t:"Breaches of the constitution are always void", correct:false,
     why:"The issue is who may sue, not automatic voidness."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.003]", takeaway:"s 39 logic limited by 'qua member' rule and corporate-wrong classification." },

{ id:"cl11-003", lesson:"11A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two registered shareholders complain that certain directors failed to resign as the company's constitution required.",
  prompt:"What did Lord Cottenham decide in Mozley v Alston?",
  options:[
    {t:"The breach was an injury to the corporation, not to the plaintiffs personally, so they could not sue", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.003] gives Mozley v Alston as an early example of a breach of the constitution being treated as a corporate wrong. Two shareholders complained that certain directors had not resigned as the constitution required. That was plainly a breach of the statutory contract, but the 'wrong' was categorised as a wrong to the company and held not personally actionable. Lord Cottenham, describing Foss v Harbottle as 'identical in principle', said that the alleged wrong, 'if it be an injury at all, it is an injury not to the Plaintiffs personally, but to the corporation of which they are members'. The case shows the courts' reluctance to let individual members litigate internal irregularities."},
    {t:"Every breach of the constitution is personally actionable", correct:false,
     why:"Mozley v Alston held the opposite on its facts."},
    {t:"The directors had validly resigned", correct:false,
     why:"They had not resigned; the case turned on who could complain."},
    {t:"The shareholders should have sought a winding up", correct:false,
     why:"The decision was about standing, not about winding up."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.003]", takeaway:"Mozley: failure to retire per articles = corporate injury." },

{ id:"cl11-004", lesson:"11A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A chairman refuses a valid demand for a poll, in breach of the articles. A shareholder seeks a declaration that the chairman acted improperly.",
  prompt:"Why did the action fail in MacDougall v Gardiner?",
  options:[
    {t:"The irregularity was something the majority are masters of, so only the majority could complain", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.004] says MacDougall v Gardiner is often cited as the leading authority denying a shareholder's personal right to enforce the constitution. The chairman refused a poll in breach of the articles, and the plaintiff sought a declaration. The Court of Appeal held the action could not be sustained, apparently because the irregularity was something 'the majority are masters of', so 'the majority are the only persons who can complain that a thing which they are entitled to do has been done irregularly'. James LJ said everything in the bill, if wrong, was a wrong to the company, since every meeting is for doing or undoing something for the company's benefit. Koh [11.007] adds that the decision has been trenchantly criticised and may be explained by practicality: by the appeal the plaintiff was 'in full possession of the company'."},
    {t:"Poll demands are not governed by the articles", correct:false,
     why:"The articles did govern polls; the refusal breached them."},
    {t:"The plaintiff had voted in favour of the resolution", correct:false,
     why:"That was not the ground of decision."},
    {t:"Declarations can never be granted to shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders can obtain declarations, as Pender v Lushington and Edwards v Halliwell show."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.004], [11.007]", takeaway:"MacDougall: majority are masters of irregularities." },

{ id:"cl11-005", lesson:"11A", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: In Devlin v Slough Estates, the article requiring accounts to comply with the companies legislation gave each shareholder a personal right to insist on compliance.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.005] describes Devlin v Slough Estates, where a shareholder complained that the annual accounts laid before the general meeting were defective, breaching an article requiring compliance with the companies legislation, and claimed that this gave him a personal right to complain. Dillon J held the proceedings misconceived: the article was 'not an article which gives any individual right to each individual shareholder to insist that the accounts prepared by the directors ... are in accordance with the provisions of the statutes'. It is a more recent example of a breach of the constitution being treated as a matter for the company."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Dillon J held the article conferred no individual right."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.005]", takeaway:"Devlin: accounts-compliance article gave no personal right." },

{ id:"cl11-006", lesson:"11A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What policy reason supports litigating procedural irregularities only through the company?",
  options:[
    {t:"Company administration is highly procedural, and letting every member sue could produce many lawsuits over inconsequential matters", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.006] asks whether these cases mean there can be no personal action for breach of the constitution because the company always has an overlapping right. There are sound policy reasons for insisting such matters go through the company. Company administration is highly procedural, and 'nothing can be more likely than that there should something more or less irregular done'. If every irregularity gave every member a right to litigate, there could be many lawsuits over inconsequential matters; as Mellish LJ said in MacDougall, all it takes is 'one cantankerous member, or one member who loves litigation'. That would waste resources. So requiring such matters to be litigated in the company's name, determined by majority rule, is 'immensely logical'."},
    {t:"Shareholders lack standing to read the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Members can inspect and rely on the constitution; the issue is litigation over irregularities."},
    {t:"Irregularities are never harmful", correct:false,
     why:"Some irregularities matter, which is why Pender v Lushington allowed a personal action."},
    {t:"Only directors are bound by the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Section 39 binds members and the company."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.006]", takeaway:"Policy: avoid floods of suits by 'one cantankerous member'." },

{ id:"cl11-007", lesson:"11A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Articles cap each member at 100 votes. A member transfers part of his holding to nominees, who are registered. The chairman rejects the nominees' votes at a general meeting.",
  prompt:"What did Jessel MR hold in Pender v Lushington?",
  options:[
    {t:"The plaintiff, as a member, had a personal right to maintain the action", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.007] says courts have intervened at a shareholder's instance especially where an irregularity may affect the result of a vote. Pender v Lushington, contemporaneous with MacDougall and described as 'irreconcilable' with it, involved articles that said the company would not be affected with notice of any trust, and gave one vote per ten shares, capped at 100 votes per member. To maximise his votes, the plaintiff transferred part of his holding to nominees who were duly registered. The chairman rejected the nominees' votes. The plaintiff sought an injunction in his own name and the company's. Jessel MR held that the plaintiff, as a member, had a personal right to maintain the action. The voting right was a personal right the court would protect."},
    {t:"Only the company could sue", correct:false,
     why:"Jessel MR recognised the member's personal right to sue."},
    {t:"Nominee holdings are void", correct:false,
     why:"The nominees were registered members, and the articles said the company was not affected by notice of trusts."},
    {t:"The vote cap was unenforceable", correct:false,
     why:"The cap was valid; the plaintiff legitimately structured his holdings within it."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.007]", takeaway:"Pender: wrongful rejection of votes = personal right to sue." },

{ id:"c11x-002", lesson:"11A", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"At Wren Ltd's general meeting, the chairman wrongly refuses to count 15% of the votes held by registered nominees of an investor, contrary to the articles. Had the votes been counted, a resolution approving a director's bonus would have failed. The investor sues personally.",
  prompt:"Which authority best supports the investor's personal action?",
  options:[
    {t:"Pender v Lushington: wrongful rejection of a member's votes, which affects the result, infringes a personal right the member may enforce", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.007] says courts have intervened at a shareholder's instance especially where an irregularity may affect the result of a vote. In Pender v Lushington, the chairman rejected votes of registered nominees, and Jessel MR held the plaintiff had a personal right to sue. Wren's facts are close: registered nominees' votes rejected in breach of the articles, changing the outcome. MacDougall v Gardiner points the other way, treating a refused poll as an irregularity of which the majority are masters, but Koh notes it has been trenchantly criticised and may be explained by its practical context. Koh [11.008] adds that today the question can be framed through s 392: an irregularity that is not merely procedural, or would cause substantial injustice, such as changing the result of a vote, should give the member a right to redress."},
    {t:"MacDougall v Gardiner: the majority are masters of irregularities", correct:false,
     why:"MacDougall is the contrary line, criticised and possibly explained by practicality; Pender fits these facts better."},
    {t:"Mozley v Alston: breaches of the articles are corporate injuries", correct:false,
     why:"Mozley concerned directors' failure to retire, not rejection of a member's own votes."},
    {t:"Devlin v Slough Estates: accounts provisions confer no personal rights", correct:false,
     why:"Devlin concerned accounts compliance, not voting rights."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.007]–[11.008]", takeaway:"Rejected votes that change the outcome → Pender-type personal action." },

{ id:"cl11-008", lesson:"11A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is a 'special interest' or 'personal impact' test hard to reconcile with Pender v Lushington and Wood v Odessa Waterworks?",
  options:[
    {t:"The rights enforced, to vote and to a dividend, were shared by every shareholder, not special to the plaintiff", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.007] says some breaches of the statutory contract give rise to a personal action and others do not, and drawing the line has been likened to 'wrestling with a greasy pig'. One suggestion is that the critical distinction is whether the non-compliance has a personal impact on the particular member; a similar one is whether he has a special interest in observance. But the right in Pender v Lushington, to vote, was one every shareholder should enjoy, and the right enforced in Wood v Odessa Waterworks, to be paid a dividend, was common to all ordinary shareholders. So it is hard to see why those plaintiffs had a 'personal' or 'special' interest beyond any other member's."},
    {t:"Neither case involved the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Both concerned rights under the articles."},
    {t:"Both plaintiffs lost", correct:false,
     why:"Both plaintiffs succeeded in enforcing their rights."},
    {t:"Both cases were decided under s 216", correct:false,
     why:"Both are old English cases on the articles as contract, not the oppression remedy."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.007]", takeaway:"Shared rights (vote, dividend) were enforced personally; 'special interest' test strains." },

{ id:"c11x-003", lesson:"11A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is the 'element of property' explanation for personal enforcement of some constitutional rights, and what are its limits?",
  options:[
    {t:"Rights to vote and to dividends are tied to ownership of the share, so they are enforceable personally; but the vote is not unfettered, and MacDougall denied a personal action despite affecting voting influence", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.007] notes the suggestion that some constitutional provisions are personally enforceable because they have an 'element of property'. The rights to vote and to receive dividends are inextricably tied to the shareholder's ownership of the share; the right to vote forms the core of his interest and underpins his participation. But the right to vote is not entirely unfettered, especially in alterations of the constitution or class rights. And the court in MacDougall did not allow a personal action even though wrongful refusal of a poll prevented the plaintiff from exerting his full voting influence. So the property explanation does not fully account for the cases either. Koh adds that MacDougall has been criticised and may reflect practicality: the plaintiff was by then 'in full possession of the company', so there was nothing substantial for the court to do."},
    {t:"All constitutional rights are property rights, so all are personally enforceable", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says the explanation is limited and does not account for MacDougall."},
    {t:"Only creditors have property in shares", correct:false,
     why:"Shares are the shareholder's property."},
    {t:"Voting rights are entirely unfettered", correct:false,
     why:"Koh notes voting is not entirely unfettered, particularly on constitution or class right changes."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.007]", takeaway:"'Property' explanation helps for votes/dividends, but MacDougall complicates it." },

{ id:"cl11-009", lesson:"11A", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Drawing the line between personal and corporate breaches of the constitution has been likened to 'wrestling with a greasy ____'.",
  accept:["pig"], answer:"pig",
  why:"Koh [11.007] says there are some breaches of the statutory contract that give rise to a personal action and others that do not, and drawing that line 'has been likened to wrestling with a greasy pig'. The proposed tests (personal impact, special interest, an 'element of property') each struggle to explain the cases, such as Pender v Lushington and Wood v Odessa Waterworks on one side and MacDougall v Gardiner, Mozley v Alston and Devlin v Slough Estates on the other. Koh [11.008] suggests s 392 offers a more workable lens today.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.007]", takeaway:"Personal vs corporate breaches: a 'greasy pig'." },

{ id:"cl11-010", lesson:"11A", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How does s 392 help frame a shareholder's personal right to have the constitution observed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Irregularities can be validated by court order unless they cause substantial injustice, so a member should get redress where the breach is not merely procedural or would cause him substantial injustice", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.008] says the issue may have lost significance because many irregularities are now governed by s 392 (8G). The section lets 'any interested person' apply for a validating order despite non-compliance with the constitution, but the court's s 392(4) power does not extend to irregularities that cause or are likely to cause substantial injustice. So the shareholder's personal right to have the company's affairs conducted according to the constitution can be understood from this perspective: if the failure to comply is not merely procedural, or, if procedural, might cause him substantial injustice, he should be entitled to seek redress. That fits Pender (votes affecting the outcome) better than the older 'majority are masters' approach."},
    {t:"It abolishes personal actions for breach of the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"Section 392 validates harmless irregularities; it does not abolish personal actions."},
    {t:"It makes every irregularity void", correct:false,
     why:"Section 392 validates, not avoids, irregularities, subject to substantial injustice."},
    {t:"It applies only to listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Section 392 applies generally."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.008]", takeaway:"s 392 lens: non-procedural or substantially unjust breaches → personal redress." }

]);

