/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 11 · Lessons 11G Unfairness & Rational Principles and 11H Contractual Expectations (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 11 [11.042]–[11.051]. Replaces the 11G and 11H questions in companyLaw11.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"cl11-037", lesson:"11G", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Lord Wilberforce in Kong Thai Sawmill: unfairness is 'a visible departure from the standards of fair ____ and a violation of the conditions of fair play'.",
  accept:["dealing"], answer:"dealing",
  why:"Koh [11.042] says the common thread through the s 216 grounds is unfairness or injustice in the commercial relationship among intra-corporate players, and the court's role is to decide whether the applicant has been treated unfairly. Lord Wilberforce in Re Kong Thai Sawmill described unfairness as 'a visible departure from the standards of fair dealing and a violation of the conditions of fair play which a shareholder is entitled to expect'. Koh calls this 'quite a nebulous concept', which is why Lord Hoffmann in O'Neill v Phillips insisted it be applied on rational principles.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.042]", takeaway:"Kong Thai Sawmill: departure from fair dealing and fair play." },

{ id:"cl11-038", lesson:"11G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What limit did Lord Hoffmann place on the court's discretion in O'Neill v Phillips?",
  options:[
    {t:"Fairness must be applied judicially on rational principles, drawn from established equitable principles, not whatever the individual judge thinks fair", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.042] says Lord Hoffmann accepted that Parliament chose unfairness 'to free the court from technical considerations of legal right and to confer a wide power to do what appeared just and equitable'. But in the same breath he said this 'does not mean that the court can do whatever the individual judge happens to think fair. The concept of fairness must be applied judicially and the content which it is given by the courts must be based upon rational principles.' Koh [11.043] adds the court does not sit 'under a palm tree', nor is it enough to have 'God and a good conscience' (Lord Clyde). Koh [11.044] says Lord Hoffmann found those rational principles in established equitable principles, justified because the company grew out of the partnership, whose governance equity regulated."},
    {t:"Only breaches of legal rights can be unfair", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Hoffmann recognised equitable considerations beyond strict legal rights (11I)."},
    {t:"The court must follow the majority's view of fairness", correct:false,
     why:"The court applies principled standards, not the majority's view."},
    {t:"The discretion is unfettered", correct:false,
     why:"He stressed the discretion must be exercised on rational principles."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.042]–[11.044]", takeaway:"O'Neill: wide but principled; content from equity." },

{ id:"c11x-019", lesson:"11G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What tension did Hammond J identify in Latimer Holdings v SEA Holdings about the oppression remedy?",
  options:[
    {t:"The section is remedial and meant to transcend the old law's limits, yet a principled approach is needed so the commercial world can measure conduct against it", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.043] describes the conundrum: a minimally restricted discretion is needed so courts can address the infinite forms of unfairness, but it should not be exercised at the expense of a rational, principled approach. Hammond J in Latimer Holdings v SEA Holdings (NZ Court of Appeal) said there was always going to be a problem: the section was 'remedial and enabling', deliberately designed to transcend the former law's limitations, but 'it is appropriate that there be a principled approach to the section, against which the commercial world, and its advisers, can measure conduct'. Koh [11.044] notes the Cohen committee had said general guidelines were impossible because the inquiry is so fact-specific, and fairness is often a matter of perspective."},
    {t:"The section is too narrow to be useful", correct:false,
     why:"The concern is breadth versus predictability, not narrowness."},
    {t:"Courts should avoid any guidance", correct:false,
     why:"Hammond J wanted a principled approach for commercial guidance."},
    {t:"Only legislation can supply principles", correct:false,
     why:"The principles are developed judicially."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.043]–[11.044]", takeaway:"Latimer: remedial breadth vs need for principled predictability." },

{ id:"cl11-039", lesson:"11G", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Australian courts have embraced Lord Hoffmann's equitable-principles approach, as Singapore has.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.045] says the Court of Appeal in Lim Swee Khiang v Borden explicitly endorsed Lord Hoffmann's approach as applicable to s 216. But that is not universal. Australian courts generally think it would unduly restrict the remedy's intended breadth. In Tomanovic v Global Mortgage Equity, the NSW Court of Appeal said it would be 'very strange' if Parliament used language different from equity's but the section were construed as 'nothing more than familiar old equity, dressed up in new clothes'. Situations where equity would give a remedy may be a significant sub-class of oppression cases but 'cannot be assumed to exhaust the scope of the term'."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Australia has generally resisted confining oppression to equitable principles (Tomanovic)."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.045]", takeaway:"Singapore follows O'Neill (Lim Swee Khiang); Australia resists (Tomanovic)." },

{ id:"cl11-040", lesson:"11G", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does the chapter value judicial guidance on how s 216 applications are assessed?",
  options:[
    {t:"Counsel can advise more definitely and gather evidence efficiently, and it reduces the risk of the remedy itself being used opportunistically to oppress", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.046] says that given the fact-intensive, context-driven inquiry, guidance has clear merit. Practically, counsel can be more definite in advice and more targeted in gathering evidence, saving time and resources. More aspirationally, guidance addresses the risk that the inexact criterion encourages opportunism, which could paradoxically make the remedy itself a means of oppression. Hoffmann J warned that 'the threat of such proceedings by a dissident and possibly legally-aided shareholder in a small company can be used to bring pressure on a majority to accept the price he demands for his shares'. This links to the abuse-of-process cases in 11E (Re Astec)."},
    {t:"It removes the need for evidence", correct:false,
     why:"Guidance makes evidence-gathering more targeted, not unnecessary."},
    {t:"It lets the court avoid considering facts", correct:false,
     why:"The inquiry remains fact-intensive."},
    {t:"It guarantees success for minority applicants", correct:false,
     why:"Guidance also protects majorities from opportunistic claims."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.046]", takeaway:"Guidance aids advice and curbs opportunistic use of s 216." },

{ id:"c11x-020", lesson:"11G", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A judge hearing a s 216 petition says: 'Whatever the agreements say, I personally find it unfair that the founder earns twice what the other directors earn, so I will order a buyout.'",
  prompt:"What is wrong with this approach?",
  options:[
    {t:"The court cannot impose its personal sense of fairness; unfairness must be applied judicially on rational principles grounded in the parties' agreed and equitable expectations", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.042] says the breadth of s 216 is not carte blanche for the court to impose personal standards of fairness. Lord Hoffmann in O'Neill v Phillips said the court cannot 'do whatever the individual judge happens to think fair'; fairness must be applied judicially, based on rational principles. Koh [11.043] adds the court does not sit under a palm tree. Singapore adopted this in Lim Swee Khiang ([11.045]). The right question is whether the founder's pay departs from what the shareholders agreed or could legitimately expect, measured against the constitution, any shareholders' agreement and equitable considerations such as a quasi-partnership understanding (11H–11I). A judge's personal distaste for a pay gap is not that."},
    {t:"Nothing: s 216 gives the judge an unfettered discretion", correct:false,
     why:"O'Neill and Lim Swee Khiang reject an unfettered, personal approach."},
    {t:"The only problem is that buyouts are never available", correct:false,
     why:"Buyouts are the most common remedy; the problem is the basis for finding unfairness."},
    {t:"The judge should have applied the Australian test", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore follows O'Neill; even Australia does not license personal standards of fairness."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.042]–[11.045]", takeaway:"No palm-tree justice: unfairness measured against principled expectations." },

{ id:"cl11-041", lesson:"11H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What two sources of shareholder 'expectations' do the cases look to?",
  options:[
    {t:"Enforceable legal rights arising from formal undertakings, and a 'fundamental understanding' between the parties not recorded in the formal documents", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.047] says unfairness is measured against the standards of corporate behaviour the shareholder is entitled to expect or rely on. Conduct may be unfair if his expectations about how the company should be run are violated. The cases look to two sources: enforceable expectations arising from formally binding undertakings, which are essentially legal rights (the constitution, shareholders' agreements, the Act); and expectations arising from some 'fundamental understanding' between the parties that has not been put into any formal document. The first is covered in 11H, the second (legitimate expectations and quasi-partnerships) in 11I."},
    {t:"Market expectations and analyst forecasts", correct:false,
     why:"The expectations are those between intra-corporate parties, not market forecasts."},
    {t:"The shareholder's subjective hopes and the board's promises", correct:false,
     why:"Subjective hopes do not suffice; expectations must be grounded in legal rights or a fundamental understanding."},
    {t:"Statute and case law only", correct:false,
     why:"Constitution, agreements and informal understandings also matter."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.047]", takeaway:"Expectations: legal rights + fundamental understandings." },

{ id:"cl11-042", lesson:"11H", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Hoffmann LJ in Re Saul D Harrison: 'keeping promises and honouring agreements is probably the most important element of commercial ____'.",
  accept:["fairness"], answer:"fairness",
  why:"Koh [11.048] says companies generally operate within the structure set by the Act and their constitutional documents, so shareholders' expectations are, for many companies, exhaustively contained there, supplemented by any shareholders' or collateral agreements. The constitution has contract-like status under s 39, and members agree to be bound by it. So the parties should not ordinarily be allowed to disregard that structure. As Hoffmann LJ said in Re Saul D Harrison, 'keeping promises and honouring agreements is probably the most important element of commercial fairness'. Sanctity of contract is the norm, and infringing the agreed basis for conducting the company's affairs supplies the 'misconduct' for s 216 to bite.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.048]", takeaway:"Saul D Harrison: honouring agreements is central to commercial fairness." },

{ id:"cl11-043", lesson:"11H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shareholders' agreement entrenches the minority's representatives on the board. The majority then orchestrates corporate acts and transactions without reference to them.",
  prompt:"What did the SICC hold in Dystar Global Holdings v Kiri Industries?",
  options:[
    {t:"The majority had 'nullified' the minority's voice in key decisions, contrary to the agreement", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.048] says whether there is a breach of the agreed basis is a question of interpretation. In Dystar Global Holdings v Kiri Industries, the SICC held that a shareholders' agreement provision entrenching the minority's representatives on the board gave the minority the means to have a 'say on key decisions that [the company] made'. By orchestrating corporate acts and transactions without reference to the minority, the majority 'nullified' the minority's voice in breach of that agreement, supplying the misconduct for s 216. Contrast Re Compound Photonics, where nothing expressly entrenched the minority on the board."},
    {t:"Board representation carries no expectation of a say", correct:false,
     why:"The SICC held entrenched representation meant a say on key decisions."},
    {t:"The minority should have sued for breach of contract only", correct:false,
     why:"The breach supported a s 216 claim."},
    {t:"Entrenchment clauses are void", correct:false,
     why:"The clause was valid and enforced."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.048]", takeaway:"Dystar: bypassing entrenched minority directors = breach of bargain." },

{ id:"c11x-021", lesson:"11H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Shareholders' agreement A requires the minority's two nominee directors to be present for a board quorum but says nothing to stop the majority removing them. Shareholders' agreement B says the minority 'shall be entitled to maintain two directors on the board at all times'. In each case the majority removes the minority's directors.",
  prompt:"Following Re Compound Photonics and Dystar, which removal is more likely to breach the bargain?",
  options:[
    {t:"B: it expressly entrenches the minority's board seats; A's quorum provision suggests continued involvement but does not expressly stop removal", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.048] contrasts two cases. In Dystar, a provision entrenching the minority's representatives gave them a say on key decisions, so bypassing them breached the agreement. In Re Compound Photonics, the minority could not show that board entrenchment was part of the bargain: provisions such as requiring their presence for a quorate board meeting suggested the parties envisaged their continued involvement, but nothing expressly prevented the majority from exercising its power to remove them, so removal was not a breach. Agreement A is like Compound Photonics; agreement B is like Dystar. Under B, removal breaches the agreed basis and can supply the misconduct for s 216. Under A, the minority would need to rely on extra-contractual expectations, such as a quasi-partnership understanding (11I), if any."},
    {t:"A, because quorum requirements entrench directors", correct:false,
     why:"Compound Photonics held a quorum requirement did not prevent removal."},
    {t:"Both equally", correct:false,
     why:"The express entrenchment in B makes the difference."},
    {t:"Neither, because directors can always be removed", correct:false,
     why:"A private company's shareholders can contractually entrench board seats (and s 152(9) allows the constitution to qualify removal)."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.048]", takeaway:"Express entrenchment (Dystar) vs implied involvement (Compound Photonics)." },

{ id:"cl11-044", lesson:"11H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shareholder-director causes related-party transactions in breach of the articles. They do the minority no harm and in fact benefit the company.",
  prompt:"Is the breach enough for oppression, per Over Over v Bonvests?",
  options:[
    {t:"Not on its own: a breach of the constitution doesn't automatically attract relief, because it may not be unfair", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.049] says a constitutional or contractual breach does not, ipso facto, attract relief under s 216, because the breach may not be unfair. In Over & Over v Bonvests Holdings, a shareholder-director orchestrated related-party transactions in breach of the articles, but they caused the minority no harm and actually benefited the company. The Court of Appeal said that on its own, this breach would not sustain an oppression claim. The question remains unfairness, not just technical breach. Koh adds that a member who can prove breach of contract may sometimes do better suing at common law (Re a company (No 00477 of 1986))."},
    {t:"Yes: any breach of the articles is oppression", correct:false,
     why:"Over & Over v Bonvests rejects automatic oppression from breach."},
    {t:"Yes, because related-party deals are always unfair", correct:false,
     why:"Here they benefited the company and harmed no one."},
    {t:"No, because the articles can't be enforced", correct:false,
     why:"The articles are enforceable; the point is that breach is not automatically unfair."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.049]", takeaway:"Breach ≠ automatic unfairness (Over & Over v Bonvests)." },

{ id:"c11x-022", lesson:"11H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What warning did Hoffmann J give in Re a company (No 00477 of 1986) about framing contract and fraud claims as unfair prejudice petitions?",
  options:[
    {t:"Petitioners whose real claim is damages for fraud or breach of contract risk being left without a remedy, since such claims may fit the letter but not the spirit of the provision", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.049] says that given how nebulous unfairness is, a member who can prove breach of contract may be better advised to sue at common law. In Re a company (No 00477 of 1986), minority shareholders petitioned for a buyout, alleging false and fraudulent representations by the controllers. Hoffmann J declined to strike out the petition but expressed 'grave doubts': the substance was a claim for damages for fraud and breach of contract, which 'may arguably fall within the letter' of the provision but not 'within its spirit'. If they pursued the petition, they ran 'a grave risk that they may be left without a remedy even though they prove facts which would entitle them to damages at common law'."},
    {t:"That fraud claims must always be brought under the unfair prejudice provision", correct:false,
     why:"He suggested the opposite: such claims belong at common law."},
    {t:"That petitioners are always better off under the statute", correct:false,
     why:"He warned they might end up with no remedy."},
    {t:"That buyout orders are unavailable in fraud cases", correct:false,
     why:"His concern was fit and risk, not a categorical bar."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.049]", takeaway:"Real contract/fraud claim? Sue at common law, not s 216." },

{ id:"c11x-023", lesson:"11H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Apart from compliance with the constitution, what else are shareholders entitled to expect, and why do breaches of directors' duties often feature in s 216 claims?",
  options:[
    {t:"That powers will be exercised within their legal limits; diversions, misuse of assets and excessive self-payment usually breach directors' duties, and enabling the court to outflank Foss v Harbottle was one of the section's purposes", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.050] says shareholders may also assume that powers given to the majority or the board will be exercised within the boundaries set by the Act and the general law. A major concern is controllers using their powers for themselves: diverting business or assets to ventures the minority has no stake in, using company funds personally, or paying themselves large salaries while excluding non-managing minorities. Such acts usually involve directors breaching fiduciary and statutory duties. Since only the company can sue, and the majority can forgive, wrongdoers blessed by the majority could escape. Before s 216A, minorities had only the difficult fraud on the minority exception. So corporate wrongs were a common means of oppression. Hoffmann LJ in Re Saul D Harrison said 'enabling the court in an appropriate case to outflank the rule in Foss v Harbottle was one of the purposes of the section'."},
    {t:"That the company will always make profits", correct:false,
     why:"Shareholders are not entitled to expect profit."},
    {t:"That directors will consult them on every decision", correct:false,
     why:"Management belongs to the board; consultation is not a general expectation."},
    {t:"That dividends will be paid annually", correct:false,
     why:"Dividends depend on the board and the constitution, not a general expectation."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.050]", takeaway:"Expect lawful use of powers; s 216 can outflank Foss (Saul D Harrison)." },

{ id:"cl11-045", lesson:"11H", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Under the Ho Yew Kong v Sakae Holdings framework, what must an applicant show when his s 216 claim involves corporate wrongs?",
  options:[
    {t:"That his 'real injury' is oppression against him as a shareholder and the 'essential remedy' is one available only under s 216", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.051] says that corporate wrongs cannot lead inexorably to actionable oppression. The Court of Appeal in Ong Heng Chuan said asserting a purely corporate wrong is 'insufficient, and inappropriate'; the claimant must 'go a step further, and to show how such a breach is also a wrong suffered by him qua shareholder'. The question is whether the 'real injury' is oppressive conduct against him as a shareholder within s 216, and whether the 'essential remedy' sought is one obtainable only under s 216. Those factors underpin the framework in Ho Yew Kong v Sakae Holdings for cases featuring both personal and corporate wrongs. The legislative scheme channels corporate claims through s 216A, respecting the proper plaintiff principle; vindicating corporate wrongs under s 216 would circumvent Salomon and that scheme, and be an abuse of process."},
    {t:"Only that the directors breached their duties", correct:false,
     why:"Ong Heng Chuan says asserting a corporate wrong alone is insufficient."},
    {t:"That the company has refused to sue", correct:false,
     why:"Refusal is relevant to s 216A, not the s 216 real-injury test."},
    {t:"That the wrong was ratified", correct:false,
     why:"Ratification is not the test under Ho Yew Kong."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.050]–[11.051]", takeaway:"Ho Yew Kong: real injury qua shareholder + essential remedy only under s 216." },

{ id:"c11x-024", lesson:"11H", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A 20% shareholder of Teal Pte Ltd files a s 216 claim. Her only complaint is that a director negligently signed a bad supply contract, causing Teal a loss. She asks the court to order the director to compensate Teal.",
  prompt:"How is the court likely to view this claim under Ong Heng Chuan and Ho Yew Kong?",
  options:[
    {t:"As a purely corporate claim: her real injury is the company's loss and the essential remedy is corporate relief, which belongs in a s 216A derivative action, so pursuing it under s 216 may be an abuse", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.051] says asserting a purely corporate wrong is insufficient for s 216; the claimant must show how the breach is also a wrong suffered by her qua shareholder (Ong Heng Chuan). Under the Ho Yew Kong framework, the court asks whether the 'real injury' is oppression against her as a shareholder and whether the 'essential remedy' is one available only under s 216. Here the injury is Teal's loss from a negligent contract, and the remedy sought is compensation to Teal. That is a corporate claim. The legislative scheme channels such claims through s 216A, with its leave filters, to respect the proper plaintiff principle. Using s 216 instead would circumvent that scheme and may be an abuse of process. She should seek leave under s 216A."},
    {t:"As a valid s 216 claim, because any loss to the company prejudices shareholders", correct:false,
     why:"That reasoning would collapse the corporate/personal distinction; Ong Heng Chuan rejects it."},
    {t:"As valid, because s 216 lets the court order anything", correct:false,
     why:"Breadth of relief does not override the requirement of personal injury qua shareholder."},
    {t:"As a reflective loss claim she can recover personally", correct:false,
     why:"Reflective loss is not personally recoverable, and she seeks relief for the company anyway."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.051]", takeaway:"Purely corporate wrong + corporate remedy → s 216A, not s 216." }

]);

;
