/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 11 · Lesson 11I Legitimate Expectations & Quasi-Partnerships (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 11 [11.052]–[11.061]. Replaces the 11I questions in companyLaw11.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"c11x-025", lesson:"11I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How can conduct be unfair under s 216 even though the constitution has been fully complied with?",
  options:[
    {t:"Equity gives effect to the parties' true intentions and may restrain the exercise of strict legal rights where that would be contrary to good faith in the relationship", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.052] says unfairness can embrace more than violation of constitutional rights; that is why Lord Hoffmann said it was chosen for the remedy. Here the 'equitable principles' matter. Equity generally gives effect to the parties' true intentions, so where an otherwise legal and valid exercise of power is contrary to what may fairly be regarded as the parties' contemplation, equity may restrain it as unfair. As Lord Hoffmann put it, 'one of the traditional roles of equity ... was to restrain the exercise of strict legal rights in certain relationships in which it considered that this would be contrary to good faith'. Koh [11.054] adds that the effect is to give the other party, often the minority, a 'correlative right' in relation to those extraneous understandings."},
    {t:"It cannot: compliance with the constitution is always a complete answer", correct:false,
     why:"Equitable considerations can make lawful exercises of power unfair."},
    {t:"Only if the majority acted fraudulently", correct:false,
     why:"Fraud is not required; the question is good faith in light of the parties' understanding."},
    {t:"Only if the constitution is ambiguous", correct:false,
     why:"Equity can restrain even clear legal rights in the right relationship."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.052], [11.054]", takeaway:"Equity can restrain lawful rights contrary to good faith → 'correlative rights'." },

{ id:"cl11-046", lesson:"11I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Lord Wilberforce recognise in Ebrahimi v Westbourne Galleries?",
  options:[
    {t:"Behind a company there may be individuals with rights, expectations and obligations among themselves not submerged in the company structure", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.053] says Lord Wilberforce, discussing the just and equitable winding-up jurisdiction in Ebrahimi v Westbourne Galleries, said a limited company 'is more than a mere legal entity': behind it 'there are individuals, with rights, expectations and obligations inter se which are not necessarily submerged in the company structure' defined by the Act and articles. So parties may operate on an unwritten, perhaps unspoken, understanding about a fundamental aspect of their association. There may then be 'considerations ... of a personal character' making it 'unjust, or inequitable, to insist on legal rights, or to exercise them in a particular way'. The understanding, though unenforceable at law, becomes binding 'in fairness and equity'. Koh [11.054] says this applies only where the association has 'something more' than a purely commercial relationship."},
    {t:"A company is nothing more than a legal entity", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Wilberforce said the opposite."},
    {t:"Informal understandings override the articles in every company", correct:false,
     why:"They matter only where the association has 'something more' (11.054)."},
    {t:"Majority rule is absolute", correct:false,
     why:"Ebrahimi recognised equitable limits on majority power."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.052]–[11.054]", takeaway:"Ebrahimi: rights inter se not submerged in the company; 'something more' needed." },

{ id:"c11x-026", lesson:"11I", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: Equitable considerations of the Ebrahimi kind can arise in any company, including one formed between strangers on a purely commercial basis with detailed agreements.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.054] says both Lord Wilberforce and Lord Hoffmann made clear the court must look closely at the relationship and the nature of the parties' dealings to see whether their association goes beyond the purely commercial. Only where it has 'something more' may it be unfair to ignore or contradict promises or expectations outside the formal documents. Koh [11.059] adds that a shareholder in a company run at arm's length will typically not be able to complain about constitutionally compliant exercises of majority power, especially where the governance structure resulted from informed negotiation between experienced businesspeople, though the specific facts remain decisive (and Over & Over v Bonvests found a quasi-partnership between two businessmen without prior dealings)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"A purely commercial association usually lacks the 'something more' equity requires."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.054], [11.059]", takeaway:"Equitable considerations need 'something more' than a commercial bargain." },

{ id:"cl11-047", lesson:"11I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why did Lord Hoffmann later call his phrase 'legitimate expectation' 'probably a mistake'?",
  options:[
    {t:"It was never meant to cover all of a shareholder's factual expectations, only those that equitable considerations elevate into a protectable right", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.055] says Hoffmann LJ used 'legitimate expectation' in Re Saul D Harrison for the correlative right. In O'Neill v Phillips he said the phrase 'was probably a mistake', because it was not meant to embrace all of a shareholder's factual expectations. The UK now prefers 'equitable considerations', though the concept is unchanged. Singapore courts still use 'legitimate expectations', but Koh warns not to be misled by its ordinary meaning. Not every expectation is legitimate; only where the relationship or circumstances bring equitable considerations into play is an expectation elevated to a protectable right. 'Legitimate expectations' should 'not be allowed to lead a life of its own'."},
    {t:"Because the concept was abolished", correct:false,
     why:"The concept continues; only the label was regretted."},
    {t:"Because it applied only to public companies", correct:false,
     why:"It applies mainly to closely held companies."},
    {t:"Because it conflicted with s 216", correct:false,
     why:"Singapore courts still use the phrase under s 216."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.055]", takeaway:"'Legitimate expectation' ≠ any expectation; it needs equitable underpinning." },

{ id:"c11x-027", lesson:"11I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Three friends set up Lime Pte Ltd, each investing equally on the understanding that all three would work in the business as directors and draw salaries instead of dividends. Nothing is written down. Two of them later remove the third as director by an ordinary resolution that fully complies with the constitution.",
  prompt:"Is the removal likely to be oppressive under s 216?",
  options:[
    {t:"Likely yes: an expectation of management involvement, in a company where members invested on the footing of working in it, can be a correlative right breached by constitutionally compliant removal", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.056] says it is common for members to associate on the premise that each will have some say in management, such as a board seat or a management role. Small companies are often run informally, and members may invest 'on the footing that dividends are unlikely but that each will earn his living by working for the company as a director'. In that case management involvement can be a 'correlative right', breached by exclusion or removal even if done in full compliance with the constitution. If the breach is unfair, s 216 relief is available. Lime's facts fit closely: equal investment by friends, an understanding that all would work as directors, and salaries in place of dividends. These are also hallmarks of a quasi-partnership ([11.057]). A buyout at fair value is a likely remedy (11L)."},
    {t:"No: the removal complied with the constitution, so it cannot be unfair", correct:false,
     why:"Equitable considerations can make compliant removal unfair."},
    {t:"No, because the understanding was not in writing", correct:false,
     why:"Unwritten understandings are exactly what equity can enforce in fairness."},
    {t:"Yes, but only if he can prove fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Fraud is not required; breach of the correlative right is enough if unfair."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.056]–[11.057]", takeaway:"Invested to work as a director → removal can be oppressive despite compliance." },

{ id:"cl11-048", lesson:"11I", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each feature or case to its place in the quasi-partnership analysis.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Personal relationship", r:"Mutual trust and confidence"},
    {l:"Participation", r:"Understanding that some or all members run the business"},
    {l:"Transfer restriction", r:"Members can't easily exit"},
    {l:"Patriarch-run family company", r:"Usually not a quasi-partnership (Thio Syn Kym Wendy)"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [11.057] says oppression cases often arise in small private companies called 'quasi-partnerships', which have the characteristics Lord Wilberforce described in Ebrahimi: an association formed or continued on a personal relationship of mutual trust and confidence; an understanding that all or some shareholders will participate in running the business; and restrictions on transferring members' interests. Companies converted from partnerships or owned entirely by family members are often quasi-partnerships, but not inevitably. In Thio Syn Kym Wendy v Thio Syn Pyn, the Court of Appeal said traditional family companies where 'the family patriarch wields extreme amounts of control ... and selectively grooms his successors' would not usually be quasi-partnerships, for lack of mutual trust and confidence. Conversely, Over & Over v Bonvests found a quasi-partnership in a joint venture between two experienced businessmen with no prior personal dealings.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.057]", takeaway:"Quasi-partnership: trust + participation + transfer limits; family ≠ automatic." },

{ id:"c11x-028", lesson:"11I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Case 1: Grandfather Tan runs Tan Holdings with an iron hand, deciding everything and choosing which grandchildren to groom as successors; the grandchildren hold small stakes. Case 2: Two experienced businessmen who had never met before form a 50-50 joint venture company, on an understanding that both will manage it together.",
  prompt:"Which is more likely to be a quasi-partnership?",
  options:[
    {t:"Case 2 can be, as in Over & Over v Bonvests; Case 1 usually is not, lacking mutual trust and confidence (Thio Syn Kym Wendy)", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.057] says classification is fact-dependent and generalisations are best avoided. In Thio Syn Kym Wendy v Thio Syn Pyn, the Court of Appeal said traditional family companies where 'the family patriarch wields extreme amounts of control over the company and selectively grooms his successors' would not usually be quasi-partnerships, because of a lack of mutual trust and confidence. Tan Holdings fits that description. By contrast, in Over & Over v Bonvests, the Court of Appeal found a quasi-partnership in a joint venture between two experienced businessmen who had no prior personal dealings, presumably because their association rested on mutual trust and an understanding of joint management. What matters is the presence of mutual trust and confidence among all members, which provides the factual basis for equitable considerations. Koh [11.059] notes that even non-quasi-partnership family companies may carry some equitable expectations."},
    {t:"Case 1, because all family companies are quasi-partnerships", correct:false,
     why:"Thio Syn Kym Wendy says patriarch-controlled family companies usually are not."},
    {t:"Neither, because quasi-partnerships require prior friendship", correct:false,
     why:"Over & Over v Bonvests found one without prior personal dealings."},
    {t:"Both equally", correct:false,
     why:"The presence of mutual trust and confidence differs markedly between them."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.057]", takeaway:"Quasi-partnership turns on mutual trust, not labels like 'family' or 'strangers'." },

{ id:"cl11-049", lesson:"11I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In a quasi-partnership, the majority shareholder ends an equal profit-sharing arrangement and won't allot further shares the petitioner expected to receive.",
  prompt:"Why did the House of Lords reject the petition in O'Neill v Phillips?",
  options:[
    {t:"The majority had made no unconditional promise; enforcing the expectation would impose an obligation he never agreed to", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.058] says that even in a quasi-partnership, not every expectation becomes a correlative right. The personal relationship must be such as to affect the conscience of the party exercising the legal right: the shareholder must have relied on the understanding, or it must have a 'sufficient degree of agreement that it can be said that there has been a breach of good faith in departing from it'. In O'Neill v Phillips, the petitioner complained that the majority ended equal profit-sharing and repudiated an alleged agreement to allot him more shares. The Court of Appeal agreed with him, but Lord Hoffmann said it had been 'misled by the expression legitimate expectation'. The petitioner had an expectation, but the majority had made no unconditional promise, so there was 'no basis, consistent with established principles of equity' for unfairness. Recognising it 'would not be restraining the exercise of legal rights. It would be imposing upon [him] an obligation to which he never agreed'."},
    {t:"It wasn't a quasi-partnership", correct:false,
     why:"The point was the absence of a binding understanding, not the company's classification."},
    {t:"The petitioner had unclean hands", correct:false,
     why:"Clean hands was not the basis."},
    {t:"Profit-sharing can never found a claim", correct:false,
     why:"It could if there were an unconditional understanding; here there was none."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.058]", takeaway:"O'Neill: no unconditional promise → no correlative right." },

{ id:"c11x-029", lesson:"11I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In Kale Pte Ltd, a quasi-partnership, the majority told the minority during negotiations: 'If profits exceed $5m, we might consider giving you more shares.' Profits reach $6m, but the majority decline to issue shares. The minority petitions under s 216.",
  prompt:"Applying O'Neill v Phillips, is the minority likely to succeed on this ground?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unlikely: a tentative statement that the majority 'might consider' shares is not an unconditional promise or a sufficiently agreed understanding, so enforcing it would impose an obligation never agreed", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.058] says that within a quasi-partnership, an expectation becomes a correlative right only if the relationship affects the conscience of the party exercising legal rights: the shareholder must have relied on the understanding, or it must have 'a sufficient degree of agreement' that departing from it breaches good faith. In O'Neill v Phillips, the majority had made no unconditional promise to allot shares, so refusing to do so was not unfair; recognising the expectation would impose an obligation 'to which he never agreed'. 'We might consider' is plainly conditional and non-committal. Even in a quasi-partnership, that does not create a binding expectation. The minority would need other grounds, such as exclusion from management, to succeed."},
    {t:"Likely: any expectation in a quasi-partnership is protected", correct:false,
     why:"O'Neill warns against protecting mere factual expectations."},
    {t:"Likely, because profits exceeded $5m", correct:false,
     why:"Meeting a condition of a non-binding statement does not create a promise."},
    {t:"Unlikely, because quasi-partnerships cannot found s 216 claims", correct:false,
     why:"Quasi-partnerships are the classic setting for s 216 claims; the problem is the lack of a binding understanding."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.058]", takeaway:"'We might consider…' ≠ binding understanding." },

{ id:"cl11-050", lesson:"11I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What unwritten expectations did the Court of Appeal in Thio Syn Kym Wendy suggest may exist in family companies?",
  options:[
    {t:"That family managers won't spend corporate resources to punish other family members, and that agreed benefits won't be arbitrarily reduced or removed", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.059] says that while extra-constitutional understandings sustain a s 216 claim only if equity makes them binding, s 216 applies well beyond quasi-partnerships. Family companies are not necessarily quasi-partnerships, but bonds of blood and kinship tend to cultivate 'considerations of a personal character'. In Thio Syn Kym Wendy v Thio Syn Pyn, the Court of Appeal said there may be 'unwritten expectations that the family shareholders in management cannot expend corporate resources for personal reasons to punish other family members and that any benefits previously agreed upon between the family members should not be arbitrarily reduced or removed'. Similarly, Tang Choon Keng Realty noted family shareholders' 'relative expectations of ... entitlement to their inheritance of the family assets'."},
    {t:"That every family member must be a director", correct:false,
     why:"No such expectation was identified."},
    {t:"That dividends must be paid annually", correct:false,
     why:"No such expectation was identified."},
    {t:"That the patriarch's decisions are final", correct:false,
     why:"The point is constraints on how family managers use power."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.059]", takeaway:"Family companies: no punitive use of company resources; no arbitrary removal of agreed benefits." },

{ id:"cl11-051", lesson:"11I", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Q holds just under 15%, R holds 84%, and three other directors hold one qualification share each. Only Q was invited to take shares beyond qualification, and Q ran the business when R was ill. After a board disagreement, Q is summarily dismissed.",
  prompt:"What did the court hold in Quinlan v Essex Hinge?",
  options:[
    {t:"Although the company wasn't a classic quasi-partnership, the specific association between Q and R involved mutual confidence and an understanding Q would participate", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.060] says that in non-quasi-partnership companies, the question is likely to be whether personal considerations exist between the complaining minority and the alleged oppressor, not among all members. In Quinlan v Essex Hinge, Q held just under 15%, R 84%, and three other directors held one qualification share each. Q was summarily dismissed after a disagreement with R. The evidence showed Q had a recognised management role beyond his service agreement (on which he had taken no legal advice). Significantly, only Q had been invited to take shares beyond the qualification requirement, and Q ran the business whenever R was indisposed. Although there were other shareholders, the specific association between Q and R involved mutual confidence and an understanding that Q would participate, supporting his unfair prejudice petition."},
    {t:"Q's service agreement defined his rights exhaustively", correct:false,
     why:"His recognised role went beyond the service agreement."},
    {t:"Other shareholders' presence defeated the claim", correct:false,
     why:"The court focused on the relationship between Q and R."},
    {t:"Directors can never be dismissed summarily", correct:false,
     why:"The point is that his dismissal breached an equitable understanding, not a general ban."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.060]", takeaway:"Quinlan: bilateral trust between minority and controller can suffice." },

{ id:"c11x-030", lesson:"11I", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does Re a company (No 00477 of 1986) and Thio Syn Kym Wendy teach about defining when equitable considerations arise?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is impossible and undesirable to define exhaustively; even a public company petition was not struck out, and whether conduct is oppression is 'a fact-specific inquiry'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.061] says that in Re a company (No 00477 of 1986), minority shareholders of a public company, who had sold their private company to it, alleged the controllers made specific representations about their management roles, so that their association was a partnership. Hoffmann J refused to strike out the petition, 'with some hesitation', saying the provision 'operates within so potentially wide a frame of reference' that he could not say no evidence could establish the director's employment was part of his legitimate expectations as a member. Koh stresses Lord Wilberforce's warning that it would be 'impossible, and wholly undesirable' to define when equitable considerations arise, and the Court of Appeal in Thio Syn Kym Wendy: 'whether an act constitutes oppression is, in the final analysis, a fact-specific inquiry'."},
    {t:"That public companies can never involve equitable expectations", correct:false,
     why:"Hoffmann J declined to strike out a public company petition."},
    {t:"That a fixed checklist determines every case", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Wilberforce said defining the circumstances is 'impossible, and wholly undesirable'."},
    {t:"That only written agreements matter", correct:false,
     why:"The whole point is that unwritten understandings can matter."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.061]", takeaway:"No exhaustive definition; oppression is fact-specific." }

]);

;
