/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 11 · Lesson 11J The Factual Inquiry & Capacity (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Koh Ch 11 [11.062]–[11.072]. Replaces the 11J questions in companyLaw11.js. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p3v2||[]).concat([

{ id:"c11x-031", lesson:"11J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Once protectable expectations are established, what more must an applicant show?",
  options:[
    {t:"That the conduct complained of departed from those expectations to the extent that it became unfair", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.062] says establishing a shareholder's protectable expectations, formal or extraneous, is only a first step. Unfairness is not shown merely by proving expectations exist; the conduct violating them must itself be unfair. Chan Seng Onn J put it as a two-part test: '(a) there are certain expectations between shareholders; and (b) that the conduct complained of is contrary to or has departed from such expectations to the extent that it has become unfair'. The court then looks at shareholders' interests and expectations to see whether, and how far, the standards of fair dealing and conditions of fair play have been departed from. So a technical departure from an expectation may not suffice if, in context, it is not unfair."},
    {t:"Nothing more: proving an expectation proves unfairness", correct:false,
     why:"Koh says expectation is only the first step."},
    {t:"That the respondents acted fraudulently", correct:false,
     why:"The test is objective; fraud or bad faith is not required."},
    {t:"That the company suffered a loss", correct:false,
     why:"The issue is unfairness to the member, not necessarily company loss."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.062]", takeaway:"Two steps: expectation exists + departure that is unfair." },

{ id:"cl11-052", lesson:"11J", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: To succeed under s 216, the applicant must show the respondents knew their conduct was unfair or acted in bad faith.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.062] says the test of unfairness is objective, so the applicant need not show the respondents acted in bad faith or knew that what they did was unfair to him. Unfairness depends on assessing the conduct against the surrounding facts: the overall relationship between the parties, the company's history and nature, and the broader context. It is a question of fact and degree, and conduct cannot be assessed in a vacuum. Because unfairness is so fact-specific, it cannot be catalogued exhaustively, and the deliberately open-ended legislative wording discourages attempts to do so."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Unfairness is objective; intention and bad faith are not required."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.062]", takeaway:"Unfairness: objective, contextual; no bad faith needed." },

{ id:"cl11-053", lesson:"11J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Who is the 'reasonable bystander' in assessing unfairness?",
  options:[
    {t:"Usually the commercial bystander, but where the complaint is about directors' discretion, the reasonable director (Wayde v NSW Rugby League)", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.063] says the test has been expressed as 'whether a reasonable bystander observing the consequences of their conduct, would regard it as having unfairly prejudiced the petitioner's interests'. The bystander changes with the complaint. Usually he is the 'commercial bystander'. Where the complaint concerns directors' exercise of discretion, as in Wayde v New South Wales Rugby League, he is the reasonable director. Koh [11.064] notes one judge's quip that it is not especially helpful to 'add the reasonable company watcher to the already substantial cast of imaginary characters', but the point stands: unfairness is contextual. As Lord Hoffmann said in O'Neill, 'conduct which is perfectly fair between competing businessmen may not be fair between members of a family'."},
    {t:"Always a reasonable shareholder", correct:false,
     why:"The bystander varies; for directors' discretion it is the reasonable director."},
    {t:"The judge's personal view", correct:false,
     why:"The test is objective, not personal."},
    {t:"The company's auditor", correct:false,
     why:"The auditor is not the reference point."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.063]–[11.064]", takeaway:"Reasonable bystander: commercial bystander or reasonable director; context matters." },

{ id:"c11x-032", lesson:"11J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"ParentCo agreed to give its 75%-owned subsidiary financial support, then withheld agreed sums. The minority shareholder of the subsidiary petitions. Evidence shows ParentCo was itself in dire financial straits, and withholding was a reasonable attempt to keep both companies afloat.",
  prompt:"Following Nicholas v Soundcraft, is the withholding likely to be unfair?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: the court balances all relevant factors, including competing interests, and a reasonable attempt to save both companies in a crisis was not unfair", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.065] says the court undertakes a balancing exercise taking account of all relevant factors, including conflicting interests of different groups within the company. In Nicholas v Soundcraft Electronics, the holding company withheld financial support despite an understanding to the contrary. But it was in dire financial straits, and withdrawing support was a reasonable attempt to keep both companies afloat. So the court held it had not acted unfairly. This shows the second step of the unfairness inquiry in action: there was an expectation (financial support) and a departure from it, but in context the departure was not unfair. Recall that the same case established that the parent's acts were part of the subsidiary's affairs ([11.036])."},
    {t:"Yes: breaking any understanding is always unfair", correct:false,
     why:"Departure from an expectation must also be unfair in context."},
    {t:"Yes, because the subsidiary is a separate company", correct:false,
     why:"Separateness was not the basis; the context of crisis was."},
    {t:"No, because parents never owe duties to subsidiaries' minorities", correct:false,
     why:"The court did consider the parent's conduct; it was found not unfair."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.065]", takeaway:"Departure in a genuine crisis may not be unfair (Nicholas v Soundcraft)." },

{ id:"c11x-033", lesson:"11J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In management-exclusion cases, why is the applicant's own conduct important?",
  options:[
    {t:"Because quasi-partnership status alone does not make exclusion unfair; if the applicant was himself at fault, for example in breach of his director's duties, removal may be justified (Grace v Biagioli)", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.066] says the parties' conduct can be decisive. In exclusion cases it is especially important to see whether there was good reason for excluding the applicant. That the company is a quasi-partnership does not by itself render exclusion unfair; fairness is assessed in context. The applicant might himself have acted wrongly. In Grace v Biagioli, the applicant was in breach of his director's duty, so his removal from the board was not unfair. As the High Court said in Ang Xing Yao Lionel v Lew Mun Hung Joseph ([11.067]), partnership in business, like friendship, is 'a two-way street: obligations flow in both directions'."},
    {t:"Because the applicant must have clean hands to apply at all", correct:false,
     why:"There is no strict clean-hands bar ([11.028]); conduct goes to fairness and relief."},
    {t:"Because exclusion is never unfair in quasi-partnerships", correct:false,
     why:"Exclusion is often unfair in quasi-partnerships, absent good reason or fair compensation."},
    {t:"Because only the majority's conduct is relevant", correct:false,
     why:"Both sides' conduct is relevant."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.066]–[11.067]", takeaway:"Applicant's own wrongdoing can justify exclusion (Grace v Biagioli)." },

{ id:"cl11-054", lesson:"11J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"After falling out with the majority, a petitioner is removed as director. He keeps his shares and accepts a weekly allowance of about half his salary for over two years. When a rights issue is proposed, he petitions.",
  prompt:"What did Hoffmann J decide in Re a company?",
  options:[
    {t:"Having accepted payment for so long, he couldn't complain of unfair exclusion; the petition was dismissed", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.067] says an applicant who does not protest his exclusion and accepts compensation may lose his complaint. In Re a company, the petitioner was removed as director after a breakdown with the majority shareholder. He did not realise his investment but accepted a weekly allowance of roughly half his salary for over two years. When a rights issue was proposed, he objected; the majority offered to buy him out, but they could not agree a price, so he petitioned, alleging his exclusion and the rights issue were unfairly prejudicial. Hoffmann J held he could not complain of unfair treatment, having accepted payment for so long, and dismissed the petition. The High Court in Ang Xing Yao Lionel echoed that fairness is 'a two-way street'."},
    {t:"His exclusion was unfair and he was bought out", correct:false,
     why:"The petition was dismissed."},
    {t:"The rights issue was set aside", correct:false,
     why:"The court did not set aside the rights issue."},
    {t:"The allowance was an unlawful distribution", correct:false,
     why:"That was not in issue."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.067]", takeaway:"Accepting long-term compensation for exclusion can defeat a later complaint." },

{ id:"cl11-055", lesson:"11J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When a member is excluded after a no-fault fallout over personality or management style, what largely decides whether it is unfair?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether a fair offer has been made for his shares or he has been fairly compensated", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.068] says some exclusions follow fallouts caused by acute differences in personality, views or management style, without blame on either side. In such cases, whether the exclusion is unfair is largely decided by whether a fair offer has been made for the applicant's shareholding, or whether he has been fairly compensated for the exclusion. This mirrors O'Neill v Phillips, where Lord Hoffmann said a reasonable offer to buy out the excluded member at fair value can prevent the exclusion from being unfair. The majority need not keep the excluded member in management, but they cannot leave him locked in without a fair exit."},
    {t:"Whether he was the founder", correct:false,
     why:"Founder status is not the deciding factor."},
    {t:"Whether the articles allowed removal", correct:false,
     why:"Constitutional compliance does not answer equitable unfairness."},
    {t:"Whether the company is profitable", correct:false,
     why:"Profitability is not the key factor."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.066]–[11.068]", takeaway:"No-fault fallout: a fair buyout offer usually answers unfairness." },

{ id:"c11x-034", lesson:"11J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two equal quasi-partners in Ivy Pte Ltd fall out over strategy; neither is at fault. One is voted off the board. Case A: the other immediately offers to buy his shares at a fair value fixed by an independent valuer. Case B: no offer is made and he is left holding 50% of a company he cannot sell.",
  prompt:"In which case is the exclusion more likely to be unfair?",
  options:[
    {t:"Case B: in no-fault fallouts, unfairness largely depends on whether a fair offer or compensation is made; leaving him locked in without one is likely unfair", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.068] says that where exclusion follows a fallout over personality, views or management style without blame on either side, whether it is unfair is largely dictated by whether a fair offer has been made for the applicant's shares, or whether he has been fairly compensated. In Case A, a prompt offer at independently determined fair value gives him a fair exit, so exclusion is unlikely to be unfair. In Case B, he is excluded from management of a quasi-partnership and locked in with no buyer, which is the classic situation the oppression remedy addresses ([11.032]). A buyout order at fair value would be the likely remedy (11L)."},
    {t:"Case A, because he was forced to sell", correct:false,
     why:"A fair exit offer usually answers the unfairness of a no-fault exclusion."},
    {t:"Both equally, because he was excluded in both", correct:false,
     why:"The fair offer in Case A makes the difference."},
    {t:"Neither, because the removal complied with the constitution", correct:false,
     why:"In a quasi-partnership, constitutional compliance does not end the inquiry."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.068]", takeaway:"Fair offer → likely not unfair; locked in with no offer → likely unfair." },

{ id:"cl11-056", lesson:"11J", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Must oppression be suffered by the applicant in his capacity as member?",
  options:[
    {t:"Yes, but not narrowly: in a small company where members expect to earn their living as directors, exclusion from management can harm their interests as members", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.069] says s 216 is couched to protect the petitioner because of his status as member, and Tan Lee Meng J in Tan Choon Yong v Goh Jon Keat held the conduct 'must affect a member in his capacity as member'. A member may also be a director or creditor, and complaints in those capacities, such as removal from the board, could be said to have 'nothing to do with [his] status as a shareholder' (Re Lundie Brothers). A rigid qua member requirement would seriously constrain the remedy, and was partly blamed for the original UK provision's failure. Koh [11.070] says English courts do not construe it too narrowly: Hoffmann J explained that where members invest 'on the footing that dividends are unlikely but that each will earn his living by working for the company as a director', dismissal may prejudice their interests as members. Koh [11.072] says Singapore follows this, as in Kitnasamy and Eng Gee Seng v Quek Choon Teck."},
    {t:"No: any capacity will do", correct:false,
     why:"The qua member requirement exists, though it is read broadly."},
    {t:"Yes, strictly: director or employee complaints never count", correct:false,
     why:"Management exclusion can be a qua member interest in the right context."},
    {t:"Only for listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"The requirement applies generally, and is broadest for small private companies."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.069]–[11.070], [11.072]", takeaway:"Qua member: required but read broadly (management expectations)." },

{ id:"c11x-035", lesson:"11J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Case 1: Ravi, managing director of a large listed company, holds 0.1% of its shares and is dismissed under his service contract. Case 2: Siti, one of three founders who each invested equally in a small private company on the footing that each would work in it as a director, is removed from the board.",
  prompt:"Following Hoffmann J in Re a company (No 00477 of 1986), whose removal is more likely to prejudice interests 'as a member'?",
  options:[
    {t:"Siti's: in a small company built on members working as directors, the expectation of continued employment can be part of her interests as a member; Ravi's complaint is as an employee", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.070] quotes Hoffmann J: for the managing director of a large public company with a small shareholding, 'it is easy to see the distinction between his interests as a managing director employed under a service contract and his interests as a member'. But in a small private company where two or three members subscribed 'on the footing that dividends are unlikely but that each will earn his living by working for the company as a director', the distinction 'may be more elusive'. Their interests as members who ventured capital may include a legitimate expectation of continued employment as directors, so dismissal and exclusion may be unfairly prejudicial to their interests as members. Ravi's grievance is about his service contract (pursue it in contract). Siti's is a qua member grievance s 216 can address."},
    {t:"Ravi's, because he is more senior", correct:false,
     why:"Seniority does not convert an employment grievance into a qua member one."},
    {t:"Both equally", correct:false,
     why:"Hoffmann J drew exactly this contrast."},
    {t:"Neither, because directorship is never a member interest", correct:false,
     why:"In small companies it can be."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.070]", takeaway:"Large-co MD: employee complaint. Small-co founder-director: member complaint." },

{ id:"cl11-057", lesson:"11J", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"One of four equal shareholders provides working capital to the company through another company he controls. After a breakdown in relations, he is removed as director and chairman.",
  prompt:"What did Walker J hold in R H Electric v Haden Bill Electrical?",
  options:[
    {t:"Providing the capital was an essential part of the original arrangement and intimately connected with his membership, so he could expect to stay involved while the loan was outstanding", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.071] says courts have accepted that benefits a member expects as a creditor can be closely enough linked to his membership to be a qua member interest. In R H Electric v Haden Bill Electrical, the petitioner, one of four equal shareholders, provided working capital through another company he controlled (R H). After a breakdown, he was removed as director and chairman. The majority argued he suffered no prejudice as a shareholder because his real involvement was as agent for a loan creditor. Walker J found the provision of capital was 'an absolutely essential part' of the shareholders' original arrangement and intimately connected with his membership, so he could expect continued involvement while the loan was outstanding. The technical separateness of the petitioner and R H did not matter, since it made no difference to the majority where the money came from. Koh [11.072] says no Singapore case has yet directly vindicated a creditor interest this way."},
    {t:"His only interest was as a creditor's agent, so no relief", correct:false,
     why:"Walker J rejected that argument."},
    {t:"The lending company should have petitioned instead", correct:false,
     why:"The petitioner himself could rely on the link to his membership."},
    {t:"Equal shareholders can't claim exclusion", correct:false,
     why:"Equal shareholders can, as here."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.071]–[11.072]", takeaway:"R H Electric: creditor role tied to membership can be a qua member interest." }

]);

;
