{ id:"cl11-062", lesson:"11L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What constrains the court's wide discretion under s 216(2)?",
  options:[
    {t:"The order must aim to bring to an end or remedy the matters complained of, granting the remedy that removes the oppression found", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.083] says s 216(2) lets the court make such order as it thinks fit, a wide discretion constrained by the requirement that the order be made 'with a view to bringing to an end or remedying the matters complained of'. Relief should, as far as practicable, eliminate the effects of the oppression. Young J in Fexuto v Bosnjak said 'it is not enough merely to find oppression and then proceed to find some remedy that might bring peace to the company generally. The court should only grant the remedy that removes the oppression found.' The section lists possible orders, all ranking equally, but the list is indicative only; the court can tailor orders to the case."},
    {t:"Only orders listed in the section are available", correct:false,
     why:"The list is indicative; tailored orders are possible."},
    {t:"The court must grant exactly what was pleaded", correct:false,
     why:"The court is not bound by pleaded remedies ([11.084])."},
    {t:"Winding up must be ordered first", correct:false,
     why:"No order of priority; all listed orders rank equally."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.083]", takeaway:"s 216(2): wide, but aimed at ending/remedying the oppression found." },

{ id:"c11x-043", lesson:"11L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Must an applicant say what remedy he wants, and is the court bound by it?",
  options:[
    {t:"He should state what he primarily wants and justify it, but the court is not bound by the pleaded remedy", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.084] quotes Lord Cooper: the court has not been 'constituted an earthly Providence', and 'it is for the petitioners to state what they primarily want and to justify their request by relevant averments'. But the court is not constrained by pleaded remedies. In Low Peng Boon v Low Janie, the Court of Appeal replaced a buyout with winding up; in Re Gee Hoe Chan Trading, the court refused proportional board representation and wound up the company because the respondents could not fund a buyout; and in Lim Swee Khiang v Borden, the court ordered a buyout though winding up was sought."},
    {t:"He need not state any remedy; the court will choose", correct:false,
     why:"Lord Cooper said the petitioner should state and justify what he wants."},
    {t:"The court must grant exactly what is pleaded", correct:false,
     why:"The cases show courts substituting other remedies."},
    {t:"Only winding up may be pleaded", correct:false,
     why:"Any appropriate remedy may be sought."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.084]", takeaway:"State and justify your remedy; the court may order another." },

{ id:"cl11-063", lesson:"11L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"An applicant obtains a buyout order at trial. In this family company, the only respondent interested in carrying on the business is the patriarch, about 85, half paralysed and in a wheelchair.",
  prompt:"What did the Court of Appeal do in Low Peng Boon v Low Janie?",
  options:[
    {t:"Set aside the buyout and ordered winding up, as being in the interest of the company as a whole", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.084] says the applicant sought, and the trial judge granted, a buyout of her shares. The Court of Appeal set it aside because of the 'very unusual circumstances': a family company in which the only respondent interested in continuing the business was the patriarch, 'about 85 years old, half paralysed and wheelchair-bound'. Forcing a buyout would have left the company in the hands of someone unable to run it. A winding-up order was the most appropriate remedy, in the interest of the company as a whole."},
    {t:"Upheld the buyout", correct:false,
     why:"The buyout was set aside."},
    {t:"Ordered the patriarch to retire", correct:false,
     why:"The court ordered winding up."},
    {t:"Dismissed the application", correct:false,
     why:"Relief was granted, in a different form."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.084]", takeaway:"Low Peng Boon: buyout replaced by winding up." },

{ id:"cl11-064", lesson:"11L", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each case to the remedy outcome.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Re Gee Hoe Chan Trading", r:"Winding up, as respondents couldn't fund a buyout"},
    {l:"Lim Swee Khiang v Borden", r:"Buyout ordered though winding up was sought"},
    {l:"Re Hailey Group", r:"No order: receivership left nothing worth buying"},
    {l:"Grace v Biagioli", r:"Clean break is usually the most effective protection"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [11.084] says in Re Gee Hoe Chan Trading the court refused the proportional board representation sought because the parties were 'at loggerheads and mutual trust and confidence were no longer there', and wound the company up since the respondents could not afford to buy the applicants' shares. In Lim Swee Khiang v Borden, the court ordered the majority to buy out the applicants though they sought winding up. Koh [11.085] says the order depends on circumstances at the hearing: in Re Hailey Group, the company had gone into receivership, so its affairs could no longer be conducted unfairly, and a buyout would be 'tantamount to imposing a fine' since there was 'nothing of any value for them to purchase'; no order was appropriate. Koh [11.086] quotes Grace v Biagioli: in serious conflict, no regime the court can impose is likely to be as effective as a clean break.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.084]–[11.086]", takeaway:"Gee Hoe Chan (wind up) · Lim Swee Khiang (buyout) · Hailey (nothing) · Grace (clean break)." },

{ id:"cl11-065", lesson:"11L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The terms of a s 216 order depend on the circumstances when proceedings began.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.085] says the precise terms depend on the matters complained of and the circumstances at the time of the hearing, not at the start of proceedings. The court takes account of intervening events that make contemplated relief inappropriate. In Re Hailey Group, receivership in the meantime meant the company's affairs could no longer be conducted unfairly to the petitioner, and a buyout would be 'tantamount to imposing a fine' as there was 'nothing of any value for them to purchase'. So no order was appropriate."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"The court looks at circumstances at the hearing."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.085]", takeaway:"Relief is shaped at the hearing date, not filing (Re Hailey)." },

{ id:"cl11-066", lesson:"11L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why is a buyout so often the remedy in oppression cases?",
  options:[
    {t:"Relationships have usually broken down, so a 'corporate divorce' is more effective than any regime the court could impose to keep the peace", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.086] says oppression applications typically involve conflict and acrimony, sometimes a 'gravely deteriorated' relationship with real risk of recurring disputes. The most practical remedy is then very often 'a corporate divorce where one party buys the other out'. Grace v Biagioli said that in serious conflict 'it is unlikely that any regime or safeguards the court can impose will be as effective'. Koh [11.087] notes a clean break is not inevitable: in Re HR Harmer, the court regulated the father-founder's role (consultant at a salary, honorary life president without powers, no interference except through the board) instead of breaking up the company."},
    {t:"Section 216 makes buyout the default", correct:false,
     why:"All listed orders rank equally; buyout is common in practice."},
    {t:"Buyouts avoid valuing the shares", correct:false,
     why:"Buyouts usually require valuation ([11.090])."},
    {t:"Winding up isn't available under s 216", correct:false,
     why:"Winding up is available (11M)."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.086]–[11.087]", takeaway:"Buyout = corporate divorce; Harmer shows regulation can suffice." },

{ id:"c11x-044", lesson:"11L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The founder of a stamp-auction company, a respected expert and valuable asset to it, keeps treating the company as his personal property, ignoring board resolutions. His sons, co-directors, petition. The founder admits he misunderstood the limits of his powers.",
  prompt:"Following Re HR Harmer, what kind of order might suffice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Regulating the founder's role, for example engaging him as a paid consultant and honorary president without powers, and barring interference except through the board, without breaking up the company", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.087] says a clean break is not always necessary. In Re HR Harmer, sons of the founder of a philatelic auction business petitioned because their father, who controlled the votes, treated the company as his own, ignoring shareholders, his co-directors and board resolutions. Roxburgh J found oppression and, with the petitioners' agreement, ordered that the company engage the father as philatelic consultant at a named salary, that he not interfere except in accordance with valid board decisions, and that he be life president with no duties, rights or powers. Since he admitted a 'misapprehension' about his powers and was 'a man of international reputation ... and an asset to this company', those orders were all that was needed. The order was affirmed on appeal."},
    {t:"Winding up, since oppression was proved", correct:false,
     why:"Harmer shows regulation can remove the oppression without winding up."},
    {t:"A buyout of the founder at a penalty price", correct:false,
     why:"No buyout was needed, and s 216 relief is not punitive."},
    {t:"No order, because the founder controls the votes", correct:false,
     why:"Control does not prevent relief; the court regulated his conduct."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.087]", takeaway:"Re HR Harmer: tailored regulation instead of a corporate divorce." },

{ id:"c11x-045", lesson:"11L", type:"match",
  prompt:"Match each form of buyout under s 216(2)(d) to its description.",
  pairs:[
    {l:"Oppressor buys applicant's shares", r:"The most frequent order"},
    {l:"Company buys applicant's shares", r:"May involve a reduction of capital"},
    {l:"Applicant buys oppressor's shares", r:"Exceptional, being expropriatory"},
    {l:"Re Brenfield Squash Racquets Club", r:"86% holder ordered to sell to the 14% petitioner"}
  ],
  why:"Koh [11.088] says the buyout is probably the most common s 216 relief. Section 216(2)(d) contemplates the applicant's shares being purchased from him, by far the usual form, letting a locked-in minority realise his investment at fair value. Koh [11.089] says a frequent order is for the oppressor specifically to buy; the section also allows purchase by other members or by the company, in which case the order may provide for a capital reduction. Less common is a minority buyout, the applicant buying out the oppressor, sought for example by a founder emotionally tied to the company. In Re Brenfield Squash Racquets Club, FMR held 86% and ran the company for its own benefit; the petitioner and family held 14%. A shareholders' agreement triggered pre-emption if FMR got into financial difficulty, and it had. The court made the 'comparatively unusual' order that FMR sell to the petitioner. Minority buyouts are generally exceptional given their expropriatory nature.",
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.088]–[11.089]", takeaway:"Usual: oppressor buys. Also company (capital reduction). Rare: minority buys (Brenfield)." },

{ id:"c11x-046", lesson:"11L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What date is the starting point for valuing shares under a buyout order, and when may another date be chosen?",
  options:[
    {t:"The date of the order (Profinance Trust; Re London School of Electronics), but fairness may dictate an earlier date, such as the petition date where the respondents improved the company by their own efforts", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.090] says the court has an unfettered discretion over price, subject only to fairness, and the valuation date can make a big difference, as in Re Quest Exploration, where later events 'severely reduced' value. Koh [11.091] says there is no immutable rule. In Profinance Trust v Gladstone, the English Court of Appeal agreed with Nourse J in Re London School of Electronics that 'an interest in a going concern ought to be valued at the date on which it is ordered to be purchased', which best reflects what the shareholder is selling. But fairness is paramount and may require an earlier date such as the petition date. In London School of Electronics itself, Nourse J chose the petition date because the respondents had improved the company entirely through their own efforts, so a later valuation would be unfair to them."},
    {t:"Always the date the shareholder first invested", correct:false,
     why:"No such rule; Tullio Planeta used the acquisition price only on special facts."},
    {t:"Always the date of the oppressive act", correct:false,
     why:"An earlier date is possible where fairness requires, but it is not the rule."},
    {t:"Whatever date gives the applicant the highest price", correct:false,
     why:"The applicant is not entitled to a 'one-way bet' ([11.092])."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.090]–[11.091]", takeaway:"Valuation date: order date by default; fairness can shift it." },

{ id:"c11x-047", lesson:"11L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Before Olive Pte Ltd's minority petitions, the majority deliberately shifts profitable contracts to their own company to depress Olive's share value. Separately, Olive's value later falls further because of a market downturn unrelated to anyone's conduct. The minority asks for valuation at the date before the contracts were shifted.",
  prompt:"How is the court likely to approach the valuation date?",
  options:[
    {t:"An earlier date before the deliberate depreciation is permissible (Re Cumana), but the court will not pick a date just to insulate the applicant from genuine market falls: no 'one-way bet'", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.092] quotes Lawton LJ in In re Cumana: if the majority 'deliberately took steps to depreciate the value of the shares in anticipation of a petition being presented, it would be permissible to value the shares at a date before such action was taken'. Shifting contracts to depress value fits exactly. But the applicant is not entitled to a 'one-way bet, able to take advantage of any rise in the share value, but insulated from any fall', and the court 'will not direct an early valuation date simply to give the applicant the most advantageous exit'. So the court may choose a date before the deliberate depreciation, or adjust for it, but should not shield the minority from an unrelated market fall merely because that would be more favourable. As Nourse J said, fairness on the facts may reduce any general rule 'to no rule at all'."},
    {t:"The court must use the date of the order whatever the majority did", correct:false,
     why:"Re Cumana permits an earlier date to counter deliberate depreciation."},
    {t:"The court must pick the date most favourable to the minority", correct:false,
     why:"No 'one-way bet' for the applicant."},
    {t:"Valuation dates are fixed by statute", correct:false,
     why:"The court has a discretion guided by fairness."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.092]", takeaway:"Earlier date to undo deliberate depreciation; no 'one-way bet'." },

{ id:"c11x-048", lesson:"11L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"When might no valuation be needed at all?",
  options:[
    {t:"Where a fair result can be reached without it, for example ordering purchase at the original subscription price (Tullio Planeta) or initial capitalised value (Tan Yong San)", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.093] gives two examples. In Tullio Planeta v Maoro Andrea G, the company was inactive and wholly owned by the respondent and his wife; the applicant was invited in and bought half the shares at $7 each, but the parties fell out before any real business began. The Court of Appeal held a valuation unnecessary: ordering the respondent to buy back at the price paid was fair. In Tan Yong San v Neo Kok Eng, the majority was ordered to buy the applicant's 0.89% at initial capitalised value, the 'fairest price', because he had not paid for the shares and had left the running to the majority with no expectation of profit-sharing. As Quentin Loh JC said, he would be 'getting what he started off with – no more, no less'."},
    {t:"Never: valuation is always mandatory", correct:false,
     why:"Tullio Planeta and Tan Yong San show it can be unnecessary."},
    {t:"Only for listed companies", correct:false,
     why:"Both examples involved private companies."},
    {t:"Only if the parties agree a price", correct:false,
     why:"In both cases the court fixed the basis itself."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.093]", takeaway:"Sometimes return of the original price is fairest (Tullio Planeta; Tan Yong San)." },

{ id:"c11x-049", lesson:"11L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Two companies need valuing for s 216 buyouts. Company A is a profitable, growing software business with few tangible assets. Company B is still trading but has a long history of losses, and its balance sheet is dominated by two buildings.",
  prompt:"Which valuation methods are more appropriate?",
  options:[
    {t:"A: a method based on future earnings and growth (Yeo Hung Khiang); B: net tangible assets may be reasonable despite it trading (Dynasty v Coombs)", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.094] says the right method depends on the circumstances. The Court of Appeal in Yeo Hung Khiang v Dickson Investment said 'different basis of valuation may be suitable for different businesses'. For a profitable going concern, a method reflecting predicted future earnings and growth was more appropriate than net asset value, which would miss earnings potential. That fits Company A. But net assets may be appropriate even for a trading company if it has been loss-making: in Dynasty v Coombs, the Federal Court of Australia found net tangible asset valuation 'quite reasonable' for a trading company with a history of losses and a balance sheet dominated by two major fixed assets. That fits Company B."},
    {t:"Both on net assets, because that is the only accepted method", correct:false,
     why:"Yeo Hung Khiang favoured an earnings method for a profitable going concern."},
    {t:"Both on future earnings, because both are trading", correct:false,
     why:"Dynasty v Coombs accepted net assets for a loss-making trading company."},
    {t:"A on net assets, B on future earnings", correct:false,
     why:"This reverses the reasoning."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.094]", takeaway:"Earnings basis for profitable growth; net assets for loss-making asset-heavy." },

{ id:"c11x-050", lesson:"11L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In Yeo Hung Khiang, the trial judge enhanced the valuer's figure by 5% a year for seven years. How is such an adjustment characterised?",
  options:[
    {t:"As an element of compensation built into a fair price, valuing the shares as they would have been, not as damages or pre-judgment interest, which the court has no jurisdiction to award on the purchase sum", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.095] says the court may adjust the valued price. In Yeo Hung Khiang, the valuer's figure was raised 5% a year for seven years, from $3.46 to $4.67 per share, under the court's 'extensive discretionary power to effect justice'. This incorporates compensation: Lord Denning in Scottish Co-operative v Meyer said it gives oppressed shareholders 'what is in effect money compensation for the injury done to them', valuing shares 'as they would have been if events had followed a different course'. The judge said the petitioner had been denied his shareholding's benefit for seven years while the respondents 'seemingly plundered the company', and ignoring that would reward the oppressor. Koh [11.096] says this is not common law damages, since oppression is not a tort or breach of contract, and compensation is available only in an 'extremely limited context'. Nor is it interest: the Court of Appeal held it had no jurisdiction to award pre-judgment interest on the purchase sum without statutory authority."},
    {t:"As common law damages for the tort of oppression", correct:false,
     why:"Oppression is not a tort; it is not damages."},
    {t:"As pre-judgment interest on the purchase price", correct:false,
     why:"The Court of Appeal said it cannot award pre-judgment interest on the purchase sum."},
    {t:"As a penalty imposed on the majority", correct:false,
     why:"It is a fair-price adjustment, not a penalty."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.095]–[11.096]", takeaway:"Price uplift = compensation within fair value, not damages or interest." },

{ id:"c11x-051", lesson:"11L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"The court orders the majority of Hemp Pte Ltd, a quasi-partnership, to buy out a 25% minority who was unfairly excluded. The majority argue for a 30% discount because a 25% stake carries no control and is unmarketable.",
  prompt:"Is a minority discount likely?",
  options:[
    {t:"Unlikely: in a forced exit, especially from a quasi-partnership, the price is usually pro rata to the whole without discount, since the buyer consolidates its position and the seller is an unwilling vendor", correct:true,
     why:"Koh [11.097] says discounts for minority stakes are common in voluntary sales of private company shares, reflecting lack of marketability and control. But an oppression buyout bears little resemblance to a voluntary sale; a buyout by existing shareholders actually consolidates the purchaser's position. So courts, especially in quasi-partnerships, have generally declined to discount. Nourse J explained that where unfair prejudice makes it intolerable for the applicant to stay, sale is his only practical exit short of winding up, and it 'would not merely not be fair, but most unfair' to buy him out on the fictional basis of a free election to sell or 'on any other basis which involved a discounted price'. The correct course is 'to fix the price pro rata according to the value of the shares as a whole and without any discount, as being the only fair method of compensating an unwilling vendor of the equivalent of a partnership share'."},
    {t:"Likely: minority stakes are always discounted", correct:false,
     why:"Courts generally refuse discounts in oppression buyouts."},
    {t:"Likely, because the minority chose to petition", correct:false,
     why:"Petitioning because of unfair prejudice does not make him a willing seller."},
    {t:"A premium must be added for control", correct:false,
     why:"The usual approach is pro rata value without discount, not a control premium."}
  ],
  cite:"Koh Ch 11 [11.097]", takeaway:"Oppression buyouts: pro rata, no minority discount (Nourse J)." }

