  "options": [
   {
    "t": "Registration requires the company's intervention (it is not purely administrative)",
    "correct": true,
    "why": "In full: Registration requires the company's intervention (it is not purely administrative), and a share carries obligations as well as rights, so registration evidences the company's consent to a new contract binding the transferee to the constitution. Tjio [12.075] says the directors' discretion not to register, however circumscribed, 'shows that the intervention of the company is required to perfect the transfer, which is not a purely administrative act'. So it is difficult to see shares as assigned like an ordinary chose in action such as an unreified debt. '[T]he word share does not denote rights only – it denotes obligations also' (Lindley LJ, fn 289). 'Consequently, the common law basis for perfected share transfers should be a form of novation, with registration being evidence of consent by the company to the new contract.' From then, the transferee is fully bound by the constitution. This also explains difficulties over whether an equitable mortgagee is a 'person entitled to transfer' shares, as opposed to having a power of sale."
   },
   {
    "t": "Because shares are negotiable instruments",
    "correct": false,
    "why": "Shares are not negotiable instruments. The novation analysis rests on the company's consent and the obligations shares carry."
   },
   {
    "t": "Because registration is purely administrative",
    "correct": false,
    "why": "Tjio's point is that registration is not purely administrative; the company's intervention is required, which is what makes novation the better analogy."
   },
   {
    "t": "Because the transferor remains liable after registration",
    "correct": false,
    "why": "Novation replaces the transferor with the transferee as party to the constitutional contract. Past members' liability in winding up (s 250(1)) is a separate statutory rule."
   }
  ],
  "cite": "Tjio Ch 12 [12.075], fns 289–290",
  "takeaway": "Transfer = novation: company's consent via registration; obligations travel too."
