    "why": "In full: It is wider: besides voting shares, it covers debentures, rights and options over shares or debentures, contracts for future delivery, participatory interests and prescribed securities; and a director or CEO is deemed interested in securities held by a family member who is not also a director or CEO. Tjio [12.102] says that under the SFA, CEOs (since the 2009 amendments) and directors (previously under ss 165–166 of the Companies Act, now s 133 SFA) of listed corporations must notify the company of their interests in its securities. 'There are important differences compared to substantial shareholder notification, in that this has a wider scope: other than voting shares, it includes interests in debentures, rights and options in shares/debentures, and contracts for future delivery of shares, participatory interests, as well as any interest in prescribed securities.' Section 133(4) also deems the CEO or director interested in securities 'held by a family member who is not also a director or CEO'. By contrast, 'the substantial shareholder is only required to give notice of his interests in issued voting shares'. The wider net reflects insiders' informational advantage: any economic exposure they have to the company's securities is relevant to the market."
