/* DealCraft — Company Law Ch 14 · Lessons 14K Fixed vs Floating Charges, 14L Conceptual Basis of the Floating Charge (audited Oct 2026).
   Source: Tjio Ch 14 [14.070]–[14.083], fns 320–369. Replaces the 14K/14L questions in companyLaw14.js (old ids cl14-049…058 kept).
   [14.070] (priority of a fixed charge) previously untested. New ids: 14K c14x-110+, 14L c14x-130+. */
window.DC_BANK = window.DC_BANK || {};
window.DC_BANK.t1p4v2 = (window.DC_BANK.t1p4v2||[]).concat([

/* ---------- 14K Fixed vs Floating Charges ---------- */
{ id:"c14x-110", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"How is priority between competing equitable fixed charges determined, and what can upset it?",
  options:[
    {t:"First in time of creation prevails where the equities are equal, but a later bona fide purchaser of the legal interest for value without notice can defeat the equitable charge, and registration can supply constructive notice", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.070] explains that an equitable charge follows the ordinary rule for equitable interests: where the equities are equal, the interest created first prevails over later equitable interests. At law the equivalent idea is expressed through nemo dat (no one can give a better legal title than he has), and fn 320 notes the underlying principle is the same.\n\nThe main caveat is 'equity's darling': a later purchaser of the legal estate or interest for value and without notice takes free of the earlier equitable charge (fn 321 adds that a mere equity can be defeated by a later purchaser of a legal or equitable interest).\n\nThe company charge registration regime then modifies this. Registration can give constructive notice of the existence of a charge (not its detailed terms) to persons expected to search the register, which may affect the later party's conscience and reverse the priority they would otherwise have had. That is why the chapter says company charges have their own internal priority rules, especially for floating charges."},
    {t:"The first charge to be registered always prevails, regardless of when it was created", correct:false,
     why:"That is a first-to-file system. For company charges in Singapore, priority runs largely from creation; registration matters mainly through validity and constructive notice."},
    {t:"The later charge prevails because it reflects the most recent bargain", correct:false,
     why:"The default is the reverse: the first equitable interest in time prevails where the equities are equal."},
    {t:"Legal and equitable charges always rank equally and share pro rata", correct:false,
     why:"There is no pro rata sharing rule. A legal purchaser for value without notice can defeat a prior equitable charge outright."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.070], fns 320–322", takeaway:"Equitable charges: first in time, subject to equity's darling and constructive notice from registration." },

{ id:"c14x-111", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Kestrel Pte Ltd grants Bank A an equitable fixed charge over a printing press. The charge is duly registered with ACRA. Six months later Kestrel grants Bank B a legal mortgage over the same press. Bank B never searched the register and says it had no actual knowledge of Bank A's charge.",
  prompt:"Can Bank B claim priority as a bona fide purchaser of the legal interest without notice?",
  options:[
    {t:"Probably not: registration can fix a party expected to search the register with constructive notice of the charge's existence, which defeats the 'without notice' claim", correct:true,
     why:"Ordinarily an equitable charge can be defeated by 'equity's darling', a later purchaser of the legal interest for value without notice (Tjio [14.070]). Bank B, as legal mortgagee for value, would look like such a purchaser.\n\nBut the company charge registration regime modifies the ordinary property rules. Registration can constitute constructive notice of the existence of a charge to persons who are expected to search the register. A bank taking security over a company's assets is the paradigm of a person expected to search. If Bank B is fixed with constructive notice, it cannot say it took without notice, its conscience is affected, and Bank A's earlier charge keeps priority under the first-in-time rule.\n\nNote the limit: constructive notice extends to the existence of the charge, not its detailed terms and conditions. That limit matters most for floating charges and negative pledge clauses, which lesson 14N develops."},
    {t:"Yes: actual knowledge is required, and Bank B had none", correct:false,
     why:"The registration regime can supply constructive notice to those expected to search, so lack of actual knowledge is not decisive."},
    {t:"Yes: a legal interest always beats an equitable one", correct:false,
     why:"Only a legal purchaser for value without notice takes free. Notice, including constructive notice, defeats the claim."},
    {t:"No, because Bank B's mortgage is void for being second in time", correct:false,
     why:"Bank B's mortgage is valid; the question is only priority. It ranks behind Bank A, but it is not void."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.070], fns 321–322", takeaway:"Registration can give constructive notice and stop a later legal chargee being equity's darling." },

{ id:"cl14-049", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why does it matter whether a charge is fixed or floating?",
  options:[
    {t:"All floating charges must be registered, and fixed charges rank ahead of certain preferential debts, such as employees' wages, in receivership or winding up, while floating charges don't", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.071] gives two reasons. The first is registration. Under s 131 of the Companies Act 1967, fixed charges are registrable only where the nature of the underlying collateral demands it (land, book debts, goodwill and so on), whereas every floating charge is registrable under s 131(3)(g) whatever the collateral. Mislabelling a floating charge as fixed can therefore leave it unregistered and void against the liquidator.\n\nThe second, which the chapter calls perhaps even more important, is priority. Sections 86(1) and 203(5) of the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act 2018 give certain preferential debts, including employees' wages and salaries, priority over floating charges (but not fixed charges) in receivership and winding up respectively.\n\nThat is why lenders try to draft charges as fixed, and why courts look past labels to the substance, especially where preferential creditors stand to lose."},
    {t:"Only fixed charges need registration", correct:false,
     why:"It is the reverse. All floating charges are registrable under s 131(3)(g); fixed charges only where the collateral falls within a listed category."},
    {t:"Floating charges rank ahead of preferential debts", correct:false,
     why:"IRDA ss 86(1) and 203(5) put certain preferential debts, such as employees' wages, ahead of floating charges, not behind them."},
    {t:"It matters only for tax", correct:false,
     why:"The distinction drives both registration and priority against preferential creditors in insolvency."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.071], fns 323–324", takeaway:"Floating: always registrable (s 131(3)(g)) and ranks behind preferential debts (IRDA ss 86, 203)." },

{ id:"c14x-112", lesson:"14K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: A floating charge must extend over the company's whole undertaking.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.073] sets out Romer LJ's three characteristics in Re Yorkshire Woolcombers Association Ltd [1903] 2 Ch 284: a charge on a class of assets both present and future; a class that would change from time to time in the ordinary course of business; and freedom for the company, until the chargee takes some step, to carry on business in the ordinary way.\n\nThose criteria are not exhaustive, and nothing in them requires the charge to cover the entire undertaking. A floating charge can exist over only a portion of it, such as stock in trade or receivables (fn 330, Dresdner Bank AG v Ho Mun-Tuke Don).\n\nThis matters for priority. The usual rule is that the first interest created prevails, but a later floating charge over specific assets can defeat an earlier floating charge over the whole undertaking, which reverses the usual order. Lesson 14M covers those priority rules."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"A floating charge can be over part of the undertaking, which can even reverse the usual priority between two floating charges."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.073], fns 329–330", takeaway:"Re Yorkshire: three non-exhaustive indicia; a floating charge can cover part of the undertaking." },

{ id:"cl14-050", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What is now the key test for a fixed charge?",
  options:[
    {t:"Whether the chargee controls the charged assets, so the chargor can't deal with them free of the charge without consent", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.073] says the nature of a charge seems to turn on the amount of control over the collateral. In Re Cosslett (Contractors) Ltd [1998] Ch 495, Millett LJ put it this way: the essence of a fixed charge is that the chargor cannot deal with the charged asset free from the charge without the chargee's consent. The question is not whether the chargor has complete freedom to run its business, but whether the chargee is in control of the charged assets (Tjio [14.075]).\n\nLater decisions show that Romer LJ's first two criteria in Re Yorkshire (present and future assets; a class that changes in the ordinary course) are probably neither sufficient nor necessary (fn 331, Lord Scott in Spectrum). So a fixed charge over a shifting fund of book debts is possible if the chargee keeps enough control and the company cannot deal with them in the ordinary course of business (Tjio [14.074])."},
    {t:"Whether the assets are present and future", correct:false,
     why:"That is Romer LJ's first criterion, which later cases treat as neither sufficient nor necessary. A fixed charge can cover future book debts with enough control."},
    {t:"Whether the document calls it fixed", correct:false,
     why:"Labels do not govern. Courts characterise the charge by its substance, particularly the chargee's control."},
    {t:"Whether a negative pledge clause exists", correct:false,
     why:"Floating charges often contain negative pledges. Some restrictions on dealing do not make a charge fixed (Re Cosslett)."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.073]–[14.075], fns 331–339", takeaway:"Fixed charge = chargee control, not the nature of the assets." },

{ id:"c14x-113", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What did Siebe Gorman v Barclays Bank hold, and how did the financial community read it?",
  options:[
    {t:"That a company could give a fixed charge over book debts even though proceeds went into an ordinary current account it could draw on; many took the fixed/floating question to be simply one of construing the loan document", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.074] explains that Siebe Gorman Co Ltd v Barclays Bank Ltd [1979] 2 Lloyd's Rep 142 was only a first-instance decision, but it seemed to tolerate ambiguity about how much actual control a fixed charge over book debts and their proceeds needed. It held that a company could create a fixed charge over book debts where the proceeds were paid into a normal current account from which the chargor could draw.\n\nMany in the financial community concluded that whether a charge was fixed or floating was simply a matter of interpreting the particular instrument, so careful drafting could secure fixed status. The 'high point of contractarianism' came in Re New Bullas Trading Ltd [1994] BCC 36, which allowed different charges over the same collateral in its different forms.\n\nSiebe Gorman was later disapproved in Agnew and overruled by the House of Lords in Re Spectrum Plus Ltd [2005] 2 AC 680."},
    {t:"That book debts can never be the subject of a fixed charge", correct:false,
     why:"No case holds that. A fixed charge over book debts is possible where there is real control, such as a blocked account."},
    {t:"That a charge over book debts is a pledge", correct:false,
     why:"Book debts are intangible and cannot be possessed, so they cannot be pledged."},
    {t:"That only the House of Lords could decide characterisation questions", correct:false,
     why:"Siebe Gorman was a first-instance decision about the substance of the charge, not about which court decides."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.074], fns 332–335", takeaway:"Siebe Gorman: fixed label with free use of proceeds; later overruled in Spectrum." },

{ id:"c14x-114", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Plover Pte Ltd charges its stock in trade to a lender under a document headed 'Fixed Charge'. Plover may sell stock in the ordinary course of business without asking the lender, but it must not grant other security over the stock (a negative pledge) and must not sell it other than in the ordinary course.",
  prompt:"Do these restrictions make the charge fixed?",
  options:[
    {t:"No: some restrictions are consistent with a floating charge; what matters is whether the lender controls the assets, and Plover can still deal with them freely in the ordinary course", correct:true,
     why:"Millett LJ in Re Cosslett (Contractors) Ltd [1998] Ch 495 pointed out that while a chargor's unfettered freedom to deal is inconsistent with a fixed charge, the reverse does not follow. A floating charge can, and usually does, carry restrictions such as negative pledge clauses (Tjio [14.075]).\n\nThe chapter identifies a logical fallacy that had been read into Romer LJ's third characteristic: the idea that because a charge contained some contractual restrictions, the chargor could not deal with its assets 'in the ordinary way', even though in practice it largely still could. Drafters used minor, often legal rather than physical, restrictions to argue that a charge was fixed. Chao J noticed the point in Re Lin Securities (fn 338).\n\nHere Plover can sell stock in the ordinary course without consent. The lender does not control the assets, so the charge is floating despite its label and must be registered under s 131(3)(g)."},
    {t:"Yes: any contractual restriction on dealing makes a charge fixed", correct:false,
     why:"That is the fallacy Millett LJ identified. Restrictions such as negative pledges are typical of floating charges."},
    {t:"Yes, because the document is headed 'Fixed Charge'", correct:false,
     why:"Labels are not decisive. The court characterises the charge by its substance."},
    {t:"It is not a charge at all, only a contractual promise", correct:false,
     why:"Plover has given security over its stock. The question is only whether that security is fixed or floating."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.075], fns 337–339", takeaway:"Restrictions ≠ control. Freedom to deal in the ordinary course → floating." },

{ id:"cl14-054", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What does the chapter say should be the default or residual category of charge?",
  options:[
    {t:"The floating charge: a charge is usually a failed fixed charge, so the burden should be on whoever says it's fixed", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.075] argues that the analysis should start by identifying what a fixed charge requires, given its advantages in registration and priority. A security that fails those requirements is a floating charge, which is registrable whatever the collateral and may lose priority to preferential creditors under s 203 IRDA. In other words, the residual category should be the floating charge, because it is usually a failed fixed charge.\n\nThe chapter suggests Romer LJ took a 'wrong turn' by describing the characteristics of a floating charge, which drafters then avoided by adding minimal restrictions. Starting from the fixed charge removes that tactic.\n\nThe theme recurs. Re Cosslett found a floating charge where the parties had not contemplated one, and Diablo (CA) treated a lien on sub-freights as a floating charge from the point of agreement. Tjio [14.080] concludes that the burden of proof should be on the person asserting that a charge is fixed."},
    {t:"The fixed charge", correct:false,
     why:"Fixed status has to be earned through real control. Treating it as the default would reward drafting over substance."},
    {t:"No charge at all", correct:false,
     why:"Diablo (CA) treated a lien on sub-freights as a floating charge, not an unregistrable contractual device, which shows the floating charge is the fallback."},
    {t:"Whatever the parties label it", correct:false,
     why:"Labels do not govern. The legal consequences are those the law attaches to the arrangement's substance."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.075], [14.080]–[14.081]", takeaway:"Floating is the default; the burden is on whoever asserts fixed." },

{ id:"cl14-051", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A bank's 'fixed charge' over book debts requires proceeds to be paid into the company's ordinary current account, which the company can draw on freely.",
  prompt:"What is the position after Agnew and Re Spectrum Plus?",
  options:[
    {t:"It is a floating charge: a fixed charge needs actual control, such as payment into a blocked account; Siebe Gorman was wrong", correct:true,
     why:"In Agnew the Privy Council (Lord Millett) held that the approach in Siebe Gorman was wrong and stressed that a fixed charge over book debts and their proceeds needs actual control. Despite strong arguments that this would disrupt banking practice, the House of Lords in Re Spectrum Plus Ltd [2005] 2 AC 680 followed (Tjio [14.076]). To create a fixed charge over book debts, the bank must actually control the debts and their collection, typically by requiring payment into a blocked account.\n\nLord Walker said there was a public interest overriding unrestrained freedom of contract: making sure preferential creditors get the protection Parliament intended. In dicta, Spectrum also said that even a well-drafted arrangement can be altered by later conduct, such as how the account was actually used.\n\nHere the company can freely draw on the proceeds, so the bank lacks control and the charge is floating. Preferential creditors under s 203 IRDA will rank ahead of it."},
    {t:"It is fixed, as Siebe Gorman held", correct:false,
     why:"Siebe Gorman was overruled in Spectrum. Free use of the proceeds is inconsistent with a fixed charge."},
    {t:"It is fixed if the document says so", correct:false,
     why:"Labels do not govern; actual control does."},
    {t:"It's not a charge at all", correct:false,
     why:"It is still security over the book debts, but floating rather than fixed."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.074], [14.076], fn 341", takeaway:"No control of proceeds → floating (Agnew, Spectrum)." },

{ id:"c14x-115", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Why was judicial scrutiny of the 'fixed' label especially intense in Agnew and Spectrum, compared with a pure registration case like Re Cosslett?",
  options:[
    {t:"Because both fixed and floating charges over book debts are registrable, so the real issue was whether preferential creditors would rank ahead of the charge", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.076] points out that in Spectrum, as in Agnew and unlike Re Cosslett, the dispute was not simply about registration. With book debts as collateral, a fixed charge and a floating charge are both registrable (s 131(3)(f) and (g)), so registration could not decide the case.\n\nThe question was priority: preferential creditors under the equivalent of s 203 IRDA rank ahead of a floating charge that has been described as fixed, but not ahead of a genuine fixed charge. Third parties who never agreed to the bargain stood to lose. The chapter links this to its wider argument on recharacterising security: serious net externalities can justify the courts' intervention even without the State's interest in strict compliance with a statute.\n\nIts conclusion is that a fixed charge cannot be created by contract just because the parties want its consequences. Party autonomy is fine where there are no proprietary effects on third parties."},
    {t:"Because book debts can never be registered", correct:false,
     why:"Charges over book debts are registrable under s 131(3)(f) whether fixed or floating."},
    {t:"Because Re Cosslett involved fraud", correct:false,
     why:"Re Cosslett turned on characterisation and non-registration, not fraud."},
    {t:"Because the banks in Spectrum had failed to register", correct:false,
     why:"Registration was not the problem. The contest was with preferential creditors over priority."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.076], fns 341–342", takeaway:"Spectrum: the stakes were priority against preferential creditors, not registration." },

{ id:"cl14-052", lesson:"14K", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: After Spectrum, a lender can take a fixed charge over uncollected book debts and a floating charge over their proceeds, as Re New Bullas allowed.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Re New Bullas Trading Ltd [1994] BCC 36 was the high point of contractarianism: the English Court of Appeal accepted that there could be a fixed charge over book debts followed by a floating charge over their proceeds once collected (Tjio [14.074]).\n\nAfter Spectrum, Tjio [14.077] says it should have been quite clear that this is not possible, and Re New Bullas was overruled in this respect too. A fixed charge over book debts that leaves the debtor free to use the proceeds in the ordinary course of business is in substance a floating charge. In the chapter's metaphor, you cannot separate the fruit from the tree as a contractual matter when the two are inextricably linked in reality, as a book debt and its proceeds are.\n\nShares and their dividends are different (fn 343): a declared dividend is a personal right of the registered shareholder rather than a right attaching to the shares (Sandz Solutions v Strategic Worldwide Assets)."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"Re New Bullas was overruled on this point. Book debts and their proceeds are too closely linked to be charged separately."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.074], [14.077], fn 343", takeaway:"You can't split book debts from their proceeds." },

{ id:"c14x-116", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"On the narrow reading of Spectrum's ratio that Tjio describes, what did the House of Lords actually decide?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only how to construe a clause leaving the debtor discretion over the type and operation of the account for proceeds; the comments on post-contractual conduct were obiter", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.077] notes that Spectrum can be read narrowly. On that reading, the decision concerned only the construction of a clause that gave the debtor discretion over the type of account into which book-debt proceeds had to be paid and how that account was operated. Because the debtor kept that discretion, and could therefore use the proceeds in the ordinary course of business, the charge showed the indicia of a floating charge.\n\nOn this view, the statements that a stipulated blocked account which is not actually operated as one could turn a fixed charge floating were strictly obiter. It would remain largely a question of contractual construction whether a fixed charge has been created.\n\nFn 344 adds that lenders appear to have responded to Spectrum by making greater use of invoice discounting. Tjio [14.078] then explains why similar narrow arguments are unlikely to succeed in Singapore, given the local hypothecation cases."},
    {t:"That fixed charges over book debts are abolished", correct:false,
     why:"Spectrum does not abolish them. A fixed charge over book debts remains possible with real control, such as a blocked account."},
    {t:"That post-contractual conduct is always decisive", correct:false,
     why:"On the narrow reading, the comments about later conduct were obiter, not part of the ratio."},
    {t:"That preferential creditors rank behind every charge", correct:false,
     why:"The opposite: preferential creditors rank ahead of floating charges, which is why characterisation mattered."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.077], fn 344", takeaway:"Narrow Spectrum: construction of a discretion clause; conduct comments obiter." },

{ id:"cl14-053", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"In the mid-1980s stockbroking collapses, banks took letters of hypothecation over share certificates held at brokers. Brokers could remove certificates if they replaced them with equivalent value or cash, and gave daily reports.",
  prompt:"How did Singapore courts characterise these charges?",
  options:[
    {t:"As floating charges, void for non-registration, because the brokers remained free to deal in and substitute the shares; labels were irrelevant", correct:true,
     why:"In the cases arising from the Pan El crisis and the later stockbroking collapse, starting with Re Lin Securities [1988] 1 SLR(R) 220, the banks failed at first instance and in the Court of Appeal to show they held fixed security over uncollected share certificates left with the brokers (Tjio [14.078]). Labels were irrelevant and enforcement paramount.\n\nIt did not help that daily certificates tracked the movement of the shares, that the shares were kept in safes labelled in the bank's name, or that removed certificates had to be replaced by others of equivalent value or a cash deposit (Tjio [14.079]). The brokers could still remove and substitute shares without consulting the banks, so the banks lacked control.\n\nA fixed charge over investment shares would not have needed registration under s 131(3), but a floating charge does whatever the collateral. Having failed to register, the banks ended up unsecured (Tjio [14.080]). It was common ground that a fixed charge would have existed if the banks had actually held and controlled the shares (Re EG Tan, fn 348)."},
    {t:"As fixed charges, because the documents said so", correct:false,
     why:"The courts held that labels were irrelevant. What counted was the brokers' freedom to deal."},
    {t:"As pledges", correct:false,
     why:"The certificates stayed with the brokers, so the banks lacked the possession a pledge requires."},
    {t:"As valid unregistered fixed charges", correct:false,
     why:"They were floating charges, and floating charges are always registrable, so non-registration was fatal."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.078]–[14.080], fns 345–348", takeaway:"Freedom to substitute → floating → void if unregistered." },

{ id:"c14x-117", lesson:"14K", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"In the Singapore hypothecation cases, a court ordered the brokers to deliver up the share certificates to the bank. What was that order treated as?",
  options:[
    {t:"The crystallisation of a floating charge that had existed, and needed registration, from the start; not evidence that the charge was fixed from inception", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.079] explains that when delivery up was ordered by a court, the event was treated as the crystallisation of the floating charge. The floating charge had been created from the outset and was registrable as such. The court's order was not evidence that the bank had held a fixed charge all along (Re City Securities Ltd [1990] 1 SLR(R) 413, affirmed in Dresdner Bank AG v Ho Mun-Tuke Don [1992] 3 SLR(R) 307, fn 347).\n\nThis follows from the control test. During the life of the arrangement the brokers could remove and substitute certificates without consulting the bank. Control obtained only at the end, by court order, cannot convert the security retrospectively.\n\nThe practical point is that a lender cannot cure a registration failure by later taking control. If the charge was floating when created, it had to be registered within the statutory period, and crystallisation does not save it."},
    {t:"A fresh fixed charge that did not need registration", correct:false,
     why:"Crystallisation does not create a new charge. The original floating charge settles on the assets."},
    {t:"Proof that the charge had been fixed from the beginning", correct:false,
     why:"The courts expressly rejected that argument. The brokers' earlier freedom to deal made the charge floating."},
    {t:"A pledge, because the bank finally obtained possession", correct:false,
     why:"Delivery up was treated as crystallisation of the existing charge, not the creation of a pledge."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.079], fns 347–348", takeaway:"Later control by court order = crystallisation, not proof of a fixed charge." },

{ id:"c14x-118", lesson:"14K", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"A shipowner's charterparty gives it a lien on all sub-hires and sub-freights due to the charterer. The charterer, a Singapore company, goes into liquidation. The lien was never registered as a company charge.",
  prompt:"Following Diablo (CA), what is the position?",
  options:[
    {t:"The lien created a floating charge from the point of agreement, which is void against the liquidator for non-registration under s 131(3)(g)", correct:true,
     why:"In Diablo Fortune Inc v Duncan, Cameron Lindsay [2018] 2 SLR 129 (Diablo (CA)), a full Court of Appeal (Chong JA) upheld the first-instance decision that a shipowner's lien on sub-hires and sub-freights belonging or due to a charterer or sub-charterer created a charge over them. It was not merely an unregistrable contractual device but a floating charge from the point of agreement, and so it was void against the liquidator for non-registration under s 131(3)(g) of the Companies Act 1967 (Tjio [14.081]).\n\nThe case sits at the other end of the spectrum from Spectrum: the contest was between a floating charge and no registrable security, not between fixed and floating. But it confirms that the default is a floating charge. Parties can contract as they like, but the legal consequences are those the law attaches, and security must fit a recognised form that may require public disclosure to protect third parties.\n\nThe Court of Appeal has since acknowledged again the inherent weakness of the floating charge (Malayan Banking v Bakri Navigation, fn 359)."},
    {t:"The lien is a purely contractual right that needs no registration", correct:false,
     why:"Diablo (CA) rejected that characterisation and held it was a floating charge."},
    {t:"The lien is a fixed charge that did not need registration", correct:false,
     why:"The court held it was floating from the point of agreement. Floating charges always need registration."},
    {t:"The lien is valid because liens are well known in shipping", correct:false,
     why:"The court accepted such liens are widely known but still held them registrable floating charges, void if unregistered."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.081], fns 356–359", takeaway:"Diablo (CA): lien on sub-freights = floating charge; void if unregistered." },

/* ---------- 14L Conceptual Basis of the Floating Charge ---------- */
{ id:"cl14-055", lesson:"14L", type:"fill",
  prompt:"Lord Macnaghten in Illingworth v Houldsworth: a floating charge is '____ and shifting in nature, hovering over and so to speak floating with the property'.",
  accept:["ambulatory"], answer:"ambulatory",
  why:"Tjio [14.072] contrasts the two charges. A fixed charge attaches to specific property and follows it wherever it goes, sometimes even into a transformed state. A floating charge does not latch permanently onto identifiable property (unless one treats a separate notional fund as that property) until it crystallises into a fixed charge. It is therefore security over a changing class of property.\n\nLord Macnaghten's description in Illingworth v Houldsworth captures this: the floating charge is 'ambulatory and shifting in nature', hovering over the property it is meant to affect until some event or act causes it to settle and fasten on the property within its reach and grasp. That settling is crystallisation.\n\nThe chapter also notes a competing view (Nolan, fn 326) that the floating charge is an existing charge over present assets which is subject to overreaching or substitution. Lesson 14L's other questions examine these theories.",
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.072], fns 325–328", takeaway:"Floating = ambulatory until crystallisation." },

{ id:"c14x-130", lesson:"14L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What legal development made the floating charge possible, and why has it been so useful?",
  options:[
    {t:"Recognising that future property could be assigned presently without a fresh act of assignment when it came into existence; it lets a company use fluctuating assets like stock in trade as collateral while it keeps trading", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.082] calls the floating charge a remarkable invention of equity. It gives the creditor security while letting the debtor carry on trading with the collateral in the ordinary course of business.\n\nThis has facilitated corporate growth, because much of an industrial or manufacturing company's assets is stock in trade, a fluctuating body of assets. A fixed charge would freeze that stock; the floating charge lets it be used as collateral while it turns over.\n\nThe doctrinal step that assisted its development was the recognition that future property could be assigned presently, without a fresh act of assignment when the property came into existence (Holroyd v Marshall (1862) 10 HL Cas 191, fn 360). Without that, each new item of stock would need a new security transaction.\n\nThe chapter then notes the continuing debate about whether a floating charge is present security at all, since present security seems to need collateral that exists now."},
    {t:"The statutory registration system, which created floating charges by legislation", correct:false,
     why:"The floating charge is a creation of equity. Registration regulates it but did not create it."},
    {t:"The rule in Dearle v Hall on notice to debtors", correct:false,
     why:"Dearle v Hall governs priority between assignments of certain choses in action, not the origin of the floating charge."},
    {t:"The abolition of fixed charges over stock", correct:false,
     why:"Fixed charges over stock were never abolished. The floating charge simply offers a workable alternative for circulating assets."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.082], fn 360", takeaway:"Equity's invention: present assignment of future property (Holroyd v Marshall)." },

{ id:"cl14-057", lesson:"14L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: When a floating charge crystallises, a new fixed charge is created.",
  options:[
    {t:"False", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.082] notes the debate over whether a floating charge is present security, since present security seems to need collateral that actually exists now. The increasingly accepted view is that the floating charge is present security.\n\nIt follows that even after crystallisation converts the floating charge into a fixed charge, it is still often treated, particularly by statute, as having begun life as a floating charge, so no new charge is created on crystallisation (Dresdner Bank AG v Ho Mun-Tuke Don [1992] 3 SLR(R) 307, fn 361).\n\nThis has practical effects. Statutory provisions that apply to floating charges, such as registration under s 131(3)(g) and the priority of preferential debts under the IRDA, continue to apply to a charge created as floating even after it crystallises. A lender cannot escape them by pointing to crystallisation."},
    {t:"True", correct:false,
     why:"No new charge arises. The original charge, which began life as a floating charge, simply attaches to specific assets."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.082], fn 361", takeaway:"Crystallisation ≠ new charge; statute still treats it as floating in origin." },

{ id:"cl14-056", lesson:"14L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"Which theories of the floating charge does the chapter describe?",
  options:[
    {t:"Present security over a notional fund of shifting contents; or fixed present security over existing assets, defeasible because the chargor is authorised to deal with them", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.083] describes two main explanations. The first treats the floating charge as present security over a notional fund whose contents constantly shift and may include future property. It depends on the idea that a fund exists separately from its components, which can seem artificial and may reflect US cases that more readily treat structures like the trust as separate entities (fns 362–363).\n\nThe second avoids the notional fund. It treats the floating charge as fixed, present security over the company's existing assets, but defeasible or limited because the assets may be overreached when the company uses them in the ordinary course of business, as the chargee has authorised (Worthington; Nolan, fns 364–365). The charge then attaches automatically to substituted assets because the agreements contemplated this.\n\nThe Court of Appeal in Diablo (CA) said these theories 'fail to capture the significance of the event of crystallisation': the charge attaches to the assets in specie only on crystallisation, not from the start (fn 367)."},
    {t:"A contractual right with no security element", correct:false,
     why:"Both theories, and the Singapore courts, treat the floating charge as a security interest."},
    {t:"A trust for the chargee", correct:false,
     why:"Trust analogies are used to explain the ambulatory nature of the interest, but neither theory says the floating charge is a trust."},
    {t:"A pledge of stock in trade", correct:false,
     why:"A pledge needs possession, which a floating chargee does not have."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.083], fns 362–367", takeaway:"Notional fund vs defeasible fixed charge; Diablo stresses crystallisation." },

{ id:"c14x-131", lesson:"14L", type:"scenario",
  scenario:"Wren Pte Ltd gave a floating charge over its stock to a lender. In the ordinary course of business Wren sells a batch of stock to a customer and buys new stock with the proceeds. The charge has not crystallised.",
  prompt:"How does the 'defeasible fixed security' theory explain what happens?",
  options:[
    {t:"The charge is a present interest in the existing stock, overreached when Wren sells in the ordinary course as authorised, and it attaches automatically to the new stock because the agreement contemplated substitution", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.083] describes a more recent argument that avoids the need for a notional fund. On this view, the floating charge is a form of fixed and present security over the company's existing assets, but it is defeasible or limited: the assets can be overreached because the chargee has authorised the company to use them in the ordinary course of business (Worthington, fn 364; Nolan, fn 365).\n\nSo when Wren sells a batch of stock in the ordinary course, the customer takes free of the charge because the sale falls within Wren's authority to deal. When Wren buys replacement stock, the charge attaches to it automatically, again because the loan and charge agreements contemplated substitution before crystallisation. The chapter notes the analogy with beneficiaries' interests under a trust fund, which are also ambulatory (fn 366).\n\nThe weakness, according to Diablo (CA), is that the theory does not give enough weight to crystallisation, which is when the charge actually attaches to the assets in specie."},
    {t:"The charge never touched the sold stock and attaches only to whatever Wren owns when it crystallises", correct:false,
     why:"That is closer to the Diablo (CA) view of crystallisation. The defeasible-security theory says the charge does affect existing assets, subject to overreaching."},
    {t:"The customer takes the stock subject to the charge", correct:false,
     why:"Sales in the ordinary course are authorised, so under either theory the customer takes free."},
    {t:"Each new purchase of stock requires a fresh charge", correct:false,
     why:"Substituted assets are caught automatically because the agreement contemplated them; no fresh charge is needed."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.083], fns 364–366", takeaway:"Defeasible theory: present charge, overreached by authorised dealing, attaches to substitutes." },

{ id:"c14x-132", lesson:"14L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"According to Chong JA in Diablo (CA), when does a floating chargee have a proprietary interest in the charged assets?",
  options:[
    {t:"Only after crystallisation, although the floating charge confers an immediate security interest", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.083] quotes Chong JA in Diablo Fortune Inc v Duncan, Cameron Lindsay [2018] 2 SLR 129 at [47]: on a conceptual level, a floating charge confers an immediate security interest, but the chargee enjoys a proprietary interest in the charged assets only after crystallisation (fn 369).\n\nThis is why the Court of Appeal said the notional-fund and defeasible-security theories fail to capture the significance of crystallisation. The charge attaches to the assets in specie only on crystallisation, not from the start.\n\nIt also fits the Singapore courts' pragmatic view: the floating charge creates an equity similar to a fixed charge, with a licence for the chargor to keep using the assets in the ordinary course of business, and that equity is a security interest that may not be proprietary (Malayan Banking v Bakri Navigation [2020] 2 SLR 167 at [82], fn 368). Fn 369 notes the contrast with Lord Walker in Spectrum, who said the chargee has a proprietary interest, but in a fund of circulating capital."},
    {t:"From the moment of creation, in every asset within the class", correct:false,
     why:"Chong JA rejected that. The interest is a security interest from creation, but proprietary only after crystallisation."},
    {t:"Never: a floating charge is purely contractual", correct:false,
     why:"It confers an immediate security interest, and a proprietary interest once it crystallises."},
    {t:"Only once the charge is registered", correct:false,
     why:"Registration protects the charge from being void against the liquidator; it does not make it proprietary."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.083], fns 367–369", takeaway:"Diablo (CA): immediate security; proprietary only on crystallisation." },

{ id:"cl14-058", lesson:"14L", type:"mcq",
  prompt:"What pragmatic view have Singapore courts taken of the floating charge?",
  options:[
    {t:"It creates an equity similar to a fixed charge, with a licence for the chargor to use the assets in the ordinary course of business; a security interest that may not be proprietary", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.083] observes that the floating charge has many underlying theories, and while each explains some feature, none explains all of them, so the charge is hard to reconcile with some property concepts.\n\nSingapore courts have responded pragmatically. They treat the floating charge as creating some form of equity similar to a fixed charge, coupled with a licence for the chargor to keep using the charged assets in the ordinary course of business (Malayan Banking Bhd v Bakri Navigation Co Ltd [2020] 2 SLR 167 at [82], citing Gough, fn 368). That equity is a security interest but may not be proprietary.\n\nThe view fits Diablo (CA): immediate security, with a proprietary interest only on crystallisation. It also explains why the Court of Appeal has stressed the floating charge's inherent weakness, with little 'asset constraint', compared with fixed security (Tjio [14.081])."},
    {t:"It is a full proprietary interest from day one in every asset", correct:false,
     why:"The courts say the equity may not be proprietary before crystallisation."},
    {t:"It is merely a personal contractual right", correct:false,
     why:"It is treated as a security interest, not a mere personal right."},
    {t:"It is void unless crystallised", correct:false,
     why:"A floating charge is valid before crystallisation, provided it is registered."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.081], [14.083], fns 359, 368", takeaway:"Singapore: equity + licence to deal; security, maybe not proprietary." },

{ id:"c14x-133", lesson:"14L", type:"tf",
  prompt:"True or false: The 'notional fund' explanation of the floating charge depends on treating the fund as existing separately from the assets that make it up from time to time.",
  options:[
    {t:"True", correct:true,
     why:"Tjio [14.083] explains that the floating charge may be present security over a notional fund whose contents are constantly shifting and may include future property (Goode and Gullifer, fn 362). This depends on the argument that a fund has an existence separate from its component parts: the chargee's interest is in the fund, not in any particular item within it.\n\nThe chapter says this notional fund may seem somewhat artificial, and may be influenced by US cases that more readily accept the separate entity status of structures such as the trust (Tjio, 'Lending to a Trust', fn 363).\n\nThe alternative, defeasible-security theory was developed partly to avoid needing a notional fund. Diablo (CA) criticised both theories for underplaying crystallisation."},
    {t:"False", correct:false,
     why:"The separate existence of the fund is exactly what the notional-fund theory relies on. That is why the chapter calls it somewhat artificial."}
  ],
  cite:"Tjio Ch 14 [14.083], fns 362–363", takeaway:"Notional fund = a fund separate from its shifting contents." }

]);

;
